// C/P Practice Test flashcards: original cards that practice what the Chem/Phys questions of AAMC Practice Exams 1,
// 3, 4, 5 and 6 ask (the pe1-cp … pe6-cp cards of data/qb-aamc-fl.js), plus the cards (cpt-st-*) that twin the daily
// quiz's Sample Test and QBank-pack questions. TODAY's C/P flashcard step uses only these cards (masteryFlashPool in js/mastery.js).
// Same ask, same setup: `twinOf` names the question(s) each card mirrors, asked the same way with the same kind of
// choices; a passage question's card carries a short twin passage (`passage`, 3–6 sentences), a stand-alone's has none.
// `from` names the spec items (peX-cp:Q#n) the card teaches; `aamc`, `disc` and `skill` come from a source's spec line.
// Original wording, numbers and compounds only: no AAMC passage, question, number or answer choice is reproduced.
// Format: {id, s, disc, aamc, skill, from, twinOf, passage?, q, choices: [4], ans: 0-3, elim, rule, fast, exp, how, teach}
// (tools/test-cp-test-cards.cjs checks all of it).
const CP_TEST_CARDS = [
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-01a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:1#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:1"],"passage":"A student separates four neutral compounds by paper chromatography. Cellulose paper holds a thin film of water on its –OH groups, and this bound water acts as the stationary phase. The mobile phase is toluene, a nonpolar solvent that climbs the strip by capillary action. Compounds that hydrogen-bond strongly with the bound water spend more time in it and lag behind the solvent front. The student loaded different amounts of each compound, so the spots differ in size.","q":"Which compound shown will travel the shortest distance from the origin?","choices":["Cyclohexanone","Tetrahydropyran","Hexane-1,5-diol","Octane"],"choiceFigs":["cyclohexanone","tetrahydropyran","hexane-1-5-diol","n-octane"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"An alkane has no polar group, so it stays with the nonpolar toluene and runs closest to the front."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A ketone C=O can only accept H-bonds; with no O–H it binds the water less than a compound with donors."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"An ether oxygen only accepts H-bonds and has no O–H to donate, so it is held only weakly."}],"rule":"On a polar stationary phase, count O–H/N–H donors: the most H-bonding compound moves least. Spot size reflects the amount loaded, not the distance.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Water-coated paper is a polar phase, so look for O–H groups. Only one compound has O–H donors (two of them); the ketone and ether only accept, and the alkane does neither."},"exp":"The bound water is a **polar stationary phase** and toluene is a nonpolar mobile phase. Whichever compound forms the **strongest hydrogen bonds** with the water lags most and travels the **shortest distance** (smallest Rf). Hexane-1,5-diol carries **a pair of O–H groups** that both donate and accept H-bonds; cyclohexanone and tetrahydropyran only accept, and octane does neither. Spot size only reflects how much was loaded.","how":{"ask":"Find the compound held most strongly by the polar stationary phase.","know":"• A polar stationary phase holds compounds that hydrogen-bond with it; they move least (small Rf).\n• O–H and N–H donate H-bonds; C=O and ether O only accept.\n• Spot size depends on the amount loaded, not on Rf.","steps":[{"text":"The stationary phase is water held on cellulose: polar.","hl":"this bound water acts as the stationary phase"},{"text":"The alkane has no polar group at all, so it rides with toluene near the front.","strike":["Octane"]},{"text":"The ketone and the ether can only accept H-bonds; neither has an O–H.","strike":["Cyclohexanone","Tetrahydropyran"]},{"text":"Two O–H groups give the strongest hold on the water. Answer: hexane-1,5-diol."}],"trap":"Linking spot size to distance, or picking the ketone for its strong C=O dipole: on a water phase, O–H donors hold hardest."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"this bound water acts as the stationary phase","note":"polar phase"},{"t":"toluene, a nonpolar solvent","note":"nonpolar mobile phase"},{"t":"hydrogen-bond strongly with the bound water","note":"held back"},{"t":"the spots differ in size","note":"amount, not distance"}]},{"text":"**Rank H-bonding** with the bound water.","calc":["two O–H: donor and acceptor","C=O or ether O: acceptor only","alkane: neither"]},{"text":"**Most H-bonding = shortest distance = smallest Rf.**"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a polar stationary phase, the compound with the most O–H/N–H donors moves least; spot size only shows how much was loaded."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-02a","s":"Research Design & Reasoning","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:2#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:2"],"passage":"In a classic experiment, a team sealed methane, ammonia, hydrogen and water vapor in a flask and passed electric sparks through the mixture for a week. The residue contained several amino acids, including glycine and alanine. Because the sparks mimic lightning, the team argued that what happened in the lab also happened in nature. They concluded that amino acids arose on the primitive Earth.","q":"According to the passage, the team's argument requires that:","choices":["those gases were present then.","the yield exceeded half the methane.","the products were optically pure.","sparks speed up every reaction."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Any yield, even a trace, shows amino acids can form; the amount isn't what the conclusion rests on."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Optical purity isn't needed: the claim is only that amino acids formed, in whatever mix of forms."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Too sweeping: the argument concerns these gases only, so a claim about every reaction isn't needed."}],"rule":"A required assumption bridges the lab result to the claim's real setting; details of the experiment's yield or purity don't.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Lab result = 'could happen'; 'did happen' needs the same inputs back then. Pick the choice saying the ingredients existed on early Earth; yield, purity and all-reactions claims are irrelevant."},"exp":"A lab synthesis shows what **can** happen. To conclude it **did** happen on the early Earth, the **starting materials and conditions** (here methane, ammonia and electrical discharge) must have existed then. Yield, purity and a claim about all reactions do not bear on that.","how":{"ask":"Find the unstated premise that must be true for the lab result to support the historical claim.","know":"• **Assumption**: a premise the conclusion needs but does not state; if it were false, the conclusion would fail.\n• A lab model of a past event supports the claim only if its **inputs and conditions** existed at that time.","steps":[{"text":"The conclusion is about the early Earth; the evidence is from a lab.","hl":"amino acids arose on the primitive Earth"},{"text":"Test each choice: if it were false, would the argument fail?","calc":["gases absent then → the lab result says nothing about then → fails","low yield → some still formed → stands"]},{"text":"Yield, optical purity and all reactions are not needed for the conclusion.","strike":["the yield exceeded half the methane.","the products were optically pure.","sparks speed up every reaction."]},{"text":"Answer: the gases must have been present on the early Earth."}],"trap":"Choosing a detail about the experiment's quality; the gap is between the lab and the early Earth."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"on the primitive Earth","note":"the conclusion"},{"t":"in the lab","note":"the evidence"},{"t":"requires","note":"find the assumption"}]},{"text":"**Split conclusion from evidence.** The evidence is a lab result; the conclusion is about the past."},{"text":"**Bridge the gap.** For the lab to stand in for the early Earth, the same ingredients had to be there."},{"text":"**Negate to test.** If the gases were absent, the argument collapses, so that is the assumption.","calc":["negate the choice → does the conclusion fail?"]},{"text":"**Also:** an assumption is the unstated premise whose denial would break the conclusion."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a lab model supports a claim about the past only if its starting materials and conditions existed then."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-03a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:3#1","pe1-cp:1#1","pe3-cp:46#1","pe6-cp:23#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:3"],"passage":"A student follows a reaction by thin-layer chromatography on a silica plate. The baseline is 1.0 cm above the plate's lower edge, and the crude product is spotted on it. After development the student measures everything from that edge: the solvent front is at 9.0 cm and the center of the product spot is at 4.2 cm. The plate is then dried and photographed for the lab notebook.","q":"What is the retention factor (Rf) of the product?","choices":["0.36","0.40","0.47","2.5"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"Rf can never exceed 1: a spot can't travel farther than the solvent front."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"That's 4.2 ÷ 9.0, the raw readings; both distances must be measured from the baseline, not the plate edge."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"The baseline was taken off the spot but not the front; subtract it from both readings."}],"rule":"Rf is between 0 and 1, and both distances are measured from the baseline. Round each distance to 1 digit, then divide.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Shift both readings down by 1: 3.2 over 8. Halve twice: 0.8 over 2 = 0.40. Anything above 1 (2.5) is out instantly."},"exp":"**Rf = spot distance ÷ solvent-front distance, both from the baseline.** Spot: 4.2 − 1.0 = 3.2 cm. Front: 9.0 − 1.0 = 8.0 cm. Rf = 3.2 ÷ 8.0 = **0.40**.","how":{"ask":"Measure both distances from the baseline, then divide.","know":"**Rf = distance moved by the compound ÷ distance moved by the solvent front**, both measured from the **baseline** where the sample was spotted. Rf is between 0 and 1.","steps":[{"text":"The distances are given from the edge, but the sample started at the baseline.","hl":"baseline is 1.0 cm above the plate's lower edge","fig":"cpt-tlc-plate"},{"text":"Subtract the baseline from both readings.","calc":["spot: 4.2 − 1.0 = **3.2 cm**","front: 9.0 − 1.0 = **8.0 cm**"]},{"text":"Divide.","calc":["Rf = 3.2 ÷ 8.0 = **0.40**"]},{"text":"0.47 skips the baseline (4.2 ÷ 9.0), 0.36 subtracts it from only the spot (3.2 ÷ 9.0), and 2.5 is inverted (8.0 ÷ 3.2).","strike":["0.47","0.36","2.5"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.40."}],"trap":"Dividing the raw readings (4.2 ÷ 9.0 = 0.47): both distances must start at the baseline."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"1.0 cm","note":"where the sample started"},{"t":"9.0 cm","note":"solvent front, from the edge"},{"t":"4.2 cm","note":"spot, from the edge"}]},{"text":"**Start at the baseline.** The race starts where the sample was spotted, not at the plate's edge.","calc":["spot: 4.2 − 1.0 = 3.2 cm","front: 9.0 − 1.0 = 8.0 cm"]},{"text":"**Divide spot by front.**","calc":["Rf = 3.2 ÷ 8.0 = 0.40"]},{"text":"**Also:** on silica, the compound with the strongest polar groups (O–H or N–H first) has the smallest Rf."},{"text":"**Also:** a low Rf means strong binding to the stationary phase; a high Rf means the compound prefers the mobile phase."},{"text":"**Also:** only N–H, O–H and F–H donate hydrogen bonds; C=O and ether oxygens can only accept them."},{"text":"**Also:** in chromatography, Rf is characteristic of the compound under fixed conditions; spot size only reflects how much was applied."},{"text":"**Also:** every Rf lies between 0 and 1; a larger value means the ratio is inverted."},{"text":"**Also:** sugar-based column materials (polysaccharides such as dextran, agarose and cellulose) are covered in –OH groups, so they interact with proteins mainly by hydrogen bonding."},{"text":"**Also:** in a binding pocket, sort each ligand atom by what it carries. An oxygen with lone pairs but no H of its own (a ketone or amide C=O, a carboxylate –COO⁻, an ether O) can only receive an H-bond, for instance from arginine's N–H; an O–H or N–H on the ligand can also give one; a C–H does neither. In acetic acid, drawn here, the C=O oxygen is a pure acceptor and the O–H is a donor.","fig":"acetic-acid","calc":["C=O oxygen: lone pairs, no H → acceptor only","O–H: H on O plus lone pairs → donor and acceptor","C–H: no lone pair, no polar H → neither"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Rf = (spot − baseline) ÷ (front − baseline), and it always falls between 0 and 1."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-04e","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:4#1","pe1-cp:4#3","pe1-cp:4#4","pe1-cp:4#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:4"],"passage":"Pentan-2-ol, shown, is heated with concentrated sulfuric acid, and the alkenes are distilled off as they form. Protonation turns the –OH into a good leaving group, and loss of one water molecule leaves a planar carbocation on C2. Three alkenes are collected: pent-1-ene and both the cis and trans isomers of pent-2-ene. Under the same conditions, the tertiary alcohol 2-methylbutan-2-ol dehydrates at a much lower temperature.","q":"Which statement about this dehydration is NOT correct?","fig":"cpt-pentan-2-ol","choices":["Via a carbocation","Loses one water","Tertiary alcohols react faster","Only trans alkene forms"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"True: the protonated –OH leaves as water and E1 runs through a carbocation, so this one stays."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"True: one H₂O leaves for each alkene formed, as the passage describes."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"True: a 3° cation forms most easily, so tertiary alcohols dehydrate fastest."}],"rule":"For a NOT question, test each statement against E1: a carbocation, one H₂O lost, 3° fastest, and a planar cation that gives both cis and trans.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"A planar carbocation can lose H⁺ toward either face, so E1 is never stereospecific. A choice claiming a single geometry is the false one."},"exp":"Acid-catalyzed dehydration is **E1**: the –OH is protonated, **one water** leaves and a **planar carbocation** forms; **tertiary** alcohols react most easily because they give the most stable cation. Because the cation is planar, the C–H can break on either side, so **both cis and trans** pent-2-ene form, as the passage reports. The claim that only the trans alkene forms is the false statement.","how":{"ask":"Find the statement that contradicts the E1 mechanism or the passage.","know":"• E1: protonate the –OH, lose one H₂O, form a planar carbocation, lose H⁺.\n• Ease of dehydration: 3° > 2° > 1°.\n• A planar cation gives cis and trans alkenes, so the reaction is not stereospecific.","steps":[{"text":"The passage names the carbocation and the single water lost: both true.","hl":"loss of one water molecule leaves a planar carbocation","strike":["Via a carbocation","Loses one water"]},{"text":"The tertiary alcohol reacts at a lower temperature: true.","hl":"dehydrates at a much lower temperature","strike":["Tertiary alcohols react faster"]},{"text":"Both cis and trans pent-2-ene were collected, so a trans-only claim is false. Answer: only trans alkene forms."}],"trap":"Treating the major (trans) alkene as the only one: a planar cation gives both isomers."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"loss of one water molecule","note":"one H₂O"},{"t":"planar carbocation","note":"E1; either face"},{"t":"both the cis and trans isomers","note":"not stereospecific"},{"t":"NOT correct","note":"three are true"}]},{"text":"**Check each statement** against E1 and the passage; cross off the true ones.","calc":["carbocation: true","one H₂O: true","3° fastest: true","a single geometry: false"]},{"text":"**Also:** in E1, the C=C always forms between the cationic carbon and a carbon directly bonded to it."},{"text":"**Also:** a direct E1 alkene always has the former C–OH carbon in its C=C."},{"text":"**Also:** E1 through a planar carbocation gives both E and Z alkenes, with the more stable E usually major."},{"text":"**Also:** tertiary alcohols dehydrate most easily because they form the most stable carbocation; primary alcohols dehydrate slowest."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in E1 dehydration a planar carbocation forms, one water is lost, 3° alcohols react fastest, and both cis and trans alkenes form."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-05a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:5#1","pe3-cp:27#3","pe3-cp:27#1","pe3-cp:27#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:5"],"passage":"N-Acetylglycine, CH₃CONHCH₂COOH, has its carboxyl group activated and is then mixed with ethanol. The two molecules join with loss of one molecule of water, giving the compound drawn, CH₃CONHCH₂COOCH₂CH₃. Its IR spectrum keeps the amide C=O stretch of N-acetylglycine and shows one new C=O stretch near 1740 cm⁻¹. No new N–H stretch appears.","q":"Which functional group now links the two former reactants?","fig":"cpt-fix-acgly-ester-hl","choices":["Ester","Amide","Ether","Carboxylic acid"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"An amide's carbonyl carbon is bonded to N; the highlighted carbonyl carbon is bonded to O."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An ether is C–O–C with no C=O; the highlighted O sits right on a carbonyl carbon."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A carboxylic acid ends in O–H; this oxygen carries an ethyl group instead of H."}],"rule":"Name a carbonyl by the atom on its carbon: N makes an amide, O–H an acid, O–C the alkoxy kind; no C=O at all means an ether.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Name a carbonyl by its neighbors: C=O next to O–R is ester, next to N is amide, next to OH is acid, no C=O is ether. Here C=O touches O–CH2CH3, so ester."},"exp":"A carbonyl carbon bonded to an **–O–R** group is an **ester**, C(=O)–O–C: here an ethyl ester. The other carbonyl in the molecule is bonded to N, so that one is the **amide**. An ether has C–O–C with no carbonyl, and a carboxylic acid has C(=O)–O**H**.","how":{"ask":"Find the atom on the new carbonyl carbon, then name the linkage it makes.","know":"• Name a carbonyl group by its other neighbor on the C=O carbon.\n• C(=O)–**O–C** → **ester**; C(=O)–**N** → **amide** (the peptide bond); C(=O)–**OH** → carboxylic acid.\n• **Ether**: C–O–C with no C=O on either carbon.\n• Carboxyl + alcohol → ester; carboxyl + amine → amide.","steps":[{"text":"The new bond joins the activated carboxyl carbon to ethanol's oxygen.","hl":"mixed with ethanol","fig":"cpt-fix-acgly-ester-hl","calc":["–C(=O)–O–CH₂CH₃","C=O carbon + O–R → **ester**"]},{"text":"The amide C=O was already in N-acetylglycine, and no new N–H appears, so the new link is not an amide.","strike":["Amide"]},{"text":"An ether has no carbonyl next to its oxygen, and an acid's oxygen carries H, not an ethyl group.","strike":["Ether","Carboxylic acid"]},{"text":"Answer: an ester."}],"trap":"Calling every new carbonyl link an amide because amino acids are involved: check whether the carbonyl carbon is bonded to N or to O–R."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"mixed with ethanol","note":"an alcohol partner"},{"t":"loss of one molecule of water","note":"condensation"},{"t":"one new C=O stretch","note":"a new carbonyl link"},{"t":"No new N–H stretch","note":"not a new amide"}]},{"text":"**Name a carbonyl by its other neighbor.**","calc":["C(=O)–O–R → ester","C(=O)–N → amide (peptide bond)","C(=O)–OH → carboxylic acid","C–O–C, no C=O → ether"]},{"text":"**Read the product.** The old carbonyl sits on N; the new one sits on O–ethyl.","fig":"cpt-fix-acgly-ester-hl","calc":["CH₃C(=O)–NH– → amide (old)","–C(=O)–O–CH₂CH₃ → ester (new)"]},{"text":"**Also:** forming a peptide (amide) bond is a condensation that releases one H₂O, 18 amu, so a dipeptide's mass is the sum of its two amino acids minus 18."},{"text":"**Also:** a peptide bond is an amide, C(=O)–N, made when a carboxyl group condenses with an amino group and releases water; carboxyl + alcohol gives an ester instead.","calc":["R–COOH + H₂N–R′ → R–CO–NH–R′ + H₂O","R–COOH + HO–R′ → R–CO–O–R′ + H₂O"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** name the new link by the partner: carboxyl + alcohol gives an ester (C(=O)–O–R); carboxyl + amine gives an amide, the peptide bond."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-06a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:6#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:6"],"passage":"Four alkyl chlorides are tested by adding each to sodium iodide dissolved in acetone at 50 °C. Iodide replaces chloride in one concerted step, and the rate law is first order in both the alkyl chloride and iodide. NaI is soluble in acetone while NaCl is not, so the first cloudiness marks how fast each chloride reacts.","q":"Which chloride would turn the solution cloudy first?","choices":["Chlorocyclopentane","1-Chloropentane","2-Chloro-2-methylbutane","1-Chloro-2,2-dimethylbutane"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"A tertiary carbon is too crowded for backside attack, so it cannot react by this one-step path at all."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"It is primary, but the next carbon carries three alkyl groups that block the backside; that check was skipped."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A secondary ring carbon is more crowded than an open CH₂, so it reacts more slowly."}],"rule":"SN2 rate: methyl > 1° > 2°, and 3° does not react. Check the neighboring carbon too: heavy branching there blocks the backside.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Concerted and second order means SN2: pick the least crowded carbon. An unbranched CH₂–Cl with an unbranched neighbor wins."},"exp":"A one-step reaction that is first order in both reactants is **SN2**, so the rate depends on **steric crowding at the carbon bearing Cl**. 1-Chloropentane is primary with no branching nearby, so iodide reaches its back side most easily and NaCl precipitates first. Chlorocyclopentane is secondary (slower), 2-chloro-2-methylbutane is tertiary (no SN2), and 1-chloro-2,2-dimethylbutane is primary but its quaternary neighbor blocks the back side.","how":{"ask":"Identify the mechanism, then pick the least hindered carbon.","know":"• Concerted + second order = SN2.\n• SN2 rate: methyl > 1° > 2°; 3° does not react.\n• Branching on the next carbon (neopentyl-type) also blocks backside attack.","steps":[{"text":"One concerted step and a rate law first order in both reactants mean SN2.","hl":"first order in both the alkyl chloride and iodide","fig":"cpt-sn2-backside"},{"text":"A tertiary carbon is too crowded for backside attack.","strike":["2-Chloro-2-methylbutane"]},{"text":"Primary, but the neighboring carbon carries two methyls and an ethyl: blocked.","strike":["1-Chloro-2,2-dimethylbutane"]},{"text":"A secondary ring carbon is slower than an open primary one.","strike":["Chlorocyclopentane"]},{"text":"Answer: 1-chloropentane."}],"trap":"Assuming every primary chloride is fast: check the next carbon for bulky branches."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"one concerted step","note":"SN2"},{"t":"first order in both","note":"bimolecular"},{"t":"NaCl is not","note":"cloudiness = reaction"}]},{"text":"**Classify each carbon bearing Cl.**","calc":["1-chloropentane: 1°, open","chlorocyclopentane: 2°","2-chloro-2-methylbutane: 3°","1-chloro-2,2-dimethylbutane: 1°, crowded neighbor"]},{"text":"**Least crowded reacts fastest** and clouds the solution first."},{"text":"**Also:** SN2 slows with crowding at the carbon being attacked (methyl > 1° > 2°, no 3°), not at the leaving group."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** SN2 slows with crowding at the attacked carbon and its neighbor: methyl > 1° > 2°, and 3° does not react."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-07b","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:7#2","pe1-cp:7#1","pe1-cp:7#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:7"],"passage":"3-Methylpentan-2-ol is heated with concentrated HBr. The protonated –OH leaves as water, giving a carbocation at C2, the carbon that held the –OH. Four products are isolated: a pair of alkyl bromides and a pair of alkenes. The carbocation intermediate can rearrange before it captures bromide or loses a proton.","q":"Which product could form only by way of a rearranged carbocation?","choices":["2-Bromo-3-methylpentane","3-Bromo-3-methylpentane","3-Methylpent-1-ene","3-Methylpent-2-ene"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Br sits on C2, the carbon that held the OH, so the first cation gives it directly."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Its C=C includes C2: losing H⁺ from C1 of the first cation gives it with no shift."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Its C=C joins C2 and C3: losing H⁺ from C3 of the first cation gives it, no shift needed."}],"rule":"Mark the carbon that held the OH. A product with the new group on a carbon that never held the + came from a shift.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Put the + where the OH left (C2). Br on C2, or a C=C touching C2, is direct. Br on the next carbon needs a hydride shift."},"exp":"Water leaves from C2, giving a **secondary cation at C2**. Br⁻ there gives 2-bromo-3-methylpentane, and a proton lost from C1 or from C3 yields 3-methylpent-1-ene or 3-methylpent-2-ene: all **direct**. C3 carries an H and would be tertiary, so a **1,2-hydride shift** moves the + to C3. Only that shifted cation puts **Br on C3**, giving 3-bromo-3-methylpentane.","how":{"ask":"Find the product whose Br or C=C could not come from the cation at C2.","know":"• The first cation forms where the –OH left.\n• A 1,2-hydride or methyl shift moves the + to a more substituted neighbor.\n• A group on a carbon that never held the + is the mark of a shift.","steps":[{"text":"The first cation sits on C2.","hl":"giving a carbocation at C2"},{"text":"Br on C2, and both alkenes whose C=C touches C2, form directly.","strike":["2-Bromo-3-methylpentane","3-Methylpent-1-ene","3-Methylpent-2-ene"]},{"text":"Br on C3 needs the + on C3: a hydride shift from C3 to C2 makes a 3° cation. Answer: 3-bromo-3-methylpentane.","calc":["2° C2⁺ → (H shift) → 3° C3⁺ → + Br⁻"]}],"trap":"Calling the more substituted alkene \"rearranged\": elimination toward C3 needs no shift."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"3-Methylpentan-2-ol","note":"OH on C2; C3 has one H"},{"t":"giving a carbocation at C2","note":"direct products start here"},{"t":"can rearrange","note":"a shift is possible"},{"t":"only by way of a rearranged carbocation","note":"one shift-only product"}]},{"text":"**Sort the products** by where the new group sits.","calc":["Br on C2 → direct","C=C touching C2 → direct","Br on C3 → shifted"]},{"text":"**Also:** when the neighboring carbon has no H, a methyl moves instead, as in 3,3-dimethylbutan-2-ol, drawn here: its 2,3-dimethyl products all come from the shifted cation.","fig":"cpt-33-dimethylbutan-2-ol"},{"text":"**Also:** a carbocation shifts H or CH₃ from a neighboring carbon whenever that makes a more stable cation."},{"text":"**Also:** a new group on a carbon that never held the leaving group is the fingerprint of a carbocation rearrangement."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a product with Br (or another group) on a carbon that never held the OH can only come from a shifted carbocation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-08d","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:8#3","pe1-cp:8#1","pe1-cp:8#2","pe4-cp:9#1","pe4-cp:9#2","pe4-cp:9#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:8"],"passage":"A crude product mixture is analyzed by gas chromatography on a nonpolar column held at 105 °C; nitrogen carries the sample through it. The mixture holds pent-1-ene (70 g/mol), pentanal (86 g/mol), pentan-1-ol (88 g/mol) and 1-chloropentane (107 g/mol). Each compound spends more or less time dissolved in the column's liquid coating depending on how easily it evaporates.","q":"What emerges from the column first, and why?","fig":["cpt-pent-1-ene","pentanal","cpt-pentan-1-ol","cpt-1-chloropentane"],"choices":["Pentan-1-ol, strongest H-bonds","Pent-1-ene, weakest forces","1-Chloropentane, heaviest","Pentanal, most polar C=O"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"H-bonding raises the boiling point, so the alcohol stays on the column longest, not shortest."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"More mass means stronger dispersion forces and a later peak, not the first one."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"A polar C=O adds dipole attractions, which hold a compound back rather than speed it up."}],"rule":"GC on a nonpolar column runs in boiling-point order: weakest intermolecular forces and lowest mass come out first; H-bonders come out last.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"First peak = most volatile. Strike anything with O–H, C=O or a heavy halogen; the light hydrocarbon with only dispersion forces is left."},"exp":"In GC the **most volatile** compound (lowest boiling point) spends the least time in the stationary liquid and reaches the detector **first**. Pent-1-ene is the lightest and has only weak dispersion forces. Pentanal adds a C=O dipole, 1-chloropentane is heavier and polar, and pentan-1-ol **hydrogen-bonds**, so it comes out last.","how":{"ask":"Rank volatility; the most volatile compound elutes first.","know":"• GC separates by volatility (boiling point).\n• Weaker intermolecular forces and lower mass → lower boiling point → earlier peak.\n• At similar size: alkene < aldehyde or alkyl halide < alcohol in boiling point.","steps":[{"text":"Nonpolar column: the order follows boiling point.","hl":"depending on how easily it evaporates"},{"text":"Strong H-bonds, a C=O dipole and the largest mass all raise the boiling point and delay the peak.","strike":["Pentan-1-ol, strongest H-bonds","Pentanal, most polar C=O","1-Chloropentane, heaviest"]},{"text":"Answer: pent-1-ene, which has the weakest intermolecular forces."}],"trap":"Thinking the most polar or heaviest compound comes out first: on GC, strong attractions mean a late peak."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"nonpolar column","note":"boiling-point order"},{"t":"70 g/mol","note":"lightest"},{"t":"how easily it evaporates","note":"volatility decides"}]},{"text":"**Rank the forces.**","calc":["pent-1-ene: dispersion only","pentanal, 1-chloropentane: dipoles","pentan-1-ol: H-bonds"]},{"text":"**Also:** in GC, compounds come off in order of increasing boiling point."},{"text":"**Also:** with similar sizes, an alkane boils lowest and an alcohol highest, because hydrogen bonding is the strongest force."},{"text":"**Also:** an O–H or N–H group raises the boiling point far more than added mass does, because of hydrogen bonding."},{"text":"**Also:** fractional distillation separates liquids by the same property, boiling point. An alcohol hydrogen-bonds, so it boils higher than an alkyl halide or ether of similar mass, and the lower-boiling liquid distills over first.","calc":["dipropyl ether or 1-chlorohexane vs hexan-1-ol","ether / chloride: lower bp → collects first","alcohol: H-bonding, higher bp → stays behind"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in GC the most volatile compound (weakest forces, lowest mass) gives the first peak; alcohols come out last."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-09b","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:9#1","pe1-cp:9#2","pe1-cp:9#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:9"],"passage":"(R)-2-Chlorobutane is stirred with sodium methanethiolate, CH₃SNa, in dry acetone. The rate doubles when either the alkyl chloride or the thiolate concentration is doubled. Methanethiolate attacks carbon readily yet is only weakly basic.","q":"Which compound forms as the main product?","choices":["(S)-2-(Methylthio)butane","(R)-2-(Methylthio)butane","1-(Methylthio)butane","(E)-But-2-ene"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Keeping R ignores backside attack: SN2 inverts the stereocenter, and here the label flips with it."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"SN2 puts the new group on the carbon that lost Cl; a CH₂ end group would need a different starting chloride."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A weakly basic thiolate favors substitution, so an alkene is not the main product."}],"rule":"SN2 at a stereocenter inverts it: redraw, then re-rank priorities before naming R or S, since the new group may change the order.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Second order → SN2 → inversion. SCH₃ takes Cl's place as the top-priority group, so the label flips from R to S."},"exp":"Doubling either reactant doubles the rate: **second order, SN2**. Thiolate attacks from the back and the stereocenter **inverts**. S outranks ethyl and methyl just as Cl did, so the priority order is unchanged and the inverted carbon is **(S)**: (S)-2-(methylthio)butane. The new group stays on C2, and a weakly basic thiolate gives little alkene.","how":{"ask":"Decide SN1 or SN2 from the rate data, then name the product and its configuration.","know":"• Rate depends on both reactants → SN2 → inversion.\n• After inversion, re-rank priorities: the label flips only if the new group holds the same rank as the leaving group.\n• SN2 keeps the new group on the same carbon.","steps":[{"text":"Doubling either reactant doubles the rate: SN2.","hl":"either the alkyl chloride or the thiolate concentration","fig":"cpt-sn2-backside"},{"text":"Inversion rules out keeping R; the group stays on C2; a weak base gives little alkene.","strike":["(R)-2-(Methylthio)butane","1-(Methylthio)butane","(E)-But-2-ene"]},{"text":"S ranks first just as Cl did, so inversion flips R to S. Answer: (S)-2-(methylthio)butane.","calc":["before: Cl > ethyl > methyl > H","after: SCH₃ > ethyl > methyl > H"]}],"trap":"Assuming the label never changes: here it flips only because S takes Cl's top rank."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"(R)-2-Chlorobutane","note":"stereocenter at C2"},{"t":"The rate doubles","note":"second order"},{"t":"only weakly basic","note":"substitution"}]},{"text":"**Invert, then re-rank.**","calc":["backside attack → inversion","SCH₃ keeps Cl's top rank → R becomes S"]},{"text":"**Also:** CIP priority is set by the first atom's atomic number; go farther out only to break a tie."},{"text":"**Also:** after a substitution, the product keeps the substrate's carbon chain and the new group's position; only the group's name changes."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** SN2 inverts the stereocenter; re-rank priorities with the new group before naming R or S, and keep the group on the same carbon."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-10a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:10#1","pe1-cp:10#2","pe1-cp:10#3","pe3-cp:10#1","pe3-cp:10#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:10"],"q":"Ethanol whose oxygen is ¹⁸O is esterified with acetic acid under acid catalysis. Where does the ¹⁸O end up?","fig":["cpt-ethanol","cpt-acetic-acid"],"choices":["In the ester","In the water","In both products","In neither product"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"There is only one ¹⁸O per ethanol, and one atom can't be split between two products."},{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"Atoms aren't destroyed: the labeled O must end up in some product."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The water forms from the acid's –OH plus H⁺, so it carries the acid's ordinary oxygen."}],"rule":"In acid-catalyzed esterification the acid loses its OH; the alcohol's O–C bond never breaks.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Fischer esterification: acid loses its OH, alcohol keeps its O. So the alcohol's labeled oxygen becomes the ester's bridging O, never the water."},"exp":"In Fischer esterification the **alcohol's oxygen is the nucleophile**: it attacks the protonated carbonyl carbon and stays bonded to it, becoming the ester's bridging –O–. The water that leaves takes an oxygen from the **acid**. So the ¹⁸O ends up **in the ester**.","how":{"ask":"Find which oxygen attacks and which one leaves, then follow the labeled one.","know":"• **Fischer esterification**: RCOOH + R′OH ⇌ RCOOR′ + H₂O (acid catalyst).\n• The **alcohol O attacks** the carbonyl carbon and is kept; **water leaves from the acid** side.\n• So the ester's C–O–R′ oxygen comes from the alcohol, and the water's O from the acid.","steps":[{"text":"The labeled O is ethanol's, the nucleophile.","hl":"oxygen is ¹⁸O","calc":["CH₃CH₂–¹⁸OH attacks the C=O carbon"]},{"text":"The ethyl–¹⁸O bond never breaks, and one ¹⁸O can't be in two places or vanish.","strike":["In both products","In neither product"]},{"text":"The water forms from the acid's –OH plus H⁺, so it carries the acid's ordinary oxygen.","strike":["In the water"]},{"text":"Answer: in the ester, as its bridging oxygen."}],"trap":"Thinking the alcohol loses its OH as water, as in an SN1 reaction: in esterification the acid loses the OH."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"esterified with acetic acid","note":"Fischer"},{"t":"oxygen is ¹⁸O","note":"the nucleophile's O"}]},{"text":"**Activate.** H⁺ protonates the acid's C=O, making the carbon more electrophilic."},{"text":"**Attack and leave.**","calc":["EtO–H attacks C → tetrahedral intermediate","acid's –OH → –OH₂⁺ → leaves as H₂O"]},{"text":"**Also:** in Fischer esterification the ester's bridging oxygen comes from the alcohol, its C=O oxygen from the acid, and the water from the acid."},{"text":"**Also:** an H on O or N exchanges rapidly with solvent water (faster with acid or base), so it cannot keep a label, so it cannot show which bond broke."},{"text":"**Also:** an H bonded to carbon, such as an aromatic ring C–H or a CH₃ hydrogen, is far too weakly acidic to swap with solvent protons, so a ³H or D placed there stays put even after hours in water. Only H on O, N or S trades places with the solvent.","calc":["O–H, N–H, S–H: swap with H₂O → label lost","C–H (aromatic or alkyl): no swap → label kept"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in Fischer esterification the alcohol keeps its oxygen as the ester's –O–, and the acid loses an oxygen as water; follow a label by asking which O attacks and which leaves."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-11a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:11#1","pe1-cp:11#2","pe1-cp:11#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:11"],"q":"A camera images a slide 20 cm from its lens onto a sensor 4.0 cm behind the lens. What is the lens's power?","choices":["+30 D","+0.30 D","+20 D","+25 D"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"That adds 1/20 and 1/4.0 in centimetres; diopters are inverse metres."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Subtracting the reciprocals treats the image as virtual; a real image on a sensor adds them."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That uses only 1/i; the object distance adds its own 1/o term to the power."}],"rule":"Convert both distances to metres and add both reciprocals for a real image: P = 1/o + 1/i, positive for a converging lens.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"In metres: 1/0.20 = 5 and 1/0.040 = 25. Add them: 30 D. A camera or eye lens making a real image is always +."},"exp":"**Power P = 1/f (f in metres) = 1/o + 1/i.** o = 0.20 m and i = 0.040 m, so P = 5 + 25 = **+30 D**. The sensor plays the retina's role: the image distance is the lens-to-sensor distance.","how":{"ask":"Find 1/f in m⁻¹ from the two distances.","know":"• 1/f = 1/o + 1/i.\n• Power (D) = 1/f with f in metres.\n• Image distance = lens to screen (or retina).","steps":[{"text":"Convert both distances to metres.","hl":"4.0 cm behind the lens","calc":["o = 0.20 m","i = 0.040 m"]},{"text":"Add the reciprocals.","calc":["P = 1/0.20 + 1/0.040 = 5 + 25 = 30 D"]},{"text":"Centimetres give 0.30; subtracting gives 20; dropping the 1/o term gives 25.","strike":["+0.30 D","+20 D","+25 D"]},{"text":"Answer: +30 D."}],"trap":"Leaving the distances in cm (0.30 D): diopters are inverse metres."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"20 cm from its lens","note":"object distance"},{"t":"4.0 cm behind the lens","note":"image distance"}]},{"text":"**Metres, then reciprocals.**","calc":["1/0.20 m = 5 D","1/0.040 m = 25 D"]},{"text":"**Add** for a real image on a screen, and keep the sign positive."},{"text":"**Also:** power in diopters = 1 ÷ (focal length in metres); converging lenses are +, diverging lenses are −."},{"text":"**Also:** in any eye problem, the image distance is the lens-to-retina distance; the object distance is lens-to-object."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** power in diopters = 1/o + 1/i with both distances in metres; a lens forming a real image on a screen has positive power."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-12a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:12#1","pe1-cp:12#3","pe1-cp:12#2","pe3-cp:14#1","pe3-cp:14#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:12"],"q":"The plot shows free energy along the reaction path for peptide hydrolysis, without and with a protease (kJ/mol). Which conclusion fits the plot?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Peptide hydrolysis with and without the protease","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Free energy (kJ/mol)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"No enzyme","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,40],[5,90],[7,30],[9,-30],[10,-30]]},{"name":"With protease","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,20],[5,50],[7,10],[9,-30],[10,-30]]}]},"choices":["Ea drops by 40","ΔG° drops by 40","Ea drops to 40","ΔG° turns positive"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"That reads the size of the drop as the new barrier; the catalyzed peak sits at 50."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Reactants and products sit at the same levels on both curves, so ΔG° is unchanged."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Products stay below reactants on both curves; a catalyst never changes ΔG°."}],"rule":"Ea = peak − reactants; ΔG° = products − reactants. A catalyst lowers only the peak, so only Ea changes.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The endpoints never move with a catalyst, so strike any ΔG° change. Then subtract the two peaks: 90 − 50."},"exp":"Read the plot: reactants at 0, products at −30, uncatalyzed peak at 90 and catalyzed peak at 50 kJ/mol. **Ea = peak − reactants**: 90 without and 50 with the protease, so the enzyme **lowers Ea by 40**. **ΔG° = products − reactants = −30** on both curves: an enzyme stabilizes the **transition state** and leaves ΔG° and the equilibrium unchanged.","how":{"ask":"Read Ea and ΔG° from each curve and compare them.","know":"• Ea = transition-state energy − reactant energy.\n• ΔG° = product energy − reactant energy.\n• A catalyst lowers the transition state only; ΔG° and K stay the same.","steps":[{"text":"Read the endpoints: the same reactants and products on both curves.","calc":["ΔG° = −30 − 0 = −30 kJ/mol on both curves"],"strike":["ΔG° drops by 40","ΔG° turns positive"]},{"text":"Read the peaks.","calc":["Ea without = 90","Ea with = 50","drop = 90 − 50 = 40"]},{"text":"The new barrier is 50, not the size of the drop.","strike":["Ea drops to 40"]},{"text":"Answer: Ea drops by 40."}],"trap":"Reading the drop as the new barrier, or thinking the catalyst also changes ΔG°."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **read the plot and the question**.","marks":[{"t":"without and with a protease","note":"two curves"},{"t":"Which conclusion fits the plot?","note":"compare Ea and ΔG°"}]},{"text":"**Measure two gaps on each curve.**","calc":["Ea: peak − start","ΔG°: end − start"]},{"text":"**A catalyst lowers only the peak.** It stabilizes the transition state, so the barrier falls while the start and end stay put."},{"text":"**Also:** a catalyst speeds both directions equally, so it shortens the time to equilibrium but leaves K, ΔG° and the equilibrium yield unchanged."},{"text":"**Also:** catalysis needs the transition state stabilized more than the substrate; extra substrate binding alone raises the barrier."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ea = peak − reactants and ΔG° = products − reactants; an enzyme lowers only the peak, never ΔG° or K."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-12b","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:50#1","pe5-cp:58#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:50"],"passage":"A drug intermediate is made by hydrogenating an alkene in ethanol over palladium on charcoal, a black powder that does not dissolve. When the reaction is complete, the mixture is passed through a filter. The recovered catalyst is reused in the next batch.","q":"What is the most likely result for the product, and why?","choices":["Pure; solid filtered off","Contaminated; Pd stays in solution","Higher yield; K increases","Lower yield; catalyst is consumed"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Contamination happens when a catalyst dissolves with the product; this one is an insoluble solid."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A catalyst speeds both directions equally, so it never shifts the equilibrium or the yield."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A catalyst is regenerated, which is why it can be reused in the next batch."}],"rule":"A solid (heterogeneous) catalyst is filtered off; a dissolved (homogeneous) one stays with the product. No catalyst changes the equilibrium yield.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ask what phase the catalyst is in. An insoluble powder in a liquid is caught by a filter, so the product leaves clean."},"exp":"Pd on charcoal is a **heterogeneous** catalyst: an insoluble solid in a liquid reaction. Filtration removes it, so the product is **free of catalyst**, and the recovered solid can be reused because catalysts are **regenerated**. A dissolved (homogeneous) catalyst that cannot be separated would stay in the product. No catalyst shifts the equilibrium or the yield; it only shortens the time to reach equilibrium.","how":{"ask":"Decide the catalyst's phase, then whether filtration can separate it.","know":"• Heterogeneous (solid) catalysts are filtered off; homogeneous (dissolved) ones stay with the product.\n• Catalysts are regenerated, not consumed.\n• Catalysts change the rate, not K or the equilibrium yield.","steps":[{"text":"The catalyst is an insoluble powder, a different phase.","hl":"a black powder that does not dissolve"},{"text":"A filter catches it, so it does not contaminate the product.","hl":"passed through a filter","strike":["Contaminated; Pd stays in solution"]},{"text":"Catalysts neither move the equilibrium nor get used up.","strike":["Higher yield; K increases","Lower yield; catalyst is consumed"]},{"text":"Answer: pure; solid filtered off."}],"trap":"Assuming a metal catalyst always ends up in the product: only a dissolved one does."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"a black powder that does not dissolve","note":"heterogeneous"},{"t":"passed through a filter","note":"separation"},{"t":"reused in the next batch","note":"not consumed"}]},{"text":"**Phase decides separation.**","calc":["solid in liquid → filter → clean product","dissolved → same phase → stays in product"]},{"text":"**Also:** an inseparable homogeneous catalyst contaminates the product; a catalyst never shifts the equilibrium."},{"text":"**Also:** an enzyme is a catalyst too. A lipase making an ester (acid + alcohol ⇌ ester + water) only brings the mixture to equilibrium sooner; doubling the enzyme leaves K and the equilibrium amount of ester unchanged. Changing concentrations (adding acid or alcohol, taking water out) shifts the equilibrium position; only a temperature change alters K.","calc":["2× enzyme → equilibrium reached faster","K and [ester] at equilibrium: unchanged"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a solid catalyst is filtered off and a dissolved one stays with the product; neither is consumed, and neither changes K or the yield."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-13a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:13#1","pe1-cp:13#2","pe1-cp:13#3","pe1-cp:13#4"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:13"],"q":"A 0.20 M solution of formic acid is prepared. Adding which substance would lower the percent ionization of the formic acid?","choices":["Nitric acid","Pure water","Sodium hydroxide","Sodium bicarbonate"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: diluting a weak acid makes it ionize to a larger fraction, not a smaller one."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"A base removes H⁺, pulling HCOOH ⇌ H⁺ + HCOO⁻ to the right: more ionization, not less."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Bicarbonate is a base; it consumes H⁺ and pushes ionization up, not down."}],"rule":"To lower a weak acid's ionization, add one of its products (H⁺ or its conjugate base); bases and water make it ionize more.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Common-ion rule: adding the ion already produced (H+ from a strong acid) pushes the weak acid back left. Bases and water pull it right, so nitric acid."},"exp":"HCOOH ⇌ H⁺ + HCOO⁻. Nitric acid is a **strong acid**, so it floods the solution with **H⁺, an ion already in the equilibrium**. By **Le Châtelier** the equilibrium shifts **left** and a smaller fraction of formic acid ionizes (the **common-ion effect**). Water dilutes the acid, and the two bases remove H⁺; all three raise ionization.","how":{"ask":"For each added substance, decide whether the formic acid equilibrium shifts left (less ionized) or right (more ionized).","know":"• **Percent ionization** = [A⁻] at equilibrium ÷ starting [HA] × 100.\n• **Le Châtelier**: adding a product pushes the equilibrium toward reactants; removing a product pulls it toward products.\n• **Common-ion effect**: a strong acid adds H⁺, a product of HA ⇌ H⁺ + A⁻, so HA ionizes less.","steps":[{"text":"Write the equilibrium for the weak acid in the stem.","hl":"0.20 M solution of formic acid","calc":["HCOOH ⇌ H⁺ + HCOO⁻   (Ka ≈ 1.8 × 10⁻⁴)","alone: x = √(Ka·C) = √(1.8 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.20) = √(3.6 × 10⁻⁵) = 6.0 × 10⁻³ M","% = 6.0 × 10⁻³ ÷ 0.20 × 100 = **3.0%**"]},{"text":"Nitric acid is a strong acid: it adds H⁺, a product, so the equilibrium shifts left. With 0.050 M HNO₃ present:","calc":["x ≈ Ka·C ÷ [H⁺] = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.20 ÷ 0.050","x = 7.2 × 10⁻⁴ M","% = 7.2 × 10⁻⁴ ÷ 0.20 × 100 = **0.36%**  (down from 3.0%)"]},{"text":"Water dilutes the acid, and a diluted weak acid ionizes to a larger fraction.","strike":["Pure water"]},{"text":"Both bases remove H⁺ (OH⁻ + H⁺ → H₂O; HCO₃⁻ + H⁺ → H₂CO₃), so the equilibrium shifts right: more ionization.","strike":["Sodium hydroxide","Sodium bicarbonate"]},{"text":"Answer: nitric acid."}],"trap":"Choosing a base because it \"neutralizes\" the acid: removing H⁺ pulls the equilibrium to the right, so the weak acid ionizes more, not less."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"formic acid","note":"weak acid: HCOOH ⇌ H⁺ + HCOO⁻"},{"t":"lower the percent ionization","note":"shift the equilibrium left"}]},{"text":"**Percent ionization** is the fraction of the acid molecules that have given up their H⁺.","calc":["% ionization = [A⁻] ÷ [HA]₀ × 100"]},{"text":"**Le Châtelier:** add a product and the equilibrium moves back toward the reactant. H⁺ is a product, so an extra source of H⁺ makes less HA ionize.","calc":["add H⁺ → shift left → % ionization down","remove H⁺ → shift right → % ionization up"]},{"text":"**Also:** diluting a weak acid lowers [H⁺] but raises its percent ionization."},{"text":"**Also:** an electron-withdrawing substituent (such as Cl) near the acidic group makes the acid stronger, so it ionizes more."},{"text":"**Also:** a drop of indicator is too small an amount to change an acid's equilibrium or its percent ionization."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** adding a strong acid (a common ion, H⁺) lowers a weak acid's percent ionization; adding a base raises it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-14a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:14#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:14"],"passage":"A glucose biosensor uses an enzyme-coated electrode whose resistance stays at 40 Ω while it operates. The electrode is wired to a supply whose output can be adjusted. At a low setting the current through the electrode is 15 mA; at a higher setting it is 45 mA.","q":"How does the voltage across the electrode change between the two settings?","choices":["Rises from 0.6 V to 1.8 V","Falls from 1.8 V to 0.6 V","Stays at 0.6 V","Rises from 600 V to 1800 V"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"That multiplies 40 Ω by 15 and 45 without turning milliamps into amps first."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"At fixed R, V = IR rises with I; a falling voltage has the proportion upside down."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Constant resistance does not mean constant voltage; V tracks the current."}],"rule":"At fixed R, V = IR is proportional to I: convert mA to A, then multiply each current by R.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"The current triples (15 → 45 mA) at fixed R, so V triples. 40 Ω × 0.015 A = 0.6 V, then × 3 = 1.8 V."},"exp":"By **V = IR** at a fixed 40 Ω: V₁ = 0.015 A × 40 Ω = **0.6 V** and V₂ = 0.045 A × 40 Ω = **1.8 V**. Tripling the current triples the voltage, because **V is proportional to I** when R is constant.","how":{"ask":"Apply V = IR at each setting with the same R.","know":"• V = IR.\n• At constant R, V is proportional to I.\n• 1 mA = 10⁻³ A.","steps":[{"text":"R is fixed.","hl":"resistance stays at 40 Ω"},{"text":"Convert and multiply.","calc":["V₁ = 0.015 A × 40 Ω = 0.6 V","V₂ = 0.045 A × 40 Ω = 1.8 V"]},{"text":"Unconverted mA gives hundreds of volts; a fall reverses V ∝ I; a constant voltage ignores the change in current.","strike":["Rises from 600 V to 1800 V","Falls from 1.8 V to 0.6 V","Stays at 0.6 V"]},{"text":"Answer: rises from 0.6 V to 1.8 V."}],"trap":"Thinking constant resistance means constant voltage: at fixed R, V follows I."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"40 Ω","note":"fixed R"},{"t":"15 mA","note":"low setting"},{"t":"45 mA","note":"high setting"}]},{"text":"**Same R, so V follows I.**","calc":["V = IR","3 × I → 3 × V"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** at constant R, V = IR rises in proportion to I; convert mA to A before multiplying."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-15a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:15#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:15"],"passage":"A plastic used for contact lenses is tested with a red laser. In vacuum the laser light travels at 300,000 km/s; inside the plastic it travels at 200,000 km/s. The frequency of the light is set by the laser and does not change when the beam crosses into the plastic.","q":"What is the plastic's index of refraction, and what happens to the light's wavelength inside it?","choices":["1.5; it shortens","1.5; it lengthens","0.67; it shortens","0.67; it lengthens"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"An index below 1 would mean light moves faster than in vacuum; that is v/c upside down."},{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"Below 1 again, and a slower wave at the same frequency cannot have a longer wavelength."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"λ = v/f: with f fixed and v smaller, the wavelength gets shorter, not longer."}],"rule":"n = c/v is at least 1; with the frequency fixed, λ = v/f shrinks by the same factor n.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"n can never be below 1, which kills two choices. Slower light at a fixed frequency packs its waves closer together: shorter λ."},"exp":"**n = c/v** = 300,000 ÷ 200,000 = **1.5**. The frequency does not change at the boundary and **λ = v/f**, so the wavelength shrinks by the same factor: λ in the plastic = λ in vacuum ÷ 1.5. Any index below 1 is impossible.","how":{"ask":"Divide c by v, then use λ = v/f with f fixed.","know":"• n = c/v, and n ≥ 1 always.\n• Frequency is unchanged crossing a boundary.\n• λ = v/f, so λ falls by the factor n.","steps":[{"text":"Pick out the two speeds.","hl":"300,000 km/s","calc":["n = 300,000 ÷ 200,000 = 1.5"]},{"text":"An index below 1 is impossible.","strike":["0.67; it shortens","0.67; it lengthens"]},{"text":"f fixed and v smaller → λ smaller.","hl":"does not change when the beam crosses","calc":["λ = v/f → λ ÷ 1.5"],"strike":["1.5; it lengthens"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.5; it shortens."}],"trap":"Writing v/c instead of c/v, or thinking slower light stretches its waves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the two speeds and what stays fixed**.","marks":[{"t":"300,000 km/s","note":"c, in vacuum"},{"t":"200,000 km/s","note":"v, in plastic"},{"t":"does not change","note":"f fixed"}]},{"text":"**Fast over slow.** The bigger speed goes on top, so n comes out at least 1.","calc":["300,000 ÷ 200,000 = 1.5"]},{"text":"**Same f, slower v, shorter λ.**","calc":["λ = v/f"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** n = c/v ≥ 1, and with f fixed the wavelength inside a material is the vacuum wavelength ÷ n."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-16a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:16#1","pe5-cp:54#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:16"],"passage":"Two lasers, X and Y, are aimed at identical photodetectors. The detectors show that the beams deliver equal intensity, the same energy per second on each square centimetre. Laser X emits light at twice the frequency of laser Y, and both beams travel through air.","q":"Compared with Y, what is doubled for X?","choices":["Energy of each photon","Photon arrival rate","Speed of the photons","Wavelength of the light"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: equal intensity with more energy per photon means fewer photons each second, not more."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: λ = c/f, so doubling f halves the wavelength."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"All light in air moves at c, whatever its frequency."}],"rule":"E = hf: frequency sets each photon's energy; speed stays c, λ = c/f shrinks, and equal intensity means fewer photons.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"E = hf: frequency sets each photon's energy, so double f means double energy per photon. Speed stays c, λ halves, and equal intensity means fewer photons."},"exp":"A photon's energy is set by its frequency: **E = hf**. Doubling f **doubles the energy of each photon**. The wavelength halves (λ = c/f), the speed stays c, and since both beams have the same intensity, X needs only **only half the photons each second** to carry the same power.","how":{"ask":"Which property of a single photon depends on frequency, and what does equal intensity then force on the photon count?","know":"• **E = hf = hc/λ**: one photon's energy is proportional to its frequency.\n• **c = fλ**: higher f means shorter λ; light speed in air stays c ≈ 3.00 × 10⁸ m/s.\n• **Intensity** = (photons per second) × (energy per photon) ÷ area.","steps":[{"text":"Apply E = hf to both lasers.","hl":"twice the frequency","calc":["E_Y = hf","E_X = h(2f) = **2E_Y**","e.g. f = 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz: E = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)(5.0 × 10¹⁴) = 3.3 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","2f = 1.0 × 10¹⁵ Hz: E = 6.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Photon energy rises in proportion to frequency (E = hf)","x":{"label":"Frequency (× 10¹⁴ Hz)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Photon energy (× 10⁻¹⁹ J)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"E = hf","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[2.5,1.66],[5,3.3],[7.5,4.97],[10,6.6]]}]}},{"text":"Equal intensity with twice the energy per photon means half the photons each second, so the arrival rate halves.","hl":"equal intensity","calc":["I = (photons/s) × E ÷ A","same I, 2E → photons/s × ½"],"strike":["Photon arrival rate"]},{"text":"Light speed in air is c at every frequency, and c = fλ means doubling f halves λ.","calc":["λ = c ÷ f → λ_X = λ_Y ÷ 2"],"strike":["Speed of the photons","Wavelength of the light"]},{"text":"Answer: the energy of each photon."}],"trap":"Thinking higher frequency means more photons arriving. Frequency sets each photon's energy; at the same intensity a higher frequency actually means fewer photons per second."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what is the same and what differs**.","marks":[{"t":"equal intensity","note":"same power per area"},{"t":"twice the frequency","note":"f doubled"},{"t":"what is doubled","note":"find the factor that grew"}]},{"text":"**Light comes in photons.** Each photon's energy is set by its frequency alone.","calc":["E = hf = hc/λ","2f → 2E"]},{"text":"**Intensity belongs to the beam**: energy per photon times photons per second, per area.","calc":["I = (photons/s × E) ÷ A","fixed I and 2E → ½ the photons/s"]},{"text":"**See it:** photon energy is a straight line through zero against frequency.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"E = hf: doubling the frequency doubles the photon energy","x":{"label":"Frequency (× 10¹⁴ Hz)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Photon energy (× 10⁻¹⁹ J)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"E = hf","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[2.5,1.66],[5,3.3],[7.5,4.97],[10,6.6]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** at one fixed color, intensity is proportional to the number of photons per second; speed, wavelength and polarization don't set it."},{"text":"**Also:** one photon has an energy, a frequency and a wavelength, linked by E = hf = hc/λ. Intensity and amplitude belong to the beam (how many photons arrive each second), so a photon with twice the energy has twice the f and half the λ, but that tells you nothing about amplitude.","calc":["one photon: E, f, λ","the whole beam: photons per second → intensity, amplitude"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hf, so photon energy rises in step with frequency (and λ falls); the photon rate sets intensity at a fixed color, and speed stays c."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-17a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:17#1","pe6-cp:51#1","pe6-cp:51#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:17"],"passage":"A light-cured dental resin contains a photoinitiator that needs a photon of at least 3.5 eV to start the curing reaction. A dentist tests a curing lamp whose LED emits light at a frequency of 750 THz. Planck's constant is 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s.","q":"How much energy does each lamp photon carry, and is it enough to start the curing?","choices":["3.1 eV; no","3.1 eV; yes","0.0031 eV; no","5.5 × 10⁻³⁰ eV; no"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Tera read as giga (10⁹): that leaves the energy a thousand times too small."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Dividing h by f gives eV·s², not an energy; E = hf multiplies them."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"The comparison is flipped: the photon's energy falls short of the threshold."}],"rule":"E = hf with tera = 10¹² and h in eV·s: multiply the leading digits, add the exponents, then compare one photon's energy with the stated threshold.","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"4 × 7.5 = 30 and 10⁻¹⁵ × 10¹⁴ = 10⁻¹, so about 3 eV. That is below 3.5 eV, so one photon cannot do it."},"exp":"**E = hf** with **tera = 10¹²**: f = 7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz, so E = (4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s)(7.5 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹) ≈ **3.1 eV**. That is **less** than the 3.5 eV the photoinitiator needs, so a single photon cannot start the curing, however bright the lamp.","how":{"ask":"Convert THz to Hz, compute E = hf, then compare E with the threshold.","know":"• E = hf.\n• tera = 10¹², giga = 10⁹.\n• One photon either carries enough energy or it does not; a brighter lamp only adds photons.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the frequency.","hl":"750 THz","calc":["750 THz = 7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz"]},{"text":"Multiply.","calc":["E = (4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵)(7.5 × 10¹⁴)","4.14 × 7.5 ≈ 31; 10⁻¹⁵ × 10¹⁴ = 10⁻¹","E ≈ 3.1 eV"]},{"text":"Giga instead of tera gives 0.0031 eV; dividing h by f gives 10⁻³⁰.","strike":["0.0031 eV; no","5.5 × 10⁻³⁰ eV; no"]},{"text":"Compare: 3.1 eV is below the 3.5-eV threshold.","hl":"at least 3.5 eV","strike":["3.1 eV; yes"]},{"text":"Answer: 3.1 eV; no."}],"trap":"Getting E right but flipping the comparison, or thinking a brighter lamp makes each photon stronger."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers and the threshold**.","marks":[{"t":"750 THz","note":"frequency"},{"t":"at least 3.5 eV","note":"threshold per photon"},{"t":"4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s","note":"h in eV·s"}]},{"text":"**Tera means 10¹².** Write f in scientific notation, then multiply.","calc":["f = 7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz","E = hf ≈ 3.1 eV"]},{"text":"**Compare one photon with the threshold.** Brightness changes how many photons arrive, never the energy of each."},{"text":"**Also:** E = hf covers radio waves too; only the prefix changes. Mega = 10⁶, so an FM station at 100 MHz emits photons at 1.0 × 10⁸ Hz, each carrying millions of times less energy than a visible photon.","calc":["E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s × 1.0 × 10⁸ Hz ≈ 6.6 × 10⁻²⁶ J","green light (5.8 × 10¹⁴ Hz): ≈ 3.8 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","FM-band photons (88–108 MHz): about 6–7 × 10⁻²⁶ J"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hf with f in hertz (THz × 10¹²); compare one photon's energy with the threshold, since brightness never makes a single photon stronger."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-18a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:18#1","pe3-cp:58#1","pe4-cp:57#1","pe6-cp:52#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:18"],"passage":"Alveolar ventilation is the volume of fresh air that reaches the alveoli each minute. It is given by V_A = f × (V_T − V_D), where f is the breathing rate, V_T is the tidal volume and V_D is the dead-space volume of the airways. A patient breathes 14 times per minute, has a dead space of 0.15 L and has an alveolar ventilation of 4.2 L/min.","q":"What tidal volume does the passage's formula give for this patient?","choices":["0.45 L","0.30 L","0.15 L","59 L"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Multiplying by the rate instead of dividing gives tens of litres per breath, far beyond a lung."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"That is V_T − V_D; the dead space still has to be added back to get V_T."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"That is the dead-space volume copied from the passage, not the tidal volume."}],"rule":"Isolate the unknown: divide by the factor outside the bracket, then undo the subtraction inside it; check that the size is sensible.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Undo the outside first: 4.2 ÷ 14 = 0.30 L. Then undo the subtraction: 0.30 + 0.15 = 0.45 L."},"exp":"Rearrange V_A = f(V_T − V_D): divide by f, then add V_D. V_T − V_D = 4.2 ÷ 14 = 0.30 L, so **V_T = 0.30 + 0.15 = 0.45 L**, a normal-sized breath.","how":{"ask":"Isolate V_T in the given formula, then substitute.","know":"• Undo operations in reverse order: divide by the factor outside the bracket, then add what was subtracted.\n• Check units and size: a resting breath is about half a litre.","steps":[{"text":"Write the formula and the knowns.","hl":"V_A = f × (V_T − V_D)","calc":["f = 14 /min, V_D = 0.15 L, V_A = 4.2 L/min"]},{"text":"Divide by f.","calc":["V_T − V_D = 4.2 ÷ 14 = 0.30 L"]},{"text":"Add V_D.","calc":["V_T = 0.30 + 0.15 = **0.45 L**"]},{"text":"59 L multiplies instead of dividing; 0.30 L stops before adding V_D; 0.15 L is just the dead space.","strike":["59 L","0.30 L","0.15 L"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.45 L."}],"trap":"Stopping at 0.30 L: that is V_T − V_D, not V_T."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and its symbol.","marks":[{"t":"14 times per minute","note":"f"},{"t":"a dead space of 0.15 L","note":"V_D"},{"t":"4.2 L/min","note":"V_A"}]},{"text":"**Undo the outside first.** Divide both sides by f.","calc":["V_T − V_D = V_A ÷ f"]},{"text":"**Then undo the inside.** Add V_D to both sides.","calc":["V_T = V_A ÷ f + V_D"]},{"text":"**Also:** for a fluid column, h = P/(ρg): atmospheric pressure can push water up an evacuated tube about 10 m.","calc":["h = P/(ρg)","1 atm ≈ 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa; water: 1.0 × 10⁵ ÷ (1000 × 10) ≈ 10 m"]},{"text":"**Also:** when a question gives a formula, name each variable and plug the values in. In a blood pressure written systolic/diastolic, the diastolic is the lower number.","calc":["given pulse pressure = P_s − P_d","120/76 mmHg: P_s = 120, P_d = 76","pulse pressure = 120 − 76 = 44 mmHg"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same plug-in works for a formula you have never met, such as a gyromagnetic ratio γ = 2πf/B. Match each symbol to its value in the passage (f, frequency; B, field), keep the units the choices use (MHz stays MHz for an answer in MHz/T), and leave 2π standing as a factor when every choice carries it.","calc":["¹H at 400 MHz in a 9.4 T field","γ = 2π × 400 MHz ÷ 9.4 T ≈ 42.6 × 2π MHz/T"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to isolate a variable inside a bracket, divide by the outside factor first, then undo the operation inside; check that the answer's size makes sense."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-19d","s":"Fluids","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:19#4","pe1-cp:19#1","pe1-cp:19#2","pe1-cp:19#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:19"],"passage":"Blood pressure is reported as systolic over diastolic pressure in mmHg. The pulse pressure equals systolic minus diastolic. During a routine exam, the reading for one patient is 118/74 mmHg, and the physician compares the pulse pressure with atmospheric pressure.","q":"The pulse pressure is about what percent of an atmosphere?","choices":["5.8%","16%","9.7%","59%"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"That divides the systolic value alone; pulse pressure is systolic minus diastolic."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"That uses the diastolic value alone; subtract the two readings first."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"That divides by the diastolic pressure instead of by 760 mmHg, the pressure of one atmosphere."}],"rule":"Pulse pressure = systolic − diastolic; percent of 1 atm = (mmHg ÷ 760) × 100.","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"118 − 74 = 44 mmHg. 38 mmHg is 5% of 760, so 44 is a bit more: about 5.8%."},"exp":"**Pulse pressure = systolic − diastolic** = 118 − 74 = 44 mmHg. **1 atm = 760 mmHg**, so 44 ÷ 760 × 100 ≈ **5.8%**. 16% and 9.7% use one reading alone, and 59% divides by the diastolic value.","how":{"ask":"Subtract to get the pulse pressure, then divide by 760 mmHg.","know":"• Blood pressure is systolic/diastolic; a typical adult is about 120/80 mmHg.\n• Pulse pressure = systolic − diastolic.\n• 1 atm = 760 mmHg; % = value ÷ 760 × 100.","steps":[{"text":"Read the two values and subtract.","hl":"118/74 mmHg","calc":["pulse = 118 − 74 = 44 mmHg"]},{"text":"Divide by 760 and multiply by 100.","calc":["44 ÷ 760 = 0.058","× 100 = **5.8%**"]},{"text":"16% and 9.7% skip the subtraction; 59% divides by the diastolic pressure.","strike":["16%","9.7%","59%"]},{"text":"Answer: 5.8%."}],"trap":"Dividing one reading by 760 instead of the difference between them."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers and the definition**.","marks":[{"t":"118/74 mmHg","note":"systolic/diastolic"},{"t":"systolic minus diastolic","note":"pulse pressure"}]},{"text":"**Subtract, then compare with 760 mmHg.**","calc":["44 ÷ 760 × 100 ≈ 5.8%"]},{"text":"**Also:** a typical healthy adult's blood pressure is about 120/80 mmHg (systolic/diastolic)."},{"text":"**Also:** pulse pressure = systolic − diastolic."},{"text":"**Also:** 1 atm = 760 mmHg; multiply atm by 760 to get mmHg."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pulse pressure = systolic − diastolic; percent of 1 atm = (mmHg ÷ 760) × 100."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-20b","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:20#2","pe1-cp:20#1","pe1-cp:20#3","pe4-cp:45#1","pe4-cp:45#2","pe6-cp:57#1","pe6-cp:57#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:20"],"passage":"A radioactive tracer for a kidney scan is prepared, and its activity is measured every 3 hours. The figure shows the readings for the first 9 hours. The tracer decays by first-order kinetics to a stable daughter nucleus.","q":"Assuming the trend continues, what percentage of the tracer will have decayed 21 hours after preparation, and what activity will remain?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Tracer activity after preparation","x":{"label":"Time (h)","min":0,"max":9,"step":3},"y":{"label":"Activity (Bq)","min":0,"max":700,"step":100},"series":[{"name":"Activity","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,640],[3,320],[6,160],[9,80]]}]},"choices":["99.2%; 5 Bq","0.8%; 5 Bq","98.4%; 10 Bq","75%; 160 Bq"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is the fraction still left; the question asks how much has decayed."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"That stops one half-life short: count every 3-h step out to the stated time."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"Those are the values read at 6 h on the graph, not extrapolated to the stated time."}],"rule":"Read the half-life off the graph, count n = t ÷ t½, then remaining = (½)ⁿ and decayed = 1 − remaining; activity falls by the same factor.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"The graph halves every 3 h, so 21 h is 7 halvings: 640 → 5 Bq, and only 1 part in 128 (about 0.8%) is left, so about 99.2% has decayed."},"exp":"The activity halves every **3 h** (640 → 320 → 160 → 80 Bq), so the half-life is 3 h. At 21 h, **n = 21 ÷ 3 = 7** half-lives: remaining = (½)⁷ = 1/128 ≈ 0.8%, so **about 99.2% has decayed**, and the activity is 640 ÷ 128 = **5 Bq**.","how":{"ask":"Find the half-life from the graph, count half-lives, then compute the decayed fraction and the activity.","know":"• n = time ÷ half-life.\n• remaining = (½)ⁿ; decayed = 1 − remaining.\n• Activity tracks how many undecayed nuclei are left.","steps":[{"text":"Read the half-life: each 3 h the activity halves.","hl":"every 3 hours","calc":["640 → 320 → 160 → 80 Bq","t½ = 3 h"]},{"text":"Count half-lives and compute.","hl":"21 hours","calc":["n = 21 ÷ 3 = 7","remaining = 1/128 ≈ 0.8%","decayed ≈ 99.2%","activity = 640 ÷ 128 = 5 Bq"]},{"text":"0.8% is what remains; 98.4% stops at six half-lives; 75% is the 6-h reading.","strike":["0.8%; 5 Bq","98.4%; 10 Bq","75%; 160 Bq"]},{"text":"Answer: 99.2% decayed; 5 Bq."}],"trap":"Reporting the fraction remaining as the fraction decayed."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"every 3 hours","note":"time step"},{"t":"21 hours","note":"target time"},{"t":"first-order kinetics","note":"constant half-life"}]},{"text":"**Half-life from the graph**, then count.","calc":["t½ = 3 h","n = 21 ÷ 3 = 7"]},{"text":"**Decayed = 1 − remaining.**","calc":["1 − 1/128 ≈ 99.2%"]},{"text":"**Also:** fraction decayed = 1 − (½)ⁿ, where n = time ÷ half-life."},{"text":"**Also:** to find the starting amount, run it backward: double once per half-life, so original = remaining × 2ⁿ.","calc":["n = 18 days ÷ 6 days = 3","original = 7 mg × 2³ = 7 × 8 = 56 mg"]},{"text":"**Also:** activity (decays per second, in Bq or Ci) is proportional to the number of nuclei left, so it falls on the same schedule: A = A₀ × (½)ⁿ, with n = elapsed time ÷ half-life.","calc":["t½ = 8.0 days, t = 24 days → n = 3","A = 600 Bq × (½)³ = 600 ÷ 8 = 75 Bq"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** n = t ÷ t½, remaining = (½)ⁿ, decayed = 1 − remaining, and activity falls by the same factor as the nuclei."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-21b","s":"Reading Data","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:21#2","pe1-cp:21#1","pe1-cp:21#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:21"],"passage":"A pump in a dialysis machine moves 24 L of fluid in 4.0 min. The manufacturer's power curve for the pump passes through 4 W at 0 L/min, 10 W at 2 L/min, 16 W at 4 L/min and 22 W at 6 L/min. The pump runs at a steady flow the whole time.","q":"How much work does the pump do during the 4.0 min?","choices":["5.3 kJ","88 J","3.8 kJ","0.96 kJ"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"22 W × 4 keeps the time in minutes; W × s gives joules, so convert the time to seconds."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"That reads the curve near zero flow, as if the per-second rate (0.1 L/s) sat on a per-minute axis."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"That reads the curve at x = 4, the number of minutes, instead of at the computed flow."}],"rule":"Get the flow in the axis's units, read the power there, then W = P × t with t in seconds.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Flow = 24 ÷ 4 = 6 L/min → 22 W. Time = 240 s. 22 × 240 ≈ 5300 J."},"exp":"Flow = 24 L ÷ 4.0 min = **6 L/min**, where the curve gives **22 W**. Work = P × t = 22 W × 240 s = 5280 J ≈ **5.3 kJ**.","how":{"ask":"Find the flow, read the power at that flow, then multiply by the time in seconds.","know":"• Flow = volume ÷ time, in the axis units (L/min).\n• Read y at that x on the curve.\n• W = P × t, with t in seconds for joules.","steps":[{"text":"Flow rate in the graph's units.","hl":"24 L of fluid in 4.0 min","calc":["Q = 24 ÷ 4.0 = 6 L/min"]},{"text":"Read the power at 6 L/min.","hl":"22 W at 6 L/min","calc":["P = 22 W"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Pump power versus flow rate","x":{"label":"Flow rate (L/min)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Power (W)","min":0,"max":30,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"Power","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,4],[2,10],[4,16],[6,22],[8,28]]}]}},{"text":"Multiply by the time in seconds.","calc":["t = 4.0 × 60 = 240 s","W = 22 × 240 = 5280 J ≈ 5.3 kJ"]},{"text":"88 J keeps minutes; 0.96 kJ reads near zero flow; 3.8 kJ reads the curve at 4.","strike":["88 J","0.96 kJ","3.8 kJ"]},{"text":"Answer: 5.3 kJ."}],"trap":"Reading the graph at a number from the question (4.0 min), or multiplying watts by minutes."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"24 L","note":"volume"},{"t":"4.0 min","note":"time"},{"t":"power curve","note":"P read from flow"}]},{"text":"**Get the x value**, then read the curve.","calc":["24 L ÷ 4.0 min = 6 L/min → 22 W"]},{"text":"**Work = power × time**, in seconds.","calc":["22 W × 240 s ≈ 5.3 kJ"]},{"text":"**Also:** compute a rate in exactly the units of the graph's axis before reading the graph."},{"text":"**Also:** work = power × time, with power in watts and time in seconds, giving joules."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compute the x value in the axis's units, read the curve there, then W = P × t with t in seconds."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-22a","s":"Protein Structure","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:22#1","pe1-cp:22#2","pe1-cp:22#3","pe6-cp:30#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:22"],"passage":"A wool-like protein fiber is spun from chains rich in cysteine, serine, methionine and lysine. When the wet fibers are left in air, they stiffen as links form between neighboring chains. Soaking the stiff fibers in mercaptoethanol, a reducing agent, makes them soft again.","q":"Which atom forms the links between the chains?","choices":["Sulfur of cysteine","Sulfur of methionine","Oxygen of serine","Nitrogen of lysine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Methionine's sulfur is a thioether with no S–H, so it cannot form a disulfide."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Serine's –OH does not form links that air makes and a reducing agent breaks."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Lysine's amine can form salt bridges, but those are not made by air oxidation or undone by reduction."}],"rule":"Links that form on oxidation (air) and break on reduction are disulfides, which need two cysteine thiols (–SH).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Oxidized together, reduced apart = S–S bond. Only a thiol S–H can form one, and only cysteine has one."},"exp":"Air **oxidizes** cysteine thiols (–SH) on neighboring chains into **disulfide (S–S) bonds**: covalent links between different chains, so the fiber stiffens. A **reducing agent** such as mercaptoethanol breaks them back into two –SH groups. Methionine's S carries no H, and serine and lysine cannot form redox-reversible links.","how":{"ask":"Match \"forms in air, breaks with a reducing agent\" to the bond and the atom that makes it.","know":"• Oxidizing two Cys –SH groups gives a covalent S–S bond.\n• Between two chains, the disulfide is an intermolecular link.\n• Reducing agents (DTT, mercaptoethanol) break S–S bonds.","steps":[{"text":"Links form in air and break with a reducing agent: disulfides.","hl":"mercaptoethanol, a reducing agent","fig":"cysteine"},{"text":"Only a thiol can form one; methionine's S sits between two carbons with no H.","strike":["Sulfur of methionine"]},{"text":"O–H and N–H groups make no redox-reversible links.","strike":["Oxygen of serine","Nitrogen of lysine"]},{"text":"Answer: sulfur of cysteine."}],"trap":"Picking any sulfur: methionine's thioether has no S–H to oxidize."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"left in air","note":"oxidation"},{"t":"links form between neighboring chains","note":"intermolecular"},{"t":"a reducing agent","note":"breaks S–S"}]},{"text":"**Oxidation joins two –SH into S–S; reduction splits it.**","calc":["2 R–SH → R–S–S–R (air)","R–S–S–R → 2 R–SH (reducing agent)"]},{"text":"**Also:** a disulfide is always covalent; it is intermolecular when its two cysteines belong to different molecules."},{"text":"**Also:** reducing agents such as β-mercaptoethanol or DTT break disulfides back into two thiols."},{"text":"**Also:** a disulfide can form only between two cysteine thiols (–SH). If a protein has no Cys, or its Cys are already paired, any oxidative cross-link in it must come from a different side chain, so look past cysteine for the residue the oxidant attacked."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** links made by air oxidation and undone by a reducing agent are disulfides, formed only by cysteine thiols."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-23c","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:23#3","pe1-cp:23#1","pe1-cp:23#2","pe5-cp:42#1","pe5-cp:42#2","pe5-cp:42#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:23"],"passage":"A designed peptide for a wound dressing has two labeled regions. Its cross-linking region A (Gly–Cys–Gly–Cys) joins neighboring strands into a gel. Its cell-binding region B (Lys–Glu–Arg–Asp) gives migrating cells a surface to grip through electrostatic contacts at body pH.","q":"Which residue gives cells a charged group to bind?","choices":["Arg in region B","Cys in region A","Gly in region A","Cys in region B"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Region A is the cross-linker: Cys forms disulfides, and its –SH is uncharged at pH 7."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Glycine's side chain is just H; it carries no charged group."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Read the stem: region B is Lys–Glu–Arg–Asp, with no Cys in it."}],"rule":"Charged side chains at pH 7 are Lys and Arg (+) and Asp and Glu (−); Gly and Cys carry no charge.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Charged at body pH: K, R, D, E (and H partly). Gly and Cys carry no charge, and region B has no Cys, so Arg in B."},"exp":"Match each residue to its region first. Region A holds **Gly** (no side chain) and **Cys** (a thiol for disulfide cross-links), so it offers no charge. Region B holds Lys, Glu, Arg and Asp, all **charged** at body pH; **Arg** is one of them. There is no Cys in region B.","how":{"ask":"List the residues in each region, then find a charged one in the region where it actually sits.","know":"• Charged at body pH: Asp −, Glu −, Lys +, Arg +.\n• **Cys** = thiol for **disulfide cross-links**, not a charge.\n• **Gly** = no side chain.\n• A choice must name a residue that is really in the region it names.","steps":[{"text":"Write out the two regions.","hl":"Gly–Cys–Gly–Cys","calc":["region A: Gly, Cys → cross-linking","region B: Lys, Glu, Arg, Asp → all charged"]},{"text":"Region A has no charged side chain: Gly has none, and Cys forms disulfides.","strike":["Cys in region A","Gly in region A"]},{"text":"Cys does not appear in region B, so that pairing is wrong.","hl":"Lys–Glu–Arg–Asp","strike":["Cys in region B"]},{"text":"Answer: Arg in region B."}],"trap":"Choosing cysteine because its sulfur looks reactive: it belongs to the cross-linking region and gives a covalent link between strands, not a charge for cells."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"cross-linking region A","note":"Cys for disulfides"},{"t":"cell-binding region B","note":"charged residues"},{"t":"charged group","note":"Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg"}]},{"text":"**Sort by job.** Cys makes S–S cross-links; Gly is a spacer; charged residues bind cells.","fig":["cysteine","glycine"]},{"text":"**Check the charged ones.**","calc":["Lys +, Arg +","Glu −, Asp −"],"fig":["arginine","glutamate"]},{"text":"**Check the location too.** A right residue in the wrong region is a wrong answer."},{"text":"**Also:** for an electrostatic contact, choose a side chain charged opposite to the partner: Lys or Arg for a − site, Asp or Glu for a + site."},{"text":"**Also:** glycine has no side chain (R = H), so it adds no charge or functional group to a binding site."},{"text":"**Also:** a binding pocket can name two contacts at once. A phosphate (−) makes a salt bridge only with a + side chain (Lys or Arg), and a flat aromatic ring π-stacks with an aromatic side chain (Phe, Tyr, Trp or His). The right pair satisfies both: Asp or Glu would repel the phosphate, and Ser or Ala cannot stack.","calc":["phosphate (−) → Lys⁺ or Arg⁺","aromatic ring → Phe, Tyr, Trp or His","pair: one from each list, e.g. Lys + Trp"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** match each residue to its labeled region and its job: Cys cross-links, Gly adds nothing, and Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg give charges; for a phosphate in a ring pocket, pair a + residue (Lys/Arg) with an aromatic one."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-24c","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:24#3","pe1-cp:24#1","pe1-cp:24#2","pe6-cp:10#1","pe6-cp:10#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:24"],"passage":"A signaling protein is switched on when a kinase adds a regulatory phosphate to one of its residues. Mass spectrometry places the phosphate on a residue whose side chain is a primary alcohol. Replacing that residue with alanine blocks the signal.","q":"In the passage's experiment, which residue carries the phosphate?","choices":["Serine","Asparagine","Tyrosine","Cysteine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Asparagine ends in an amide, –C(=O)NH₂; it has no –OH at all."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Tyrosine's OH sits on a ring, which makes it a phenol, not a primary alcohol."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Cysteine has –SH, a thiol, not an alcohol."}],"rule":"Primary alcohol means –CH₂OH; Thr's OH is secondary, Tyr's is a phenol, Cys has SH.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Phosphate goes on OH residues: Ser, Thr, Tyr. Primary alcohol = CH2–OH, which is serine; Tyr's OH sits on a ring (phenol)."},"exp":"A **primary alcohol** has its –OH on a carbon bonded to only one other carbon: **–CH₂–OH**. That is serine's side chain. Tyrosine's –OH is on a ring (a phenol), asparagine ends in an amide with no –OH, and cysteine has –SH, not –OH.","how":{"ask":"Which side chain matches the description the report gives: an –OH on a carbon with one carbon neighbor?","know":"• **Primary alcohol**: –CH₂–OH (1 C on the carbinol carbon).\n• Ser –CH₂OH (1°); Thr –CH(OH)CH₃ (2°); Tyr ring–OH (phenol); Asn –CH₂CONH₂ (amide, no OH); Cys –CH₂SH (thiol).\n• Use the description given, not the residue you expect to see phosphorylated.","steps":[{"text":"The report's clue is the class of alcohol.","hl":"side chain is a primary alcohol"},{"text":"Find the side chain that ends in –CH₂–OH.","fig":["serine-sc","t1-phosphoserine"],"calc":["Ser: –CH₂–OH → C bearing OH has 1 C neighbor → **1°** ✓"]},{"text":"Tyrosine's OH sits on a ring (a phenol), and asparagine ends in an amide with no OH.","strike":["Asparagine","Tyrosine"]},{"text":"Cysteine ends in –CH₂–SH: a thiol, not an alcohol.","strike":["Cysteine"]},{"text":"Answer: serine."}],"trap":"Picking tyrosine because it is also phosphorylated: the report names a primary alcohol, and only serine fits."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"primary alcohol","note":"–CH₂–OH"},{"t":"regulatory phosphate","note":"on an –OH"}]},{"text":"**Translate the description into a structure.** Primary means the –OH carbon has one carbon neighbor.","calc":["primary: R–CH₂–OH","secondary: R–CH(OH)–R′","phenol: ring–OH"]},{"text":"**Match it to a side chain.** Only serine ends in –CH₂–OH.","fig":"serine-sc"},{"text":"**Also:** serine is a primary alcohol, threonine a secondary alcohol, tyrosine a phenol, and alanine only a methyl group."},{"text":"**Also:** phosphorylation goes on a side-chain –OH, so look for Ser, Thr or Tyr and reject side chains with no hydroxyl."},{"text":"**Also:** in a kinase assay with [γ-³²P]ATP, the label ends up only where the enzyme can build a phosphoester, on a side-chain hydroxyl. Look at the one residue that differs between the peptides: Ser, Thr or Tyr can take the ³²P, while a residue with no hydroxyl, such as alanine, leucine or valine, leaves its peptide unlabeled.","calc":["–OH present → phosphoester → ³²P in the peptide","no –OH (Ala, Leu, Val) → no ³²P"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** turn the description into a structure first (primary alcohol → –CH₂OH → serine), then pick the residue that matches it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-25c","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:25#3","pe1-cp:25#1","pe1-cp:25#2","pe3-cp:43#1","pe5-cp:31#3","pe5-cp:31#1","pe5-cp:31#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:25"],"passage":"A designed peptide contains six glutamate residues, side-chain pKa about 4.3, and no basic residues. Samples of the peptide are equilibrated at different pH values, and the percentage found in fibers is measured. The figure shows the results.","q":"Which explanation best fits the data?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Peptide found in fibers after equilibration","x":{"label":"pH","min":2,"max":8,"step":1},"y":{"label":"In fibers (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Fibers","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[2,95],[3,90],[4,65],[5,20],[6,5],[7,2],[8,2]]}]},"choices":["Protonated glutamates stop repelling","Deprotonated glutamates attract","Protonated glutamates form ion pairs","Deprotonated glutamates shed water"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Deprotonated glutamates all carry −1, so they repel; that is why fibers vanish at high pH."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Charged carboxylates hold water more tightly, not less, and fibers fall rather than rise as they form."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An ion pair needs a + partner; this peptide has no basic residue, and a neutral COOH carries no charge."}],"rule":"Compare pH with the side chain's pKa: charged chains repel and stay apart; when the pH neutralizes them, they can pack together.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Fibers appear below about pH 4, near the glutamate pKa. Below it COO⁻ becomes neutral COOH, so the strands stop repelling and pack."},"exp":"Fibers form **below pH ≈ 4.3**, the glutamate side-chain pKa. Under that pH, the side chains are **protonated and neutral (–COOH)**, so the strands **no longer repel** and can pack into fibers. Above it they are **–COO⁻**, all negative, and like charges push the strands apart. With no basic residues there is no + partner for salt bridges.","how":{"ask":"Link the pH where fibers form to the side chain's pKa and charge.","know":"• Acidic side chain: pH < pKa → –COOH (0); pH > pKa → –COO⁻ (−1).\n• Like charges repel and keep strands apart; neutral strands can pack.\n• A salt bridge needs opposite charges.","steps":[{"text":"Fibers form only at low pH, below the pKa.","hl":"side-chain pKa about 4.3"},{"text":"Above the pKa every glutamate is −1: they repel rather than attract, and they hold water more tightly.","strike":["Deprotonated glutamates attract","Deprotonated glutamates shed water"]},{"text":"No basic residues means no + partner for a salt bridge.","hl":"no basic residues","strike":["Protonated glutamates form ion pairs"]},{"text":"Answer: protonated glutamates stop repelling."}],"trap":"Reaching for salt bridges whenever charge matters: they need a + partner."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"six glutamate residues","note":"acidic side chains"},{"t":"pKa about 4.3","note":"charge switch"},{"t":"no basic residues","note":"no + partner"}]},{"text":"**Charge on each side of the pKa.**","calc":["pH < 4.3: –COOH, 0","pH > 4.3: –COO⁻, −1"]},{"text":"**Neutral strands pack; charged strands repel.**"},{"text":"**Also:** a group grafted onto a residue sets its charge the same way: an added –COOH (pKa ≈ 4) is ionized at pH 7, so the residue becomes acidic; an added amine (pKa ≈ 9–10) is protonated, so it becomes basic."},{"text":"**Also:** a side chain's charge changes only when the pH moves past its pKa; acids go 0 → −1, bases go +1 → 0 as pH rises."},{"text":"**Also:** charged side chains repel and keep peptides apart; when the pH neutralizes them, the strands can pack together."},{"text":"**Also:** at a fixed pH, lowering a group's pKa means less of it is protonated, so a structure held together by the protonated form (such as C·C⁺ pairs in an i-motif) is less stable; compare pK values to see which change lowered it.","calc":["pKa 6.5 → 6.0 at pH 6.5: 50% → 24% protonated → less stable"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same pKa-versus-pH test works for a group added to a residue. A grafted –CH₂NH₃⁺ with pKa 9.5 sits about 2 units above pH 7.4, so it stays protonated (+1) and makes the side chain basic.","calc":["9.5 − 7.4 = 2.1 units","[–CH₂NH₂] : [–CH₂NH₃⁺] = 10^(7.4 − 9.5) ≈ 1 : 126","≈ 99% carries +1"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare the pH with each side chain's pKa: charged chains repel and stay apart, and neutralized chains can pack together."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-26a","s":"Fluids","disc":"biology","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:26#1","pe3-cp:50#1","pe3-cp:52#1","pe3-cp:52#2","pe5-cp:28#1","pe5-cp:28#2","pe3-cp:50#2","pe3-cp:50#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:26"],"q":"In a muscle bed, blood moves at 2.0 cm/s through arterioles with a combined cross-sectional area of 40 cm². The capillaries they feed total 800 cm². What is the capillary blood speed, and why?","choices":["0.10 cm/s, larger combined area","0.10 cm/s, each capillary is thinnest","40 cm/s, larger combined area","2.0 cm/s, equal volume per second"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"The same speed through twenty times the area would carry twenty times the flow; flow cannot grow."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: more combined area means slower blood (Q = Av), so the speed must fall below 2.0."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The number is right but not the reason: one thin tube would speed blood up; the many capillaries in parallel slow it."}],"rule":"Q = A·v is the same in every section, so speed scales by old area ÷ new area: N times the area gives N times slower flow.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Speed ∝ 1/total area. Area grows 800/40 = 20×, so speed drops 20×: 2.0 ÷ 20 = 0.10 cm/s."},"exp":"**Continuity: Q = A·v is the same at every level.** Q = 40 cm² × 2.0 cm/s = 80 cm³/s, so v = 80 ÷ 800 = **0.10 cm/s**. Blood crawls through capillaries because their **total** area is twenty times larger, not because each one is thin: one thin tube alone would speed blood up.","how":{"ask":"The same volume per second passes the arterioles and the capillaries. How fast must it move through twenty times the total area?","know":"• **Continuity: Q = A·v** (Q = flow rate in cm³/s, A = cross-sectional area, v = mean speed). For blood, Q is the same at every level of the circulation.\n• Vessels in parallel: use their **combined** area.\n• Larger total A → smaller v. Capillaries have the largest total area, so blood is slowest there.","steps":[{"text":"Find the flow rate in the arterioles.","hl":"2.0 cm/s through arterioles with a combined cross-sectional area of 40 cm²","calc":["Q = A × v","Q = 40 cm² × 2.0 cm/s = **80 cm³/s**"]},{"text":"The same Q passes the capillaries; divide by their total area.","hl":"total 800 cm²","calc":["v = Q ÷ A = 80 cm³/s ÷ 800 cm²","v = **0.10 cm/s**","area × 20 → speed ÷ 20"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Same flow (80 cm³/s): speed falls as total area grows","x":{"label":"Total cross-sectional area (cm²)","min":0,"max":800,"step":200},"y":{"label":"Mean speed (cm/s)","min":0,"max":2,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"v = Q ÷ A","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[40,2],[80,1],[160,0.5],[400,0.2],[800,0.1]]}]}},{"text":"Equal speed would need equal area; 40 cm/s treats the capillaries like a nozzle; \"each capillary is thinnest\" is the wrong reason, since one thin tube would speed blood up.","strike":["2.0 cm/s, equal volume per second","40 cm/s, larger combined area","0.10 cm/s, each capillary is thinnest"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.10 cm/s, because the combined area is larger."}],"trap":"Thinking narrow vessels speed blood up. One narrow tube would, but the blood is split among countless capillaries whose combined area is far larger than the arterioles'."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 cm/s","note":"arteriole speed"},{"t":"40 cm²","note":"arteriole total area"},{"t":"800 cm²","note":"capillary total area"}]},{"text":"**Continuity:** blood is not lost between levels, so the volume per second is the same everywhere.","calc":["Q = A·v = constant"]},{"text":"**Combined area decides the speed.** More total area for the same Q means slower blood.","calc":["v = Q ÷ A"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Speed versus total area at a fixed flow of 80 cm³/s","x":{"label":"Total cross-sectional area (cm²)","min":0,"max":800,"step":200},"y":{"label":"Mean speed (cm/s)","min":0,"max":2,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"v = Q ÷ A","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[40,2],[80,1],[160,0.5],[400,0.2],[800,0.1]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** given n vessels each of area a, total area = n·a, so u = Av/(na).","calc":["n = 1.6 × 10⁹ capillaries, a = 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ cm² each","n·a = 1.6 × 10⁹ × 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ = 800 cm²","u = Av/(na) = (40 × 2.0) ÷ 800 = 0.10 cm/s"]},{"text":"**Also:** for one tube that narrows (no branching), A₁v₁ = A₂v₂ with A = πd²/4, so area goes as d² and v₂ = v₁(d₁/d₂)². Halving the diameter gives 4× the speed; a diameter one-third as wide gives 9×.","calc":["d: 1.2 cm → 0.6 cm, v₁ = 15 cm/s","v₂ = 15 × (1.2/0.6)² = 15 × 4 = 60 cm/s","d₁/d₂ = 3 → (3)² = 9× faster"]},{"text":"**Also:** when a passage gives a tube's diameter instead of its area, halve it to get r and use A = πr². If the speed is in mm/s and the area in μm², multiply the speed by 1000 (1 mm = 1000 μm) before Q = A·v. Using d in place of r makes A four times too big.","calc":["d = 20 μm → r = 10 μm","A = π(10 μm)² ≈ 314 μm²","v = 0.75 mm/s × 1000 = 750 μm/s","Q = 314 μm² × 750 μm/s ≈ 2.4 × 10⁵ μm³/s"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Q = A·v is the same at every level, so speed is smallest where the total cross-sectional area is largest: the capillaries."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-27a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:27#1","pe1-cp:27#2","pe1-cp:27#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:27"],"q":"On a potential–pH diagram, the boundary between Cu²⁺ and copper metal stays at +0.34 V from pH 0 to 5. Which species is absent from its half-reaction?","choices":["H⁺ and OH⁻","The two electrons","The Cu²⁺ ions","The copper metal"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A boundary at a set potential is a redox couple, so electrons must appear."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Cu²⁺ is one of the two species the line separates, so it must be in the half-reaction."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Cu metal is one of the two species the line separates, so it must be in the half-reaction."}],"rule":"On a potential–pH diagram, a flat line has electrons but no H⁺/OH⁻; a vertical line has H⁺/OH⁻ but no electrons.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Flat line vs pH means pH has zero effect, so the half-reaction contains no H+ or OH−. Electrons and both copper forms must still be there."},"exp":"A **horizontal** boundary keeps the same potential whatever the pH, so the equilibrium **does not depend on pH**: its half-reaction, Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu, contains **no H⁺ or OH⁻**. It still transfers electrons, and both copper forms appear in it.","how":{"ask":"Decide what a boundary at one fixed potential across a range of pH says about the half-reaction.","know":"• Pourbaix diagram: potential E (y) versus pH (x); each line is an equilibrium between two forms.\n• **Horizontal line**: E does not change with pH → **pH-independent** → no H⁺ or OH⁻ in the half-reaction, but electrons are transferred.","steps":[{"text":"The boundary is flat: one potential across several pH values.","hl":"stays at +0.34 V from pH 0 to 5","calc":["E constant while pH changes → pH-independent"]},{"text":"Write the half-reaction it represents.","calc":["Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu   (E° = +0.34 V)","no H⁺, no OH⁻"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Boundary lines on a simplified iron potential–pH diagram","x":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":8,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Potential (V)","min":-0.8,"max":1.2,"step":0.4},"series":[{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺: horizontal","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0.77],[2,0.77]]},{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe(OH)₃: vertical","marker":"square-open","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[2,1.2]]},{"name":"Fe(OH)₃/Fe²⁺","marker":"diamond","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[8,-0.29]]},{"name":"Fe²⁺/Fe: horizontal","marker":"triangle","line":"straight","points":[[0,-0.44],[8,-0.44]]}]}},{"text":"A line set at a potential is a redox couple, so electrons must be in it; Cu²⁺ and Cu are the two forms the line separates.","strike":["The two electrons","The Cu²⁺ ions","The copper metal"]},{"text":"Answer: H⁺ and OH⁻."}],"trap":"Thinking a flat line means \"no electrons\": that describes a vertical line. A flat line is a redox couple with no H⁺ or OH⁻."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"potential–pH diagram","note":"E on y, pH on x"},{"t":"stays at +0.34 V","note":"horizontal line"},{"t":"from pH 0 to 5","note":"pH changes, E does not"}]},{"text":"**Read the slope of each boundary.** A flat line sits at one potential, whatever the pH.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Boundary lines on a simplified iron potential–pH diagram","x":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":8,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Potential (V)","min":-0.8,"max":1.2,"step":0.4},"series":[{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺: horizontal","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0.77],[2,0.77]]},{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe(OH)₃: vertical","marker":"square-open","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[2,1.2]]},{"name":"Fe(OH)₃/Fe²⁺","marker":"diamond","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[8,-0.29]]},{"name":"Fe²⁺/Fe: horizontal","marker":"triangle","line":"straight","points":[[0,-0.44],[8,-0.44]]}]}},{"text":"**Flat = pH has no say.** If changing pH does not move the line, H⁺ and OH⁻ are not in the equilibrium.","calc":["horizontal → electrons yes, H⁺/OH⁻ no"]},{"text":"**Also:** a vertical Pourbaix boundary means the equilibrium depends on pH but transfers no electrons."},{"text":"**Also:** the higher a region lies on a potential–pH diagram, the more oxidized the species stable there."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a horizontal Pourbaix boundary is a redox equilibrium that does not depend on pH (no H⁺ or OH⁻ involved)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-28a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:28#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:28"],"q":"Phosphorus pentachloride, PCl₅, has five chlorine atoms bonded to a central phosphorus atom that carries no lone pairs. Which hybridization describes the phosphorus?","choices":["dsp³","sp³d²","sp²","sp"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"That set makes six orbitals, for six domains; PCl₅'s phosphorus has five bonds and no lone pair."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"sp² gives 3 orbitals: enough for the 3 equatorial Cl only, not the 2 axial ones."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"sp gives only 2 orbitals; phosphorus needs 5, one for each P–Cl bond."}],"rule":"Count domains on the central atom (bonds + lone pairs); the hybrid set must have exactly that many orbitals.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count domains, add up orbitals: 2 sp, 3 sp², 4 sp³, 5 sp³d (dsp³), 6 sp³d². Five Cl, no lone pairs = 5 → dsp³."},"exp":"Five bonded atoms and no lone pairs = **5 electron domains**, so phosphorus needs **5 hybrid orbitals**: dsp³, also written **sp³d** (1 s + 3 p + 1 d = 5), pointing to the corners of a **trigonal bipyramid**. sp³d² makes 6 (octahedral), sp² makes 3 and sp makes 2.","how":{"ask":"Count phosphorus's electron domains, then pick the hybridization label that makes exactly that many orbitals.","know":"• Hybrid orbitals needed = electron domains (bonded atoms + lone pairs).\n• **2 → sp; 3 → sp²; 4 → sp³ (tetrahedral); 5 → sp³d = dsp³ (trigonal bipyramidal); 6 → sp³d² or d²sp³ (octahedral)**.\n• Count the orbitals in a label: s = 1, then read each superscript for p and d (no superscript = 1).","steps":[{"text":"Count phosphorus's domains: five bonds and no lone pairs.","hl":"five chlorine atoms bonded to a central phosphorus atom that carries no lone pairs","calc":["domains = 5 bonds + 0 lone pairs = **5**","5 domains → **5** hybrid orbitals → trigonal bipyramidal"]},{"text":"Count the orbitals each label makes.","calc":["dsp³ (= sp³d): 1 + 1 + 3 = **5** → trigonal bipyramidal","sp³d²: 1 + 3 + 2 = 6 → octahedral","sp²: 1 + 2 = 3 → trigonal planar","sp: 1 + 1 = 2 → linear"]},{"text":"sp³d² makes six orbitals, which would need a sixth domain; phosphorus's 5 valence electrons all go into the five P–Cl bonds, leaving no lone pair.","strike":["sp³d²"]},{"text":"sp² covers only the three equatorial Cl, and sp only the two axial ones; five bonds need five orbitals.","strike":["sp²","sp"]},{"text":"Answer: dsp³ (sp³d)."}],"trap":"sp³d² also uses d orbitals, so it looks right for any expanded octet, but it makes 6 orbitals: the octahedral set of SF₆, not the 5-orbital trigonal bipyramidal set of PCl₅."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"five chlorine atoms","note":"5 bonded atoms"},{"t":"no lone pairs","note":"0 extra domains"},{"t":"hybridization","note":"how many orbitals mix"}]},{"text":"**Electron domains** = atoms bonded + lone pairs. Each domain needs one hybrid orbital.","calc":["PCl₅: 5 + 0 = 5 domains → 5 orbitals"]},{"text":"**Match the count to a label.**","calc":["4 → sp³ (CH₄, tetrahedral)","5 → sp³d = dsp³ (PCl₅, trigonal bipyramidal)","6 → sp³d² or d²sp³ (SF₆, octahedral)"]},{"text":"**Also:** the order of letters doesn't change the count: dsp³ and sp³d both mix one s, three p and one d orbital; d²sp³ and sp³d² both make six, the octahedral set seen in six-coordinate metal complexes."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** 5 domains → trigonal bipyramidal → sp³d (dsp³); 6 → sp³d²; 4 → sp³."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-29b","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:29#2","pe1-cp:29#1","pe5-cp:15#1","pe5-cp:15#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:29"],"q":"A chemist compares 0.200 mol of neon at 127 °C and 0.820 atm with the ideal-gas model, taking R = 0.08206 L·atm/(mol·K). Which option lists one ideal-gas assumption and the volume the model gives?","choices":["No attractions; 8.0 L","No attractions; 4.5 L","Weak attractions; 8.0 L","Weak attractions; 4.5 L"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"22.4 L/mol holds only at STP; this gas is hot and below 1 atm, so use PV = nRT."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The ideal model assumes no intermolecular attractions at all, not weak ones."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Wrong premise and an STP volume: ideal particles do not attract, and 22.4 L/mol needs 0 °C and 1 atm."}],"rule":"Ideal gas: point particles with no attractions. Use V = nRT/P with T in kelvin; 22.4 L/mol works only at STP.","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"nRT ≈ 0.2 × 0.082 × 400 ≈ 6.6; ÷ 0.82 ≈ 8 L. Then pick the premise with zero attractions."},"exp":"The ideal-gas model treats particles as points with **no intermolecular attractions** (and no volume of their own). Volume: V = nRT/P = (0.200)(0.0821)(400 K) ÷ 0.820 ≈ **8.0 L**. 4.5 L is 0.200 × 22.4, valid only at STP.","how":{"ask":"Pick the true premise, then compute V = nRT/P.","know":"• Ideal gas: negligible particle volume, no attractions, elastic collisions.\n• V = nRT/P with T in K.\n• 22.4 L/mol only at STP (0 °C, 1 atm).","steps":[{"text":"Convert T to kelvin.","hl":"127 °C","calc":["T = 127 + 273 = 400 K"]},{"text":"Compute V.","calc":["V = (0.200)(0.0821)(400) ÷ 0.820","= 6.57 ÷ 0.820 ≈ 8.0 L"]},{"text":"22.4 L/mol is for STP only.","strike":["No attractions; 4.5 L","Weak attractions; 4.5 L"]},{"text":"Ideal particles have no attractions, not weak ones.","strike":["Weak attractions; 8.0 L"]},{"text":"Answer: no attractions; 8.0 L."}],"trap":"Using 22.4 L/mol away from STP, or calling weak attractions ideal."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"0.200 mol","note":"n"},{"t":"127 °C","note":"convert to K"},{"t":"0.820 atm","note":"P"}]},{"text":"**Premise:** ideal particles occupy no volume and feel no attractions."},{"text":"**Volume:** V = nRT/P in kelvin.","calc":["V ≈ 8.0 L"]},{"text":"**Also:** a gas is most ideal at high T and low P, where particle volume and intermolecular forces are both negligible."},{"text":"**Also:** run it backward to count moles: at STP, n = V ÷ 22.4 L/mol, the same answer PV/RT gives. Put the volume in litres first: 1 cm³ = 1 mL = 10⁻³ L.","calc":["112 cm³ = 112 mL = 0.112 L","n = 0.112 L ÷ 22.4 L/mol = 0.0050 mol","check: n = PV/RT = (1 atm)(0.112 L) ÷ (0.0821 × 273) = 0.0050 mol ✓"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ideal gas = point particles with no attractions; V = nRT/P in kelvin, and 22.4 L/mol only at STP."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-30a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:30#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:30"],"passage":"A deep-sea fish has rods with a single visual pigment, whose absorbance spectrum is shown in the figure. A researcher shines a 450-nm light and then a 530-nm light at the eye, adjusting their intensities so that the pigment captures equal numbers of photons per second from each. Each rod reports only how many photons its pigment absorbs.","q":"Can the fish tell the two lights apart?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Absorbance of the fish's rod pigment","x":{"label":"Wavelength (nm)","min":400,"max":650,"step":50},"y":{"label":"Relative absorbance","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Fish pigment (490 nm)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[400,0.1],[430,0.4],[450,0.7],[470,0.92],[490,1],[510,0.92],[530,0.7],[550,0.4],[580,0.1],[600,0.03]]}]},"choices":["No; one pigment, one signal","Yes; 530-nm light signals more","Yes; bluer photons signal more","Yes; by comparing two pigments"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Absorption was matched on purpose, so the longer-wavelength light gives the same signal, not a bigger one."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A rod counts photons; once the counts match, photon energy does not change the signal."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"There is only one pigment here, so there is no second signal to compare."}],"rule":"One pigment reports only photons absorbed; telling wavelengths apart needs two or more pigments with different peaks, compared by ratio.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Equal photons absorbed by the only pigment means identical signals. With nothing to compare, the two lights look the same."},"exp":"A photoreceptor reports only **how many photons it absorbs**, not their wavelength. The intensities were set so the single pigment absorbs equal numbers from both lights, so the rods send **identical signals** and the fish cannot tell them apart. Color vision needs **a second pigment with a different peak**, so the brain can compare the two responses.","how":{"ask":"Does anything differ between the two signals once absorption is matched?","know":"• A pigment signals photons absorbed, not wavelength (univariance).\n• Color vision needs two or more pigments with different peaks; the brain compares their ratio.\n• Matching absorption for the only pigment erases any difference.","steps":[{"text":"Only one pigment exists.","hl":"a single visual pigment"},{"text":"Its absorption was matched for the two lights, so the signals are equal whatever the photon energy.","hl":"captures equal numbers of photons per second from each","strike":["Yes; 530-nm light signals more","Yes; bluer photons signal more"]},{"text":"With one pigment there is no second signal to compare.","strike":["Yes; by comparing two pigments"]},{"text":"Answer: no; one pigment, one signal."}],"trap":"Thinking the wavelength itself is signaled: only the count of absorbed photons is."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"a single visual pigment","note":"one kind only"},{"t":"equal numbers of photons per second","note":"matched absorption"},{"t":"only how many photons","note":"no wavelength signal"}]},{"text":"**One pigment sends one number**, so matched lights look identical."},{"text":"**Add a second pigment** with another peak and the two lights give different ratios: that comparison is color vision.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Two pigments, two peaks","x":{"label":"Wavelength (nm)","min":400,"max":650,"step":50},"y":{"label":"Relative absorbance","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Fish pigment (490 nm)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[400,0.1],[430,0.4],[450,0.7],[470,0.92],[490,1],[510,0.92],[530,0.7],[550,0.4],[580,0.1],[600,0.03]]},{"name":"Second pigment (560 nm)","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[450,0.01],[480,0.1],[510,0.4],[530,0.7],[545,0.92],[560,1],[575,0.92],[590,0.7],[610,0.4],[640,0.1]]}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a single pigment reports only photons absorbed; color vision needs two or more pigments with different peaks whose responses the brain compares."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-31a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:31#1","pe1-cp:31#2","pe4-cp:36#1","pe4-cp:36#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:31"],"passage":"An insect odorant-binding protein carries limonene, shown, a small hydrocarbon odorant. Crystal structures place the odorant inside a buried cavity sealed off from the surrounding water. Mutations that put charged residues into the cavity wall abolish binding.","q":"Which residue would best suit the cavity wall?","fig":"limonene","choices":["Methionine","Asparagine","Glutamine","Histidine"],"choiceFigs":["methionine-sc","asparagine-sc","glutamine-sc","histidine-sc"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Asparagine's amide side chain is polar and H-bonds to water, a poor match for a hydrocarbon."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Glutamine's amide is polar too; it would rather face water than a nonpolar guest."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Histidine's imidazole ring is polar and can be charged, so it suits a water-facing surface."}],"rule":"Match the pocket to the ligand: a nonpolar guest away from water needs nonpolar side chains (Leu, Ile, Val, Phe, Met); polar or charged ones face water.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Hydrocarbon ligand + no water = hydrophobic lining. Only one choice has a side chain with no N–H, O–H or charge."},"exp":"Limonene is a **hydrocarbon** with no polar groups, and the cavity is **sealed from water**, so it is held by **hydrophobic (dispersion) contacts**. Methionine's side chain (–CH₂CH₂–S–CH₃) is nonpolar. Asparagine and glutamine carry polar amides, and histidine has a polar, ionizable ring; these line water-facing surfaces.","how":{"ask":"Classify the ligand and the pocket, then pick the side chain that matches.","know":"• Nonpolar ligand in a water-free pocket → hydrophobic lining (Leu, Ile, Val, Phe, Met).\n• Polar (Asn, Gln, Ser, Thr) and charged or ionizable (Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg, His) side chains prefer water.","steps":[{"text":"The odorant is a hydrocarbon held away from water.","hl":"sealed off from the surrounding water","fig":"limonene"},{"text":"Amide side chains are polar.","strike":["Asparagine","Glutamine"]},{"text":"Histidine's ring is polar and can carry a + charge.","strike":["Histidine"]},{"text":"Answer: methionine, whose thioether side chain is nonpolar."}],"trap":"Picking any long side chain: polar amides do not suit a hydrocarbon."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"a small hydrocarbon odorant","note":"nonpolar ligand"},{"t":"sealed off from the surrounding water","note":"water-free pocket"},{"t":"charged residues","note":"charge hurts binding"}]},{"text":"**Like holds like.** A nonpolar guest is held by nonpolar side chains through dispersion contacts.","calc":["nonpolar: Leu, Ile, Val, Phe, Met","polar: Asn, Gln, Ser, Thr","ionizable: Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg, His"]},{"text":"**Also:** charged or polar side chains don't fit a pocket for a nonpolar ligand; swapping in Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg, Ser or Thr weakens binding."},{"text":"**Also:** when a nonpolar part of a drug sits in a water-free pocket, its main interaction is hydrophobic contact (London dispersion) with nonpolar side chains such as Leu, Ile, Val and Phe, not hydrogen bonds or salt bridges."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a nonpolar ligand in a water-free pocket is lined by nonpolar side chains (Leu, Ile, Val, Phe, Met); polar and charged side chains face water."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-32a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:32#1","pe1-cp:32#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:32"],"passage":"Some bacterial toxins switch off host proteins by AMPylation: the enzyme attaches an AMP unit from ATP to a threonine side chain of the target and releases pyrophosphate. To detect the modification, researchers use ATP carrying ³³P at one or more phosphate positions. Only the target protein is then checked for radioactivity.","q":"Which ATP label would end up on the target protein?","fig":"atp","choices":["[α-³³P]ATP","[β-³³P]ATP","[γ-³³P]ATP","[β,γ-³³P]ATP"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"The γ phosphate is what kinases transfer; in AMPylation it leaves with the pyrophosphate."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The β phosphate stays bonded to γ and leaves as pyrophosphate, not on the protein."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Both labeled phosphates sit in the pyrophosphate that is released."}],"rule":"Find which piece of ATP the enzyme moves: kinases move the γ phosphate; AMP transfer moves adenosine plus the α phosphate, and β + γ leave as PPi.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"AMP = adenosine + the α phosphate. Pyrophosphate = β + γ. Only a label in the AMP part reaches the protein."},"exp":"AMPylation transfers **AMP**, adenosine plus the **α phosphate**, to the threonine and releases **pyrophosphate (PPi)**, which holds the β and γ phosphates. So only an **α label** ends up on the protein. A kinase is different: it transfers the terminal **γ phosphate**, and the kinase itself is the catalyst, not the source.","how":{"ask":"Split ATP into the part transferred and the part released, then follow each label.","know":"• ATP = adenosine–Pα–Pβ–Pγ.\n• Kinases move Pγ; ADP is left behind.\n• AMP transfer moves adenosine–Pα; Pβ–Pγ leaves as pyrophosphate.\n• The enzyme is the catalyst, not the source of the group.","steps":[{"text":"The enzyme moves AMP and releases pyrophosphate.","hl":"attaches an AMP unit from ATP","fig":"atp","calc":["ATP → AMP (adenosine + Pα) on the protein + PPi (Pβ–Pγ)"]},{"text":"γ and β labels leave in the pyrophosphate.","hl":"releases pyrophosphate","strike":["[γ-³³P]ATP","[β-³³P]ATP","[β,γ-³³P]ATP"]},{"text":"Answer: [α-³³P]ATP."}],"trap":"Reaching for γ by habit: that is the kinase answer; here AMP, with the α phosphate, is transferred."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what is moved and what is released**.","marks":[{"t":"attaches an AMP unit","note":"adenosine + Pα"},{"t":"releases pyrophosphate","note":"Pβ–Pγ leaves"},{"t":"Only the target protein","note":"follow the transferred part"}]},{"text":"**Map each phosphate.**","calc":["Pα → with AMP → protein","Pβ, Pγ → PPi → released"]},{"text":"**Also:** a kinase is the catalyst; the phosphate it attaches comes from ATP's γ phosphate."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** follow the piece of ATP the enzyme transfers: γ for a kinase, α (with adenosine) for AMP transfer; labels elsewhere leave in ADP or PPi."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-33a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:33#1","pe1-cp:33#2","pe6-cp:13#1","pe6-cp:13#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:33"],"passage":"(E)-4-Chlorooct-4-ene, shown, and its Z isomer can be separated by gas chromatography. A sample of either pure isomer stays unchanged for weeks at room temperature. The two interconvert only when the sample is irradiated with ultraviolet light, which briefly breaks the π bond.","q":"Which carbon is sp² and belongs to the non-rotating bond?","fig":"cpd-e-4-chlorooct-4-ene","choices":["C1","C4","C7","C8"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"C1 is the CH₃ end carbon: sp³, tetrahedral and free to rotate."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"C7 is a CH₂ in the chain with four single bonds, so it is sp³."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"C8 is the terminal CH₃: sp³ and tetrahedral."}],"rule":"Only the two carbons of the C=C are sp² (trigonal planar, about 120°) and locked against rotation; every carbon with four single bonds is sp³.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The name says where the double bond is: oct-4-ene means C4=C5. Pick C4 or C5."},"exp":"Geometric isomers exist because the **C4=C5 double bond** cannot rotate without breaking its π bond, which the UV light does briefly. Each carbon of a C=C has three electron domains, so it is **sp², trigonal planar**. C4 is one of them; C1, C7 and C8 have four single bonds and are sp³.","how":{"ask":"Find the C=C from the name, then pick a carbon in it.","know":"• E/Z (cis/trans) isomers come from a C=C, which cannot rotate.\n• C=C carbons have 3 domains → sp², trigonal planar.\n• Carbons with four single bonds are sp³, tetrahedral.","steps":[{"text":"The isomers interconvert only when the π bond breaks, so the C=C is the locked bond.","hl":"briefly breaks the π bond","fig":"cpd-e-4-chlorooct-4-ene"},{"text":"\"Oct-4-ene\" puts the double bond between C4 and C5.","hl":"(E)-4-Chlorooct-4-ene"},{"text":"C1, C7 and C8 have only single bonds: sp³.","strike":["C1","C7","C8"]},{"text":"Answer: C4."}],"trap":"Picking a carbon next to the double bond: an allylic CH₂ is still sp³."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"its Z isomer","note":"geometric isomers"},{"t":"stays unchanged for weeks","note":"no rotation"},{"t":"briefly breaks the π bond","note":"the C=C is the locked bond"}]},{"text":"**Count domains on each carbon.**","calc":["C4, C5: three domains → sp², planar","C1–C3, C6–C8: four → sp³"]},{"text":"**Also:** CO₂'s carbon works the same way: two O atoms, two double bonds, each counted once, no lone pair → 2 domains → sp, linear, 180°."},{"text":"**Also:** cis/trans isomers exist because a C=C cannot rotate without breaking its π bond."},{"text":"**Also:** a Schiff base is an imine, R₂C=N–R′, made when an amine condenses with an aldehyde or ketone. Count its C=N carbon like an alkene carbon: two single-bonded neighbors plus the N, with the double bond counted once, and no lone pair. So it is sp², trigonal planar, with angles near 120°.","calc":["imine C: 3 atoms + 0 lone pairs = 3 → sp², ≈120°","imine N: 2 atoms + 1 lone pair = 3 → sp² as well"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the carbons of a C=C are sp² and trigonal planar, and the C=C cannot rotate, which is why cis/trans isomers exist."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-34b","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:34#2","pe1-cp:34#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:34"],"passage":"A fungal lipase is incubated with triolein, the triacylglycerol drawn, in an unbuffered emulsion. The enzyme hydrolyzes only the ester on the middle carbon of glycerol and leaves the two end esters intact. As the reaction runs, the pH of the emulsion falls.","q":"What is the other product, apart from oleic acid?","fig":"triacylglycerol","choices":["1,3-Dioleoylglycerol","1,2-Dioleoylglycerol","2-Oleoylglycerol","1-Oleoylglycerol"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is what pancreatic lipase leaves (ends cut, middle kept); this enzyme does the opposite."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Cutting the middle ester frees C2's –OH; a 1,2-diester would still hold the middle ester."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A monoester at C1 would need an end ester cut too, and this enzyme leaves both ends intact."}],"rule":"Lipases hydrolyze ester bonds: remove only the acyl groups at the positions the passage says are cut, and keep the rest.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Only C2 is cut: the two end oleates stay, so the glycerol keeps its acyl groups at 1 and 3."},"exp":"A lipase **hydrolyzes ester bonds**, releasing fatty acids, so the pH drops as oleic acid forms. This enzyme cuts **only the C2 ester**, so the glycerol keeps its oleoyl groups on **C1 and C3**: **1,3-dioleoylglycerol**. Pancreatic lipase does the reverse, cutting at C1 and C3 and leaving a 2-monoacylglycerol.","how":{"ask":"Remove only the acyl groups the passage says are cut, then name what is left.","know":"• Lipases hydrolyze the ester bonds of triacylglycerols, freeing fatty acids.\n• Number glycerol C1–C3; the middle carbon is C2.\n• Pancreatic lipase cuts at 1 and 3 and leaves a 2-monoacylglycerol.","steps":[{"text":"Only the middle ester is cut.","hl":"only the ester on the middle carbon","fig":"triacylglycerol"},{"text":"Both end esters stay, so the product still has acyl groups at C1 and C3.","strike":["2-Oleoylglycerol","1-Oleoylglycerol"]},{"text":"A 1,2-diester would still hold the middle ester.","strike":["1,2-Dioleoylglycerol"]},{"text":"Answer: 1,3-dioleoylglycerol."}],"trap":"Answering with the pancreatic-lipase product (a 2-monoacylglycerol) by habit."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"only the ester on the middle carbon","note":"C2 cut"},{"t":"leaves the two end esters intact","note":"C1, C3 kept"},{"t":"the pH of the emulsion falls","note":"a fatty acid forms"}]},{"text":"**Cut, then name.**","calc":["triolein − C2 oleate → oleic acid + 1,3-dioleoylglycerol"]},{"text":"**Also:** pancreatic lipase cuts only the esters at positions 1 and 3, freeing two fatty acids and leaving the chain on C2: a 2-monoacylglycerol.","calc":["3 chains − 2 cut = 1 left, on C2"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a lipase hydrolyzes the ester bonds the passage names; remove those acyl groups and name the glycerol by the positions still esterified."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-35b","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:35#2","pe1-cp:35#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:35"],"passage":"Engineers want a protein that binds a positively charged chromatography resin more tightly. Four residues of a soluble protein are replaced: Lys→Glu, Arg→Asp, Lys→Asp and Ala→His. The variant folds normally and keeps its activity.","q":"What is the change in the protein's net charge at pH 7?","choices":["A decrease of 6","A decrease of 5","No change","An increase of 6"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign: + residues (Lys, Arg) become − ones (Glu, Asp), so the net charge must fall."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Counts His as +1, but with a pKa near 6 it is mostly neutral at pH 7."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Each Lys/Arg → Glu/Asp swap is +1 → −1, a change of −2, so the total can't be zero."}],"rule":"At pH 7 Lys and Arg are +1, Asp and Glu −1, His about 0; add up each swap's change (new minus old).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"At pH 7: K, R = +1; D, E = −1; A, H ≈ 0. Each + to − swap is −2; three swaps = −6. Ala→His adds nothing."},"exp":"At pH 7, Lys and Arg are **+1**, Glu and Asp are **−1**, and Ala and His are **≈ 0**. Each basic-to-acidic swap goes from +1 to −1, a change of **−2**; there are three of them. Ala→His changes nothing. Total: −2 − 2 − 2 + 0 = **−6**.","how":{"ask":"For each substitution, subtract the old side-chain charge from the new one at pH 7, then add the four changes.","know":"• Charges at pH 7: Arg/Lys **+1**, Asp/Glu **−1**, His **≈ 0**, others **0**.\n• Change at each site = **new − old**.\n• Net change = sum of the site changes.","steps":[{"text":"List the swaps.","hl":"Lys→Glu, Arg→Asp, Lys→Asp and Ala→His"},{"text":"Compute new − old at each site.","fig":["lysine-sc","arginine-sc","glutamate","aspartate-sc","histidine-sc"],"calc":["Lys→Glu: (−1) − (+1) = **−2**","Arg→Asp: (−1) − (+1) = **−2**","Lys→Asp: (−1) − (+1) = **−2**","Ala→His: 0 − 0 = **0**"]},{"text":"Add them.","calc":["Δ = −2 + (−2) + (−2) + 0 = **−6**"]},{"text":"Counting His as +1 gives −2 − 2 − 2 + 1 = −5, but at pH 7 His is ≈ 0.","strike":["A decrease of 5"]},{"text":"Seeing three charged residues swapped for three charged residues as no change ignores the sign: each swap trades +1 for −1, a change of −2.","strike":["No change"]},{"text":"Positive charges are replaced by negative ones, so the net charge falls; an increase has the sign flipped.","strike":["An increase of 6"]},{"text":"Answer: a decrease of 6 (Δ = −6)."}],"trap":"Counting His as +1 at pH 7: its side-chain pKa is about 6, so at pH 7 it is mostly neutral and Ala→His adds nothing."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Four residues","note":"four sites to add"},{"t":"pH 7","note":"use pH 7 charges"},{"t":"net charge","note":"sum of changes"}]},{"text":"**Assign charges at pH 7.**","calc":["Lys, Arg: +1","Glu, Asp: −1","Ala, His: 0"]},{"text":"**Change per site = new − old.** A + → − swap is worth 2.","calc":["(−1) − (+1) = −2"]},{"text":"**Also:** at pH 7, count Arg and Lys as +1, Asp and Glu as −1, and His and everything else as 0."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for each substitution take new charge minus old charge at the stated pH, then add all the site changes."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-36a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:36#1","pe1-cp:36#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:36"],"passage":"Researchers made two mutants of a lipase, A and B, by changing residues in a flexible surface loop. Each enzyme was heated, cooled and assayed. After 15 min at 55 °C and cooling, wild-type lipase kept 30% of its activity, mutant A kept 65% and mutant B kept 25%. Unheated samples of all three had equal activity.","q":"Which conclusion do these results support?","choices":["Only mutant A is more stable","Both mutants are more stable","Only mutant B is more stable","Neither mutant is more stable"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Mutant B kept 25%, below the wild type's 30%, so B is less stable, not more."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"B kept 25% vs 30% for wild type: less activity left means less stable, not more."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Mutant A kept 65% vs the wild type's 30%, clearly more activity left, so a mutant did gain stability."}],"rule":"Compare each mutant with the wild type on its own: more activity left after heating means more stable.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Higher retained % = more heat-stable. Compare to wild type's 30: A at 65 beats it, B at 25 falls short. So only A."},"exp":"Retained activity is the measure of heat stability: more activity left means less damage. Mutant A kept **65% vs the wild type's 30%**, so it is more stable. Mutant B kept **25%**, slightly **less** than the wild type, so it is not more stable.","how":{"ask":"Line up each mutant's retained activity against the wild type's and decide which are higher.","know":"• **% activity kept after heating**: higher → more heat-stable.\n• Compare every variant with the **wild type**, the reference.\n• A conclusion about \"both\" fails if even one variant does not fit.","steps":[{"text":"Find the reference and each variant's value.","hl":"wild-type lipase kept 30% of its activity, mutant A kept 65% and mutant B kept 25%"},{"text":"Compare each with the wild type.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Activity kept after heating","x":{"label":"Enzyme"},"y":{"label":"Activity kept (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"categories":["Wild type","Mutant A","Mutant B"],"series":[{"name":"Kept","values":[30,65,25]}]},"calc":["A: 65% > 30% → **more stable**","B: 25% ≤ 30% → **not more stable**"]},{"text":"Mutant B kept less than the wild type, so it is neither more stable alone nor along with A.","strike":["Both mutants are more stable","Only mutant B is more stable"]},{"text":"Mutant A clearly kept more, so it is wrong that neither is more stable.","strike":["Neither mutant is more stable"]},{"text":"Answer: only mutant A is more stable."}],"trap":"Assuming every mutant behaves alike: check each one against the wild type separately."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"55 °C","note":"heating step"},{"t":"30%","note":"wild type, the reference"},{"t":"65%","note":"mutant A"},{"t":"25%","note":"mutant B"}]},{"text":"**Heat, cool, assay.** The activity that survives measures how well the enzyme resists heat.","calc":["% kept ↑ → heat stability ↑"]},{"text":"**Subtract the reference.**","calc":["A: 65 − 30 = +35","B: 25 − 30 = −5"]},{"text":"**Also:** keep only the claim the numbers show; reject absolute words like all, fully or every when any activity remains."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare each variant's retained activity with the wild type's; higher means more heat-stable, lower means less."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-37a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:37#1","pe6-cp:7#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:37"],"passage":"A clinic tests urine pH to monitor the risk of kidney stones. A urine sample from patient A has a pH of 5.8, and a sample from patient B has a pH of 7.8. Both samples are read at the same temperature with a calibrated meter.","q":"How does [H⁺] in sample B compare with sample A?","choices":["1/100 as much","100 times as much","1/2 as much","1/20 as much"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: higher pH means less H⁺, and B has the higher pH."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Multiplies 2 × 10 instead of raising 10 to the power 2; each pH unit is its own factor of ten."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"pH is a log scale; a 2-unit gap is a power of ten, not a factor of 2."}],"rule":"Each pH unit is a factor of 10 in [H⁺], and higher pH means less H⁺: the factor is 10 raised to the pH difference.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Each pH unit = 10× in [H+], and higher pH = less H+. 7.8 − 5.8 = 2 units → 10² = 1/100."},"exp":"Because **pH = −log[H⁺]**, one pH unit corresponds to a **tenfold** change in [H⁺]. Sample B is 7.8 − 5.8 = 2 units higher, so its [H⁺] is 10² = 100 times lower, **1/100 as much**: about 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ M versus 1.6 × 10⁻⁶ M.","how":{"ask":"Find the pH difference, turn it into a power of ten, and decide whether a higher pH means more or less H⁺.","know":"• **pH = −log[H⁺]** → [H⁺] = 10^(−pH).\n• A difference of Δ pH units = a factor of **10^Δ** in [H⁺].\n• **Higher pH = less H⁺**.","steps":[{"text":"Find the pH change from A to B.","hl":"a pH of 5.8","calc":["ΔpH = 7.8 − 5.8 = **2** units"]},{"text":"Convert to a factor and check with real concentrations.","hl":"a pH of 7.8","calc":["factor = 10² = **100**","pH 5.8: [H⁺] = 10^−5.8 ≈ 1.6 × 10⁻⁶ M","pH 7.8: [H⁺] = 10^−7.8 ≈ 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ M","1.6 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ = **100**"],"strike":["1/2 as much","1/20 as much"]},{"text":"Direction: B has the higher pH, so it has less H⁺.","strike":["100 times as much"]},{"text":"Answer: 1/100 as much."}],"trap":"\"1/2 as much\" treats pH as linear; every pH unit is a tenfold change, so 2 units is 10 × 10 = 100."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers** and what you must compare.","marks":[{"t":"a pH of 5.8","note":"sample A"},{"t":"a pH of 7.8","note":"sample B"},{"t":"[H⁺]","note":"H⁺ concentration"}]},{"text":"**pH counts powers of ten:** [H⁺] = 10^(−pH).","calc":["pH 5 → 10⁻⁵ M","pH 6 → 10⁻⁶ M (10 times less)"]},{"text":"**Subtract, then raise 10 to that power.** Then set the direction: higher pH, less H⁺.","calc":["factor = 10^(ΔpH)"]},{"text":"**Also:** compare any solution with pure water (pH 7.0 at 25 °C) the same way. Each pH unit above 7 divides [H⁺] by 10, and since [H⁺][OH⁻] = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ stays fixed, [OH⁻] grows by that same factor. A fractional gap works too: 10 raised to the gap.","calc":["pH 9.0 vs water: gap 2.0 → [H⁺] 100 times lower","[OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 10⁻⁹ = 10⁻⁵ M, 100 times water's 10⁻⁷ M","gap 0.5 → 10^0.5 ≈ 3.2"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a change of Δ pH units changes [H⁺] by a factor of 10^Δ, and a lower pH means more H⁺."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-38b","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:38#2","pe1-cp:38#1","pe1-cp:38#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:38"],"passage":"Glycogen phosphorylase removes glucose units from the ends of glycogen chains by phosphorolysis. The product is glucose 1-phosphate, which phosphoglucomutase then converts to glucose 6-phosphate for glycolysis. Both sugar phosphates exist mainly in the pyranose form.","q":"To which carbon of glucose does phosphorylase attach the phosphate?","choices":["C-1","C-4","C-5","C-6"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"C-6 belongs to glucose 6-phosphate, the later product named in the passage, not to phosphorylase's product."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"C-4 is the ring carbon that links to the next glucose in a glycogen chain; no phosphate goes there."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"C-5 bonds the ring oxygen and carries the CH₂OH; neither compound has a phosphate on it."}],"rule":"Read the number in the name: glucose n-phosphate carries its phosphate on Cn. Number from the anomeric C1; C6 is the CH₂OH outside the ring.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The passage names phosphorylase's product: glucose 1-phosphate. The number in the name is the carbon."},"exp":"Phosphorylase makes **glucose 1-phosphate**, so the phosphate sits on **C1, the anomeric carbon**, bonded to the ring O and to an O outside the ring. C6 is the familiar position, but it belongs to glucose 6-phosphate, the later product. In glucopyranose, C1 is anomeric, C2–C4 are ring carbons, C5 bonds the ring O, and C6 is the CH₂OH.","how":{"ask":"Take the carbon number from the compound the passage names for this enzyme.","know":"• Glucopyranose numbering: C1 anomeric; C2–C4 in the ring; C5 bonded to the ring O; C6 = CH₂OH.\n• Glucose n-phosphate → phosphate on Cn.","steps":[{"text":"The phosphorylase product is named in the passage.","hl":"The product is glucose 1-phosphate","fig":"alpha-glucopyranose"},{"text":"C-6 belongs to the later product, glucose 6-phosphate.","strike":["C-6"]},{"text":"C-4 and C-5 carry no phosphate in either compound.","strike":["C-4","C-5"]},{"text":"Answer: C-1."}],"trap":"Defaulting to C6 because glucose 6-phosphate is the familiar one."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"glucose 1-phosphate","note":"phosphorylase product"},{"t":"glucose 6-phosphate","note":"the later product"},{"t":"pyranose form","note":"six-membered ring"}]},{"text":"**Number the ring.**","fig":"alpha-glucopyranose","calc":["C1: anomeric","C2–C4: ring","C5: bonded to ring O","C6: CH₂OH"]},{"text":"**Also:** in glucopyranose, C1 is anomeric, C2–C4 are ring carbons, C5 is bonded to the ring O, and C6 is the CH₂OH."},{"text":"**Also:** always put the phosphate where the passage says, then number the ring from C1; do not default to C6."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** glucose n-phosphate has its phosphate on Cn; number from the anomeric C1 and use the compound the passage names, not the familiar C6."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-39b","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:39#2","pe1-cp:39#1","pe1-cp:43#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:43"],"passage":"In Andersen disease, the glycogen branching enzyme is deficient. Liver glycogen from these patients has long, unbranched outer chains and very few branch points. Normal glycogen, by contrast, branches every few residues along each chain.","q":"Compared with normal glycogen of the same mass, the patients' glycogen has fewer:","choices":["Nonreducing ends","α-1,4 glycosidic links","β-1,4 glycosidic links","Reducing ends"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Fewer branches leave longer chains, so the α-1,4 links do not fall; they slightly rise."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Each glycogen molecule has one reducing end, however much it branches."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Glycogen has no β-1,4 links at all; those belong to cellulose."}],"rule":"Each branch point is one α-1,6 bond and adds one more nonreducing end; fewer branches means fewer of both.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count ends: a molecule with b branch points has b + 1 nonreducing ends and still one reducing end."},"exp":"Each branch hangs from an **α-1,6 bond** made by the branching enzyme, and each one starts a new chain with its own **nonreducing end**. With few branches the glycogen has **fewer α-1,6 bonds and fewer nonreducing ends**. It still has one reducing end per molecule, more α-1,4 links in its long chains, and no β-1,4 links at all.","how":{"ask":"Trace what each branch point adds, then take most of them away.","know":"• Branch points are α-1,6 bonds; chains are α-1,4.\n• Each branch adds one nonreducing end; a molecule has one reducing end.\n• β-1,4 links are in cellulose, not glycogen.","steps":[{"text":"The branching enzyme is missing, so few α-1,6 branch points form.","hl":"very few branch points","fig":"alpha-glucopyranose"},{"text":"Fewer branches → fewer chain tips → fewer nonreducing ends."},{"text":"α-1,4 links do not drop, the reducing end stays at one, and glycogen has no β-1,4 links.","strike":["α-1,4 glycosidic links","Reducing ends","β-1,4 glycosidic links"]},{"text":"Answer: nonreducing ends."}],"trap":"Thinking every bond count drops: only the branch-linked features (α-1,6 bonds, nonreducing ends) fall."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"branching enzyme is deficient","note":"few α-1,6 bonds"},{"t":"very few branch points","note":"fewer chain tips"},{"t":"of the same mass","note":"same number of glucoses"}]},{"text":"**Count what one branch adds.**","calc":["1 branch = 1 α-1,6 bond + 1 nonreducing end","reducing ends: always 1"]},{"text":"**Also:** glycogen synthase forms α-1,4 bonds; the branching enzyme forms α-1,6 bonds."},{"text":"**Also:** branch points are α-1,6 bonds, so fewer branches means fewer α-1,6 bonds."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** each branch point is one α-1,6 bond and one extra nonreducing end; fewer branches means fewer of both, while the single reducing end stays."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-39c","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:39#3","pe1-cp:39#1","pe1-cp:39#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:39"],"passage":"Isomaltose is a disaccharide of two glucose units released when the branch points of starch are digested. Drawn as a Haworth projection, the first glucose has its CH₂OH group above the ring, while its C1–O link to the second sugar points below the ring. That oxygen bridges to the second glucose at its exocyclic –CH₂– carbon.","q":"The linkage in this disaccharide is best classified as:","choices":["α-1,4","α-1,6","β-1,4","β-1,6"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"β would put C1's bond on the same face as the CH₂OH; here they are on opposite faces."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Wrong on both counts: opposite faces means α, and the partner is the CH₂OH carbon, not a ring C4."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A 1,4 link joins C1 to a ring C4–OH; this oxygen sits on the CH₂OH carbon instead."}],"rule":"Haworth: C1's bond on the same face as CH₂OH is β, on the opposite face α; the partner carbon (C4 in the ring, C6 = CH₂OH) gives the number.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Opposite faces → α. Linked to the CH₂OH carbon → 1,6. A branch-point bond, like glycogen's."},"exp":"On a Haworth drawing, **C1's bond on the face opposite the CH₂OH** marks an **α** link; the same face would be β. The bridging O joins the second glucose's **CH₂OH carbon, C6**, so the bond is **α-1,6**, the branch-point bond of glycogen and amylopectin. Glycogen synthase makes the α-1,4 chain bonds.","how":{"ask":"Decide α or β from the faces, then read the partner carbon's number.","know":"• Haworth: C1–O bond and CH₂OH on the same face → β; opposite faces → α.\n• C6 is the CH₂OH carbon; C4 is a ring carbon.\n• Glycogen synthase makes α-1,4; the branching enzyme makes α-1,6.","steps":[{"text":"CH₂OH above, C1 bond below: opposite faces, so α.","hl":"points below the ring","strike":["β-1,4","β-1,6"]},{"text":"The partner is the CH₂OH carbon, C6, not a ring C4.","hl":"its exocyclic –CH₂– carbon","strike":["α-1,4"]},{"text":"Answer: α-1,6."}],"trap":"Reading \"below the ring\" as β: compare C1's bond with the CH₂OH, not with up or down alone."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"CH₂OH group above the ring","note":"reference face"},{"t":"points below the ring","note":"opposite face"},{"t":"its exocyclic –CH₂– carbon","note":"the CH₂OH carbon, C6"}]},{"text":"**Compare faces** on the glucose that donates C1.","fig":["alpha-glucopyranose","beta-glucopyranose"],"calc":["same face → β","opposite faces → α"]},{"text":"**Number the partner.** A ring C4–OH gives 1,4; the CH₂OH carbon gives 1,6."},{"text":"**Also:** glycogen synthase makes the α-1,4 bonds of each chain, and the branching enzyme makes the α-1,6 branch points."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a Haworth drawing, C1's bond on the same face as CH₂OH is β and on the opposite face α; the partner carbon gives 1,4 or 1,6."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-40a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:40#1","pe1-cp:40#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:40"],"passage":"In the mammary gland, lactose synthase builds lactose from glucose and an activated sugar. In lactose synthesis, UDP-galactose donates its galactose. When a sample of UDP-galactose is completely hydrolyzed, the products include galactose, uracil, phosphate and a second sugar with formula C₅H₁₀O₅.","q":"Apart from galactose, which sugar ring is part of UDP-galactose?","choices":["Ribofuranose","Deoxyribofuranose","Glucopyranose","Fructofuranose"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"UDP has no \"d\" prefix, so its sugar keeps the 2′ –OH: it is not deoxyribose."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A nucleotide's own sugar is a five-carbon pentose; glucose is a six-carbon hexose."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Fructose is a hexose too; a nucleotide's sugar is always ribose or deoxyribose."}],"rule":"A nucleotide's own sugar is a pentose: ribose in UDP, ATP and the like; only the 'd' nucleotides (dATP, dTTP) use deoxyribose.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"U in UDP is uridine, an RNA nucleoside, so its sugar is ribose (5-ring, furanose). DNA ones are deoxy, and dUDP isn't the carrier."},"exp":"UDP is **uridine diphosphate**. Uridine is uracil bonded to **ribose**, a five-membered (furanose) ring with –OH groups on C2′ and C3′. UDP is an RNA-type nucleotide, so its sugar is ribose, not deoxyribose; glucose and fructose are not part of it.","how":{"ask":"What does UDP stand for, and which sugar is in that nucleoside?","know":"• **UDP** = uridine diphosphate; **uridine** = uracil + **ribose**.\n• Nucleotides without a \"d\" (ATP, UTP, UDP, CTP) carry **ribose**.\n• Deoxyribose appears in DNA nucleotides (dATP, dTTP…).","steps":[{"text":"The molecule is galactose attached to UDP.","hl":"UDP-galactose"},{"text":"Expand the name.","fig":["uracil","ribose"],"calc":["UDP = uridine diphosphate","uridine = uracil + **ribose** (furanose)"]},{"text":"UDP has no \"d\" prefix, so its sugar keeps the 2′ –OH: it is not deoxyribose.","strike":["Deoxyribofuranose"]},{"text":"Glucose and fructose are not part of a uridine nucleotide.","strike":["Glucopyranose","Fructofuranose"]},{"text":"Answer: ribofuranose."}],"trap":"Picking glucose because UDP-sugars are usually UDP-glucose: the attached sugar varies, but the nucleotide's own sugar is always ribose."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"UDP-galactose","note":"galactose + UDP"},{"t":"Apart from galactose","note":"the nucleotide's sugar"}]},{"text":"**Read UDP.** Uridine diphosphate: uracil + ribose + two phosphates.","fig":"ribose","calc":["U = uridine","D = di (2 phosphates)","P = phosphate"]},{"text":"**Ribose vs deoxyribose.** Ribose has –OH on C2′ and C3′; deoxyribose lacks the 2′ –OH.","fig":["t1-ribose-c2oh","t1-dr-c2"]},{"text":"**Also:** deoxyribose is found in DNA nucleotides such as dTTP; nucleotides without the \"d\" carry ribose."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** UDP is uridine diphosphate, and uridine contains ribose."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-41a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:41#1","pe1-cp:41#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:41"],"passage":"Researchers label a regulatory enzyme in two ways at once. A phosphorylated enzyme carries ³²P in its phosphoserines and ³H in its leucines. It is incubated with a protein phosphatase and then separated from small molecules on a gel.","q":"Which label remains on the enzyme?","choices":["³H only","³²P only","Both labels","Neither label"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: the phosphatase removes exactly the phosphate that carries ³²P, so ³²P can't be what's left."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A phosphatase strips the phosphate, so ³²P can't still be there alongside ³H."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Losing ³H would mean cutting out leucines; a phosphatase only cleaves phosphate esters, not the protein."}],"rule":"A phosphatase removes only phosphate groups (a kinase adds them); labels anywhere else on the protein stay.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Phosphatases remove only phosphate groups. ³²P leaves with the phosphate; ³H in leucine is untouched, so ³H stays."},"exp":"A phosphatase **hydrolyzes phosphate esters**, releasing the phosphate as free Pᵢ. The ³²P rides on that phosphate, so it leaves the enzyme. The ³H is built into the leucine side chains, which the phosphatase does not touch, so **³H is the label that stays**.","how":{"ask":"Place each label on its chemical group, then ask which group the phosphatase leaves alone.","know":"• **Phosphatase**: R–O–PO₃²⁻ + H₂O → R–OH + Pᵢ (removes phosphate).\n• A radioactive label goes wherever its atom goes.\n• Kinase adds phosphate; phosphatase takes it off.","steps":[{"text":"Locate each label.","hl":"³²P in its phosphoserines and ³H in its leucines","calc":["³²P → phosphate groups","³H → leucine side chains"]},{"text":"The phosphatase cuts off the phosphate.","calc":["Ser–O–PO₃²⁻(³²P) + H₂O → Ser–OH + Pᵢ(³²P)","³²P **leaves**; ³H **stays**"]},{"text":"Removing the phosphate takes ³²P with it, so ³²P cannot remain, alone or alongside ³H.","strike":["³²P only","Both labels"]},{"text":"Leucine is untouched, so ³H is not lost.","strike":["Neither label"]},{"text":"Answer: ³H only."}],"trap":"Mixing up kinase and phosphatase: a kinase adds phosphate (³²P would stay or grow); a phosphatase removes it, leaving only labels on the rest of the protein."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"³²P in its phosphoserines","note":"label on phosphate"},{"t":"³H in its leucines","note":"label on the protein"},{"t":"protein phosphatase","note":"removes phosphate"}]},{"text":"**Each isotope tags one part.** Phosphorus is only in the phosphate; hydrogen here is in leucine.","fig":"leucine"},{"text":"**Phosphatase = phosphate off.** The phosphate leaves as free Pᵢ.","calc":["phospho-protein + H₂O → protein + Pᵢ"]},{"text":"**Also:** a phosphatase removes only phosphate, so a ¹⁴C label in the sugar backbone stays with the sugar."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a phosphatase removes phosphate groups, so a ³²P label on them leaves while labels elsewhere (³H in leucine, ¹⁴C in a sugar) stay."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-42b","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:42#2","pe1-cp:42#1","pe4-cp:49#1","pe4-cp:49#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:42"],"passage":"Strontium-90 and iodine-131, two fission products in reactor waste, are both β⁻ emitters. In each decay, an electron and an antineutrino leave the nucleus. Their daughter nuclides are monitored in groundwater near storage sites.","q":"Which daughter nuclides do they form?","choices":["Yttrium-90 and xenon-131","Rubidium-90 and tellurium-131","Yttrium-90 and tellurium-131","Krypton-86 and antimony-127"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Both daughters sit one place left (lower Z), which is β⁺ emission; β⁻ raises Z by one."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Strontium's daughter is fine, but tellurium is one place left of iodine; β⁻ moves one place right."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Mass numbers fell by 4, the mark of α decay; β⁻ emission leaves A unchanged."}],"rule":"β⁻ turns a neutron into a proton: the mass number stays, Z rises by one, so step one place right on the periodic table.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"β⁻: mass same, Z + 1, so one step right on the periodic table. Sr → Y, I → Xe, both keeping 90 and 131."},"exp":"β⁻ decay keeps **A** and raises **Z by 1**. Sr (Z = 38) → **Y (39)**, A stays 90; I (Z = 53) → **Xe (54)**, A stays 131. Rubidium and tellurium lower Z (β⁺), and krypton-86 with antimony-127 lose 4 mass units (α).","how":{"ask":"Apply A unchanged, Z + 1 to each parent, then name the element with the new Z.","know":"• **β⁻ decay**: A unchanged, **Z + 1**.\n• Z sets the element: Kr 36, Rb 37, Sr 38, Y 39; Sb 51, Te 52, I 53, Xe 54.","steps":[{"text":"Both parents are β⁻ emitters: keep A, add 1 to Z.","hl":"both β⁻ emitters","calc":["A → A, Z → Z + 1"]},{"text":"Strontium-90 (Z = 38).","hl":"Strontium-90","calc":["⁹⁰₃₈Sr → ⁹⁰₃₉Y + ⁰₋₁e + ν̄","Z: 38 = 39 + (−1) ✓   A: 90 = 90 ✓"]},{"text":"Iodine-131 (Z = 53).","hl":"iodine-131","calc":["¹³¹₅₃I → ¹³¹₅₄Xe + ⁰₋₁e + ν̄","Z: 53 = 54 + (−1) ✓   A: 131 = 131 ✓"]},{"text":"Rubidium and tellurium have Z − 1 (positron emission); one correct daughter paired with tellurium is still wrong; krypton-86 and antimony-127 are alpha products.","strike":["Rubidium-90 and tellurium-131","Yttrium-90 and tellurium-131","Krypton-86 and antimony-127"]},{"text":"Answer: yttrium-90 and xenon-131."}],"trap":"Moving one step left on the periodic table (rubidium, tellurium): that is β⁺ decay. β⁻ moves one step right."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the parents and the decay type**.","marks":[{"t":"Strontium-90","note":"Z = 38, A = 90"},{"t":"iodine-131","note":"Z = 53, A = 131"},{"t":"β⁻ emitters","note":"A same, Z + 1"}]},{"text":"**Keep the mass number.** The emitted electron carries no nucleons.","calc":["daughters: A = 90 and A = 131"]},{"text":"**Step one element to the right.** Z + 1 is the next element in the periodic table.","calc":["Sr (38) → Y (39)","I (53) → Xe (54)"]},{"text":"**Also:** in β⁻ decay one neutron turns into a proton, emitting the electron and an antineutrino, so the daughter has one more proton and one fewer neutron.","calc":["n → p + e⁻ + antineutrino","Sr-90: 38 p, 52 n → Y-90: 39 p, 51 n"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for β⁻ decay, keep A and move one element right (Z + 1) to name the daughter."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-44c","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:44#3","pe1-cp:44#1","pe1-cp:44#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:44"],"q":"Arsenic acid, H₃AsO₄, is triprotic. Which of these acids dissociates to the smallest extent when dissolved in water?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Titration of 30.0 mL of 0.050 M H₃AsO₄ with 0.10 M NaOH","x":{"label":"Volume of NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":45,"step":5},"y":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"pH","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,1.8],[3,2],[7.5,2.4],[12,2.9],[14,3.4],[15,4.6],[16,5.9],[18,6.4],[22.5,7],[27,7.6],[29,8.1],[30,9.2],[31,10.3],[33,10.8],[37.5,11.3],[42,11.7],[45,11.8]]}]},"choices":["HAsO₄²⁻","H₂AsO₄⁻","H₃AsO₄","HCl"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"HCl is a strong acid: it dissociates completely, the most, not the least."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The neutral parent acid always has the largest Ka (Ka1) of a polyprotic series."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Its Ka2 is weaker than Ka1 but stronger than the next step, so it isn't the weakest."}],"rule":"In a polyprotic series each step is weaker: the more negative the species, the less it gives up H⁺; strong acids dissociate fully.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Each H+ pulled off a more negative ion is harder. The 2− ion (HAsO4²⁻) holds its last H+ hardest, so it dissociates least; HCl is strong."},"exp":"HCl is a strong acid. Arsenic acid's three Ka values fall steeply (pKa ≈ 2.2, 7.0, 11.5) because each H⁺ comes off a more negative ion. **HAsO₄²⁻** must lose H⁺ from a 2− ion, so it has the smallest Ka and dissociates least, just as HPO₄²⁻ does in the phosphate series.","how":{"ask":"Rank the four by Ka: the strong acid first, then each successive arsenic-acid step.","know":"• A strong acid (HCl) ionizes completely.\n• Triprotic acid: H₃A → H₂A⁻ → HA²⁻ → A³⁻, with Ka₁ ≫ Ka₂ ≫ Ka₃.\n• The most negative species that still has an H⁺ is the weakest acid.","steps":[{"text":"HCl is a strong acid: it dissociates completely.","strike":["HCl"]},{"text":"Write the three arsenic-acid steps.","hl":"is triprotic","calc":["H₃AsO₄ ⇌ H⁺ + H₂AsO₄⁻   pKa₁ ≈ 2.2","H₂AsO₄⁻ ⇌ H⁺ + HAsO₄²⁻   pKa₂ ≈ 7.0","HAsO₄²⁻ ⇌ H⁺ + AsO₄³⁻   pKa₃ ≈ 11.5"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":7.5,"text":"buffer 1","at":"above"},{"series":"pH","x":22.5,"text":"pH 7.0 = pKa₂","at":"left"},{"series":"pH","x":37.5,"text":"buffer 3","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"Each step is about 10⁴–10⁵ times weaker than the one before, because H⁺ leaves an ever more negative ion.","calc":["Ka₁ ≈ 6 × 10⁻³ > Ka₂ ≈ 1 × 10⁻⁷ > Ka₃ ≈ 3 × 10⁻¹²"],"strike":["H₃AsO₄","H₂AsO₄⁻"]},{"text":"Answer: HAsO₄²⁻."}],"trap":"H₃AsO₄ is the weak acid named in the stem, but it is the strongest of the three arsenic species; its ions are weaker still."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"triprotic","note":"three H⁺ to lose"},{"t":"smallest extent","note":"weakest acid, smallest Ka"}]},{"text":"**Strong acids are out:** HCl ionizes completely, so it dissociates most."},{"text":"**Line up the arsenic species by charge:** neutral H₃AsO₄, then 1− H₂AsO₄⁻, then 2− HAsO₄²⁻. The more negative the ion, the more tightly it holds H⁺.","calc":["0 → 1− → 2−","Ka₁ ≫ Ka₂ ≫ Ka₃"]},{"text":"**Also:** HCl and H₂SO₄ (first H⁺) are strong acids; HF, H₃PO₄ and H₂CO₃ are weak."},{"text":"**Also:** each successive Ka of a polyprotic acid is much smaller, because H⁺ is harder to remove from a more negative ion."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a polyprotic series, the most negative ion that still carries an H⁺ (HPO₄²⁻, HAsO₄²⁻, HCO₃⁻) is the weakest acid."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-45a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:45#1","pe1-cp:45#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:45"],"q":"An object sits 15 cm from a converging lens (f = 12 cm). How many times taller is the image, and which way up is it?","choices":["4.0, inverted","4.0, upright","0.25, upright","0.80, inverted"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"A real image from a converging lens has m = −i/o below zero, so it is inverted, not upright."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is o/i with the sign dropped: the ratio is flipped and the orientation is wrong."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That is f/o; it skips finding the image distance from 1/f = 1/o + 1/i."}],"rule":"Solve 1/f = 1/o + 1/i for i, then m = −i/o: |m| is the height ratio and a negative m means inverted.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"1/i = 1/12 − 1/15 = 1/60, so i = 60 cm. m = −60/15 = −4: four times taller, inverted."},"exp":"1/i = 1/f − 1/o = 1/12 − 1/15 = 1/60, so **i = 60 cm**, a real image. **m = −i/o = −60/15 = −4**: the image is **4.0 times** as tall as the object, and the minus sign marks it as **inverted**. An object between f and 2f always gives an enlarged, inverted real image.","how":{"ask":"Find i from the thin-lens equation, then m = −i/o.","know":"• 1/f = 1/o + 1/i.\n• m = −i/o = image height ÷ object height.\n• m < 0 → inverted (real); m > 0 → upright (virtual).","steps":[{"text":"Solve for the image distance.","hl":"sits 15 cm from","calc":["1/i = 1/12 − 1/15 = 1/60","i = 60 cm"]},{"text":"Magnification.","calc":["m = −60/15 = −4.0"]},{"text":"Upright ignores the sign; 0.25 is o/i; 0.80 is f/o.","strike":["4.0, upright","0.25, upright","0.80, inverted"]},{"text":"Answer: 4.0, inverted."}],"trap":"Dropping the minus sign: a real image from a single converging lens is always inverted."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"f = 12 cm","note":"focal length"},{"t":"sits 15 cm from","note":"o, between f and 2f"}]},{"text":"**Image distance first**, then the ratio.","calc":["i = 60 cm","m = −i/o = −4.0"]},{"text":"**Also:** solve 1/f = 1/o + 1/i for i, then m = −i/o: |m| scales the height and a negative m means inverted."},{"text":"**Also:** for an object beyond 2f, find i from 1/f = 1/o + 1/i and use m = −i/o: the real image is inverted and smaller, shrinking as the object moves away."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** find i from 1/f = 1/o + 1/i, then m = −i/o: |m| is the height ratio and a negative m means an inverted image."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-46a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:46#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:46"],"q":"Phosphorus-32 decays to sulfur-32, raising Z by one at constant A, and its radiation bends toward a positive plate. Which particle is emitted?","choices":["An electron","A photon","A positron","An alpha particle"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"A positron would lower the atomic number by one and bend toward the negative plate."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A photon carries no charge, so the plates could not bend it, and it would leave Z unchanged."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An alpha particle would cut the mass number by four and the atomic number by two."}],"rule":"Read A and Z: same A with Z + 1 is β⁻; same A with Z − 1 is β⁺; A − 4 is α; no change at all is γ. Check the charge from the deflection.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Z up by one with A fixed means a neutron became a proton and threw out a negative charge; bending toward + confirms it."},"exp":"Same A and **Z up by one** means a neutron turned into a proton, and the nucleus emitted an **electron (β⁻)** plus an antineutrino. Its negative charge explains the bend **toward the positive plate**. A photon (γ) has no charge and changes neither A nor Z; a positron lowers Z; an alpha particle lowers A by 4.","how":{"ask":"Use the change in A and Z, then the charge shown by the deflection.","know":"• α: A − 4, Z − 2 (charge +2).\n• β⁻: A same, Z + 1 (an electron, −1).\n• β⁺: A same, Z − 1 (a positron, +1).\n• γ: no change in A or Z (a photon, no charge).","steps":[{"text":"Same mass number, atomic number one higher.","hl":"raising Z by one at constant A","calc":["n → p + e⁻ + antineutrino"]},{"text":"A positron lowers Z; an alpha lowers A; a photon changes nothing and is not deflected.","strike":["A positron","An alpha particle","A photon"]},{"text":"Bending toward + means a negative charge. Answer: an electron."}],"trap":"Picking γ whenever A is unchanged: γ leaves Z unchanged too, and it is not deflected."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"decays to sulfur-32","note":"A same, Z + 1"},{"t":"raising Z by one","note":"n → p"},{"t":"bends toward a positive plate","note":"negative charge"}]},{"text":"**Match the change to the decay.**","calc":["A same, Z + 1 → β⁻","A same, Z − 1 → β⁺","A − 4, Z − 2 → α","no change → γ"]},{"text":"**Also:** gamma emission is the one decay that changes neither A nor Z: an excited nucleus drops to a lower state and releases a photon, which has no mass and no charge (X* → X + γ)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** identify an emission from the change in A and Z and from its charge: β⁻ raises Z with A fixed, β⁺ lowers Z, α lowers A by 4, and γ changes neither."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-47c","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:47#3","pe1-cp:47#1","pe1-cp:47#2","pe4-cp:1#1","pe4-cp:1#2","pe4-cp:1#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:47"],"q":"Magnesium carbonate does not decompose at room temperature but does when strongly heated: MgCO₃(s) → MgO(s) + CO₂(g), ΔH° ≈ +100 kJ/mol. At room temperature, what are the signs of ΔG° and ΔS°?","choices":["ΔG°: positive; ΔS°: positive","ΔG°: negative; ΔS°: positive","ΔG°: positive; ΔS°: negative","ΔG°: negative; ΔS°: negative"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"No change at room temperature means not spontaneous there, so ΔG° is not negative."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A gas forms from a solid, so the products are more disordered and ΔS° cannot be negative."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Both signs are off: nothing happens at room temperature, and a gas forms from a solid."}],"rule":"No reaction under the stated conditions means ΔG > 0 there; a gas formed from solids or solution means ΔS > 0. Then check ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Observation first: nothing happens at room T, so ΔG° > 0. Gas made from a solid, so ΔS° > 0. Heating works because −TΔS grows until it beats ΔH."},"exp":"Magnesium carbonate is stable at room temperature, so the decomposition is **non-spontaneous there: ΔG° > 0**. It makes a **gas from a solid**, so **ΔS° > 0**. With ΔH° > 0 and ΔS° > 0, ΔG = ΔH − TΔS is positive at low T and turns negative only at high T, which is why strong heating works.","how":{"ask":"Read spontaneity from the observation and entropy from the phases, then check with ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.","know":"• Spontaneous ⇔ ΔG < 0; no reaction ⇒ ΔG > 0.\n• A gas formed from solids or solution ⇒ ΔS > 0.\n• ΔG = ΔH − TΔS: ΔH > 0 with ΔS > 0 → spontaneous only at high T.","steps":[{"text":"Nothing happens at room temperature: ΔG° > 0.","hl":"does not decompose at room temperature","strike":["ΔG°: negative; ΔS°: positive","ΔG°: negative; ΔS°: negative"]},{"text":"A gas forms from a solid: ΔS° > 0.","hl":"CO₂(g)","strike":["ΔG°: positive; ΔS°: negative"]},{"text":"Check: +ΔH and +ΔS give ΔG > 0 at low T and ΔG < 0 at high T, matching the heating. Answer: both positive."}],"trap":"Assuming any gas-forming reaction is spontaneous: the observation at room temperature says otherwise."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"does not decompose at room temperature","note":"ΔG > 0 there"},{"t":"when strongly heated","note":"spontaneous at high T"},{"t":"CO₂(g)","note":"gas formed: ΔS > 0"}]},{"text":"**Fit the signs to ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.**","calc":["ΔH > 0, ΔS > 0","low T: ΔG > 0","high T: ΔG < 0"]},{"text":"**Also:** spontaneous means ΔG < 0, however slow the reaction."},{"text":"**Also:** a reaction that makes gas from a solid or solution has ΔS > 0."},{"text":"**Also:** for an endothermic reaction (ΔH > 0) with ΔS > 0, ΔG = ΔH − TΔS stays positive, so the reaction is non-spontaneous, as long as ΔH is greater than TΔS. It becomes spontaneous once TΔS > ΔH, i.e. above T = ΔH/ΔS = 90 ÷ 0.20 = 450 K.","calc":["ΔH = +90 kJ/mol, ΔS = +0.20 kJ/(mol·K), T = 300 K","TΔS = 300 × 0.20 = 60 kJ/mol < ΔH","ΔG = 90 − 60 = +30 kJ/mol → non-spontaneous","T > 90 ÷ 0.20 = 450 K → TΔS > ΔH → ΔG < 0"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** read ΔG from what happens (no reaction → ΔG > 0) and ΔS from the phases (gas formed → ΔS > 0), then check against ΔG = ΔH − TΔS."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-48a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:48#1","pe1-cp:48#2","pe3-cp:29#1","pe3-cp:29#2","pe3-cp:29#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:48"],"passage":"In the stratosphere, chlorine atoms released from old refrigerants destroy ozone. Chlorine atoms destroy ozone in two steps: Cl + O₃ → ClO + O₂, then ClO + O → Cl + O₂. The oxygen atoms come from sunlight splitting O₂ and O₃ molecules.","q":"Based on the passage, what is the overall reaction?","choices":["O₃ + O → 2O₂","Cl + O₃ + O → Cl + 2O₂","O₃ + ClO → Cl + 2O₂","2O₃ → 3O₂"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Cl appears on both sides and was never cancelled; a species on both sides drops out of a net equation."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Keeps ClO, an intermediate (formed, then consumed), and drops the O atom; the steps weren't fully added."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Balanced, but it uses no free O atom, so it isn't the sum of these two steps."}],"rule":"Add the steps, then cancel anything on both sides (catalysts and intermediates); the net equation shows neither.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Add the steps and strike what shows on both sides: Cl (catalyst) and ClO (intermediate) cancel, leaving O3 + O → 2O2."},"exp":"Add the two steps and cross out anything on both sides: Cl + O₃ + ClO + O → ClO + O₂ + Cl + O₂ becomes **O₃ + O → 2O₂**. Cl is used and then regenerated (a catalyst), and ClO is made and then used (an intermediate), so neither appears overall. Leaving Cl on both sides is an unfinished sum; O₃ + ClO → Cl + 2O₂ drops the O-atom reactant and leaves ClO, an intermediate, uncancelled; 2O₃ → 3O₂ is a different net reaction.","how":{"ask":"Add the steps, then cancel each species that appears on both sides.","know":"• **Overall reaction** = sum of the elementary steps (multiply a step if needed so shared species cancel).\n• Cancel species found on both sides: **catalysts** (used, then regenerated; a regenerated cofactor such as enzyme-bound FAD → FADH₂ → FAD cancels like a catalyst) and **intermediates** (made, then used).\n• What's left holds only the true reactants and products.","steps":[{"text":"Write both steps and add them.","hl":"Cl + O₃ → ClO + O₂, then ClO + O → Cl + O₂","calc":["Cl + O₃ → ClO + O₂","ClO + O → Cl + O₂","sum: Cl + O₃ + ClO + O → ClO + O₂ + Cl + O₂"]},{"text":"Cancel Cl and ClO, each found on both sides, and combine the O₂.","calc":["cancel Cl (left and right)","cancel ClO (left and right)","**O₃ + O → 2O₂**","check: O atoms 3 + 1 = 4 = 2 × 2; charge 0 = 0 ✓"],"fig":"cpt-cl-cycle"},{"text":"Leaving Cl on both sides is a sum that was never simplified.","strike":["Cl + O₃ + O → Cl + 2O₂"]},{"text":"O₃ + ClO → Cl + 2O₂ drops the O-atom reactant and leaves ClO, an intermediate, uncancelled.","strike":["O₃ + ClO → Cl + 2O₂"]},{"text":"2O₃ → 3O₂ is balanced, but it uses no free O atom, so it is not the sum of these steps.","strike":["2O₃ → 3O₂"]},{"text":"Answer: O₃ + O → 2O₂."}],"trap":"Cl + O₃ + O → Cl + 2O₂ is balanced and looks complete, but a species on both sides must be cancelled; an overall equation never shows a catalyst."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"two steps","note":"a mechanism"},{"t":"Cl + O₃ → ClO + O₂","note":"step one"},{"t":"ClO + O → Cl + O₂","note":"step two"},{"t":"overall reaction","note":"sum and cancel"}]},{"text":"**A mechanism** is the list of elementary steps a reaction actually goes through. Adding them gives the overall equation."},{"text":"**Cancel like terms**, just as in algebra: a species on both sides drops out.","calc":["A + B → C","C + D → A + E","sum: B + D → E"]},{"text":"**Also:** after summing a mechanism, confirm that atoms and total charge (coefficient × charge) balance on both sides."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** add the steps, then cancel every species on both sides (catalysts and intermediates) to get the overall reaction."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-48c","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:48#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:48"],"passage":"A little FeSO₄ (Fe²⁺) speeds up the oxidation of iodide by persulfate. The proposed mechanism has two steps: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺, then 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂. During a run, [Fe³⁺] stays very small, while [I₂] rises steadily until the persulfate is used up.","q":"What roles do Fe³⁺ and I₂ play in this reaction?","choices":["Intermediate; product","Catalyst; product","Intermediate; catalyst","Reactant; intermediate"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Fe³⁺ is made first and used up later; a catalyst is the reverse (used first, then regenerated)."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"I₂ is formed and never consumed, so it is a final product, not a catalyst."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Fe³⁺ never appears as a starting material, and I₂ is never used up, so neither label fits."}],"rule":"Catalyst: consumed in an early step and regenerated later. Intermediate: made in an early step and consumed later. Product: made and kept.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Check where each species first appears: on the right first → intermediate or product (is it used later?); on the left first → reactant or catalyst (does it come back?)."},"exp":"**Fe³⁺** is **made in the first step and used up in the second**, so it is an **intermediate**, which is why its concentration stays tiny. **I₂** is formed in the second step and never consumed: a **product**. Fe²⁺, used in the first step and regenerated in the second, is the catalyst.","how":{"ask":"Track each species: where it first appears and whether it comes back.","know":"• Catalyst: used early, regenerated later; absent from the overall equation.\n• Intermediate: made early, used later; absent from the overall equation.\n• Product: made and kept; on the right of the overall equation.","steps":[{"text":"Fe³⁺ is made in the first step and consumed in the second.","hl":"2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂","fig":"cpt-fe-cycle","strike":["Catalyst; product"]},{"text":"I₂ is never consumed, so it is a product, not a catalyst or an intermediate.","strike":["Intermediate; catalyst","Reactant; intermediate"]},{"text":"Answer: intermediate; product."}],"trap":"Swapping catalyst and intermediate: a catalyst is consumed first and regenerated; an intermediate is formed first and consumed."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"A little FeSO₄ (Fe²⁺)","note":"the catalyst"},{"t":"[Fe³⁺] stays very small","note":"made, then used"},{"t":"[I₂] rises steadily","note":"made and kept"}]},{"text":"**Add the steps and cancel.**","calc":["S₂O₈²⁻ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂","Fe²⁺ cancels (catalyst); Fe³⁺ cancels (intermediate)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a catalyst is used and then regenerated, an intermediate is made and then used, and a product is made and kept; only products and reactants survive in the overall equation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-49b","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:49#2","pe1-cp:49#1","pe1-cp:49#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:49"],"passage":"Sucrose in water hydrolyzes into glucose and fructose only very slowly at room temperature. Adding a little hydrochloric acid makes the reaction fast, and the H⁺ ions mix evenly through the solution. When the reaction is over, the pH of the solution is the same as just after the acid was added.","q":"In this reaction, H⁺ is best described as which kind of catalyst?","choices":["Homogeneous; regenerated","Heterogeneous; regenerated","Homogeneous; used up","Heterogeneous; used up"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Heterogeneous means a different phase; H⁺ is dissolved in the same solution as the sucrose."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Wrong phase and wrong fate: the H⁺ is dissolved, and all of it is still there at the end."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The pH ends where it started, so all the H⁺ added is still present: it was not consumed."}],"rule":"Same phase as the reactants = homogeneous; a different phase (such as a solid acting on gases) = heterogeneous. A catalyst is regenerated, never used up.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Dissolved catalyst in a dissolved reaction → same phase → homogeneous. Same pH at the end → not consumed."},"exp":"H⁺ is **dissolved in the same aqueous phase** as the sucrose, so it is a **homogeneous** catalyst. It speeds the reaction and is **regenerated**: the pH, and so the amount of H⁺, is unchanged at the end. A solid catalyst acting on gases, like the platinum in a catalytic converter, would be heterogeneous.","how":{"ask":"Compare the catalyst's phase with the reactants', then check whether it is consumed.","know":"• Homogeneous: same phase as the reactants; heterogeneous: a different phase.\n• A catalyst is regenerated, not consumed.\n• A catalytic converter is a heterogeneous solid catalyst: it oxidizes CO and hydrocarbons to CO₂ and H₂O and reduces NOₓ to N₂.","steps":[{"text":"H⁺ mixes evenly through the solution: the same phase as the sucrose.","hl":"mix evenly through the solution","strike":["Heterogeneous; regenerated","Heterogeneous; used up"]},{"text":"The pH is unchanged at the end, so the H⁺ is not consumed.","hl":"the pH of the solution is the same","strike":["Homogeneous; used up"]},{"text":"Answer: homogeneous; regenerated."}],"trap":"Calling every metal catalyst heterogeneous: dissolved ions share the reactants' phase."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"mix evenly through the solution","note":"same phase"},{"t":"the pH of the solution is the same","note":"not consumed"}]},{"text":"**Two questions classify any catalyst.**","calc":["same phase as reactants? → homogeneous","different phase? → heterogeneous","consumed? → never"]},{"text":"**Also:** a solid catalyst acting on gases (a catalytic converter) is a heterogeneous catalyst."},{"text":"**Also:** a catalytic converter reduces nitrogen oxides (NOₓ) to N₂ while it oxidizes CO and hydrocarbons."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a catalyst in the reactants' phase is homogeneous and one in a different phase is heterogeneous; either way it is regenerated, not used up."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-51a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:51#1","pe1-cp:51#2","pe1-cp:51#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:51"],"passage":"Manganese dioxide catalyzes the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide. A student compares one cube of MnO₂, 1.5 cm on each side, with the same mass of MnO₂ cut into identical cubes 0.050 cm on each side. The rate of O₂ formation rises in step with the catalyst's exposed surface area.","q":"How many times faster is O₂ released after cutting?","choices":["30","900","27000","0.033"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Smaller pieces expose more surface, so the rate must rise, not fall."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"That squares the edge ratio; at fixed mass the total area grows by the edge ratio itself."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"That is the number of small cubes; each one has far less surface than the big cube."}],"rule":"Same mass cut into small cubes: total area grows by the ratio of the edge lengths (big ÷ small), not by its square or cube.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Edge ratio = 1.5 ÷ 0.050 = 30. Total surface, and so the rate, goes up by that same factor."},"exp":"Number of small cubes = (1.5/0.050)³ = 27,000, each with area 6(0.050)² = 0.015 cm², for 405 cm² in total. The big cube has 6(1.5)² = 13.5 cm². Ratio = 405 ÷ 13.5 = **30**, the **edge ratio**. Rate ∝ area, so O₂ forms **30 times faster**; the mass of catalyst is unchanged.","how":{"ask":"Compare the total surface area before and after, at the same mass.","know":"• Rate ∝ exposed surface area.\n• Cutting a cube of edge L into cubes of edge l: N = (L/l)³, and the total area grows by L/l.\n• The mass of catalyst does not change.","steps":[{"text":"Edge ratio.","hl":"0.050 cm on each side","calc":["1.5 ÷ 0.050 = 30"]},{"text":"Total area.","calc":["big: 6 × 1.5² = 13.5 cm²","small: 27,000 × 6 × 0.050² = 405 cm²","405 ÷ 13.5 = 30"]},{"text":"900 squares the ratio; 27000 counts cubes; 0.033 has the rate falling.","strike":["900","27000","0.033"]},{"text":"Answer: 30."}],"trap":"Using the number of pieces (27,000) as the area factor."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"1.5 cm on each side","note":"L"},{"t":"0.050 cm on each side","note":"l"},{"t":"rises in step with the catalyst's exposed surface area","note":"rate ∝ area"}]},{"text":"**Area factor = edge ratio** at fixed mass.","calc":["N × 6l² ÷ 6L² = (L/l)³ × (l/L)² = L/l"]},{"text":"**Also:** grinding or cutting a catalyst raises its surface area but leaves its mass unchanged."},{"text":"**Also:** cutting a cube into smaller cubes multiplies the total surface area by the ratio of the edge lengths."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** cutting a solid catalyst into cubes at fixed mass multiplies its surface, and the rate, by the edge ratio L/l."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-52a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:52#1","pe1-cp:52#2","pe1-cp:52#3"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:52"],"passage":"A hobbyist builds a battery-powered hand warmer. A 9.0-V battery powers a 30-Ω heater coil through short copper leads. Spare coils of other resistances are on hand, and the battery's voltage stays constant.","q":"Which replacement coil would draw the largest current from the battery?","choices":["A 12-Ω coil","A 45-Ω coil","A 60-Ω coil","A 90-Ω coil"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"45 Ω is more than the present 30 Ω, so by I = V/R it lowers the current."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"60 Ω is twice the present 30 Ω, so by I = V/R it halves the current."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"I = V/R: at fixed V a bigger R gives a smaller current, and 90 Ω is the biggest R offered."}],"rule":"At fixed voltage I = V/R: more resistance means less current.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"I ∝ 1/R at fixed voltage, so the smallest resistance draws the most: 12 Ω. No division needed."},"exp":"By **Ohm's law, I = V/R**, a fixed battery voltage drives more current through a smaller resistance. The 12-Ω coil draws 9.0 V ÷ 12 Ω = **0.75 A**, more than the 0.30 A now.","how":{"ask":"With the battery voltage fixed, which resistance gives the biggest I = V/R?","know":"• **Ohm's law: I = V/R**.\n• Fixed V → current is **inversely proportional** to resistance: lower R, more current.","steps":[{"text":"The battery sets V; only R changes.","hl":"A 9.0-V battery","calc":["I = V ÷ R"]},{"text":"Current now.","hl":"30-Ω heater coil","calc":["I = 9.0 V ÷ 30 Ω = **0.30 A**"]},{"text":"Compare the replacements.","calc":["12 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 12 = **0.75 A**","45 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 45 = 0.20 A","60 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 60 = 0.15 A","90 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 90 = 0.10 A"]},{"text":"The larger coils all lower the current below 0.30 A.","strike":["A 45-Ω coil","A 60-Ω coil","A 90-Ω coil"]},{"text":"Answer: the 12-Ω coil."}],"trap":"Thinking a bigger resistor \"uses more\" current. At fixed voltage, more resistance means less current."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"9.0-V battery","note":"V fixed"},{"t":"30-Ω heater coil","note":"R now"},{"t":"largest current","note":"goal"}]},{"text":"**Ohm's law:** the battery's push divided by the resistance gives the current.","calc":["I = V ÷ R"]},{"text":"**Smaller R, bigger I.** Halve R and the current doubles.","calc":["R ↓ → I ↑"]},{"text":"**Also:** a longer, thinner or hotter metal wire has more resistance; shorter, thicker or cooler has less."},{"text":"**Also:** lowering the ion concentrations a cell runs on lowers its voltage, and at fixed R that lowers the current."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to raise the current from a fixed voltage, lower the resistance (I = V/R)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-53a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:53#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:53"],"passage":"Standard reduction potentials: Ag⁺/Ag, +0.80 V; Mg²⁺/Mg, −2.37 V; the hydrogen electrode is defined as zero. A strip of silver is placed in hydrochloric acid at standard concentration. A strip of magnesium in the same acid fizzes vigorously.","q":"Will H₂ gas form at the silver strip?","choices":["No; Ag holds its electrons","Yes; Ag gives electrons to H⁺","Yes; chloride attacks silver","No; Ag⁺ forms but no gas"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Silver's reduction potential is above hydrogen's, so electrons would flow the other way."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Chloride is not an oxidizing agent; the only species here that could be reduced is H⁺."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"If silver were oxidized, its electrons would have to reduce H⁺ to H₂; ions cannot form without a gas."}],"rule":"A metal releases H₂ from acid only if it is easier to oxidize than H₂ (E° below zero); above zero, nothing happens.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Compare with zero: Ag⁺/Ag is +0.80 V, above hydrogen, so silver stays put; magnesium, far below zero, fizzes."},"exp":"For H₂ to form, the metal must give its electrons to H⁺, which happens only if the metal is **easier to oxidize than H₂**, i.e. its **E° is below 0 V**. Silver's E° is **+0.80 V**, so silver will not reduce H⁺ and **no H₂** forms. Magnesium (−2.37 V) is far below zero, which is why it fizzes.","how":{"ask":"Compare the metal's reduction potential with hydrogen's (0 V).","know":"• E° < 0: the metal is oxidized by H⁺, releasing H₂.\n• E° > 0: the metal does not react with a non-oxidizing acid.\n• Cl⁻ is not an oxidizing agent.","steps":[{"text":"Silver's E° is above zero.","hl":"Ag⁺/Ag, +0.80 V"},{"text":"So silver cannot hand electrons to H⁺, chloride oxidizes nothing, and any silver oxidation would have to make H₂.","strike":["Yes; Ag gives electrons to H⁺","Yes; chloride attacks silver","No; Ag⁺ forms but no gas"]},{"text":"Answer: no; Ag holds its electrons."}],"trap":"Assuming every metal dissolves in acid: only metals below hydrogen (E° < 0) release H₂."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"+0.80 V","note":"above hydrogen"},{"t":"−2.37 V","note":"below hydrogen"},{"t":"defined as zero","note":"the reference"}]},{"text":"**Compare each metal with hydrogen.**","calc":["E° < 0 → oxidized by H⁺ → H₂ forms","E° > 0 → no reaction"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a metal releases H₂ from acid only if its reduction potential is below hydrogen's (E° < 0): it is oxidized and H⁺ is reduced."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-54a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:54#1","pe1-cp:54#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:54"],"passage":"A strip of zinc is placed in a colorless solution of silver nitrate, and gray-black crystals of silver grow on the strip. Standard reduction potentials: Zn²⁺/Zn, −0.76 V; Ag⁺/Ag, +0.80 V. Nitrate ions take no part in the reaction.","q":"What is the balanced net ionic equation for this reaction?","choices":["Zn + 2Ag⁺ → Zn²⁺ + 2Ag","Zn + Ag⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Ag","Zn²⁺ + 2Ag → Zn + 2Ag⁺","2Zn + Ag⁺ → 2Zn²⁺ + Ag"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"That runs the reaction in reverse; zinc, with the lower E°, is the metal oxidized."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Atoms balance but charge does not: +1 on the left and +2 on the right."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Charge is off: +1 on the left and +4 on the right; electrons lost must equal electrons gained."}],"rule":"The metal with the lower E° is oxidized; multiply the half-reactions so electrons lost equal electrons gained, then check the charge.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Zn gives 2e⁻ and each Ag⁺ takes 1e⁻, so two Ag⁺ per Zn. Charge check: +2 on both sides."},"exp":"Zinc has the **lower E°**, so it is **oxidized**: Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻. Silver ions are reduced: Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag. Balance the electrons with **two Ag⁺ per Zn**: **Zn + 2Ag⁺ → Zn²⁺ + 2Ag**, with a charge of +2 on each side.","how":{"ask":"Pick the oxidized metal from E°, then balance electrons and charge.","know":"• Lower E° → oxidized; higher E° → reduced.\n• Electrons lost = electrons gained.\n• Check that atoms and total charge balance.","steps":[{"text":"Zinc has the lower E°, so zinc metal is oxidized and silver ions are reduced.","hl":"Zn²⁺/Zn, −0.76 V","strike":["Zn²⁺ + 2Ag → Zn + 2Ag⁺"]},{"text":"Balance electrons: Zn gives 2, each Ag⁺ takes 1.","calc":["Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻","2Ag⁺ + 2e⁻ → 2Ag"]},{"text":"The one-to-one and two-to-one versions leave the charge unbalanced.","strike":["Zn + Ag⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Ag","2Zn + Ag⁺ → 2Zn²⁺ + Ag"]},{"text":"Answer: Zn + 2Ag⁺ → Zn²⁺ + 2Ag."}],"trap":"Balancing atoms but not charge."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"gray-black crystals of silver","note":"Ag⁺ reduced"},{"t":"−0.76 V","note":"Zn: lower, oxidized"},{"t":"+0.80 V","note":"Ag: higher, reduced"}]},{"text":"**Balance electrons, then charge.**","calc":["Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻","2 × (Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag)","sum: Zn + 2Ag⁺ → Zn²⁺ + 2Ag"]},{"text":"**Also:** balance a redox equation by multiplying the half-reactions so the electrons lost equal the electrons gained, then check the charge."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** oxidize the metal with the lower E°, scale the half-reactions until electrons lost equal electrons gained, and check that charge balances."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-55a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:55#1","pe1-cp:55#2","pe1-cp:55#3","pe6-cp:41#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:55"],"passage":"A student builds a galvanic cell from an iron electrode in iron(II) sulfate solution and a copper electrode in copper(II) sulfate solution, joined by a salt bridge. As the cell runs, the copper electrode gains mass and the iron electrode loses mass. Standard reduction potentials: Cu²⁺/Cu, +0.34 V; Fe²⁺/Fe, −0.44 V.","q":"What is the cell's standard potential, and does its reaction run spontaneously?","choices":["+0.78 V, spontaneous","−0.10 V, not spontaneous","+0.10 V, spontaneous","+0.78 V, not spontaneous"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"That adds the two values as written; subtract the anode's E° from the cathode's."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"That drops the minus sign on iron's potential; subtracting a negative value adds it."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A positive E°cell means ΔG° < 0, so the reaction is spontaneous."}],"rule":"E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode, with the higher reduction potential as the cathode; positive means spontaneous.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Copper gains mass, so it is the cathode. 0.34 − (−0.44) = +0.78 V, and positive means spontaneous."},"exp":"Copper gains mass, so Cu²⁺ is reduced there: copper is the **cathode**, with the higher E°. Subtract the anode's value from the cathode's: **0.34 − (−0.44) = +0.78 V**. A **positive** E°cell means ΔG° = −nFE° < 0, so the reaction is **spontaneous**, as a working galvanic cell must be.","how":{"ask":"Identify the cathode and anode, subtract, then read the sign.","know":"• E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode.\n• The cathode has the higher reduction potential (and gains mass when metal plates out).\n• E°cell > 0 → spontaneous.","steps":[{"text":"Copper gains mass: it is the cathode.","hl":"the copper electrode gains mass"},{"text":"Subtract.","calc":["E°cell = +0.34 − (−0.44) = +0.78 V"],"strike":["−0.10 V, not spontaneous","+0.10 V, spontaneous"]},{"text":"Positive means spontaneous.","strike":["+0.78 V, not spontaneous"]},{"text":"Answer: +0.78 V, spontaneous."}],"trap":"Adding the potentials as written, or swapping cathode and anode."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"gains mass","note":"cathode"},{"t":"+0.34 V","note":"Cu, higher"},{"t":"−0.44 V","note":"Fe, lower"}]},{"text":"**Cathode minus anode, then the sign.**","calc":["0.34 − (−0.44) = +0.78 V","+ → spontaneous"]},{"text":"**Also:** in a galvanic cell, the cathode is the half-cell with the higher standard reduction potential."},{"text":"**Also:** a positive E°cell means spontaneous as written; a negative E°cell means not spontaneous (the reverse reaction is)."},{"text":"**Also:** when E°cell and one electrode are known, solve the same formula for the other: E°anode = E°cathode − E°cell. A strongly negative result marks a metal that gives up electrons easily.","calc":["this cell backward: +0.34 − (+0.78) = −0.44 V (iron ✓)","Cu cathode, E°cell = +1.10 V: E°anode = 0.34 − 1.10 = −0.76 V (zinc)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode with the higher reduction potential as the cathode; a positive E°cell means spontaneous."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-56a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:56#1"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:56"],"passage":"A defroster grid on a car window is made of thin conducting strips. One strip is a heating element with a resistance of 250 mΩ. It is connected across a 4.5-V supply, and its resistance stays nearly constant as it warms.","q":"About how much current does the strip draw?","choices":["18 A","18 mA","56 A","0.056 A"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Treats 250 mΩ as 250 Ω, a resistance a thousand times too big, so the current comes out in milliamps."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Divides 250 by 4.5: R ÷ V with the milli left off, so it is both upside down and a thousand off."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"That's R ÷ V, upside down; with R under 1 Ω the current must exceed 4.5 A."}],"rule":"I = V ÷ R with R in ohms (mΩ ÷ 1000); a resistance under 1 Ω draws more amps than the volts. Round both to 1 digit first.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"250 mΩ is a quarter ohm; dividing by 1/4 is multiplying by 4: 4.5 × 4 = 18 A. R under 1 Ω means I bigger than the volt number, so not mA."},"exp":"Convert first: **250 mΩ = 0.250 Ω**. Then **Ohm's law, I = V/R** = 4.5 V ÷ 0.250 Ω = **18 A**. A resistance under 1 Ω gives a current larger than the voltage number.","how":{"ask":"Put the resistance in ohms, then divide the voltage by it.","know":"• **Ohm's law: I = V/R** (A = V ÷ Ω).\n• **m (milli) = 10⁻³**: 1 mΩ = 0.001 Ω. Capital **M (mega) = 10⁶**.","steps":[{"text":"Convert milliohms to ohms.","hl":"a resistance of 250 mΩ","calc":["250 mΩ = 250 × 10⁻³ Ω = **0.250 Ω**"]},{"text":"Apply Ohm's law.","hl":"4.5-V supply","calc":["I = V ÷ R","I = 4.5 V ÷ 0.250 Ω","I = **18 A**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Current from a 4.5-V supply versus resistance","x":{"label":"Resistance (Ω)","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Current (A)","min":0,"max":20,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"I = V ÷ R","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0.25,18],[0.5,9],[0.75,6],[1,4.5],[1.5,3],[2,2.25],[3,1.5]]}]}},{"text":"18 mA divides by 250 Ω, skipping the milli conversion. 56 A divides 250 by 4.5, which is R ÷ V and unconverted. 0.056 A is R ÷ V in ohms, the ratio upside down.","strike":["18 mA","56 A","0.056 A"]},{"text":"Answer: 18 A."}],"trap":"Treating 250 mΩ as 250 Ω gives 18 mA, a thousand times too small."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"250 mΩ","note":"resistance R (milliohms)"},{"t":"4.5-V supply","note":"voltage V"},{"t":"how much current","note":"I in amps"}]},{"text":"**Milli means one thousandth.** Convert before dividing.","calc":["250 mΩ = 250 ÷ 1000 Ω = 0.250 Ω"]},{"text":"**Ohm's law:** current = voltage ÷ resistance.","calc":["I = V ÷ R = 4.5 ÷ 0.250 = 18 A"]},{"text":"**Also:** at constant resistance, V = IR makes voltage directly proportional to current."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** I = V/R with R in ohms; convert mΩ by dividing by 1000 first, and expect more amps than volts when R is under 1 Ω."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-57a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:57#1","pe1-cp:57#2","pe1-cp:57#3","pe3-cp:56#1","pe3-cp:56#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:57"],"q":"Solid potassium iodide is dissolved in a saturated lead(II) iodide solution standing over undissolved PbI₂. Which best describes the result?","choices":["PbI₂ forms; Q exceeds Ksp","Solid PbI₂ forms; Ksp drops","More solid enters solution; Ksp rises","No shift; Ksp is unchanged"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Adding iodide raises Q above Ksp, which pushes PbI₂ out of solution, not into it."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Ksp is a constant at a given temperature; adding an ion changes Q, not Ksp."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Ksp stays fixed, but Q does not: the added iodide raises Q, so the system must respond."}],"rule":"Adding a common ion raises Q above Ksp, so the salt precipitates until Q = Ksp again; Ksp itself changes only with temperature.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Common ion added → Q goes up → Q > Ksp → shift left → solid forms. Ksp never moves at constant temperature."},"exp":"Dissolving KI adds **I⁻, a common ion**. The ion product **Q = [Pb²⁺][I⁻]² rises above Ksp**, so PbI₂(s) ⇌ Pb²⁺ + 2I⁻ **shifts left** and PbI₂ precipitates until Q = Ksp again. Ksp itself is unchanged at constant temperature.","how":{"ask":"Compare Q with Ksp after the common ion is added.","know":"• Ksp is fixed at a given T.\n• Q > Ksp → precipitation; Q < Ksp → more dissolves.\n• A common ion raises Q (Le Châtelier: adding a product shifts the equilibrium left).","steps":[{"text":"KI adds iodide, a common ion.","hl":"potassium iodide","calc":["Q = [Pb²⁺][I⁻]² rises"]},{"text":"Q > Ksp, so the shift is toward the solid.","strike":["More solid enters solution; Ksp rises","No shift; Ksp is unchanged"]},{"text":"Ksp does not change; only Q does.","strike":["Solid PbI₂ forms; Ksp drops"]},{"text":"Answer: PbI₂ forms; Q exceeds Ksp."}],"trap":"Saying Ksp drops: the constant stays put; Q is what moves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"potassium iodide","note":"common ion I⁻"},{"t":"saturated lead(II) iodide solution","note":"Q = Ksp at the start"},{"t":"undissolved PbI₂","note":"solid present"}]},{"text":"**Compare Q with Ksp.**","calc":["add I⁻ → Q > Ksp → PbI₂(s) forms"]},{"text":"**Also (Le Châtelier):** adding a product, here the common ion, shifts the equilibrium left, so the sparingly soluble salt precipitates."},{"text":"**Also:** adding a common ion lowers a salt's solubility, but Ksp itself doesn't change unless temperature does."},{"text":"**Also:** a pure solid is left out of K and Q (its activity is 1), so adding or removing one (more undissolved AgCl, an ion-exchange resin, a precipitate filtered off) doesn't shift the equilibrium. Only changes in dissolved concentrations or gas pressures do.","calc":["AgCl(s) ⇌ Ag⁺ + Cl⁻: Q = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻], no AgCl term","more AgCl(s) → Q unchanged → no shift"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** adding a common ion raises Q above Ksp, so the salt precipitates; Ksp itself changes only with temperature."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-58a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:58#1","pe3-cp:21#3","pe5-cp:1#1","pe5-cp:24#1","pe6-cp:35#2","pe3-cp:21#1","pe3-cp:21#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:58"],"q":"A recipe needs 2.22 g of calcium chloride (molar mass 111 g/mol), measured out from a 0.400 M CaCl₂ stock. What volume of stock is required?","choices":["50.0 mL","8.0 mL","20.0 mL","5.55 L"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Wrong operation: 8.0 comes from n × M (0.0200 × 0.400); volume is n ÷ M."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Stops at the moles (0.0200) and reads them as litres; volume still needs ÷ molarity."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skips the grams-to-moles step: 5.55 L of 0.400 M would hold about 246 g, not 2.22 g."}],"rule":"Grams → moles (÷ molar mass), then volume = moles ÷ molarity, then L → mL. Round every number to 1 digit first.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"2.22 ÷ 111 = 0.02 mol (111 × 2 = 222). Then 0.02 ÷ 0.4 = 0.05 L = 50 mL."},"exp":"First grams to moles: 2.22 g ÷ 111 g/mol = **0.0200 mol**. Then **V = n ÷ M** = 0.0200 mol ÷ 0.400 mol/L = 0.0500 L = **50.0 mL**.","how":{"ask":"Convert grams to moles, then divide moles by molarity to get litres, then convert to mL.","know":"• **n = mass ÷ molar mass**.\n• **M = n ÷ V**, so **V = n ÷ M** (V in litres).\n• 1 L = 1000 mL.","steps":[{"text":"Note the mass, molar mass and molarity.","hl":"2.22 g of calcium chloride","calc":["m = 2.22 g","molar mass = 111 g/mol","M = 0.400 mol/L"]},{"text":"Grams to moles.","calc":["n = 2.22 g ÷ 111 g/mol","n = 0.0200 mol"]},{"text":"Volume from V = n ÷ M, then mL.","calc":["V = 0.0200 mol ÷ 0.400 mol/L","V = 0.0500 L","V = 0.0500 × 1000 = **50.0 mL**"]},{"text":"Multiplying n × M instead of dividing gives 8.0 mL, and stopping at the moles (0.0200 read as litres) gives 20.0 mL.","calc":["0.0200 × 0.400 = 0.0080 L = 8.0 mL ✗","0.0200 → 20.0 mL skips ÷ M ✗"],"strike":["8.0 mL","20.0 mL"]},{"text":"Skipping the grams-to-moles step divides grams by molarity.","calc":["2.22 ÷ 0.400 = 5.55 ✗"],"strike":["5.55 L"]},{"text":"Answer: 50.0 mL."}],"trap":"Dividing grams by molarity: molarity is moles per litre, so the mass must become moles first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"2.22 g","note":"mass"},{"t":"111 g/mol","note":"molar mass"},{"t":"0.400 M","note":"mol per L"}]},{"text":"**Grams → moles.**","calc":["n = mass ÷ molar mass"]},{"text":"**Moles → litres → mL.** Rearrange M = n/V.","calc":["V = n ÷ M","mL = L × 1000"]},{"text":"**Also:** going the other way, mass = M × V(L) × molar mass. Turn both prefixes into base units before multiplying: μM × 10⁻⁶ = M and mL × 10⁻³ = L.","calc":["40 μM = 4.0 × 10⁻⁵ M; 750 mL = 0.750 L","n = 4.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol/L × 0.750 L = 3.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol","mass = 3.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol × 342 g/mol ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻² g ≈ 10 mg"]},{"text":"**Also:** when only the moles are wanted, n = M × V(L). A millimolar value must become molar first (mM ÷ 1000), and mL ÷ 1000 gives litres.","calc":["600 mM = 0.600 M; 400 mL = 0.400 L","n = 0.600 mol/L × 0.400 L = 0.240 mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** to make a solution from the solid, chain the units forward: mass = M × V × molar mass, with V in litres. Change mL to L first, or the mass comes out 1000 times too big.","calc":["0.050 M CaCl₂ in 300 mL = 0.300 L","mass = 0.050 mol/L × 0.300 L × 111 g/mol ≈ 1.7 g"]},{"text":"**Also:** for a concentration given in g/L, mass = concentration (g/L) × volume (L), and no molar mass enters. A microlitre is 10⁻⁶ L (a millilitre is 10⁻³ L); mixing them up makes the mass 1000 times too big.","calc":["40 g/L protein; drop = 8.0 μL = 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ L","mass = 40 g/L × 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ L = 3.2 × 10⁻⁴ g = 0.32 mg","μL read as mL: 40 × 8.0 × 10⁻³ = 0.32 g ✗ (1000× too big)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** V = n ÷ M gives litres; convert grams to moles first and litres to mL at the end; mass = M × V(L) × molar mass, or (g/L) × V(L) with μL = 10⁻⁶ L."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-59c","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:59#3","pe1-cp:59#1","pe1-cp:59#2","pe3-cp:7#1","pe3-cp:7#2"],"twinOf":["pe1-cp:59"],"q":"An engineer reports a lab centrifuge motor's output using different unit expressions. Which one is not equivalent to the watt?","choices":["V·A","Pa·m³·s⁻¹","kg·m·s⁻²","A²·Ω"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"Volts × amps = (J/C)(C/s) = J/s, which is a watt."},{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Pressure × volume flow = (N/m²)(m³/s) = N·m/s = J/s, a watt."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Current squared × resistance = I²R = A × V = J/s, a watt."}],"rule":"Reduce each expression to base units and compare with kg·m²·s⁻³ (J/s). A newton has one metre; a joule has m² and s⁻²; power needs m² and s⁻³.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Power always carries m² (J = N·m). An expression whose base units show only one metre is a force, not a power."},"exp":"A watt is a joule per second, which in base units is **kg·m²·s⁻³**. V·A = (J/C)(C/s) = J/s; Pa·m³·s⁻¹ = (N/m²)(m³/s) = N·m/s = J/s; A²·Ω = I²R = J/s. **kg·m·s⁻²** is a **newton**, a unit of force: it lacks the extra metre that makes a joule and the extra s⁻¹ that makes power.","how":{"ask":"Convert each expression to base units and compare with kg·m²·s⁻³.","know":"• W = J/s = kg·m²·s⁻³.\n• J = N·m = kg·m²·s⁻²; N = kg·m·s⁻².\n• V = J/C, A = C/s, Ω = V/A, Pa = N/m².","steps":[{"text":"Electrical products: V·A and A²·Ω both reduce to J/s.","calc":["V·A = (J/C)(C/s) = J/s","A²·Ω = A·(A·Ω) = A·V = J/s"],"strike":["V·A","A²·Ω"]},{"text":"Pressure × volume per time is energy per time.","calc":["Pa·m³·s⁻¹ = N·m·s⁻¹ = J/s"],"strike":["Pa·m³·s⁻¹"]},{"text":"kg·m·s⁻² is a newton, a force. Answer: kg·m·s⁻²."}],"trap":"Stopping at \"it has kg, m and s\": count the powers; one metre with s⁻² makes a force."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the ask**.","marks":[{"t":"not equivalent to the watt","note":"find the odd one out"},{"t":"different unit expressions","note":"reduce each to base units"}]},{"text":"**Build the ladder.**","calc":["N = kg·m·s⁻²","J = N·m = kg·m²·s⁻²","W = J/s = kg·m²·s⁻³"]},{"text":"**Also:** W = J/s = kg·m²·s⁻³; one more s⁻¹ than a joule means power."},{"text":"**Also:** kg·m²·s⁻² is a joule (energy), not power; power needs kg·m²·s⁻³."},{"text":"**Also:** with no change in speed there is no acceleration (ΣF = 0), so the driving force equals the resisting force; then P = F·v.","calc":["constant speed: driving force = resisting force = 120 N","P = F·v = 120 N × 2.5 m/s = 300 W"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** reduce every unit expression to base units: power is kg·m²·s⁻³ (J/s); kg·m²·s⁻² is energy and kg·m·s⁻² is force."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-01a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:1#1","pe3-cp:1#2","pe3-cp:1#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:1"],"passage":"A short route to cyclopentenones closes a ring by an intramolecular aldol condensation. With base and heat, 1-phenylpentane-1,4-dione, C₆H₅–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃, cyclizes to 3-phenylcyclopent-2-en-1-one. The chain carbons are numbered C1, the phenyl ketone carbon, through C5, the methyl carbon. Water is lost only after the ring has formed.","q":"Which two starting carbons become the ring's C=C?","choices":["C1 and C5","C2 and C3","C3 and C4","C4 and C5"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"C2 and C3 stay CH₂ groups in the ring; neither becomes part of the C=C."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"C4 is the ketone that survives as the enone's C=O, so it can't be in the C=C."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Again C4: it stays the ring C=O, so any pair that includes it is wrong."}],"rule":"In an intramolecular aldol condensation, the surviving ketone keeps its C=O; the C=C forms where the other carbonyl lost its O.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ring closures favor 5 or 6 members. Only the CH3 end (C5) can reach C1's carbonyl for a 5-ring; dehydration makes C=C right at the new bond, C1=C5."},"exp":"Only the CH₃ (C5) can reach a carbonyl and close a five-membered ring: its enolate carbon bonds to **C1**, the phenyl ketone carbon. Losing water then puts the **C=C between C5 and C1**, conjugated with the C4 ketone, which stays a C=O.","how":{"ask":"Find which α-carbon attacks which carbonyl, then place the C=C made by dehydration.","know":"• **Aldol bond**: the enolate's **α-carbon** (nucleophile) bonds to the **other carbonyl carbon** (electrophile).\n• **Dehydration**: the C=C forms between the **former α-carbon** and the **former carbonyl carbon**, conjugated with the remaining C=O.\n• Number the chain, then follow each carbon by its neighbors.","steps":[{"text":"Number the chain from the phenyl end: C1 is the phenyl ketone carbon, C4 the methyl ketone carbon, C5 the CH₃.","hl":"C₆H₅–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃","calc":["C₆H₅–C1(=O)–C2H₂–C3H₂–C4(=O)–C5H₃"]},{"text":"Test each α-carbon. C2 or C3 attacking a carbonyl would make a three-membered ring; C5 attacking C1 makes a five-membered ring.","calc":["C5 → C1: ring = C5, C4, C3, C2, C1 = **5 atoms** ✓","C3 → C1 or C2 → C4: **3 atoms** ✗"]},{"text":"The new C5–C1 bond puts an OH on C1 (β to the C4 ketone). Dehydration removes that OH and an H from C5.","calc":["C=C: **C5 = C1**, conjugated with C4=O"]},{"text":"Check by neighbors: the phenyl is bonded to C1 at the start, and in the product it sits on ring C3, an alkene carbon.","hl":"3-phenylcyclopent-2-en-1-one"},{"text":"C2 and C3 stay CH₂ groups in the ring; C4 stays the ketone, so it is never part of the C=C.","strike":["C2 and C3","C3 and C4","C4 and C5"]},{"text":"Answer: C1 and C5."}],"trap":"Putting the surviving C=O carbon (C4) into the C=C: that carbon keeps its oxygen, and the alkene forms at C1, the carbon that lost its OH."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"1-phenylpentane-1,4-dione","note":"two ketones, 1,4 apart"},{"t":"With base and heat","note":"aldol, then dehydration"},{"t":"3-phenylcyclopent-2-en-1-one","note":"five-membered enone"}]},{"text":"**Number and label.** Write C1–C5 under the chain; mark which carbons are carbonyls (C1, C4) and which are α (C2, C3, C5)."},{"text":"**Make the aldol bond.** An α-carbon (as its enolate) bonds to the other carbonyl carbon. Pick the pair that closes a five- or six-membered ring.","calc":["C5 + C1 → 5-membered ring"]},{"text":"**Dehydrate.** The OH on the former carbonyl carbon leaves with an H from the former α-carbon, so the C=C joins those two and is conjugated with the C=O.","calc":["former α-C = former C=O carbon → α,β-unsaturated ketone"]},{"text":"**Also:** track carbons by their neighbors: a carbon bonded to a phenyl, or one next to a quaternary carbon (no H), keeps that neighbor in the product, so you can find it in the ring."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in an intramolecular aldol condensation, the α-carbon bonds to the other carbonyl carbon, and the C=C forms between those same two carbons."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-02a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:2#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:2"],"passage":"Naphthalene reacts with concentrated sulfuric acid to give two isomeric sulfonic acids. Sulfonating naphthalene briefly at low temperature gives mainly the 1-isomer; heating for hours at high temperature, where the isomers interconvert, gives mainly the 2-isomer. The chart shows the product mix from each run.","q":"Which conclusion do these runs support?","choices":["The 2-isomer is lower in energy","The 1-isomer is lower in energy","The 2-isomer has the lower Ea","Both isomers are equally low in energy"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: the 1-isomer wins only under kinetic control (fast, cold), so it forms faster but is less stable."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: the 1-isomer forms faster in the cold, so it, not the 2-isomer, has the lower Ea."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Equal stability would give a roughly even equilibrium mixture, not a dominant 2-isomer."}],"rule":"Short, cold run = kinetic product (lower Ea); long, hot run where isomers interconvert = thermodynamic product (more stable).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Short and cold = kinetic (fastest-forming, lower Ea); long and hot = thermodynamic (most stable). The hot product, 2-isomer, is lower in energy."},"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Isomer mix from sulfonating naphthalene","x":{"label":"Conditions"},"y":{"label":"Percent of product","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"categories":["Brief, cold","Hours, hot"],"series":[{"name":"1-isomer","values":[94,12]},{"name":"2-isomer","values":[6,88]}]},"exp":"Only the long, hot run lets the isomers interconvert and reach **equilibrium**, where the **more stable** (lower-energy) product dominates: the 2-isomer. The brief, cold run shows **kinetic control**: the 1-isomer forms faster, so it has the lower Ea.","how":{"ask":"Decide which run reached equilibrium, then read stability from that run only.","know":"• **Thermodynamic control** (reversible, long time, higher T): the major product is the **more stable** one.\n• **Kinetic control** (irreversible, short time, low T): the major product is the one that **forms faster** (lower Ea).","steps":[{"text":"The hot, long run is reversible, so it reflects stability.","hl":"where the isomers interconvert","calc":["equilibrium → major = more stable = **2-isomer**"],"graph":{"bars":[["Hours, hot","2-isomer"]]}},{"text":"The brief, cold run is irreversible, so it reflects rate.","hl":"briefly at low temperature","calc":["kinetic → major = faster = 1-isomer → lower Ea"],"graph":{"bars":[["Brief, cold","1-isomer"]]}},{"text":"The 1-isomer wins only under kinetic control, so it is not the more stable; and since it forms faster, the 2-isomer has the higher Ea, not the lower.","strike":["The 1-isomer is lower in energy","The 2-isomer has the lower Ea"]},{"text":"Equal stability would give a roughly even equilibrium mixture, not a dominant 2-isomer.","strike":["Both isomers are equally low in energy"]},{"text":"Answer: the 2-isomer is lower in energy."}],"trap":"Reading stability from the kinetic run: at low temperature and short times the faster-forming product wins, whatever its stability."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"briefly at low temperature","note":"kinetic conditions"},{"t":"heating for hours at high temperature","note":"thermodynamic conditions"},{"t":"interconvert","note":"equilibrium reached"}]},{"text":"**Sort the runs.** Interconversion means equilibrium; no interconversion means the first-formed product stays."},{"text":"**Equilibrium → stability.** The product favored at equilibrium has the lower free energy.","calc":["K = [2-isomer]/[1-isomer] > 1 → 2-isomer more stable"]},{"text":"**Kinetic → rate.** The product favored when nothing reverses is the one with the lower activation energy."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** under equilibrium (thermodynamic) conditions the more stable product is major; under kinetic conditions the faster-forming product is major."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-03a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:3#1","pe3-cp:3#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:3"],"passage":"Aldol reactions join two carbonyl compounds through a new C–C bond. Propanal is treated with cold, dilute NaOH, and the product is isolated before any water is eliminated. Its formula is C₆H₁₂O₂, and its IR spectrum shows a broad band near 3400 cm⁻¹ and a strong band near 1720 cm⁻¹.","q":"After the workup described in the passage, which compound is isolated?","choices":["3-Hydroxy-2-methylpentanal","2-Methylpent-2-enal","2-Hydroxy-3-methylpentanal","3-Hydroxyhexanal"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal","cpt-2-methylpent-2-enal","cpt-2-hydroxy-3-methylpentanal","cpt-3-hydroxyhexanal"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"OH and methyl swapped: the OH goes on the β-carbon (C3), the α-carbon's methyl on C2."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"No OH: \"-enal\" names the dehydrated product, which the question rules out."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Straight chain: propanal's α-carbon carries a CH₃, so the aldol product must branch at C2."}],"rule":"Aldol addition: one aldehyde's α-carbon bonds to the other's C=O carbon; the OH ends on C3 and the α-substituent on C2.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Aldol dimer of an n-carbon aldehyde: 2n carbons, OH on C3, the α-carbon's substituent on C2. Propanal: 6 C, R = methyl → 3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal."},"exp":"The enolate carbon of one propanal (C2, which carries the CH₃) bonds to the carbonyl carbon of a second molecule. Without dehydration this **aldol addition** leaves a **β-OH**, two carbons from the aldehyde C=O: CH₃CH₂–CH(OH)–CH(CH₃)–CHO, **3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal**. Losing water would give 2-methylpent-2-enal.","how":{"ask":"Join an α-carbon of one aldehyde to the carbonyl carbon of the other, and keep the OH.","know":"• **Aldol addition** gives a **β-hydroxy carbonyl**: the old C=O becomes C–OH, two carbons from the remaining C=O.\n• **Dehydration** (heat or more base) removes that OH and an α-H, giving the **α,β-unsaturated carbonyl**.","steps":[{"text":"No water is lost, so the product keeps every atom of both molecules: an addition, not a condensation.","hl":"isolated before any water is eliminated","calc":["2 C₃H₆O → C₆H₁₂O₂"]},{"text":"Base removes an α-H from C2 of propanal; that carbon bonds to the carbonyl carbon of a second propanal.","calc":["CH₃CH₂–CH(O⁻)–CH(CH₃)–CHO → protonate → CH₃CH₂–CH(OH)–CH(CH₃)–CHO"]},{"text":"Name it: five-carbon chain with CHO as C1, CH₃ on C2, OH on C3.","calc":["3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal (C₆H₁₂O₂ ✓)"]},{"text":"2-Methylpent-2-enal is the dehydrated (condensation) product, which this question rules out.","strike":["2-Methylpent-2-enal"]},{"text":"The OH must be on the β-carbon and the methyl on the α-carbon, so the swapped name is wrong; a straight six-carbon chain would need the terminal CH₃ to be the nucleophile, but it is not α to the C=O.","strike":["2-Hydroxy-3-methylpentanal","3-Hydroxyhexanal"]},{"text":"Answer: 3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal."}],"trap":"Choosing the α,β-unsaturated aldehyde out of habit: that forms only after dehydration, which the stem excludes."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"cold, dilute NaOH","note":"mild: aldol addition"},{"t":"before any water is eliminated","note":"no dehydration"},{"t":"Propanal","note":"CH₃CH₂CHO"}]},{"text":"**Find the α-carbon.** In CH₃CH₂CHO the α-carbon is the CH₂ next to C=O; its enolate is the nucleophile."},{"text":"**Join and protonate.** α-Carbon to the other C=O carbon; the O⁻ picks up H⁺, giving an OH on the β-carbon.","calc":["α-C–C(OH) next to CHO → β-hydroxy aldehyde"]},{"text":"**Also:** if the mixture is heated, the β-OH and an α-H leave as water, giving the conjugated α,β-unsaturated aldehyde.","calc":["β-hydroxy carbonyl − H₂O → α,β-unsaturated carbonyl"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** aldol addition gives a β-hydroxy carbonyl; only dehydration turns it into the α,β-unsaturated carbonyl."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-04a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:4#1","pe3-cp:4#2","pe3-cp:4#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:4"],"passage":"The Robinson annulation builds a new six-membered ring in two stages. First, the enolate of cyclohexanone adds to methyl vinyl ketone, CH₂=CH–CO–CH₃, in a Michael addition. Then an intramolecular aldol reaction and loss of water close the ring to give a bicyclic enone. In the new ring, C1 is the C=O carbon, C2 the alkene carbon beside it, C3 the alkene carbon at the ring fusion, and C6 the CH₂ bonded to C1. A chemist repeats the reaction with ethyl vinyl ketone, CH₂=CH–CO–CH₂–CH₃, in place of methyl vinyl ketone.","q":"On which ring carbon of the new enone does the extra methyl group appear?","choices":["C1","C2","C3","C6"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"C1 keeps its C=O and is bonded to two ring carbons, so it has no free bond left for a methyl."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"C6 comes from the vinyl end, the carbon the enolate attacks; the extra CH₃ sits on the ethyl side instead."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"C3 is the old cyclohexanone carbonyl carbon at the ring fusion; nothing from the ethyl group reaches it."}],"rule":"Map atoms: the Michael donor bonds to the enone β-carbon, the far α-carbon closes the aldol ring, and any extra group stays on the atom that carried it.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The methyl rides on the ethyl CH₂. That carbon becomes the enolate that closes the ring, so after dehydration it is the alkene carbon right next to the C=O."},"exp":"**Michael**: the cyclohexanone enolate bonds to the **β-carbon** (the CH₂= end) of ethyl vinyl ketone. **Aldol**: the ketone's other α-carbon, the CH₂ of the ethyl group, attacks the ring C=O and closes a six-membered ring; **dehydration** puts the C=C between that carbon and the old ring carbonyl carbon. The extra CH₃ rides along on that α-carbon, so it sits on **C2**.","how":{"ask":"Track the carbon that carries the extra methyl through the Michael addition, the ring-closing aldol and the dehydration.","know":"• **Michael addition**: an enolate bonds to the **β-carbon** of an α,β-unsaturated ketone.\n• **Intramolecular aldol + dehydration**: an α-carbon attacks the other C=O to close a six-membered ring, and the C=C forms between the former α-carbon and the former carbonyl carbon.\n• A group that does not react **rides along** on the atom that carried it.","steps":[{"text":"Michael step: the enolate adds to the vinyl end, the β-carbon of the new enone.","hl":"CH₂=CH–CO–CH₂–CH₃","calc":["ring C–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₂–CH₃ (chain hangs off the ring)"]},{"text":"Aldol step: the ethyl CH₂, the α-carbon that carries the CH₃, attacks the ring C=O; that closes a six-membered ring.","calc":["ring = old C=O carbon, old α-carbon, CH₂, CH₂, C=O, CH(CH₃) = **6 atoms**"]},{"text":"Dehydration: water leaves, and the C=C joins the CH(CH₃) carbon to the old ring carbonyl carbon. The CH₃ now sits on the alkene carbon beside the C=O.","hl":"C2 the alkene carbon beside it","calc":["C1 = C=O, **C2 = C(CH₃)=**, C3 = ring fusion"]},{"text":"C1 keeps its C=O and has no spare bond; C6 comes from the vinyl end; C3 is the old ring carbonyl carbon.","strike":["C1","C6","C3"]},{"text":"Answer: C2."}],"trap":"Putting the extra group on the vinyl end: the enolate attacks the CH₂= carbon, but the methyl rides on the ethyl side, which becomes the ring-closing α-carbon."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Michael addition","note":"enolate → β-carbon"},{"t":"intramolecular aldol reaction and loss of water","note":"ring closes, then C=C forms"},{"t":"ethyl vinyl ketone","note":"one extra CH₃"}]},{"text":"**Michael first.** The enolate carbon bonds to the β-carbon, the far end of the C=C, so a chain hangs off the ring with its ketone three atoms out."},{"text":"**Ring closure.** The α-carbon that can reach the ring C=O to make a six-membered ring is the one that attacks; water then leaves and the C=C forms at the new bond, conjugated with the C=O."},{"text":"**Ride-along groups.** A substituent that does not react stays on its own carbon, so follow that carbon, not the substituent."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a Michael-then-aldol ring closure, the enolate hits the β-carbon, the far α-carbon closes a six-membered ring, and every extra group stays on the atom it started on."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-05a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:5#1","pe3-cp:5#2","pe3-cp:5#3","pe3-cp:5#4"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:5"],"passage":"A training defibrillator stores energy in a capacitor before each pulse. A 25 mF capacitor is fully charged by a 12 V battery with 0.5 Ω internal resistance. Charging stops once no more current flows in the circuit. The stored charge is later released through a model of the chest.","q":"What charge and energy does the capacitor store?","choices":["0.30 C; 1.8 J","0.30 C; 3.6 J","300 C; 1800 J","0.30 C; 0.15 J"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"25 mF is 0.025 F, not 25 F; reading it as farads makes the charge a thousand times too big."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Dropped the ½ in U = ½CV², doubling the energy."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"½CV gives coulombs, not joules; energy needs V squared (½CV²)."}],"rule":"Convert mF to F first (÷ 1000), then Q = CV and U = ½CV²; a series resistor drops no voltage once charging stops.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"25 mF = 0.025 F; × 12 = 0.30 C. Then U = ½QV = ½ × 0.3 × 12 = 0.15 × 12 = 1.8 J. Resistance drops no voltage once current stops."},"exp":"Convert first: **25 mF = 0.025 F**. **Q = CV** = 0.025 F × 12 V = **0.30 C**. The stored energy is **U = ½CV²**, here 0.0125 × 144 = **1.8 J** (or ½QV = ½ × 0.30 × 12 = 1.8 J). Once charged, no current flows, so the 0.5 Ω internal resistance drops no voltage and changes neither value.","how":{"ask":"Put the capacitance in farads, find the voltage across the capacitor, then apply Q = CV and U = ½CV².","know":"• **Q = CV** (coulombs = farads × volts).\n• **U = ½CV² = ½QV** (joules).\n• **m (milli) = 10⁻³**: 1 mF = 0.001 F.\n• A **fully charged** capacitor passes no current, so a resistor in series with it drops I·r = 0 V.","steps":[{"text":"Convert milli-farads to farads.","hl":"25 mF","calc":["25 mF = 25 × 10⁻³ F = **0.025 F**"]},{"text":"Fully charged means the current has stopped. With I = 0, the internal resistance drops no voltage, so the capacitor holds the full battery voltage.","hl":"0.5 Ω internal resistance","calc":["V across r = I·r = 0 × 0.5 Ω = 0 V","V across C = **12 V**"]},{"text":"Charge from Q = CV. 300 C comes from leaving 25 mF as 25 F.","hl":"12 V battery","calc":["Q = CV","Q = 0.025 F × 12 V","Q = **0.30 C**"],"strike":["300 C; 1800 J"]},{"text":"Energy from U = ½CV², checked with ½QV. 3.6 J drops the ½, and 0.15 J forgets to square V (½CV).","calc":["U = ½CV²","U = ½ × 0.025 F × (12 V)²","U = ½ × 0.025 × 144 = **1.8 J**","check: U = ½QV = ½ × 0.30 C × 12 V = **1.8 J**"],"strike":["0.30 C; 3.6 J","0.30 C; 0.15 J"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.30 C; 1.8 J."}],"trap":"Reading mF as farads (300 C) or dropping the ½ in U = ½CV² (3.6 J). The internal resistance only slows the charging; it does not change the final charge."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"25 mF","note":"capacitance, in millifarads"},{"t":"fully charged","note":"no current now"},{"t":"12 V battery","note":"voltage across C"},{"t":"0.5 Ω internal resistance","note":"drops 0 V once I = 0"}]},{"text":"**Milli means one thousandth.** Convert before multiplying.","calc":["25 mF = 25 ÷ 1000 F = 0.025 F"]},{"text":"**Charge:** Q = CV.","calc":["Q = 0.025 F × 12 V = 0.30 C"]},{"text":"**Energy:** U = ½CV², or the same thing written ½QV.","calc":["½CV² = ½ × 0.025 × 12² = 1.8 J","½QV = ½ × 0.30 × 12 = 1.8 J"]},{"text":"**Also:** once a capacitor is fully charged, no current flows, so a series or internal resistance carries no voltage (V = IR = 0). It sets how fast the capacitor charges, never the final Q or U."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Q = CV and U = ½CV² = ½QV, with C in farads (1 mF = 10⁻³ F) and V the full source voltage once charging has stopped."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-06a","s":"Waves & Sound","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:6#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:6"],"passage":"A jogger runs at 7 m/s past a parked ambulance's 700 Hz siren. Her path is a straight line that passes close to the siren. The air is still, and sound travels at 343 m/s.","q":"Compared with 700 Hz, what does she hear approaching, then receding?","choices":["Higher, then lower","Lower, then higher","Higher, then higher","Equal, then equal"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Order flipped: closing in on a source raises the pitch you hear and moving away lowers it, not the reverse."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Approaching and receding shift the pitch opposite ways, so it can't stay high both times."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"She moves straight toward, then away from, the siren, so the Doppler shift can't be zero."}],"rule":"Closing in on a sound source raises the pitch you hear; moving apart lowers it, whether the source or the listener moves.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Gap closing = more crests per second = higher pitch; gap opening = lower. Works whether listener or source moves. Run toward the siren, then past it: higher, then lower."},"exp":"A listener moving **toward** a still source meets more crests each second, so the heard **frequency is higher**; moving **away**, she meets fewer, so it is **lower**. Taking sound at 350 m/s: 700 × 357/350 = 714 Hz approaching and 700 × 343/350 = 686 Hz receding.","how":{"ask":"Decide whether the listener is closing on or leaving the source in each half of the run, and how that changes the crests she meets per second.","know":"• Moving listener, still source: **f′ = f(v ± v_L)/v**, with v the speed of sound.\n• Use **+** when the listener moves **toward** the source (higher f′), **−** when moving **away** (lower f′).\n• Only motion along the line joining them shifts the frequency.","steps":[{"text":"The source is parked; only the listener moves. While approaching she runs into the crests, so she meets more per second. Take sound at 350 m/s.","hl":"runs at 7 m/s","calc":["f′ = f × (v + v_L) ÷ v","f′ = 700 Hz × (350 + 7) ÷ 350","f′ = 700 × 1.02 = **714 Hz** > 700 Hz"]},{"text":"After passing she runs away from the crests, so fewer reach her each second. The pitch drops below 700 Hz, so neither the reversed order nor staying high fits.","calc":["f′ = f × (v − v_L) ÷ v","f′ = 700 Hz × (350 − 7) ÷ 350","f′ = 700 × 0.98 = **686 Hz** < 700 Hz"],"strike":["Lower, then higher","Higher, then higher"]},{"text":"She hears exactly 700 Hz only when she has no velocity toward or away from the siren; while running along the road she hears a shift.","strike":["Equal, then equal"]},{"text":"Answer: higher, then lower."}],"trap":"Thinking only a moving source causes a Doppler shift. A moving listener shifts the heard frequency too: up while closing in, down while leaving."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find who moves**.","marks":[{"t":"runs at 7 m/s","note":"the listener moves"},{"t":"parked ambulance's 700 Hz siren","note":"the source is still"},{"t":"approaching, then receding","note":"two directions"}]},{"text":"**Count crests.** Running toward the siren, she meets the crests sooner than if she stood still, so more arrive each second: higher pitch."},{"text":"**Put numbers on it** (sound at 350 m/s).","calc":["toward: 700 × 357/350 = 714 Hz","away: 700 × 343/350 = 686 Hz"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** moving toward a source raises the frequency you hear, and moving away lowers it, whether the source or the listener is the one moving."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-07a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:7#1","pe3-cp:7#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:7"],"passage":"An electric bicycle is tested on a flat, straight path on a calm day. The rider and bike together have a mass of 85 kg. Once the bike settles at a steady 9.0 m/s, air drag and rolling resistance together oppose the motion with 40 N. The motor supplies all of the driving force; the rider does not pedal.","q":"What power must the motor deliver at the steady speed?","choices":["360 W","4.4 W","3.4 kW","7.5 kW"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"That is the kinetic energy ½mv², an energy in joules labeled as a power; power is a force times a speed."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"This divides the force by the speed; power is force times speed, so it grows with both."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"This uses the weight, mg, as the force; on level ground the motor only has to match the 40 N of resistance."}],"rule":"At constant speed the driving force equals the total resistance, so P = F_resist × v; mass and weight drop out on level ground.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"P = Fv. Constant speed means the motor force is 40 N, so P = 40 × 9.0."},"exp":"Because the speed is steady, the forces balance: the motor's driving force equals the **40 N** of resistance. **P = F·v** = 40 N × 9.0 m/s = **360 W**. The 85 kg mass does not matter on level ground at steady speed.","how":{"ask":"Find the driving force at steady speed, then multiply it by the speed.","know":"• **Constant velocity → net force = 0**, so driving force = total resistive force.\n• **P = F·v** (watts = newtons × meters per second).\n• On level ground the weight is held up by the road; it does not enter P.","steps":[{"text":"Constant speed means the forces balance.","hl":"a steady 9.0 m/s","calc":["F_drive = F_resist = **40 N**"]},{"text":"Multiply force by speed.","calc":["P = 40 N × 9.0 m/s = **360 W**"]},{"text":"3.4 kW is ½mv², an energy; 4.4 W is F ÷ v; 7.5 kW uses the weight instead of the 40 N resistance.","strike":["3.4 kW","4.4 W","7.5 kW"]},{"text":"Answer: 360 W."}],"trap":"Using the weight (mg) or the kinetic energy: on level ground at steady speed, the motor only replaces what drag and rolling resistance take away."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"85 kg","note":"not needed on level ground"},{"t":"a steady 9.0 m/s","note":"v; constant → forces balance"},{"t":"40 N","note":"the force the motor must match"}]},{"text":"**Balance the forces.** No acceleration means the driving force exactly cancels drag plus rolling resistance."},{"text":"**Power = force × speed.**","calc":["P = Fv = 40 × 9.0 = 360 W"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** at constant speed, P = F_resist × v; ignore the mass unless the speed or the height is changing."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-08a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:8#1","pe3-cp:8#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:8"],"passage":"Some roller coasters slow their cars without contact, using magnets that sweep past a metal fin under each car. A 400-kg car enters such a braking zone on a level track at 12 m/s, with friction negligible. Sensors record its speed every second; Figure 1 shows 12, 6.0, 3.0 and 1.5 m/s at the first four readings. The car never quite stops inside the zone.","q":"What does Figure 1 show about the braking force?","choices":["Same at every speed","Weakens as the car slows","Largest just before the car stops","Zero once the speed halves"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"With no force the speed would level off, but the curve keeps dropping after the speed has halved."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"The drops shrink each second (6, then 3, then 1.5 m/s), so the force is getting smaller near the end, not larger."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A constant force gives a constant deceleration and a straight line; this curve flattens instead."}],"rule":"Straight-line drop on a speed–time graph = constant force. A curve that halves in equal times = force proportional to speed (exponential slowing).","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Compare the speed drops in equal times: 6, 3, 1.5 m/s. Shrinking drops mean shrinking deceleration, so F = ma shrinks along with v."},"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Figure 1: speed of the car in the braking zone","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Speed (m/s)","min":0,"max":12,"step":3},"series":[{"name":"Car","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,12],[0.5,8.49],[1,6],[1.5,4.24],[2,3],[2.5,2.12],[3,1.5],[3.5,1.06],[4,0.75]]}]},"exp":"The speed **halves every second** (12 → 6.0 → 3.0 → 1.5 m/s), so each second's drop is half the one before. The deceleration, and so the braking force (F = ma), is **proportional to the speed**: large at entry, smaller as the car slows. A constant force would give a straight line.","how":{"ask":"Read how the slope of the speed–time curve changes, then turn slope into force with F = ma.","know":"• On a speed–time graph, **slope = acceleration**, and F = ma.\n• **Straight line** → constant slope → constant force.\n• **Curve that flattens**, halving in equal times → exponential slowing → force ∝ speed (magnetic, eddy-current braking).","steps":[{"text":"Read the speeds at equal time steps.","hl":"12, 6.0, 3.0 and 1.5 m/s","calc":["drops: 6, 3, 1.5 m/s in successive seconds"]},{"text":"Each drop is half the one before, just as the speed itself halves: the deceleration tracks the speed.","calc":["a ∝ v → F = ma ∝ v"]},{"text":"Zero force would leave the speed flat; a growing force would make the drops grow; a constant force would make them equal.","strike":["Zero once the speed halves","Largest just before the car stops","Same at every speed"]},{"text":"Answer: the force weakens as the car slows."}],"trap":"Reading the flat tail of the curve as a strong force: a flatter slope means less deceleration, so less force."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"12, 6.0, 3.0 and 1.5 m/s","note":"halves each second"},{"t":"friction negligible","note":"the magnets are the only brake"}]},{"text":"**Slope is acceleration.** Steep at entry means a large force; nearly flat later means a small force."},{"text":"**Equal halving times** are the signature of a force proportional to speed: the car slows exponentially and never quite stops.","calc":["v = v₀(½)^(t / 1 s)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a straight speed–time line means a constant force; a curve that flattens means the force shrinks with speed, as in magnetic braking."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-09a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe3-cp:9#1","pe3-cp:9#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:9"],"q":"Four C₄H₈ isomers, W–Z, each burn completely to 4 CO₂ + 4 H₂O. Their heats of combustion are W 2717, X 2710, Y 2707 and Z 2701 kJ/mol. Which isomer is most stable?","choices":["W","X","Y","Z"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"W releases the most heat, so it starts highest in energy: the least stable, not the most."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"X releases more heat than two other isomers, so it starts above them in energy."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Y still releases more heat than one other isomer, so it isn't the lowest in energy."}],"rule":"Same products, so less heat released means a lower starting energy: the smallest heat of combustion is the most stable.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Same products, so less heat out means it started lower in energy. Smallest heat of combustion = most stable: 2701 kJ/mol, Z."},"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Heat released on burning one mole of each C₄H₈ isomer","x":{"label":"Isomer"},"y":{"label":"Heat released (kJ/mol)","min":2690,"max":2720,"step":10},"categories":["W","X","Y","Z"],"series":[{"name":"Heat released","values":[2717,2710,2707,2701]}]},"exp":"All four isomers end at the **same products**, so the difference in heat released is the difference in their starting enthalpies. Z releases the **least heat**, so it began lowest in energy and is the most stable; W, releasing the most, is the least stable.","how":{"ask":"Compare the heats of combustion; the smallest one marks the most stable isomer.","know":"• Isomers that burn to the **same products** can be ranked by heat of combustion: **less heat released = lower starting enthalpy = more stable**.\n• Boiling points, UV spectra and mass spectra do **not** measure thermodynamic stability.","steps":[{"text":"Same formula, same products, so all four reactions end at the same enthalpy.","hl":"each burn completely to 4 CO₂ + 4 H₂O"},{"text":"Rank by heat released; the gap from the products is the starting enthalpy.","hl":"W 2717, X 2710, Y 2707 and Z 2701 kJ/mol","calc":["Z 2701 < Y 2707 < X 2710 < W 2717 kJ/mol","most stable: **Z**; Z is 2717 − 2701 = 16 kJ/mol below W"],"graph":{"bars":[["Z","Heat released"]]}},{"text":"W releases the most heat, so it starts highest and is least stable; X and Y lie in between.","strike":["W","X","Y"]},{"text":"Answer: Z."}],"trap":"Thinking more heat released means a 'stronger' or more stable molecule: more heat out means it started higher in energy."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"C₄H₈ isomers","note":"same formula"},{"t":"4 CO₂ + 4 H₂O","note":"same products"},{"t":"W 2717","note":"heat released, kJ/mol"},{"t":"Z 2701 kJ/mol","note":"smallest heat"}]},{"text":"**Common endpoint.** Products are identical, so each heat of combustion measures how far its isomer sits above the same floor.","calc":["ΔH_comb = H(products) − H(isomer)"]},{"text":"**Smaller drop, lower start.** The isomer giving off the smallest amount of heat starts lowest in enthalpy: most stable.","calc":["2701 kJ/mol → lowest start → Z"]},{"text":"**Also:** boiling points reflect intermolecular forces, UV absorption reflects conjugation, and mass-spec patterns reflect mass and fragmentation; none of them measures stability."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** among isomers, the smallest heat of combustion marks the most stable one."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-10a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe3-cp:10#1","pe3-cp:10#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:10"],"q":"Tritiated N-(2-hydroxyethyl)acetamide, with atoms labeled I–IV, is kept in water for two days. Only labeled atoms bearing H can hold tritium. Which site best keeps its label?","choices":["Site I","Site II","Site III","Site IV"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"Site I is the carbonyl oxygen, which carries no hydrogen, so it cannot hold a tritium label at all."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Site II is an O–H; hydrogens on oxygen trade places with water's hydrogens within seconds."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Site III is the amide N–H; hydrogens on nitrogen also exchange with the solvent."}],"rule":"In water, H on O or N swaps with the solvent; only H on carbon keeps an isotope label.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Scan for the one labeled atom that is a carbon bearing H. O–H and N–H exchange with water; C–H does not."},"fig":"hydroxyethylacetamide-sites","exp":"Hydrogens on **O or N** are acidic enough to **exchange with water** quickly, so tritium there is washed into the solvent. Hydrogens on **carbon** do not exchange, so a label on the **CH₃ (site IV)** stays put. Site I, the C=O oxygen, has no H to label.","how":{"ask":"Classify each labeled atom by what it is and what it carries, then keep only a C–H.","know":"• **O–H and N–H** hydrogens exchange with solvent water (fast proton transfer).\n• **C–H** hydrogens, alkyl or aromatic, do not exchange under these conditions.\n• An atom with no H cannot carry a hydrogen label.","steps":[{"text":"Identify each labeled atom: I is the C=O oxygen, II the O–H, III the N–H, IV the CH₃ carbon.","hl":"atoms labeled I–IV"},{"text":"Site I bears no hydrogen, so it cannot carry tritium.","strike":["Site I"]},{"text":"Sites II and III are H on O and on N: they swap with water's H over two days.","strike":["Site II","Site III"]},{"text":"Answer: Site IV."}],"trap":"Choosing a polar O–H or N–H because it looks reactive: those are exactly the hydrogens that wash out."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"kept in water for two days","note":"time for exchange"},{"t":"best keeps its label","note":"non-exchangeable H"}]},{"text":"**Exchangeable H.** Any H on O or N is passed back and forth with water, so its tritium ends up in the solvent.","fig":"hydroxyethylacetamide-sites"},{"text":"**Also:** an aromatic ring C–H is just as stable as an alkyl C–H, so on a drawn ring the C–H site is the one that keeps a label."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** only hydrogens on carbon keep an isotope label in water; H on O or N exchanges."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-11a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:11#1","pe3-cp:11#2","pe3-cp:11#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:11"],"q":"A balloon of volume V holds gas at pressure P and 25 °C. Using R and N_A, how many gas molecules does it hold?","choices":["PV·N_A/(R·298)","PV/(R·298)","PV·N_A/(R·25)","PV/(R·298·N_A)"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"25 is the Celsius reading; the gas law needs kelvin, so 273 must be added first."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Dividing by N_A shrinks the count; molecules = moles × N_A, so N_A belongs on top."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"PV/RT stops at moles; one more step, × N_A, turns moles into molecules."}],"rule":"Molecules = (PV/RT) × N_A with T in kelvin: check that N_A multiplies and that 273 was added to the Celsius value.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"PV/RT is moles; mol × (molecules/mol) = molecules, so N_A multiplies. T must be 25 + 273 K."},"exp":"**n = PV/RT** gives moles, with T in **kelvin**: 25 + 273 = 298 K. Multiplying by **N_A** (molecules per mole) gives the number of molecules: **PV·N_A/(R·298)**.","how":{"ask":"Build the count from n = PV/RT, then convert moles to molecules, with T in kelvin.","know":"• **Ideal gas law**: n = PV/RT, with T in **kelvin** (K = °C + 273).\n• **Molecules = n × N_A**.\n• Units: (Pa·m³) / (J/(mol·K) × K) = mol.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the temperature.","hl":"25 °C","calc":["T = 25 + 273 = **298 K**"]},{"text":"Moles from the gas law.","calc":["n = PV/(R·298)"]},{"text":"Molecules = moles × N_A.","calc":["N = PV·N_A/(R·298)"]},{"text":"25 is a Celsius value; dividing by N_A runs the conversion backwards; PV/(R·298) stops at moles.","strike":["PV·N_A/(R·25)","PV/(R·298·N_A)","PV/(R·298)"]},{"text":"Answer: PV·N_A/(R·298)."}],"trap":"Stopping at moles: PV/RT counts moles, and the question asks for molecules."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"25 °C","note":"convert to kelvin"},{"t":"how many gas molecules","note":"not moles: × N_A"}]},{"text":"**Kelvin first.** Gas laws use absolute temperature.","calc":["25 °C + 273 = 298 K"]},{"text":"**Moles to molecules.**","calc":["N = n·N_A = PV·N_A/(RT)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** particles = PV·N_A/RT, with T in kelvin."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-12a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:12#1","pe3-cp:12#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:12"],"q":"H₂SO₃ has Ka1 = 1.4 × 10⁻² and Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸ (Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴). In water, HSO₃⁻ is best classified as:","choices":["an acid; H⁺ loss wins.","a base; H⁺ gain wins.","a base; its Ka1 is largest.","neither; the two balance."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"This takes Kw/Ka2 as HSO₃⁻'s Kb, but Ka2 is its own acid constant; its Kb comes from Kw/Ka1."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"Ka1 belongs to H₂SO₃, not to HSO₃⁻; the biggest number in the stem is not the ion's own constant."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The ion's Ka and Kb differ by a factor of about 10⁵, so one process clearly dominates."}],"rule":"For an amphoteric ion HA⁻: its Ka is Ka2, its Kb is Kw/Ka1 (from its conjugate acid H₂A). Whichever is larger decides acid or base.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Kb(HSO₃⁻) = Kw/Ka1 = 10⁻¹⁴/1.4 × 10⁻² ≈ 7 × 10⁻¹³. Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸ is about 10⁵ times larger, so it acts as an acid."},"exp":"As an acid, HSO₃⁻ has **Ka = Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸**. As a base it becomes H₂SO₃, so its **Kb = Kw/Ka1** = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 1.4 × 10⁻² ≈ **7.1 × 10⁻¹³**. Ka is far larger, so HSO₃⁻ behaves as an **acid**: losing H⁺ wins over gaining it.","how":{"ask":"Get the ion's Ka and its Kb, then compare them.","know":"• For HA⁻ made from H₂A: **Ka(HA⁻) = Ka2**.\n• **Kb(HA⁻) = Kw/Ka1**, because its conjugate acid is H₂A.\n• Ka > Kb → acts as an acid; Kb > Ka → acts as a base.","steps":[{"text":"The ion's own acid constant is Ka2.","hl":"Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸"},{"text":"Its base constant comes from its conjugate acid, H₂SO₃.","hl":"Ka1 = 1.4 × 10⁻²","calc":["Kb = Kw/Ka1 = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 1.4 × 10⁻²","Kb ≈ **7.1 × 10⁻¹³**"]},{"text":"Compare: Ka2 is about 10⁵ times Kb, so losing H⁺ wins.","calc":["6.3 × 10⁻⁸ ≫ 7.1 × 10⁻¹³"]},{"text":"Kw/Ka2 is not this ion's Kb; Ka1 belongs to H₂SO₃; the two constants are nowhere near equal.","strike":["a base; H⁺ gain wins.","a base; its Ka1 is largest.","neither; the two balance."]},{"text":"Answer: an acid."}],"trap":"Dividing Kw by Ka2 to get the ion's Kb: that gives the Kb of SO₃²⁻, not of HSO₃⁻."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"Ka1 = 1.4 × 10⁻²","note":"H₂SO₃ → HSO₃⁻"},{"t":"Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸","note":"the ion's own Ka"},{"t":"HSO₃⁻","note":"amphoteric ion"}]},{"text":"**Two jobs, two constants.** As an acid HSO₃⁻ uses Ka2; as a base it uses Kw divided by the Ka of its conjugate acid, Ka1.","calc":["Kb = Kw/Ka1"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an amphoteric ion's Ka is the constant for losing its H⁺, its Kb is Kw over the Ka of the acid it forms; the larger one decides."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-13a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:13#1","pe3-cp:13#2","pe3-cp:13#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:13"],"passage":"Anhydrous aluminum chloride, a common Lewis acid catalyst, can be made directly from the elements: 2 Al + 3 Cl₂ → 2 AlCl₃. A chemist mixes 5.4 g of Al (27 g/mol) and 14.2 g of Cl₂ (71 g/mol) and lets the reaction run to completion. Afterward, shiny aluminum is still left in the flask. The molar mass of AlCl₃ is 133.5 g/mol.","q":"Which reactant limits the reaction, and how much AlCl₃ can form at most?","choices":["Cl₂; 17.8 g","Al; 26.7 g","Cl₂; 26.7 g","Al; 19.6 g"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Aluminum is the excess reagent, since some is left over; this yield assumes all of the Al reacts."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Right limiting reagent, but 3 mol Cl₂ give only 2 mol AlCl₃; the 3:2 mole ratio was skipped."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"This just adds the two reactant masses; the leftover Al shows that not all of it ends up in product."}],"rule":"Moles ÷ coefficient: the smallest is limiting; product moles come from the limiting reagent through the mole ratio; leftover reactant was in excess.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Both reactants are 0.200 mol. Cl₂ needs 1.5 times as many moles as Al, so Cl₂ runs out first. AlCl₃ = ⅔ × 0.200 ≈ 0.133 mol × 133.5 g/mol."},"exp":"Moles: Al = 5.4/27 = **0.200**; Cl₂ = 14.2/71 = **0.200**. Divided by coefficients: Al 0.100, Cl₂ 0.067, so **Cl₂ limits** (and Al is left over, as observed). AlCl₃ = 0.200 × 2/3 = 0.133 mol × 133.5 g/mol ≈ **17.8 g**.","how":{"ask":"Find which reactant runs out first, then convert its moles into grams of AlCl₃.","know":"• Convert each mass to **moles**.\n• Divide by its **coefficient**; the smallest result is the **limiting reagent**.\n• Reactant left over at the end was in **excess**.\n• Product moles = limiting moles × (product coefficient ÷ its coefficient).","steps":[{"text":"Convert each reactant to moles.","hl":"5.4 g of Al (27 g/mol)","calc":["Al: 5.4 ÷ 27 = 0.200 mol","Cl₂: 14.2 ÷ 71 = 0.200 mol"]},{"text":"Divide by the coefficients; the smaller value limits.","calc":["Al: 0.200 ÷ 2 = 0.100","Cl₂: 0.200 ÷ 3 = **0.067** → limiting"]},{"text":"The passage confirms it: aluminum is left over, so Al cannot be limiting.","hl":"shiny aluminum is still left in the flask","strike":["Al; 26.7 g","Al; 19.6 g"]},{"text":"Use the 3:2 ratio to get AlCl₃.","calc":["0.200 mol Cl₂ × 2/3 = 0.133 mol AlCl₃","0.133 × 133.5 ≈ **17.8 g**"],"strike":["Cl₂; 26.7 g"]},{"text":"Answer: Cl₂; 17.8 g."}],"trap":"Treating equal moles as equal amounts: the coefficients (2 versus 3) decide which reactant runs out first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"2 Al + 3 Cl₂ → 2 AlCl₃","note":"mole ratios"},{"t":"5.4 g of Al (27 g/mol)","note":"0.200 mol"},{"t":"14.2 g of Cl₂ (71 g/mol)","note":"0.200 mol"},{"t":"aluminum is still left","note":"Al in excess"}]},{"text":"**Moles per coefficient.** Dividing by the coefficient puts both reactants on the same footing.","calc":["Al 0.100 vs Cl₂ 0.067"]},{"text":"**Yield from the limiting reagent only.**","calc":["0.133 mol × 133.5 g/mol ≈ 17.8 g"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** convert to moles, divide by coefficients, and the smallest limits; any reactant left over was in excess."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-14a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:14#1","pe3-cp:14#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:14"],"passage":"Hydrogen peroxide breaks down slowly in a sealed bottle unless a catalyst is added. A student models one uncatalyzed step, P → Q, with the energy diagram in Figure 1. The reactant P sits at 20 kJ/mol, the highest point on the path reaches 95 kJ/mol, and the product Q lies at −25 kJ/mol.","q":"Based on Figure 1, what is Ea per mole for P → Q?","choices":["75 kJ","95 kJ","120 kJ","−45 kJ"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"An activation energy is a climb up to the peak, so it can never be negative; this value is ΔH, product minus reactant."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is the climb from Q up to the peak, the reverse barrier, not the forward one."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"That is the peak's reading on the axis; the barrier is measured from where P starts, not from zero."}],"rule":"Ea(forward) = transition state − reactants; Ea(reverse) = transition state − products; ΔH = products − reactants.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ea is always peak minus start: 95 − 20. It must be positive and smaller than the peak's own reading."},"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Figure 1: energy along the path P → Q","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Energy (kJ/mol)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"P → Q","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,20],[1,20],[3,60],[5,95],[7,40],[9,-25],[10,-25]]}]},"exp":"**Ea = E(transition state) − E(reactants)** = 95 − 20 = **75 kJ/mol**. The peak's reading alone (95) ignores where P starts; 120 is the reverse barrier, 95 − (−25); −45 is ΔH, −25 − 20.","how":{"ask":"Read the reactant level and the peak, then subtract.","know":"• **Ea (forward)** = energy of the transition state (activated complex) − energy of the reactants.\n• **Ea (reverse)** = transition state − products.\n• **ΔH** = products − reactants; it can be negative, Ea cannot.","steps":[{"text":"Read the reactant level and the peak.","hl":"P sits at 20 kJ/mol","calc":["E(P) = 20 kJ/mol","E(peak) = 95 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Subtract.","calc":["Ea = 95 − 20 = **75 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"−45 is ΔH, not a barrier; 120 is measured from Q; 95 is the peak's raw reading.","strike":["−45 kJ","120 kJ","95 kJ"]},{"text":"Answer: 75 kJ per mole."}],"trap":"Reading the peak's height straight off the axis: the barrier is the climb from the reactant level, not from zero."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"P sits at 20 kJ/mol","note":"start"},{"t":"reaches 95 kJ/mol","note":"transition state"},{"t":"lies at −25 kJ/mol","note":"end"}]},{"text":"**Barrier = climb.** Ea is how far the path rises from the reactants to the transition state."},{"text":"**Also:** ΔH compares only the two ends; here −25 − 20 = −45 kJ/mol, an exothermic step."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ea = transition state − reactants; ΔH = products − reactants."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-15a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:15#1","pe3-cp:15#2","pe3-cp:15#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:15"],"passage":"Cadmium, Z = 48, lies directly below zinc in group 12. Like zinc, it forms only the Cd²⁺ ion, and its salts are colorless. The krypton core, [Kr], holds 36 electrons, and after it the 5s subshell fills before the 4d.","q":"Which describes the outer subshells of a neutral cadmium atom in its ground state?","choices":["Filled 4d, filled 5s","Filled 4d, half-filled 5s","Half-filled 4d, filled 5s","Filled 4d, empty 5s"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"impossible","why":"A full 4d with a half-filled 5s holds 11 electrons past [Kr], one short of what the neutral atom needs."},{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"A half-filled 4d plus a full 5s is only 7 electrons past [Kr], far short of the neutral atom's count."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"An empty 5s describes Cd²⁺, the ion named in the passage; the ion loses the 5s pair, but the neutral atom keeps it."}],"rule":"Count electrons past the noble-gas core first; neutral atoms keep their s electrons, while cations lose the outer s electrons before any d.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Group 12 = d¹⁰s². Cd: 48 − 36 = 12 electrons past [Kr] = 4d¹⁰5s². The 2+ ion drops the 5s pair."},"exp":"Neutral Cd has **48 − 36 = 12** electrons beyond [Kr]: **5s²** (filled) and **4d¹⁰** (filled), [Kr]4d¹⁰5s². The ion Cd²⁺ loses the two **5s** electrons first, leaving [Kr]4d¹⁰, all paired, which is why its salts are colorless.","how":{"ask":"Count the electrons past the core, fill 5s and 4d, then describe each subshell.","know":"• Group 12 atoms (Zn, Cd) end in **d¹⁰s²**: both subshells filled (Zn is [Ar]3d¹⁰4s²).\n• Cations lose the outer **s electrons before d** electrons (Zn²⁺ is [Ar]3d¹⁰).\n• \"Half-filled\" means **d⁵** or **s¹**.","steps":[{"text":"Count the electrons past the krypton core.","hl":"Z = 48","calc":["48 − 36 = 12 electrons"]},{"text":"Fill in aufbau order: 5s first (2), then 4d (10).","calc":["5s² + 4d¹⁰ = 12 ✓"]},{"text":"A half-filled 4d (7 in all) or a half-filled 5s (11 in all) has too few electrons; an empty 5s is the 2+ ion, not the atom.","strike":["Half-filled 4d, filled 5s","Filled 4d, half-filled 5s","Filled 4d, empty 5s"]},{"text":"Answer: filled 4d, filled 5s."}],"trap":"Describing the ion when the question asks about the neutral atom: Cd²⁺ has an empty 5s, but the atom keeps both 5s electrons."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Z = 48","note":"48 electrons"},{"t":"it forms only the Cd²⁺ ion","note":"loses 2 electrons"},{"t":"neutral cadmium atom","note":"no electrons removed"}]},{"text":"**Count past the core.**","calc":["[Kr] = 36; 48 − 36 = 12 → 4d¹⁰5s²"]},{"text":"**Also:** the 2+ ion removes the 5s electrons first, giving [Kr]4d¹⁰ with every electron paired; zinc does the same, Zn [Ar]3d¹⁰4s² → Zn²⁺ [Ar]3d¹⁰."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** group 12 atoms are d¹⁰s² with both subshells filled; their 2+ ions lose the s pair first."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-16a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:16#1","pe3-cp:32#1","pe3-cp:32#2","pe3-cp:32#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:32"],"passage":"Silver can be recovered from used fixer solutions by a simple metal displacement. In aqueous AgNO₃, silver crystals grow on a copper wire as the solution turns blue. The blue color comes from Cu²⁺(aq), while the nitrate ions stay in solution unchanged.","q":"Which species in this reaction is reduced?","choices":["Ag⁺","Cu","NO₃⁻","H₂O"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Copper goes from 0 to +2, losing electrons: it is oxidized, the reducing agent, not the species reduced."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Nitrate is a spectator ion: N stays +5 and O stays −2 before and after, so it gains no electrons."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Water is only the solvent: no H₂ or O₂ forms, so H stays +1 and O stays −2."}],"rule":"Reduced = gains electrons = oxidation number falls. The metal that dissolves and colors the solution is oxidized.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Metal that plates out was reduced; metal that dissolves was oxidized. Ag⁺ (+1) → Ag (0) is reduced; Cu → blue Cu²⁺ is oxidized."},"exp":"Cu(s) + 2 Ag⁺ → Cu²⁺ + 2 Ag(s). Silver's oxidation state falls from +1 to 0, so **Ag⁺ gains an electron and is reduced**; it is the oxidizing agent. Copper rises from 0 to +2 and is oxidized; it is the reducing agent. Nitrate and water keep their oxidation states.","how":{"ask":"Assign oxidation states before and after, and find the species whose oxidation state falls.","know":"• **Oxidation** = loss of electrons (state rises); **reduction** = gain of electrons (state falls).\n• The species reduced is the **oxidizing agent**; the species oxidized is the **reducing agent**.\n• Elements (Cu, Ag, O₂) are 0; in NO₃⁻, N is +5 and O is −2; in H₂O, H is +1 and O is −2 (O is −1 in peroxides such as H₂O₂).\n• **Any change in oxidation state makes a reaction redox.**","steps":[{"text":"Write the change: silver metal forms and blue Cu²⁺ appears.","hl":"silver crystals grow on a copper wire","calc":["Cu(s) + 2 Ag⁺(aq) → Cu²⁺(aq) + 2 Ag(s)"]},{"text":"Assign oxidation states. Silver's falls, so Ag⁺ gains electrons and is reduced. Copper's rises, so Cu is oxidized; calling it reduced mixes up the reducing agent with the species reduced.","calc":["Ag: +1 → 0 (gains 1 e⁻, **reduced**)","Cu: 0 → +2 (loses 2 e⁻, oxidized)"],"strike":["Cu"]},{"text":"Nitrate and water keep their oxidation states: NO₃⁻ is a spectator ion and water is the solvent.","calc":["NO₃⁻: N + 3(−2) = −1 → N = +5, before and after","H₂O: H +1, O −2, before and after"],"strike":["NO₃⁻","H₂O"]},{"text":"**Also, oxygen:** O₂ → H₂O₂ lowers O from 0 to −1; O₂ → H₂O lowers it to −2. O gains electrons, so O₂ is reduced.","calc":["O₂ (0) → H₂O₂ (−1) → H₂O (−2)"]},{"text":"**Also, cofactors:** a cofactor that is reduced and then reoxidized (e.g. FAD → FADH₂ → FAD) ends where it started, so it is not net changed; ask which species is reduced overall."},{"text":"Answer: Ag⁺."}],"trap":"Picking Cu because it is the reducing agent: the reducing agent is itself oxidized. The species reduced is the one whose oxidation state falls."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key observations**.","marks":[{"t":"copper wire","note":"Cu metal, oxidation state 0"},{"t":"AgNO₃","note":"Ag⁺, oxidation state +1"},{"t":"turns blue","note":"Cu²⁺ forming"}]},{"text":"**Track oxidation states.** Cu 0 → +2 and Ag +1 → 0: states change, so the reaction is redox, even though a solid appears."},{"text":"**Falls = gains electrons = reduced.** Ag⁺ is reduced (the oxidizing agent); Cu is oxidized (the reducing agent)."},{"text":"**Also:** O₂ to H₂O₂ or H₂O lowers oxygen from 0 to −1 or −2, so O₂ is reduced (it is the oxidizing agent)."},{"text":"**Also:** in a multistep pathway, a cofactor that is reduced and then regenerated is not net changed; the overall reduced species is the one whose oxidation state ends lower."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** assign oxidation states; the species whose state falls gains electrons and is reduced (the oxidizing agent), and any change at all makes the reaction redox."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-16b","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:16#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:16"],"passage":"Lead(II) iodide is a bright yellow salt used in some solar-cell materials. A student pours colorless aqueous Pb(NO₃)₂ into colorless aqueous KI. A bright yellow solid forms at once and settles out of the solution. The liquid above the solid stays colorless and still conducts electricity, because K⁺ and NO₃⁻ ions remain dissolved.","q":"How is this reaction best classified?","choices":["precipitation; no e⁻ move","redox; Pb²⁺ is reduced","redox; I⁻ is oxidized","Lewis acid–base; adduct forms"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Lead stays +2 in the solid; a reduction would need its oxidation state to drop, and it does not."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Iodine stays −1 in PbI₂; oxidized iodide would give I₂, which is brown, not a yellow solid."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"No electron pair is donated to form a covalent adduct; the ions simply pack into an insoluble lattice."}],"rule":"Assign oxidation states before and after: if none change, it is not redox; then name what the observations show forming, such as an insoluble solid from two ion solutions.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Check oxidation numbers: Pb +2 → +2, I −1 → −1. Nothing changes, and an insoluble solid appears from mixed ions."},"exp":"Pb²⁺(aq) + 2 I⁻(aq) → PbI₂(s). **No oxidation state changes**: Pb stays +2 and I stays −1, so it is not redox. An **insoluble solid forms from two ion solutions**, which defines a precipitation; K⁺ and NO₃⁻ are spectators, so the liquid still conducts.","how":{"ask":"Check the oxidation states first, then match the observations to a reaction type.","know":"• **Redox**: some oxidation state changes (for example Zn 0 → +2 while I 0 → −1).\n• **Precipitation**: ions in solution combine into an insoluble solid; oxidation states stay put.\n• **Acid–base**: H⁺ transfer, or an electron-pair donor bonding to an acceptor.","steps":[{"text":"Write the net ionic equation.","hl":"A bright yellow solid forms at once","calc":["Pb²⁺(aq) + 2 I⁻(aq) → PbI₂(s)"]},{"text":"Assign oxidation states on both sides.","calc":["Pb: +2 → +2","I: −1 → −1","no change → not redox"],"strike":["redox; Pb²⁺ is reduced","redox; I⁻ is oxidized"]},{"text":"No electron pair is shared into a new covalent adduct; the ions just pack into a lattice.","strike":["Lewis acid–base; adduct forms"]},{"text":"Answer: precipitation."}],"trap":"Calling any reaction with a metal ion redox: check whether an oxidation number actually changes."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"colorless aqueous Pb(NO₃)₂","note":"Pb²⁺ ions"},{"t":"bright yellow solid forms","note":"insoluble product"},{"t":"K⁺ and NO₃⁻ ions remain dissolved","note":"spectators"}]},{"text":"**Oxidation states decide redox.**","calc":["Zn + I₂ → ZnI₂: Zn 0 → +2, I 0 → −1 → redox","Pb²⁺ + 2 I⁻ → PbI₂: no change → not redox"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** if no oxidation number changes it is not redox; ions combining into an insoluble solid is a precipitation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-17a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:17#1","pe3-cp:17#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:17"],"passage":"Gas-law calculations such as PV = nRT need temperature on the absolute scale. A sauna heater keeps the air in its cabin warm and dry. The sauna's air temperature reads 122 °F.","q":"Expressed in kelvin, this temperature is about:","choices":["323 K","50 K","395 K","341 K"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"impossible","why":"Sense check: 50 K is colder than liquid nitrogen; warm air is near 300 K, so 50 is really a °C value."},{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"395 K is about 122 °C, above water's boiling point: it adds 273 to the °F value without converting to °C first."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skipped subtracting 32 before multiplying by 5/9, so the value comes out too hot."}],"rule":"°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9, then add 273. Room-to-hot air sits roughly between 290 and 340 K, so values far outside that are out.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"122 − 32 = 90; 90 × 5/9 = 10 × 5 = 50 °C; 50 + 273 = 323 K."},"exp":"**°F → °C: subtract 32, then × 5/9**: (122 − 32) × 5/9 = 90 × 5/9 = 50 °C. **K = °C + 273** = 323 K.","how":{"ask":"Convert °F to °C, then °C to kelvin.","know":"• **°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9**.\n• **K = °C + 273**.\n• Never add 273 to a Fahrenheit value.","steps":[{"text":"Start from the Fahrenheit reading.","hl":"122 °F","calc":["°C = (122 − 32) × 5/9","= 90 × 5/9","= **50 °C**"]},{"text":"Add 273 to reach kelvin.","calc":["K = 50 + 273 = **323 K**"]},{"text":"50 K stops at Celsius; 395 K adds 273 to the Fahrenheit value; 341 K skips subtracting 32 (122 × 5/9 + 273).","strike":["50 K","395 K","341 K"]},{"text":"Answer: 323 K."}],"trap":"Skipping the −32 (122 × 5/9 + 273 ≈ 341 K): subtract 32 first, then scale by 5/9."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"122 °F","note":"Fahrenheit: convert to °C first"},{"t":"absolute scale","note":"kelvin"}]},{"text":"**°F → °C.** Subtract 32, then multiply by 5/9.","calc":["(122 − 32) × 5/9 = 50 °C"]},{"text":"**Also:** °C → K: add 273.","calc":["50 + 273 = 323 K"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** °C = (°F − 32) × 5/9 and K = °C + 273; gas laws always use kelvin."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-18a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:18#1","pe3-cp:18#2","pe4-cp:15#1","pe4-cp:15#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:18"],"passage":"Transcutaneous nerve stimulators send current through the skin in short square pulses. A stimulator outputs 0.30 ms pulses separated by 7.7 ms of zero output, repeating steadily. Every pulse has the same height.","q":"What are the pulse train's period and frequency?","choices":["8.0 ms; 125 Hz","7.7 ms; 130 Hz","0.30 ms; 3333 Hz","8.0 ms; 0.125 Hz"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"This divides 1 by the period still in ms; convert to seconds first, which makes the frequency a thousand times larger."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"7.7 ms is only the off time; one full cycle also includes the pulse itself."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"0.30 ms is just the pulse width, so its inverse describes one pulse, not how often pulses repeat."}],"rule":"Period = on time + off time (one full repeat); f = 1/T with T in seconds.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"T = 0.30 + 7.7 = 8.0 ms = 0.0080 s; f = 1/0.0080 s. Units check: 1/ms is kHz, so a period in ms must be converted before reporting Hz."},"exp":"One **period** is one full repeat: on time + off time = 0.30 + 7.7 = **8.0 ms**. **f = 1/T** = 1/(8.0 × 10⁻³ s) = **125 Hz**.","how":{"ask":"Add the on and off times for the period, then invert it in seconds.","know":"• **Period T** = time from the start of one pulse to the start of the next (pulse + gap).\n• **f = 1/T**, with T in seconds for hertz.\n• 1 ms = 10⁻³ s.","steps":[{"text":"Add the pulse and the gap.","hl":"0.30 ms pulses separated by 7.7 ms","calc":["T = 0.30 + 7.7 = **8.0 ms**"]},{"text":"Invert in seconds.","calc":["f = 1/(8.0 × 10⁻³ s) = **125 Hz**"]},{"text":"0.125 Hz forgets the ms → s conversion; 7.7 ms leaves out the pulse; 0.30 ms leaves out the gap.","strike":["8.0 ms; 0.125 Hz","7.7 ms; 130 Hz","0.30 ms; 3333 Hz"]},{"text":"Answer: 8.0 ms; 125 Hz."}],"trap":"Using only the gap or only the pulse as the period: a period always spans one complete cycle."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"0.30 ms pulses","note":"on time"},{"t":"7.7 ms of zero output","note":"off time"},{"t":"repeating steadily","note":"periodic"}]},{"text":"**One cycle = on + off.** Measure from the start of one pulse to the start of the next.","calc":["T = 8.0 ms"]},{"text":"**Frequency = 1/period**, with the period in seconds.","calc":["1/0.0080 s = 125 Hz"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** period = pulse + gap; f = 1/T with T in seconds (1 ms = 10⁻³ s)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-19a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:19#1","pe3-cp:19#2","pe3-cp:19#3","pe4-cp:6#1","pe4-cp:6#2","pe4-cp:6#3","pe5-cp:56#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:19"],"passage":"Dental curing lamps harden resin fillings with short-wavelength light. One lamp emits a continuous band of wavelengths between 330 nm and 550 nm. Each photon's energy depends only on its wavelength. Take hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm.","q":"What is the greatest energy of a single photon from the lamp, and at which wavelength?","choices":["3.8 eV, at 330 nm","2.3 eV, at 550 nm","3.8 × 10⁻⁹ eV, at 330 nm","0.27 eV, at 330 nm"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"This mixes hc in eV·m with λ in nm; keep both lengths in the same unit, or the energy shrinks by a factor of 10⁹."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"This divides λ by hc; photon energy is E = hc/λ, with hc on top."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"The long-wavelength end gives the lowest photon energy, not the highest; E rises as λ falls."}],"rule":"E = hc/λ: the shortest wavelength gives the most energetic photon; keep hc and λ in the same length unit (1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m).","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"The maximum E sits at the short-λ edge of the band. E(eV) ≈ 1240/λ(nm) = 1240/330, a bit under 4 eV."},"exp":"**E = hc/λ**, so the most energetic photon has the **shortest** wavelength, 330 nm. E = 1240 eV·nm ÷ 330 nm ≈ **3.8 eV** (about 6.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J).","how":{"ask":"Pick the wavelength that gives the most energy, then compute E = hc/λ with matching length units.","know":"• **E = hc/λ**: shorter λ, larger E.\n• **1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m**; hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm ≈ 1.99 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m.\n• E(eV) = E(J) ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹; E(eV) ≈ 1240/λ(nm), and λ(nm) ≈ 1240/E(eV).","steps":[{"text":"Highest energy means shortest wavelength.","hl":"between 330 nm and 550 nm","strike":["2.3 eV, at 550 nm"]},{"text":"Divide hc by λ, both in nm.","calc":["E = 1240 eV·nm / 330 nm ≈ **3.8 eV**"]},{"text":"3.8 × 10⁻⁹ eV comes from hc in eV·m over λ in nm; 0.27 eV divides λ by hc.","strike":["3.8 × 10⁻⁹ eV, at 330 nm","0.27 eV, at 330 nm"]},{"text":"**Also:** in joules, E = 1.99 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m ÷ 3.3 × 10⁻⁷ m ≈ 6.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, and 6.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ ≈ 3.8 eV."},{"text":"Answer: 3.8 eV, at 330 nm."}],"trap":"Picking the long-wavelength end: energy and wavelength are inversely related."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"between 330 nm and 550 nm","note":"use the short end"},{"t":"hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm","note":"λ in nm gives E in eV"}]},{"text":"**Short λ, high E.**","calc":["E = hc/λ"]},{"text":"**Units.** 1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m; 1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J; E(eV) ≈ 1240/λ(nm)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hc/λ with matching units; the shortest wavelength carries the most energy."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-20a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:20#1","pe3-cp:20#2","pe4-cp:14#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:20"],"passage":"Electrostatic air cleaners pass room air between charged metal plates. Two large parallel plates are held at 12 V, the left one positive. The gap between them is small compared with the plate size.","q":"Which describes the field lines between them, away from the edges?","choices":["Evenly spaced; left to right","Evenly spaced; right to left","Denser near plates; left to right","Curved outward; right to left"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Field lines leave the positive plate and end on the negative one, so they can't point toward the positive plate."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Wrong way and wrong shape: lines run from + to −, and they curve only at the plate edges."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Large plates, away from the edges: the field is uniform, so lines can't crowd near a plate."}],"rule":"Between large parallel plates the field is uniform (straight, equally spaced lines) and points from the + plate to the − plate.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Large parallel plates = uniform field: straight, evenly spaced lines. Lines always point from + to −, so from the left plate to the right."},"exp":"Between large parallel plates the field has the **same strength and direction at every point**, so its lines are straight and **evenly spaced**. Field lines start on positive charge and end on negative charge, so they run **from the + plate to the − plate**: left to right.","how":{"ask":"Decide what a uniform field looks like, then which way its lines point.","know":"• **Line spacing shows strength**: closer lines mean a stronger field.\n• Between large parallel plates the field is **uniform**: E = V/d at every point, so lines are straight, parallel and evenly spaced.\n• Field lines point **from + to −**, the way a positive test charge would be pushed.","steps":[{"text":"Large plates, away from the edges: the field is uniform. If the gap were 4.0 mm, E would be the same everywhere, so there is no crowding near a plate and no curving (lines bulge only at the edges).","hl":"Two large parallel plates","calc":["E = V ÷ d","E = 12 V ÷ 0.0040 m = **3000 V/m** at every point"],"strike":["Denser near plates; left to right","Curved outward; right to left"]},{"text":"A positive test charge is pushed away from the positive plate and toward the negative one, so the lines run from left to right.","hl":"the left one positive","strike":["Evenly spaced; right to left"]},{"text":"Answer: evenly spaced; left to right."}],"trap":"Drawing the lines from − to +: field lines leave positive charge and end on negative charge."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"large parallel plates","note":"uniform field"},{"t":"the left one positive","note":"sets the direction"},{"t":"away from the edges","note":"no fringing"}]},{"text":"**Uniform means the same everywhere.** Equal strength means equal line spacing; equal direction means straight, parallel lines.","calc":["E = V/d, the same at every point between the plates"]},{"text":"**Also:** field lines point from + to −: they start on the positive plate and end on the negative plate."},{"text":"**Also:** the field's size is E = V/d, with d in metres and 1 kV = 10³ V.","calc":["12 kV across 5.0 mm","E = 12 × 10³ V ÷ 5.0 × 10⁻³ m = 2.4 × 10⁶ V/m"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** between parallel plates the field lines are straight, parallel and evenly spaced, pointing from the positive plate to the negative plate."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-21a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:21#1","pe3-cp:21#2","pe3-cp:21#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:21"],"passage":"Handheld lactate meters help coaches track exercise intensity. Each meter is checked with a lactate standard of 2.0 mM (lactate: 89 g/mol). The test strip draws in a 3.0 μL drop of the standard, and its enzyme layer reacts with every lactate ion in the drop.","q":"What mass of lactate does one drop deliver to the strip?","choices":["5.3 × 10⁻⁷ g","5.3 × 10⁻⁴ g","6.0 × 10⁻⁹ g","6.7 × 10⁻¹¹ g"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Treating μL as mL makes the volume 1000 times too big; 1 μL = 10⁻⁶ L."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Dividing moles by the molar mass gives mol²/g; grams = moles × g/mol."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"This is the amount in moles; one more step, × 89 g/mol, turns it into grams."}],"rule":"mass = M × V(L) × molar mass; convert μL to L (× 10⁻⁶) and check that the units end in grams.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"mol/L × L × g/mol = g: 2.0 × 10⁻³ × 3.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 89 ≈ 6 × 89 × 10⁻⁹ g."},"exp":"Moles = **M × V** = (2.0 × 10⁻³ mol/L)(3.0 × 10⁻⁶ L) = 6.0 × 10⁻⁹ mol. Mass = moles × **molar mass** = 6.0 × 10⁻⁹ × 89 g/mol ≈ **5.3 × 10⁻⁷ g**.","how":{"ask":"Turn concentration and volume into moles, then moles into grams.","know":"• **moles = M × V**, with V in liters (1 μL = 10⁻⁶ L, 1 mM = 10⁻³ M).\n• **mass = moles × molar mass**.\n• For a g/L value, mass = (g/L) × V with no molar mass needed.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the units.","hl":"2.0 mM","calc":["2.0 mM = 2.0 × 10⁻³ mol/L","3.0 μL = 3.0 × 10⁻⁶ L"]},{"text":"Moles in the drop.","calc":["n = 2.0 × 10⁻³ × 3.0 × 10⁻⁶ = 6.0 × 10⁻⁹ mol"]},{"text":"Grams.","calc":["m = 6.0 × 10⁻⁹ × 89 ≈ **5.3 × 10⁻⁷ g**"]},{"text":"5.3 × 10⁻⁴ g uses mL for μL; 6.7 × 10⁻¹¹ g divides by the molar mass; 6.0 × 10⁻⁹ g stops at moles.","strike":["5.3 × 10⁻⁴ g","6.7 × 10⁻¹¹ g","6.0 × 10⁻⁹ g"]},{"text":"Answer: 5.3 × 10⁻⁷ g."}],"trap":"Stopping at moles, or mixing up μL and mL: carry the units all the way to grams."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 mM","note":"mol/L × 10⁻³"},{"t":"89 g/mol","note":"grams per mole"},{"t":"3.0 μL","note":"× 10⁻⁶ L"}]},{"text":"**Moles from M and V.**","calc":["n = MV = 6.0 × 10⁻⁹ mol"]},{"text":"**Grams from moles.**","calc":["m = n × 89 g/mol"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mass = molarity × volume (in liters) × molar mass; for a g/L value, just multiply it by the volume in liters."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-22a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:22#1","pe3-cp:22#2","pe3-cp:40#1","pe3-cp:40#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:22"],"passage":"Breath-alcohol strips can use an enzyme that works much like the glucose oxidase in a glucose meter. The enzyme converts the alcohol on the left into the ketone on the right. It removes two hydrogen atoms from the alcohol and hands them to O₂, which leaves as H₂O₂. Nothing else is used up.","q":"To which enzyme class does this enzyme belong?","choices":["Oxidoreductase: H removed","Transferase: passes H to O₂","Lyase: forms a C=O","Isomerase: same formula"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"The product has two fewer H atoms than the alcohol, so the formula changes; an isomerase keeps it the same."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A lyase adds or removes groups to make or break double bonds without redox; here two H go to O₂, an oxidation."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Transferases move a functional group between substrates; handing hydrogens to O₂ is an oxidation–reduction."}],"rule":"Name the change: H removed or O added → oxidoreductase; group moved → transferase; water splits a bond → hydrolase; atoms rearranged → isomerase.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count H: alcohol → ketone loses 2 H to O₂. Losing hydrogen is oxidation, so the class is the redox one."},"fig":["1-phenylethanol","acetophenone"],"exp":"The alcohol loses **two hydrogens** (from its O–H and C–H) and becomes a ketone, while O₂ is reduced to H₂O₂. Any enzyme that catalyzes an **oxidation–reduction** is an **oxidoreductase**, just like glucose oxidase in a glucose meter.","how":{"ask":"Compare substrate and product, decide what kind of change it is, and match it to one of the six classes.","know":"• **Oxidoreductase**: oxidation–reduction (H removed, O added, electrons moved).\n• **Transferase**: moves a group (methyl, phosphate) from one molecule to another.\n• **Hydrolase**: splits a bond with water.\n• **Lyase**: makes or breaks a double bond or ring by adding or removing a group, without hydrolysis or redox.\n• **Isomerase**: rearranges atoms within one molecule.\n• **Ligase**: joins two molecules, using ATP.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the structures: the CH–OH becomes C=O.","hl":"removes two hydrogen atoms from the alcohol","calc":["C₈H₁₀O → C₈H₈O + 2 H (to O₂)"]},{"text":"The formula changed, so it is not an isomerase.","strike":["Isomerase: same formula"]},{"text":"Removing H and reducing O₂ is oxidation–reduction, not a group transfer or a lyase addition.","strike":["Transferase: passes H to O₂","Lyase: forms a C=O"]},{"text":"Answer: oxidoreductase."}],"trap":"Calling it a transferase because hydrogens move to O₂: moving electrons with hydrogens is redox; transferases move whole groups such as methyl or phosphate."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"removes two hydrogen atoms","note":"oxidation"},{"t":"hands them to O₂","note":"O₂ is reduced"},{"t":"Nothing else is used up","note":"no group donor"}]},{"text":"**Six classes, six kinds of change.**","calc":["redox → oxidoreductase","group moved → transferase","water splits a bond → hydrolase","double bond made or broken → lyase","atoms rearranged → isomerase","two joined with ATP → ligase"]},{"text":"**Also:** glucose oxidase in a glucose meter oxidizes glucose and reduces O₂, so it too is an oxidoreductase; an enzyme that moves a methyl group from SAM is a transferase."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** decide what changed (redox, group transfer, hydrolysis, double bond, rearrangement, joining), then name the class."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-23a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:23#1","pe3-cp:23#2","pe3-cp:23#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:23"],"passage":"A hydrazine fuel cell powers a remote weather sensor. A fuel-cell anode oxidizes hydrazine: N₂H₄ → N₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻. During one test, 193 C of charge flows through the circuit (F = 96,500 C/mol).","q":"What mass of hydrazine (32 g/mol) was consumed?","choices":["16 mg","32 mg","64 mg","256 mg"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: more electrons per molecule means fewer molecules per coulomb, so × 4 goes the wrong way."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"64 mg treats each N₂H₄ as giving 1 e⁻; the half-reaction shows 4 e⁻, so a ÷ 4 is missing."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"32 mg divides by 2 electrons; the half-reaction releases 4 e⁻ per N₂H₄."}],"rule":"mol e⁻ = charge ÷ F, then ÷ electrons per molecule, then × molar mass. Round F to 10⁵ C/mol and each number to 1 digit.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"96,500 × 2 = 193,000, so 193 C = 2 × 10⁻³ mol e⁻. ÷ 4 e⁻ per N₂H₄ = 5 × 10⁻⁴ mol; × 32 g/mol = 160 × 10⁻⁴ g = 16 mg."},"exp":"**mol e⁻ = Q/F** = 193 ÷ 96,500 = 2.00 × 10⁻³ mol. Each N₂H₄ gives 4 e⁻, so **mol N₂H₄ = mol e⁻ ÷ 4** = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol. **× molar mass**: 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ × 32 g/mol = 0.016 g = 16 mg.","how":{"ask":"Charge → moles of electrons → moles of fuel → grams.","know":"• **mol e⁻ = Q / F** (F = 96,500 C/mol).\n• **mol substance = mol e⁻ ÷ electrons per molecule** (from the half-reaction).\n• **mass = mol × molar mass**.","steps":[{"text":"Convert charge to moles of electrons.","hl":"193 C","calc":["mol e⁻ = Q / F","= 193 ÷ 96,500","= **2.00 × 10⁻³ mol**"]},{"text":"The half-reaction releases 4 e⁻ per N₂H₄.","hl":"4e⁻","calc":["mol N₂H₄ = 2.00 × 10⁻³ ÷ 4 = **5.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol**"]},{"text":"Convert to mass.","calc":["m = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol × 32 g/mol","= 0.016 g = **16 mg**"]},{"text":"32 mg divides by 2 instead of 4; 64 mg skips the ÷ 4; 256 mg multiplies by 4.","strike":["32 mg","64 mg","256 mg"]},{"text":"Answer: 16 mg."}],"trap":"Treating moles of electrons as moles of substance: divide by the electrons each molecule transfers."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"193 C","note":"charge Q"},{"t":"96,500 C/mol","note":"Faraday's constant"},{"t":"4e⁻","note":"electrons per N₂H₄"},{"t":"32 g/mol","note":"molar mass"}]},{"text":"**Charge to electrons.**","calc":["mol e⁻ = Q/F = 193 ÷ 96,500 = 2.00 × 10⁻³ mol"]},{"text":"**Electrons to molecules.** Divide by the electrons each molecule gives up.","calc":["2.00 × 10⁻³ ÷ 4 = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol N₂H₄"]},{"text":"**Also:** moles × molar mass gives the mass.","calc":["5.0 × 10⁻⁴ × 32 = 0.016 g = 16 mg"]},{"text":"**Also:** a sensor that oxidizes glucose collects 2 e⁻ per glucose, so there mol glucose = mol e⁻ ÷ 2.","calc":["2.00 × 10⁻³ mol e⁻ ÷ 2 = 1.00 × 10⁻³ mol glucose","1.00 × 10⁻³ mol × 180 g/mol = 0.180 g"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mol e⁻ = Q/F; divide by electrons per molecule; multiply by molar mass."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-24a","s":"Metabolism & Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:24#1","pe3-cp:24#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:24"],"passage":"Lactate meters used in sports medicine rely on lactate oxidase. The enzyme strips two hydrogen atoms, as two protons and two electrons, from lactate to make pyruvate. A cofactor made from riboflavin, smaller than FAD because it lacks FAD's AMP portion, is held tightly within the enzyme. It accepts the two hydrogens, then hands both to O₂, which leaves as H₂O₂.","q":"Which cofactor takes up the two hydrogens, and how does it change?","choices":["FMN; it gains two H","FMNH₂; it loses two H","NAD⁺; it gains two H","Heme; Fe gains two e⁻"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"FMNH₂ is the form that already holds the hydrogens; the acceptor has to start oxidized and gain them."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"NAD⁺ is a loosely bound cosubstrate that takes a hydride; it is not the riboflavin-derived cofactor described."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Heme is an iron porphyrin, not made from riboflavin, and its iron takes electrons, not hydrogen atoms."}],"rule":"The acceptor of 2 H starts oxidized and ends reduced: FAD → FADH₂, FMN → FMNH₂. Tightly bound and riboflavin-derived means a flavin.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Riboflavin-derived, tightly bound, carries 2 H → a flavin. Smaller than FAD → FMN. The acceptor is the oxidized form, so it gains the 2 H."},"exp":"The cofactor described is a **flavin** made from riboflavin but smaller than FAD: **FMN**. Like FAD in glucose oxidase, it **accepts 2 H (2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻)** and is **reduced to FMNH₂**; it then hands them to O₂ and returns to FMN.","how":{"ask":"Identify the bound cofactor from the clues, then decide whether taking the hydrogens reduces or oxidizes it.","know":"• **FAD** (glucose oxidase, succinate dehydrogenase) and **FMN** are flavins made from riboflavin; they are tightly bound and carry **2 H**: FAD → FADH₂, FMN → FMNH₂.\n• **NAD⁺/NADP⁺** are loosely bound cosubstrates that take a hydride (H⁻).\n• Gaining H (electrons) = **reduction**.","steps":[{"text":"Read the clues: riboflavin-derived, smaller than FAD, tightly bound.","hl":"smaller than FAD because it lacks FAD's AMP portion","calc":["→ FMN, a flavin"]},{"text":"NAD⁺ is not riboflavin-based and is not tightly bound; heme is an iron porphyrin.","strike":["NAD⁺; it gains two H","Heme; Fe gains two e⁻"]},{"text":"The acceptor gains the hydrogens, so it starts as FMN, not FMNH₂.","calc":["FMN + 2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻ → FMNH₂ (reduced)"],"strike":["FMNH₂; it loses two H"]},{"text":"Answer: FMN, which gains 2 H."}],"trap":"Picking the reduced form (FMNH₂ or FADH₂) as the acceptor: the acceptor has to start oxidized."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"made from riboflavin","note":"a flavin"},{"t":"held tightly within the enzyme","note":"not NAD⁺"},{"t":"accepts the two hydrogens","note":"gets reduced"}]},{"text":"**Flavins carry two hydrogens.**","calc":["FAD + 2 H → FADH₂","FMN + 2 H → FMNH₂"]},{"text":"**Also:** glucose oxidase uses bound FAD in the same way: the flavin takes two hydrogens from glucose, is reduced, and O₂ then reoxidizes it."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a bound flavin (FAD or FMN) accepts 2 H and is reduced to its H₂ form; O₂ or another acceptor reoxidizes it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-25a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:25#1","pe3-cp:25#2","pe5-cp:21#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:25"],"q":"A hormone enters its target cell and binds an intracellular steroid receptor. Which molecule has the ring skeleton of a steroid?","choices":["Estradiol","Epinephrine","Thyroxine","Prostaglandin E₂"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Epinephrine is an amine (catecholamine) hormone with one ring; one ring can't be a four-ring core."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Thyroxine has two benzene rings linked through an O, not four fused rings."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Prostaglandins come from a 20-C fatty acid; one five-membered ring, not four fused rings."}],"rule":"Steroid core: 6-6-6-5 fused ring system; sex hormones and cortisol are steroids, amines and eicosanoids are not.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Steroid = four fused rings, 6-6-6-5, like cholesterol. Sex hormones (estr-, andr-, -diol, -one) are steroids; catecholamines, thyroid hormones and eicosanoids aren't."},"exp":"A steroid is defined by its **6-6-6-5 fused ring core** (rings A, B and C of six atoms, ring D of five). Estradiol has it. Epinephrine has one benzene ring, thyroxine has two separate rings joined by an ether O, and prostaglandin E₂ has a single five-membered ring with two chains.","how":{"ask":"Look for four fused rings, three of six atoms and one of five, in each molecule.","know":"• **Steroid skeleton**: four **fused** rings, **6-6-6-5** (rings A, B, C, D), 17 ring carbons, derived from cholesterol.\n• Fused = neighboring rings share a bond.\n• Rings joined by a chain or an O are not fused, and a single ring is never a steroid.","steps":[{"text":"A steroid receptor binds molecules built on the steroid core.","hl":"steroid receptor"},{"text":"Draw the core to search for: three six-membered rings and one five-membered ring, all fused.","fig":["t3-steroid-abcd","cholesterol"],"calc":["rings: 6 + 6 + 6 + 5","fused: each pair shares one bond"]},{"text":"Estradiol has rings A–D (ring A aromatic): the 6-6-6-5 core."},{"text":"Epinephrine is a catecholamine with one benzene ring; thyroxine has two benzene rings linked through an O, not fused.","strike":["Epinephrine","Thyroxine"]},{"text":"Prostaglandin E₂ has a single five-membered ring with two side chains, a fatty-acid derivative.","strike":["Prostaglandin E₂"]},{"text":"Answer: estradiol."}],"trap":"Choosing any lipid-soluble or ring-containing hormone: only the fused 6-6-6-5 skeleton makes a steroid."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"steroid receptor","note":"needs a steroid"},{"t":"ring skeleton","note":"count and fuse the rings"}]},{"text":"**Know the core.** Four rings fused edge to edge: three six-membered (A, B, C) and one five-membered (D).","fig":"t3-steroid-abcd","calc":["6 + 6 + 6 + 5 = four rings, 17 ring C"]},{"text":"**Spot it in a drawing.** Find the five-membered ring, then check that it is fused to three six-membered rings in a row; extra groups (OH, C=O, a side chain) do not matter.","fig":"cholesterol"},{"text":"**Rule out look-alikes.** One ring, rings joined by a linker, or no rings at all: not a steroid."},{"text":"**Also:** every steroid hormone (estradiol, cortisol, progesterone) is built from cholesterol, which already has the 6-6-6-5 core; the body shortens its 8-carbon side chain (to 2 carbons in progesterone and cortisol, none in estradiol) and keeps rings A–D. Asked for a steroid's precursor, choose the lipid drawn with those four rings, not a fatty acid or another open-chain lipid.","fig":"cholesterol"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a steroid has four fused rings, three of six atoms and one of five; look for that 6-6-6-5 skeleton and ignore the groups on it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-26a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe3-cp:26#1","pe3-cp:26#2","pe3-cp:26#3","pe4-cp:40#1","pe6-cp:8#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:26"],"q":"A carbonate buffer (HCO₃⁻/CO₃²⁻, pKa 10.3) reads pH 10.8. What should be added to reach its greatest buffer capacity, and how must base:acid change?","choices":["NaHCO₃; ratio 3.2 → 1.0","Na₂CO₃; ratio 3.2 → 1.0","NaHCO₃; ratio 0.32 → 1.0","NaOH; ratio 0.32 → 1.0"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Na₂CO₃ adds more of the base form, so the ratio and the pH climb further above pKa instead of falling."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"The ratio is base over acid; with the pH above pKa it is greater than one now, not below one."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"NaOH turns HCO₃⁻ into CO₃²⁻, raising the ratio and the pH: the wrong direction, from a ratio read upside down."}],"rule":"Buffer capacity peaks at pH = pKa. If the pH is above pKa, add the acid form; if below, add the base form.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Capacity peaks at pH = pKa. pH 10.8 sits above 10.3, so there is too much base form: add the acid form, HCO₃⁻ (NaHCO₃), to pull pH down."},"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Titration of 30.0 mL of 0.040 M NaHCO₃ with 0.080 M NaOH","x":{"label":"Volume of NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":15,"step":3},"y":{"label":"pH","min":9,"max":12,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"pH","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[1.5,9.35],[3,9.7],[6,10.12],[7.5,10.3],[9,10.48],[11.4,10.8],[13.5,11.25]]}]},"exp":"**pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid])**. At pH 10.8 the ratio [CO₃²⁻]/[HCO₃⁻] = 10^0.5 ≈ 3.2. Capacity is greatest at **pH = pKa**, where the ratio is 1, so the pH must fall: add more of the **acid form**, NaHCO₃.","how":{"ask":"Decide whether the pH must rise or fall to reach pKa, find the base:acid ratio now and at pKa, then add the form that moves it that way.","know":"• **Henderson–Hasselbalch**: pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid]).\n• A buffer works best at **pH = pKa** ([base] = [acid]).\n• To **lower** pH, raise the share of the **acid** form; to raise pH, add the base form.","steps":[{"text":"Read the buffer pair and its pKa.","hl":"HCO₃⁻/CO₃²⁻, pKa 10.3","calc":["acid = HCO₃⁻, base = CO₃²⁻"]},{"text":"Find the current base:acid ratio.","hl":"pH 10.8","calc":["10.8 = 10.3 + log(ratio)","log(ratio) = 0.5","ratio = 10^0.5 ≈ **3.2**"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":11.4,"text":"now: pH 10.8","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"Best capacity is at pH = pKa = 10.3, ratio 1. The ratio must fall, so the pH must drop by 0.5: add the acid form.","calc":["pH 10.8 → 10.3: add HCO₃⁻ (as NaHCO₃)"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":7.5,"text":"pH = pKa 10.3","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"Na₂CO₃ and NaOH raise the base share and push the pH higher; a ratio below one would mean the pH is already under pKa.","strike":["Na₂CO₃; ratio 3.2 → 1.0","NaOH; ratio 0.32 → 1.0","NaHCO₃; ratio 0.32 → 1.0"]},{"text":"Answer: NaHCO₃, so the ratio falls from about 3.2 to 1.0."}],"trap":"Adding the base form because the pH must \"reach\" pKa: here the buffer is above its pKa, so it needs more acid form."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"pKa 10.3","note":"best buffering pH"},{"t":"pH 10.8","note":"current pH, above pKa"},{"t":"greatest buffer capacity","note":"pH = pKa"}]},{"text":"**Write Henderson–Hasselbalch.** It links pH to the base:acid ratio.","calc":["pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid])","10.8 − 10.3 = 0.5 → ratio ≈ 3.2"]},{"text":"**Aim for pH = pKa.** There the ratio is 1, and the buffer resists added acid and base equally well."},{"text":"**Lower pH = more acid form.** The pH is too high, so raise [HCO₃⁻]."},{"text":"**Also:** for a phosphate buffer, the acid form is H₂PO₄⁻ (the monosodium salt, NaH₂PO₄); adding it lowers the pH, while Na₂HPO₄ raises it."},{"text":"**Also:** a buffer works only within pKa ± 1, where the base:acid ratio stays between one-tenth and ten; check each pH against that window.","calc":["pKa 10.3 → useful from pH 9.3 to 11.3"]},{"text":"**Also:** write Henderson–Hasselbalch with the two forms named before plugging in: the conjugate base (A⁻, the salt form) goes on top and the weak acid (HA) on the bottom. Then undo the log to get the ratio.","calc":["pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA])","phosphate, pKa 7.2, target pH 6.9: log ratio = −0.3","[A⁻]/[HA] = 10^−0.3 ≈ 0.5, i.e. 1 : 2"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid]); to lower the pH add the acid form, to raise it add the base form, and capacity peaks at pH = pKa."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-27a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:27#1","pe3-cp:27#2","pe3-cp:27#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:27"],"q":"Glycine (75 Da) and leucine (131 Da) join by one peptide bond into the dipeptide shown, 188 Da. What mass is released?","choices":["18 Da","17 Da","206 Da","36 Da"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"Adding the two amino-acid masses gives the starting total, not what leaves; the dipeptide is lighter than that."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Ammonia is not lost: the amine N stays in the new amide bond; the carboxyl gives up OH and the amine gives up one H."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"One peptide bond releases one water, not one water per amino acid."}],"rule":"Each peptide bond is a condensation: one water leaves, so the dipeptide = sum of the amino acids − one water.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Lost mass = (75 + 131) − 188. Then check that it matches a small molecule you know."},"fig":"cpt-gly-leu-sites","exp":"Forming a peptide bond is a **condensation**: the carboxyl's OH and one H from the amine leave together as **H₂O**. Check: 75 + 131 = 206; 206 − 188 = **18 Da**, the mass of one water.","how":{"ask":"Find the mass lost when the two amino acids join, and name what left.","know":"• A peptide bond links the **carbonyl C** of one amino acid to the **N** of the next.\n• Forming it releases **one H₂O** (18 Da): a condensation (dehydration) reaction.\n• Dipeptide mass = sum of the amino acids − 18.","steps":[{"text":"Add the two amino-acid masses.","hl":"Glycine (75 Da) and leucine (131 Da)","calc":["75 + 131 = 206 Da"]},{"text":"Subtract the dipeptide's mass.","hl":"188 Da","calc":["206 − 188 = **18 Da** = H₂O"]},{"text":"206 Da is the starting total; one bond releases one water, not two; the N stays in the amide.","strike":["206 Da","36 Da","17 Da"]},{"text":"Answer: 18 Da."}],"trap":"Thinking the amine nitrogen leaves as NH₃: the N stays in the amide bond, and only H and OH leave, as water."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"Glycine (75 Da)","note":"amino acid 1"},{"t":"leucine (131 Da)","note":"amino acid 2"},{"t":"188 Da","note":"after joining"}]},{"text":"**Condensation.** The bond forms between glycine's carbonyl C and leucine's N; the carboxyl OH and an amine H leave together.","fig":"cpt-gly-leu-sites"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** every peptide bond formed releases one water, 18 Da."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-28a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe3-cp:28#1","pe3-cp:28#2","pe3-cp:28#3","pe3-cp:28#4"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:28"],"q":"A student is planning Michaelis–Menten measurements on a purified enzyme. Which condition is NOT required for valid data?","choices":["Initial rates measured","Constant buffered pH","Far less enzyme than substrate","Reaction run to equilibrium"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Initial rates (v₀) are the very data a Michaelis–Menten plot uses, so they are required."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Enzyme rates shift with pH, so a fixed, buffered pH is required for comparable rates."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"[E] ≪ [S] keeps free [S] at its set value, an assumption the Michaelis–Menten equation needs."}],"rule":"Michaelis–Menten needs initial rates under fixed conditions with [E] ≪ [S]; the odd one out is the condition that is not part of that setup.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Picture the end point: at equilibrium [S] has dropped and the net rate is zero, so no v₀ is left to measure. The other three are the standard set-up; equilibrium is the odd one."},"exp":"Michaelis–Menten analysis needs **initial rates (v₀)** under **steady state**, a **constant pH**, and **[E] ≪ [S]** so [S] stays at its set value. Letting the reaction reach **equilibrium** is not wanted: by then [S] has fallen, product has built up and the net rate is zero.","how":{"ask":"Check each condition against the assumptions behind v₀ = Vmax[S]/(Km + [S]).","know":"• **v₀ = Vmax[S]/(Km + [S])** assumes **initial rates** (little product, [S] ≈ starting value) and a **steady state** of ES.\n• **Constant pH** (a buffer) keeps the enzyme's charges and activity fixed.\n• **[E] ≪ [S]** so binding barely depletes free substrate.\n• At **equilibrium** the net rate is zero, so it gives no v₀.","steps":[{"text":"This is a NOT question: find the condition the analysis does not want.","hl":"NOT required"},{"text":"Initial rates are the data Michaelis–Menten uses: the slope at the start of the product-versus-time curve.","strike":["Initial rates measured"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Product formed over time in one enzyme run","x":{"label":"Time (min)","min":0,"max":30,"step":5},"y":{"label":"Product (μM)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Product","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[2,22],[5,46],[10,68],[15,80],[20,86],[25,88],[30,89]]}]}},{"text":"A buffered pH holds the enzyme's ionizable groups steady, and [E] ≪ [S] keeps free [S] at its set value.","strike":["Constant buffered pH","Far less enzyme than substrate"]},{"text":"At equilibrium the curve is flat: net rate zero, [S] no longer the starting value. That is not wanted."},{"text":"Answer: reaction run to equilibrium."}],"trap":"Thinking a reaction must be “complete” to be measured: Michaelis–Menten uses only the first, straight part of the curve."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Michaelis–Menten","note":"uses v₀"},{"t":"NOT required","note":"find the odd one out"}]},{"text":"**Initial rates, steady state.** Measure v₀ while [ES] is steady and little product has formed; later the curve bends over.","calc":["v₀ = slope at t ≈ 0"]},{"text":"**Also:** keep the pH constant with a buffer; a changing pH changes the enzyme's activity during the run."},{"text":"**Also:** keep [E] much less than [S], so the enzyme does not tie up a significant share of the substrate.","calc":["[E] ≪ [S] → free [S] ≈ total [S]"]},{"text":"**Also:** letting the reaction reach equilibrium is not wanted: the net rate there is zero and [S] has changed, so rates must be initial."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** valid Michaelis–Menten data use initial rates at steady state, constant pH and [E] ≪ [S]; never wait for equilibrium."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-29a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:29#1","pe3-cp:29#2","pe3-cp:29#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:29"],"passage":"Catalase protects cells from hydrogen peroxide, and its heme iron cycles between two forms. In the first step, E–Fe + H₂O₂ → E–Fe=O + H₂O. In the second step, E–Fe=O + H₂O₂ → E–Fe + H₂O + O₂. At the end of each cycle the enzyme is back in its starting form.","q":"Which is the overall balanced reaction that catalase catalyzes?","choices":["2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂","2H₂O₂ → H₂O + 2O₂","E–Fe + H₂O₂ → E–Fe=O + H₂O","2H₂O₂ + E–Fe → 2H₂O + O₂ + E–Fe=O"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"impossible","why":"Count H: four on the left, two on the right, so this equation is not balanced."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"This is only one step; the net reaction adds both steps and cancels what is regenerated."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"The cofactor is not used up: E–Fe=O made in one step is consumed in the next, so no enzyme form belongs in the net equation."}],"rule":"Add the steps, cancel every species found on both sides (bound cofactor forms included), then check atoms and charge.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A catalyst never appears in the net equation. Strike every choice with E–Fe, then keep the one that balances."},"exp":"Add the steps: E–Fe + 2 H₂O₂ + E–Fe=O → E–Fe=O + 2 H₂O + O₂ + E–Fe. **E–Fe and E–Fe=O appear on both sides and cancel**, leaving **2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂**. Check: 4 H and 4 O on each side.","how":{"ask":"Sum the two steps, cancel the enzyme forms that are regenerated, and check the balance.","know":"• **Net equation** = sum of the steps, with species on both sides cancelled.\n• An enzyme-bound cofactor that is regenerated (FAD/FADH₂, heme Fe/Fe=O) **does not appear** overall.\n• Check atoms and charge at the end.","steps":[{"text":"Write both steps together.","hl":"E–Fe=O + H₂O₂ → E–Fe + H₂O + O₂","calc":["E–Fe + H₂O₂ + E–Fe=O + H₂O₂ → E–Fe=O + H₂O + E–Fe + H₂O + O₂"]},{"text":"Cancel the enzyme forms on both sides.","calc":["2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂"],"strike":["E–Fe + H₂O₂ → E–Fe=O + H₂O","2H₂O₂ + E–Fe → 2H₂O + O₂ + E–Fe=O"]},{"text":"Check atoms: 4 H and 4 O on each side. The version with a single H₂O on the right has 4 H on the left but only 2 on the right.","strike":["2H₂O₂ → H₂O + 2O₂"]},{"text":"Answer: 2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂."}],"trap":"Leaving the enzyme in the net equation: whatever a cycle regenerates cancels out."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"cycles between two forms","note":"regenerated"},{"t":"back in its starting form","note":"cancels in the net equation"}]},{"text":"**Add, then cancel.**","calc":["step 1 + step 2","E–Fe and E–Fe=O cancel"]},{"text":"**Also:** a flavoenzyme cycle works the same way: E–FAD → E–FADH₂ → E–FAD cancels, leaving only the substrates and products."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to get a net equation, add the steps, cancel the regenerated cofactor, and check that atoms and charge balance."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-30a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:30#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:30"],"passage":"Many metal-chelating drugs, such as vorinostat, contain a hydroxamic acid group. A hydroxamic acid forms when an acyl group is attached to the nitrogen of hydroxylamine, NH₂–OH. Its N–OH oxygen and its C=O oxygen together grip a metal ion such as Zn²⁺.","q":"Which compound has the drugs' metal-binding group (Ph = phenyl)?","choices":["PhC(=O)NH–OH","PhC(=O)O–NH₂","PhCH=N–OH","PhC(=O)NH₂"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Here the acyl group is bonded to O, with NH₂ on that O; a hydroxamic acid has the acyl on N."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"No C=O: a hydroxamic acid is an acyl (amide-type) group, and C=N–OH is an oxime."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"No OH at all: 'hydroxamic' needs an OH on the nitrogen."}],"rule":"A hydroxamic acid is an amide whose nitrogen carries an OH: C=O bonded to N, and that N bonded to OH.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Hydroxamic acid = amide with OH on the N: C(=O)–NH–OH. Acyl on O = O-acyl hydroxylamine; C=N–OH = oxime; no N–OH = ordinary amide."},"exp":"A hydroxamic acid is an **amide whose nitrogen carries an OH**: R–C(=O)–N(OH)–R′, here with R = phenyl and R′ = H. Moving the acyl group to oxygen gives an O-acyl hydroxylamine, C=N–OH is an oxime, and an amide without the N–OH is a plain amide.","how":{"ask":"Look for a C=O bonded directly to an N that also bears an OH.","know":"• **Hydroxamic acid**: **R–C(=O)–N(OH)–R′**, an amide whose N carries an OH (R′ may be H).\n• Contrast: oxime R–CH=N–OH; O-acyl hydroxylamine R–C(=O)–O–NH₂; amide R–C(=O)–NH₂.","steps":[{"text":"Write the target pattern.","calc":["R–C(=O)–N(OH)–R′"]},{"text":"PhC(=O)NH–OH has C=O on N, and N bears OH: R = Ph, R′ = H.","hl":"PhC(=O)NH–OH"},{"text":"PhC(=O)O–NH₂ bonds the acyl group to O, not N; PhCH=N–OH has no C=O (an oxime); PhC(=O)NH₂ lacks the N–OH.","strike":["PhC(=O)O–NH₂","PhCH=N–OH","PhC(=O)NH₂"]},{"text":"Answer: PhC(=O)NH–OH."}],"trap":"Picking the O-acyl isomer: both contain C=O, N and O, but in a hydroxamic acid the carbonyl carbon is bonded to nitrogen."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"hydroxamic acid","note":"amide + N–OH"},{"t":"metal-chelating","note":"the C=O oxygen and the N–O oxygen bind the metal together"}]},{"text":"**Start from an amide.** R–C(=O)–NH–R′ is an amide."},{"text":"**Put OH on the nitrogen.** Replace the N–H with N–OH.","calc":["R–C(=O)–NH–R′ → R–C(=O)–N(OH)–R′"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a hydroxamic acid is R–C(=O)–N(OH)–R′: an amide whose N carries an OH."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-31a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:31#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:31"],"passage":"One oxidase can act on more than one substrate. At pH 7.0, one enzyme oxidizes substrate X with a rate constant of 5.6 s⁻¹ and substrate Y with one of 1.6 s⁻¹. Each rate equals the rate constant times the amount of substrate available.","q":"If both substrates are equally available, X is oxidized how many times as fast as Y?","choices":["0.29","3.5","4.0","7.2"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: X has the larger k, so X is faster and the ratio must be above 1; 0.29 is upside down."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"'Times as fast' asks for a ratio; 4.0 is the difference 5.6 − 1.6."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"'Times as fast' asks for a ratio; 7.2 is the sum 5.6 + 1.6."}],"rule":"'Times as fast' = larger rate constant ÷ smaller, so it is above 1. Round both constants to 1 digit for the ballpark.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Equal availability cancels, leaving k_X ÷ k_Y. 5.6 ÷ 1.6 = 56 ÷ 16 = 7 ÷ 2 = 3.5."},"exp":"With the same availability, everything but k cancels: rate X ÷ rate Y = k_X ÷ k_Y = 5.6 ÷ 1.6 = **3.5**. 0.29 is the ratio upside down, 4.0 the difference, and 7.2 the sum.","how":{"ask":"Write each rate as k × availability, then divide the two rates.","know":"• **rate = k × [substrate]** (first order in each).\n• With **equal availability**, the concentrations cancel: **rate₁/rate₂ = k₁/k₂**.\n• “How many times faster” is a ratio, not a difference.","steps":[{"text":"Pick out the two rate constants.","hl":"5.6 s⁻¹","calc":["k_X = 5.6 s⁻¹","k_Y = 1.6 s⁻¹"]},{"text":"Write the ratio of rates; equal availability cancels.","calc":["rate X ÷ rate Y = (k_X·[S]) ÷ (k_Y·[S])","= k_X ÷ k_Y"]},{"text":"Divide.","calc":["5.6 ÷ 1.6 = **3.5**"]},{"text":"0.29 is 1.6 ÷ 5.6 (upside down), 4.0 is 5.6 − 1.6, and 7.2 is 5.6 + 1.6.","strike":["0.29","4.0","7.2"]},{"text":"Answer: 3.5."}],"trap":"Subtracting the rate constants (4.0): “times faster” asks for a ratio."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"5.6 s⁻¹","note":"k for X"},{"t":"1.6 s⁻¹","note":"k for Y"},{"t":"equally available","note":"concentrations cancel"}]},{"text":"**Rate = k × amount.** With the same amount of each substrate, only k differs.","calc":["rate X/rate Y = k_X/k_Y"]},{"text":"**Divide, larger over smaller** for “how many times faster”.","calc":["5.6 ÷ 1.6 = 3.5"]},{"text":"**Also:** rate constants change with pH; from a table, take both k values at the stated pH before dividing."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** with equal availability, the ratio of two rates equals the ratio of their rate constants."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-33a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:33#1","pe3-cp:33#2","pe3-cp:33#3","pe3-cp:33#4"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:33"],"passage":"DNA-binding proteins often line their binding grooves with residues that pair with the negative phosphate backbone. A biochemist needs two residues whose side chains are basic and hydrophilic to line a DNA-binding groove. At body pH, acidic side chains carry a negative charge, and nonpolar side chains carry none.","q":"To meet the passage's goal, which pair should she choose?","choices":["Lysine and histidine","Aspartate and glutamate","Leucine and valine","Phenylalanine and isoleucine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Aspartate and glutamate carry side-chain carboxylates: acidic, the opposite of basic."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Leu and Val side chains are only C and H: nonpolar, not hydrophilic."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Phe and Ile side chains are pure hydrocarbon: nonpolar, not hydrophilic."}],"rule":"Basic side chains carry extra N (Lys, Arg, His); acidic ones carry COO⁻ (Asp, Glu); side chains of only C and H are nonpolar.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Basic side chains = the three that take H⁺: Lys (amine), Arg (guanidine), His (imidazole). Asp/Glu are acidic; Leu, Val, Phe, Ile are nonpolar hydrocarbons. Only Lys + His fits."},"exp":"**Lysine, arginine and histidine** have nitrogen-containing side chains that accept protons: they are **basic and hydrophilic**. Aspartate and glutamate carry carboxylates (acidic); leucine, valine, phenylalanine and isoleucine have hydrocarbon side chains (nonpolar).","how":{"ask":"Sort each amino acid by its side chain: basic, acidic or nonpolar.","know":"• **Basic, hydrophilic**: Lys, Arg, His (N-containing side chains that take H⁺).\n• **Acidic**: Asp, Glu (side-chain COOH/COO⁻).\n• **Nonpolar**: Leu, Ile, Val, Phe (hydrocarbon side chains).\n• Arg's guanidino side chain (pKa ≈ 12.5) is protonated at pH 7.4, so free Arg is **+1** overall.","steps":[{"text":"The groove needs basic, water-loving side chains.","hl":"basic and hydrophilic"},{"text":"Lysine's side chain ends in –NH₃⁺ and histidine's in an imidazole: both basic.","hl":"Lysine and histidine","fig":"lysine"},{"text":"Aspartate and glutamate have side-chain carboxylates, so they are acidic.","strike":["Aspartate and glutamate"]},{"text":"Leucine, valine, phenylalanine and isoleucine have only C and H in their side chains, so they are nonpolar.","strike":["Leucine and valine","Phenylalanine and isoleucine"]},{"text":"Also check arginine's charge at pH 7.4: compare the pH with each pKa.","calc":["α-COOH (≈2): pH above → COO⁻, −1","α-NH₂ (≈9): pH below → NH₃⁺, +1","guanidino (≈12.5): pH below → protonated, +1","net = −1 + 1 + 1 = **+1**"]},{"text":"Answer: lysine and histidine."}],"trap":"Calling histidine nonpolar because of its ring: its imidazole nitrogens take H⁺, so it is grouped with the basic amino acids."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"basic and hydrophilic","note":"N-containing side chains"},{"t":"DNA-binding groove","note":"DNA is negatively charged"}]},{"text":"**Basic three.** Lys, Arg and His have side chains that accept protons; they are basic and hydrophilic."},{"text":"**Acidic two.** Asp and Glu have side-chain carboxyl groups; they are acidic and negatively charged at pH 7.4."},{"text":"**Nonpolar group.** Leu, Ile, Val and Phe have hydrocarbon side chains; they are hydrophobic."},{"text":"**Also:** at pH 7.4 arginine's side chain is fully protonated (pKa far above 7.4), so with COO⁻ and NH₃⁺ the molecule carries a net +1 charge."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Lys, Arg and His are basic and hydrophilic; Asp and Glu are acidic; Leu, Ile, Val and Phe are nonpolar."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-34a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:34#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:34"],"passage":"Household ammonia cleaners are dilute aqueous NH₃ solutions. A diluted household ammonia cleaner measures pH 11.3 at room temperature. At this temperature, pH + pOH = 14.","q":"What is the hydroxide ion concentration, [OH⁻], in the cleaner?","choices":["2.0 × 10⁻³ M","5.0 × 10⁻¹² M","2.7 × 10⁻³ M","5.0 × 10⁻³ M"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"impossible","why":"Basic solution (pH > 7) needs [OH⁻] > 10⁻⁷ M; 5.0 × 10⁻¹² M is the [H⁺]."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"Digits trap: pOH 2.7 means 10^−2.7, not 2.7 × 10⁻³."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"The 5.0 digits belong to [H⁺]; pasting them onto the pOH's power of ten mixes two different ions."}],"rule":"pOH = 14 − pH, then [OH⁻] = 10^−pOH. The whole-number part of the pOH tells you which two powers of 10 bracket [OH⁻].","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"pOH = 14 − 11.3 = 2.7. Anchor log 2 ≈ 0.3: 10⁻²·⁷ = 10⁰·³ × 10⁻³ = 2 × 10⁻³ M."},"exp":"At room temperature **pOH = 14 − pH** = 14 − 11.3 = 2.7, and **[OH⁻] = 10^−pOH** = 10^−2.7 ≈ 2.0 × 10⁻³ M. The value 5.0 × 10⁻¹² M is [H⁺], not [OH⁻].","how":{"ask":"Convert pH to pOH, then pOH to [OH⁻].","know":"• At 25 °C, **pH + pOH = 14** (Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴).\n• **[OH⁻] = 10^−pOH**; [H⁺] = 10^−pH.\n• 10^−2.7 = 10^0.3 × 10⁻³ ≈ 2 × 10⁻³.","steps":[{"text":"Start from the pH.","hl":"pH 11.3","calc":["pOH = 14 − 11.3 = **2.7**"]},{"text":"Undo the log.","calc":["[OH⁻] = 10^−2.7","= 10^0.3 × 10⁻³","≈ **2.0 × 10⁻³ M**"]},{"text":"5.0 × 10⁻¹² M is 10^−11.3, the [H⁺]; 2.7 × 10⁻³ M puts the pOH in as the digits; 5.0 × 10⁻³ M mixes the [H⁺] digits with the pOH exponent.","strike":["5.0 × 10⁻¹² M","2.7 × 10⁻³ M","5.0 × 10⁻³ M"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.0 × 10⁻³ M."}],"trap":"Computing 10^−pH and calling it [OH⁻]: that is [H⁺]. A basic solution has [OH⁻] larger than [H⁺]."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"pH 11.3","note":"basic, so [OH⁻] > [H⁺]"},{"t":"room temperature","note":"pH + pOH = 14"},{"t":"[OH⁻]","note":"use pOH, not pH"}]},{"text":"**Switch to pOH.**","calc":["pOH = 14 − pH = 14 − 11.3 = 2.7"]},{"text":"**Undo the log.** Split the exponent into a whole number and a decimal.","calc":["10^−2.7 = 10^0.3 × 10⁻³ ≈ 2.0 × 10⁻³ M"]},{"text":"**Check.** [H⁺][OH⁻] should be about 10⁻¹⁴.","calc":["(5.0 × 10⁻¹²)(2.0 × 10⁻³) = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ ✓"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pOH = 14 − pH, then [OH⁻] = 10^−pOH."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-35a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:35#1","pe3-cp:36#1","pe3-cp:36#2","pe3-cp:36#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:35"],"passage":"Peroxidases use H₂O₂ to oxidize phenols one electron at a time. A peroxidase pulls one proton and one electron off the O–H group of 4-methylphenol. Two of the resulting species can then couple to each other.","q":"Which species results from that step?","choices":["4-Methylphenoxyl radical","4-Methylphenoxide anion","4-Methylphenol radical cation","4-Methylbenzyl cation"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Losing only H⁺ gives the anion; the electron was removed too, so H⁺ + e⁻ (a neutral H•) leaves no charge."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Losing only e⁻ gives a radical cation; the proton left too, so the charge cancels."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Nothing leaves the CH₃ group, so no benzyl cation forms; the change is at the O–H."}],"rule":"H⁺ + e⁻ = H•: removing a hydrogen atom leaves a neutral radical; removing only H⁺ leaves an anion, only e⁻ a radical cation.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"H⁺ + e⁻ = one H atom. Take H• off O–H and the O is left neutral with one unpaired electron: phenoxyl radical. H⁺ alone → anion; e⁻ alone → radical cation."},"exp":"A proton plus an electron is one **H• atom**. Once H• is gone from Ar–OH, the O keeps a **single, unpaired electron**: the **phenoxyl radical Ar–O•**, neutral. Losing only H⁺ would give the phenoxide anion; losing only an electron gives a radical cation; a benzyl cation would need loss from the CH₃ group.","how":{"ask":"Add up what leaves (H⁺ + e⁻) and see what the oxygen keeps.","know":"• **H⁺ + e⁻ = H•** (one hydrogen atom).\n• **Ar–OH − H• → Ar–O•**, a **phenoxyl radical** (neutral, unpaired electron on O).\n• Ar–OH − H⁺ → Ar–O⁻ (anion); Ar–OH − e⁻ → Ar–OH•⁺ (radical cation).\n• **Peroxidases** oxidize **phenols** this way.","steps":[{"text":"Both a proton and an electron leave from the O–H.","hl":"one proton and one electron","calc":["H⁺ + e⁻ = H•"]},{"text":"Remove H• from the phenol: O keeps one electron of the old O–H bond.","fig":"phenol","calc":["Ar–OH → Ar–O• + (H⁺ + e⁻)","charge: 0 → 0, one unpaired electron"]},{"text":"Losing only H⁺ gives the anion; losing only e⁻ gives a radical cation.","strike":["4-Methylphenoxide anion","4-Methylphenol radical cation"]},{"text":"Nothing leaves the CH₃ group, so no benzyl cation forms.","strike":["4-Methylbenzyl cation"]},{"text":"Answer: the 4-methylphenoxyl radical."}],"trap":"Stopping at deprotonation (phenoxide): the electron leaves too, so the oxygen ends neutral with one unpaired electron."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"peroxidase","note":"oxidizes phenols"},{"t":"one proton and one electron","note":"= one H atom"},{"t":"4-methylphenol","note":"a phenol, like Tyr's side chain"}]},{"text":"**Count what leaves.** H⁺ + e⁻ = H•, so the O–H loses a whole hydrogen atom.","calc":["Ar–O–H → Ar–O• + H•"]},{"text":"**Also:** peroxidases act on phenols: they use H₂O₂ to pull H• from an aromatic O–H."},{"text":"**Also:** tyrosine is the amino acid with a phenol side chain, so it is the residue a peroxidase can oxidize (to a tyrosyl radical).","fig":"tyrosine-sc"},{"text":"**Also:** the look-alikes are not phenols: lysine's side chain ends in an amine, leucine's is an alkyl group, and glutamine's ends in an amide.","fig":["lysine-sc","leucine-sc","glutamine-sc"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** removing H• (H⁺ + e⁻) from a phenol gives a phenoxyl radical, Ar–O•; peroxidases do this to phenols such as Tyr's side chain."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-36a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:36#1","pe3-cp:36#2","pe3-cp:36#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:36"],"passage":"Some oxidizing enzymes cross-link proteins through their side chains. One such enzyme removes a hydrogen atom from an S–H group, leaving a sulfur-centered radical; two such radicals then join into an S–S bond. The enzyme cannot remove hydrogen from O–H, N–H or C–H bonds, and it does not act on a sulfur that lacks an H.","q":"Which amino-acid side chain could the enzyme act on?","choices":["Cysteine","Methionine","Serine","Lysine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Methionine's sulfur is a thioether, C–S–C, with no S–H to lose."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Serine's side chain ends in an O–H, an alcohol, which the enzyme cannot use."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Lysine ends in an amine, with N–H bonds, not a thiol."}],"rule":"Match the group the enzyme needs to the side chain: thiol = Cys, thioether = Met, alcohol = Ser/Thr, phenol = Tyr, amine = Lys, amide = Asn/Gln.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Only one standard amino acid has an S–H: find it."},"choiceFigs":["cysteine-sc","methionine-sc","serine-sc","lysine-sc"],"exp":"The enzyme needs an **S–H (thiol)**. **Cysteine** is the only amino acid with a thiol side chain, –CH₂SH. Methionine has sulfur but as a **thioether** (no S–H); serine has an O–H; lysine has N–H bonds.","how":{"ask":"Identify the group the enzyme acts on, then find the side chain that carries it.","know":"• Side-chain groups: **Cys** thiol (–SH); **Met** thioether (–S–CH₃); **Ser/Thr** alcohol; **Tyr** phenol; **Lys** amine; **Asn/Gln** amide; **Leu** alkyl.\n• A **peroxidase** removes H from a **phenol**, so among amino acids it acts on **Tyr**.","steps":[{"text":"The enzyme needs an S–H group.","hl":"removes a hydrogen atom from an S–H group"},{"text":"Scan each side chain: only cysteine carries –SH.","calc":["Cys: –CH₂–SH ✓"]},{"text":"Methionine's S has no H; serine is O–H; lysine is N–H.","strike":["Methionine","Serine","Lysine"]},{"text":"Answer: cysteine."}],"trap":"Choosing methionine because it contains sulfur: its sulfur is bonded to two carbons, with no S–H."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"S–H group","note":"thiol"},{"t":"does not act on a sulfur that lacks an H","note":"rules out a thioether"}]},{"text":"**Group → residue.**","calc":["S–H → Cys","C–S–C → Met","O–H → Ser","N–H → Lys"]},{"text":"**Also:** a peroxidase removes H from a phenol O–H, so the residue it attacks is tyrosine, not lysine (amine), leucine (alkyl) or glutamine (amide).","fig":"tyrosine-sc"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** find the functional group the enzyme acts on, then pick the one side chain that carries it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-37a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:37#1","pe6-cp:42#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:37"],"passage":"Many plant lectins need a bound Ca²⁺ ion before they can grip sugars. In one lectin, the ion is held by several nearby residues, including the side chain of an asparagine. The asparagine side chain, shown, ends in a carboxamide, –C(=O)NH₂. A 2+ metal ion is drawn to an atom that carries a partial negative charge and an available lone pair.","q":"Which side-chain atom of the asparagine is best placed to bind the Ca²⁺?","choices":["The C=O oxygen","The NH₂ nitrogen","The C=O carbon","The β-carbon"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"A CH₂ carbon has no lone pair to donate, so it cannot coordinate a metal ion."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The carbonyl carbon carries a partial positive charge and has no lone pair, so it repels a cation."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"An amide N's lone pair is delocalized into the C=O, which makes the N a poor donor and the O electron-rich."}],"rule":"Ca²⁺ binds lone-pair oxygens: carboxylate O (Asp, Glu), carbonyl O (backbone, Asn, Gln) and water; not amide N, and never carbon.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ca²⁺ is a hard cation that favors oxygen donors. Pick the electron-rich O."},"fig":"asparagine-sc","exp":"Ca²⁺ is held by **oxygen** lone pairs: carboxylate O of Asp/Glu and **carbonyl O** of the backbone or of Asn/Gln side chains. In the amide, the N lone pair is delocalized into the C=O, which makes the **carbonyl oxygen** electron-rich (δ−) and the best donor.","how":{"ask":"Find the atom with an available lone pair and a partial negative charge.","know":"• **Ca²⁺ binds oxygen**: carboxylate O (Asp, Glu), carbonyl O (backbone, Asn, Gln), water.\n• Amide N lone pairs are tied up in resonance with the C=O.\n• Carbon atoms have no lone pair to give a metal.","steps":[{"text":"Read the side chain.","hl":"–C(=O)NH₂","calc":["–CH₂–C(=O)–NH₂"]},{"text":"Carbons have no lone pair, and the C=O carbon is δ+.","strike":["The β-carbon","The C=O carbon"]},{"text":"Amide resonance pulls the N lone pair toward O, so the O is the electron-rich donor.","calc":["O=C–NH₂ ↔ ⁻O–C=NH₂⁺"],"strike":["The NH₂ nitrogen"]},{"text":"Answer: the C=O oxygen."}],"trap":"Picking the nitrogen because amines bind metals: an amide N is not basic; its lone pair is shared with the carbonyl."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"held by several nearby residues","note":"coordination"},{"t":"–C(=O)NH₂","note":"carboxamide"},{"t":"partial negative charge and an available lone pair","note":"what a cation needs"}]},{"text":"**Oxygen donors.** Carbonyl and carboxylate oxygens point lone pairs at the metal.","fig":"asparagine-sc"},{"text":"**Also:** aspartate and glutamate bind Ca²⁺ or Zn²⁺ through their carboxylate oxygens; lowering the pH protonates those groups and releases the metal."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** metal cations bind lone-pair donors with partial negative charge, carboxylate and carbonyl oxygens first."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-38a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:38#1","pe3-cp:38#2","pe3-cp:38#3","pe5-cp:14#2","pe5-cp:14#3","pe5-cp:14#1","pe5-cp:14#4"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:38"],"passage":"Horseradish peroxidase carries a heme cofactor, an iron ion held in a porphyrin ring. Heme's porphyrin ring is built by joining four aromatic rings of the same type through one-carbon bridges. Each building-block ring offers one nitrogen toward the central iron.","q":"Which ring is that building block?","choices":["Five-membered, one N","Five-membered, two N","Six-membered, one N","Six-membered, two N"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Five-membered with two N is imidazole, histidine's ring; it binds the heme iron but isn't part of the porphyrin."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Six-membered with one N is pyridine; a porphyrin is made of pyrroles, which are five-membered."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Six-membered with two N is a pyrimidine, a nucleobase ring, not a porphyrin unit."}],"rule":"A porphyrin is four pyrrole rings joined by CH bridges. Know the rings: pyrrole, imidazole, pyridine, pyrimidine.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Porphyrin = 4 pyrroles. Ring key: pyrrole 5/1 N, imidazole 5/2 N, pyridine 6/1 N, pyrimidine 6/2 N. So five-membered, one N."},"exp":"A porphyrin is four **pyrrole** rings linked by =CH– bridges, and a pyrrole is **five-membered with one N**. The five-membered ring with two N is imidazole (histidine's side chain, which binds the heme iron from below); six-membered with one N is pyridine, and with two N a pyrimidine.","how":{"ask":"Name the unit heme is made of, then describe that ring by its size and its count of nitrogens.","know":"• **Heme** = a porphyrin ring (four **pyrrole** units joined by =CH– bridges) with Fe²⁺ held by the four inward-facing N.\n• **Pyrrole**: 5 ring atoms, **1 N** (an N–H).\n• **Imidazole**: 5 ring atoms, **2 N**; the side chain of **His**, whose free N binds the heme Fe.\n• **Pyridine**: 6 ring atoms, 1 N. **Pyrimidine**: 6 ring atoms, 2 N.","steps":[{"text":"The question asks for the repeating unit of the porphyrin ring.","hl":"four aromatic rings of the same type"},{"text":"That unit is pyrrole. Count its ring atoms and its nitrogens.","fig":"pyrrole","calc":["ring atoms: 4 C + 1 N = **5**","nitrogens: **1** (N–H)"]},{"text":"Five-membered with two N is imidazole, histidine's ring. It is not part of the porphyrin; it is the residue whose free N lone pair binds the heme iron from below.","fig":["imidazole","t3-his-ring"],"strike":["Five-membered, two N"]},{"text":"Six-membered rings are pyridine (one N) and pyrimidine (two N); neither builds a porphyrin.","fig":"pyridine","strike":["Six-membered, one N","Six-membered, two N"]},{"text":"Answer: five-membered, one N (pyrrole)."}],"trap":"Picking imidazole because histidine is drawn next to heme: His only binds the iron; the ring itself is made of pyrroles."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"porphyrin ring","note":"the ring of heme"},{"t":"four aromatic rings of the same type","note":"the repeating unit"}]},{"text":"**Heme = porphyrin + Fe.** The porphyrin is four pyrrole rings joined by one-carbon bridges, with their four N pointing at the central iron."},{"text":"**Tell the rings apart by size and N count.**","fig":["pyrrole","imidazole","pyridine"],"calc":["pyrrole: 5 atoms, 1 N","imidazole: 5 atoms, 2 N","pyridine: 6 atoms, 1 N"]},{"text":"**Also:** imidazole (two N) is the ring on histidine's R group; in heme proteins, His binds the heme iron through its N that carries a free lone pair.","fig":"t3-his-ring"},{"text":"**Also:** to name any ring, count its atoms first (5 or 6), then count the nitrogens (1 or 2)."},{"text":"**Also:** to write a ring's formula, give every ring atom its full set of bonds: each C makes four (fill with H), each N three. Pyridine (6 atoms, 1 N with no H) is C₅H₅N; pyrrole (5 atoms, 1 N–H) is C₄H₅N. Imidazole works the same way: 5 ring atoms, two of them N (one N–H, one with only a lone pair); count its C and H as above.","calc":["pyridine: 5 C–H, N has only a lone pair → C₅H₅N","pyridine mass: 5(12) + 5(1) + 14 = 79 g/mol","pyrrole: 4 C–H + 1 N–H → C₄H₅N"]},{"text":"**Also:** to write a ring's formula, give every ring atom its full set of bonds: pyrrole has four C–H and one N–H, so it is C₄H₅N; imidazole is C₃H₄N₂ and pyridine C₅H₅N."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** heme's porphyrin is four pyrroles (5 atoms, 1 N); imidazole (5 atoms, 2 N) is His, which binds the iron; pyridine has 6 atoms and 1 N."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-39a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:39#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:39"],"passage":"Bioimpedance devices estimate body composition from how easily tissues carry current. At 37 °C, muscle has a conductivity of 2.5 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹ and fat has 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ (Ω·cm)⁻¹. Resistivity sets how much a tissue resists current for a given length and cross-section.","q":"From the passage's measurements, which value is muscle's resistivity?","choices":["400 Ω·cm","2500 Ω·cm","40 Ω·cm","2.5 × 10⁻³ Ω·cm"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Decimal slipped by 10: that is 1 ÷ 0.025, not 1 ÷ 0.0025."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"ρ = 1/σ turns a small σ into a big ρ; keeping σ's number and swapping only the unit is wrong."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Wrong tissue: 2500 Ω·cm is 1 ÷ (4.0 × 10⁻⁴), fat's value; the question asks about muscle."}],"rule":"Resistivity = 1 ÷ conductivity, for the tissue asked about. Write the conductivity as 1 digit × a power of 10, then flip both parts.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Answer wants Ω·cm and σ is in (Ω·cm)⁻¹, so just invert: 1 ÷ (2.5 × 10⁻³) = 1000 ÷ 2.5 = 400 Ω·cm."},"exp":"**Resistivity is the reciprocal of conductivity: ρ = 1/σ.** For muscle, ρ = 1 ÷ (2.5 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹) = **400 Ω·cm**; taking 1/σ also flips the (Ω·cm)⁻¹ unit to Ω·cm. 2500 Ω·cm belongs to fat.","how":{"ask":"Pick the muscle value and take its reciprocal, unit included.","know":"• **ρ = 1/σ**: resistivity and conductivity are reciprocals.\n• Units flip too: (Ω·cm)⁻¹ ↔ Ω·cm.\n• A good conductor (large σ) has a small ρ.","steps":[{"text":"Use the muscle value, not the fat value. 2500 Ω·cm is fat's resistivity (1 ÷ 4.0 × 10⁻⁴).","hl":"2.5 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹","strike":["2500 Ω·cm"]},{"text":"Take the reciprocal.","calc":["ρ = 1 ÷ σ","ρ = 1 ÷ (2.5 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹)","ρ = 0.4 × 10³ = **400 Ω·cm**"]},{"text":"2.5 × 10⁻³ Ω·cm keeps σ's number and only swaps the unit. 40 Ω·cm misplaces the decimal (1 ÷ 0.025).","strike":["2.5 × 10⁻³ Ω·cm","40 Ω·cm"]},{"text":"Answer: 400 Ω·cm."}],"trap":"Copying the conductivity value and writing Ω·cm after it: an inverted unit, (Ω·cm)⁻¹, means you must take the reciprocal of the number too."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the right number**.","marks":[{"t":"muscle has a conductivity of 2.5 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹","note":"σ for muscle"},{"t":"fat has 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ (Ω·cm)⁻¹","note":"not needed"},{"t":"muscle's resistivity","note":"want ρ"}]},{"text":"**Reciprocals.** Conductivity measures how easily current flows; resistivity measures how hard it is. One is 1 over the other.","calc":["ρ = 1/σ","unit: 1 ÷ (Ω·cm)⁻¹ = Ω·cm"]},{"text":"**Compute.**","calc":["1 ÷ 0.0025 = 400","ρ = 400 Ω·cm"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ρ = 1/σ, and the unit flips from (Ω·cm)⁻¹ to Ω·cm; read the value for the material asked about."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-40a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:40#1","pe3-cp:40#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:40"],"passage":"Some soil bacteria degrade alkenes in two enzyme steps, drawn in order. Enzyme A adds water across the C=C of (E)-hept-3-ene to give heptan-4-ol; no bond in the substrate is broken by the water. Enzyme B then consumes O₂ and converts heptan-4-ol to heptan-4-one, releasing H₂O₂.","q":"To which classes do enzyme A and enzyme B belong?","choices":["Lyase, then oxidoreductase","Hydrolase, then oxidoreductase","Oxidoreductase, then lyase","Lyase, then transferase"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Order reversed: the O₂-consuming step comes second, after water is added."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A hydrolase uses water to split a bond; here water adds across a C=C and nothing is split."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Consuming O₂ to turn an alcohol into a ketone is oxidation, not moving a group between molecules."}],"rule":"Name each step by its change: water splits a bond → hydrolase; water adds to C=C → lyase; O₂ used or H removed → oxidoreductase; methyl moved → transferase.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"O₂ consumed = oxidoreductase, every time. Then decide whether water broke a bond (hydrolase) or just added across C=C (lyase)."},"fig":["e-3-heptene","4-heptanol","4-heptanone"],"exp":"**Enzyme A** adds H₂O **across a C=C** without breaking a bond: a hydratase, which is a **lyase**. **Enzyme B** uses **O₂** to remove two hydrogens, turning the alcohol into a ketone: an **oxidoreductase**. Moving a methyl group from a donor such as SAM would instead be a transferase.","how":{"ask":"Classify each step by its chemical change, in order.","know":"• **Transferase**: moves a group such as CH₃ from one molecule to another.\n• **Oxidoreductase**: redox; O₂ consumed or hydrogens removed (CH₃ → CH₂OH, CH–OH → C=O).\n• **Hydrolase**: water splits a bond.\n• **Lyase**: adds or removes a group to form or break a double bond, with no hydrolysis.","steps":[{"text":"Step A: water adds across the C=C, and nothing is split.","hl":"no bond in the substrate is broken by the water","calc":["alkene + H₂O → alcohol: lyase (hydratase)"],"strike":["Hydrolase, then oxidoreductase"]},{"text":"Step B: O₂ is consumed and the alcohol loses 2 H.","hl":"consumes O₂","calc":["CH–OH → C=O: oxidoreductase"],"strike":["Lyase, then transferase"]},{"text":"Keep the order: A comes first.","strike":["Oxidoreductase, then lyase"]},{"text":"Answer: lyase, then oxidoreductase."}],"trap":"Calling any step that uses water a hydrolase: hydrolysis splits a bond, while adding water across a double bond is a lyase reaction."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"adds water across the C=C","note":"lyase"},{"t":"consumes O₂","note":"oxidoreductase"},{"t":"drawn in order","note":"A, then B"}]},{"text":"**Read the drawing step by step.**","fig":["e-3-heptene","4-heptanol","4-heptanone"]},{"text":"**Also:** if a step moved a methyl group from SAM onto a ring, it would be a transferase; if O₂ then turned that CH₃ into CH₂OH, that second step would be an oxidoreductase."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** classify each step by what changed, and keep the order the pathway gives."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-41a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:41#1","pe3-cp:41#2","pe3-cp:41#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:41"],"passage":"A small protein domain switches between a native form, N, and a denatured form, D: N ⇌ D. Its tryptophan fluorescence changes when it denatures, so the fraction denatured can be measured. At 300 K, 12% of the molecules are denatured at equilibrium. ΔG°′ refers to the reaction as written.","q":"What is ΔG°′ for N ⇌ D, and what sign does it have?","choices":["−R·T·ln([D]/[N]); positive","−R·T·ln([N]/[D]); negative","−R·T·ln([D]/[N]); negative","−R·T·e^([D]/[N]); positive"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"K is products over reactants, D over N; flipping it to N/D also flips the sign."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"With fewer than half denatured, D/N is below one, so ln K is negative and −RT ln K is positive; the sign step was skipped."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The relation uses the natural log of K, not e raised to K; e^x is the inverse operation."}],"rule":"For A ⇌ B, K = [B]/[A] and ΔG°′ = −RT ln K: a K below 1 (less than half converted) gives a positive ΔG°′.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"12% denatured → K = 12/88 < 1 → ln K < 0 → ΔG°′ > 0. Then keep K = [D]/[N] with ln, not e."},"exp":"For N ⇌ D, **K = [D]/[N]** = 12/88 ≈ 0.14. **ΔG°′ = −RT ln K**; since K < 1, ln K < 0, so ΔG°′ is **positive**: denaturation is unfavorable at 300 K.","how":{"ask":"Write K for N ⇌ D, put it into ΔG°′ = −RT ln K, then get the sign from the measured fraction.","know":"• K = products/reactants as written: **K = [D]/[N]**.\n• **ΔG°′ = −RT ln K** (natural log, never e^K).\n• K < 1 → ΔG°′ > 0; K > 1 → ΔG°′ < 0.","steps":[{"text":"Write K for the reaction as written.","hl":"N ⇌ D","calc":["K = [D]/[N]"],"strike":["−R·T·ln([N]/[D]); negative"]},{"text":"Use ln, not e.","calc":["ΔG°′ = −RT ln([D]/[N])"],"strike":["−R·T·e^([D]/[N]); positive"]},{"text":"Get the sign from the data.","hl":"12% of the molecules are denatured","calc":["K = 12/88 ≈ 0.14 < 1","ln K < 0 → ΔG°′ > 0"],"strike":["−R·T·ln([D]/[N]); negative"]},{"text":"Answer: −R·T·ln([D]/[N]), positive."}],"trap":"Reading \"less than half denatured\" as a negative ΔG°′: K < 1 makes ln K negative, and the minus sign in front makes ΔG°′ positive."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"N ⇌ D","note":"product = D"},{"t":"12% of the molecules are denatured","note":"K < 1"}]},{"text":"**K first.**","calc":["K = [D]/[N] = 12/88 ≈ 0.14"]},{"text":"**Sign from K.** ln of a number below 1 is negative, so −RT ln K is positive."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ΔG°′ = −RT ln K with K = products/reactants; K < 1 means ΔG°′ > 0."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-42a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:42#1","pe3-cp:42#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:42"],"passage":"Some DNA folds hold together only while certain bases are protonated, so they unfold as the pH rises. Variant R of a DNA fold unfolds between pH 6.3 and 6.7, with pK 6.5; variant S unfolds between pH 5.8 and 8.6, with pK 7.2. The pK is the pH at which half the molecules are folded.","q":"Which is more cooperative, and what is its pK?","choices":["R; pK 6.5","S; pK 7.2","R; pK 7.2","S; pK 6.5"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Narrow range = steep = cooperative: R spans 0.4 pH units, S spans 2.8, so not S."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"S has the broad 2.8-unit transition, so it is the less cooperative one."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"7.2 is S's pK pasted onto R; each variant keeps its own midpoint."}],"rule":"Cooperative = sharp: the narrower the pH range of the transition, the more cooperative. The pK is that curve's own midpoint.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Cooperative = steep, all-or-none: narrowest window wins. R spans 0.4 pH units, S spans 2.8, so R; report R's own midpoint, pK 6.5."},"exp":"Cooperativity is read from **steepness**: R goes from folded to unfolded within 0.4 pH units, S over 2.8 units. The narrow, steep transition (R) is the more cooperative, all-or-none one, and its midpoint, its pK, is 6.5. The pK of S (7.2) says where S unfolds, not how sharply.","how":{"ask":"Compare the width of each transition, then read the pK of the narrower one.","know":"• A **cooperative** transition is all-or-none: the structure unfolds over a **narrow** range, so the curve is **steep**.\n• A broad, shallow curve means a gradual, less cooperative change.\n• The **midpoint** (half folded) gives the pK (or Tm); it tells **where**, not **how sharply**.","steps":[{"text":"Read each variant's range.","hl":"between pH 6.3 and 6.7","calc":["R: 6.7 − 6.3 = **0.4** pH units","S: 8.6 − 5.8 = **2.8** pH units"]},{"text":"Plot both: R drops like a cliff, S slopes gently.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Fraction folded of two DNA variants versus pH","x":{"label":"pH","min":5,"max":9,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Fraction folded","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Variant R","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[5,1],[6,0.99],[6.3,0.95],[6.5,0.5],[6.7,0.05],[7,0.01],[9,0]]},{"name":"Variant S","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[5,0.99],[5.8,0.95],[6.5,0.78],[7.2,0.5],[7.9,0.22],[8.6,0.05],[9,0.02]]}]}},{"text":"The narrower, steeper transition is the more cooperative one: R, not S.","strike":["S; pK 7.2","S; pK 6.5"]},{"text":"Match the curve to its own pK: R's midpoint is 6.5; 7.2 belongs to S.","strike":["R; pK 7.2"]},{"text":"Answer: R; pK 6.5."}],"trap":"Judging cooperativity by the midpoint (a higher pK or Tm): the midpoint gives position; only the steepness gives cooperativity."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"between pH 6.3 and 6.7","note":"R's range"},{"t":"pK 6.5","note":"R's midpoint"},{"t":"between pH 5.8 and 8.6","note":"S's range"},{"t":"pK 7.2","note":"S's midpoint"}]},{"text":"**Width = cooperativity.** Subtract the ends of each range; the smaller width is the steeper, more cooperative curve.","calc":["R: 0.4 units","S: 2.8 units"]},{"text":"**Midpoint = pK.** On a curve, the pK is the pH where the fraction folded is one half.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Steep (cooperative) versus shallow transition","x":{"label":"pH","min":5,"max":9,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Fraction folded","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Variant R","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[5,1],[6,0.99],[6.3,0.95],[6.5,0.5],[6.7,0.05],[7,0.01],[9,0]]},{"name":"Variant S","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[5,0.99],[5.8,0.95],[6.5,0.78],[7.2,0.5],[7.9,0.22],[8.6,0.05],[9,0.02]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** read each curve separately: its steepness and its own pK belong together."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a steeper, narrower unfolding transition means more cooperative unfolding; the midpoint (pK or Tm) tells only where it happens."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-43a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:43#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:43"],"passage":"The i-motif is a four-stranded DNA structure held together by C·C⁺ base pairs, in which one cytosine of each pair must be protonated at N3. Unmodified cytosine has a pKa of 4.5 at its N3. A chemist replaces the cytosines with an analog whose N3 pKa is 5.3. Both versions are then studied at pH 5.5.","q":"Compared with the unmodified i-motif, the one built from the analog is:","choices":["more stable; its N3 holds H⁺ more.","less stable; its N3 holds H⁺ more.","less stable; its N3 holds H⁺ less.","no change; only H-bonds matter."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"More protonated N3 means more C·C⁺ pairs, which holds the structure together; it cannot make it less stable."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"A higher pKa means the N3 holds its H⁺ more tightly, so the analog is protonated more, not less."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The C·C⁺ pairs need a protonated N3, so how readily N3 takes up H⁺ directly sets stability."}],"rule":"Higher pKa = more protonated at a given pH. If a structure needs the protonated form, raising the pKa stabilizes it and lowering it destabilizes it.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Compare pH with each pKa: at pH 5.5 the analog (pKa 5.3) is about 40% protonated, normal C (4.5) only about 9%. More C⁺, more pairs."},"exp":"The i-motif needs **protonated N3**. A **higher pKa** keeps N3 protonated at higher pH: at pH 5.5 about 39% of analog N3 is protonated versus about 9% for normal cytosine. More C·C⁺ pairs form, so the analog i-motif is **more stable**.","how":{"ask":"Use each pKa to judge how much protonated cytosine exists at pH 5.5, then link that to stability.","know":"• At pH < pKa a site is mostly protonated; at pH > pKa mostly not.\n• Fraction protonated = 1/(1 + 10^(pH − pKa)).\n• **Higher pKa → more protonated** at a given pH; lower pKa → less.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the two pKa values.","hl":"whose N3 pKa is 5.3","calc":["analog 5.3 > normal 4.5"]},{"text":"At pH 5.5, find the protonated fraction of each.","calc":["analog: 1/(1 + 10^0.2) ≈ 0.39","normal: 1/(1 + 10^1.0) ≈ 0.09"]},{"text":"More protonated N3 → more C·C⁺ pairs → more stable.","strike":["less stable; its N3 holds H⁺ more.","less stable; its N3 holds H⁺ less."]},{"text":"Stability depends on that protonation, so it cannot be unchanged.","strike":["no change; only H-bonds matter."]},{"text":"Answer: more stable."}],"trap":"Thinking a higher pKa means a stronger acid that gives up its H⁺: a higher pKa is a weaker acid, so it keeps its H⁺."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"must be protonated at N3","note":"needs C⁺"},{"t":"pKa of 4.5","note":"normal"},{"t":"N3 pKa is 5.3","note":"analog holds H⁺ more"},{"t":"pH 5.5","note":"between, nearer the analog"}]},{"text":"**pKa vs pH.** The closer the pKa sits to (or above) the pH, the larger the protonated share."},{"text":"**Also:** a modification that lowers the pKa, say from 4.5 to 4.1, has the reverse effect: fewer C⁺, a less stable structure."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** if a structure needs a protonated base, raising that base's pKa stabilizes it and lowering the pKa destabilizes it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-44a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biology","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:44#1","pe3-cp:44#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:44"],"passage":"Single-stranded DNA probes are checked by base composition before use. One probe strand is 28% A, 35% G and 37% C, with no T. A polymerase then copies it into a complementary strand.","q":"Which nucleoside is absent from the new strand, and what percentage of it is deoxyguanosine?","choices":["Deoxyadenosine; 37%","Deoxythymidine; 37%","Deoxyadenosine; 35%","Deoxyguanosine; 37%"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"A nucleoside cannot be missing from the strand and also make up a share of it."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"T is missing from the template, so its partner, A, is what the new strand lacks; T appears opposite every A."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"35% is the template's own G; the new strand's G pairs with the template's C."}],"rule":"Complement = swap each base for its partner: new A = old T, new T = old A, new G = old C, new C = old G.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Swap partners: no T in the template means no A opposite it; new G equals template C."},"exp":"Each new base pairs with its partner: **A↔T, G↔C**. The template has **no T**, so the new strand has **no A** (deoxyadenosine is absent). New G pairs with template C, so G = **37%**.","how":{"ask":"Pair each template base with its partner, then read off the missing nucleoside and the G content.","know":"• **A pairs with T; G pairs with C**.\n• New strand %X = template % of X's partner.\n• A base missing from the template means its **partner** is missing from the complement.","steps":[{"text":"Read the template composition.","hl":"28% A, 35% G and 37% C, with no T"},{"text":"Swap each base for its partner.","calc":["new T = 28%, new C = 35%, new G = 37%, new A = none"]},{"text":"So A is absent and G is 37%. T is present opposite A; 35% is the template's G; G cannot be absent and present.","strike":["Deoxythymidine; 37%","Deoxyadenosine; 35%","Deoxyguanosine; 37%"]},{"text":"Answer: deoxyadenosine; 37%."}],"trap":"Naming the base the template lacks (T) instead of its partner (A)."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"with no T","note":"partner A will be missing"},{"t":"37% C","note":"becomes the new G"}]},{"text":"**Partners.** A↔T and G↔C, so each percentage moves to the partner base."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a complementary strand, each base's share equals its partner's share in the template."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-45a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe3-cp:45#1","pe3-cp:45#2","pe3-cp:45#3","pe5-cp:35#1","pe5-cp:35#2","pe6-cp:29#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:45"],"q":"Proteins with pI 4.6, 5.9, 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4 are loaded onto a cation-exchange column (negative beads). At which pH will exactly three bind?","choices":["pH 6.8","pH 7.8","pH 7.3","pH 9.8"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"At this pH three proteins are negative and only two are positive; negative beads hold the positive ones."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Above every pI all five proteins are negative, so none sticks to negative beads."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"A pI copied as the buffer pH: that protein is neutral at its own pI, so only two proteins carry a positive charge."}],"rule":"A protein is positive when the buffer pH is below its pI. For a cation exchanger, pick the pH that sits below exactly the needed number of pIs.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count the pIs above each candidate pH: pH 6.8 sits below 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4, so three proteins are positive and bind."},"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Net charge versus pH for the five proteins","x":{"label":"pH","min":3,"max":11,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Net charge","min":-10,"max":10,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"Protein A (pI 4.6)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[3,6.4],[4,2.9],[4.6,0],[5,-2],[6,-5.9],[6.8,-7.6],[7,-7.8],[8,-8.6],[9,-8.9],[10,-9],[11,-9]]},{"name":"Protein B (pI 5.9)","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[3,5.5],[4,4.7],[5,2.8],[5.9,0],[6,-0.3],[6.8,-2.8],[7,-3.3],[8,-4.9],[9,-5.6],[10,-5.9],[11,-6]]},{"name":"Protein C (pI 7.3)","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[3,8],[4,7.9],[5,7.6],[6,6.2],[6.8,3],[7,1.9],[7.3,0],[8,-4.1],[9,-7],[10,-7.8],[11,-8]]},{"name":"Protein D (pI 8.2)","marker":"circle-open","line":"smooth","points":[[3,6],[4,6],[5,5.9],[6,5.7],[6.8,4.8],[7,4.5],[8,1],[8.2,0],[9,-3.4],[10,-5.4],[11,-5.9]]},{"name":"Protein E (pI 9.4)","marker":"diamond","line":"smooth","points":[[3,7],[4,7],[5,6.9],[6,6.7],[6.8,6.3],[7,6.1],[8,4.6],[9,1.5],[9.4,0],[10,-2.3],[11,-5]]}]},"exp":"Below its pI a protein is **positive** and binds the **negative beads** of a cation exchanger. At **pH 6.8**, only the pI 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4 proteins are below their pI, so exactly **three** bind; the pI 4.6 and 5.9 proteins are negative and flow through.","how":{"ask":"For each candidate pH, count the proteins whose pI lies above it (the positive ones), and find the pH that gives three.","know":"• **pH < pI → net positive**; **pH > pI → net negative**; pH = pI → no net charge.\n• **Cation exchanger** = negative beads → binds **positive** proteins.\n• **Anion exchanger** = positive beads → binds **negative** proteins.","steps":[{"text":"Read the pIs.","hl":"4.6, 5.9, 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4"},{"text":"Count positive proteins (pI above the buffer pH) at each candidate pH.","calc":["pH 6.8: pI 7.3, 8.2, 9.4 → **3**","pH 7.3: 8.2, 9.4 → 2 (the pI 7.3 protein is neutral)","pH 7.8: 8.2, 9.4 → 2","pH 9.8: none → 0"],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Protein A (pI 4.6)","x":6.8},{"series":"Protein B (pI 5.9)","x":6.8},{"series":"Protein C (pI 7.3)","x":6.8},{"series":"Protein D (pI 8.2)","x":6.8},{"series":"Protein E (pI 9.4)","x":6.8}]}},{"text":"At pH 7.8 three proteins are negative, not positive; at pH 9.8 all five are negative; at pH 7.3 one protein is neutral, leaving two.","strike":["pH 7.8","pH 9.8","pH 7.3"]},{"text":"Answer: pH 6.8."}],"trap":"Counting the negative proteins: a cation exchanger has negative beads, so it holds the proteins that are positive, those with pI above the buffer pH."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"4.6, 5.9, 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4","note":"the pIs"},{"t":"cation-exchange column","note":"negative beads"},{"t":"exactly three bind","note":"three positive proteins"}]},{"text":"**Charge from pI.** Below its pI a protein holds extra H⁺ and is positive; above its pI it is negative.","calc":["pH < pI → +","pH > pI → −"]},{"text":"**Also:** a cation exchanger has negative beads, so it binds the positive proteins: at pH 6.8 those with pI 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4, three of them."},{"text":"**Also:** an anion exchanger has positive beads and binds the negative proteins: at pH 6.8 those with pI 4.6 and 5.9, two of them."},{"text":"**Also:** two forms of one protein with almost the same mass but different net charge (say −3 and −5 at the buffer pH) cannot be split by size, so use ion exchange. Both are negative, so an anion exchanger holds both, and the −5 form clings tighter: it stays on the column longer and comes off only at a higher salt concentration.","calc":["same size → size exclusion fails","−3 form: released first, at lower salt","−5 form: released later, at higher salt"]},{"text":"**Also:** in electrophoresis the same sign rule sets the direction. When the buffer pH equals the pI, the charges cancel and the protein stays in its well; below its pI it is positive and drifts to the negative electrode (cathode); above its pI it is negative and drifts to the positive electrode (anode).","calc":["pH 6.8, pI 4.6: negative → toward the anode (+)","pH 6.8, pI 9.4: positive → toward the cathode (−)","a protein with pI 6.8 would not migrate"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** proteins are positive below their pI and negative above it; a cation exchanger binds the positive ones, an anion exchanger the negative ones."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-46a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:46#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:46"],"q":"Cellulose beads, whose repeating unit is shown, are used to separate peptides by size. A few small peptides come off later than their size predicts. What weak interaction with the beads most likely explains this?","choices":["H-bonds to ring hydroxyls","Salt bridges to carboxylates","Aromatic π–π contacts","Hydrophobic contact with CH₂ chains"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"impossible","why":"The repeating unit has no carboxylate or other charged group, so there is nothing to form an ion pair with."},{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"The rings drawn are saturated sugar rings, not aromatic, so there are no π systems to stack on."},{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"Only short CH and CH₂ pieces sit between polar OH groups; there is no long nonpolar chain."}],"rule":"Read the polymer's functional groups first: sugar resins are covered in OH groups, so peptides interact with them by hydrogen bonding.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Sugar polymer = –OH everywhere = hydrogen bonding. No charge, no aromatic ring, no long alkyl chain."},"fig":"p1-cellobiose","exp":"Cellulose is a chain of **glucose rings** covered in **–OH groups**, with no charges, aromatic rings or long alkyl chains. Polar peptide groups (N–H, C=O, side-chain OH) can form **hydrogen bonds** with those hydroxyls, which slows them slightly on the column.","how":{"ask":"Identify the functional groups on the resin, then match the interaction they allow.","know":"• **Polysaccharides** (cellulose, dextran, agarose) are covered in **–OH**, so they hydrogen-bond.\n• Ionic attraction needs **charged groups**; π-stacking needs **aromatic rings**; hydrophobic contact needs **long nonpolar chains**.\n• Polyacrylamide resins offer **amide** groups, which also hydrogen-bond.","steps":[{"text":"Scan the repeating unit: glucose rings joined by an O bridge, with OH on almost every carbon.","hl":"whose repeating unit is shown"},{"text":"No ionized group, no aromatic ring, no long CH₂ chain.","strike":["Salt bridges to carboxylates","Aromatic π–π contacts","Hydrophobic contact with CH₂ chains"]},{"text":"The OH groups both give and accept H-bonds with peptide N–H and C=O groups.","calc":["resin O–H ··· O=C (peptide)"]},{"text":"Answer: H-bonds to ring hydroxyls."}],"trap":"Calling the saturated sugar rings aromatic: π-stacking needs flat, conjugated rings such as phenylalanine's."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Cellulose beads","note":"a polysaccharide"},{"t":"later than their size predicts","note":"weak attraction"}]},{"text":"**Groups decide interactions.**","calc":["–OH → hydrogen bonding","–COO⁻ → ionic","aryl ring → π-stacking","long CH₂ chain → hydrophobic"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** list the resin's functional groups, then name the only interaction they can make."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-47a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:47#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:47"],"q":"Of four nonmetals in groups 16 and 17 of periods 3 and 4, which needs the most energy to lose its first electron, and why?","choices":["Sulfur; it is the smallest atom","Chlorine; its electrons sit closest","Selenium; it has more protons than S","Bromine; it has the most protons"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Atoms shrink across a period toward group 17, so sulfur is larger than its period-3 neighbor, not smaller."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Selenium's extra protons are outweighed by an extra shell: down a group, ionization energy falls."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Its extra protons come with another shell that shields them; bromine sits below its period-3 group-mate, so its IE is lower."}],"rule":"First ionization energy rises up a group and to the right across a period: pick the element highest and farthest right.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ionization energy climbs toward the upper right of the table. Of S, Cl, Se, Br the upper-right one is Cl (group 17, period 3)."},"exp":"First ionization energy **rises across a period** (more protons, same shell) and **rises up a group** (fewer shells, so the outer electrons sit nearer the protons). The group 17, period 3 atom, chlorine, is the smallest of the four and holds its electrons most tightly.","how":{"ask":"Place the four atoms on the table and apply both ionization-energy trends.","know":"• First ionization energy **increases left → right** across a period and **increases bottom → top** in a group.\n• Smaller atom → outer electrons nearer the protons → harder to remove.\n• Among halogens, **F** (smallest) is the hardest to ionize.","steps":[{"text":"Place the atoms: period 3 holds S (16) and Cl (17); period 4 holds Se (16) and Br (17).","hl":"groups 16 and 17 of periods 3 and 4"},{"text":"Across a period, group 17 beats group 16, and the group 16 atoms are the larger ones: S and Se lose.","strike":["Sulfur; it is the smallest atom","Selenium; it has more protons than S"]},{"text":"Up a group, period 3 beats period 4: Br has the most protons but one more shell, so it loses.","strike":["Bromine; it has the most protons"]},{"text":"**Also:** extend the trend up group 17: F > Cl > Br > I, so fluorine, the smallest halogen, is the hardest of them all to ionize."},{"text":"Answer: chlorine, because its valence electrons sit closest to the nucleus."}],"trap":"Thinking a heavier atom with more protons holds its electrons more tightly: extra shells put them farther away and shield them."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"lose its first electron","note":"first ionization energy"},{"t":"groups 16 and 17","note":"across a period"},{"t":"periods 3 and 4","note":"up and down a group"}]},{"text":"**Across a period:** more protons pull on the same shell, so ionization energy rises toward group 17 (and 18)."},{"text":"**Up a group:** fewer shells, a smaller atom, less shielding, so ionization energy rises toward the top."},{"text":"**Also:** of the halogens, fluorine is the hardest atom to ionize, because it is the smallest."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** first ionization energy rises up a group and across a period; the smallest atom, top right, holds its electrons tightest."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-48a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:48#1","pe3-cp:48#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:48"],"q":"A toy sled, released from rest, glides down a ramp with steady acceleration; the graph plots its position x against time. Which relationship fits the data, and what is the sled's acceleration?","choices":["x ∝ t²; a = 3 m/s²","x ∝ t²; a = 1.5 m/s²","x ∝ t²; a = 6 m/s²","x ∝ t; constant speed"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"That reads x/t² as a; from rest x = ½at², so a = 2x/t², double that ratio."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"6 is the position reading at the 2 s mark, in meters, not an acceleration."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Equal time steps cover growing distances, so the graph curves upward; a straight line would mean no acceleration."}],"rule":"From rest at constant a, x = ½at² (a parabola); read one point and use a = 2x/t². A straight x–t line means constant velocity.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Pick a clean point: x = 6 m at t = 2 s. a = 2x/t² = 12/4."},"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Position of the sled","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Position x (m)","min":0,"max":24,"step":6},"series":[{"name":"Sled","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,1.5],[2,6],[3,13.5],[4,24]]}]},"exp":"From rest with constant acceleration, **x = ½at²**, so x grows with **t²** and the curve bends upward. At t = 2 s, x = 6 m, so a = 2x/t² = 2(6)/2² = **3 m/s²**. Check: at t = 4 s, x = ½(3)(16) = 24 m.","how":{"ask":"Decide whether the curve is a line or a parabola, then read a point and solve x = ½at² for a.","know":"• From rest, constant a: **x = ½at²** → x ∝ t² (parabola).\n• Constant velocity: x = vt → x ∝ t (straight line).\n• Solve for a: **a = 2x/t²**.","steps":[{"text":"The curve bends upward: the distance covered each second keeps growing.","calc":["0 → 1.5 → 6 → 13.5 → 24 m"],"strike":["x ∝ t; constant speed"]},{"text":"Read a point and solve x = ½at² for a.","calc":["x = 6 m at t = 2 s","a = 2x/t² = 2(6)/4 = **3 m/s²**"]},{"text":"1.5 m/s² drops the ½ (a = x/t²); 6 m/s² is the position reading itself.","strike":["x ∝ t²; a = 1.5 m/s²","x ∝ t²; a = 6 m/s²"]},{"text":"Answer: x ∝ t²; a = 3 m/s²."}],"trap":"Forgetting the ½ in x = ½at², which halves the acceleration."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"released from rest","note":"v₀ = 0"},{"t":"steady acceleration","note":"x = ½at²"}]},{"text":"**Shape first.** A parabola that bends upward means constant acceleration; a straight line means constant velocity."},{"text":"**Also:** for the same motion, the velocity–time graph is a straight line through the origin (v = at), while the position–time graph is the parabola."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** from rest, x = ½at², so x ∝ t² and a = 2x/t²."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-49a","s":"Waves & Sound","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:49#1","pe3-cp:51#1","pe5-cp:53#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:49"],"passage":"Ultrasound probes send short pulses through several tissues on the way to an organ. A 4.0 MHz pulse travels from the liver, where sound moves at 1560 m/s, into a layer of fat, where it moves at 1440 m/s. The probe's crystal sets how many wave crests leave it each second.","q":"Which describes the pulse once it is in the fat?","choices":["Same f; λ shrinks","Same f; λ grows","Lower f; λ unchanged","Higher f; λ unchanged"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sound slows from 1560 to 1440 m/s, so at a fixed frequency the wavelength gets smaller, not larger."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The source fixes the frequency, so it cannot change at a boundary; the speed change shows up in the wavelength."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A boundary cannot add wave crests per second; if the speed changes, λ = v/f must change instead."}],"rule":"Crossing into a new medium, frequency stays fixed (set by the source); speed and wavelength change together, λ = v/f.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"f never changes at a boundary. v drops, so λ = v/f drops with it."},"exp":"The **frequency is fixed by the source**, so it stays 4.0 MHz in fat. Speed drops from 1560 to 1440 m/s, so **λ = v/f** gets shorter: about 0.39 mm in liver → 0.36 mm in fat.","how":{"ask":"Keep the frequency, then let the wavelength follow the speed.","know":"• At a boundary, **frequency stays the same** (and so does the period).\n• **Speed** depends on the medium; **λ = v/f** changes with it.\n• Amplitude can also change, since some energy reflects.","steps":[{"text":"The source fixes the frequency, so it stays the same.","hl":"4.0 MHz pulse","strike":["Lower f; λ unchanged","Higher f; λ unchanged"]},{"text":"Compare the speeds.","hl":"where it moves at 1440 m/s","calc":["1560 → 1440 m/s: slower"]},{"text":"At fixed f, λ ∝ v.","calc":["λ(liver) = 1560/(4.0 × 10⁶) ≈ 0.39 mm","λ(fat) = 1440/(4.0 × 10⁶) ≈ 0.36 mm"],"strike":["Same f; λ grows"]},{"text":"Answer: 4.0 MHz, with a shorter wavelength."}],"trap":"Thinking the frequency changes with the speed: the medium changes v and λ, never f."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"4.0 MHz pulse","note":"f: fixed"},{"t":"where sound moves at 1560 m/s","note":"faster"},{"t":"where it moves at 1440 m/s","note":"slower"}]},{"text":"**Source sets f; medium sets v.** The wavelength is whatever fits: λ = v/f."},{"text":"**Also:** for light in vacuum, c = fλ is fixed, so tuning a laser to double its wavelength halves its frequency."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a new medium f stays the same; λ scales with the wave speed."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-50a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:50#1","pe3-cp:50#2","pe3-cp:50#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:50"],"passage":"Water channels in a filtration membrane are modeled as straight cylindrical pores. One pore is 45 μm long and has a cross-sectional area of 2.5 × 10³ μm². Fluid moves through it at an average speed of 0.30 mm/s, and the flow is steady.","q":"How much fluid volume passes through the pore each second?","choices":["7.5 × 10⁵ μm³/s","7.5 × 10² μm³/s","3.4 × 10⁷ μm³/s","8.3 μm³/s"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"This leaves the speed in mm/s; 1 mm = 1000 μm, so the speed must be multiplied by 1000 first."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Area divided by speed has units of μm·s, not volume per time; flow rate is area times speed."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"The pore length is not part of the flow rate; multiplying by it gives μm⁴/s, not μm³/s."}],"rule":"Volume flow rate Q = A × v; put area and speed in matching length units (μm² with μm/s) and ignore the length of the pore.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"μm² × μm/s = μm³/s. Convert 0.30 mm/s = 300 μm/s, then multiply by 2.5 × 10³."},"exp":"**Q = A·v**. Convert the speed: 0.30 mm/s = **300 μm/s**. Q = 2.5 × 10³ μm² × 300 μm/s = **7.5 × 10⁵ μm³/s**. The 45 μm length does not enter.","how":{"ask":"Multiply the cross-sectional area by the average speed, in matching units.","know":"• **Volume flow rate Q = A·v** (area × average speed).\n• Units must match: μm² × μm/s = μm³/s; 1 mm = 10³ μm.\n• If a diameter is given, A = πr².","steps":[{"text":"Convert the speed to μm/s.","hl":"0.30 mm/s","calc":["0.30 mm/s × 1000 = 300 μm/s"],"strike":["7.5 × 10² μm³/s"]},{"text":"Multiply area by speed.","hl":"2.5 × 10³ μm²","calc":["Q = 2.5 × 10³ × 300 = **7.5 × 10⁵ μm³/s**"]},{"text":"Dividing area by speed, or multiplying by the 45 μm length, gives the wrong units.","strike":["8.3 μm³/s","3.4 × 10⁷ μm³/s"]},{"text":"Answer: 7.5 × 10⁵ μm³/s."}],"trap":"Multiplying by the pore length: Q = Av needs only the cross-section and the speed."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"45 μm long","note":"not needed"},{"t":"2.5 × 10³ μm²","note":"A"},{"t":"0.30 mm/s","note":"v: convert to μm/s"}]},{"text":"**Flow rate = area × speed.**","calc":["Q = Av"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Q = A × v with matching length units; the length of the channel never enters."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-51a","s":"Waves & Sound","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:51#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:51"],"passage":"A therapeutic ultrasound unit heats deep tissue with a continuous wave. Its transducer face is 2.0 cm across and vibrates at 3.5 MHz. In the soft tissue being treated, sound travels at 1540 m/s.","q":"What wavelength does the wave have inside the tissue?","choices":["0.44 mm","440 m","20 mm","5.4 × 10⁹ m"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"This divides by 3.5 instead of 3.5 × 10⁶ Hz; leaving out the mega prefix makes λ a million times too long."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Multiplying speed by frequency gives m/s², not meters; wavelength is speed divided by frequency."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"That is the transducer's width, a size of the source, not the spacing between wave crests."}],"rule":"λ = v/f with f in hertz (MHz = 10⁶ Hz); sizes of the source are distractors.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"λ = v/f = 1540 / 3.5 × 10⁶ ≈ 440 × 10⁻⁶ m."},"exp":"**λ = v/f** = 1540 m/s ÷ 3.5 × 10⁶ Hz ≈ 4.4 × 10⁻⁴ m = **0.44 mm**. The 2.0 cm transducer width does not set the wavelength.","how":{"ask":"Take v and f from the passage and divide, with f in hertz.","know":"• **v = fλ**, so **λ = v/f**.\n• **MHz = 10⁶ Hz**.\n• 1 m = 10³ mm.","steps":[{"text":"Pull v and f from the passage.","hl":"3.5 MHz","calc":["f = 3.5 × 10⁶ Hz","v = 1540 m/s"]},{"text":"Divide.","calc":["λ = 1540 / (3.5 × 10⁶) ≈ 4.4 × 10⁻⁴ m = **0.44 mm**"]},{"text":"440 m drops the 10⁶; 5.4 × 10⁹ m multiplies; 20 mm is the transducer width.","strike":["440 m","5.4 × 10⁹ m","20 mm"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.44 mm."}],"trap":"Forgetting that MHz means 10⁶ Hz, which makes λ a million times too long."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 cm across","note":"distractor"},{"t":"3.5 MHz","note":"f"},{"t":"1540 m/s","note":"v"}]},{"text":"**Wavelength = speed ÷ frequency.**","calc":["λ = v/f"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** λ = v/f, with MHz converted to 10⁶ Hz."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-52a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:52#1","pe3-cp:52#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:52"],"passage":"In the kidney, each small artery feeding a glomerulus splits into a tuft of capillaries. Model the feeding vessel as one tube of cross-sectional area S, carrying blood at average speed w. It branches into N identical capillaries, each with cross-sectional area s, and the combined area Ns is larger than S. Blood is treated as incompressible.","q":"How fast does blood move through each capillary, and is that faster or slower than w?","choices":["Sw/(Ns); slower than w","Nsw/S; faster than w","Sw/(Ns); faster than w","Sw/s; slower than w"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Speed is flow rate ÷ area; putting Ns on top inverts the ratio."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Since Ns is bigger than S, the ratio S/(Ns) is below one, so the capillary speed must be lower."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Dividing by one capillary's area ignores that the flow is shared among all N capillaries."}],"rule":"Continuity: A₁v₁ = (total A₂)v₂. Speed = flow rate ÷ total area, so a larger total area means slower flow.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Flow in = flow out: Sw = N·s·u. Total area grew, so speed fell."},"exp":"Incompressible flow obeys **continuity**: the volume per second in the vessel equals the total in the capillaries. **S·w = N·s·u**, so **u = Sw/(Ns)**. Because the total area Ns exceeds S, u is **slower** than w.","how":{"ask":"Set flow in equal to flow out, solve for the capillary speed, then compare it with w.","know":"• **Continuity**: Q = A·v is the same at every cross-section of an incompressible flow.\n• For branches, use the **total** area of all branches.\n• Bigger total area → slower speed.","steps":[{"text":"Set flow in equal to flow out.","hl":"N identical capillaries, each with cross-sectional area s","calc":["S·w = (N·s)·u"]},{"text":"Solve for u.","calc":["u = Sw/(Ns)"],"strike":["Nsw/S; faster than w","Sw/s; slower than w"]},{"text":"Compare: Ns > S, so u < w.","hl":"the combined area Ns is larger than S","strike":["Sw/(Ns); faster than w"]},{"text":"Answer: Sw/(Ns); slower than w."}],"trap":"Using one capillary's area instead of the total: the flow splits among all N."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"area S, carrying blood at average speed w","note":"flow in = Sw"},{"t":"each with cross-sectional area s","note":"total = Ns"},{"t":"incompressible","note":"continuity"}]},{"text":"**Continuity.** What flows in each second must flow out each second.","calc":["A₁v₁ = A₂v₂"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** speed = flow rate ÷ total cross-sectional area; more total area means slower flow."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-53a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:53#1","pe3-cp:53#2","pe3-cp:53#3","pe4-cp:2#1","pe4-cp:2#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:53"],"passage":"Silver chromate, Ag₂CrO₄, is a brick-red solid that signals the endpoint of some chloride titrations. A saturated solution is kept in contact with excess solid: Ag₂CrO₄(s) ⇌ 2Ag⁺(aq) + CrO₄²⁻(aq). In this solution, [Ag⁺] = 1.4 × 10⁻⁴ M and [CrO₄²⁻] = 7.0 × 10⁻⁵ M.","q":"What are Ksp's expression and value for silver chromate?","choices":["[Ag⁺]²[CrO₄²⁻] = 1.4 × 10⁻¹²","[Ag⁺][CrO₄²⁻] = 9.8 × 10⁻⁹","[Ag⁺]²[CrO₄²⁻]² = 9.6 × 10⁻¹⁷","[Ag⁺] + [CrO₄²⁻] = 2.1 × 10⁻⁴"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Every concentration takes its coefficient as an exponent; the silver term needs a square since two Ag⁺ form per unit."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Only the 2 in front of Ag⁺ becomes an exponent; chromate's coefficient is one, so its term is not squared."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Ksp is a product of ion concentrations, never a sum."}],"rule":"Ksp = product of the ion concentrations, each raised to its coefficient; leave out the solid.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Write Ksp = [Ag⁺]²[CrO₄²⁻] first; that leaves one candidate. Check: (1.4 × 10⁻⁴)² × 7.0 × 10⁻⁵ ≈ 2 × 10⁻⁸ × 7 × 10⁻⁵."},"exp":"Leave out the solid, and give each concentration its coefficient as an exponent: **Ksp = [Ag⁺]²[CrO₄²⁻]** = (1.4 × 10⁻⁴)²(7.0 × 10⁻⁵) = (1.96 × 10⁻⁸)(7.0 × 10⁻⁵) ≈ **1.4 × 10⁻¹²**.","how":{"ask":"Write Ksp from the balanced equation, then plug in the concentrations.","know":"• **Ksp** = product of the dissolved ion concentrations, each raised to its **coefficient**.\n• **Pure solids and liquids are left out** of K, so adding more solid does not shift the equilibrium.\n• For CaCO₃(s) ⇌ Ca²⁺ + CO₃²⁻, Ksp = [Ca²⁺][CO₃²⁻].","steps":[{"text":"Read the coefficients from the equation and leave out the solid.","hl":"Ag₂CrO₄(s) ⇌ 2Ag⁺(aq) + CrO₄²⁻(aq)","calc":["Ksp = [Ag⁺]²[CrO₄²⁻]"],"strike":["[Ag⁺][CrO₄²⁻] = 9.8 × 10⁻⁹","[Ag⁺]²[CrO₄²⁻]² = 9.6 × 10⁻¹⁷","[Ag⁺] + [CrO₄²⁻] = 2.1 × 10⁻⁴"]},{"text":"Plug in.","calc":["(1.4 × 10⁻⁴)² = 1.96 × 10⁻⁸","× 7.0 × 10⁻⁵ ≈ **1.4 × 10⁻¹²**"]},{"text":"Answer: [Ag⁺]²[CrO₄²⁻] = 1.4 × 10⁻¹²."}],"trap":"Forgetting to square [Ag⁺]: the coefficient 2 becomes an exponent."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"excess solid","note":"left out of Ksp"},{"t":"2Ag⁺(aq)","note":"square [Ag⁺]"},{"t":"[Ag⁺] = 1.4 × 10⁻⁴ M","note":"plug in"}]},{"text":"**Coefficients become exponents.**","calc":["Ca₃(PO₄)₂ → Ksp = [Ca²⁺]³[PO₄³⁻]²"]},{"text":"**Also:** for a solid that gives off a gas, such as CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g), K is the CO₂ pressure alone, and adding more of either solid changes nothing."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ksp = product of ion concentrations raised to their coefficients; solids never appear."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-54a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:54#1","pe3-cp:54#2","pe4-cp:20#1","pe4-cp:20#2","pe4-cp:20#3","pe4-cp:52#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:54"],"passage":"Hard water contains dissolved ions of metals that give up their valence electrons easily. A metal's first three ionization energies are 480, 4400 and 6800 kJ/mol. A large jump between two successive ionization energies shows that the next electron sits in a filled inner shell.","q":"To which family of the periodic table does the metal belong?","choices":["alkali metals","alkaline earth metals","inner transition metals","halogens"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Group 2 metals rise only modestly from IE₁ to IE₂ and jump after the 2nd electron; here the jump comes after the 1st."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Inner transition metals usually form 3+ ions, so their jump comes after the 3rd electron; here 4400 → 6800 is under a 2× rise."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The stem says it is a metal; halogens are nonmetals."}],"rule":"Find the big jump between successive ionization energies: the electrons removed before it are the valence electrons, which sets the group.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Electrons before the big jump = valence electrons. 480 → 4400 is about 9×, right after the 1st, so one valence electron: group 1, alkali metals."},"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Successive ionization energies of the metal","x":{"label":"Electron removed"},"y":{"label":"Ionization energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":8000,"step":1000},"categories":["IE₁","IE₂","IE₃"],"series":[{"name":"Ionization energy","values":[480,4400,6800]}]},"exp":"Ionization energy leaps **right after the first electron** (480 → 4400 kJ/mol, about 9×), so the atom has one valence electron and forms **1+ ions**: group 1, the **alkali metals**.","how":{"ask":"Find where the big jump in ionization energy falls; that counts the valence electrons and names the group.","know":"• **Group names:** alkali metals = group 1; alkaline earth metals = group 2; halogens = group 17; noble gases = group 18; metalloids = the staircase elements (B, Si, Ge, As, Sb, Te).\n• A huge jump in successive ionization energies comes once the valence shell is empty.\n• Group 1 loses 1 electron → **1+ ions**, so its jump comes right after the 1st electron; group 2 jumps after the 2nd.","steps":[{"text":"Compare each step with the one before it.","hl":"480, 4400 and 6800 kJ/mol","calc":["4400 ÷ 480 ≈ 9.2 (**big jump**)","6800 ÷ 4400 ≈ 1.5 (modest)"],"graph":{"bars":[["IE₁","Ionization energy"],["IE₂","Ionization energy"]]}},{"text":"The jump comes after electron 1: one valence electron, group 1, 1+ ions."},{"text":"Alkaline earth metals rise only modestly from IE₁ to IE₂ and jump after the 2nd electron; inner transition metals typically form 3+ ions, so their jump comes after the 3rd; halogens are nonmetals that gain one electron.","strike":["alkaline earth metals","inner transition metals","halogens"]},{"text":"**Also:** group 17 = halogens, group 18 = noble gases, and the metalloids sit on the staircase between metals and nonmetals."},{"text":"Answer: alkali metals."}],"trap":"Confusing alkali (group 1, 1+) with alkaline earth (group 2, 2+): count the electrons removed before the jump."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"480","note":"1st electron"},{"t":"4400","note":"2nd electron: from the core"},{"t":"6800","note":"3rd electron"}]},{"text":"**Find the jump.** Divide each ionization energy by the one before it.","calc":["4400/480 ≈ 9.2 → jump after the 1st electron","6800/4400 ≈ 1.5"]},{"text":"**Count valence electrons.** One before the jump → group 1 → forms 1+ ions (Li⁺, Na⁺, K⁺)."},{"text":"**Also:** a group 2 metal shows its jump after the 2nd electron and forms 2+ ions; calcium is one, so its compounds, such as CaO, contain Ca²⁺. The transition metals fill the d block in the middle of the table."},{"text":"**Also:** sodium (group 1) forms only 1+ ions, while Mg and the transition metals Zn and Fe can form 2+, so Na cannot stand in for a divalent metal ion."},{"text":"**Also:** the group names to know: alkali metals (1), alkaline earth metals (2), halogens (17), noble gases (18), and metalloids along the staircase."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the big ionization-energy jump comes right after the last valence electron; after the 1st means group 1, the alkali metals (1+ ions), and after the 2nd means group 2 (2+ ions)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-55a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:55#1","pe3-cp:41#1","pe3-cp:41#2","pe3-cp:41#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:55"],"passage":"A reaction is studied at 360 K, where RT = 3.0 kJ/mol. Its standard free energy change is ΔG° = −6.0 kJ/mol. The equilibrium constant follows from ΔG°; use e² ≈ 7.4.","q":"What is K, and which side does it favor?","choices":["K ≈ 0.14; reactants win","K ≈ 0.50; reactants win","K ≈ 2.0; products win","K ≈ 7.4; products win"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign: ΔG° < 0 means K > 1 and products favored, so 0.14 (the sign of ΔG° flipped, e⁻²) is out."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign: ΔG° < 0 means K > 1, so a K of 0.50 with reactants favored is out."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"2.0 is ln K, not K: the log was never undone with e^x."}],"rule":"ln K = −ΔG°/RT, so a negative ΔG° means K > 1. Find the exponent first, then undo the log.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"ln K = −ΔG°/RT = 6 ÷ 3 = 2, so K = e² ≈ 7.4. Check: negative ΔG° must give K > 1, which already kills 0.14 and 0.50."},"exp":"**ΔG° = −RT ln K**, so ln K = −ΔG° ÷ RT = 6.0 ÷ 3.0 = 2.0, and K = e^2.0 ≈ **7.4**. A negative ΔG° always gives K > 1: products are favored once equilibrium is reached.","how":{"ask":"Rearrange ΔG° = −RT ln K for K, then undo the natural log.","know":"• **ΔG° = −RT ln K** (natural log). Rearranged: **K = e^(−ΔG°/RT)**.\n• **ΔG° < 0 ⇔ K > 1** (products favored); ΔG° > 0 ⇔ K < 1; ΔG° = 0 ⇔ K = 1.\n• K is always products over reactants as the reaction is written.","steps":[{"text":"Pick out ΔG° and RT. Both are in kJ/mol, so the units cancel.","hl":"ΔG° = −6.0 kJ/mol"},{"text":"Solve ΔG° = −RT ln K for ln K.","calc":["ln K = −ΔG° ÷ RT","ln K = −(−6.0 kJ/mol) ÷ 3.0 kJ/mol","ln K = **2.0**"]},{"text":"Undo the natural log: raise e to that power.","calc":["K = e^2.0 ≈ **7.4**"]},{"text":"Check the sign: a negative ΔG° means K exceeds 1. That rules out 0.14 (sign of ΔG° flipped, e^−2) and 0.50 (RT ÷ ΔG°, with no log at all).","strike":["K ≈ 0.14; reactants win","K ≈ 0.50; reactants win"]},{"text":"2.0 is ln K, not K: the log was never undone.","strike":["K ≈ 2.0; products win"]},{"text":"**Also, for unfolding:** for native ⇌ unfolded, products over reactants gives **K = [U]/[N]**, and **ΔG°′ = −RT ln K** with the natural log, never e. With 10% unfolded: K = 10/90 ≈ 0.11, ln K < 0, so ΔG°′ > 0 (unfolding unfavorable).","calc":["K = [U]/[N] = 10 ÷ 90 ≈ 0.11","ΔG°′ = −RT ln(0.11) > 0"]},{"text":"Answer: K ≈ 7.4, so products are favored."}],"trap":"Stopping at ln K = 2.0, or writing ΔG° = −RT e^K: the formula uses ln K, so K itself is e raised to −ΔG°/RT."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"ΔG° = −6.0 kJ/mol","note":"negative: products favored"},{"t":"RT = 3.0 kJ/mol","note":"same units as ΔG°"},{"t":"e² ≈ 7.4","note":"the antilog you will need"}]},{"text":"**Link ΔG° and K.** The standard free energy and the equilibrium constant are tied by one formula with a natural log.","calc":["ΔG° = −RT ln K"]},{"text":"**Solve for K.**","calc":["ln K = −ΔG°/RT = 6.0/3.0 = 2.0","K = e^2.0 ≈ 7.4"]},{"text":"**Sign check.** ΔG° < 0 ⇔ ln K > 0 ⇔ K > 1. A positive ΔG° gives K < 1; ΔG° = 0 gives K = 1."},{"text":"**Also:** for a native → unfolded change, K = [U]/[N] (product over reactant), so ΔG°′ = −RT ln([U]/[N]). If fewer than half the molecules are unfolded, K < 1 and ΔG°′ is positive."},{"text":"**Also:** the log is ln, not e: ΔG°′ = −RT ln K, while K = e^(−ΔG°′/RT) is the same law solved the other way."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ΔG° = −RT ln K with K = products/reactants; ΔG° < 0 means K > 1."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-56a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:56#1","pe3-cp:56#2"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:56"],"passage":"Deionizers for laboratory water use a cation-exchange resin in its acid form. As water flows through, sodium ions are traded for H⁺: Na⁺(aq) + H–R(s) ⇌ Na–R(s) + H⁺(aq). Here R stands for an anionic group bound to the resin beads. When the resin is spent, workers flush it with a concentrated solution that drives this equilibrium to the left.","q":"What should the flushing solution be, and why?","choices":["HCl; extra H⁺ reverses it","NaNO₃; extra Na⁺ reverses it","Pure water; it dilutes H⁺","Fresh resin; more H–R"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Na⁺ is a reactant here; adding it pushes the exchange forward, loading even more Na⁺ onto the resin."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Lowering [H⁺] removes a product, which pulls the equilibrium further toward Na–R, not away from it."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"H–R is a solid, so it is not in the K expression; adding more of it does not shift the equilibrium."}],"rule":"Le Châtelier: add a product (or remove a reactant) to shift left; pure solids and liquids never shift an equilibrium.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"To push left, add a product. The dissolved product is H⁺, so flush with a strong acid."},"exp":"Regeneration must shift **Na⁺ + H–R ⇌ Na–R + H⁺** to the **left**. By **Le Châtelier**, adding a **product** does that: concentrated **HCl** floods the resin with H⁺, which displaces Na⁺ and restores H–R. Solids such as the resin are not in K, so adding resin does nothing.","how":{"ask":"Find which dissolved species is a product, then add it to push the equilibrium left.","know":"• **Le Châtelier**: adding a product shifts an equilibrium left; adding a reactant shifts it right.\n• Removing a product shifts it right.\n• **Solids (the resin) are not in K** and do not shift it.","steps":[{"text":"Identify reactants and products.","hl":"Na⁺(aq) + H–R(s) ⇌ Na–R(s) + H⁺(aq)","calc":["dissolved reactant: Na⁺; dissolved product: H⁺"]},{"text":"Shift left by adding the dissolved product, H⁺.","calc":["add H⁺ (as HCl) → equilibrium moves left"]},{"text":"Adding Na⁺ or removing H⁺ both shift right; adding solid resin does not shift it at all.","strike":["NaNO₃; extra Na⁺ reverses it","Pure water; it dilutes H⁺","Fresh resin; more H–R"]},{"text":"Answer: HCl."}],"trap":"Adding the ion the resin is meant to remove: Na⁺ is a reactant, so it drives the exchange forward."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"traded for H⁺","note":"H⁺ is a product"},{"t":"an anionic group bound to the resin beads","note":"a solid"},{"t":"drives this equilibrium to the left","note":"add a product"}]},{"text":"**Le Châtelier.** The system uses up whatever is added: more product means the reverse reaction speeds up."},{"text":"**Also:** a home water softener loads its resin with Na⁺ instead, so it is recharged with a strong salt solution whose Na⁺ plays the product role."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to push an equilibrium left, add a dissolved product; solids never shift it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-57a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:57#1","pe3-cp:57#2","pe3-cp:57#3","pe3-cp:57#4","pe3-cp:57#5"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:57"],"q":"In each species below, the central atom is bonded only to halogen atoms. Which one has an octahedral molecular geometry?","fig":"cpt-halide-centers","choices":["TeF₆","ICl₄⁻","ClF₃","SiCl₄"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"ICl₄⁻ has only four bonded atoms (two lone pairs on opposite corners): square planar, not octahedral."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Octahedral molecular geometry needs six bonded atoms; ClF₃ has only three."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Octahedral needs six bonded atoms; SiCl₄ has four and no lone pairs: tetrahedral."}],"rule":"Molecular geometry counts bonded atoms only: octahedral needs six atoms around the center with no lone pairs (AX₆).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Octahedral = 6 domains, 0 lone pairs (AX₆): only TeF₆ has 6 bonds. ICl₄⁻ is square planar, ClF₃ T-shaped, SiCl₄ tetrahedral."},"exp":"Count electron domains on the central atom. **TeF₆**: 6 bonds, 0 lone pairs → **octahedral**. ICl₄⁻: 4 bonds + 2 lone pairs → square planar. ClF₃: 3 bonds + 2 lone pairs → T-shaped. SiCl₄: 4 bonds → tetrahedral.","how":{"ask":"For each central atom, count bonds and lone pairs, then name the shape.","know":"• **Lone pairs** = (valence e⁻ − bonding e⁻) ÷ 2, after adding 1 e⁻ per negative charge and subtracting 1 per positive charge; each single bond uses 1 e⁻ of the central atom.\n• 6 bonds, 0 LP → **octahedral**.\n• 4 bonds + 2 LP → **square planar**.\n• 3 bonds + 2 LP → **T-shaped**.\n• 4 bonds, 0 LP → **tetrahedral**.","steps":[{"text":"Count the central atom's electron domains in each species.","hl":"octahedral molecular geometry","calc":["TeF₆: Te 6 e⁻, 6 bonds → 0 LP → 6 domains","ICl₄⁻: I 7 + 1 = 8 e⁻, 4 bonds → 2 LP → 6 domains","ClF₃: Cl 7 e⁻, 3 bonds → 2 LP → 5 domains","SiCl₄: Si 4 e⁻, 4 bonds → 0 LP → 4 domains"]},{"text":"Only TeF₆ has all six domains as bonds, so atoms sit at all six octahedral corners.","calc":["TeF₆ → **octahedral**"]},{"text":"ICl₄⁻ is square planar (two lone pairs take opposite corners), ClF₃ is T-shaped, and SiCl₄ is tetrahedral.","strike":["ICl₄⁻","ClF₃","SiCl₄"]},{"text":"Answer: TeF₆."}],"trap":"Picking ICl₄⁻ because it has six electron domains: its electron geometry is octahedral, but with two lone pairs the molecular shape is square planar."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"central atom","note":"count its domains"},{"t":"octahedral molecular geometry","note":"6 bonds, no lone pairs"}]},{"text":"**Count electron domains.** Bonds plus lone pairs on the central atom set the electron geometry; only the bonded atoms set the molecular shape."},{"text":"**6 bonds, 0 lone pairs = octahedral.** TeF₆: Te's six valence electrons all go into six Te–F bonds."},{"text":"**Also:** 4 bonds + 2 lone pairs is square planar: in ICl₄⁻ the lone pairs sit opposite each other."},{"text":"**Also:** 3 bonds + 2 lone pairs is T-shaped: ClF₃'s lone pairs take two equatorial positions of a trigonal bipyramid."},{"text":"**Also:** 4 bonds and no lone pairs is tetrahedral, as in SiCl₄ (109.5° angles)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count domains on the central atom; 6 bonds/0 LP octahedral, 4 bonds/2 LP square planar, 3 bonds/2 LP T-shaped, 4 bonds/0 LP tetrahedral."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-58a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:58#1"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:58"],"q":"A tube sealed at the upper end stands in ethanol (ρ = 790 kg/m³), and the air inside is pumped out. Air pressure is 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa and g = 9.8 m/s². How high can the ethanol rise?","choices":["13 m","0.76 m","1.3 m","7.8 × 10⁸ m"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Off by a factor of 10: recheck the powers of ten when dividing 10⁵ Pa by about 7.7 × 10³."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Multiplying P by ρ and g gives a height far beyond the atmosphere; solve P = ρgh for h by dividing."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"0.76 m is the mercury barometer height; ethanol is far less dense, so it rises much higher."}],"rule":"An evacuated column rises until ρgh equals air pressure: h = P/(ρg). Less dense liquids rise higher (water about 10 m, mercury 0.76 m).","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"h = P/(ρg) ≈ 10⁵ / (800 × 10) ≈ 12.5 m; the exact numbers land just above that."},"exp":"Nothing presses down on the liquid inside the tube, so it climbs until the pressure of the column at its base matches air pressure, **P = ρgh**, so the height is h = P/(ρg) = 1.01 × 10⁵ / (790 × 9.8) ≈ **13 m**, higher than water's 10.3 m because ethanol is less dense.","how":{"ask":"Set the column's pressure ρgh equal to air pressure and solve for h.","know":"• A column under vacuum is held up by air pressure: **P_atm = ρgh**.\n• **h = P/(ρg)**: about 10 m for water and 0.76 m for mercury at 1 atm.\n• Lower density → taller column.","steps":[{"text":"Set air pressure equal to the column's pressure.","hl":"Air pressure is 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa","calc":["P = ρgh"]},{"text":"Solve for h.","calc":["h = 1.01 × 10⁵ / (790 × 9.8)","= 1.01 × 10⁵ / 7.7 × 10³ ≈ **13 m**"]},{"text":"1.3 m slips a power of ten; 7.8 × 10⁸ m multiplies instead of dividing; 0.76 m is mercury's height.","strike":["1.3 m","7.8 × 10⁸ m","0.76 m"]},{"text":"Answer: 13 m."}],"trap":"Recalling the 0.76 m mercury height: that works only for mercury's high density."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"pumped out","note":"vacuum above the liquid"},{"t":"ρ = 790 kg/m³","note":"ρ"},{"t":"1.01 × 10⁵ Pa","note":"P"}]},{"text":"**Pressure balance.** Air pushes on the open surface; nothing pushes down inside the tube except the liquid's own weight.","calc":["P_atm = ρgh"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** h = P/(ρg); the less dense the liquid, the taller the column air pressure can hold up."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-59a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:59#1","pe3-cp:59#2","pe3-cp:59#3"],"twinOf":["pe3-cp:59"],"q":"Consider these period-2 elements and their melting points: Li 180 °C; B 2076 °C; F₂ −220 °C; Ne −249 °C. Which one is a covalent network solid in its standard state, and why?","choices":["Boron: a 3-D net of B–B bonds","Lithium: it is solid at 25 °C","Fluorine: F–F bonds join atoms","Neon: its octet is complete"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Lithium is a metal: its atoms are held by a sea of delocalized electrons, not by localized covalent bonds."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Each F–F bond joins only two atoms into an F₂ molecule; weak forces between molecules give the very low melting point."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A full octet leaves neon as lone, unbonded atoms held only by weak dispersion forces."}],"rule":"Network solids melt very high because melting breaks covalent bonds; small molecules and noble gases melt far below 0 °C, and metals bond metallically.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The highest melting point among the nonmetals flags a covalent network."},"exp":"**Boron** forms a covalent network: each B atom is covalently bonded to several neighbors in a 3-D framework, so it melts only when covalent bonds break, hence **2076 °C**. F₂ and Ne are discrete molecules or atoms held by weak dispersion forces; lithium is a **metallic** solid.","how":{"ask":"Sort the elements by bonding type (network, molecular, atomic, metallic), using the melting points as a check.","know":"• **Covalent network solids** (diamond and graphite C, B, Si, SiO₂): every atom bonded covalently to several neighbors; very high melting points.\n• **Molecular** elements (N₂, O₂, F₂) and noble gases: weak forces between particles; low melting points.\n• **Metals** (Li, Be): metallic bonding, not covalent.","steps":[{"text":"Sort by melting point: thousands of degrees signals a network.","hl":"B 2076 °C"},{"text":"F₂ and Ne melt far below zero: separate molecules and lone atoms.","strike":["Fluorine: F–F bonds join atoms","Neon: its octet is complete"]},{"text":"Lithium is solid, but its bonding is metallic, not covalent.","strike":["Lithium: it is solid at 25 °C"]},{"text":"Answer: boron."}],"trap":"Taking \"solid at room temperature\" as proof of a network: metals and ionic solids are solids too."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"B 2076 °C","note":"very high: network"},{"t":"F₂ −220 °C","note":"molecular"},{"t":"covalent network solid","note":"atoms linked in a 3-D web"}]},{"text":"**Bonding type sets the melting point.** Breaking covalent bonds takes far more energy than separating molecules."},{"text":"**Also:** in period 2, carbon (diamond, graphite) is the classic network solid alongside boron; N₂ and O₂ are small molecules, and P or I can be ruled out at once because they are not in period 2."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a covalent network solid has every atom covalently bonded to several neighbors, so it melts at very high temperature."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-01a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:1#1","pe4-cp:1#2","pe4-cp:1#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:1"],"passage":"Ammonium chloride is used to lay down thin salt films, because on heating the solid breaks down into two gases: NH₄Cl(s) → NH₃(g) + HCl(g) (Reaction 1). For Reaction 1, ΔH° = +176 kJ/mol and ΔS° = +285 J/(mol·K). In a test, a weighed sample was held in a furnace at 450 K under standard conditions for an hour. The solid was recovered with no loss of mass, so no net decomposition had occurred.","q":"Which statement best describes Reaction 1 in the 450 K furnace test?","choices":["Nonspontaneous; ΔG ≈ +48 kJ/mol","Spontaneous; ΔG ≈ −1.3 × 10⁵ kJ/mol","Spontaneous; ΔG ≈ −48 kJ/mol","Nonspontaneous; ΔG ≈ +304 kJ/mol"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Multiplying 450 K by 285 without turning J into kJ inflates TΔS a thousandfold and flips the verdict."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is TΔS − ΔH: the subtraction runs the wrong way, so the sign of ΔG flips."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Adding TΔS to ΔH treats the entropy term as a cost; in ΔG = ΔH − TΔS it is subtracted."}],"rule":"Put ΔS in kJ, multiply by T, then subtract from ΔH. ΔG > 0 (ΔH larger) means no reaction; it turns spontaneous only above T = ΔH/ΔS.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"ΔS = 0.285 kJ/(mol·K), so TΔS = 450 × 0.285 ≈ 128 kJ/mol, below ΔH = 176. ΔG = 176 − 128 > 0: no decomposition, as observed."},"exp":"Convert ΔS to kJ first: TΔS = 450 K × 0.285 kJ/(mol·K) ≈ **128 kJ/mol**. That is less than ΔH = **176 kJ/mol**, so ΔG = ΔH − TΔS ≈ **+48 kJ/mol**: the reaction is **nonspontaneous** at 450 K, which is why the solid kept its mass. It becomes spontaneous only above T = ΔH/ΔS ≈ 618 K.","how":{"ask":"Decide the sign of ΔG at 450 K by comparing the enthalpy term with the entropy term.","know":"• **ΔG = ΔH − TΔS**; ΔG < 0 is spontaneous, ΔG > 0 is not.\n• ΔH is in kJ but ΔS is usually in J: divide ΔS by 1000 first.\n• With ΔH > 0 and ΔS > 0, the reaction turns spontaneous only above **T = ΔH/ΔS**.","steps":[{"text":"Pull ΔH and ΔS from the passage and put ΔS in kJ.","hl":"ΔH° = +176 kJ/mol and ΔS° = +285 J/(mol·K)","calc":["ΔS = 285 J/(mol·K) = 0.285 kJ/(mol·K)"]},{"text":"Compute the entropy term at the furnace temperature.","hl":"at 450 K","calc":["TΔS = 450 × 0.285 ≈ **128 kJ/mol**","ΔH = 176 kJ/mol > TΔS"]},{"text":"Subtract: ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.","calc":["ΔG ≈ 176 − 128 = **+48 kJ/mol** > 0"]},{"text":"TΔS only dwarfs ΔH if you forget the J → kJ step.","strike":["Spontaneous; ΔG ≈ −1.3 × 10⁵ kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Subtracting the wrong way, or adding the two terms, misuses ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.","strike":["Spontaneous; ΔG ≈ −48 kJ/mol","Nonspontaneous; ΔG ≈ +304 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Answer: Nonspontaneous; ΔG ≈ +48 kJ/mol, matching the unchanged mass."}],"trap":"Seeing a gas-forming, entropy-raising reaction and calling it spontaneous without multiplying by T: at low temperature the endothermic ΔH still wins."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"ΔH° = +176 kJ/mol","note":"endothermic"},{"t":"ΔS° = +285 J/(mol·K)","note":"J, not kJ"},{"t":"at 450 K","note":"the T to use"},{"t":"no net decomposition","note":"the observation: ΔG > 0"}]},{"text":"**Compare the two terms** in ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, in the same units.","calc":["TΔS = 450 × 0.285 ≈ 128 kJ/mol","ΔG ≈ 176 − 128 = +48 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"**Find the crossover.** Above T = ΔH/ΔS the entropy term wins and the solid would break down.","calc":["T = 176 ÷ 0.285 ≈ 618 K"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for ΔH > 0 and ΔS > 0, the reaction is nonspontaneous while ΔH > TΔS and turns spontaneous above T = ΔH/ΔS; always put ΔS in kJ first."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-02a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:2#1","pe4-cp:2#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:2"],"passage":"Blue copper(II) sulfate pentahydrate is used as a moisture indicator. In a closed jar it slowly loses water to form a paler trihydrate: CuSO₄·5H₂O(s) ⇌ CuSO₄·3H₂O(s) + 2 H₂O(g) (Reaction 2). A jar holding both solids and water vapor was brought to equilibrium at 298 K. More pentahydrate crystals were then dropped in while the temperature was held constant.","q":"Which expression gives K for Reaction 2, and how does the water vapor pressure respond to the added crystals?","choices":["K = (P_H₂O)²; no change","K = (P_H₂O)²; P_H₂O rises","K = 2·P_H₂O; no change","K includes both solids; P_H₂O rises"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A coefficient in the equation becomes an exponent in K, not a multiplier."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Pure solids have activity 1 and are left out of K, so neither hydrate appears in the expression."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Adding a pure solid does not change its activity, so Q still equals K and nothing shifts."}],"rule":"Leave pure solids and liquids out of K; raise each gas pressure to its coefficient. Adding or removing a pure solid never shifts the equilibrium.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Only the gas survives in K, squared for its coefficient of 2. Solids are fixed at activity 1, so more crystals leave Q = K: the vapor pressure stays put."},"exp":"Pure solids are left out of the equilibrium expression, so only the gas remains, raised to its coefficient: **K = (P_H₂O)²**. A pure solid's activity is 1 however much is present, so adding pentahydrate leaves Q = K and **P_H₂O does not change**.","how":{"ask":"Write K from the balanced equation, then decide whether adding a solid shifts the equilibrium.","know":"• K uses gas pressures (or solution concentrations) raised to their coefficients.\n• **Pure solids and liquids are left out** (activity = 1).\n• Adding more of a pure solid changes neither Q nor K, so nothing shifts.","steps":[{"text":"Find the phases in Reaction 2.","hl":"CuSO₄·5H₂O(s) ⇌ CuSO₄·3H₂O(s) + 2 H₂O(g)","calc":["two solids (left out) + one gas, coefficient 2"]},{"text":"Write K: only the gas, with its coefficient as an exponent.","calc":["K = (P_H₂O)²"]},{"text":"The coefficient is an exponent, and the solids stay out of K.","strike":["K = 2·P_H₂O; no change","K includes both solids; P_H₂O rises"]},{"text":"Extra pure solid leaves Q = K, so the vapor pressure does not rise.","hl":"More pentahydrate crystals were then dropped in","strike":["K = (P_H₂O)²; P_H₂O rises"]},{"text":"Answer: K = (P_H₂O)²; no change."}],"trap":"Applying Le Châtelier to a solid: adding more of a pure solid does not raise its \"concentration\", so it pushes the equilibrium nowhere."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"(s)","note":"solids: out of K"},{"t":"2 H₂O(g)","note":"gas, exponent 2"},{"t":"held constant","note":"K is fixed"}]},{"text":"**Build K.** Gases and dissolved species only, each raised to its coefficient.","calc":["K = (P_H₂O)²"]},{"text":"**Test the change.** A pure solid's activity is always 1, so adding it changes neither Q nor K: no shift. Only a change in a gas pressure, a concentration or the temperature can shift it."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pure solids and liquids are left out of K, and adding or removing them never shifts the equilibrium."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-03a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:3#1","pe4-cp:3#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:3"],"passage":"Phosphate buffers are common in cell-culture media. Their main buffering pair is dihydrogen phosphate and hydrogen phosphate. In water, dihydrogen phosphate gives up a proton: H₂PO₄⁻ + H₂O → HPO₄²⁻ + H₃O⁺ (Reaction 1). A student claims that the phosphorus atom is oxidized in this step, because the ion loses a hydrogen.","q":"In Reaction 1, what happens to phosphorus's oxidation state, and what type of reaction is this?","choices":["Stays +5; acid–base","Stays +5; redox","Rises to +6; redox","Rises to +7; acid–base"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"P is in group 15, with five valence electrons, so +5 is its highest oxidation state; +6 is impossible."},{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"P can't exceed +5 (group 15), so +7 is impossible."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Nothing changes oxidation state; only a proton is transferred, so the reaction is not redox."}],"rule":"If only H⁺ moves between species, no oxidation state changes, so it isn't redox. A group 15 element tops out at +5.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"If only an H⁺ moves (staying +1), nothing changes oxidation state: acid–base, not redox. P is +5 both times (2 + P − 8 = −1)."},"exp":"In H₂PO₄⁻: 2(+1) + P + 4(−2) = −1, so P = +5. In HPO₄²⁻: (+1) + P + 4(−2) = −2, so P = +5. **Only an H⁺ moves**, with its oxidation state of +1 unchanged, so no element changes oxidation state: it is **acid–base, not redox**.","how":{"ask":"Assign P's oxidation state before and after, then decide whether anything was oxidized or reduced.","know":"• Oxidation states: H = +1, O = −2; they add up to the species' charge.\n• **Redox** needs an element whose oxidation state changes. Moving H⁺ (still +1) changes no oxidation state: that is **acid–base**.","steps":[{"text":"Before: phosphorus in the acid.","hl":"H₂PO₄⁻","calc":["2(+1) + P + 4(−2) = −1","P = −1 − 2 + 8 = **+5**"]},{"text":"After: phosphorus in the conjugate base.","hl":"HPO₄²⁻","calc":["(+1) + P + 4(−2) = −2","P = −2 − 1 + 8 = **+5**"]},{"text":"P stays +5, so the rising values are wrong.","strike":["Rises to +6; redox","Rises to +7; acid–base"]},{"text":"Nothing changes oxidation state; only a proton is transferred, so the reaction is not redox.","strike":["Stays +5; redox"]},{"text":"Answer: Stays +5; acid–base."}],"trap":"Thinking that losing a bond to H oxidizes the central atom: the H leaves as H⁺, taking no electrons, so the central atom's oxidation state is unchanged."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"gives up a proton","note":"H⁺ transfer"},{"t":"H₂PO₄⁻","note":"charge −1"},{"t":"HPO₄²⁻","note":"charge −2"}]},{"text":"**Assign oxidation states.** H = +1, O = −2, and the sum equals the charge.","calc":["H₂PO₄⁻: P = +5","HPO₄²⁻: P = +5"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same holds for nitrogen: in NH₃, N + 3(+1) = 0 → N = −3; in NH₄⁺, N + 4(+1) = +1 → N = −3. Gaining H⁺ leaves N at −3.","calc":["NH₃: N = −3","NH₄⁺: N = −3"]},{"text":"**Classify.** No oxidation state changes and only H⁺ moves: acid–base, not redox."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a reaction is redox only if some element's oxidation state changes; a pure H⁺ transfer is acid–base."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-04a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:4#1","pe4-cp:4#2","pe6-cp:26#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:4"],"passage":"Portable camp stoves burn propane from small steel canisters. When enough air reaches the flame, combustion is complete: C₃H₈ + 5 O₂ → 4 H₂O + 3 CO₂. In a test, a stove burned 8.8 g of propane (44 g/mol) under a ventilated hood. The exhaust was collected, the water was condensed out, and the gas volume was reported at STP.","q":"What volume of CO₂ was released in the test?","choices":["4.5 L","13.4 L","14.7 L","17.9 L"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"Too small: 4.5 L is 0.20 × 22.4, the propane moles; the × 3 for 3 CO₂ per propane was skipped."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"14.7 L uses 24.5 L/mol, the molar volume at 25 °C; at STP a mole of gas is 22.4 L."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"17.9 L is 0.80 × 22.4, the 4 H₂O coefficient, not the 3 CO₂."}],"rule":"Moles of fuel × the CO₂ coefficient × 22.4 L at STP. Round the grams and molar mass to easy numbers, and 22.4 to about 22.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"22.4 L/mol at STP. 8.8 ÷ 44 = 0.2 mol propane; × 3 = 0.6 mol CO₂; 0.6 × 22.4 = 13.44 ≈ 13.4 L."},"exp":"8.8 g ÷ 44 g/mol = 0.20 mol propane. The equation gives 3 CO₂ per propane: 0.60 mol CO₂. **At STP one mole of ideal gas occupies 22.4 L**: 0.60 × 22.4 = 13.44 ≈ **13.4 L**.","how":{"ask":"Convert grams to moles, use the coefficients to get moles of CO₂, then multiply by the STP molar volume.","know":"• moles = mass ÷ molar mass.\n• The coefficients give the **mole ratio** of product to reactant.\n• **V = n × 22.4 L/mol**, true only at STP (0 °C, 1 atm); at 25 °C the molar volume is about 24.5 L/mol.","steps":[{"text":"Moles of propane.","hl":"8.8 g of propane (44 g/mol)","calc":["n = 8.8 g ÷ 44 g/mol = **0.20 mol**"]},{"text":"Use the ratio from the equation.","hl":"3 CO₂","calc":["n(CO₂) = 0.20 mol × 3 = **0.60 mol**"]},{"text":"Convert to volume at STP.","hl":"at STP","calc":["V = 0.60 mol × 22.4 L/mol = 13.44 L ≈ **13.4 L**"]},{"text":"4.5 L skips the 3 : 1 ratio (0.20 × 22.4); 17.9 L uses the water coefficient (0.80 × 22.4); 14.7 L uses 24.5 L/mol, the molar volume at 25 °C, not at STP.","strike":["4.5 L","17.9 L","14.7 L"]},{"text":"Answer: 13.4 L."}],"trap":"Using 22.4 L/mol without the mole ratio, or at a temperature other than STP."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"8.8 g","note":"mass burned"},{"t":"44 g/mol","note":"molar mass"},{"t":"3 CO₂","note":"3 mol CO₂ per mol propane"},{"t":"at STP","note":"22.4 L/mol applies"}]},{"text":"**Grams to moles.**","calc":["8.8 ÷ 44 = 0.20 mol"]},{"text":"**Moles of reactant to moles of product** with the coefficients.","calc":["0.20 × 3 = 0.60 mol CO₂"]},{"text":"**Moles to liters at STP.** One mole of any ideal gas fills 22.4 L at 0 °C and 1 atm; at other conditions use PV = nRT instead.","calc":["0.60 × 22.4 = 13.4 L"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same coefficients give the oxygen used up. Moles of O₂ = moles of fuel × (O₂ coefficient ÷ fuel coefficient), then × 22.4 L/mol at STP.","calc":["0.20 mol C₃H₈ × 5/1 = 1.00 mol O₂","V(O₂) = 1.00 mol × 22.4 L/mol = 22.4 L at STP"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** V(gas, STP) = (moles of reactant × product/reactant coefficient ratio) × 22.4 L/mol, and 22.4 L/mol holds at STP only."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-05a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe4-cp:5#1","pe4-cp:5#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:5"],"passage":"A student follows NADH by its absorbance at 340 nm. The spectrophotometer is blanked with buffer in a clean quartz cuvette. The sample is then read in a second cuvette whose wall is clouded by a fingerprint, giving A = 0.71. That cuvette is then emptied, rinsed and filled with buffer alone, and it reads A = 0.09.","q":"What absorbance comes from NADH itself?","choices":["0.62: the smudge adds 0.09","0.71: the blank was buffer","0.80: smudges scatter light","0.09: only the gap counts"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"The smudged cuvette adds its own absorbance to the reading, so it must be subtracted, not added."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"The blank was read in a different, clean cuvette, so it skips the smudged cuvette's own absorbance."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"That copies the empty smudged cuvette's reading, which contains no NADH at all."}],"rule":"A reading is analyte + everything else in the light path. Blank in the same (or an optically identical) cuvette, then subtract what the cuvette and buffer give alone.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Absorbances add along the light path: sample reading − smudged cuvette with buffer = NADH alone."},"exp":"Absorbances in the light path **add**. The reading of 0.71 includes the smudged wall, which absorbs or scatters 0.09 on its own (measured with buffer only). The NADH contributes **0.71 − 0.09 = 0.62**. This is why reference and sample cuvettes must be **optically identical**: then the only difference between the two readings is the substance being measured.","how":{"ask":"Separate the analyte's absorbance from the extra absorbance of the flawed cuvette.","know":"• Absorbances along one light path add: A(total) = A(analyte) + A(cuvette, buffer).\n• Blanking zeroes only what was in the blank cuvette.\n• Matched (optically identical) cuvettes make the sample and reference differ only in the analyte.","steps":[{"text":"The total reading in the smudged cuvette.","hl":"giving A = 0.71"},{"text":"What the smudged cuvette gives with no NADH.","hl":"it reads A = 0.09"},{"text":"Subtract the cuvette's share.","calc":["A(NADH) = 0.71 − 0.09 = **0.62**"]},{"text":"The cuvette's absorbance is extra, so adding it is backwards; the clean-cuvette blank never removed it; and the buffer-only reading contains no NADH.","strike":["0.80: smudges scatter light","0.71: the blank was buffer","0.09: only the gap counts"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.62: the smudge adds 0.09."}],"trap":"Trusting the blank: zeroing on a clean cuvette does not cancel a flaw in a different cuvette."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"blanked with buffer in a clean quartz cuvette","note":"zero set on another cuvette"},{"t":"clouded by a fingerprint","note":"adds its own A"},{"t":"filled with buffer alone","note":"measures the flaw by itself"}]},{"text":"**Absorbances add.** The beam passes the wall, the buffer and the NADH; each absorbs or scatters a share.","calc":["0.71 = A(NADH) + 0.09"]},{"text":"**Why matched cuvettes:** if both cuvettes are matched, the cuvette's share cancels and what is left between the two readings is the analyte alone."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** sample and reference must differ only in the analyte; any extra absorber in the sample's path (a scratch, a smudge, a different cuvette) must be measured and subtracted."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-06a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:6#1","pe4-cp:6#2","pe4-cp:6#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:6"],"passage":"Cyanobacteria harvest light with phycocyanin, a blue protein pigment. Phycocyanin absorbs orange light most strongly, with its peak near 620 nm, where chlorophyll absorbs poorly. The absorbed energy is passed along to chlorophyll in the reaction center. Useful values: h = 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s and c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s, so hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm.","q":"About how much energy is carried by one photon at phycocyanin's absorption peak?","choices":["0.50 eV","2.0 eV","20 eV","3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ eV"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"That is the energy in joules with an eV label; a visible photon carries a few eV."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"A power-of-ten slip in the nm conversion; a visible photon carries only a few eV, not tens."},{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Dividing λ by hc inverts the relation; energy falls as wavelength grows, E = hc/λ."}],"rule":"E(eV) ≈ 1240/λ(nm). Visible light (400–700 nm) gives about 1.8–3.1 eV, so any other size is a units slip.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"E (eV) ≈ 1240 ÷ λ (nm) = 1240 ÷ 620 = 2.0 eV, which sits inside the visible range of about 1.8–3.1 eV."},"exp":"E = hc/λ. With hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm, E = 1240 eV·nm ÷ 620 nm = **2.0 eV**. In joules this is 2.0 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J; quoting that number in eV is the units trap.","how":{"ask":"Convert the absorption wavelength into photon energy in electron-volts.","know":"• **E = hc/λ**: shorter wavelength, more energy.\n• hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm, so **E(eV) ≈ 1240/λ(nm)**.\n• In joules: E = (6.6 × 10⁻³⁴)(3.0 × 10⁸)/λ in m; divide by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV.\n• Visible photons: about 1.8–3.1 eV.","steps":[{"text":"Take the peak wavelength from the passage.","hl":"its peak near 620 nm"},{"text":"Use the eV·nm shortcut.","hl":"hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm","calc":["E = 1240 ÷ 620 = **2.0 eV**"]},{"text":"A joule value labeled eV and a tenfold slip are both outside the visible range.","strike":["3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ eV","20 eV"]},{"text":"Dividing λ by hc inverts E = hc/λ.","strike":["0.50 eV"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.0 eV."}],"trap":"Stopping at joules: hc/λ in SI units gives about 3 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, which must still be divided by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"peak near 620 nm","note":"λ to use"},{"t":"hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm","note":"shortcut"}]},{"text":"**Long route, in joules.**","calc":["E = (4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s)(3.0 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ (620 × 10⁻⁹ m)","E ≈ 2.0 eV (≈ 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J)"]},{"text":"**Sanity check.** Red ≈ 700 nm ≈ 1.8 eV; violet ≈ 400 nm ≈ 3.1 eV. Orange light must fall in between."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E(eV) ≈ 1240/λ(nm); to go from joules to eV, divide by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-07a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:7#1","pe4-cp:7#2","pe4-cp:8#1","pe4-cp:8#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:7"],"passage":"Blood glucose can be measured with an enzyme assay that turns glucose into a colored dye. Glucose standards of 0.8, 1.6 and 2.4 mM read A = 0.16, 0.32 and 0.48, and the reagent blank reads zero. A serum sample read 0.84, above the top standard. The sample was therefore diluted 10-fold and reread at 0.28.","q":"What was the serum sample's original glucose concentration?","choices":["1.4 mM","4.2 mM","14 mM","42 mM"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"1.4 mM is the diluted sample; the 10-fold dilution must be multiplied back."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"4.2 mM comes from 0.84, which lies above the top standard (0.48), off the calibrated line."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"42 mM converts the out-of-range 0.84 reading and then multiplies by 10; only the in-range reread is valid."}],"rule":"Use only a reading inside the standards' range; convert it with the slope of the standards, then multiply by the dilution factor.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"A ∝ c: the standards give 0.2 A per mM. Use the in-range diluted reading: 0.28 ÷ 0.2 = 1.4 mM; × 10 for the dilution = 14 mM."},"exp":"The standards rise 0.20 absorbance unit per mM (**A ∝ c**). The first reading, 0.84, lies above the top standard, so it cannot be trusted; the diluted reading, 0.28, lies inside the range and gives 0.28 ÷ 0.20 = 1.4 mM. Undo the 10-fold dilution: 1.4 × 10 = **14 mM**.","how":{"ask":"Read the in-range reading off the standard line, then multiply by the dilution factor.","know":"• **Beer's law**: A = εbc, so at a fixed path length **A ∝ c**; the standards give the slope (absorbance per unit concentration).\n• Only **interpolate**: a reading above the highest standard must be diluted into the standard range, never read off an extended line.\n• Original concentration = **diluted concentration × dilution factor**.","steps":[{"text":"The standards set the line; find its slope.","hl":"0.8, 1.6 and 2.4 mM read A = 0.16, 0.32 and 0.48","calc":["slope = 0.16 ÷ 0.8 = **0.20 per mM**","check: 0.48 ÷ 2.4 = 0.20 per mM"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Glucose standards: absorbance rises in step with concentration","x":{"label":"Glucose (mM)","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Absorbance","min":0,"max":0.6,"step":0.1},"series":[{"name":"Standards (with blank)","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[0.8,0.16],[1.6,0.32],[2.4,0.48]]}]}},{"text":"The first reading lies above the top standard (0.48), outside the proven straight range, so it is not used.","hl":"A serum sample read 0.84"},{"text":"Use the diluted reading, which falls inside the range.","hl":"reread at 0.28","calc":["c(diluted) = A ÷ slope","c(diluted) = 0.28 ÷ 0.20 per mM = **1.4 mM**"]},{"text":"Undo the dilution.","hl":"diluted 10-fold","calc":["c(original) = 1.4 mM × 10 = **14 mM**"]},{"text":"1.4 mM forgets to multiply back by the dilution factor. 4.2 mM extrapolates the line to the out-of-range 0.84 (0.84 ÷ 0.20) and 42 mM then multiplies that by 10 as well.","strike":["1.4 mM","4.2 mM","42 mM"]},{"text":"Answer: 14 mM."}],"trap":"Reporting the concentration in the cuvette (1.4 mM): the sample was diluted, so multiply by 10."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"0.8, 1.6 and 2.4 mM","note":"standard concentrations"},{"t":"0.16, 0.32 and 0.48","note":"their absorbances"},{"t":"read 0.84","note":"above the top standard"},{"t":"diluted 10-fold","note":"dilution factor"},{"t":"reread at 0.28","note":"the in-range reading"}]},{"text":"**The standards give the slope.** Absorbance is proportional to concentration, so divide any standard's A by its c.","calc":["0.32 ÷ 1.6 = 0.20 per mM"]},{"text":"**Interpolate, don't extrapolate.** The standards only show the line is straight up to A = 0.48. A reading above that is diluted until it lands between the standards."},{"text":"**Read, then multiply back.** The line gives the concentration in the diluted sample; the dilution factor returns it to the original.","calc":["0.28 ÷ 0.20 = 1.4 mM","1.4 mM × 10 = 14 mM"]},{"text":"**Also:** with no reading above range, the same two steps apply: a sample diluted 5-fold that reads A = 0.24 contains 0.24 ÷ 0.20 = 1.2 mM in the cuvette and 1.2 × 5 = 6.0 mM originally."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** c(original) = (A ÷ slope of the standards) × dilution factor, using only a reading that falls inside the range of the standards."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-08a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe4-cp:8#1","pe4-cp:8#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:8"],"passage":"A colorimetric assay measures phosphate in pond water. Standards of 0, 5, 10, 15 and 20 μM phosphate give the absorbances plotted in the chart, which rise in a straight line up to A = 0.80. A sample taken just after a fertilizer spill reads A = 1.32 in the same cuvette. The analyst must report its phosphate concentration.","q":"Which procedure gives the most reliable phosphate value for the spill sample?","choices":["Extrapolate the line to A = 1.32","Use 20 μM, the highest standard","Dilute 4-fold, rerun, scale by 4","Lengthen the light path fivefold"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"A longer path raises A (A = εbc), pushing the reading even further above the standards."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"No standard was run above 0.80, so the line is not known to stay straight that high; extrapolating skips that check."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"That copies the top standard; the sample read well above it, so its concentration is higher."}],"rule":"Read unknowns only inside the calibrated range: interpolate, never extrapolate. Too high → dilute into range, then multiply back by the dilution factor.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Bring the reading inside the standards: 1.32 ÷ 4 ≈ 0.33, inside 0–0.80. Read that value off the line, then multiply by 4."},"exp":"The standards prove the line is straight only up to **A = 0.80**. A reading of 1.32 lies past that range, where Beer's law may fail. A **fourfold dilution** brings it near A ≈ 0.33, inside the range; the line gives about 8.3 μM, and **multiplying by 4** undoes the dilution (about 33 μM). A longer path or extrapolation does not keep the reading inside the calibrated range.","how":{"ask":"Choose the step that brings the sample's reading into the range the standards cover, then recovers the original concentration.","know":"• A calibration line is trusted only **between** its lowest and highest standards (interpolation).\n• Above the top standard: **dilute** (or shorten the path) to bring A into range.\n• Multiply the result by the dilution factor to get the original concentration.","steps":[{"text":"The standards reach only A = 0.80.","hl":"rise in a straight line up to A = 0.80"},{"text":"The sample reads above that.","hl":"reads A = 1.32 in the same cuvette"},{"text":"A longer path makes A larger still, and the line beyond 0.80 was never tested.","strike":["Lengthen the light path fivefold","Extrapolate the line to A = 1.32"]},{"text":"The sample is more concentrated than the top standard, so it cannot be reported as that standard.","strike":["Use 20 μM, the highest standard"]},{"text":"A fourfold dilution puts it near A ≈ 0.33, inside the range; multiply the result by 4.","calc":["1.32 ÷ 4 ≈ 0.33 → ≈ 8.3 μM on the line","8.3 μM × 4 ≈ 33 μM"]},{"text":"Answer: Dilute 4-fold, rerun, scale by 4."}],"trap":"Extending the line: Beer's law often bends at high absorbance, so a value read past the top standard is unreliable."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"up to A = 0.80","note":"top of the calibrated range"},{"t":"reads A = 1.32","note":"out of range"}]},{"text":"**Interpolate, don't extrapolate.** Read unknowns only between the standards."},{"text":"**Dilute into range, then multiply back.**","calc":["dilution factor 4 → reading ≈ 0.33","result × 4 = original"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** when a sample reads above the top standard, dilute it into the calibrated range, rerun, and multiply by the dilution factor."}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phosphate standards","x":{"label":"Phosphate (μM)","min":0,"max":40,"step":5},"y":{"label":"Absorbance","min":0,"max":1.4,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Standards","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[5,0.2],[10,0.4],[15,0.6],[20,0.8]]}]}},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-09a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:9#1","pe4-cp:9#2","pe4-cp:9#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:9"],"q":"The four isomers of formula C₅H₁₀O shown are separated by fractional distillation at atmospheric pressure. Which isomer leaves the column first?","choices":["Tetrahydropyran","Pentan-3-one","Pentanal","Cyclopentyl alcohol"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"The O–H group hydrogen-bonds, giving the highest boiling point, so this isomer distills last, not first."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A ketone C=O makes a strong dipole, so dipole–dipole attraction raises its boiling point above that of an ether."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An aldehyde C=O is strongly polar too, so it boils higher than a weakly polar ether of the same mass."}],"rule":"Same mass, so rank by intermolecular forces: H-bonding (O–H, N–H) > strong dipoles (C=O) > weak dipoles (ethers). Lowest boiling point distills first.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Equal masses: the fraction that comes over first is the one with the weakest attractions. No O–H and only a weak C–O–C dipole: the cyclic ether."},"exp":"Fractional distillation separates liquids by **boiling point**; the lowest-boiling one distills first. All four have the same formula, so dispersion forces are similar and polarity decides. Cyclopentanol **hydrogen-bonds** (bp ≈ 140 °C). The ketone and aldehyde have strong C=O dipoles (bp ≈ 102–103 °C). The cyclic ether tetrahydropyran has only a weak C–O–C dipole and no O–H (bp ≈ 88 °C), so it comes over **first**.","how":{"ask":"Find the isomer with the lowest boiling point, since it vaporizes and reaches the top of the column first.","know":"• Fractional distillation separates by **boiling point**.\n• At similar molar mass: **H-bonding** (alcohols, acids, amines) > **dipole–dipole** (ketones, aldehydes) > weak dipoles (ethers, alkyl halides).\n• An alcohol boils far higher than an ether or alkyl halide of similar mass.","steps":[{"text":"All four share a formula, so their masses are equal.","hl":"formula C₅H₁₀O"},{"text":"Classify each by its strongest attraction.","calc":["cyclopentanol: O–H → H-bonds","3-pentanone, pentanal: C=O → strong dipoles","tetrahydropyran: C–O–C → weak dipole only"]},{"text":"The H-bonding alcohol boils highest, so it comes over last.","strike":["Cyclopentyl alcohol"]},{"text":"The C=O compounds boil above the ether.","strike":["Pentan-3-one","Pentanal"]},{"text":"Answer: Tetrahydropyran."}],"trap":"Thinking a ring or a compact shape raises the boiling point here: with equal masses, polarity and H-bonding decide the order."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"isomers of formula C₅H₁₀O","note":"equal molar mass"},{"t":"fractional distillation","note":"separates by boiling point"},{"t":"leaves the column first","note":"lowest boiling point"}]},{"text":"**Rank the forces.** H-bonding beats dipole–dipole, which beats a weak dipole.","fig":"cyclopentanol","calc":["bp: cyclopentanol ≈ 140 °C","pentanal ≈ 103 °C, 3-pentanone ≈ 102 °C","tetrahydropyran ≈ 88 °C"]},{"text":"**Also:** an alcohol boils well above an alkyl halide or ether of similar mass, because only the alcohol has an O–H to hydrogen-bond."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a distillation, the liquid with the weakest intermolecular forces (lowest boiling point) collects first."}],"choiceFigs":["tetrahydropyran","3-pentanone","pentanal","cyclopentanol"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-10a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:10#1","pe4-cp:10#2","pe4-cp:10#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:10"],"q":"Four alcohols are each heated with chromic acid, H₂CrO₄, a strong oxidizing agent. Which alcohol is NOT oxidized?","choices":["Pentan-1-ol","Pentan-2-ol","3-Methylbutan-2-ol","2-Methylbutan-2-ol"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"'-1-ol' puts the OH on an end CH₂: a primary alcohol, readily oxidized."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Unbranched pentan-2-ol has its OH on a CH between two carbons: secondary, so it becomes a ketone."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"In 3-methylbutan-2-ol the OH carbon is a CH bonded to two carbons: secondary, so it oxidizes to a ketone."}],"rule":"Chromic acid needs an H on the carbon holding the OH: primary and secondary alcohols oxidize; tertiary alcohols do not.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Chromic acid needs an H on the C bearing the OH. A tertiary alcohol (that carbon has three C's, no H) won't oxidize: 2-methylbutan-2-ol."},"exp":"Oxidizing an alcohol removes **the H on the carbon bearing the OH** and turns C–OH into C=O. In **2-methylbutan-2-ol** that carbon carries three carbon groups and no H, so it is a tertiary alcohol and resists oxidation. Pentan-1-ol (primary) goes to pentanal and then pentanoic acid; the two secondary alcohols become ketones.","how":{"ask":"Classify each alcohol as 1°, 2° or 3° by counting the carbons on the carbinol carbon, then find the one with no H there.","know":"• **Carbinol carbon** = the carbon holding the OH. Oxidation needs an **H on that carbon**.\n• **1° alcohol → aldehyde → carboxylic acid** (a strong oxidant like H₂CrO₄ goes all the way to the acid).\n• **2° alcohol → ketone** (stops there).\n• **3° alcohol → no reaction** (no H on the carbinol carbon).","steps":[{"text":"The reagent is a strong oxidant, so any alcohol that can be oxidized will be.","hl":"chromic acid, H₂CrO₄"},{"text":"Pentan-1-ol: the OH carbon is CH₂, bonded to one carbon, so it is primary. It is oxidized to an aldehyde and then on to the acid.","calc":["CH₃(CH₂)₃CH₂OH → CH₃(CH₂)₃CHO → CH₃(CH₂)₃COOH"],"strike":["Pentan-1-ol"]},{"text":"Pentan-2-ol and 3-methylbutan-2-ol: the OH carbon is CH, bonded to two carbons, so both are secondary and become ketones.","calc":["CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₂CH₃ → pentan-2-one","CH₃CH(OH)CH(CH₃)₂ → 3-methylbutan-2-one"],"strike":["Pentan-2-ol","3-Methylbutan-2-ol"]},{"text":"2-Methylbutan-2-ol: the OH carbon holds two CH₃ groups and an ethyl, three carbons and no H. It is tertiary and cannot form a C=O without breaking a C–C bond.","calc":["(CH₃)₂C(OH)CH₂CH₃: carbinol C has 0 H → no oxidation"]},{"text":"Answer: 2-Methylbutan-2-ol."}],"trap":"Picking a branched secondary alcohol because it looks crowded: branching elsewhere does not matter; only the H on the carbinol carbon does."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"chromic acid","note":"strong oxidant"},{"t":"NOT oxidized","note":"look for the alcohol that cannot lose an H"}]},{"text":"**Find the carbinol carbon** in each name: the number before \"-ol\" tells you which carbon holds the OH. Count how many carbons are bonded to it: one = 1°, two = 2°, three = 3°."},{"text":"**Primary alcohols** have two H's on the carbinol carbon: they go to an **aldehyde**, and a strong oxidant carries them on to the **carboxylic acid**.","calc":["RCH₂OH → RCHO → RCOOH"]},{"text":"**Secondary alcohols** have one H there: they become a **ketone** and stop.","calc":["R₂CHOH → R₂C=O"]},{"text":"**Tertiary alcohols** have no H there: nothing to remove, so **no oxidation**.","calc":["R₃COH → no reaction"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an alcohol is oxidized only if its carbinol carbon carries an H: 1° gives aldehyde then acid, 2° gives ketone, 3° does not react."}],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pentan-1-ol","cpt-pentan-2-ol","cpt-3-methylbutan-2-ol","cpt-2-methylbutan-2-ol"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-11a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:11#1","pe4-cp:11#2","pe4-cp:11#3","pe4-cp:11#4"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:11"],"q":"In CH₃–CH=C(Br)–CH₂CH₃, shown, the bromine and the terminal CH₃ bonded to the CH carbon are cis to each other. What is the compound's name?","choices":["(Z)-3-bromopent-2-ene","(E)-3-bromopent-2-ene","(Z)-3-bromopent-3-ene","(E)-3-bromopent-3-ene"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"The higher-priority groups, Br and the C1 CH₃, are on the same side; E would need them opposite."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Both wrong: Br and the C1 CH₃ are cis, not E, and 'pent-3-ene' renumbers to a lower locant from the other end."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Locant rule: number from the end nearer the C=C; in a 5-carbon chain a C=C at 3 is at 2 counted from the other end."}],"rule":"Number from the end nearest the C=C, then compare the higher-priority group on each alkene carbon: same side = Z, opposite = E.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"E/Z compares the higher-priority group on each alkene C, not the main chain. C2: CH₃ > H; C3: Br > ethyl. Br cis to CH₃ → Z; lowest C=C locant → pent-2-ene."},"exp":"The five-carbon chain holds the C=C, and numbering from the CH₃–CH= end puts it at **C2**, with Br on **C3**: 3-bromopent-2-ene. On C2, **CH₃ beats H**; on C3, **Br (atomic number 35) beats the ethyl carbon (6)**. Those two winners are on the same side, so the alkene is **Z**, even though the methyl and the ethyl are on opposite sides.","how":{"ask":"Number the chain to give the C=C the lowest locant, name the substituent, then compare the higher-priority group on each alkene carbon.","know":"• **Parent chain**: the longest chain that contains the C=C, numbered from the end **nearer the C=C**.\n• **Substituents** get the locant of the carbon they sit on.\n• **CIP priority**: on each alkene carbon, the atom of higher atomic number wins.\n• **Z** = the two winners on the **same side**; **E** = opposite sides.","steps":[{"text":"The longest chain through the C=C has five carbons. From the left, the C=C starts at C2; from the right, at C3. Number from the left.","hl":"CH₃–CH=C(Br)–CH₂CH₃","calc":["left: C1H₃–C2H=C3(Br)–C4H₂–C5H₃ → C=C at 2 ✓","right: C=C at 3 ✗"]},{"text":"Numbering from the ethyl end gives the C=C a locant of 3, which is higher than needed.","strike":["(Z)-3-bromopent-3-ene","(E)-3-bromopent-3-ene"]},{"text":"The only substituent is Br, on C3.","calc":["3-bromopent-2-ene"]},{"text":"Rank the groups on each alkene carbon by the atomic number of the first atom.","calc":["C2: CH₃ (C, 6) > H (1) → CH₃ wins","C3: Br (35) > CH₂CH₃ (C, 6) → Br wins"]},{"text":"The winners, Br and the C1 CH₃, are on the same side, so the alkene is Z. Calling it E comes from comparing the two alkyl groups instead of Br.","hl":"are cis to each other","strike":["(E)-3-bromopent-2-ene"]},{"text":"Answer: (Z)-3-bromopent-2-ene."}],"trap":"Reading E/Z as cis/trans of the carbon chain: the ethyl and the methyl are on opposite sides, but Br outranks the ethyl, so the winners are cis and the name is Z."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"CH₃–CH=C(Br)–CH₂CH₃","note":"five carbons, one Br on an alkene carbon"},{"t":"the bromine and the terminal CH₃","note":"the groups whose positions are given"},{"t":"cis to each other","note":"same side; compare with the priorities"}]},{"text":"**Pick and number the chain.** Take the longest chain that includes both alkene carbons, and number from the end that reaches the C=C first.","calc":["C=C at 2 from the left vs 3 from the right → left"]},{"text":"**Name the substituents** with their locants and put them in front of the parent, in alphabetical order if there are several.","calc":["Br on C3 → 3-bromo + pent-2-ene"]},{"text":"**Rank by CIP.** On each alkene carbon, compare the two attached atoms; higher atomic number wins. If they tie, move out to the next atoms.","calc":["Br (35) > C (6) > H (1)"]},{"text":"**Assign Z or E.** Winners on the same side = Z (zusammen); on opposite sides = E (entgegen)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** number the longest chain containing the C=C from its nearer end, name the substituents, then put Z when the higher-priority groups on the two alkene carbons share a side and E when they do not."}],"fig":"cpt-z-3-bromopent-2-ene"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-12a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:12#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:12"],"q":"A hiker walks 11 km in a straight line, turns through any angle, then walks 7 km more. Which distance from the start is impossible?","choices":["4 km","10 km","18 km","19 km"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"4 km = 11 − 7, the turn-around case, so it can happen."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"10 km lies between 11 − 7 and 11 + 7, so some turn angle gives it."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"18 km = 11 + 7, walking straight on, so it can happen."}],"rule":"After two straight legs, the distance from the start lies between the difference and the sum of the legs; anything outside cannot happen.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Test the two extremes: turn straight back gives 11 − 7 = 4 km; keep going gives 11 + 7 = 18 km. Every result lies in 4–18, so 19 km can't happen."},"exp":"Two displacements add as vectors: the resultant lies between **|11 − 7| = 4 km** (turning back) and **11 + 7 = 18 km** (going straight on). **19 km** is outside that range, so it can't happen.","how":{"ask":"Find the smallest and largest possible magnitudes of the vector sum.","know":"• For vectors of magnitudes A and B: **|A − B| ≤ |A + B| ≤ A + B**.\n• Minimum when they point opposite (180°), maximum when parallel (0°); every value between is reachable at some angle.","steps":[{"text":"The two legs are vectors of 11 km and 7 km at an adjustable angle.","hl":"turns through any angle"},{"text":"Compute the two extremes.","calc":["min = |11 − 7| = **4 km** (walks back)","max = 11 + 7 = **18 km** (keeps straight)","at 90°: √(11² + 7²) = √170 ≈ 13 km"]},{"text":"4 km and 18 km are the ends of the range, and 10 km lies inside it.","strike":["4 km","18 km","10 km"]},{"text":"Answer: 19 km."}],"trap":"Thinking the endpoints are impossible: turning fully back or not at all gives exactly 4 km or 18 km."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words and numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"11 km","note":"vector A"},{"t":"7 km","note":"vector B"},{"t":"any angle","note":"the sum can vary"},{"t":"impossible","note":"look outside the range"}]},{"text":"**Smallest sum:** the vectors point opposite, so they subtract.","calc":["|A − B| = |11 − 7| = 4 km"]},{"text":"**Largest sum:** the vectors point the same way, so they add.","calc":["A + B = 11 + 7 = 18 km"]},{"text":"**Everything between is possible** at some angle; anything outside is not.","calc":["4 km ≤ R ≤ 18 km","19 km > 18 km → impossible"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** |A − B| ≤ |A + B| ≤ A + B; a magnitude outside that range is impossible."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-13a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe4-cp:13#1","pe4-cp:13#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:13"],"passage":"In a myopic (nearsighted) eye, light from distant objects comes to a focus short of the retina. The cornea supplies most of the eye's focusing power. In orthokeratology, rigid contact lenses worn overnight flatten the center of the cornea of a myopic eye so distant objects focus on the retina during the day. The effect fades within a few days if the lenses are not worn.","q":"What does flattening the cornea change?","choices":["Larger radius; lower power","Larger radius; higher power","Smaller radius; lower power","Smaller radius; higher power"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Higher power means a shorter focal length, which would pull the focus further forward and worsen myopia."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Flatter means less curved, part of a bigger sphere: the radius grows, not shrinks."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"A smaller radius means more curvature, the opposite of flattening."}],"rule":"A flatter curve belongs to a bigger circle, and a gentler curve bends light less: lens power falls as the radius grows.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Flatter = part of a bigger sphere = larger radius. Less curvature bends light less → longer f → lower power (P = 1/f), which is what a myopic eye needs."},"exp":"A **flatter** surface is part of a bigger sphere: its **radius of curvature increases**. A less curved surface bends light less, so the **focal length gets longer** (lower power, P = 1/f) and the focus moves back from in front of the retina onto it.","how":{"ask":"Link flatter shape → radius of curvature → focal length → where the image lands.","know":"• **Flatter = larger radius of curvature** (a flat surface has an infinite radius).\n• For a refracting surface or lens, **f grows with R** (lensmaker: 1/f ∝ 1/R); power P = 1/f falls.\n• **Myopia**: the eye is too converging, so the image forms in front of the retina; weakening it moves the focus back.","steps":[{"text":"Flattening means less curvature, so the radius of curvature grows.","hl":"flatten the center of the cornea of a myopic eye","calc":["flatter → R larger"]},{"text":"A larger radius bends light less: the cornea is less converging.","calc":["1/f ∝ 1/R → R up → f up","power P = 1/f → down"]},{"text":"A smaller radius means more curvature, the opposite of flattening.","strike":["Smaller radius; lower power","Smaller radius; higher power"]},{"text":"Higher power means a shorter focal length, which would pull the focus further forward and worsen myopia.","strike":["Larger radius; higher power"]},{"text":"Answer: larger radius; lower power (longer focal length)."}],"trap":"Pairing a larger radius with higher power (shorter focal length): a flatter surface is weaker, not stronger."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"myopic","note":"focus falls in front of the retina"},{"t":"flatten","note":"less curved surface"},{"t":"focus on the retina","note":"the goal"}]},{"text":"**Flatter means a bigger circle.** A gently curved surface belongs to a sphere with a large radius.","calc":["more curved → small R","flatter → large R"]},{"text":"**Bigger radius, weaker focusing.** Focal length rises with R, so the cornea converges light less.","calc":["R up → f up → power down"]},{"text":"**Also:** a myopic eye focuses too strongly, in front of the retina; a longer focal length moves the image back onto the retina.","calc":["too strong → focus in front","weaker cornea → focus moves back onto retina"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** flattening the cornea raises its radius of curvature, which lengthens the focal length and pushes a nearsighted eye's focus back onto the retina."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-14a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:14#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:14"],"passage":"Electrofusion joins two cells by briefly breaking down their membranes where they touch. The cells are lined up between two parallel plate electrodes set 5.0 mm apart. A 1.2 kV pulse lasting 50 μs is then applied across the electrodes. Between the plates the field is nearly uniform.","q":"How strong is the field between the electrodes during the pulse?","choices":["2.4 × 10² V/m","2.4 × 10⁵ V/m","2.4 × 10⁸ V/m","4.2 × 10⁻⁶ V/m"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"Leaving the gap in millimetres while V is in volts makes E a thousand times too small."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Treating mm as 10⁻⁶ m (a micrometre) makes the field a thousand times too large."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is d/V; the field is voltage divided by gap, E = V/d."}],"rule":"Uniform field between plates: E = V/d, with V in volts and d in metres (kV = 10³ V, mm = 10⁻³ m). Units V/m mean volts over metres.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Convert first: 1.2 kV = 1.2 × 10³ V and 5.0 mm = 5.0 × 10⁻³ m. Then E = V/d = (1.2/5.0) × 10⁶ V/m."},"exp":"Between parallel plates the field is uniform and **E = V/d**. In SI units, V = 1.2 × 10³ V and d = 5.0 × 10⁻³ m, so E = 1.2 × 10³ ÷ 5.0 × 10⁻³ = **2.4 × 10⁵ V/m**.","how":{"ask":"Divide the applied voltage by the plate gap, both in SI units.","know":"• Parallel plates: **E = V/d** (V/m = N/C).\n• **kV = 10³ V**; **mm = 10⁻³ m**.\n• The pulse length does not enter E.","steps":[{"text":"Pull the voltage and gap.","hl":"1.2 kV pulse","calc":["V = 1.2 × 10³ V"]},{"text":"Convert the gap to metres.","hl":"5.0 mm apart","calc":["d = 5.0 × 10⁻³ m"]},{"text":"Divide.","calc":["E = 1.2 × 10³ ÷ 5.0 × 10⁻³ = **2.4 × 10⁵ V/m**"]},{"text":"Mm left unconverted or turned into μm gives the wrong power of ten.","strike":["2.4 × 10² V/m","2.4 × 10⁸ V/m"]},{"text":"Gap over voltage inverts the formula.","strike":["4.2 × 10⁻⁶ V/m"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.4 × 10⁵ V/m."}],"trap":"Using the gap in millimetres: E = V/d needs d in metres, or the power of ten is off by 10³."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"parallel plate electrodes","note":"uniform field, E = V/d"},{"t":"5.0 mm apart","note":"d, convert to m"},{"t":"1.2 kV","note":"V, convert to V"},{"t":"lasting 50 μs","note":"not needed for E"}]},{"text":"**Prefixes:** kilo = 10³, milli = 10⁻³, micro = 10⁻⁶."},{"text":"**Compute.**","calc":["E = (1.2 × 10³ V)/(5.0 × 10⁻³ m)","= 0.24 × 10⁶ = 2.4 × 10⁵ V/m"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for parallel plates, E = V/d in V/m; convert kV to V and mm to m before dividing."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-15a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:15#1","pe4-cp:15#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:15"],"passage":"Some hair-removal devices use a pulsed alexandrite laser. In one device, a new pulse begins every 25 ms. Each pulse is only 8.0 ns long and carries 0.30 J of light energy. Between pulses the laser emits nothing.","q":"What are the device's pulse repetition rate and average output power?","choices":["40 Hz; 12 W","0.040 Hz; 0.012 W","40 Hz; 3.75 × 10⁷ W","125 MHz; 3.75 × 10⁷ W"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Using 25 instead of 0.025 s leaves the ms prefix unconverted, so both values come out a thousand times too small."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Energy ÷ pulse length is the peak power during a pulse; the average spreads that energy over the whole period."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"That takes the nanosecond pulse length as the period; pulses repeat every 25 ms, not every few ns."}],"rule":"Repetition rate f = 1/T, with T the start-to-start time. Mean power = (energy of one pulse) × f; peak power = that energy ÷ the pulse length.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"T = 25 ms = 0.025 s → f = 1/0.025 = 40 Hz. Average P = 0.30 J × 40 /s = 12 W."},"exp":"Pulses start every **25 ms = 0.025 s**, so **f = 1/T = 40 Hz**. Each second carries 40 pulses × 0.30 J = **12 J, an average power of 12 W**. Dividing 0.30 J by the 8.0 ns pulse length instead gives the much larger peak power (3.75 × 10⁷ W), which lasts only during each pulse.","how":{"ask":"Find the pulse frequency from the period, then the average power from energy per second.","know":"• **f = 1/T**, with T in seconds (ms = 10⁻³ s).\n• **Average power** = energy per pulse × f.\n• **Peak power** = energy per pulse ÷ pulse duration (much larger).","steps":[{"text":"The period is the time between pulse starts.","hl":"a new pulse begins every 25 ms","calc":["T = 0.025 s → f = 1/0.025 = **40 Hz**"]},{"text":"Energy per second is the average power.","hl":"carries 0.30 J","calc":["P = 0.30 J × 40 s⁻¹ = **12 W**"]},{"text":"Leaving ms unconverted shrinks both numbers by 10³.","strike":["0.040 Hz; 0.012 W"]},{"text":"Dividing by the 8.0 ns pulse length gives the peak power, not the average; using it as the period also gives the wrong rate.","strike":["40 Hz; 3.75 × 10⁷ W","125 MHz; 3.75 × 10⁷ W"]},{"text":"Answer: 40 Hz; 12 W."}],"trap":"Using the pulse duration where the period belongs: the 8.0 ns is how long each flash lasts, not how often flashes repeat."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"every 25 ms","note":"period T"},{"t":"8.0 ns long","note":"pulse duration, for peak power"},{"t":"0.30 J","note":"energy per pulse"}]},{"text":"**Two different powers.**","calc":["average: 0.30 J × 40 Hz = 12 W","peak: 0.30 J ÷ 8.0 × 10⁻⁹ s = 3.75 × 10⁷ W"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** f = 1/(start-to-start time); average power = energy per pulse × f, and peak power = energy per pulse ÷ pulse duration."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-16a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"physics","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:16#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:16"],"passage":"Lasers used to remove skin lesions turn absorbed light into heat in the target. One device can fire continuously or in 4-ms pulses every 20 ms at equal peak power. Heat deposited in the target spreads into the surrounding skin over a few milliseconds. Clinicians report fewer burns of the surrounding skin in pulsed mode.","q":"Why is the pulsed mode gentler on nearby tissue?","choices":["Gaps let the heat dissipate","Its mean output is larger","Its wavelength becomes shorter","More light is reflected"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"The pulsed beam is dark most of the time, so its mean output is smaller, not larger."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Switching a beam on and off changes its timing, not its wavelength."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Pulsing changes only when the light arrives, not how much the surface reflects."}],"rule":"Pulsing at the same peak power lowers the average power and adds off-time between pulses; it doesn't change the light's wavelength or reflection.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Same peak power, but the beam is dark 16 of every 20 ms: in each gap the heat spreads away before the next pulse. Wavelength is untouched and the mean output is lower, so the gaps let heat dissipate."},"exp":"The pulsed laser is on only 4 of every 20 ms, so it is **off 16 of every 20 ms**. During each 16-ms off-time, **deposited heat diffuses away**, so the surrounding tissue stays at a lower average temperature than under a beam that never stops.","how":{"ask":"What happens to the heat in the tissue while the pulsed laser is off?","know":"Heat flows from a warm spot into cooler surroundings over time, so any off-time lets the tissue cool before the next burst. The pulsed beam is on 4 ms and **off 16 ms** of every 20-ms cycle.","steps":[{"text":"Find the timing: same strength while on, but the pulsed beam is on only part of each cycle.","hl":"4-ms pulses every 20 ms at equal peak power"},{"text":"Find the off-time in each cycle.","calc":["off-time = 20 − 4 = **16 ms** per cycle"]},{"text":"The pulsed beam is dark most of the time, so it can't deliver more energy.","strike":["Its mean output is larger"]},{"text":"Pulsing changes only when the light arrives, not its wavelength or how much the surface reflects.","strike":["Its wavelength becomes shorter","More light is reflected"]},{"text":"During each 16-ms gap, heat diffuses out of the treated spot, so the nearby tissue's average temperature stays lower.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Nearby tissue temperature (sketch)","x":{"label":"Time (ms)","min":0,"max":60,"step":10},"y":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":35,"max":55,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"Continuous","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,37],[20,42],[40,47],[60,52]]},{"name":"Pulsed","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,37],[4,38],[20,37.3],[24,38.3],[40,37.6],[44,38.6],[60,37.9]]}]}},{"text":"Answer: gaps let the heat dissipate."}],"trap":"Equal peak power does not mean equal energy per second. The pulsed beam is off 16 of every 20 ms, so heat has time to leave between pulses."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"continuously","note":"always on"},{"t":"4-ms pulses","note":"on-time"},{"t":"every 20 ms","note":"one full cycle"},{"t":"equal peak power","note":"same strength while on"}]},{"text":"**Heat spreads** from a hot spot into cooler surroundings over time. Each off-time lets it leave before the next pulse arrives.","calc":["off-time = 20 − 4 = 16 ms","continuous beam: off-time = 0 → heat piles up"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pulsed light at the same peak power heats tissue less, because the gaps let heat dissipate and the average temperature stays lower."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-17a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:17#1","pe4-cp:17#2","pe4-cp:17#3","pe4-cp:17#4"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:17"],"passage":"A chemist screened four liquids, shown in the figure, as solvents for an extraction. Their boiling points are 1-propanol, 97 °C; tetrahydropyran, 88 °C; 3-pentanone, 102 °C; and ethyl acetate, 77 °C. 1-Propanol boils near the top of the group even though its molar mass, 60 g/mol, is the lowest of the four. The other three have molar masses of 86 to 88 g/mol.","q":"In which pure liquid do the molecules hydrogen-bond to one another?","choices":["1-Propanol","Tetrahydropyran","3-Pentanone","Ethyl acetate"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The ether O can accept an H-bond, but the molecule has no H on O, so two of them cannot H-bond to each other."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A ketone is polar (dipole–dipole) but has no O–H, so it cannot donate a hydrogen bond to its neighbors."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Esters accept H-bonds from water but have no H on O or N, so the pure liquid has only dipole and London forces."}],"rule":"A pure liquid hydrogen-bonds only if its molecules carry H on N, O or F (a donor). Lone-pair O alone (ethers, ketones, esters) makes an acceptor, not a pair.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Look for an O–H, N–H or F–H in each structure: only that molecule both donates and accepts, so only it H-bonds to its own kind. The odd high boiling point flags it."},"exp":"A hydrogen bond needs a **donor** (an H bonded to N, O or F) plus an **acceptor** (a lone pair on N, O or F). Only 1-propanol has an O–H, so only its molecules H-bond to each other, which is why it boils near the top of the group despite the **lowest molar mass**. Tetrahydropyran, 3-pentanone and ethyl acetate have O acceptors but no O–H, so their pure liquids have only **dipole–dipole and London** forces. All four, like every molecule, have London forces.","how":{"ask":"Find the molecule that is both an H-bond donor and an acceptor.","know":"• Every molecule has **London dispersion** forces.\n• Polar molecules add **dipole–dipole** forces.\n• **Hydrogen bonding** is a strong dipole–dipole force that needs H on N, O or F in the same liquid.\n• Ethers, ketones and esters accept H-bonds but cannot donate them.","steps":[{"text":"The boiling point is high for so light a molecule, a hint of extra attraction.","hl":"even though its molar mass, 60 g/mol, is the lowest of the four"},{"text":"Check each structure for H on N, O or F.","calc":["1-propanol: O–H ✓","tetrahydropyran: C–O–C, no O–H","3-pentanone: C=O, no O–H","ethyl acetate: C(=O)–O, no O–H"]},{"text":"The ether, ketone and ester can only accept, so their own molecules cannot H-bond to each other.","strike":["Tetrahydropyran","3-Pentanone","Ethyl acetate"]},{"text":"Answer: 1-Propanol."}],"trap":"Counting any O atom as H-bonding: an O with lone pairs but no attached H is only an acceptor, so the pure liquid has dipole–dipole forces, not H-bonds."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"boils near the top of the group","note":"stronger attractions"},{"t":"molar mass, 60 g/mol, is the lowest","note":"not from London forces"}]},{"text":"**Donor + acceptor.** Only an O–H, N–H or F–H can donate.","fig":"cpt-methanol"},{"text":"**Also:** ammonia, NH₃, has N–H bonds and a lone pair, so pure liquid NH₃ has hydrogen bonds (a strong dipole–dipole force) plus London forces; a polar molecule without N–H, O–H or F–H has only dipole–dipole and London forces."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** list the forces from the bottom up: London (always), dipole–dipole (if polar), hydrogen bonding (only with H on N, O or F)."}],"choiceFigs":["1-propanol","tetrahydropyran","3-pentanone","ethyl-acetate"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-18a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:18#1","pe4-cp:18#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:18"],"passage":"Hydroxylamine, NH₂OH, is used to turn aldehydes and ketones into oximes. In water it acts as a weak base with Kb = 9.0 × 10⁻⁹, far weaker than ammonia. A chemist prepares a 0.40 M hydroxylamine stock for an oxime synthesis. Only a tiny fraction of the base is protonated at equilibrium, so [NH₃OH⁺] equals [OH⁻].","q":"Approximately what is [OH⁻] in the stock solution at equilibrium?","choices":["3.6 × 10⁻⁹ M","9.5 × 10⁻⁵ M","6.0 × 10⁻⁵ M","1.9 × 10⁻⁴ M"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"No square root: [OH⁻] = √(Kb·C), and 3.6 × 10⁻⁹ is just Kb·C."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"9.5 × 10⁻⁵ M is √Kb alone; the 0.40 M concentration was left out."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Too big: with C = 0.40 M (< 1 M), √(Kb·C) must be below √Kb ≈ 9.5 × 10⁻⁵ M; the exponent wasn't made even first."}],"rule":"Weak base: [OH⁻] ≈ √(Kb × C). Rewrite Kb × C with an even power of ten before taking the root.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"x = √(Kb·C) = √(9 × 10⁻⁹ × 0.4) = √(3.6 × 10⁻⁹). Make the exponent even: √(36 × 10⁻¹⁰) = 6 × 10⁻⁵ M."},"exp":"For a weak base, **Kb = x²/C**, so **x = √(Kb·C)** = √(9.0 × 10⁻⁹ × 0.40) = √(3.6 × 10⁻⁹). Rewrite with an even power: √(36 × 10⁻¹⁰) = 6.0 × 10⁻⁵ M. So little base reacts that C barely changes.","how":{"ask":"Set up Kb with x for the ions formed, solve x = √(Kb·C), and take the root of the power of ten carefully.","know":"• B + H₂O ⇌ BH⁺ + OH⁻, so Kb = [BH⁺][OH⁻]/[B] = **x²/C** when x is small next to C.\n• **x = √(Kb·C)**; x is both [OH⁻] and [BH⁺].\n• To take a square root of 10ⁿ, first make n even (move a factor of 10 into the coefficient), then halve it.","steps":[{"text":"Collect the base's Kb and its starting concentration.","hl":"Kb = 9.0 × 10⁻⁹","calc":["C = 0.40 M"]},{"text":"Write the small-x expression and solve for x.","calc":["Kb = x² / C","x = √(Kb · C)","Kb · C = 9.0 × 10⁻⁹ × 0.40 = 3.6 × 10⁻⁹"]},{"text":"Make the exponent even before taking the root.","calc":["3.6 × 10⁻⁹ = 36 × 10⁻¹⁰","√36 = 6.0; √10⁻¹⁰ = 10⁻⁵","x = **6.0 × 10⁻⁵ M**"]},{"text":"Check the approximation: x is far below C.","calc":["6.0 × 10⁻⁵ ÷ 0.40 ≈ 0.015 %"]},{"text":"3.6 × 10⁻⁹ M is Kb·C with no square root; 9.5 × 10⁻⁵ M is √Kb, which leaves out C; 1.9 × 10⁻⁴ M takes √3.6 without first making the exponent even.","strike":["3.6 × 10⁻⁹ M","9.5 × 10⁻⁵ M","1.9 × 10⁻⁴ M"]},{"text":"Answer: 6.0 × 10⁻⁵ M."}],"trap":"Halving an odd exponent as if it were even: rewrite 3.6 × 10⁻⁹ as 36 × 10⁻¹⁰ first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words and numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"weak base","note":"only a little reacts"},{"t":"Kb = 9.0 × 10⁻⁹","note":"base constant"},{"t":"0.40 M","note":"starting concentration C"},{"t":"[OH⁻]","note":"x, the amount that reacts"}]},{"text":"**Write Kb with x.** Each base that reacts makes one BH⁺ and one OH⁻, and the base left is still about C.","calc":["Kb = x·x / (C − x) ≈ x² / C"]},{"text":"**Solve for x.**","calc":["x = √(Kb · C) = √(3.6 × 10⁻⁹)"]},{"text":"**Estimate the root of the power of ten.** Shift one factor of 10 so the exponent is even, then halve it; the coefficient's root is a nearby perfect square.","calc":["3.6 × 10⁻⁹ = 36 × 10⁻¹⁰","√36 × 10⁻⁵ = 6.0 × 10⁻⁵ M"]},{"text":"**Also:** e.g. Kb = 2.5 × 10⁻⁵, C = 0.10 M: x = √(2.5 × 10⁻⁶) ≈ 1.6 × 10⁻³ M (√2.5 ≈ 1.6, √10⁻⁶ = 10⁻³)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a weak base, [OH⁻] = [BH⁺] ≈ √(Kb·C); make the power of ten even, then take half of it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-19a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:19#1","pe4-cp:19#2","pe5-cp:3#1","pe5-cp:3#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:19"],"passage":"In a classroom demonstration, a thin plastic bottle is filled with dry hydrogen chloride gas. About 15 mL of water is squirted in, and the bottle is capped and shaken. Within seconds the bottle crumples inward, because the gas dissolves and the pressure inside falls far below atmospheric. The teacher plans to repeat the demonstration with other gases.","q":"If the bottle were filled with sulfur dioxide, SO₂, instead, which result would be expected?","choices":["Crumples; polar SO₂ dissolves","Stays intact; SO₂ is nonpolar","Stays intact; gases never dissolve","Crumples; SO₂ forms a base"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"The passage itself shows a gas dissolving in water, so \"gases never dissolve\" cannot be right."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"SO₂ dissolves to give sulfurous acid, an acid; calling it a base gets the chemistry backwards."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"SO₂ is bent, with an unshared pair on S, so its bond dipoles do not cancel: it is polar."}],"rule":"Like dissolves like: a polar or water-reactive gas dissolves and its pressure drops; a nonpolar gas (CH₄, C₂H₆, N₂) barely dissolves and its pressure holds.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ask one thing: is the new gas polar or reactive with water? SO₂ is bent and forms an acid, so it dissolves like HCl did, and the bottle still crumples."},"exp":"The bottle crumples only if the gas **leaves the gas phase by dissolving**. SO₂ is **bent and polar** and reacts with water to form sulfurous acid, so it dissolves readily, much as HCl does: the pressure falls and the bottle **still crumples**. Had the gas been **nonpolar**, such as ethane or methane, it would barely dissolve, the pressure would hold and the bottle would stay intact.","how":{"ask":"Predict whether the new gas dissolves in the water, since only dissolving lowers the pressure.","know":"• **Like dissolves like**: polar or H-bonding molecules dissolve in water; nonpolar ones barely do.\n• A sealed container's gas pressure falls only if gas molecules leave the gas phase.\n• SO₂ is bent (polar); CO₂ is linear (nonpolar); hydrocarbons are nonpolar.","steps":[{"text":"The collapse comes from the gas dissolving.","hl":"because the gas dissolves and the pressure inside falls far below atmospheric"},{"text":"Classify the replacement gas.","hl":"sulfur dioxide, SO₂","calc":["SO₂: bent, lone pair on S → polar","SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃ (an acid)"]},{"text":"The passage already shows a gas dissolving, and SO₂ gives an acid, not a base.","strike":["Stays intact; gases never dissolve","Crumples; SO₂ forms a base"]},{"text":"Bent SO₂ is polar, so it dissolves.","strike":["Stays intact; SO₂ is nonpolar"]},{"text":"Answer: Crumples; polar SO₂ dissolves."}],"trap":"Assuming any swap of the gas stops the effect: the outcome depends only on whether the new gas is polar (or water-reactive) enough to dissolve."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"hydrogen chloride gas","note":"polar, dissolves"},{"t":"crumples inward","note":"pressure fell"},{"t":"sulfur dioxide, SO₂","note":"bent → polar"}]},{"text":"**Link solubility to pressure.** Dissolved gas no longer pushes on the walls; gas that stays above the water keeps the pressure up."},{"text":"**Contrast:** a nonpolar gas such as ethane or methane barely dissolves in water, so its pressure stays near atmospheric and the bottle stays intact."},{"text":"**Also:** the same rule ranks analogs of a drug. Replacing a ring C–H with N (benzene ring → pyridine ring), or adding an O or N that water can H-bond to, raises solubility. Adding CH₃ groups, or trading a polar group for C–H or C–Cl, lowers it.","fig":"pyridine","calc":["ring CH → N: a lone pair for water to H-bond → more soluble","–OH → –OCH₃, ring H → –CH₃ or –Cl: more nonpolar surface → less soluble"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** like dissolves like: a polar or water-reactive gas dissolves and lowers the pressure, a nonpolar gas does not; a ring CH → N or an added O/N raises water solubility, added CH₃ lowers it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-20a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:20#1","pe4-cp:20#2","pe4-cp:20#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:20"],"passage":"Metal M is a trace additive in a bone-graft cement. Its compounds are white, and its salts dissolve in water to give colorless solutions. The chart plots the successive ionization energies of a gaseous M atom, IE₁ through IE₄. The cement's chemists want to know which ion M forms in the cement.","q":"Based on the chart, which family does M belong to, and which ion does it usually form?","choices":["Na's group; M⁺","Mg's group; M²⁺","Al's group; M³⁺","Si's group; M⁴⁺"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A group 1 metal shows its huge jump right after the first electron; here the second is still easy to remove."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A group 13 metal loses three electrons before the jump; here the jump already comes at the third."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Four easy removals would mean four valence electrons; the third already costs about four times the second."}],"rule":"Find the big jump in successive ionization energies: the number of electrons removed before it is the valence count, which gives the group and the usual ion charge.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Look for the cliff: IE₁ and IE₂ are low, then IE₃ is about four times IE₂. Two valence electrons → group 2, 2+ ion."},"exp":"Successive ionization energies rise gradually while valence electrons are removed, then **jump** when a core electron must go. For M, IE₁ (590) and IE₂ (1145 kJ/mol) are low, but IE₃ (4912 kJ/mol) is about four times IE₂. So M has **two valence electrons**: it is an **alkaline earth metal (group 2)**, like Mg and Ca, and forms the **M²⁺** ion.","how":{"ask":"Count how many electrons come off before the big jump in ionization energy.","know":"• Group 1 (alkali metals) jump after IE₁ → 1+; group 2 (alkaline earth metals) after IE₂ → 2+; group 13 after IE₃ → 3+.\n• Halogens (group 17) gain an electron; metalloids sit on the stair-step line; transition metals form several ions.","steps":[{"text":"Read the four bars.","calc":["IE₁ ≈ 590, IE₂ ≈ 1145","IE₃ ≈ 4912, IE₄ ≈ 6474 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Find the largest ratio between neighbors.","calc":["IE₂/IE₁ ≈ 1.9","IE₃/IE₂ ≈ 4.3 ← the jump","IE₄/IE₃ ≈ 1.3"]},{"text":"Two electrons come off before the jump, so one, three or four valence electrons are ruled out.","strike":["Na's group; M⁺","Al's group; M³⁺","Si's group; M⁴⁺"]},{"text":"Answer: Mg's group; M²⁺."}],"trap":"Picking the largest bar: IE₄ is the biggest value, but the group comes from where the jump happens, not from the tallest bar."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"successive ionization energies","note":"look for the jump"},{"t":"which ion M forms","note":"charge = electrons before the jump"}]},{"text":"**Why the jump:** after the valence electrons are gone, the next one comes from a filled inner shell, much closer to the nucleus."},{"text":"**Group names:** alkali metals (group 1, 1+), alkaline earth metals (group 2, 2+), transition metals (groups 3–12), halogens (group 17, 1−), noble gases (group 18). Calcium is an alkaline earth metal and forms Ca²⁺."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the number of electrons removed before the big ionization-energy jump equals the valence count, the group (for main-group metals) and the usual positive charge."}],"chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Successive ionization energies of metal M","x":{"label":"Electron removed"},"y":{"label":"Ionization energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":7000,"step":1000},"categories":["IE₁","IE₂","IE₃","IE₄"],"series":[{"name":"M","values":[590,1145,4912,6474]}]}},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-21a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:21#1","pe4-cp:21#2","pe4-cp:21#3","pe4-cp:23#1","pe4-cp:23#2","pe4-cp:24#1","pe4-cp:24#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:21"],"passage":"Leukemia cells often survive chemotherapy by switching on a survival pathway. Researchers tested whether compound R, a pathway blocker that is not toxic by itself at the doses used, makes drug Q more effective. They measured the dose of Q that kills 90% of cells, with and without R. Drug Q's lethal dose is 60 μM alone and 6 μM with compound R in leukemia cells; in healthy cells it is 50 μM alone and 48 μM with R.","q":"Which conclusion do these dose data best support?","choices":["R sensitizes only the leukemia cells.","R sensitizes healthy and leukemia cells alike.","R is toxic to leukemia cells on its own.","Q has no effect unless R is present."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"In healthy cells the lethal dose barely moves with R (50 vs 48 μM), so R does not act on both alike."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Untested: no condition gives R alone, so the data can't show R's own toxicity."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Q alone kills leukemia cells at 60 μM, so Q works without R."}],"rule":"Compare each cell type with and without the add-in: a big drop in lethal dose is sensitization. Reject claims about conditions never tested.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Take each ratio: leukemia 60 ÷ 6 = 10× less Q needed with R; healthy 50 ÷ 48 ≈ 1×. Only the leukemia cells shift, so R sensitizes them selectively."},"exp":"Compare each cell type with and without R. Leukemia cells need **10-fold less** Q once R is added (60 ÷ 6), while healthy cells barely shift (50 ÷ 48 ≈ 1.04). A dose that falls for one cell type only means R is a **selective** sensitizer. R was never tested by itself, and Q kills leukemia cells alone at 60 μM.","how":{"ask":"For each cell type, how much does the dose of Q drop when R is added, and does it drop for both types or one?","know":"• A **sensitizer** lowers the dose of a drug needed for the same effect.\n• Compare **with vs without** the sensitizer within each cell type, then compare the cell types.\n• **Selective** = the dose falls for the target (cancer) cells but not for normal cells.\n• **Enhancement = dose alone ÷ dose with the sensitizer** (a fold change, not a difference).\n• Reject any claim about a condition the data never tested.","steps":[{"text":"Lay out the four doses as a small table: two cell types, each without and with R.","hl":"60 μM alone and 6 μM with compound R in leukemia cells","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Lethal dose of drug Q, without and with compound R","x":{"label":"Cell type"},"y":{"label":"Lethal dose of Q (μM)","min":0,"max":70,"step":10},"categories":["Leukemia","Healthy"],"series":[{"name":"Q alone","values":[60,50]},{"name":"Q + R","values":[6,48]}]}},{"text":"Find the fold change for each cell type: dose alone ÷ dose with R.","calc":["leukemia: 60 μM ÷ 6 μM = **10-fold**","healthy: 50 μM ÷ 48 μM ≈ **1.04-fold** (essentially no change)"]},{"text":"R helps Q only in the leukemia cells, so it does not act on both types equally.","strike":["R sensitizes healthy and leukemia cells alike."]},{"text":"No row gives R by itself, so its own toxicity is untested; and Q already kills leukemia cells at 60 μM without R.","strike":["R is toxic to leukemia cells on its own.","Q has no effect unless R is present."]},{"text":"Answer: R sensitizes only the leukemia cells."}],"trap":"Reading a claim the data never tested (R alone) as supported, or calling R a general sensitizer because some number moved slightly."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"60 μM alone","note":"leukemia, no R"},{"t":"6 μM with compound R","note":"leukemia, with R"},{"t":"50 μM alone","note":"healthy, no R"},{"t":"48 μM with R","note":"healthy, with R"}]},{"text":"**Compare within each cell type first.** Does adding R lower the dose?","calc":["leukemia: 60 → 6 μM (large drop)","healthy: 50 → 48 μM (tiny drop)"]},{"text":"**Selective means one type only.** The dose falls for the cancer cells and stays put for the normal cells, so the effect is selective."},{"text":"**Test only what was measured.** No condition gives R alone, so a claim about R killing cells by itself is unsupported either way."},{"text":"**Also:** enhancement is a **ratio**, dose alone ÷ dose with the sensitizer, never the difference. Compute it for every line and pick the largest.","calc":["line X: 90 → 30 μM: 90 ÷ 30 = 3-fold (drop 60 μM)","line Y: 8 → 0.5 μM: 8 ÷ 0.5 = 16-fold (drop 7.5 μM)","line Y is enhanced most, despite the smaller drop"]},{"text":"**Also:** order matters when a sensitizer blocks a pathway. Give the sensitizer **first**, long enough for it to act, then give the drug that works best once that pathway is blocked; giving the drug first or both together lets the pathway run while the drug is present."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare dose alone vs with the sensitizer in each cell type as a fold change (alone ÷ with); selective means only the cancer cells' dose falls, and claims about untested conditions are rejected."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-22a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:22#1","pe4-cp:22#2","pe4-cp:42#1","pe4-cp:42#2","pe5-cp:1#2","pe5-cp:1#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:22"],"passage":"A clinical research team tracks a peptide hormone in plasma by immunoassay. A blood test reports a peptide hormone at 640 nM, but the lab's software accepts only micromolar units. Entries in the wrong unit are flagged at review and must be redone.","q":"Which entry belongs in the software for this sample?","choices":["0.064 μM","0.64 μM","64 μM","6.4 × 10⁵ μM"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"Divided by 10,000; nano to micro is only a factor of 1000."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Sanity: 64 μM would be 64,000 nM, 100 times the 640 nM given."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: converting to a larger unit (μM) must give a smaller number than 640."}],"rule":"1 μM = 1000 nM, so nM → μM means divide by 1000: a bigger unit always gives a smaller number.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"1 μM = 1000 nM; a bigger unit means a smaller number. 640 ÷ 1000 = 0.64 μM."},"exp":"**nM = 10⁻⁹ M and μM = 10⁻⁶ M**, so 1 μM = 1000 nM. Going to the larger unit gives a smaller number: 640 nM ÷ 1000 = **0.64 μM**.","how":{"ask":"Write each prefix as a power of ten, then convert from nM to μM.","know":"• **Prefixes**: m = 10⁻³, μ = 10⁻⁶, n = 10⁻⁹, p = 10⁻¹².\n• 1 μM = 1000 nM, so **nM → μM: divide by 1000**.\n• **[H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M**; **M → μM: × 10⁶**.","steps":[{"text":"Start from the given value and unit.","hl":"640 nM"},{"text":"Convert through molar.","calc":["640 nM = 640 × 10⁻⁹ M = 6.4 × 10⁻⁷ M","6.4 × 10⁻⁷ M ÷ 10⁻⁶ M/μM = **0.64 μM**"]},{"text":"Shortcut: nM → μM is ÷ 1000.","calc":["640 ÷ 1000 = 0.64"]},{"text":"6.4 × 10⁵ μM multiplies by 1000 instead of dividing; 64 μM divides by only 10; 0.064 μM divides by 10⁴.","strike":["6.4 × 10⁵ μM","64 μM","0.064 μM"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.64 μM."}],"trap":"Multiplying by 1000: a micromole is larger than a nanomole, so the number must get smaller."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words and numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"640 nM","note":"value in nanomolar"},{"t":"micromolar","note":"target unit, μM"}]},{"text":"**Write the prefixes as powers of ten.** n = 10⁻⁹ and μ = 10⁻⁶: they differ by 10³."},{"text":"**Bigger unit, smaller number.** nM → μM: divide by 1000.","calc":["640 nM ÷ 1000 = 0.64 μM"]},{"text":"**Also:** from pH to μM: [H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M, then × 10⁶ for μM. At pH 6.3, [H₃O⁺] = 10⁻⁶·³ M ≈ 5 × 10⁻⁷ M = 0.5 μM.","calc":["[H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M","5 × 10⁻⁷ M × 10⁶ μM/M = 0.5 μM"]},{"text":"**Also:** in a weighing problem, chain the prefixes. Going in, μM → M (× 10⁻⁶) and mL → L (× 10⁻³); coming out, grams → milligrams (× 1000).","calc":["25 μM, 40 mL, solute 300 g/mol","n = 25 × 10⁻⁶ mol/L × 40 × 10⁻³ L = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ mol","mass = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ mol × 300 g/mol = 3.0 × 10⁻⁴ g","3.0 × 10⁻⁴ g × 1000 mg/g = 0.30 mg"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** convert through powers of ten: nM → μM ÷ 1000, M → μM × 10⁶, and [H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-23a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:23#1","pe4-cp:23#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:23"],"passage":"An enhancer compound was tested for its ability to boost a kinase inhibitor in four cancer cell lines. For each line, the inhibitor concentration needed to kill 90% of cells was found without and with the enhancer. Line W: 45 μM alone, 9 μM with enhancer. Line X: 18 μM alone, 0.9 μM with enhancer. Line Y: 120 μM alone, 40 μM with enhancer. Line Z: 7 μM both with and without the enhancer.","q":"In which line does the enhancer boost the inhibitor the most?","choices":["Line W","Line X","Line Y","Line Z"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Its dose did not move at all, so the enhancer did nothing there: that line shows the least effect, not the most."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"It has the biggest drop in μM, but enhancement is a fold change; 120 → 40 is only threefold."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"45 → 9 is a fivefold drop, which is smaller than another line's fold change."}],"rule":"Enhancement is a ratio: dose alone ÷ dose with the enhancer. Compute it for every line and pick the largest; never compare raw differences.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Divide each pair: W 45/9 = 5, X 18/0.9 = 20, Y 120/40 = 3, Z 7/7 = 1. The biggest fold change wins."},"exp":"Enhancement is the **fold drop** in the dose needed: dose alone ÷ dose with enhancer. Line W: 45/9 = 5. Line X: 18/0.9 = **20**. Line Y: 120/40 = 3. Line Z: 7/7 = 1 (no effect). Line Y has the largest drop in μM (80 μM), but only a threefold change; **Line X** is enhanced most.","how":{"ask":"Compute the fold change (alone ÷ with enhancer) for each line and compare.","know":"• Potency changes are compared as **ratios**, not differences.\n• Fold enhancement = dose alone ÷ dose with the enhancer; 1 means no effect.","steps":[{"text":"Pull each line's pair of doses.","hl":"Line X: 18 μM alone, 0.9 μM with enhancer"},{"text":"Divide alone by with-enhancer.","calc":["W: 45 ÷ 9 = 5","X: 18 ÷ 0.9 = **20**","Y: 120 ÷ 40 = 3","Z: 7 ÷ 7 = 1"]},{"text":"No change means no enhancement.","strike":["Line Z"]},{"text":"The largest μM drop is not the largest fold drop, and fivefold is smaller than twentyfold.","strike":["Line Y","Line W"]},{"text":"Answer: Line X."}],"trap":"Subtracting instead of dividing: a line that starts at a high dose shows a big μM drop even when the fold change is small."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"kill 90% of cells","note":"same endpoint for all lines"},{"t":"Line Y: 120 μM alone, 40 μM with enhancer","note":"big difference, small ratio"}]},{"text":"**Ratios, not differences.**","calc":["Y: 120 − 40 = 80 μM, but 120/40 = 3","X: 18 − 0.9 ≈ 17 μM, but 18/0.9 = 20"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to compare how much a treatment helps, divide the before value by the after value in each case and rank the ratios."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-24a","s":"Research Design & Reasoning","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe4-cp:24#1","pe4-cp:24#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:24"],"passage":"Drug T stops dividing cells in mitosis, and this arrest takes about 12 hours to build up after a dose. Compound R kills only cells that are already arrested in mitosis; it has no effect on cells in other phases. R is cleared from the body within about 2 hours of a dose. A clinical team must choose a schedule for giving T and R.","q":"Which schedule should kill the most tumor cells?","choices":["R, then T after 12 h","T and R together","T, then R after 12 h","T, then R after 2 h"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"R acts only on cells T has already arrested, so giving R first reverses the needed order."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Given together, R is cleared within hours, long before the arrest has built up."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"At 2 h few cells are arrested yet, so most of R is wasted before its targets exist."}],"rule":"Order the doses by the mechanism: give the agent that creates the vulnerable state first, wait for that state to build, then give the agent that exploits it.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ask which drug makes the target and which hits it. T makes arrested cells (after about 12 h); R hits them and is short-lived. So T first, then R once arrest is complete."},"exp":"R kills only **cells already arrested in mitosis**, and T needs about **12 hours** to arrest them. R is cleared in about 2 hours, so it must arrive when the arrested cells exist: **give T first, then R about 12 hours later**. Giving R first, together with T, or only 2 hours after T wastes R before its targets have formed.","how":{"ask":"Decide which agent must act first and how long to wait before the second, from the stated mechanism and timing.","know":"• A sequence works when the first agent sets up the state the second one needs.\n• Match the delay to how long the first agent takes to act.\n• A short-lived second agent must arrive after the state exists.","steps":[{"text":"T creates the target state, slowly.","hl":"this arrest takes about 12 hours to build up after a dose"},{"text":"R needs that state and does not last long.","hl":"R is cleared from the body within about 2 hours of a dose"},{"text":"R before T has nothing to act on.","strike":["R, then T after 12 h"]},{"text":"Together, or after only 2 h, R is gone before the arrest is complete.","strike":["T and R together","T, then R after 2 h"]},{"text":"Answer: T, then R after 12 h."}],"trap":"Assuming the drugs should simply be given together: when one agent needs the other's effect, the timing has to follow the mechanism."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"kills only cells that are already arrested in mitosis","note":"R needs T's effect"},{"t":"about 12 hours","note":"wait this long"},{"t":"within about 2 hours","note":"R is short-lived"}]},{"text":"**Mechanism → order.** First the agent that sets up the state; then, once the state exists, the agent that exploits it. If a sensitizer must block a pathway before a drug can work, the sensitizer goes first."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** give first the agent whose effect the other depends on, and time the second dose to arrive when that effect is fully in place."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-25a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:25#1","pe4-cp:25#2","pe4-cp:25#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:25"],"q":"N₂ has an N–N distance of 110 pm, versus 145 pm for the N–N single bond in hydrazine, H₂N–NH₂. Which bonding in N₂ explains its shorter bond?","choices":["σ + π","σ + 2π","3σ","2σ + π"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"Two atoms share only one σ bond, along the axis between them; any further bonds must be π."},{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"A second σ bond between the same two atoms cannot form; extra bonds are π bonds."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"One σ plus one π is a double bond, which is shorter than a single bond but not as short as this one."}],"rule":"Two atoms share at most one σ bond; every extra bond between them is a π bond. More bonds between the same two atoms → shorter, stronger bond.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Each N has 5 valence electrons: N≡N with one lone pair each. A triple bond is always one σ and two π, and it is the shortest."},"exp":"N₂ is **N≡N**: a triple bond made of **a σ bond plus two π bonds**. Only one σ bond can lie along the internuclear axis; each extra shared pair is a π bond from sideways p-orbital overlap. More bonding pairs pull the atoms closer, so the triple bond (110 pm) is much shorter than hydrazine's single bond (145 pm), and also shorter than an N=N double bond (σ + π, about 125 pm).","how":{"ask":"Match the very short bond to its bond order, then break that bond into σ and π parts.","know":"• Single bond = 1 σ; double = 1 σ + 1 π; triple = 1 σ + 2 π.\n• Only one σ bond can join two atoms.\n• Higher bond order → shorter, stronger bond (C–O 143 pm vs C=O ≈ 122 pm is the same pattern).","steps":[{"text":"Compare the two lengths.","hl":"110 pm, versus 145 pm","calc":["110 pm ≪ 145 pm → more than a single bond"]},{"text":"Two atoms can share only one σ bond.","strike":["3σ","2σ + π"]},{"text":"Lewis structure of N₂: :N≡N: (10 valence electrons), a triple bond.","strike":["σ + π"]},{"text":"Answer: σ + 2π."}],"trap":"Counting every shared pair as σ: after the first σ bond, each additional bond between the same atoms is π."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"N₂","note":"N≡N"},{"t":"N–N single bond","note":"σ only"},{"t":"shorter bond","note":"higher bond order"}]},{"text":"**σ vs π.** σ = head-on overlap along the axis (one per atom pair); π = side-by-side p overlap above and below the axis."},{"text":"**Also:** a carbonyl C=O is one σ plus one π, and it is shorter than a C–O single bond; a C≡N or C≡C is one σ plus two π."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** single = σ, double = σ + π, triple = σ + 2π; the more bonds, the shorter the bond."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-26a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:26#1","pe4-cp:26#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:26"],"q":"Four neutral atoms have the ground-state configurations 1s²2s²2p⁶, [Ne]3s²3p⁵, [Ne]3s² and [Ar]4s¹. Which atom accepts an extra electron most readily, and why?","choices":["Ne, 2p not full","Cl, filling 3p","Mg, 3s has a vacancy","K, pairing in 4s"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"The configuration ends in 3s², a full s subshell, so it has no vacancy there."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"2p⁶ is a full subshell; an added electron would have to start a new, higher shell, which is unfavorable."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A 4s electron is far from a weakly shielded nucleus, so potassium gains little energy by taking one."}],"rule":"An atom one electron short of a filled p subshell (a halogen) gains an electron most readily. Full subshells (noble gases, s²) and metals do not.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Look for p⁵: one electron completes the subshell. That configuration belongs to the halogen."},"exp":"The [Ne]3s²3p⁵ atom is **chlorine**, a halogen: **one more electron fills its 3p subshell**, giving the stable [Ar] configuration and releasing much energy (the most favorable electron affinity of the four). Neon (2p⁶) is already full, so an extra electron must go into a new shell. Magnesium's 3s² is full too, and potassium, a metal, tends to lose its 4s electron rather than gain one.","how":{"ask":"Find the configuration that one added electron would complete, and say why that is favorable.","know":"• **Electron affinity** is most favorable for halogens (p⁵ → p⁶).\n• Noble gases (full p) and s² atoms have no low-energy vacancy.\n• Metals (low ionization energy) tend to lose electrons, not gain them.","steps":[{"text":"Name each configuration.","calc":["1s²2s²2p⁶ = Ne","[Ne]3s²3p⁵ = Cl","[Ne]3s² = Mg","[Ar]4s¹ = K"]},{"text":"Magnesium's 3s is full, so it has no vacancy there.","hl":"[Ne]3s²","strike":["Mg, 3s has a vacancy"]},{"text":"Neon's 2p is already full; potassium, a metal, holds an extra electron weakly.","hl":"1s²2s²2p⁶","strike":["Ne, 2p not full","K, pairing in 4s"]},{"text":"One electron completes chlorine's 3p subshell.","hl":"[Ne]3s²3p⁵"},{"text":"Answer: Cl, filling 3p."}],"trap":"Thinking any atom with room in an s orbital gains electrons easily: metals have low ionization energies and prefer to lose electrons."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"3p⁵","note":"one short of full"},{"t":"1s²2s²2p⁶","note":"already full"},{"t":"accepts an extra electron most readily","note":"most favorable electron affinity"}]},{"text":"**Electron affinity trend:** most favorable toward the upper right (halogens); noble gases are the exception, with no room in their shell."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the atom that gains an electron most readily is the one an extra electron would bring to a filled p subshell: a halogen, not a noble gas or a metal."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-27a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"physics","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:27#1","pe4-cp:27#2","pe4-cp:27#3","pe4-cp:27#4","pe6-cp:54#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:27"],"q":"Compound Z's triple point is 0.40 atm and 15 °C. Its vapor, held at 0.10 atm, is cooled until frost forms at −10 °C. Which process makes the frost?","choices":["Deposition","Freezing","Condensation","Sublimation"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sublimation is solid → gas, made on warming; this vapor is cooling into a solid, the reverse crossing."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Freezing starts from a liquid, and no liquid exists below the triple-point pressure (0.10 < 0.40 atm)."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Condensation makes a liquid, which cannot exist below the triple-point pressure of 0.40 atm."}],"rule":"Below the triple-point pressure there is no liquid, so gas and solid convert directly: gas to solid on cooling, solid to gas on warming.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Below the triple-point pressure (0.10 < 0.40 atm) no liquid can exist, so gas goes straight to solid: deposition, the reverse of sublimation."},"exp":"At 0.10 atm, below the triple-point pressure, Z has no liquid phase, so cooling the vapor reaches only the **solid–gas line** (at −10 °C). There the gas turns straight into solid: **deposition**, the reverse of sublimation.","how":{"ask":"Which boundary does the cooling vapor cross below the triple-point pressure, and in which direction does it cross it?","know":"• A **phase diagram** plots pressure (up) against temperature (across). Solid sits at low T and high P, gas at high T and low P, liquid in between.\n• Each line is where two phases coexist, crossed one way or the other: **solid–gas = sublimation (solid → gas) / deposition (gas → solid)**, **solid–liquid = melting / freezing**, **liquid–gas = vaporization / condensation**.\n• The **triple point** is where all three lines meet; the **critical point** ends the liquid–gas line.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the path with the triple point.","hl":"held at 0.10 atm","calc":["0.10 atm < 0.40 atm (below the triple-point pressure)","−10 °C < 15 °C (colder than the triple point)"]},{"text":"Below the triple-point pressure, liquid cannot exist, so the only border on this path is the solid–gas line, which the vapor reaches at −10 °C.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of compound Z (sketch)","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":3.5,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[-40,0.02],[-10,0.1],[15,0.4]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[15,0.4],[18,1.8],[21,3.2]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[15,0.4],[50,1.2],[80,2.4],[100,3.2]]}]}},{"text":"Freezing and condensation both involve a liquid, which has no region below 0.40 atm.","strike":["Freezing","Condensation"]},{"text":"The vapor is cooling, so it crosses the solid–gas line from the gas side to the solid side. Sublimation (solid → gas) is the reverse crossing, made on warming.","hl":"frost forms at −10 °C","strike":["Sublimation"]},{"text":"Answer: deposition (gas → solid)."}],"trap":"Naming the right line but the wrong direction: sublimation and deposition share the solid–gas line, and vapor cooling into frost is deposition."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"triple point","note":"where all three lines meet"},{"t":"0.40 atm and 15 °C","note":"triple-point P and T"},{"t":"held at 0.10 atm","note":"the path's pressure"},{"t":"frost forms at −10 °C","note":"solid appears from the gas"}]},{"text":"**Read the map:** up = more pressure, right = hotter. Cold and squeezed = solid; hot and low pressure = gas; liquid is the wedge in between.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of compound Z (sketch)","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":3.5,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[-40,0.02],[-10,0.1],[15,0.4]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[15,0.4],[18,1.8],[21,3.2]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[15,0.4],[50,1.2],[80,2.4],[100,3.2]]}]}},{"text":"**Place the path:** cooling at a fixed 0.10 atm moves left along a horizontal line from the gas region into the solid region, crossing only the solid–gas line.","calc":["0.10 < 0.40 atm → no liquid region on this path","gas → solid at −10 °C: deposition"]},{"text":"**Also:** warming the solid back along the same line reverses the crossing: solid → gas with no liquid stage, which is **sublimation**."},{"text":"**Also:** a point on the solid–liquid line (nearly vertical, above the triple point) is **melting** or freezing; a point on the liquid–gas line is **boiling** (vaporization) or condensation, and at 1 atm it gives the normal boiling point."},{"text":"**Also:** two points are special. The **triple point** is the one T and P where solid, liquid and gas all coexist. The **critical point** ends the liquid–gas line; beyond it liquid and gas merge into one supercritical fluid."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** find which line the path crosses and in which direction: solid–gas = sublimation (warming) or deposition (cooling), solid–liquid = melting or freezing, liquid–gas = vaporization or condensation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-28a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:28#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:28"],"q":"Electron levels: E₁ = −12.0 eV, E₂ = −7.0 eV, E₃ = −5.5 eV, E₄ = −5.0 eV. Which downward jump emits the longest-wavelength photon?","choices":["E₄ → E₃","E₄ → E₁","E₂ → E₁","E₃ → E₂"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Largest gap (7.0 eV) means the shortest λ, the opposite of what's asked."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Drops all the way to E₁ have big gaps (5.0 eV here): high energy, short λ."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"E₃ → E₂ spans 1.5 eV; another listed jump has a smaller gap, so its photon is longer than this one."}],"rule":"Longest wavelength = smallest energy gap (λ = hc/ΔE): find the two closest levels.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"λ ∝ 1/ΔE, so longest λ = smallest gap. Gaps: E₄→E₃ 0.5, E₃→E₂ 1.5, E₂→E₁ 5, E₄→E₁ 7 eV. Smallest is 0.5 eV: E₄ → E₃."},"exp":"**A photon carries the energy gap, and λ = hc/ΔE**, so the smallest gap gives the longest wavelength. The gaps are 0.5 eV (E₄ → E₃), 1.5 eV (E₃ → E₂), 5.0 eV (E₂ → E₁) and 7.0 eV (E₄ → E₁); the 0.5 eV drop emits light near 1240 ÷ 0.5 ≈ 2500 nm, the longest.","how":{"ask":"Find the energy gap of each transition and pick the smallest one.","know":"• An emitted photon's energy equals the gap: **ΔE = hc/λ**, so **λ = hc/ΔE**.\n• Wavelength is inversely proportional to the gap: **smallest ΔE → longest λ**.\n• Shortcut: λ(nm) ≈ 1240/ΔE(eV).","steps":[{"text":"List the levels.","hl":"E₁ = −12.0 eV, E₂ = −7.0 eV, E₃ = −5.5 eV, E₄ = −5.0 eV"},{"text":"Compute each choice's gap.","calc":["E₄ → E₃: −5.0 − (−5.5) = **0.5 eV**","E₃ → E₂: −5.5 − (−7.0) = 1.5 eV","E₂ → E₁: −7.0 − (−12.0) = 5.0 eV","E₄ → E₁: −5.0 − (−12.0) = 7.0 eV"]},{"text":"λ = hc/ΔE, so convert the smallest and largest gaps to wavelengths.","calc":["0.5 eV: λ ≈ 1240 ÷ 0.5 ≈ 2500 nm","7.0 eV: λ ≈ 1240 ÷ 7.0 ≈ 177 nm"]},{"text":"The three larger gaps all give shorter wavelengths; E₄ → E₁, the largest gap, gives the shortest.","strike":["E₄ → E₁","E₂ → E₁","E₃ → E₂"]},{"text":"Answer: E₄ → E₃."}],"trap":"Picking the biggest drop: a large gap means a high-energy photon, which has the shortest wavelength, not the longest."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"E₁ = −12.0 eV","note":"lowest level"},{"t":"E₄ = −5.0 eV","note":"highest level"},{"t":"longest-wavelength photon","note":"smallest gap"}]},{"text":"**The photon carries the gap.** A falling electron emits one photon with ΔE = E(upper) − E(lower).","calc":["E₄ → E₃: −5.0 − (−5.5) = 0.5 eV"]},{"text":"**Flip to wavelength:** λ = hc/ΔE, so wavelength and gap are inversely related.","calc":["λ(nm) ≈ 1240 ÷ ΔE(eV)","1.5 eV → ≈ 830 nm; 0.5 eV → ≈ 2500 nm"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** λ = hc/ΔE, so the transition with the smallest energy gap emits the longest wavelength, and the largest gap the shortest."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-29a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:29#1","pe4-cp:29#2","pe4-cp:29#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:29"],"passage":"Compound 5, a candidate antihistamine, contains four nitrogen atoms, labeled Nα to Nδ. Nα sits in a six-membered saturated ring, bonded to two ring CH₂ carbons and to a methyl group. Nβ is bonded to one H, to a CH₂ group and to the carbon of a C=O group. Nγ is a ring atom of a pyridine ring, double-bonded to one of its two ring carbons. Nδ carries two H atoms and is bonded to one CH₂ carbon.","q":"Which nitrogen of Compound 5 is part of a tertiary amine?","choices":["Nα","Nβ","Nγ","Nδ"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"An N bonded to the carbon of a C=O is an amide nitrogen, not an amine."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A pyridine N is part of an aromatic ring with a C=N bond; it is not an amine with three single-bonded carbons."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An N with two H atoms and one carbon is a primary amine; tertiary needs three carbons on N."}],"rule":"Amine = N with only C and H neighbors by single bonds (no C=O, no C=N). Count the carbons on N: one = 1°, two = 2°, three = 3°.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Scan each N for a C=O (amide) or C=N (imine/aromatic N) first; of the true amines, pick the one bonded to three carbons and no H."},"exp":"An **amine** nitrogen has only single bonds to C or H and no attached C=O. **Nα** is bonded to three carbons (two ring CH₂ groups and a methyl) and no H: a **tertiary amine**. Nβ is attached to a C=O carbon, making it an **amide** N. Nγ is the ring N of **pyridine**, joined to a ring carbon by a C=N bond. Nδ has two H and one C: a **primary amine**.","how":{"ask":"Classify each nitrogen by its neighbors, then find the amine with three carbons on N.","know":"• **Amine**: N–C and N–H single bonds only.\n• 1°, 2°, 3° = one, two or three carbons on N.\n• **Amide**: N–C(=O); **imine**: C=N; **carbamate**: N–C(=O)–O.","steps":[{"text":"Nα: three carbons, no H, no C=O.","hl":"bonded to two ring CH₂ carbons and to a methyl group","calc":["3 C on N → tertiary amine"]},{"text":"Nβ is attached to a carbonyl carbon: an amide.","hl":"to the carbon of a C=O group","strike":["Nβ"]},{"text":"Nγ is double-bonded within an aromatic ring, and Nδ has only one carbon.","strike":["Nγ","Nδ"]},{"text":"Answer: Nα."}],"trap":"Calling an amide nitrogen an amine: once N is bonded to a C=O carbon, it is an amide, however many carbons it carries."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"two ring CH₂ carbons and to a methyl group","note":"three C on N"},{"t":"the carbon of a C=O group","note":"amide"},{"t":"pyridine ring","note":"aromatic C=N"},{"t":"carries two H atoms","note":"primary"}]},{"text":"**A tertiary amine** has three carbon groups on N and a lone pair, like triethylamine.","fig":"triethylamine"},{"text":"**Also:** an ester is C(=O)–O–C; next to an N, a C=O makes an amide, and N–C(=O)–O makes a carbamate.","fig":"cpt-fix-amino-ester-co-hl"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** rule out amides (N–C=O) and imines (C=N) first, then count carbons on the amine N: three carbons, no H = tertiary."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-30a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:30#1","pe4-cp:30#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:30"],"passage":"Levulinic acid, made from plant cellulose, is a building block for green solvents. Its methyl ester, methyl 4-oxopentanoate, CH₃CO–CH₂CH₂–CO₂CH₃, shown, reacts with NaBH₄; this reagent reduces ketones but leaves esters intact. The mixture is then warmed with a trace of acid, and methanol is lost as a ring closes.","q":"After the steps in the passage, which organic product is isolated?","choices":["γ-Valerolactone","δ-Valerolactone","Pentane-1,4-diol","Methyl 4-hydroxypentanoate"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Warm acid lets a γ-OH close onto the ester, so the open hydroxy ester doesn't survive."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A δ-lactone would need the OH one carbon farther out, on C5; here the new OH is on C4."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"NaBH₄ reduces ketones but not esters; a diol would need LiAlH₄."}],"rule":"NaBH₄ reduces ketones, not esters; a hydroxy ester with the OH γ or δ to the C=O then closes to a ring on warming with acid.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"NaBH₄ reduces ketones, not esters. New OH on C4 (γ) closes onto C1: ring C1–C4 + O = 5 atoms. 5-ring lactone = γ, 6-ring = δ: γ-valerolactone."},"exp":"NaBH₄ is a mild hydride donor: it reduces the **ketone** at C4 to a secondary alcohol but leaves the **ester** alone. The new OH sits on the γ-carbon of the ester, so it attacks the ester carbonyl in the same molecule, expels **methanol** and closes a ring of C1–C4 plus the O: five atoms, **γ-valerolactone**.","how":{"ask":"Decide which carbonyl NaBH₄ reduces, then count the ring formed when the new OH attacks the ester.","know":"• **NaBH₄** reduces aldehydes and ketones to alcohols; it does **not** reduce esters (that needs LiAlH₄).\n• A **γ-hydroxy ester** (OH on the γ-carbon, C4, two carbons past the α-carbon) cyclizes to a **five-membered lactone**: count the ring as the carbons from C1 to the OH carbon, plus the O. The ester's alcohol part (here methanol) leaves.","steps":[{"text":"Number from the ester carbon: C1 is the CO₂CH₃ carbon, C4 the ketone carbon.","hl":"CH₃CO–CH₂CH₂–CO₂CH₃","calc":["C5H₃–C4(=O)–C3H₂–C2H₂–C1(=O)OCH₃"]},{"text":"NaBH₄ reduces only the ketone, so the ester survives. A diol would need the ester reduced too, which takes LiAlH₄.","hl":"NaBH₄","calc":["C4=O → C4H–OH"],"strike":["Pentane-1,4-diol"]},{"text":"The C4 OH is γ to the ester carbonyl. With acid and warmth it attacks C1 and methanol leaves, so the open hydroxy ester does not survive.","hl":"warmed with a trace of acid","strike":["Methyl 4-hydroxypentanoate"]},{"text":"Count the ring atoms: C1, C2, C3, C4 and the O.","calc":["ring = 4 C + 1 O = 5 atoms → γ-lactone"]},{"text":"A δ-lactone would need the OH one carbon farther out, on C5.","strike":["δ-Valerolactone"]},{"text":"Answer: γ-Valerolactone."}],"trap":"Letting NaBH₄ reduce the ester as well: it is too mild, so the ester stays to be attacked by the new OH."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"4-oxopentanoate","note":"ketone at C4 of an ester"},{"t":"NaBH₄","note":"reduces the ketone only"},{"t":"warmed with a trace of acid","note":"ring closure"}]},{"text":"**Step 1: what does the reducing agent touch?** NaBH₄ turns a C=O of an aldehyde or ketone into C–OH and leaves esters, acids and amides unchanged.","calc":["R₂C=O → R₂CH–OH; RCO₂R′ unchanged"]},{"text":"**Step 2: find the hydroxy ester.** Locate the new OH relative to the ester carbon: on the γ-carbon (C4) or the δ-carbon (C5)."},{"text":"**Step 3: cyclize.** The OH attacks the ester C=O inside the molecule, the OR′ group leaves as R′OH, and a lactone forms. Ring size = carbons from C1 to the OH carbon, plus the O.","calc":["γ: 4 C + O = 5-membered"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** NaBH₄ reduces a ketone to an alcohol but leaves an ester; a γ- or δ-hydroxy ester then closes to a five- or six-membered lactone and releases the ester's alcohol."}],"fig":"cpt-methyl-4-oxopentanoate","choiceFigs":["cpt-gamma-valerolactone","cpt-delta-valerolactone","cpt-pentane-14-diol","cpt-methyl-4-hydroxypentanoate"]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-31a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:31#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:31"],"passage":"Compound 9, an intermediate in a natural-product synthesis, carries two ethyl ester groups and one six-membered lactone ring. To unmask its acid groups, the chemist heats it under reflux with excess aqueous KOH, about six equivalents. After the mixture cools, the product is kept in basic solution for the next step.","q":"What happens to Compound 9 in the KOH step?","choices":["All three are hydrolyzed","Only the ethyl esters react","Just the lactone is opened","Nothing is hydrolyzed"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Hot hydroxide is a strong nucleophile that attacks ester carbonyls; that is saponification."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A lactone is a cyclic ester, so excess hot base opens it just as it cleaves the open-chain esters."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The open-chain esters are not protected; with base in excess they are cleaved too."}],"rule":"Excess hot hydroxide saponifies every ester in the molecule, cyclic (lactone) or not, giving one carboxylate per ester group.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count the ester carbonyls, lactone included: with base in excess, every one becomes a carboxylate."},"exp":"Heating with **excess hydroxide** hydrolyzes esters (**saponification**). A **lactone**, being an ester closed into a ring, opens too, giving a carboxylate and an alcohol on the same chain. With six equivalents of KOH for three ester groups, **all three are hydrolyzed**: two carboxylates from the ethyl esters (plus ethanol) and one from the lactone, which stay as carboxylates in the basic solution.","how":{"ask":"Decide which ester groups excess hot base cleaves.","know":"• **Saponification**: RCOOR' + OH⁻ → RCOO⁻ + R'OH, essentially irreversible.\n• A **lactone** is a cyclic ester and saponifies the same way (ring opens).\n• Excess base → every ester reacts.","steps":[{"text":"Count the ester groups.","hl":"two ethyl ester groups and one six-membered lactone ring","calc":["2 esters + 1 lactone = 3 ester carbonyls"]},{"text":"Check the base: excess and hot.","hl":"excess aqueous KOH, about six equivalents"},{"text":"Hot hydroxide does cleave esters.","strike":["Nothing is hydrolyzed"]},{"text":"Neither the lactone nor the open esters are spared when base is in excess.","strike":["Only the ethyl esters react","Just the lactone is opened"]},{"text":"Answer: All three are hydrolyzed."}],"trap":"Forgetting that a lactone is an ester: the ring opens under the same conditions that cleave the ethyl esters."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"two ethyl ester groups","note":"two esters"},{"t":"lactone","note":"a cyclic ester"},{"t":"excess aqueous KOH","note":"saponifies all"}]},{"text":"**Saponification.** OH⁻ adds to the C=O, the alkoxide leaves, and the acid is deprotonated to a carboxylate.","calc":["R–CO–OEt + OH⁻ → R–COO⁻ + EtOH"]},{"text":"**Lactone:** the same reaction opens the ring, leaving –COO⁻ and –OH on one chain."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** excess hot hydroxide hydrolyzes every ester and lactone into a carboxylate."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-32a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:32#1","pe4-cp:32#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:32"],"passage":"Piperidine is sold as its hydrochloride salt, a stable, odorless solid. To make N-benzylpiperidine, aqueous NaOH is first mixed with the salt. Benzyl bromide is then added for an SN2 reaction. The piperidinium ion has pKa ≈ 11.1, and water, the conjugate acid of hydroxide, has pKa ≈ 15.7.","q":"Which species attacks the benzyl bromide?","choices":["Piperidinium ion","Free piperidine","Piperidide anion","Benzyl carbanion"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"In the ammonium ion the N lone pair is used in the N–H⁺ bond, so it has no free pair to attack carbon."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Removing the N–H proton (pKa about 35) needs a far stronger base than hydroxide."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Benzyl bromide is the electrophile; nothing here pulls a proton from its carbon to make a carbanion."}],"rule":"A base frees an amine from its salt only as far as pKa allows. The neutral amine, with its lone pair free, is the nucleophile in SN2.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Hydroxide (water pKa 15.7) easily deprotonates R₂NH₂⁺ (pKa 11), but not R₂NH (pKa ~35). So the lone-pair amine is what attacks."},"exp":"Hydroxide is a stronger base than piperidine (water pKa 15.7 > piperidinium pKa 11.1), so it **removes H⁺ from the piperidinium ion** and frees **neutral piperidine**. Only the neutral amine has a **free lone pair**, so it is the nucleophile that attacks the benzylic carbon and displaces bromide. Hydroxide is far too weak to strip the N–H proton (pKa ≈ 35) to form the anion.","how":{"ask":"Use the pKa values to see what NaOH does to the salt, then find the species with a free lone pair.","know":"• An acid–base reaction favors the side with the weaker acid (higher pKa).\n• **Ammonium salts** are not nucleophilic: the N lone pair is tied up by H⁺.\n• **Free amines** (lone pair) are good SN2 nucleophiles.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the acids.","hl":"The piperidinium ion has pKa ≈ 11.1, and water, the conjugate acid of hydroxide, has pKa ≈ 15.7","calc":["11.1 < 15.7 → OH⁻ deprotonates piperidinium"]},{"text":"The salt's N has no free lone pair, so it cannot attack.","strike":["Piperidinium ion"]},{"text":"OH⁻ cannot remove the N–H of the neutral amine, and the electrophile is not deprotonated.","strike":["Piperidide anion","Benzyl carbanion"]},{"text":"Answer: Free piperidine.","fig":"cpt-piperidine"}],"trap":"Thinking the base makes an even stronger nucleophile (the amide anion): hydroxide only reaches the free amine."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"hydrochloride salt","note":"R₂NH₂⁺ Cl⁻"},{"t":"aqueous NaOH","note":"frees the amine"},{"t":"SN2 reaction","note":"needs a lone pair"}]},{"text":"**Why base first.** NaOH removes H⁺ from the ammonium salt, freeing the amine; only the free amine has a lone pair to attack carbon."},{"text":"**Too far:** the amide anion would need a base stronger than pKa ≈ 35.","fig":"cpt-piperidide"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an amine salt must be deprotonated by a base stronger than the ammonium ion; the resulting neutral amine is the nucleophile."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-33a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:33#1","pe4-cp:33#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:33"],"passage":"In a synthesis of a neurotransmitter analog, an amine was protected as an ethyl carbamate, R–NH–CO₂Et, while other groups were changed. A carbamate joins an amide and an ester on a single carbonyl group. In the last step, the compound was heated under reflux with aqueous KOH. The free amine was then extracted into ether.","q":"What does complete hydrolysis in the final KOH step release?","choices":["R–NH₂, CO₃²⁻ and EtOH","R–NH–CO₂H plus C₂H₆","R–NH₂ and ethyl formate","R–NH–CO₂⁻ plus EtOH"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"The carbamate anion is only halfway; it loses CO₂ and the free amine is set free, as the extraction shows."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Hydrolysis breaks the C–O bond to give ethanol, not ethane; no C–H bond forms."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The ethyl group leaves as ethanol, and the carbonyl carbon ends up as carbonate, so no ester survives."}],"rule":"Fully hydrolyzing a carbamate cuts both sides of the carbonyl: amine + alcohol + CO₂ (carbonate in base).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Split at the C=O: the N side becomes the free amine, the O side becomes the alcohol, and the carbonyl carbon leaves as CO₂ (carbonate in base)."},"exp":"Hot aqueous base cleaves **both** acyl bonds of a carbamate. Hydroxide attack releases **ethanol** and a carbamate ion, R–NH–CO₂⁻, which loses **CO₂** to set free the **amine R–NH₂**. In KOH the CO₂ is trapped as **carbonate**. That is why the free amine could be extracted into ether.","how":{"ask":"Break the carbamate on both sides of its carbonyl and name the three pieces.","know":"• **Carbamate** = R₂N–C(=O)–OR': amide and ester on one carbonyl.\n• Hydrolysis gives **amine + CO₂ + alcohol**; in base, CO₂ → CO₃²⁻.\n• Hydrolysis needs strong base (or acid) and heat.","steps":[{"text":"Locate the carbonyl and its two sides.","hl":"R–NH–CO₂Et","calc":["N side → R–NH₂","O side → EtOH","C=O carbon → CO₂ → CO₃²⁻ in base"]},{"text":"The carbamate anion still has to lose CO₂.","strike":["R–NH–CO₂⁻ plus EtOH"]},{"text":"The ethyl group leaves as ethanol, not ethane or an ester.","strike":["R–NH–CO₂H plus C₂H₆","R–NH₂ and ethyl formate"]},{"text":"Answer: R–NH₂, CO₃²⁻ and EtOH."}],"trap":"Stopping at the carbamic acid or its anion: these lose CO₂ on their own, so complete hydrolysis frees the amine."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"ethyl carbamate","note":"N–C(=O)–OEt"},{"t":"heated under reflux with aqueous KOH","note":"hydrolysis conditions"},{"t":"free amine was then extracted","note":"the amine was released"}]},{"text":"**Two cleavages.** Ester side → alcohol; amide side → amine; the carbonyl carbon becomes CO₂."},{"text":"**Also:** only a step with strong base or acid and heat cleaves a carbamate; NaBH₄ at 0 °C or a tosylation leaves it intact."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** complete hydrolysis of a carbamate gives the amine, the alcohol and CO₂ (as carbonate in base)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-34a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:34#1","pe4-cp:34#2","pe4-cp:34#3","pe4-cp:34#4","pe4-cp:34#5","pe6-cp:15#1","pe6-cp:15#2","pe6-cp:15#3","pe6-cp:15#4"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:34"],"passage":"Compound K was screened as an inhibitor of a bacterial dehydrogenase. Initial rates at several substrate concentrations were collected in the absence and the presence of K, and the double-reciprocal plot is shown. With K added, the plot keeps its y-intercept at 0.25 s/μM, but its x-intercept moves from −0.50 to −0.125 μM⁻¹.","q":"Which type of inhibition does K show, and how does it affect KM?","choices":["Competitive; KM rises","Noncompetitive; KM rises","Uncompetitive; KM falls","Mixed; KM falls"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"The x-intercept (−1/KM) moved toward zero, so KM grew; a falling KM reads the shift backwards."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"KM went up, not down, and mixed inhibition would also lift the y-intercept, which stayed put."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Noncompetitive inhibition leaves KM unchanged and lowers Vmax, raising the y-intercept; that is not what happened."}],"rule":"Lineweaver–Burk: y-intercept = 1/Vmax, x-intercept = −1/KM. Same y, x toward zero → KM up with Vmax fixed; match that to the inhibitor type.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"LB plot: y-intercept = 1/Vmax, x-intercept = −1/KM. Same y → Vmax unchanged; x from −0.5 to −0.125 → KM 2 → 8 μM, up. Competitive pattern."},"exp":"On a double-reciprocal plot the **y-intercept is 1/Vmax** and the **x-intercept is −1/KM**. The y-intercept stays at 0.25, so **Vmax stays 4 μM/s**. The x-intercept moves toward zero, so **KM rises from 2 to 8 μM**. A larger apparent KM at the same Vmax is the signature of a competitive inhibitor: the lines cross on the y-axis.","how":{"ask":"Translate each intercept into Vmax or KM, then match the pattern to an inhibitor type.","know":"• Lineweaver–Burk: 1/v = (KM/Vmax)(1/[S]) + 1/Vmax.\n• **y-intercept = 1/Vmax**; **x-intercept = −1/KM**; slope = KM/Vmax.\n• Competitive: Vmax same, KM up (lines cross on the y-axis).\n• Noncompetitive: Vmax down, KM same (lines cross on the x-axis).\n• Mixed: Vmax down and KM changed (lines cross off both axes).\n• Uncompetitive: Vmax and KM both fall by the same factor (parallel lines).","steps":[{"text":"The y-intercept gives Vmax; it did not move.","hl":"keeps its y-intercept at 0.25 s/μM","calc":["Vmax = 1 ÷ 0.25 s/μM = **4 μM/s** (with and without inhibitor)"]},{"text":"The x-intercept gives KM; it moved toward zero.","hl":"from −0.50 to −0.125 μM⁻¹","calc":["−1/KM = −0.50 μM⁻¹ → KM = **2 μM**","−1/KM = −0.125 μM⁻¹ → KM = **8 μM**"]},{"text":"Vmax did not change, and KM went up, not down.","strike":["Uncompetitive; KM falls","Mixed; KM falls"]},{"text":"Noncompetitive inhibition leaves KM unchanged and lowers Vmax; here KM rose with Vmax fixed.","strike":["Noncompetitive; KM rises"]},{"text":"Answer: Competitive; KM rises (Vmax unchanged; the lines cross on the y-axis)."}],"trap":"Mixing up the axes: the y-intercept is 1/Vmax and the x-intercept is −1/KM, so an x-intercept closer to zero means a larger KM, not a smaller one."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and which intercept it belongs to.","marks":[{"t":"y-intercept at 0.25 s/μM","note":"1/Vmax"},{"t":"x-intercept","note":"−1/KM"},{"t":"−0.50 to −0.125","note":"moves toward zero"}]},{"text":"**Read the intercepts.** y-intercept = 1/Vmax; x-intercept = −1/KM.","calc":["Vmax = 1/0.25 = 4 μM/s, both lines","KM: 1/0.50 = 2 μM → 1/0.125 = 8 μM"]},{"text":"**Competitive:** the inhibitor competes for the active site, so enough substrate wins it back: Vmax is unchanged and KM rises. The lines meet on the y-axis."},{"text":"**Also:** noncompetitive inhibition lowers Vmax with KM unchanged: the y-intercept rises and the lines meet on the x-axis."},{"text":"**Also:** mixed inhibition lowers Vmax and changes KM: the lines meet away from both axes."},{"text":"**Also:** uncompetitive inhibition lowers both Vmax and KM by the same factor: the lines are parallel (same slope, higher intercepts)."},{"text":"**Also:** a table of KM and Vmax, with and without the inhibitor, names the type with no plot at all. Compare each column with the control: KM up and Vmax the same → competitive; Vmax down and KM the same → noncompetitive; both down by the same factor → uncompetitive; Vmax down and KM moved either way → mixed.","calc":["control: KM 3 mM, Vmax 60 μM/min","+ inhibitor: KM 3 mM, Vmax 20 μM/min","Vmax ÷ 3, KM unchanged → noncompetitive"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a Lineweaver–Burk plot, y-intercept = 1/Vmax and x-intercept = −1/KM; competitive keeps Vmax and raises KM, noncompetitive lowers Vmax only, mixed lowers Vmax and shifts KM, uncompetitive lowers both."}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Lineweaver–Burk plot with and without the inhibitor","x":{"label":"1/[S] (μM⁻¹)","min":-0.5,"max":1,"step":0.25},"y":{"label":"1/v (s/μM)","min":0,"max":2.5,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"No inhibitor","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-0.5,0],[0,0.25],[0.5,0.5],[1,0.75]]},{"name":"+ Inhibitor","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-0.125,0],[0,0.25],[0.5,1.25],[1,2.25]]}]}},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-35a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:35#1","pe4-cp:35#2","pe4-cp:35#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:35"],"passage":"Enzyme D, a dehydrogenase from a parasite, uses NADH and an α-keto acid as its two substrates. In binding studies, NADH bound the free enzyme tightly. The α-keto acid also bound the free enzyme, with similar affinity, when no NADH was present. No product was released until both substrates were bound together, and no covalent enzyme intermediate was detected.","q":"Which mechanism best fits these findings?","choices":["Random sequential","Ordered, NADH first","Ordered, keto acid first","Ping-pong"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"An ordered path needs one substrate before the other, but the keto acid also binds the free enzyme."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"NADH binds the free enzyme too, so neither substrate is required first."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Ping-pong releases a product (and forms a modified enzyme) before the second substrate binds; neither was seen."}],"rule":"Sequential = no product leaves until the enzyme holds both substrates. Ordered if only one can bind free enzyme; random if either can. Ping-pong = a product leaves first.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Two questions: does a product leave before both bind (ping-pong)? If not, can either substrate bind free enzyme (random) or only one (ordered)?"},"exp":"No product leaves until **both substrates are bound** and no covalent intermediate forms, so the mechanism is **sequential**, not ping-pong. Since **either substrate can bind the free enzyme** first, the order is not fixed: it is **random sequential**.","how":{"ask":"Decide sequential vs ping-pong from product release, then ordered vs random from which substrate can bind free enzyme.","know":"• **Sequential**: a ternary complex (E·A·B) forms before any product leaves.\n• **Ordered**: one substrate must bind first; **random**: either can.\n• **Ping-pong**: the first product leaves (enzyme modified) before the second substrate binds.","steps":[{"text":"No product leaves before both are bound, and the enzyme is never modified.","hl":"No product was released until both substrates were bound together","strike":["Ping-pong"]},{"text":"Each substrate binds the free enzyme by itself.","hl":"also bound the free enzyme","strike":["Ordered, NADH first","Ordered, keto acid first"]},{"text":"Answer: Random sequential."}],"trap":"Reading \"NADH binds tightly\" as \"NADH binds first\": tight binding does not make the order compulsory when the other substrate also binds free enzyme."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"bound the free enzyme tightly","note":"NADH can go first"},{"t":"also bound the free enzyme","note":"so can the keto acid"},{"t":"until both substrates were bound together","note":"sequential"}]},{"text":"**Ordered sequential:** substrate B binds only the E·A complex, never free enzyme. **Random:** E·A and E·B both form."},{"text":"**Ping-pong:** E + A → F + P, then F + B → E + Q; a product leaves first, often with a covalent enzyme intermediate."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** product out before the second substrate = ping-pong; otherwise sequential, ordered if only one substrate binds free enzyme, random if either does."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-36a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:36#1","pe4-cp:36#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:36"],"passage":"Octyl glucoside is a mild detergent used to lift membrane proteins out of lipid bilayers. In a crystal structure of a bacterial transporter, one detergent molecule stays bound. Its highlighted octyl tail, shown, lies deep in a narrow pocket from which water is excluded. The glucose head stays at the mouth of the pocket, hydrogen-bonded to the solvent.","q":"The tail's part of the pocket is most likely lined by which residue?","choices":["Valine","Glutamate","Threonine","Arginine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Its side chain is a charged carboxylate, which needs water or a partner charge, not a dry pocket around a hydrocarbon."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Its side-chain O–H is polar and would rather hydrogen-bond, so it suits the head, not an all-carbon tail."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Its guanidinium group carries a positive charge, which is costly to bury against a nonpolar chain."}],"rule":"Match the ligand part to the residue: a nonpolar chain or ring in a water-free pocket sits against nonpolar side chains (Leu, Ile, Val, Phe, Ala, Met).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Highlighted part all C and H, and no water: pick the only side chain that is pure hydrocarbon."},"exp":"The octyl tail is **all C–H**, and the pocket **excludes water**, so the tail is held by **hydrophobic (London) contacts** with nonpolar side chains such as **valine**, leucine, isoleucine and phenylalanine. Glutamate and arginine carry charges and threonine has a polar O–H; those suit the glucose head at the water-exposed mouth, not the buried tail.","how":{"ask":"Classify the highlighted part of the ligand, then pick the side chain that interacts best with it in a dry pocket.","know":"• **Nonpolar side chains**: Ala, Val, Leu, Ile, Met, Phe, Trp, Pro.\n• **Polar**: Ser, Thr, Asn, Gln, Tyr, Cys; **charged**: Asp, Glu (−), Lys, Arg, His (+).\n• A hydrocarbon buried away from water makes hydrophobic contacts.","steps":[{"text":"The highlighted part is a hydrocarbon chain.","hl":"Its highlighted octyl tail"},{"text":"Its surroundings are dry.","hl":"from which water is excluded","calc":["nonpolar + no water → hydrophobic contacts"]},{"text":"Charged and polar side chains do not suit a buried hydrocarbon.","strike":["Glutamate","Arginine","Threonine"]},{"text":"Answer: Valine."}],"trap":"Picking a residue that could hydrogen-bond to the head group: the question asks about the part of the pocket around the tail."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"octyl tail","note":"eight-carbon hydrocarbon"},{"t":"water is excluded","note":"hydrophobic pocket"},{"t":"hydrogen-bonded to the solvent","note":"the polar head is elsewhere"}]},{"text":"**Like with like.** Nonpolar ligand parts pack against nonpolar side chains; the main force is hydrophobic (London dispersion) contact.","fig":"leucine-sc"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a nonpolar part of a drug buried in a water-free pocket is lined by nonpolar side chains such as Leu, Ile, Val and Phe."}],"fig":"octyl-glucoside-tail"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-37a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:37#1","pe4-cp:37#2","pe4-cp:37#3","pe4-cp:37#4"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:37"],"passage":"Covalent drugs often carry an electron-poor C=C that a cysteine thiol of the target protein can add to. In one study, the probe compound shown was made for this purpose. The molecule shown contains one benzene ring, one ketone C=O, one C=C outside the ring, and two C≡N groups. The two nitrile groups make the C=C especially electron-poor.","q":"How many π bonds does the probe compound contain?","choices":["5","7","9","11"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"Too few: the ring's 3 π plus the C=O and C=C already make 5 before any C≡N is counted."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"7 counts each C≡N as one π bond; a triple bond holds 2 π bonds."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Too many: a triple bond is 1 σ + 2 π, so 3 π per C≡N overcounts."}],"rule":"Count π bonds: each double bond 1, each triple bond 2, a benzene ring 3; then add them.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"π bonds = bonds beyond the first: double 1, triple 2, benzene ring 3. 3 + 1 (C=O) + 1 (C=C) + 2 × 2 (two C≡N) = 9."},"exp":"Any pair of bonded atoms shares exactly one **σ bond**; every extra bond joining that pair is a **π bond**. So the C=O gives 1 π, the C=C 1 π, each C≡N 2 π, and the benzene ring (three C=C) 3 π: **3 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 = 9**.","how":{"ask":"Give each multiple bond its π count, then add group by group.","know":"• **Single bond** = 1 σ, 0 π.\n• **Double bond** (C=O, C=C) = 1 σ + **1 π**.\n• **Triple bond** (C≡N, C≡C) = 1 σ + **2 π**.\n• **Benzene ring** = three C=C = **3 π**.\n• More shared pairs pull atoms closer: a **double bond is shorter than a single bond**.","steps":[{"text":"List the groups with multiple bonds.","hl":"one benzene ring, one ketone C=O, one C=C outside the ring, and two C≡N groups"},{"text":"The ketone C=O is a double bond: one σ bond holds the atoms, and the second bond is π.","calc":["C=O = 1 σ + 1 π → 1 π"]},{"text":"Each C≡N is a triple bond: one σ and two π.","calc":["2 × C≡N = 2 × 2 π = 4 π"]},{"text":"The ring has three C=C (3 π), and the extra C=C has 1 π. Add everything.","calc":["3 (ring) + 1 (C=O) + 1 (C=C) + 4 (C≡N) = 9 π"]},{"text":"Counting each C≡N as only 1 π gives 7; also counting the ring as a single π gives 5; counting all three lines of each triple bond as π gives 11.","calc":["3 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 7","1 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 5","3 + 1 + 1 + 6 = 11"],"strike":["7","5","11"]},{"text":"**Also:** the π bond adds a second shared pair, so C=O (about 120 pm) is shorter than a C–O single bond (about 140 pm)."},{"text":"Answer: 9."}],"trap":"Counting a triple bond as one π bond: only its first bond is σ, so the other two are both π."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"one benzene ring","note":"three C=C"},{"t":"one ketone C=O","note":"a double bond"},{"t":"two C≡N groups","note":"two triple bonds"},{"t":"π bonds","note":"count only the extra bonds"}]},{"text":"**σ first, then π.** Between two atoms the first bond is always σ (head-on overlap); every extra bond is π (side-by-side p overlap).","calc":["double = 1 σ + 1 π","triple = 1 σ + 2 π"]},{"text":"**A carbonyl** C=O is one σ plus one π, not two σ bonds and not a lone π bond."},{"text":"**Count by group.** Benzene ring 3 π; each C=O or C=C 1 π; each C≡N or C≡C 2 π. Then add.","calc":["ring 3 + C=O 1 + C=C 1 + C≡N 2 + C≡N 2 = 9"]},{"text":"**Also:** more bonds between the same two atoms means a shorter, stronger link: triple < double < single in length."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count one π per double bond and two per triple bond; a benzene ring has three; add them group by group, and remember a double bond is shorter than a single one."}],"fig":"cpt-benzoyl-methylene-malononitrile"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-38a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:38#1","pe4-cp:38#2","pe4-cp:39#1","pe4-cp:5#1","pe4-cp:5#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:38"],"passage":"Tartrazine, a yellow food dye, is measured in a sports drink. The dye has two absorption bands: its molar absorptivity is 2.1 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ for 430 nm and 7.0 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ for 550 nm. A diluted drink sample in a 0.40 cm cell absorbs A = 0.63 at 430 nm. No other ingredient absorbs at that wavelength.","q":"What is the dye's concentration in the diluted sample?","choices":["12 μM","30 μM","75 μM","225 μM"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"That multiplies by the path length instead of dividing by it; a cell under 1 cm must make c bigger than A/ε, not smaller."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Forgot b: that is A/ε alone, as if the cell were 1 cm; divide by the 0.40 cm path too."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Uses the 550 nm ε; ε depends on wavelength, so always use the ε at the wavelength you measured."}],"rule":"c = A ÷ (εb), with ε at the measured wavelength; a cell shorter than 1 cm raises c. Round each number to 1 digit, then × 10⁶ for μM.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Use the 430 nm ε only. c = A ÷ (εb): pre-divide 0.63 ÷ 2.1 = 0.30, then 0.30 ÷ 0.40 = 0.75; times 10⁻⁴ gives 7.5 × 10⁻⁵ M = 75 μM."},"exp":"**Beer's law, A = εbc, so c = A/(εb).** Use the ε at the measured wavelength (430 nm): c = 0.63 ÷ (2.1 × 10⁴ × 0.40) = 0.63 ÷ 8400 = 7.5 × 10⁻⁵ M = **75 μM**.","how":{"ask":"Rearrange Beer's law for c, using the ε that matches the wavelength of the reading.","know":"• **Beer's law: A = εbc** (ε in M⁻¹cm⁻¹, b = path length in cm, c in M), so **c = A/(εb)**.\n• ε depends on wavelength: use ε for the wavelength at which you measured A.\n• A blank subtracts what the beam passes through only if the reference and sample cells are identical except for the analyte.","steps":[{"text":"The reading was taken at 430 nm, so use ε at 430 nm and ignore the 550 nm value.","hl":"2.1 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ for 430 nm"},{"text":"Rearrange Beer's law and substitute, keeping the path length.","hl":"0.40 cm cell","calc":["A = εbc → c = A ÷ (εb)","εb = 2.1 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ × 0.40 cm = 8.4 × 10³ M⁻¹","c = 0.63 ÷ 8.4 × 10³ M⁻¹ = **7.5 × 10⁻⁵ M**","7.5 × 10⁻⁵ M × 10⁶ μM/M = **75 μM**"]},{"text":"225 μM uses the 550 nm absorptivity (0.63 ÷ 2800). 30 μM drops the path length (0.63 ÷ 2.1 × 10⁴). 12 μM multiplies by b instead of dividing.","strike":["225 μM","30 μM","12 μM"]},{"text":"If A were too high for the detector, dilute the sample or use a shorter cell: A = εbc falls in proportion to c or b.","calc":["halve c → A halves","halve b → A halves"]},{"text":"Answer: 75 μM."}],"trap":"Grabbing the other wavelength's ε, or forgetting to divide by a path length that is not 1 cm."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"0.40 cm cell","note":"path length b"},{"t":"A = 0.63 at 430 nm","note":"absorbance, read at 430 nm"},{"t":"2.1 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ for 430 nm","note":"the ε to use"},{"t":"7.0 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ for 550 nm","note":"wrong wavelength: a distractor"}]},{"text":"**Beer's law:** absorbance grows with ε, path and concentration. Solve for c.","calc":["A = εbc","c = A ÷ (εb)"]},{"text":"**Match the wavelength.** ε is a property of the compound at one wavelength; the reading was at 430 nm.","calc":["c = 0.63 ÷ (2.1 × 10⁴ × 0.40)","c = 0.63 ÷ 8400 = 7.5 × 10⁻⁵ M = 75 μM"]},{"text":"**Also:** a reading above the detector's linear range is fixed by lowering A: dilute the sample (smaller c) or use a shorter path (smaller b). A longer cell makes A larger, not smaller.","calc":["A = εbc: dilute 3-fold → A ÷ 3","1.0 cm → 0.20 cm cell → A ÷ 5"]},{"text":"**Also:** the reference (blank) and sample cells must differ only in the analyte, so the two readings can be compared. A cell's own absorbance (a scratch, a different glass) would add to the sample reading.","calc":["A(read) = A(analyte) + A(cell difference)","cell difference 0.10 → reading too high by 0.10"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** c = A/(εb) with ε at the measured wavelength; lower a too-high A by diluting or shortening the path, and blank with an identical cell."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-39a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe4-cp:39#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:39"],"passage":"A protein purification system monitors fractions with a UV detector at 280 nm. The detector responds linearly only up to A = 1.2. Fractions read in its standard 10 mm flow cell give absorbances from 0.4 to 3.1. The peak fractions must be measured accurately to pool them.","q":"How can the strongest fractions be read within the detector's linear range?","choices":["Shorter (2 mm) flow cell","Longer (20 mm) flow cell","Twofold concentration","Brighter lamp"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"A longer path raises A in proportion (A = εbc), pushing the strong fractions further out of range."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Concentrating raises c, and so A; the readings need to come down, not up."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Absorbance is a ratio of light in to light out, so a brighter source leaves A unchanged."}],"rule":"A = εbc: to lower an absorbance that is too high, shorten the path b or dilute (lower c). Lamp brightness does not change A.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"A scales with path: 3.1 × (2/10) ≈ 0.62, inside the 1.2 limit. A shorter path (or a dilution) is the fix."},"exp":"By **A = εbc**, absorbance is proportional to path length and concentration. Cutting the path from 10 mm to **2 mm** divides every reading by 5: the top fraction drops from 3.1 to about 0.62, inside the linear range. Diluting the fractions would also work. A longer path or concentrating raises A, and a brighter lamp leaves A unchanged.","how":{"ask":"Find the change that lowers A enough to bring the highest reading under the detector's limit.","know":"• **A = εbc**: A ∝ path length b and concentration c.\n• To lower A: shorten b or dilute.\n• A = log(I₀/I) does not depend on how bright the lamp is.","steps":[{"text":"The limit and the highest reading.","hl":"linearly only up to A = 1.2","calc":["need 3.1 → ≤ 1.2: a factor of at least 2.6"]},{"text":"The current path.","hl":"standard 10 mm flow cell","calc":["2 mm: A × 0.2 → 3.1 × 0.2 ≈ 0.62 ✓"]},{"text":"A longer path or a more concentrated sample raises A.","strike":["Longer (20 mm) flow cell","Twofold concentration"]},{"text":"A brighter source does not change A.","strike":["Brighter lamp"]},{"text":"Answer: 2 mm flow cell."}],"trap":"Reaching for more signal: the problem is too much absorbance, so the fix is less sample in the beam (shorter path or dilution)."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"linearly only up to A = 1.2","note":"upper limit"},{"t":"from 0.4 to 3.1","note":"top readings too high"},{"t":"10 mm flow cell","note":"path b"}]},{"text":"**Two handles on A.** Path length and concentration; both lower A in proportion.","calc":["A = εbc"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to bring an absorbance down into a detector's range, dilute the sample or use a shorter path, then correct back by the same factor."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-40a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:40#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:40"],"passage":"Weak acid HB is a candidate buffer for cell culture. A solution of HB was titrated with NaOH, giving the curve shown. A buffer made from HB and its conjugate base B⁻ is considered useful only while neither form outnumbers the other by more than tenfold. The culture may need to be held anywhere from about pH 6.5 to 9.","q":"At which pH would an HB/B⁻ buffer NOT be useful?","choices":["pH 6.6","pH 7.4","pH 8.1","pH 8.7"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is the half-equivalence pH, where acid and base are equal: the best buffering point, the opposite of what is asked."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"It lies less than one unit below the pKa, so the acid outnumbers the base by under tenfold: still useful."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"It lies less than one unit above the pKa, so base outnumbers acid by under tenfold: still useful."}],"rule":"Read pKa at half-equivalence on the titration curve. A tenfold ratio limit means the buffer works only from pKa − 1 to pKa + 1.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Half of 20 mL is 10 mL, where pH = pKa ≈ 7.4. Window 6.4–8.4: the only choice outside it is the answer."},"exp":"The equivalence point is at 20 mL, so **at 10 mL, halfway there, pH = pKa ≈ 7.4**. By Henderson–Hasselbalch, pH = pKa + log([B⁻]/[HB]); a tenfold ratio either way means **pH = pKa ± 1, i.e. about 6.4–8.4**. pH 6.6, 7.4 and 8.1 fall inside; at **pH 8.7**, [B⁻]/[HB] ≈ 10^1.3 ≈ 20 : 1, so the buffer is not useful.","how":{"ask":"Find the pKa from the curve, set the useful window, and pick the pH outside it.","know":"• At the **half-equivalence point, pH = pKa**.\n• **Henderson–Hasselbalch**: pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]).\n• Ratio between 1 : 10 and 10 : 1 ↔ **pH within pKa ± 1**.","steps":[{"text":"Read the equivalence volume and halve it.","calc":["steep rise at 20 mL → half-equivalence at 10 mL","pH at 10 mL ≈ **7.4** = pKa"]},{"text":"Turn the tenfold rule into a pH window.","hl":"neither form outnumbers the other by more than tenfold","calc":["pKa ± 1 → 6.4 to 8.4"]},{"text":"The pKa itself is the best buffering pH.","strike":["pH 7.4"]},{"text":"These lie inside the window.","strike":["pH 6.6","pH 8.1"]},{"text":"Answer: pH 8.7."}],"trap":"Reading the pKa at the equivalence point instead of halfway to it: the steep jump marks complete neutralization, not equal acid and base."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"titrated with NaOH","note":"read pKa at half-equivalence"},{"t":"by more than tenfold","note":"ratio limit → pKa ± 1"}]},{"text":"**Henderson–Hasselbalch at the edges.**","calc":["[B⁻]/[HB] = 10 → pH = pKa + 1","[B⁻]/[HB] = 0.1 → pH = pKa − 1"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a buffer is effective within about one pH unit of its pKa; check each pH against pKa ± 1."}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Titration of weak acid HB with NaOH","x":{"label":"NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":25,"step":5},"y":{"label":"pH","min":2,"max":13,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"HB + NaOH","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,4.2],[2,6.45],[5,6.92],[10,7.4],[15,7.88],[18,8.35],[19,8.68],[19.8,9.7],[20,10.4],[20.5,11.6],[22,12.2],[25,12.5]]}]}},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-41a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:41#1","pe4-cp:41#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:41"],"passage":"Liver carboxylesterases help clear ester-containing drugs from the body. An esterase cleaves ethyl acetate, shown, through a covalent acyl-enzyme intermediate formed by an active-site serine. A nearby histidine first takes the serine O–H proton, making its oxygen strongly nucleophilic. Ethanol leaves when the intermediate forms, and acetate is set free later when water attacks.","q":"Which substrate atom does the serine oxygen attack?","choices":["Carbonyl carbon","Carbonyl oxygen","Bridging ester oxygen","Ethyl CH₂ carbon"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The carbonyl O is δ− and electron-rich, so a nucleophile won't attack it; it takes the electrons as the C=O opens."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The bridging O is electron-rich too; it is the leaving group, departing with the ethyl group as ethanol."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Attack at the ethyl CH₂ would put ethyl, not acetyl, on the enzyme; an acyl-enzyme needs the new bond at the acyl group."}],"rule":"A nucleophile bonds to the most δ+ atom, the one pulled on by two oxygens; oxygens are electron-rich and never the target.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Nucleophiles go for the δ+ atom. In an ester that's the C=O carbon, tugged by two oxygens at once; O atoms are lone-pair donors and an ethyl CH₂ is not electrophilic. Carbonyl carbon."},"exp":"The serine oxygen is a **nucleophile**, so it bonds to the most **electrophilic** atom: the **carbonyl carbon**, made δ+ by two oxygens. Attack gives a tetrahedral intermediate; the C–O bond to the ethoxy group then breaks, ethanol leaves, and the acetyl group sits on the enzyme as an **acyl-enzyme** (Ser–O–C(=O)CH₃), later hydrolyzed by water.","how":{"ask":"The attacking oxygen has lone pairs; which atom of the ester is electron-poor enough to accept them, and which bond must break to leave acetyl on the enzyme?","know":"• A nucleophile (Ser O, Cys S, His N, Asp O) attacks an **electrophilic** atom.\n• In an ester R–C(=O)–O–R′, the **carbonyl carbon** is bonded to two O atoms and is strongly δ+.\n• Oxygens are electron-rich: a nucleophile does not attack them.\n• Attack at the carbonyl C gives a tetrahedral intermediate; the alkoxy group then leaves and the **acyl group stays on the enzyme**.\n• In a phosphate ester the same role falls to **P**, bonded to four O atoms.","steps":[{"text":"The serine oxygen is the nucleophile in this step.","hl":"serine oxygen attack","fig":"serine-sc"},{"text":"Find the electron-poor atom: the carbonyl carbon, pulled on by two oxygens.","calc":["C=O carbon bonded to 2 O → δ+ → the electrophile","Ser–OH + CH₃C(=O)OCH₂CH₃ → Ser–O–C(=O)CH₃ + HOCH₂CH₃"]},{"text":"Oxygen atoms are electron-rich, so the nucleophile attacks neither the carbonyl nor the bridging oxygen; the bridging O leaves with the ethyl group.","strike":["Carbonyl oxygen","Bridging ester oxygen"]},{"text":"Attack at the ethyl CH₂ would break the CH₂–O bond and put ethyl, not acetyl, on the enzyme; an acyl-enzyme needs the new bond at the acyl carbon.","strike":["Ethyl CH₂ carbon"]},{"text":"Answer: the carbonyl carbon."}],"trap":"Picking the bridging oxygen because the bond that breaks is the acyl C–O bond: the nucleophile forms its new bond to the carbonyl C, and the bridging O leaves with the ethyl group."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"acyl-enzyme intermediate","note":"acyl group bonded to the enzyme"},{"t":"serine","note":"hydroxyl O nucleophile"},{"t":"ethyl acetate","note":"an ester"}]},{"text":"**Nucleophile meets electrophile.** Lone pairs go to the δ+ atom; in an ester, that is the carbonyl carbon."},{"text":"**Follow the acyl group.** The Ser O–C bond forms as the C–O(ethyl) bond breaks, so the acetyl group is transferred to the enzyme.","calc":["E–Ser–OH + CH₃CO–OEt → E–Ser–O–COCH₃ + EtOH","E–Ser–O–COCH₃ + H₂O → E–Ser–OH + CH₃COOH"]},{"text":"**Also:** a phosphatase does the same at phosphorus: an Asp O or His N attacks the δ+ P of a phosphate ester, the alcohol leaves, and a phospho-enzyme forms (phospho-Asp or phosphohistidine).","fig":"histidine-sc"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the enzyme nucleophile bonds to the δ+ center, never to an oxygen: the carbonyl carbon of an ester (giving an acyl-enzyme) or the phosphorus of a phosphate ester (giving a phospho-enzyme)."}],"fig":"ethyl-acetate"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-42a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:42#1","pe4-cp:42#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:42"],"passage":"Gastric organoids, small models of the stomach lining, are grown in an acidified medium. The medium is held at pH 4.0 to mimic the conditions near the stomach wall. The lab's software records hydronium concentrations in micromolar units. Pure water at the same temperature has [H₃O⁺] = 0.1 μM.","q":"Which entry is correct for the medium's [H₃O⁺], and how does it compare with the neutral value of pure water?","choices":["100 μM; above neutral","4.0 μM; above neutral","100 μM; below neutral","0.0001 μM; above neutral"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"10⁻⁴ is the concentration in mol/L; written as μM it must be multiplied by 10⁶."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"A pH below 7 means more hydronium than pure water, not less."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"That copies the pH value as a concentration; pH is a negative logarithm, not an amount."}],"rule":"[H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH in M; multiply by 10⁶ for μM. Lower pH = more hydronium than pure water (0.1 μM at pH 7).","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"pH 4.0 → 10⁻⁴ M. M → μM is × 10⁶: 10⁻⁴ × 10⁶ = 10² μM. pH below 7, so above the neutral 0.1 μM."},"exp":"[H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH = **10⁻⁴ M**. Converting to micromolar multiplies by 10⁶: 10⁻⁴ × 10⁶ = **100 μM**. Pure water (pH 7) holds 0.1 μM hydronium (10⁻⁷ M), so the medium has **a thousand times more** hydronium: it is more acidic than water.","how":{"ask":"Convert the pH to [H₃O⁺] in M, then to μM, and compare with pure water.","know":"• **[H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH** (in M).\n• **μM = 10⁻⁶ M**, so M → μM: × 10⁶.\n• Pure water: pH 7, [H₃O⁺] = 10⁻⁷ M = 0.1 μM.","steps":[{"text":"Turn the pH into a molar concentration.","hl":"held at pH 4.0","calc":["[H₃O⁺] = 10⁻⁴ M"]},{"text":"Convert to micromolar.","calc":["10⁻⁴ M × 10⁶ μM/M = **100 μM**"]},{"text":"Leaving the value in M, or using the pH number itself, gives the wrong entry.","strike":["0.0001 μM; above neutral","4.0 μM; above neutral"]},{"text":"pH 4 is more acidic than water, so its hydronium is higher.","hl":"[H₃O⁺] = 0.1 μM","strike":["100 μM; below neutral"]},{"text":"Answer: 100 μM; above neutral."}],"trap":"Changing the unit label without changing the number: 10⁻⁴ M is 100 μM, not 10⁻⁴ μM."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"pH 4.0","note":"[H₃O⁺] = 10⁻⁴ M"},{"t":"micromolar units","note":"× 10⁶"},{"t":"Pure water","note":"0.1 μM"}]},{"text":"**Prefix ladder:** M → mM (× 10³) → μM (× 10⁶) → nM (× 10⁹)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** [H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M; to express it in μM multiply by 10⁶, and remember each pH unit lower means ten times more hydronium."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-43a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:43#1","pe4-cp:43#2","pe4-cp:43#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:43"],"passage":"A bacterial hydrolase was assayed at two pH values with the same enzyme concentration, 5.0 nM. Fitting the rate data gave: pH 6, kcat 300 s⁻¹, KM 50 μM; pH 8, kcat 600 s⁻¹, KM 200 μM. The rate curves calculated from these constants are shown in the chart.","q":"How do kcat/KM and Vmax change as the pH rises?","choices":["kcat/KM halves; Vmax doubles","kcat/KM doubles; Vmax doubles","kcat/KM halves; Vmax is unchanged","kcat/KM quadruples; Vmax halves"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: kcat doubled but KM quadrupled, so kcat/KM falls; it can't double."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Both parts flipped: efficiency falls because KM grew more than kcat, and Vmax = kcat[E] rises with kcat."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Vmax = kcat[E]; with [E] fixed and kcat doubled, Vmax can't stay the same."}],"rule":"Divide each pH's kcat by its KM and compare the two ratios. Vmax = kcat × [E], so at fixed [E] it moves with kcat.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Ratios: pH 6 gives 300/50 = 6; pH 8 gives 600/200 = 3, so kcat/KM halves. Same 5 nM enzyme, so Vmax follows kcat: 300 → 600 doubles."},"exp":"**Catalytic efficiency = kcat/KM**: 300/50 = 6 μM⁻¹s⁻¹ at pH 6 and 600/200 = 3 μM⁻¹s⁻¹ at pH 8, so it **halves**. **Vmax = kcat[E]total** with the same enzyme amount: 1.5 μM/s rises to 3.0 μM/s, so it **doubles**. A faster kcat does not mean higher efficiency when KM grows even more.","how":{"ask":"Compute kcat/KM at each pH, then Vmax = kcat × [E]total at each pH.","know":"• **Catalytic efficiency = kcat/KM** (units μM⁻¹s⁻¹ or M⁻¹s⁻¹); higher means better at low [S].\n• **Vmax = kcat[E]total**: with the same enzyme concentration, Vmax tracks kcat.\n• KM is the [S] at half Vmax; a larger KM means weaker apparent binding.\n• Read a table row by row: compute each quantity at each condition, then compare.","steps":[{"text":"Pull the values for each pH.","hl":"pH 6, kcat 300 s⁻¹, KM 50 μM; pH 8, kcat 600 s⁻¹, KM 200 μM","calc":["[E]total = 5.0 nM = 0.0050 μM"]},{"text":"Catalytic efficiency at each pH.","calc":["pH 6: 300 s⁻¹ ÷ 50 μM = **6 μM⁻¹s⁻¹**","pH 8: 600 s⁻¹ ÷ 200 μM = **3 μM⁻¹s⁻¹**","6 → 3: **halves**"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH 6","x":25,"text":"pH 6 faster at low [S]","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"Vmax at each pH.","calc":["pH 6: 300 s⁻¹ × 0.0050 μM = **1.5 μM/s**","pH 8: 600 s⁻¹ × 0.0050 μM = **3.0 μM/s**","1.5 → 3.0: **doubles**"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH 8","x":600,"text":"pH 8: still rising toward 3.0","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"Efficiency falls, so it does not double or quadruple; Vmax follows kcat, so it neither stays the same nor halves.","strike":["kcat/KM doubles; Vmax doubles","kcat/KM halves; Vmax is unchanged","kcat/KM quadruples; Vmax halves"]},{"text":"Answer: kcat/KM halves; Vmax doubles."}],"trap":"Judging efficiency from kcat alone: kcat doubled, but KM quadrupled, so kcat/KM fell."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"5.0 nM","note":"[E]total, same at both pH"},{"t":"kcat 300 s⁻¹","note":"pH 6 turnover"},{"t":"KM 50 μM","note":"pH 6"},{"t":"kcat 600 s⁻¹","note":"pH 8 turnover"},{"t":"KM 200 μM","note":"pH 8"}]},{"text":"**Efficiency is a ratio.** kcat/KM, so look at how both numbers change.","calc":["kcat × 2, KM × 4 → kcat/KM × 2/4 = × ½"]},{"text":"**Also:** Vmax = kcat[E]total, so at a fixed enzyme concentration Vmax changes by the same factor as kcat.","calc":["kcat × 2 → Vmax × 2"]},{"text":"**Also:** for a pH table, compute kcat/KM and Vmax in each row and state the direction of change as the pH rises; the two can move in opposite directions."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** catalytic efficiency = kcat/KM and Vmax = kcat[E]total; compute each at every condition in the table before comparing."}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Initial rate vs [S] at 5.0 nM enzyme, from each pH's kcat and KM","x":{"label":"[S] (μM)","min":0,"max":600,"step":100},"y":{"label":"v₀ (μM/s)","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"pH 6","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[10,0.25],[25,0.5],[50,0.75],[100,1],[200,1.2],[300,1.286],[400,1.333],[600,1.385]]},{"name":"pH 8","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[10,0.143],[25,0.333],[50,0.6],[100,1],[200,1.5],[300,1.8],[400,2],[600,2.25]]}]}},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-44a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:44#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:44"],"q":"A 6.0 g cork floats on a pond, partly submerged, pushing aside 6.0 cm³ of water (1 g/cm³). Which property of water mainly holds the cork up?","choices":["Buoyancy","Surface tension","Viscosity","Capillary action"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Surface tension can hold only a few milligrams on a dimpled surface; this object sank in until it displaced a lot of water."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Viscosity resists flow and motion through a fluid; it supplies no upward force on an object at rest."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Capillary action lifts water up narrow tubes by adhesion; it does not hold a floating object up."}],"rule":"Compare the weight of displaced water with the object's weight: if they match, Archimedes' upward force does the job; if almost nothing is displaced, surface tension does.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Displaced mass = 6.0 cm³ × 1 g/cm³ = 6.0 g, equal to the cork's mass: the buoyant force alone balances its weight."},"exp":"The displaced water has mass 6.0 cm³ × 1 g/cm³ = **6.0 g**, the same as the cork. By Archimedes' principle the upward **buoyant force equals the weight of the displaced water**, so buoyancy alone balances the cork's weight. Surface tension matters only for very light objects, such as a water strider, whose legs dimple the surface while displacing almost no water.","how":{"ask":"Decide which property of water supplies the upward force, using the displaced volume as the clue.","know":"• **Buoyancy**: F_b = ρ_water × V_displaced × g (Archimedes).\n• **Surface tension**: cohesion among surface water molecules; supports only tiny weights on a dimpled surface.\n• Viscosity resists flow; capillary action lifts water in narrow tubes.","steps":[{"text":"Weigh the displaced water.","hl":"pushing aside 6.0 cm³ of water","calc":["m = 6.0 cm³ × 1 g/cm³ = 6.0 g"]},{"text":"Compare with the cork.","hl":"A 6.0 g cork","calc":["6.0 g displaced = 6.0 g cork → buoyant force = weight"]},{"text":"Surface tension supports only tiny weights that displace almost nothing; viscosity and capillarity give no upward support.","strike":["Surface tension","Viscosity","Capillary action"]},{"text":"Answer: Buoyancy."}],"trap":"Seeing \"floats on water\" and thinking surface tension: check how much water the object displaces first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"partly submerged","note":"it sank in and displaced water"},{"t":"6.0 cm³","note":"displaced volume"},{"t":"1 g/cm³","note":"turns volume into mass"}]},{"text":"**Contrast:** a 10 mg insect whose legs displace a fraction of a cubic millimetre is held up by **surface tension**, the cohesion between water molecules at the surface; buoyancy there is far too small."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** displaced weight equal to the object's weight → buoyancy; displaced weight negligible and the surface dimpled → surface tension."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-45a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:45#1","pe4-cp:45#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:45"],"q":"Fluorine-18 has a half-life of 110 min. A PET tracer dose made at 8:00 a.m. contains 12 mg of ¹⁸F at 3:20 p.m. the same day. How much ¹⁸F did it contain when made?","choices":["0.75 mg","48 mg","192 mg","384 mg"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Dividing by 16 runs the decay forward; going back in time, the amount must be larger, not smaller."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Multiplying by the number of half-lives treats decay as linear; each half-life halves the amount, so use 2ⁿ."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"That doubles once too often; 8:00 a.m. to 3:20 p.m. is 440 min, exactly four half-lives, not five."}],"rule":"n = elapsed time ÷ half-life; original = remaining × 2ⁿ. Count the elapsed time carefully across a.m./p.m.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"8:00 → 3:20 is 7 h 20 min = 440 min; 440 ÷ 110 = 4 half-lives. Original = 12 mg × 2⁴ = 12 × 16."},"exp":"From 8:00 a.m. to 3:20 p.m. is **440 min**, and 440 ÷ 110 = **4 half-lives**. Each half-life halves the amount, so the original was the remaining amount doubled four times: 12 mg × 2⁴ = **192 mg**.","how":{"ask":"Count the half-lives between the two times, then double the remaining amount that many times.","know":"• **n = t ÷ t½**.\n• Remaining = original ÷ 2ⁿ, so **original = remaining × 2ⁿ**.","steps":[{"text":"Elapsed time.","hl":"made at 8:00 a.m. contains 12 mg of ¹⁸F at 3:20 p.m.","calc":["8:00 a.m. → 3:20 p.m. = 7 h 20 min = 440 min"]},{"text":"Number of half-lives.","hl":"half-life of 110 min","calc":["n = 440 ÷ 110 = 4"]},{"text":"Work back in time.","calc":["12 mg × 2⁴ = 12 × 16 = **192 mg**"]},{"text":"Dividing goes the wrong way in time; ×4 is linear decay; ×32 counts five half-lives.","strike":["0.75 mg","48 mg","384 mg"]},{"text":"Answer: 192 mg."}],"trap":"Multiplying by n instead of 2ⁿ: radioactive decay halves the amount each period; it does not lose a fixed amount."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"half-life of 110 min","note":"t½"},{"t":"8:00 a.m.","note":"start"},{"t":"3:20 p.m.","note":"end: 440 min later"},{"t":"contains 12 mg","note":"what is left"}]},{"text":"**Doubling ladder back in time:** 12 → 24 → 48 → 96 → 192 mg (four steps)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** original = remaining × 2^(t/t½); always check that the answer is larger than what remains."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-46a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:46#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:46"],"q":"Viewed from the side in white light, a 7 mm pencil standing in a glass of water (n = 1.33) looks broken at the water line, with no colored fringes. What causes this?","choices":["Refraction","Diffraction","Dispersion","Polarization"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Diffraction shows only for objects near the light's wavelength; a 7 mm pencil is about 10⁴ times bigger than visible λ."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Dispersion spreads white light into colors, but the stem says the image shows no colored fringes."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Polarization concerns the orientation of the light's field; it does not bend or shift an image."}],"rule":"An image bent at a surface between two media comes from light changing speed; colored fringes mean dispersion, wavelength-sized gaps mean diffraction.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Image bent where light crosses a boundary, no color: refraction (light slows in water). Color fringes → dispersion; slit-sized openings → diffraction; filters/glare → polarization."},"exp":"Light from the underwater part of the pencil **changes speed as it crosses the water–air surface** (v = c/n, slower in water) and bends away from the normal as it exits, so that part appears shifted. Bending at a boundary between two media is **refraction**, described by Snell's law, n₁sinθ₁ = n₂sinθ₂.","how":{"ask":"Is the light crossing into a new medium, or bending around an edge close to its wavelength in size?","know":"• **Refraction**: light changes speed at a boundary between media (v = c/n) and bends: n₁sinθ₁ = n₂sinθ₂.\n• **Diffraction**: bending around an edge or through a gap, strong only when its size is near the wavelength (visible λ ≈ 0.4–0.7 μm).\n• **Dispersion**: n varies with λ, so white light spreads into colors.\n• **Interference**: overlapping waves add (bright) or cancel (dark), making fringes.","steps":[{"text":"The light leaving the underwater pencil crosses from water into air, a change of medium, so its speed and direction change.","hl":"looks broken at the water line","calc":["v in water = c/n = 3.0 × 10⁸ ÷ 1.33 ≈ 2.3 × 10⁸ m/s","faster in air → bends away from the normal at the surface"]},{"text":"Compare the pencil with the wavelength: diffraction is noticeable only for objects near λ in size.","hl":"7 mm pencil","calc":["visible λ ≈ 0.5 μm","7 mm ÷ 0.5 μm = 14,000 wavelengths → diffraction negligible"],"strike":["Diffraction"]},{"text":"Dispersion would fringe the image with colors, which the stem rules out; polarization changes the field's orientation, not the light's direction.","hl":"no colored fringes","strike":["Dispersion","Polarization"]},{"text":"Answer: refraction."}],"trap":"Choosing diffraction because the light bends: bending around an edge needs an object near the wavelength, while bending at a boundary between media is refraction."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"glass of water (n = 1.33)","note":"a second medium"},{"t":"7 mm pencil","note":"far larger than λ"},{"t":"broken at the water line","note":"image shifted at the boundary"},{"t":"no colored fringes","note":"not dispersion"}]},{"text":"**New medium → speed change → bending.** That is refraction, with Snell's law n₁sinθ₁ = n₂sinθ₂."},{"text":"**Size versus wavelength.** Diffraction matters only when an object or gap is near λ: bright and dark rings around 1 μm specks in green laser light are diffraction, not refraction.","calc":["7 mm ≫ 0.5 μm → refraction dominates","1 μm ≈ 2 × 0.532 μm → diffraction rings"]},{"text":"**Also:** bright and dark fringes form by interference: waves in step add (bright), and waves half a wavelength out of step cancel (dark)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** bending where light enters a new medium is refraction (Snell's law); bending around an obstacle near λ in size is diffraction; colors split because n varies with λ is dispersion."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-47a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:47#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:47"],"q":"Saturated fatty acids of molar mass 144, 172, 312 and 340 g/mol are compared. Which dissolves best in water at room temperature?","choices":["C₇H₁₅COOH","C₉H₁₉COOH","C₁₉H₃₉COOH","C₂₁H₄₃COOH"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"The longest tail is the most hydrophobic, so it is the least soluble of the four, the opposite of what is asked."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"A twenty-carbon chain is almost all nonpolar surface, so it ranks near the bottom for water solubility."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Its chain is two carbons longer than another choice's, so it is more hydrophobic and less soluble."}],"rule":"All saturated fatty acids share a single polar –COOH group; the shorter the hydrocarbon tail (smaller molar mass), the more water-soluble it is.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Same head group, so solubility tracks tail length: pick the smallest molar mass, which has the fewest CH₂ groups."},"exp":"Each acid has the same polar **–COOH head**; they differ only in the length of the **nonpolar tail**. The longer the tail, the more hydrophobic the molecule and the less it dissolves. The 144 g/mol acid, **C₇H₁₅COOH** (octanoic acid, eight carbons), has the shortest tail and so the greatest water solubility.","how":{"ask":"Rank the acids by hydrocarbon chain length and pick the shortest.","know":"• Saturated fatty acid: CH₃(CH₂)ₙCOOH, one polar head.\n• Each extra CH₂ adds nonpolar surface → lower water solubility.\n• Molar mass rises with chain length.","steps":[{"text":"Match each formula to its chain length.","calc":["C₇H₁₅COOH: 8 C, 144 g/mol","C₉H₁₉COOH: 10 C, 172 g/mol","C₁₉H₃₉COOH: 20 C, 312 g/mol","C₂₁H₄₃COOH: 22 C, 340 g/mol"]},{"text":"The long chains are the most hydrophobic.","strike":["C₂₁H₄₃COOH","C₁₉H₃₉COOH"]},{"text":"Ten carbons is longer than eight.","strike":["C₉H₁₉COOH"]},{"text":"Answer: C₇H₁₅COOH."}],"trap":"Thinking a bigger molecule has more polar contacts: the head group is the same in all four, so extra size is all nonpolar."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Saturated fatty acids","note":"same head, different tails"},{"t":"dissolves best in water","note":"least hydrophobic"}]},{"text":"**Head vs tail.** The –COOH head H-bonds with water; the tail does not.","fig":"palmitic-acid"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** among molecules with the same polar group, the one with the shortest hydrocarbon chain is the most water-soluble."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-48a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:48#1","pe4-cp:48#2","pe4-cp:51#1","pe4-cp:51#2","pe4-cp:51#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:48"],"passage":"Spontaneous point mutations can arise when a DNA base briefly adopts a rare tautomer during replication. Guanine's tautomers differ only in where one hydrogen sits: on ring N1 or on O6, and on the exocyclic 2-amino N or on a ring N. A rare guanine tautomer presents a different pattern of H-bond donors and acceptors and can pair with thymine. Such mispairs, if not repaired, become G→A mutations after the next round of replication.","q":"Which form of guanine is dominant in DNA under physiological conditions and makes the normal Watson–Crick pair?","choices":["Keto–amino form","Enol–amino form","Keto–imino form","Enol–imino form"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Moving the N1 hydrogen onto O6 gives a rare enol that pairs with thymine; it is a minor form at pH 7.4."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Shifting an amino H onto a ring N gives a rare imino form, which also mispairs."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Two rare shifts at once give an even rarer form; it is not what sits in DNA."}],"rule":"Under physiological conditions G, T and U sit as keto (C=O, ring N–H) and A, C as amino (–NH₂). Enol and imino tautomers are rare and cause mispairing.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Default forms: C=O stays C=O (keto), –NH₂ stays –NH₂ (amino). Any choice with \"enol\" or \"imino\" is a rare, mutagenic form."},"exp":"At physiological pH, guanine exists almost entirely as the **keto–amino** tautomer: a C6=O, an N1–H and a 2-NH₂ group. That face (acceptor, donor, donor) pairs with cytosine through **three hydrogen bonds**. The **enol** (O6–H) and **imino** (=NH) tautomers are rare; each swaps a donor for an acceptor, so the base mispairs, typically with thymine.","how":{"ask":"Pick the tautomer that dominates at physiological pH and gives standard pairing.","know":"• G, T and U are mainly **keto** (lactam: C=O with ring N–H); A and C are mainly **amino**.\n• **Enol** (C–OH) and **imino** (C=NH) forms are rare and change the donor/acceptor pattern → mispairing.\n• G·C: 3 H-bonds; A·T: 2.","steps":[{"text":"The passage says the rare tautomers are the ones that mispair.","hl":"A rare guanine tautomer presents a different pattern of H-bond donors and acceptors"},{"text":"The normal form keeps C6=O and the 2-NH₂ group.","calc":["keto at O6, amino at C2 → G·C with 3 H-bonds"]},{"text":"Any enol or imino shift is a rare, mutagenic form.","strike":["Enol–amino form","Keto–imino form","Enol–imino form"]},{"text":"Answer: Keto–amino form."}],"trap":"Thinking the enol is favored because it is \"aromatic-looking\": in nucleobases the keto (lactam) form dominates."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"rare tautomer","note":"minor forms mutate"},{"t":"on ring N1 or on O6","note":"keto vs enol"},{"t":"can pair with thymine","note":"mispairing"}]},{"text":"**Keto wins.** G, T and U sit mainly as keto (C=O, ring N–H); A and C as amino.","fig":"guanine"},{"text":"**Count the H-bonds pair by pair.** An H on N or O facing a lone-pair N or O is one hydrogen bond: keto G with C gives 3; A with T gives 2.","fig":"cytosine"},{"text":"**Also:** a rare enol tautomer mispairs. Enol guanine has O6–H (donor) and N1 (acceptor) in place of C6=O and N1–H, so its face changes and the H-bond count in a drawn pair changes with it."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** G, T and U are mainly keto at physiological pH and A, C mainly amino; rare enol or imino tautomers mispair."}],"fig":"guanine"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-49a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:49#1","pe4-cp:49#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:49"],"passage":"Carbon-14 (Z = 6) is used to label drug molecules in metabolism studies. A ¹⁴C nucleus undergoes β⁻ decay, giving off a β⁻ particle along with an antineutrino, with a half-life of about 5700 years. Because the label decays so slowly, nearly all labeled molecules stay intact during a study. Now and then, though, a labeled atom decays, and the molecule then holds a different element at that position.","q":"When a ¹⁴C atom decays, what element takes its place, and how do its proton count Z and neutron count N change?","choices":["Nitrogen; Z +1, N −1","Boron; Z −1, N +1","Nitrogen; A rises by 1","Carbon-13; Z same, N −1"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"β⁻ decay turns a neutron into a proton, so Z goes up, not down; losing a proton is β⁺ decay or electron capture."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"β⁻ decay keeps the mass number the same; the new proton replaces a neutron rather than adding a nucleon."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Losing a neutron with Z unchanged is neutron emission; β⁻ decay changes the element."}],"rule":"β⁻: n → p + e⁻ + antineutrino. Z rises by 1, N falls by 1, A is unchanged, so the daughter is the next element up.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"β⁻ = one neutron becomes a proton: Z 6 → 7 (nitrogen), A stays 14, so N goes 8 → 7."},"exp":"In **β⁻ decay** a neutron becomes a proton, emitting an electron (β⁻) and an antineutrino. **Z rises from 6 to 7**, so carbon becomes **nitrogen**; the mass number stays **14**, so the neutron count falls from 8 to 7: ¹⁴C → ¹⁴N + β⁻ + antineutrino.","how":{"ask":"Apply β⁻ decay to Z and A, then name the daughter and compare neutron counts.","know":"• **β⁻**: Z + 1, A unchanged (n → p).\n• **β⁺** / electron capture: Z − 1, A unchanged.\n• **α**: Z − 2, A − 4.\n• N = A − Z.","steps":[{"text":"Start with the parent.","hl":"Carbon-14 (Z = 6)","calc":["A = 14, Z = 6, N = 8"]},{"text":"β⁻ turns one neutron into a proton.","hl":"giving off a β⁻ particle","calc":["Z = 7 (nitrogen), A = 14, N = 7"]},{"text":"Z falls only in β⁺ decay; A cannot change, and the element must change.","strike":["Boron; Z −1, N +1","Nitrogen; A rises by 1","Carbon-13; Z same, N −1"]},{"text":"Answer: Nitrogen; Z +1, N −1."}],"trap":"Thinking emitting an electron lowers Z: the electron is created when a neutron becomes a proton, so the nucleus gains positive charge."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Z = 6","note":"carbon"},{"t":"β⁻ particle","note":"n → p"},{"t":"a different element","note":"Z changes"}]},{"text":"**Balance the decay.**","calc":["¹⁴₆C → ¹⁴₇N + ⁰₋₁e + antineutrino","top: 14 = 14 + 0; bottom: 6 = 7 − 1"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in β⁻ decay a neutron turns into a proton: Z + 1 and N − 1 with A unchanged, giving the next element in the periodic table."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-50a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:50#1","pe4-cp:50#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:50"],"passage":"Many esterases cleave their substrates with a Ser–His–Asp catalytic triad. An esterase's active-site aspartate uses its negatively charged side chain to hold a protonated histidine in place. That histidine, in turn, pulls the proton off the serine so the serine oxygen can attack the ester. Point mutants of the aspartate were made, and their activities compared with the wild type.","q":"Which aspartate mutation most likely preserves activity?","choices":["Asp → Glu","Asp → Ala","Asp → Lys","Asp → Phe"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Alanine's side chain is an uncharged methyl, so the ion pair with the protonated His is lost."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Lysine is positive at pH 7; it would repel the protonated His, not hold it."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Phe is nonpolar and uncharged, so the ion pair is lost."}],"rule":"To keep an ion pair, keep the charge: replace an acidic residue with another acidic one, never a neutral or basic one.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Keep the charge to keep the ion pair. Asp and Glu are the two acidic (–COO⁻) residues, so Asp → Glu. Ala and Phe drop the charge; Lys reverses it."},"exp":"The job depends on the **negative carboxylate** pairing with the positive histidine. Glutamate keeps a –COO⁻, one CH₂ longer, so it is a **conservative substitution**. Alanine and phenylalanine are nonpolar and carry no charge, and lysine is positive, so it would repel the histidine.","how":{"ask":"What chemical feature of the aspartate side chain does the job, and which replacement keeps that feature?","know":"• **Conservative substitution**: replace a residue with one of similar chemistry (charge, polarity, size), usually keeping function.\n• At pH 7: Asp and Glu are −1 (carboxylates); Lys and Arg are +1; Ala and Phe are nonpolar and neutral.\n• Asp and Glu differ only by one CH₂ in the side chain.","steps":[{"text":"The key feature is the negative charge.","hl":"negatively charged side chain","fig":"aspartate"},{"text":"Glutamate also ends in a carboxylate, so the ion pair survives.","hl":"negatively charged side chain","fig":"glutamate"},{"text":"Alanine and phenylalanine have no charge, so the ion pair is lost.","strike":["Asp → Ala","Asp → Phe"]},{"text":"Lysine is positive at pH 7 and would repel the protonated histidine.","strike":["Asp → Lys"]},{"text":"Answer: Asp → Glu."}],"trap":"Choosing by size alone: the carboxylate charge, not the side-chain length, carries the function."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"negatively charged side chain","note":"the carboxylate"},{"t":"protonated histidine","note":"positive partner"},{"t":"preserves activity","note":"conservative swap"}]},{"text":"**Name the job.** An Asp that pairs with a positive group needs a –COO⁻."},{"text":"**Keep the chemistry.** Glu is the only standard residue besides Asp with a side-chain carboxylate.","calc":["Asp: –CH₂–COO⁻","Glu: –CH₂–CH₂–COO⁻"]},{"text":"**Also:** swapping Asp for Ala, Lys or Phe loses the negative charge (Lys even reverses it), so activity is lost."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to keep an Asp's function, swap it for Glu, since both carry a carboxylate; Ala, Lys or Phe lose the negative charge."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-51a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:51#1","pe4-cp:51#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:51"],"passage":"The binding site of a restriction enzyme was studied with a synthetic DNA duplex 80 base pairs long. Base analysis showed that adenine accounts for 15% of the duplex's bases. Every base is paired by standard Watson–Crick rules, with no mismatches or overhangs. The duplex melts only above 80 °C, consistent with its high GC content.","q":"What is the total number of base-pairing hydrogen bonds in the duplex?","choices":["184","216","228","432"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"This gives A·T three H-bonds and G·C two; the counts are the other way around."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Taking 15% of the 80 pairs skips the step that the percentage is of all 160 bases."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Counting each base separately skips that two bases share each pair's H-bonds, doubling the total."}],"rule":"Turn base % into pairs: A = T and G = C, bases = 2 × pairs. Then H-bonds = 2 × (A·T pairs) + 3 × (G·C pairs).","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"160 bases × 15% = 24 A = 24 A·T pairs; 80 − 24 = 56 G·C pairs. 24 × 2 + 56 × 3 = 48 + 168."},"exp":"The duplex has 80 pairs = **160 bases**. Adenine is 15% of them: 0.15 × 160 = **24 A**, so there are **24 A·T pairs** and 80 − 24 = **56 G·C pairs**. A·T pairs have 2 H-bonds and G·C pairs 3: 24 × 2 + 56 × 3 = 48 + 168 = **216**.","how":{"ask":"Convert the base composition into numbers of A·T and G·C pairs, then add up their hydrogen bonds.","know":"• Chargaff: A = T and G = C in a duplex.\n• Total bases = 2 × base pairs.\n• **A·T: 2 H-bonds; G·C: 3 H-bonds.**","steps":[{"text":"Count all the bases.","hl":"80 base pairs long","calc":["80 pairs × 2 = 160 bases"]},{"text":"Turn the adenine share into pairs.","hl":"adenine accounts for 15% of the duplex's bases","calc":["A = 0.15 × 160 = 24 → 24 A·T pairs","G·C pairs = 80 − 24 = 56"]},{"text":"Add the H-bonds.","calc":["24 × 2 + 56 × 3 = 48 + 168 = **216**"]},{"text":"Swapping 2 and 3, taking 15% of pairs, or counting per base all miss.","strike":["184","228","432"]},{"text":"Answer: 216."}],"trap":"Applying the percentage to base pairs instead of bases: \"15% of the bases\" means 15% of 160, not of 80."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"80 base pairs","note":"160 bases"},{"t":"15% of the duplex's bases","note":"A count"},{"t":"standard Watson–Crick rules","note":"A·T 2, G·C 3"}]},{"text":"**Why 2 and 3.** In a drawn pair, each H on N or O facing a lone-pair N or O is one H-bond: A·T has two such contacts, G·C three."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** bases → pairs with Chargaff's rule, then H-bonds = 2(A·T) + 3(G·C)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-52a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:52#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:52"],"passage":"Many nucleases cut DNA only when a divalent (2+) metal ion sits in the active site, where it holds a water molecule and steadies the negative charge that builds up on the phosphate. In the lab, the natural metal can often be swapped for another 2+ ion. Four metals were considered as substitutes. Their valence configurations are Ni, [Ar]3d⁸4s²; Sr, [Kr]5s²; Cd, [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²; and Rb, [Kr]5s¹.","q":"Which metal could NOT readily supply the required ion, and why?","choices":["Sr, ion too large","Ni, d⁸ unstable","Cd, full 4d","Rb, 5s¹ only"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Losing its two 5s electrons gives a stable 2+ ion with a noble-gas core; size does not stop it from forming."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"It loses its two 4s electrons easily to form a common, stable 2+ ion; partly filled d subshells are normal for transition metals."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A full d subshell is stable; the two 5s electrons come off to give a common 2+ ion."}],"rule":"Count the valence electrons that come off easily: s² metals and most transition metals form 2+; an s¹ metal forms only 1+ (a second electron would come from the core).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Look at the outer s subshell: s² → 2+ is easy; s¹ (an alkali metal) gives only 1+."},"exp":"Ni, Sr and Cd each have **two outer s electrons** that come off easily, giving common **2+ ions**. Rb has a single 5s electron: removing it gives a stable noble-gas core, and a second electron would have to come from that core at a huge energy cost. So **Rb forms only Rb⁺** and cannot supply a 2+ ion.","how":{"ask":"For each metal, decide whether it readily forms a 2+ ion, from its valence configuration.","know":"• Group 1 (s¹): forms only **1+**.\n• Group 2 (s²): forms **2+**.\n• Transition metals lose their outer s electrons first, so 2+ is common (Ni²⁺, Cd²⁺, Zn²⁺, Fe²⁺).","steps":[{"text":"Read each outer subshell.","hl":"Rb, [Kr]5s¹","calc":["Ni: 4s² → Ni²⁺","Sr: 5s² → Sr²⁺","Cd: 5s² → Cd²⁺","Rb: 5s¹ → Rb⁺ only"]},{"text":"These three each lose two s electrons easily; size, d⁸ and a full d subshell do not block a 2+ ion.","strike":["Sr, ion too large","Ni, d⁸ unstable","Cd, full 4d"]},{"text":"Answer: Rb, 5s¹ only."}],"trap":"Rejecting a transition metal because of its d electrons: the d count does not stop a metal from losing its two outer s electrons."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"divalent (2+) metal ion","note":"need a 2+ ion"},{"t":"Rb, [Kr]5s¹","note":"one valence electron"}]},{"text":"**Why not Rb²⁺:** the second ionization energy of an alkali metal is enormous, because it would break into a filled shell."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a metal can stand in for a divalent ion only if it readily forms 2+; alkali metals (s¹, like Na, K, Rb) form only 1+."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-53a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:53#1","pe4-cp:53#2","pe4-cp:53#3"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:53"],"passage":"A pulsed-power bench in a teaching lab stores energy in a capacitor and dumps it into a coil in microseconds. An 8.0 μF capacitor holds 7.0 kV when the bench is armed. To give students a feel for that energy, the instructor compares it with lifting a 20 kg crate against gravity (g = 9.8 m/s²).","q":"How high must the crate rise to match the capacitor's stored energy?","choices":["1.0 m","2.0 m","1.0 × 10⁶ m","1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Reads μF as F: a small capacitor can't lift a crate 1000 km; C must be in farads (× 10⁻⁶)."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Reads kV as V: using 7.0 V instead of 7000 V shrinks V² by 10⁶."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Dropped the ½: CV² alone is twice the stored energy, so the height comes out doubled."}],"rule":"U = ½CV² with C in F and V in V, then h = U ÷ (mg). Round to 1 digit × a power of 10 and handle the exponents separately.","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"U = ½CV² = 4 × 10⁻⁶ × 49 × 10⁶ = 4 × 49 = 196 J (the powers cancel). mg = 20 × 9.8 = 196 N. h = 196 ÷ 196 = 1.0 m."},"exp":"**Set the capacitor's energy equal to mgh.** U = ½CV² = ½ × 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ F × (7.0 × 10³ V)² = 196 J. Then h = U/(mg) = 196 J ÷ (20 kg × 9.8 m/s²) = 196 ÷ 196 = **1.0 m**.","how":{"ask":"Find the capacitor's stored energy in joules, then find the height that gives the crate the same potential energy.","know":"• **Capacitor energy: U = ½CV²** (C in farads, V in volts).\n• **Gravitational potential energy: U = mgh**, so h = U/(mg).\n• Prefixes: **μF = 10⁻⁶ F**, **kV = 10³ V**.","steps":[{"text":"Convert both prefixed values to SI units first.","hl":"8.0 μF capacitor holds 7.0 kV","calc":["C = 8.0 μF = 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ F","V = 7.0 kV = 7.0 × 10³ V"]},{"text":"Compute the stored energy with U = ½CV².","calc":["V² = (7.0 × 10³)² = 4.9 × 10⁷ V²","U = ½ × 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 4.9 × 10⁷ = **196 J**"]},{"text":"Set U = mgh and solve for h.","hl":"20 kg crate","calc":["mg = 20 kg × 9.8 m/s² = 196 N","h = U/(mg) = 196 J ÷ 196 N = **1.0 m**"]},{"text":"2.0 m drops the ½ (CV² = 392 J). 1.0 × 10⁶ m leaves C in μF as if it were farads. 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m leaves V in kV as if it were volts.","strike":["2.0 m","1.0 × 10⁶ m","1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.0 m."}],"trap":"Skipping a prefix: a missed 10⁻⁶ or a missed (10³)² moves the answer by a factor of a million."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"8.0 μF","note":"capacitance C"},{"t":"7.0 kV","note":"voltage V"},{"t":"20 kg","note":"mass m"},{"t":"g = 9.8 m/s²","note":"gravity"}]},{"text":"**Convert the prefixes.** Micro is 10⁻⁶ and kilo is 10³.","calc":["8.0 μF = 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ F","7.0 kV = 7.0 × 10³ V"]},{"text":"**Capacitor energy:** U = ½CV². Square the voltage, exponent included.","calc":["U = ½ × 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 4.9 × 10⁷ = 196 J"]},{"text":"**Equal energies:** set ½CV² = mgh and solve for h.","calc":["h = ½CV²/(mg)","h = 196 ÷ (20 × 9.8) = 1.0 m"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to match a capacitor's energy with a lifted mass, convert to F and V, compute U = ½CV², then h = U/(mg)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-54a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:54#1","pe4-cp:54#2","pe4-cp:54#3","pe6-cp:40#1","pe6-cp:40#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:54"],"passage":"Racing drones run on lightweight lithium-polymer packs. A 3.7 V drone battery is rated at 5.0 A·h, meaning the current it can supply multiplied by the hours it lasts. A pilot wants to know how much charge passes through the motors on one full discharge. The motors draw a varying current during a flight.","q":"What total charge flows through the battery from full to empty?","choices":["5.0 C","300 C","1.8 × 10⁴ C","6.7 × 10⁴ C"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"5.0 is in A·h, not C; an hour is thousands of seconds, so Q must be thousands of coulombs."},{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Uses 60 s per hour; an hour is 3600 s, sixty times more."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"A·h × V gives an energy (W·h, or J), not a charge; the battery voltage isn't needed."}],"rule":"Q = I × t with t in seconds: 1 A·h = 3600 C. Round the current and 3600 to 1 digit; ignore the voltage.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"A·h becomes C by × 3600 s/h: 5 × 3600 = 18,000 = 1.8 × 10⁴ C. Voltage is not in the charge; it's a distractor."},"exp":"Amp-hours measure **charge, since Q = I·t**. 1 A·h = 1 A × 3600 s = 3600 C, so 5.0 A·h × 3600 C/(A·h) = **1.8 × 10⁴ C**. The 3.7 V is not needed; multiplying by it gives energy in joules.","how":{"ask":"Convert the amp-hour rating to coulombs.","know":"• **Q = I·t** (C = A × s).\n• **1 A·h = 1 A × 3600 s = 3600 C**.\n• Voltage × charge = energy (J), not charge.","steps":[{"text":"Amp-hours multiply current by time, which gives charge.","hl":"rated at 5.0 A·h","calc":["Q = I·t → A·h is a charge unit"]},{"text":"Convert hours to seconds and multiply.","calc":["1 h = 3600 s","Q = 5.0 A × 3600 s","Q = 18 000 C = **1.8 × 10⁴ C**"]},{"text":"The voltage plays no part in the charge.","hl":"3.7 V","calc":["3.7 V × 1.8 × 10⁴ C ≈ 6.7 × 10⁴ J: energy, not charge"]},{"text":"6.7 × 10⁴ C multiplies by the voltage, 300 C uses 60 s per hour, and 5.0 C skips the time conversion.","strike":["6.7 × 10⁴ C","300 C","5.0 C"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.8 × 10⁴ C."}],"trap":"Multiplying by the voltage: that gives energy in joules, not charge in coulombs."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers** and what they are.","marks":[{"t":"3.7 V","note":"voltage: not needed"},{"t":"5.0 A·h","note":"charge rating"},{"t":"What total charge","note":"answer in coulombs"}]},{"text":"**Charge = current × time.** An amp-hour already is a charge.","calc":["Q = I·t"]},{"text":"**Also:** convert with 1 A·h = 3600 C.","calc":["1 A·h = 1 A × 3600 s = 3600 C","5.0 A·h × 3600 C = 1.8 × 10⁴ C"]},{"text":"**Also:** the voltage doesn't matter for charge; it only enters if you want energy (E = QV)."},{"text":"**Also:** when an electrode material is rated per gram, multiply by its mass in grams (mg × 10⁻³) to get the capacity, then convert to coulombs: 1 mAh = 10⁻³ A × 3600 s = 3.6 C.","calc":["150 mAh/g × 20 mg = 150 × 0.020 g = 3.0 mAh","3.0 mAh × 3.6 C/mAh ≈ 11 C"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Q = I·t, and 1 A·h = 3600 C; ignore the voltage when the question asks for charge."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-55a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:55#1","pe4-cp:55#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:55"],"passage":"A physics kit uses a stretched rubber band to launch a toy cart along a level track. In one trial, a 75 g toy cart speeds up from 15 cm/s to 95 cm/s in 0.40 s while the band pushes it. Friction on the track is negligible during the push. A motion sensor records the speeds.","q":"Find the mean net force on the cart during the push:","choices":["0.15 N","0.18 N","15 N","150 N"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Leaves the mass in grams; 75 g is 0.075 kg, a factor of 1000 smaller."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Leaves the speeds in cm/s; convert to m/s (÷ 100) first."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Uses the final speed instead of the change; subtract the starting speed from the final one first."}],"rule":"F = m·Δv ÷ Δt with m in kg and v in m/s. Convert grams first, then round every number to 1 digit.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Find a first: Δv = 80 cm/s = 0.80 m/s, ÷ 0.40 s = 2 m/s². Then F = 0.075 kg × 2 = 0.15 N."},"exp":"**F = mΔv/Δt** in SI units: m = 0.075 kg, Δv = 0.95 − 0.15 = 0.80 m/s. F = 0.075 kg × 0.80 m/s ÷ 0.40 s = **0.15 N**.","how":{"ask":"Convert to kg and m/s, then divide the momentum change by the time.","know":"• **F = ma = mΔv/Δt** (N = kg·m/s²).\n• **1 g = 10⁻³ kg; 1 cm/s = 10⁻² m/s**: convert before multiplying.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the mass and speeds to SI.","hl":"75 g","calc":["m = 75 g = **0.075 kg**","15 cm/s = 0.15 m/s; 95 cm/s = 0.95 m/s"]},{"text":"Find the change in velocity.","hl":"from 15 cm/s to 95 cm/s","calc":["Δv = 0.95 − 0.15 = **0.80 m/s**"]},{"text":"Apply F = mΔv/Δt.","hl":"0.40 s","calc":["F = 0.075 kg × 0.80 m/s ÷ 0.40 s","F = 0.060 ÷ 0.40 = **0.15 N**"]},{"text":"0.18 N uses the final speed instead of the change; 15 N leaves speeds in cm/s; 150 N leaves mass in grams.","strike":["0.18 N","15 N","150 N"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.15 N."}],"trap":"Skipping a unit conversion: grams or cm/s each make the force off by a power of ten."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and its unit.","marks":[{"t":"75 g","note":"mass in grams"},{"t":"15 cm/s","note":"initial speed"},{"t":"95 cm/s","note":"final speed"},{"t":"0.40 s","note":"time interval"}]},{"text":"**Average force = mass × velocity change ÷ time.**","calc":["F = mΔv/Δt"]},{"text":"**Also:** convert to SI first: g → kg (÷ 1000) and cm/s → m/s (÷ 100).","calc":["75 g = 0.075 kg","Δv = 80 cm/s = 0.80 m/s"]},{"text":"**Compute.**","calc":["F = 0.075 × 0.80 ÷ 0.40 = 0.15 N"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** F = mΔv/Δt with mass in kg and speeds in m/s."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-56a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:56#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:56"],"passage":"Sensitive electronics are often housed in metal boxes to shield them from outside electric fields. In a test, a solid aluminum block sits in a uniform field of 3.0 × 10³ N/C. Its free electrons drift for a moment and then settle. Once its charges stop moving, a probe finds no electric field anywhere inside the metal.","q":"At equilibrium, the field inside it is zero because:","choices":["mobile electrons rearrange.","the metal absorbs it.","its electrons are all bound.","the block has no net charge."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Metals don't absorb static fields; the field inside is cancelled, not soaked up."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Aluminum is a metal, so it has free electrons; they are not all bound."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A neutral block still has free charges that separate; zero net charge doesn't cancel an outside field by itself."}],"rule":"Inside a conductor at equilibrium E = 0: free charges shift to its surfaces until their own field cancels the applied one.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Conductor in equilibrium: E inside = 0 always, because its free electrons slide to the surfaces until their field cancels the outside one. Mobile electrons rearrange."},"exp":"A metal's **free electrons** drift in the outside field and pile up on one face, leaving the other face positive. They keep moving until **their field exactly cancels** the outside one inside the metal: **electrostatic equilibrium**, E = 0 inside.","how":{"ask":"Explain what the free electrons do until they stop moving.","know":"• A conductor has **free electrons** that move whenever a field acts on them.\n• They pile up on the **surface**, and the field they make opposes the applied one.\n• **Electrostatic equilibrium**: charges stopped, so E inside = 0 and any excess charge sits on the surface.","steps":[{"text":"The outside field pushes the block's free electrons toward one face.","hl":"uniform field of 3.0 × 10³ N/C","calc":["electrons → one face (−)","other face left (+)"]},{"text":"Those surface charges make a field opposite to the applied one; motion stops only when the two cancel inside.","hl":"Once its charges stop moving","calc":["E(inside) = E(applied) − E(surface charges)","stop only when E(inside) = 0"]},{"text":"Metals don't absorb fields, aluminum has free electrons, and a neutral block still has free charges that separate.","strike":["the metal absorbs it.","its electrons are all bound.","the block has no net charge."]},{"text":"Answer: mobile electrons rearrange, and their surface charge cancels the field inside."}],"trap":"Saying the metal blocks or absorbs the field: the zero is the result of induced surface charges cancelling it."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"solid aluminum block","note":"a conductor: free electrons"},{"t":"Once its charges stop moving","note":"electrostatic equilibrium"},{"t":"field inside it is zero","note":"explain why"}]},{"text":"**Free electrons move in a field.** They drift until they reach the surface and pile up there."},{"text":"**Their field opposes the outside field.** If any field were left inside, electrons would keep moving.","calc":["E inside ≠ 0 → electrons move","electrons stop → E inside = 0"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a conductor at electrostatic equilibrium, free electrons move to the surface until their field cancels any field inside, so E = 0 there."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-57a","s":"Fluids","disc":"biology","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:57#1"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:57"],"q":"Blood pressure in a clinic patient reads 144/96 mmHg, with a heart rate of 64 beats/min. Using MAP ≈ P_d + ⅓(P_s − P_d), with P_s systolic and P_d diastolic pressure, what is the mean arterial pressure?","choices":["96 mmHg","112 mmHg","120 mmHg","128 mmHg"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"That starts from systolic and subtracts a third; the formula starts from the lower, diastolic value and adds."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"A plain average of the two pressures skips the given formula, which weights diastole more heavily."},{"c":0,"tag":"copied","why":"That copies the diastolic pressure and leaves out the added third of the pulse pressure."}],"rule":"Plug the numbers into the formula given: diastolic is the lower number. Heart rate is a distractor unless the formula uses it.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Pulse pressure 144 − 96 = 48; a third of it is 16; MAP = 96 + 16. The heart rate is not needed."},"exp":"Systolic P_s = 144 mmHg and diastolic P_d = 96 mmHg (the lower number). Pulse pressure = 144 − 96 = 48 mmHg; a third of it is 16 mmHg. MAP ≈ 96 + 16 = **112 mmHg**. The heart rate does not enter the formula.","how":{"ask":"Identify systolic and diastolic, then substitute into the given formula.","know":"• A blood pressure is written systolic/diastolic: the first (higher) number is systolic.\n• MAP sits closer to diastolic because the heart spends longer in diastole.\n• Use only the quantities the formula asks for.","steps":[{"text":"Read the two pressures.","hl":"144/96 mmHg","calc":["P_s = 144, P_d = 96"]},{"text":"Apply the formula.","hl":"MAP ≈ P_d + ⅓(P_s − P_d)","calc":["⅓(144 − 96) = 16","MAP ≈ 96 + 16 = **112 mmHg**"]},{"text":"Starting from systolic reverses the formula, and the plain average or the diastolic value alone skip it.","strike":["128 mmHg","120 mmHg","96 mmHg"]},{"text":"Answer: 112 mmHg."}],"trap":"Using the heart rate or a simple average: the question supplies the formula, so plug in exactly what it asks for."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"144/96 mmHg","note":"systolic/diastolic"},{"t":"64 beats/min","note":"not in the formula"},{"t":"MAP ≈ P_d + ⅓(P_s − P_d)","note":"the formula given"}]},{"text":"**Same formula, other form:** P_d + ⅓(P_s − P_d) = (P_s + 2P_d)/3.","calc":["(144 + 2 × 96)/3 = 336/3 = 112"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** with a formula given, identify each symbol (diastolic = the lower number), substitute, and ignore data the formula does not use."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-58a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"biology","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:58#1","pe4-cp:58#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:58"],"q":"A neuron's voltage at its axon hillock is graphed over a single spike. What chiefly drives the voltage change at 1.8 ms?","choices":["Na⁺ flows in","K⁺ flows out","Sodium pump action","Ca²⁺ flows in"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"K⁺ leaving drives the falling phase after the peak; at this time the voltage is still climbing."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The pump is slow and keeps the resting gradients; it does not cause the rapid swing of a spike."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Ca²⁺ enters at the axon terminal, later, to trigger transmitter release, not at the hillock during the upstroke."}],"rule":"Locate the time on the trace: rising toward the peak = Na⁺ in; falling after the peak = K⁺ out; dip below rest = K⁺ channels still open.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Find 1.8 ms on the trace: between threshold (about 1.6 ms) and the peak (about 2.1 ms), the steep upstroke, which is Na⁺ rushing in."},"exp":"At **1.8 ms** the trace is in its steep **rising phase**, between threshold (≈ −55 mV at 1.6 ms) and the peak (≈ +35 mV at 2.1 ms). Depolarization opens **voltage-gated Na⁺ channels**, and Na⁺ flows in down its gradient, driving the potential up. K⁺ efflux causes the later fall, the pump only restores gradients slowly, and Ca²⁺ entry happens at the terminal.","how":{"ask":"Find where 1.8 ms falls on the trace (rising, falling or undershoot) and match that phase to its ion movement.","know":"• **Threshold** (≈ −55 mV) at the hillock → voltage-gated **Na⁺ channels open: Na⁺ in** (depolarization).\n• Then voltage-gated **K⁺ channels: K⁺ out** (repolarization) and an undershoot (hyperpolarization).\n• Ca²⁺ influx and transmitter release come later, at the terminal.","steps":[{"text":"Place 1.8 ms on the trace.","hl":"at 1.8 ms","calc":["1.6 ms ≈ −55 mV (threshold) → 2.1 ms ≈ +35 mV (peak)","1.8 ms: steep rise"]},{"text":"A rising potential means positive charge entering: Na⁺."},{"text":"K⁺ out is the falling phase, the pump is too slow, and Ca²⁺ enters at the terminal.","strike":["K⁺ flows out","Sodium pump action","Ca²⁺ flows in"]},{"text":"Answer: Na⁺ flows in."}],"trap":"Reading the time as after the peak: check whether the trace is still climbing at that time before choosing K⁺."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"axon hillock","note":"where the spike starts"},{"t":"single spike","note":"one action potential"},{"t":"at 1.8 ms","note":"read the phase here"}]},{"text":"**Order of events:** graded potentials reach threshold at the hillock → Na⁺ channels open, Na⁺ in → K⁺ channels open, K⁺ out → hyperpolarization → rest."},{"text":"**Also:** Ca²⁺ influx and neurotransmitter release come last, at the axon terminal."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on an action-potential trace, upstroke = Na⁺ in, downstroke = K⁺ out, undershoot = K⁺ channels slow to close."}],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Membrane potential at the axon hillock (sketch)","x":{"label":"Time (ms)","min":0,"max":6,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Membrane potential (mV)","min":-100,"max":40,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Vm","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,-70],[1,-70],[1.6,-55],[2.1,35],[2.7,-40],[3.2,-82],[4.5,-73],[6,-70]]}]}},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-59a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:59#1","pe4-cp:59#2"],"twinOf":["pe4-cp:59"],"q":"A 300 W heater warms 0.50 kg of ethanol (c = 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C)) from 18 °C to 68 °C. If no heat is lost, how long does this take?","choices":["0.20 s","72 s","200 s","272 s"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"Divides kJ by W without converting to J; warming half a kilogram in a fraction of a second is absurd."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Uses 68 °C as ΔT; subtract the starting temperature to get the change."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"Uses the starting reading, 18 °C, as if it were ΔT; neither reading alone is the change."}],"rule":"t = m·c·ΔT ÷ P, with c in J (kJ × 1000) and ΔT = final − start. Round each factor to 1 digit before multiplying.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"t = mcΔT ÷ P. ΔT = 50; 0.50 × 50 = 25; 25 × 2400 J = 60,000 J; 60,000 ÷ 300 = 200 s."},"exp":"**q = mcΔT** = 0.50 kg × 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C) × 50 °C = 60 kJ = 6.0 × 10⁴ J. **t = q/P** = 6.0 × 10⁴ J ÷ 300 J/s = **200 s**.","how":{"ask":"Find the heat the ethanol absorbs, convert it to joules, then divide by the heater's power.","know":"**q = mcΔT**, with m in the unit c uses (kJ/(kg·°C) takes kg; J/(g·°C) takes g) and **ΔT = T_final − T_initial**. **P = q/t**, so **t = q/P**; 1 W = 1 J/s and 1 kJ = 1000 J.","steps":[{"text":"Note the values; c is per kilogram, so the mass stays in kg.","hl":"0.50 kg of ethanol (c = 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C)) from 18 °C to 68 °C"},{"text":"Use the temperature change, not either reading: ΔT = 18 gives 72 s and ΔT = 68 gives 272 s.","calc":["ΔT = 68 − 18 = **50 °C**"],"strike":["72 s","272 s"]},{"text":"Compute the heat.","calc":["q = m c ΔT","q = 0.50 × 2.4 × 50","q = **60 kJ** = 6.0 × 10⁴ J"]},{"text":"Divide by the power, in joules per second.","hl":"300 W","calc":["t = q ÷ P","t = 60 000 J ÷ 300 J/s","t = **200 s**"]},{"text":"Dividing 60 kJ by 300 W without changing kJ to J gives a time 1000 times too short.","strike":["0.20 s"]},{"text":"Answer: 200 s."}],"trap":"Mixing kJ with W (J/s): 60 ÷ 300 = 0.20 s. Convert kJ to J first. Using 18 or 68 as ΔT gives 72 s or 272 s."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"300 W","note":"power: 300 J each second"},{"t":"0.50 kg","note":"mass"},{"t":"c = 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C)","note":"per kg, so keep kg"},{"t":"from 18 °C to 68 °C","note":"start → end"}]},{"text":"**Match the units:** c is in kJ per kg per °C, so the mass goes in as 0.50 kg; no gram conversion.","calc":["ΔT = 68 − 18 = 50 °C"]},{"text":"**Heat needed: q = mcΔT.**","calc":["q = 0.50 × 2.4 × 50","0.50 × 2.4 = 1.2","1.2 × 50 = **60 kJ**"]},{"text":"**Time from power: t = q/P.** Power is in W = J/s, so change kJ to J first.","calc":["60 kJ = 60 000 J","t = 60 000 ÷ 300 = **200 s**"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** q = mcΔT with the mass in the unit c uses, then t = q ÷ P with q in joules (1 W = 1 J/s)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-01a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:1#1","pe5-cp:1#2","pe5-cp:1#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:1"],"passage":"A lab is testing compound F, an antifungal agent with a molar mass of 312 g/mol, against a yeast enzyme. F is a solid that dissolves fully in the assay buffer, which has a density of 1.02 g/mL. For each run, the team prepares 250.0 mL of a 40 μM solution of F. The solution is then diluted into wells that hold the enzyme.","q":"About what mass of F must be weighed out for one run?","choices":["3.1 mg","31 mg","0.31 mg","3.1 g"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Reads μM as mM: the mass comes out a thousand times too large."},{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Turns 250.0 mL into 2.5 L, a factor-of-ten slip in the volume."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Turns 250.0 mL into 0.025 L, a factor-of-ten slip the other way."}],"rule":"Mass to weigh = M × V(L) × molar mass. Convert μM to M (× 10⁻⁶) and mL to L (× 10⁻³) first; the density is a distractor.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Moles first: 40 μM × 0.25 L = 10 μmol. Then 10 μmol × 312 g/mol ≈ 3100 μg, which is about 3 mg."},"exp":"Moles = M × V = (40 × 10⁻⁶ mol/L)(0.2500 L) = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol. Mass = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol × 312 g/mol ≈ 3.1 × 10⁻³ g, so **about 3.1 mg**. The buffer density is not needed.","how":{"ask":"How many moles of F does the solution hold, and what mass is that?","know":"• **mol = M × V**, with V in litres.\n• **μM = 10⁻⁶ M**, **mL = 10⁻³ L**.\n• **mass = mol × molar mass**; 1 g = 1000 mg.\n• A solvent density matters only when a mass of solution must be turned into a volume.","steps":[{"text":"Pull the concentration, volume and molar mass from the passage.","hl":"250.0 mL of a 40 μM solution","calc":["M = 40 × 10⁻⁶ mol/L","V = 0.2500 L","molar mass = 312 g/mol"]},{"text":"Moles, then grams, then milligrams.","calc":["n = (40 × 10⁻⁶)(0.2500) = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol","m = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵ × 312 ≈ 3.1 × 10⁻³ g ≈ 3.1 mg"]},{"text":"Reading μM as mM gives a mass a thousand times too big.","strike":["3.1 g"]},{"text":"A slip in the mL → L step moves the answer by ten either way.","strike":["31 mg","0.31 mg"]},{"text":"Answer: about 3.1 mg."}],"trap":"Using the 1.02 g/mL density: it describes the buffer, not F, and plays no part in mol = M × V."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"molar mass of 312 g/mol","note":"grams per mole of F"},{"t":"density of 1.02 g/mL","note":"not needed"},{"t":"250.0 mL of a 40 μM solution","note":"V and M"}]},{"text":"**Units first.** Put every quantity in mol, L and g before multiplying.","calc":["40 μM = 4.0 × 10⁻⁵ M","250.0 mL = 0.2500 L"]},{"text":"**Chain it.** mol = M × V; mass = mol × molar mass; then g → mg (× 1000)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mass of solute = molarity (M) × volume (L) × molar mass; convert μM and mL first, and ignore a solvent density that the math does not use."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-02a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:2#1","pe5-cp:2#2","pe5-cp:58#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:2"],"passage":"Iron(III) and thiocyanate ions form a blood-red complex: Fe³⁺ + SCN⁻ ⇌ FeSCN²⁺. A student prepares a red solution that sits at equilibrium at 25 °C. Adding a few drops of AgNO₃ precipitates SCN⁻ as solid AgSCN, and the red color fades. The equilibrium constant, measured before and after the addition at 25 °C, is the same.","q":"What best accounts for the color change?","choices":["Less FeSCN²⁺; K unchanged","More FeSCN²⁺; K unchanged","Less FeSCN²⁺; K smaller","No shift; K constant"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: removing a reactant shifts the equilibrium left; more product would need added reactant."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"K changes only with temperature, and the passage says it stayed the same at 25 °C."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"K stays fixed, but Q doesn't: losing SCN⁻ raises Q above K, so the mixture must shift."}],"rule":"A concentration stress shifts the position of an equilibrium (Le Châtelier) while K stays put; only temperature changes K.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Le Châtelier: pulling out a reactant (SCN⁻ as AgSCN) pushes the reaction backward, so the red complex falls apart; the temperature never changed, so K did not either."},"exp":"Ag⁺ removes SCN⁻, a reactant, as solid AgSCN. **Removing a reactant shifts the equilibrium toward reactants** (Le Châtelier's principle), so red FeSCN²⁺ dissociates and the color fades. The temperature is unchanged, so **K is unchanged**: only Q moved.","how":{"ask":"Which way did the equilibrium shift, and did K change?","know":"• **Le Châtelier's principle**: a system at equilibrium shifts to offset a stress.\n• **Removing a reactant** (or adding a product) raises Q above K → shift toward reactants.\n• **Adding a reactant** (or removing a product) lowers Q below K → shift toward products.\n• A precipitating ion acts by lowering the free concentration of the ion it grabs.\n• **K changes only with temperature.**","steps":[{"text":"Find what Ag⁺ removes and which side of the equation it is on.","hl":"precipitates SCN⁻ as solid AgSCN","calc":["Fe³⁺ + SCN⁻ ⇌ FeSCN²⁺ (red)","Ag⁺ + SCN⁻ → AgSCN(s): free [SCN⁻] drops"]},{"text":"A reactant was removed, so Q rises above K and the reverse reaction runs: FeSCN²⁺ falls apart and the red fades.","calc":["Q = [FeSCN²⁺]/([Fe³⁺][SCN⁻])","[SCN⁻] ↓ → Q > K → shift left"],"strike":["More FeSCN²⁺; K unchanged"]},{"text":"The temperature never changed, so K is the same, as the passage measured; and the mixture did shift.","hl":"is the same","strike":["Less FeSCN²⁺; K smaller","No shift; K constant"]},{"text":"Answer: less FeSCN²⁺, with K unchanged."}],"trap":"Thinking K must drop because less product is present: a stress moves the concentrations, not the constant."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"sits at equilibrium","note":"Q = K before the stress"},{"t":"precipitates SCN⁻","note":"a reactant is removed"},{"t":"is the same","note":"K did not change"}]},{"text":"**Stress and response.** Removing a reactant makes Q > K, so the reverse reaction runs until Q = K again; the constant itself never moves at fixed temperature.","calc":["SCN⁻ removed → Q > K → shift left","FeSCN²⁺ (red) ↓ → color fades"]},{"text":"**Name the principle.** This is Le Châtelier's principle. Other named principles describe electrons, not equilibria: the Pauli exclusion principle (no two electrons in an atom share all four quantum numbers) and the uncertainty principle (position and momentum cannot both be known exactly)."},{"text":"**Also:** removing a product shifts right. In an esterification, RCOOH + R′OH ⇌ RCOOR′ + H₂O, pulling water out makes Q < K and **raises the ester yield**; adding a reactant does the same.","calc":["remove H₂O → Q falls below K","→ shift right → more ester"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** adding or removing a reactant or product shifts an equilibrium to offset the change (Le Châtelier's principle), while K stays constant unless the temperature changes."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-03a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:3#1","pe5-cp:3#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:3"],"passage":"Compound K, shown, blocks a fungal enzyme but is only slightly soluble in water, which caps its dose. Chemists made four analogs, each differing from K by a single change. Analog P replaces the CH₃ with CH₂CH₃, and Analog Q replaces the –OH with –H. Analog R adds an –OH to the ring opposite the side chain, and Analog S adds a –Br at that same ring position. Solubilities will be compared in pH 7 buffer.","q":"Which analog is most likely more soluble in water than compound K?","fig":"1-phenylethanol","choices":["Analog P","Analog Q","Analog R","Analog S"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"An extra CH₂ adds nonpolar surface and no polar group, so water solubility drops."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Swapping –OH for –H removes the only group that H-bonds to water, so solubility falls."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A ring Br adds bulk and no H-bond donor or acceptor, so the molecule gets greasier."}],"rule":"For water solubility, add a group that H-bonds to water (–OH, ring N, –NH₂, an ionizable acid); adding C–H, CH₃ or halogens, or removing a polar group, lowers it.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Scan each change for a new H-bonding group. Only one change adds an O–H that water can bond to; every other change adds grease or removes the only polar group."},"exp":"Water dissolves molecules it can **hydrogen bond** to. Analog R adds a phenol **–OH**, a new H-bond donor and acceptor, so it should be more soluble. Analog P adds a CH₂, Analog S a Br, and Analog Q loses the only –OH: all are **less** soluble.","how":{"ask":"Which single change gives water a new place to hydrogen bond, without adding nonpolar bulk?","know":"• **Raise solubility**: add –OH, –NH₂, a ring N (CH → N), or a group that ionizes at the pH.\n• **Lower solubility**: add CH₂/CH₃ or halogens, or swap a polar group for C–H or an ether.\n• Compare each analog with the lead, one change at a time.","steps":[{"text":"The lead has one polar group, the –OH on the side chain; the rest is a benzene ring and C–H.","hl":"only slightly soluble in water"},{"text":"Sort each change: polar group added, or nonpolar group added or polar group lost.","calc":["P: + CH₂ → less polar","Q: –OH → –H → loses H-bonding","R: + ring –OH → more H-bonding","S: + ring Br → less polar"]},{"text":"Adding a CH₂ or a Br adds grease with no new H-bonding.","strike":["Analog P","Analog S"]},{"text":"Losing the –OH removes the one group water binds.","strike":["Analog Q"]},{"text":"Answer: Analog R, the one that adds an –OH."}],"trap":"Picking the bromo analog because a C–Br bond is polar: a halogen adds a dipole but cannot hydrogen bond, so it does not help water dissolve the molecule."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"only slightly soluble in water","note":"goal: raise solubility"},{"t":"a single change","note":"compare each analog with K"},{"t":"adds an –OH to the ring","note":"new H-bond donor and acceptor"}]},{"text":"**Like dissolves like.** Water dissolves what it can hydrogen bond to; nonpolar C–H surface pushes a molecule out of water."},{"text":"**Also:** the same logic runs other changes: replacing a ring CH with N (benzene → pyridine) adds an H-bond acceptor and raises solubility, while turning –OH into –OCH₃ or adding a methyl lowers it."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an analog is more water-soluble when its change adds a group that hydrogen bonds to water (–OH, N, NH, an ion); added carbons, methyls or halogens lower solubility."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-04a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe5-cp:4#1","pe5-cp:4#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:4"],"passage":"A soil bacterium makes a hydrolase that a sugar-like compound, I, inhibits competitively. Separate assays at fixed T and pH give Ki = 5.0 μM for the inhibitor and an intrinsic kcat/KM of 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹ for the enzyme. In a follow-up trial, the assay [I] is raised from 15 μM to 45 μM while the substrate concentration, temperature and pH stay the same. The measured initial rate falls further at the higher [I].","q":"In the follow-up trial, what are Ki and the intrinsic kcat/KM?","choices":["5.0 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹","15 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹","5.0 μM; 1.3 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹","1.7 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"15 μM is the stem's starting [I], not Ki; a binding constant doesn't take on the assay concentration."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Ki doesn't scale down with [I] either; 1.7 μM is just 5.0 ÷ 3."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Intrinsic kcat/KM belongs to the enzyme at fixed T and pH; more inhibitor slows the observed rate, not this constant."}],"rule":"Ki and intrinsic kcat/KM are constants at fixed T and pH; changing [I] changes the observed rate, never the constants.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Ki and intrinsic kcat/KM are constants of the molecules at set T and pH; they'd be the same at [I] = 0 or 1 M. Changing [I] changes only the observed rate: 5.0 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶."},"exp":"**Ki** (the EI dissociation constant) and **kcat/KM** (the enzyme's catalytic efficiency) are **constants** of the enzyme–inhibitor pair at a fixed temperature and pH. Tripling [I] inhibits more enzyme and slows the observed rate, but both values stay **5.0 μM and 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹**, read straight from the data.","how":{"ask":"Which quantities change when [I] changes, and which are fixed by the enzyme, the inhibitor, T and pH?","know":"• **Ki** = [E][I]/[EI], an equilibrium constant: set by the enzyme, inhibitor, T and pH, not by how much inhibitor is added.\n• **KM, kcat and kcat/KM** are properties of the enzyme with its substrate under fixed conditions.\n• Raising [I] changes the **observed** rate (and the apparent KM or Vmax), not these constants.\n• The apparent kcat/KM measured with a competitive inhibitor present falls as [I] rises; the intrinsic constant does not.","steps":[{"text":"Temperature and pH stay fixed; only the inhibitor concentration changes.","hl":"at fixed T and pH","calc":["[I]: 15 μM → 45 μM (× 3)"]},{"text":"As a dissociation constant, Ki does not scale with [I]: tripling it or dividing it by 3 confuses the constant with the concentration.","strike":["15 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹","1.7 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹"]},{"text":"kcat/KM belongs to the enzyme itself; dividing it by 3 treats the slower observed rate as a new constant.","strike":["5.0 μM; 1.3 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹"]},{"text":"Read both constants straight from the given data: Ki for the inhibitor and the intrinsic kcat/KM for the enzyme.","hl":"Ki = 5.0 μM for the inhibitor"},{"text":"Answer: 5.0 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹."}],"trap":"Scaling a constant with [I] (Ki × 3 = 15 μM): more inhibitor means more inhibition, not a new Ki."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"fixed T and pH","note":"held fixed"},{"t":"Ki = 5.0 μM","note":"a constant"},{"t":"intrinsic kcat/KM","note":"the enzyme's own constant"},{"t":"raised from 15 μM to 45 μM","note":"3× more inhibitor"}]},{"text":"**Constants versus conditions.** A constant is fixed by the identity of the molecules and by T and pH; a concentration is something the experimenter sets.","calc":["Ki = [E][I]/[EI]: more I → more EI, same ratio"]},{"text":"**Read, don't recompute.** When a question asks for a constant under a new [I], copy the value from the data table unchanged."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ki, KM, kcat and kcat/KM are constants of the enzyme–inhibitor pair at fixed T and pH, so raising [I] changes the observed rate but none of these values."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-05a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:5#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:5"],"passage":"Yeast turn several small carbonyl compounds into alcohols that add to the aroma of fermented drinks. An alcohol dehydrogenase reduces butanal, shown, transferring a hydride from NADH and a proton from water to its C=O. No carbon–carbon bond is made or broken. The product is released from the enzyme unchanged.","q":"Under the passage's conditions, which product forms from butanal?","fig":"butanal","choices":["2-Butanol","1-Butanol","Butane","Butanoic acid"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe5-2-butanol","1-butanol","cpt-pe5-butane","cpt-pe5-butanoic-acid"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: NADH reduces, but an acid is an oxidation product."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Carbon 2 never held the oxygen; the hydride adds to the existing C=O carbon, at the chain end."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Removing the O entirely takes 4 e⁻; one hydride plus one H⁺ adds only 2 e⁻."}],"rule":"Hydride + H⁺ on a C=O gives an alcohol on that same carbon: an aldehyde gives a primary alcohol, a ketone a secondary one; the chain and the O position don't move.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"H⁻ + H⁺ added across C=O gives an OH on that same carbon. The aldehyde carbon is the chain end, so the OH stays on C1."},"exp":"The hydride and the proton add **H₂ across the C=O**, so the CHO carbon (C1) becomes CH₂OH: **1-butanol, a primary alcohol**. 2-Butanol would put the O on C2, butane would need 4 e⁻, and butanoic acid is an oxidation.","how":{"ask":"A hydride plus a proton add H₂ across the C=O. Which product keeps the oxygen, now as OH, on the carbon that held it?","know":"• **Reduction** of a carbonyl adds 2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺ (here H⁻ from NADH and H⁺ from water) across C=O; no C–C bond is made or broken.\n• An **aldehyde** carbon has one C neighbor, so it becomes a **primary** alcohol; a **ketone** carbon has two, so it becomes a **secondary** alcohol.\n• Removing the O altogether (to an alkane) takes 2 more electrons; a carboxylic acid is an oxidation.","steps":[{"text":"The C=O is at the chain end, on carbon 1, with an H: an aldehyde.","hl":"reduces butanal"},{"text":"Put the H⁻ on the carbon and the H⁺ on the oxygen; C1 now holds OH, two H and the propyl chain.","hl":"a hydride from NADH and a proton from water","calc":["CH₃CH₂CH₂–CHO + H⁻ + H⁺ → CH₃CH₂CH₂–CH₂OH","C1 oxidation state: **+1 → −1**, a gain of 2 e⁻"]},{"text":"2-Butanol has its OH on carbon 2, which never held the oxygen.","strike":["2-Butanol"]},{"text":"Butane has lost the O (4 e⁻ gained, not 2). Butanoic acid has more bonds to O: an oxidation, not a reduction.","strike":["Butane","Butanoic acid"]},{"text":"Answer: 1-butanol, a primary alcohol."}],"trap":"Thinking any carbonyl reduction gives a secondary alcohol: that holds for ketones; an aldehyde gives a primary alcohol."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"reduces","note":"gain of electrons"},{"t":"butanal","note":"aldehyde at C1"},{"t":"a hydride from NADH and a proton from water","note":"2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺ in all"}]},{"text":"**Count what is added.** A hydride is H with 2 electrons; a proton is H⁺ with none. Together they make H₂: 2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺."},{"text":"**Open one bond of the C=O.** The H⁻ bonds to the carbon and the H⁺ to the oxygen, leaving C–OH on the same carbon."},{"text":"**Also:** a ketone gives a **secondary** alcohol the same way: 2-butanone (C=O on C2, between CH₃ and CH₂CH₃) is reduced to 2-butanol, R–CH(OH)–R.","fig":"cpt-pe5-2-butanone"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** 2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺ on a C=O gives H–C–OH on the same carbon: an aldehyde becomes a primary alcohol and a ketone a secondary alcohol, with no change in the carbon skeleton."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-06a","s":"Metabolism & Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:6#1","pe5-cp:6#2","pe5-cp:6#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:6"],"passage":"A bacterial oxidase binds three different flavin cofactors over its catalytic cycle: riboflavin, FMN and FAD. FMN is riboflavin with a phosphate on its ribityl chain, and FAD is FMN joined to AMP through a second phosphate. In the FAD structure drawn here, atoms W–Z mark four regions of the molecule. Only the part that riboflavin, FMN and FAD all contain accepts the electrons.","q":"Which labeled atom lies in the ring system all three cofactors share?","fig":"cpt-pe5-fad-parts","choices":["W","X","Y","Z"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"X is in adenine, part of the AMP half that only FAD has."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Y is the ribose ring O of the AMP half, another FAD-only piece."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Z is a phosphorus outside any ring, and riboflavin has no phosphate at all."}],"rule":"Riboflavin, FMN and FAD share only the flavin ring (three fused six-rings, four N); phosphates and the AMP part are added on.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The common core of riboflavin, FMN and FAD is the flavin: the three fused rings holding four N. Pick the atom in that tricyclic part, W; phosphate, ribose and adenine are add-ons."},"exp":"All three flavin cofactors are built on the **isoalloxazine ring**: three fused six-membered rings with four N, where W sits. FMN adds a phosphate (Z) to riboflavin, and only FAD adds the AMP part, with its ribose (Y) and **adenine (X)**.","how":{"ask":"Which part of FAD is the flavin, the piece common to all three cofactors, and which labeled atom sits in it?","know":"• **Riboflavin** (vitamin B₂) = isoalloxazine ring + ribityl chain. **FMN** = riboflavin + one phosphate. **FAD** = FMN + AMP (a second phosphate, ribose and adenine).\n• **Isoalloxazine**: three fused six-membered rings in a row holding four N and two C=O; it carries the electrons.\n• Adenine is a purine (a six- fused to a five-membered ring); among the three cofactors only FAD has it.","steps":[{"text":"Find the three fused rings with four ring N and two C=O: the isoalloxazine. W, highlighted, is one of its nitrogens.","hl":"riboflavin, FMN and FAD all contain","fig":"cpt-pe5-fad-flavin"},{"text":"Build the three cofactors by adding pieces.","calc":["riboflavin = isoalloxazine + ribityl chain","FMN = riboflavin + phosphate","FAD = FMN + AMP (phosphate + ribose + adenine)"]},{"text":"X is in adenine and Y is the ring O of ribose: both belong to the AMP half, which only FAD has.","strike":["X","Y"]},{"text":"Z is a phosphorus outside any ring, and riboflavin has no phosphate at all.","strike":["Z"]},{"text":"Answer: W, on the isoalloxazine ring."}],"trap":"Choosing the adenine because it is also a fused, nitrogen-rich ring system; it has only two rings and comes with FAD's AMP part alone."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"FAD","note":"flavin adenine dinucleotide"},{"t":"atoms W–Z mark four regions","note":"one label per part"},{"t":"riboflavin, FMN and FAD all contain","note":"the shared core"}]},{"text":"**Recognize the flavin.** Three six-membered rings fused in a row, four ring N and two C=O: the isoalloxazine ring, the redox part of every flavin cofactor."},{"text":"**Also:** read the name, flavin **adenine** dinucleotide. Adenine (X) and its ribose (Y) arrive only with the AMP that FAD adds to FMN; riboflavin and FMN have no adenine."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** riboflavin, FMN and FAD share the isoalloxazine ring (three fused rings, four N); FMN adds a phosphate, and only FAD adds AMP with its adenine."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-06b","s":"Metabolism & Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:6#4"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:6"],"passage":"A bacterial dehydrogenase can use any of three hydride carriers: NAD⁺, NADP⁺ and nicotinamide mononucleotide (NMN). NMN is a ribose phosphate bearing one pyridinium ring with a –CONH₂ group. NAD⁺ is NMN joined to AMP, and NADP⁺ adds one more phosphate to the AMP part. In each carrier, the hydride is accepted by the ring part that all three share.","q":"Which fragment shown is the shared part that accepts the hydride?","choices":["Flavin ring system","Nicotinamide ring","Adenine","Ubiquinone"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe5-lumiflavin","cpt-pe5-nicotinamide-ring","adenine","cpt-pe5-ubiquinone"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Adenine is in NAD⁺ and NADP⁺ only; NMN has no AMP, and adenine carries no electrons anyway."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Three fused rings with four N belong to FMN and FAD, not to any of these three carriers."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A quinone ring on a long isoprenoid tail is the membrane carrier CoQ, not part of a nucleotide."}],"rule":"For the part several cofactors share, list what each one contains and keep only the piece found in all of them; that is usually the redox-active ring.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"NMN is the smallest of the three, and the other two are built on it. Its only ring is the pyridinium with –CONH₂, so that ring is the shared part."},"exp":"NMN is ribose phosphate + **nicotinamide**; NAD⁺ adds AMP (adenine), and NADP⁺ adds a phosphate. Only the **nicotinamide ring** is in all three, and it accepts the hydride (NAD⁺ → NADH). Adenine is missing from NMN; the flavin ring belongs to FMN/FAD; ubiquinone is a separate membrane carrier.","how":{"ask":"Which piece appears in NMN, NAD⁺ and NADP⁺ alike?","know":"• **Nicotinamide**: a pyridinium ring with –CONH₂; accepts H⁻ (NAD⁺ → NADH).\n• **Flavin** (isoalloxazine): three fused rings, four N; the redox part of riboflavin, FMN and FAD.\n• **Adenine**: two fused rings, five N; in AMP-containing cofactors, carries no electrons.\n• **Ubiquinone (CoQ)**: a quinone ring on a long isoprenoid tail; lipid-soluble, it shuttles electrons in the inner mitochondrial membrane.","steps":[{"text":"List each carrier's parts.","hl":"one pyridinium ring with a –CONH₂ group","calc":["NMN: nicotinamide + ribose + P","NAD⁺: NMN + AMP","NADP⁺: NAD⁺ + P"]},{"text":"Adenine comes with the AMP, which NMN lacks.","strike":["Adenine"]},{"text":"The flavin ring and the quinone are in other carriers entirely.","strike":["Flavin ring system","Ubiquinone"]},{"text":"Answer: the nicotinamide ring."}],"trap":"Picking adenine because NAD⁺ and NADP⁺ both have it: \"shared by all\" must include NMN."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"NAD⁺, NADP⁺ and nicotinamide mononucleotide (NMN)","note":"three carriers"},{"t":"that all three share","note":"common part only"},{"t":"accepted by the ring part","note":"the redox ring"}]},{"text":"**Build-up cofactors.** Many cofactors are a small core plus handles: NMN → NAD⁺ → NADP⁺, and likewise riboflavin → FMN → FAD. The core ring does the chemistry."},{"text":"**Also:** ubiquinone is the odd one out among carriers: a quinone ring with two C=O across from each other, on a long nonpolar tail that lets it move within the inner mitochondrial membrane from complexes I and II to complex III."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the part shared by a family of cofactors is the redox ring of the smallest member (nicotinamide for NMN/NAD⁺/NADP⁺, flavin for riboflavin/FMN/FAD); handles such as adenine are not in every member."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-07a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:7#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:7"],"passage":"Ion binding to a membrane receptor was studied in solution. A binding assay contains 18 mM MgCl₂, 20 mM HEPES buffer and 4.0 μM receptor protein. MgCl₂ dissolves completely into Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻ ions, and the receptor stays a monomer under these conditions.","q":"About how many Cl⁻ ions are present per receptor molecule?","choices":["9000","4500","9","4.5"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Way too small: skips the 1000-fold mM → μM factor and also counts one Cl⁻ per formula."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Divides mM by μM without converting; 1 mM = 1000 μM."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Counts one Cl⁻ per MgCl₂; each formula unit releases two, so multiply by 2."}],"rule":"Ions per molecule = (salt conc × ions per formula) ÷ protein conc, in the same units (1 mM = 1000 μM). Round to 1 digit first.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"MgCl₂ gives 2 Cl⁻: 2 × 18 = 36 mM = 36,000 μM. Per receptor: 36,000 ÷ 4 = 9000. HEPES is irrelevant."},"exp":"Only MgCl₂ supplies Cl⁻, two per formula unit: [Cl⁻] = 2 × 18 mM = 36 mM = 36,000 μM. **Divide in the same units:** 36,000 μM ÷ 4.0 μM = **9000** Cl⁻ per receptor.","how":{"ask":"Find the chloride concentration, put both in the same unit, then divide.","know":"• In one solution, a **particle ratio = concentration ratio**, because the volume cancels.\n• Count only the salt that supplies the ion, times the ions per formula unit (MgCl₂ → 2 Cl⁻).\n• Put both in one unit first: 1 mM = 1000 μM.","steps":[{"text":"Only the magnesium salt contains chloride; the buffer is a distractor.","hl":"18 mM MgCl₂","calc":["[Cl⁻] = 2 × 18 mM = 36 mM"]},{"text":"Convert to the protein's unit.","calc":["36 mM × 1000 μM/mM = 36,000 μM"]},{"text":"Divide.","calc":["36,000 μM ÷ 4.0 μM = **9000**"]},{"text":"4500 counts one Cl⁻ per MgCl₂, 9 divides mM by μM without converting, and 4.5 makes both mistakes.","strike":["4500","9","4.5"]},{"text":"Answer: about 9000 Cl⁻ ions per receptor."}],"trap":"Dividing 36 by 4.0 directly: the two numbers are in different units (mM and μM)."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and its unit.","marks":[{"t":"18 mM MgCl₂","note":"2 Cl⁻ each"},{"t":"20 mM HEPES","note":"no Cl⁻"},{"t":"4.0 μM","note":"different unit"}]},{"text":"**Count the ion.** Each MgCl₂ gives two Cl⁻.","calc":["2 × 18 mM = 36 mM Cl⁻"]},{"text":"**Match the units, then divide.**","calc":["36 mM = 36,000 μM","36,000 ÷ 4.0 = 9000 per receptor"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a ratio of particles in one solution is the ratio of their concentrations in the same unit, counting ions per formula unit."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-08a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:8#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:8"],"passage":"In a marine bacterium, a membrane enzyme passes electrons from NADH to a quinone. Its standard free-energy change is strongly negative, an exergonic step, yet calorimetry shows it releases little heat in intact membranes. In detergent-dissolved enzyme with no sealed membrane, the same transfer warms the solution far more. The bacterium lives in seawater rich in Na⁺.","q":"Where does most of the energy go in intact membranes?","choices":["Pumping Na⁺ outward","Re-forming NADH","Speeding the electrons","Warming the cytoplasm"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: re-forming NADH reverses an exergonic step; it costs energy rather than storing it."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Electrons hop between carriers; their speed isn't where redox energy is stored."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The step releases little heat, so the energy can't be warming the cytoplasm."}],"rule":"Energy from a membrane redox step that isn't lost as heat is stored, usually as an ion gradient pumped across the membrane.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Membrane redox energy not lost as heat is banked as an ion gradient; marine bacteria often pump Na⁺. Pumping Na⁺ outward."},"exp":"ΔG for the transfer is fixed by its start and end states, so the energy cannot simply vanish. The enzyme **couples** the electron transfer to **pumping Na⁺ out against its gradient**, storing the free energy as an ion gradient instead of releasing it as heat.","how":{"ask":"Decide what work an exergonic membrane redox step can do, so that its energy does not come out as heat.","know":"• An exergonic step (ΔG < 0) can drive an uphill one if one enzyme **couples** them.\n• Membrane redox complexes couple electron flow to **moving ions (H⁺ or Na⁺) up their gradient**, storing energy as an electrochemical gradient.\n• Energy that does work is not released as heat.","steps":[{"text":"The step is exergonic, and it happens inside a membrane enzyme.","hl":"a membrane enzyme passes electrons from NADH to a quinone"},{"text":"Little heat means the free energy did work instead; the work a membrane enzyme can do is moving ions across the membrane.","hl":"releases little heat"},{"text":"In many marine bacteria the ion is Na⁺: pumping it out builds a Na⁺ gradient that later drives ATP synthesis or the flagellum."},{"text":"Re-forming NADH would run the step backward and cost energy; warming the cytoplasm is exactly the heat that is missing.","strike":["Re-forming NADH","Warming the cytoplasm"]},{"text":"The electrons end up bound to the quinone; their speed is not a store of energy.","strike":["Speeding the electrons"]},{"text":"Answer: pumping Na⁺ outward."}],"trap":"Looking for an enzyme trick that makes the reaction release less energy; catalysts never change ΔG, so the energy must go into work."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"membrane enzyme","note":"can move ions across"},{"t":"exergonic step","note":"ΔG < 0: energy available"},{"t":"releases little heat","note":"energy went somewhere else"}]},{"text":"**Energy coupling:** an exergonic reaction can drive an uphill process when one enzyme links them; the energy is then stored, not lost as heat."},{"text":"**In membranes, the uphill process is ion pumping.** Mitochondria pump H⁺; many marine bacteria pump Na⁺. Either way the gradient stores the energy for later work."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** when a membrane redox step releases far less heat than its ΔG, the energy was coupled to pumping ions (H⁺ or Na⁺) against their gradient."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-09a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:9#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:9"],"q":"Open tank A, 0.40 m wide, holds water (1.0 g/cm³); open tank B, 4.0 m wide, holds oil (0.80 g/cm³). Both liquids are 1.5 m deep. How does the absolute pressure at the bottom of A compare with that in B?","choices":["Greater in A; denser liquid","Equal; h is the same","Greater in B; more liquid","Greater in B; larger base area"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Same depth is not enough: P = P_atm + ρgh also depends on the liquid's density."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The total volume of liquid does not set the pressure at a point; only ρ, g and depth do."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A wider base spreads more weight over more area; the ratio, the pressure, depends only on ρgh."}],"rule":"Pressure at a depth in an open container: P = P_atm + ρgh. Compare density and depth only; width, shape and volume never matter.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Same depth and same atmosphere, so only ρ differs. Denser liquid gives more pressure at that depth."},"exp":"P = P_atm + ρgh. Both bottoms are 1.5 m down and both tanks are open, so the pressures differ only through ρ: water (1000 kg/m³) beats oil (800 kg/m³), so **P_A > P_B**. Tank width and the amount of liquid do not matter.","how":{"ask":"In P = P_atm + ρgh, which factor differs between the two bottoms?","know":"• **P = P_atm + ρgh** for an open container.\n• Pressure at a depth depends only on ρ, g and h, not on the container's width, shape or volume.\n• Same liquid, same depth → same pressure; same depth, denser liquid → more pressure.","steps":[{"text":"Write P for each bottom.","calc":["P_A = P_atm + (1000)(9.8)(1.5) ≈ P_atm + 14.7 kPa","P_B = P_atm + (800)(9.8)(1.5) ≈ P_atm + 11.8 kPa"]},{"text":"The depths match, but the densities don't, so the pressures can't be equal.","hl":"Both liquids are 1.5 m deep","strike":["Equal; h is the same"]},{"text":"Width and the amount of liquid never enter ρgh.","strike":["Greater in B; more liquid","Greater in B; larger base area"]},{"text":"Answer: greater in A, because water is denser."}],"trap":"Thinking the big tank, with far more liquid, must press harder on its bottom: pressure at a depth ignores the container size."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"water (1.0 g/cm³)","note":"ρ_A"},{"t":"oil (0.80 g/cm³)","note":"ρ_B"},{"t":"Both liquids are 1.5 m deep","note":"same h"}]},{"text":"**Only three things count.** At a point below an open surface, the pressure is P_atm plus ρgh: density, gravity and depth."},{"text":"**Also:** with the same liquid in both tanks, the pressures at equal depth would be exactly equal, however different the widths or volumes."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to compare pressures at points in open containers, compare ρ × depth; ignore the container's width, shape and volume."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-09b","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:9#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:9"],"q":"A wide basin and a thin tube, both open, hold ethanol. How does the pressure 20 cm below the basin's surface compare with that 35 cm below the tube's surface?","choices":["Greater in the tube","Greater in the basin","Equal, since ρ is the same","Equal, since air presses on each"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Same liquid is not enough: the depths differ, and P = P_atm + ρgh grows with depth."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Both surfaces share P_atm, but that skips the ρgh term, which differs with depth."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The basin's extra width and volume aren't in ρgh; its extra weight rests on extra floor area."}],"rule":"Pressure in a liquid depends only on its density, g and depth (P = P_atm + ρgh), never on the container's shape, width or volume.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Same liquid and same air on top, so only depth matters. The deeper point has the higher pressure, whatever the container."},"exp":"**P = P_atm + ρgh** contains only the liquid's density, g and the depth below an open surface. Same ethanol, same air above, but the point in the tube is **deeper** (35 cm vs 20 cm), so the pressure there is **greater**, however thin the tube is.","how":{"ask":"Which quantities in the depth-pressure formula differ between the two points?","know":"• **P = P_atm + ρgh**: only ρ, g, h and the surface pressure appear.\n• Width, shape and volume are not in the formula (the hydrostatic paradox).\n• Same liquid + same surface pressure → the deeper point has the higher pressure.","steps":[{"text":"List what the two points share and what differs.","hl":"hold ethanol","calc":["ρ: same (ethanol)","surface: open to the air in both","h: 20 cm (basin) vs 35 cm (tube)"]},{"text":"Compute ρgh for each.","calc":["basin: 789 × 9.8 × 0.20 ≈ 1.5 kPa","tube: 789 × 9.8 × 0.35 ≈ 2.7 kPa"]},{"text":"Equal ρ and equal air pressure don't make the pressures equal when the depths differ.","strike":["Equal, since ρ is the same","Equal, since air presses on each"]},{"text":"The basin's width and volume never enter ρgh.","strike":["Greater in the basin"]},{"text":"Answer: greater in the tube, the deeper point."}],"trap":"Thinking the basin, with far more liquid, must have the greater pressure: only depth below the surface counts."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"both open","note":"same P_atm on each surface"},{"t":"hold ethanol","note":"same ρ"},{"t":"20 cm below the basin's surface","note":"h₁"},{"t":"35 cm below the tube's surface","note":"h₂"}]},{"text":"**Pressure at depth: P = P_atm + ρgh.** Check which letters change between the points: only h."},{"text":"**Why shape drops out:** in a wide vessel the extra liquid presses on extra floor; in a flared one the slanted walls hold up the overhanging liquid. At equal depths in the same liquid, the pressures would be equal."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to compare pressures in open containers of the same liquid, compare depths only; the container's width, shape and volume never matter."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-10a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:10#1","pe5-cp:10#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:10"],"q":"Every nucleus in a sample has a mass number of 32 and contains 17 neutrons. A student identifies the sample as sulfur, since ³²S is sulfur's most common isotope. Is the student's identification correct?","choices":["Yes; mass number 32 fits sulfur","No; Z = 17, chlorine","No; Z = 15, phosphorus","No; Z = 32, germanium"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"Uses the neutron count as the atomic number; Z counts protons, not neutrons."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"Uses the mass number as the atomic number; A counts protons plus neutrons."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Many elements have isotopes of the same mass number; only the proton count identifies an element."}],"rule":"Element = proton count. Protons = mass number − neutrons; a matching mass number never identifies an element.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Z = A − N = 32 − 17. Sulfur is Z = 16, so one proton fewer is the element just before it."},"exp":"Z = A − N = 32 − 17 = **15**, which is phosphorus (³²P, a radioactive isotope). Sulfur has Z = 16, so ³²S has 16 neutrons. The student matched the mass number, which does not identify an element.","how":{"ask":"How many protons does each nucleus have, and which element has that atomic number?","know":"• **Atomic number Z** = protons; it alone identifies the element.\n• **Mass number A** = protons + neutrons.\n• So **Z = A − N**. Isotopes of different elements can share A (isobars).","steps":[{"text":"Find the protons.","hl":"contains 17 neutrons","calc":["Z = A − N = 32 − 17 = 15"]},{"text":"Z = 15 is phosphorus; sulfur is Z = 16."},{"text":"Neither N nor A is the atomic number.","strike":["No; Z = 17, chlorine","No; Z = 32, germanium"]},{"text":"A shared mass number does not make the same element.","strike":["Yes; mass number 32 fits sulfur"]},{"text":"Answer: no; Z = 15, phosphorus."}],"trap":"Accepting the student's match on mass number: ³²S and ³²P have the same A but different Z."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"mass number of 32","note":"A"},{"t":"contains 17 neutrons","note":"N"},{"t":"Is the student's identification correct?","note":"check the student's logic"}]},{"text":"**Protons decide.** Subtract the neutrons from the mass number; the result is the atomic number, the element's ID.","calc":["Z = A − N"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to identify an element from a nucleus, compute protons = mass number − neutrons and look up that atomic number; never identify it by mass number."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-11a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:11#1","pe5-cp:11#2","pe5-cp:11#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:11"],"q":"Each answer choice shows a molecule's bonds, without the lone pairs. Which molecule is polar?","choices":["Beryllium chloride","Sulfur trioxide","Carbon tetrafluoride","Nitrogen trichloride"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe5-becl2","cpt-pe5-so3","cpt-pe5-cf4","cpt-pe5-ncl3"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"BeCl₂ is linear with two identical bonds, so the dipoles cancel."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"SO₃ is trigonal planar with three equivalent S–O bonds and no lone pair on S, so its dipoles cancel."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"CF₄ is a symmetric tetrahedron with four identical bonds: nonpolar."}],"rule":"Symmetric shapes with identical bonds (linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral) are nonpolar; a lone pair on the center (pyramidal, bent) makes it polar.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Symmetric shapes cancel bond dipoles: BeCl₂ linear, SO₃ trigonal planar, CF₄ tetrahedral are nonpolar. NCl₃'s lone pair makes it pyramidal, so its dipoles don't cancel: polar."},"exp":"In **NCl₃**, nitrogen holds three N–Cl bonds plus **a lone pair** (4 domains), which makes a pyramid whose bond dipoles cannot cancel. BeCl₂ is linear, SO₃ trigonal planar and CF₄ tetrahedral, each with identical bonds and no lone pair on the middle atom, so their dipoles cancel.","how":{"ask":"Find each shape by VSEPR, then ask whether the bond dipoles cancel.","know":"• **VSEPR**: count bonding domains and lone pairs around the middle atom.\n• Identical bonds in a **symmetric shape** (linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral) cancel: nonpolar.\n• A **central-atom lone pair** (trigonal pyramidal, bent) leaves a net dipole: polar.","steps":[{"text":"Assign each shape.","hl":"Which molecule is polar?","calc":["BeCl₂: 2 domains, 0 lone pairs → linear","SO₃: 3 domains, 0 lone pairs → trigonal planar","CF₄: 4 domains, 0 lone pairs → tetrahedral","NCl₃: 3 bonds + 1 lone pair → trigonal pyramidal"]},{"text":"The three symmetric shapes with identical bonds cancel their dipoles.","strike":["Beryllium chloride","Sulfur trioxide","Carbon tetrafluoride"]},{"text":"Answer: nitrogen trichloride."}],"trap":"Judging from the drawing alone: the drawings omit lone pairs, so count N's five valence electrons to find the hidden pair."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"without the lone pairs","note":"count them yourself"},{"t":"polar","note":"net dipole ≠ 0"}]},{"text":"**VSEPR first:** count the bonding domains plus lone pairs around the middle atom; that sets the shape."},{"text":"**Count electrons on the central atom.** N has 5 valence electrons: 3 go to bonds, 2 stay as a lone pair.","calc":["N: 5 − 3 = 2 → one lone pair"]},{"text":"**Shape decides cancellation.** A lone pair pushes the three N–Cl bonds into a pyramid, so their dipoles add up instead of canceling."},{"text":"**Also:** symmetric shapes with identical bonds, such as linear BeCl₂, trigonal planar SO₃ and tetrahedral CF₄, are nonpolar even though each bond is polar."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** symmetric shapes with identical bonds cancel their dipoles; a central-atom lone pair leaves a net dipole."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-12a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:12#1","pe5-cp:12#2","pe5-cp:12#3","pe6-cp:53#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:12"],"q":"A gas under a free piston is at 50 °C, 2.4 atm and 0.80 mol. All else constant, which change would NOT double its volume?","choices":["Heating it to 100 °C","Doubling the moles","Dropping P to 1.2 atm","Heating it to 646 K"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"V ∝ n, so doubling the moles of gas doubles V."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"V ∝ 1/P, and 2.4 → 1.2 atm halves P, so V doubles."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"50 °C is 323 K, and 646 K is twice that: T doubles in kelvin, so V doubles."}],"rule":"Gas laws use kelvin: add 273 before comparing temperatures. V ∝ nT/P, so only a doubled n, doubled K or halved P doubles V.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Gas laws run in kelvin: 50 °C → 100 °C is 323 → 373 K, only about 15% more, not double. 646 K is 2 × 323, so that one does double V. Heating to 100 °C."},"exp":"From PV = nRT, **V ∝ nT/P** with T in **kelvin**. 50 °C is 323 K and 100 °C is 373 K, so V grows only by 373/323 ≈ 1.15. Doubling n (0.80 → 1.60 mol), halving P (2.4 → 1.2 atm) or doubling the kelvin temperature (323 → 646 K) each doubles V.","how":{"ask":"Turn each change into a factor on n, T (in kelvin) or P, and find the one that does not give ×2.","know":"• **Ideal gas: V = nRT/P**, so **V ∝ nT/P**.\n• T must be in **kelvin**: T(K) = T(°C) + 273.\n• ×2 n or ×2 T(K) doubles V; ×2 P halves V; ×½ P doubles V.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the starting temperature to kelvin.","hl":"50 °C","calc":["T = 50 + 273 = **323 K**"]},{"text":"Heating to 100 °C: find the kelvin factor.","hl":"Heating it to 100 °C","calc":["100 °C = 373 K","factor = 373 ÷ 323 ≈ **1.15**","V rises about 15%, not ×2"]},{"text":"Heating to 646 K is 2 × 323 K: T doubles in kelvin, so V doubles.","calc":["646 ÷ 323 = 2"],"strike":["Heating it to 646 K"]},{"text":"Doubling the amount takes n from 0.80 to 1.60 mol, and V ∝ n.","strike":["Doubling the moles"]},{"text":"Dropping P to 1.2 atm halves P; V ∝ 1/P, so V doubles. (Raising P to 4.8 atm would halve V instead.)","strike":["Dropping P to 1.2 atm"]},{"text":"Answer: heating it to 100 °C."}],"trap":"Doubling the Celsius number: 50 → 100 °C is only 323 → 373 K, a 15% change."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"free piston","note":"V is free to change"},{"t":"50 °C","note":"convert to K"},{"t":"NOT double","note":"find the odd one out"}]},{"text":"**Write V in terms of the others:** V = nRT/P, so V ∝ nT/P.","calc":["V₂/V₁ = (n₂/n₁)(T₂/T₁)(P₁/P₂)"]},{"text":"**Also: pressure sits in the denominator.** Doubling P halves V; halving P doubles V.","calc":["P ×2 → V ×½","P ×½ → V ×2"]},{"text":"**Also: always use kelvin.** Doubling a Celsius temperature does not double the kelvin temperature.","calc":["50 °C → 323 K","100 °C → 373 K","373/323 ≈ 1.15"]},{"text":"**Also:** with P and n both fixed, V ∝ T in kelvin (Charles's law). Cooling shrinks a gas sample even when none is lost: the same moles simply fill less space.","calc":["60 °C → 0 °C: 333 K → 273 K","V₂ = V₁ × 273/333 ≈ 0.82 V₁, about 18% smaller","the °C ratio (0/60) would wrongly give zero"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** V ∝ nT/P with T in kelvin: ×2 n or ×2 T(K) doubles V, ×2 P halves it, and doubling °C does not double V."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-13a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:13#1","pe6-cp:20#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:13"],"passage":"Silver is used to make antimicrobial wound dressings. Silver has an average atomic mass of 107.87 u and two stable isotopes, ¹⁰⁷Ag and ¹⁰⁹Ag, present in nearly equal amounts. A lab studying the release of silver ions from dressings tags them by enriching the heavier isotope. Mass spectrometry then tracks the ¹⁰⁹Ag nucleus signal over time.","q":"How many neutrons does each tracked silver nucleus contain?","choices":["62","60","61","47"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"Uses the other isotope's mass number, 107; take A from this nucleus's own superscript."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"Uses the rounded average atomic mass, 108; one nucleus has its own whole-number A."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"47 is Z, the proton count, not the neutron count."}],"rule":"N = A − Z, with A from the isotope's own superscript and Z from the periodic table; the average atomic mass is never a nucleus's A.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ag is element 47. Use the isotope's own mass number: N = 109 − 47 = 62. The 107.87 average is a distractor."},"exp":"Silver is Z = 47 on the periodic table. **N = A − Z** = 109 − 47 = **62**. The ¹⁰⁷Ag isotope has 60, two fewer, matching its mass number being 2 lower; the average mass 107.87 u describes the mixture, not one nucleus.","how":{"ask":"Read Z from the periodic table, then subtract it from the isotope's mass number.","know":"• **N = A − Z**, with A the isotope's mass number (the superscript) and Z from the periodic table.\n• Isotopes of one element share Z, so their neutron counts differ by exactly their difference in A.\n• The **average atomic mass** is a weighted mean of the mixture, never a mass number.","steps":[{"text":"Use the isotope's own mass number.","hl":"¹⁰⁹Ag nucleus","calc":["A = 109; Z(Ag) = 47"]},{"text":"Subtract.","calc":["N = 109 − 47 = **62**"]},{"text":"60 belongs to ¹⁰⁷Ag (107 − 47), 61 uses the rounded average mass (108 − 47), and 47 is the proton count.","strike":["60","61","47"]},{"text":"Answer: 62 neutrons."}],"trap":"Using the average atomic mass as if it were a mass number: 107.87 u describes the natural mix of isotopes."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"107.87 u","note":"average, not A"},{"t":"¹⁰⁹Ag","note":"A = 109"}]},{"text":"**Get Z.** Silver is element 47, so every Ag nucleus has 47 protons."},{"text":"**Subtract.**","calc":["N = A − Z = 109 − 47 = 62"]},{"text":"**Check with the other isotope.** Same Z, so A differs only by neutrons.","calc":["¹⁰⁷Ag: 107 − 47 = 60","109 − 107 = 2 = 62 − 60 ✓"]},{"text":"**Also:** two isotopes of one element share Z, so a mass number one lower means exactly one neutron fewer, and a lower neutron-to-proton ratio. Carbon-11, a PET tracer, against stable carbon-12:","calc":["Z(C) = 6 for both","¹¹C: N = 11 − 6 = 5; ¹²C: N = 12 − 6 = 6","N/Z: 5/6 ≈ 0.83 versus 6/6 = 1.00"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** neutrons = mass number − atomic number, with Z from the periodic table; the average atomic mass is never a mass number."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-14a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:14#1","pe5-cp:14#4","pe5-cp:14#2","pe5-cp:14#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:14"],"passage":"Histidine side chains bind metal ions in many enzymes. To study this binding without a protein, chemists use 4-Methylimidazole, shown, as a small model of histidine's aromatic side chain. Its ring keeps the two nitrogens of histidine's ring, one bearing H. The lab needs the molecular formula to calculate the amount to weigh out.","q":"What is the molecular formula of the model compound?","fig":"cpt-pe5-4-methylimidazole","choices":["C₄H₅N₂","C₄H₆N₂","C₄H₇N₂","C₄H₁₀N₂"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"Odd H with two N: a neutral molecule of C, H and an even number of N must have an even H count; this one forgets the N–H."},{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"Odd H again, impossible for a neutral C, H, N₂ molecule; it adds an H to the N that already has three bonds."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Too many H: that is the saturated ring, but this aromatic ring keeps its two double bonds."}],"rule":"Count H atom by atom (C makes 4 bonds, N makes 3). With an even number of N, a neutral molecule has an even number of H.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Two N → even H count, so H₅ and H₇ are out. H₁₀ would be a ring with no double bonds; aromatic imidazole has two, 4 H fewer: C₄H₆N₂."},"exp":"The ring has five atoms, **3 C and 2 N**, plus a CH₃ carbon: **4 C**. Two ring carbons each hide **one H**, the ring carbon holding the methyl has none, the CH₃ has **3 H**, and the N drawn with H adds **one more**: 2 + 3 + 1 = 6, so **C₄H₆N₂**. Missing the N–H gives C₄H₅N₂, an H on both N gives C₄H₇N₂, and saturating the ring gives C₄H₁₀N₂.","how":{"ask":"How many carbons and nitrogens are there, and how many hidden H does each atom need to reach its full set of bonds?","know":"• Unlabeled corners and line ends are C; each C makes **4 bonds** and each neutral N makes **3**; missing bonds are hidden H.\n• An aromatic ring keeps its double bonds, so count them when filling each atom.\n• An H drawn on a heteroatom (N–H) counts too; an N with a single and a double ring bond needs no H.\n• With an even number of N, a neutral molecule has an even number of H.","steps":[{"text":"The five-membered ring has three C corners and two N (only one drawn with H); the line off the ring is a CH₃.","hl":"4-Methylimidazole, shown","calc":["C: 3 ring + 1 methyl = **4 C**","N: **2**"]},{"text":"Fill each atom to its full set of bonds.","calc":["2 ring C (2 ring bonds + 1 double-bond extra = 3) → **1 H each** = 2 H","ring C with CH₃: 4 bonds already → **0 H**","CH₃ → **3 H**","N–H → **1 H**; other N (single + double) → **0 H**","H total: 2 + 3 + 1 = **6** → **C₄H₆N₂**"]},{"text":"C₄H₅N₂ forgets the N–H, and C₄H₇N₂ adds an H to the N that already has three bonds.","strike":["C₄H₅N₂","C₄H₇N₂"]},{"text":"C₄H₁₀N₂ fills the ring as if both double bonds were gone (the saturated ring).","strike":["C₄H₁₀N₂"]},{"text":"Answer: C₄H₆N₂."}],"trap":"Giving both nitrogens an H: the N with a double bond already has three bonds, so only the drawn N–H counts."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"4-Methylimidazole","note":"5-membered ring, two N, one CH₃"},{"t":"aromatic","note":"double bonds stay"},{"t":"molecular formula","note":"count C, H, N"}]},{"text":"**Worked example: pyridine.** Its six-membered ring has 5 C corners and one N. Each C shows one single and one double ring bond (3 bonds), so it hides 1 H; the N has a single and a double ring bond (3 bonds), so it has none.","fig":"pyridine","calc":["5 C × 1 H = 5 H","N: 0 H","→ **C₅H₅N**"]},{"text":"**Same count here.** Two ring C hide 1 H each, the ring C bearing the methyl hides none, the CH₃ has 3, one N carries its drawn H, and the other N, like pyridine's, has three bonds and no H.","calc":["2 + 0 + 3 + 1 = 6 H","→ **C₄H₆N₂**"]},{"text":"**Also:** the parent rings to know by formula: imidazole (five-membered, two N, one N–H) is C₃H₄N₂; pyrrole (five-membered, one N–H) is C₄H₅N; pyridine (six-membered, one N, no N–H) is C₅H₅N. Methyl on a ring carbon adds CH₂ to the formula."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count every C and N, then give every C four bonds and every N three; the missing bonds are H, and only an N drawn with H carries one. A substituted ring carbon loses the H it would otherwise hide."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-15a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:15#1","pe5-cp:15#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:15"],"passage":"A respiratory lab calibrates a CO₂ sensor with sealed glass ampoules of pure gas. Each ampoule has an internal volume of 33.6 cm³ and is filled with CO₂ at 273 K and 1 atm. At these conditions, one mole of any ideal gas fills 22.4 L. The ampoules are broken inside a closed chamber to release a known amount of gas.","q":"About how many moles of CO₂ does one ampoule hold?","choices":["1.5 × 10⁻³ mol","1.5 mol","1.5 × 10⁻⁶ mol","6.7 × 10² mol"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Treats cm³ as litres, so the volume is a thousand times too big."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Converts cm³ to L twice, shrinking the answer by another factor of a thousand."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Divides the molar volume by the gas volume, inverting the ratio."}],"rule":"At STP, n = V(L) ÷ 22.4 L/mol; 1 cm³ = 1 mL = 10⁻³ L. A few tens of cm³ of gas is a few thousandths of a mole.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"22.4 L is 22,400 cm³ per mole. The ampoule is about 1/670 of that, so n is a bit over one-thousandth of a mole."},"exp":"V = 33.6 cm³ = 0.0336 L. At 273 K and 1 atm, n = V/22.4 L·mol⁻¹ = 0.0336/22.4 = **1.5 × 10⁻³ mol**. Using n = PV/RT at 273 K gives the same result.","how":{"ask":"What is the ampoule volume in litres, and what fraction of a mole is that at STP?","know":"• **STP**: 273 K, 1 atm; 1 mol of ideal gas = 22.4 L.\n• **1 cm³ = 1 mL = 10⁻³ L**.\n• Or n = PV/RT with V in L and T in K.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the volume to litres.","hl":"internal volume of 33.6 cm³","calc":["33.6 cm³ = 33.6 mL = 0.0336 L"]},{"text":"Divide by the molar volume at STP.","calc":["n = 0.0336 L ÷ 22.4 L/mol = 1.5 × 10⁻³ mol"]},{"text":"Skipping the cm³ → L step, or doing it twice, misses by a thousand.","strike":["1.5 mol","1.5 × 10⁻⁶ mol"]},{"text":"22.4 ÷ 0.0336 is litres per litre, not moles: the ratio is upside down.","strike":["6.7 × 10² mol"]},{"text":"Answer: about 1.5 × 10⁻³ mol."}],"trap":"Dividing 33.6 by 22.4 without converting cm³ to L, which gives a full mole of gas in a small ampoule."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"33.6 cm³","note":"convert to L"},{"t":"273 K and 1 atm","note":"STP"},{"t":"one mole of any ideal gas fills 22.4 L","note":"molar volume"}]},{"text":"**Sense check.** A mole of gas fills 22.4 L, about a large bucket. A small ampoule holds only thousandths of a mole."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** moles of gas at STP = volume in litres ÷ 22.4 L/mol (or PV/RT); convert cm³ or mL to litres first."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-16a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:16#1","pe5-cp:16#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:16"],"passage":"A protein label fluoresces green at 520 nm when it is excited at 470 nm. A student tries a cheaper 590 nm laser as the light source instead. Even at the laser's highest power, no 520 nm emission is detected. The buffer does not absorb visible light.","q":"Which statement best explains this result?","choices":["Photons at 590 nm are too low in energy","Photons at 590 nm hold excess energy as heat","The laser at 590 nm is too dim","The buffer absorbs light at 590 nm"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"A longer wavelength carries less energy per photon, not more, so there is no excess to lose."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Brightness adds photons, not energy per photon; the passage used the highest power anyway."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The passage says the buffer absorbs no visible light, so nothing blocks the laser."}],"rule":"Fluorescence emits less energy than it absorbs, so the exciting photon must have a shorter wavelength (more energy) than the emitted one; intensity can't make up the gap.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Compare wavelengths: excitation must be shorter than emission. 590 nm is longer than 520 nm, so each photon falls short."},"exp":"E = hc/λ, so a 590 nm photon (≈ 2.1 eV) has **less** energy than a 520 nm photon (≈ 2.4 eV). Fluorescence emits less energy than was absorbed, never more, so **no photon can be emitted at 520 nm**. More laser power only adds photons.","how":{"ask":"Does one 590 nm photon have enough energy to produce one 520 nm photon?","know":"• **E = hc/λ**: shorter λ means more energy per photon (E in eV ≈ 1240 ÷ λ in nm).\n• **Fluorescence** emits less energy than it absorbs; the difference is lost as heat.\n• So the exciting λ must be **shorter** than the emitted λ. Intensity changes how many photons arrive, not the energy of each.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the energy per photon.","hl":"cheaper 590 nm laser","calc":["E(590) ≈ 1240/590 ≈ 2.1 eV","E(520) ≈ 1240/520 ≈ 2.4 eV"]},{"text":"The absorbed photon has less energy than the emission needs, so it has no excess to shed.","strike":["Photons at 590 nm hold excess energy as heat"]},{"text":"Power and the buffer are ruled out by the passage.","hl":"Even at the laser's highest power","strike":["The laser at 590 nm is too dim","The buffer absorbs light at 590 nm"]},{"text":"Answer: 590 nm photons are too low in energy."}],"trap":"Thinking a brighter beam can make up the gap: each photon still carries too little energy."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"fluoresces green at 520 nm","note":"emitted photon"},{"t":"excited at 470 nm","note":"shorter λ works"},{"t":"highest power","note":"intensity is not the issue"}]},{"text":"**Energy runs downhill in fluorescence.** The molecule absorbs a photon, loses a little energy as heat, then emits a photon of lower energy (longer λ)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an excitation wavelength longer than the emission wavelength can never produce that fluorescence, because each absorbed photon must carry more energy than the photon emitted."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-17a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:17#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:17"],"passage":"Circular dichroism of proteins needs chiral centers: only a chiral molecule absorbs left- and right-circularly polarized light differently. Most amino acids have one stereocenter, the α-carbon, but a few have two. L-threonine, shown with atoms labeled W–Z, has two stereocenters: the unlabeled α-carbon and one labeled atom. A stereocenter is a carbon with four different groups.","q":"Which labeled atom is threonine's second stereocenter?","fig":"cpt-pe5-threonine-sites","choices":["W","X","Y","Z"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"The carboxyl carbon is trigonal planar with only three groups, so it can't be a stereocenter."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A CH₃ carbon has three identical H, so it can't be a stereocenter."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The amine N carries two identical H and inverts freely, so it isn't a stereocenter."}],"rule":"A stereocenter is an sp³ atom with four different groups; any atom with two identical groups (H, H) or a double bond is out.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Stereocenter = sp³ with four different groups. The β-carbon holds H, OH, CH₃ and Cα, so X. The carboxyl C is planar, CH₃ has three H, NH₂ has two H."},"exp":"Besides the α-carbon, only the **β-carbon (X)** holds four different groups: H, OH, CH₃ and the α-carbon. The carboxyl carbon (W) is planar with three groups, the methyl carbon (Y) has three identical H, and the amine N (Z) has two identical H. Threonine's two stereocenters both make it chiral, which is what a CD signal needs.","how":{"ask":"Apart from the α-carbon, which labeled atom carries four different groups?","know":"• A **stereocenter** (chiral carbon) bears four different groups, so the molecule is not superimposable on its mirror image.\n• **CD** works because a chiral molecule absorbs the two circular polarizations of light by different amounts; in proteins the chiral α-carbons start it.\n• Every standard amino acid except **glycine** has a chiral α-carbon; **threonine** and **isoleucine** also have a chiral β-carbon.","steps":[{"text":"List the groups on each labeled atom; X is highlighted.","fig":"cpt-pe5-threonine-beta","calc":["W, carboxyl C: =O, OH, Cα → **3 groups, planar**","X, Cβ: H, OH, CH₃, Cα → **4 different**","Y, CH₃: three identical H","Z, N: H, H, Cα → **not a stereocenter**"]},{"text":"The methyl carbon carries three identical H, and the amine N carries two identical H and inverts freely.","strike":["Y","Z"]},{"text":"The carboxyl carbon is trigonal planar with only three groups.","strike":["W"]},{"text":"Answer: X, the β-carbon."}],"trap":"Picking the carboxyl carbon because its neighbors look varied; a planar carbon with only three groups cannot be a stereocenter."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Circular dichroism","note":"needs chirality"},{"t":"L-threonine","note":"Cα and Cβ both chiral"},{"t":"unlabeled α-carbon","note":"already counted"},{"t":"two stereocenters","note":"find the second"}]},{"text":"**Test each atom.** Count its groups, hidden H included; all four must differ."},{"text":"**Also:** the α-carbon (H, NH₂, COOH, side chain) is the stereocenter in every amino acid but glycine, whose α-carbon carries two H. Isoleucine, like threonine, adds a second one at Cβ."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a stereocenter is an sp³ carbon with four different groups; besides the α-carbon of every amino acid but glycine, threonine's β-carbon (H, OH, CH₃, Cα) is one too."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-18a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:18#1","pe5-cp:18#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:18"],"passage":"To test whether a surface salt bridge stabilizes an enzyme, a glutamate is mutated to glutamine. Both side chains are drawn. At pH 7.0, the glutamate carboxyl (pKa ≈ 4.2) is ionized, while the glutamine amide does not ionize. Glutamine carries –CONH₂ where glutamate carries –COO⁻.","q":"Which side-chain property is unchanged by the mutation?","fig":["glutamate-sc","glutamine-sc"],"choices":["Net charge","Carbon count","H-bond donors","Salt-bridge ability"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"The carboxylate is −1 at pH 7.0, and the amide is neutral, so the net charge changes."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An ionized –COO⁻ has no N–H or O–H to donate; the amide –NH₂ adds two H-bond donors."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A salt bridge needs a charge; the neutral amide cannot form one, which is the point of the test."}],"rule":"For a substitution, compare the two side chains one property at a time (charge at the pH, H-bonding, size, atoms); keep only the property both share.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Only O⁻ swaps for NH₂; the CH₂–CH₂–C skeleton is untouched. Anything tied to the charge or the N–H changes; the skeleton does not."},"exp":"Glutamate (–CH₂CH₂COO⁻) and glutamine (–CH₂CH₂CONH₂) have the **same three-carbon side chain**; only O⁻ becomes NH₂. That swap removes the −1 charge, so salt bridges are lost, and adds two N–H donors. The **carbon count** is unchanged.","how":{"ask":"Line up the two side chains: what stays the same once O⁻ is swapped for NH₂?","know":"• Glu: –CH₂CH₂COO⁻ (pKa ≈ 4, charged at pH 7). Gln: –CH₂CH₂CONH₂ (never ionizes).\n• A **salt bridge** needs opposite charges.\n• H-bond donors are N–H and O–H; a carboxylate has none, an amide has two.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the side chains atom by atom.","hl":"Glutamine carries –CONH₂ where glutamate carries –COO⁻","calc":["Glu: C₃, –COO⁻, charge −1, 0 donors","Gln: C₃, –CONH₂, charge 0, 2 donors"]},{"text":"The charge goes from −1 to 0, so the salt bridge goes with it.","strike":["Net charge","Salt-bridge ability"]},{"text":"The amide brings N–H donors the carboxylate lacked.","strike":["H-bond donors"]},{"text":"Answer: the carbon count, three side-chain carbons in both."}],"trap":"Assuming a \"conservative\" swap keeps the charge: Glu → Gln looks small but removes a full negative charge."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"surface salt bridge","note":"what is being tested"},{"t":"ionized","note":"Glu is −1 at pH 7.0"},{"t":"does not ionize","note":"Gln is neutral"}]},{"text":"**Property by property.** Charge, H-bonding, size and skeleton each get a yes or no; the unchanged property is the one with the same answer for both."},{"text":"**Also:** when both side chains are uncharged at the pH, for example Phe → Tyr at pH 7 (Tyr –OH pKa ≈ 10), the net charge is what stays the same, while mass and H-bonding change."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to find what a mutation leaves unchanged, write both side chains at the stated pH and compare charge, H-bonding, size and carbon skeleton one at a time."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-19a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:19#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:19"],"passage":"An E3 ligase attaches ubiquitin, a small 8.5-kDa protein, to a 40-kDa substrate, one ubiquitin at a time, so chains of several can build up. The ligase does not cut any protein. Samples of the reaction were taken at 2, 10, 30 and 90 min, heated in SDS loading buffer, and loaded side by side on one SDS-PAGE gel.","q":"As sampling time increases, what change in the lanes is expected?","choices":["40-kDa band weakens; heavier ladder forms","40-kDa band weakens; smaller fragments form","No band changes; lanes match","Each band moves down; none fades"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Adding ubiquitin makes the substrate heavier, and heavier chains run a shorter distance, not farther."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Boiling in SDS stops each sample at its own time point, so later lanes hold more modified substrate."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"SDS gives every chain the same charge per mass, so heavier products move less, not more."}],"rule":"SDS-PAGE sorts by size: added mass shifts bands up (shorter runs), removed mass shifts them down; over a time course the starting band fades as new bands grow.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Is mass being added or removed? Ubiquitin is added, so new bands sit above the starting one, in steps of about one ubiquitin each."},"exp":"Each ubiquitin adds about 8.5 kDa, so the substrate becomes **heavier species** (≈ 48.5, 57, 65.5 kDa …). On SDS-PAGE, heavier chains run **less far**, so over time the 40-kDa band **fades** and a **ladder of higher bands** grows. Boiling in SDS stops each sample at its time point.","how":{"ask":"Does the reaction add or remove mass, and where do heavier chains run on the gel?","know":"• **SDS-PAGE** coats chains with SDS, giving the same charge per mass, so they separate by **size** alone.\n• **Smaller** chains run **farther** from the well; larger chains stay nearer.\n• Boiling in SDS buffer denatures the enzymes and stops the reaction in each sample.","steps":[{"text":"Ubiquitin is added, never removed, and nothing is cut.","hl":"The ligase does not cut any protein","calc":["40 + 8.5 = 48.5 kDa","40 + 2(8.5) = 57 kDa …"]},{"text":"Heavier species run a shorter distance, above the 40-kDa band.","strike":["40-kDa band weakens; smaller fragments form","Each band moves down; none fades"]},{"text":"Each lane captures a later moment of the reaction, so the lanes differ.","strike":["No band changes; lanes match"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Band strength in each lane (illustrative)","x":{"label":"Sampling time (min)","min":0,"max":90,"step":15},"y":{"label":"Share of substrate (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"40-kDa band","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[2,95],[10,78],[30,45],[90,12]]},{"name":"Ubiquitinated bands","marker":"triangle-open","line":"smooth","points":[[2,5],[10,22],[30,55],[90,88]]}]}},{"text":"Answer: the 40-kDa band weakens while a ladder of heavier bands forms."}],"trap":"Reading every time course as cleavage: here mass is added, so new bands appear above the start, not below."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"8.5-kDa protein","note":"mass added per step"},{"t":"does not cut any protein","note":"no smaller pieces"},{"t":"heated in SDS loading buffer","note":"each sample frozen in time"}]},{"text":"**Gel geography.** On SDS-PAGE, the well is at the top; light chains travel farthest, heavy chains stay near the top."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a time course on SDS-PAGE, decide whether the reaction adds or removes mass; the starting band fades and new bands grow above it (mass added) or below it (mass removed)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-19b","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:19#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:19"],"passage":"A 28-kDa enzyme with one exposed cysteine slowly forms disulfide-linked dimers when its solution is left open to air. Samples were taken at 3, 20 and 120 min. Each was mixed with SDS sample buffer containing no reducing agent, so disulfide bonds stay intact, and run on SDS-PAGE.","q":"How do later lanes differ from earlier ones?","choices":["28-kDa band weaker; 56-kDa band stronger","28-kDa band stronger; 56-kDa band weaker","28-kDa band weaker; 14-kDa band stronger","All lanes alike; SDS breaks the dimers"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards in time: dimers accumulate as the sample sits in air, so the monomer band shrinks."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Halving the mass would need cleavage; joining two chains doubles it instead."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"SDS breaks noncovalent contacts but not a covalent S–S bond, and no reducing agent was added."}],"rule":"On SDS-PAGE over time, track mass: the starting band fades and the product band grows at the product's mass; covalent links survive SDS unless a reducing agent cuts S–S.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Two 28-kDa chains joined by S–S run as one chain of double mass. Over time, more monomer turns into dimer."},"exp":"Each disulfide-linked dimer weighs 2 × 28 = **56 kDa**. Without a reducing agent the S–S bond survives SDS, so over time the **28-kDa band fades** and the **56-kDa band grows**.","how":{"ask":"What mass does the product have, and does SDS without a reducing agent keep it intact?","know":"• **SDS** unfolds proteins and breaks noncovalent contacts; **reducing agents** (DTT, β-mercaptoethanol) are needed to break S–S bonds.\n• Smaller chains run farther; a dimer runs at twice the monomer mass.\n• In a time course, the starting band weakens as the product band strengthens.","steps":[{"text":"The product is two chains joined covalently.","hl":"disulfide-linked dimers","calc":["2 × 28 = 56 kDa"]},{"text":"No reducing agent, so the dimer stays intact in SDS.","hl":"containing no reducing agent","strike":["All lanes alike; SDS breaks the dimers"]},{"text":"Dimers build up with time, so the monomer band shrinks, and no half-size piece forms.","strike":["28-kDa band stronger; 56-kDa band weaker","28-kDa band weaker; 14-kDa band stronger"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Band strength in each lane (illustrative)","x":{"label":"Sampling time (min)","min":0,"max":120,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Share of total protein (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"28-kDa band","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[3,96],[20,74],[120,30]]},{"name":"56-kDa band","marker":"triangle-open","line":"smooth","points":[[3,4],[20,26],[120,70]]}]}},{"text":"Answer: the 28-kDa band weakens and the 56-kDa band strengthens."}],"trap":"Assuming SDS takes every complex apart: it does not break covalent disulfide bonds."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"28-kDa enzyme","note":"monomer mass"},{"t":"disulfide-linked dimers","note":"product = 2 chains"},{"t":"no reducing agent","note":"S–S survives"}]},{"text":"**Product mass decides where the new band sits.** Joining chains moves material up the gel; cutting moves it down."},{"text":"**Also:** a protease does the opposite. If a 90-kDa chain is cut into 54- and 36-kDa pieces, later lanes show a fainter 90-kDa band and stronger bands below it, which run farther."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** over a time course on SDS-PAGE, the starting band fades while bands at the product masses grow: above it for joined chains, below it for cleaved pieces."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-20a","s":"Metabolism & Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:20#1","pe5-cp:20#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:20"],"passage":"Hexokinase traps glucose inside cells by transferring a phosphoryl group from ATP (507 g/mol) to glucose (180 g/mol). The products are ADP (427 g/mol) and glucose 6-phosphate. No water, proton donor or other molecule takes part in the overall reaction. The phosphoryl group simply moves from one molecule to the other.","q":"Glucose 6-phosphate has what molar mass, and by how much does the combined mass of glucose plus ATP change?","choices":["260 g/mol; no net change","260 g/mol; +80 g/mol gain","80 g/mol; no net change","687 g/mol; no net change"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Counts the group gained by glucose but skips the same mass lost when ATP becomes ADP."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Adds ATP and glucose together and skips subtracting the ADP left behind."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"That is the mass of the transferred group itself, not of the product that carries it."}],"rule":"In a group transfer, mass is conserved: product = substrate + cofactor − spent cofactor, and the combined mass of everything does not change.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"The group that leaves ATP is ATP − ADP = 80 g/mol, and it all lands on glucose. Add it to 180; total mass is unchanged."},"exp":"The group moved is ATP − ADP = 507 − 427 = 80 g/mol, so glucose 6-phosphate = 180 + 80 = **260 g/mol**. Mass is conserved: 507 + 180 = 427 + 260 = 687 g/mol on each side, a **net change of zero**.","how":{"ask":"What mass does the cofactor lose, where does it go, and does the total change?","know":"• In a **group transfer**, one molecule's loss is the other's gain.\n• **Mass is conserved**: (substrate + cofactor) = (product + spent cofactor).\n• Group mass = cofactor − spent cofactor.","steps":[{"text":"Find the mass of the group that moves.","hl":"ATP (507 g/mol)","calc":["507 − 427 = 80 g/mol"]},{"text":"Add it to glucose.","calc":["180 + 80 = 260 g/mol"]},{"text":"Check the total: both sides weigh the same.","calc":["507 + 180 = 687 = 427 + 260"],"strike":["260 g/mol; +80 g/mol gain","687 g/mol; no net change"]},{"text":"The 80 g/mol is the group, not the product.","strike":["80 g/mol; no net change"]},{"text":"Answer: 260 g/mol, with no net change."}],"trap":"Treating the phosphoryl group as new mass gained: it came off ATP, so the total is unchanged."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"transferring a phosphoryl group","note":"group moves, nothing is made"},{"t":"ADP (427 g/mol)","note":"spent cofactor"},{"t":"combined mass of glucose plus ATP","note":"conservation"}]},{"text":"**Bookkeeping.** What leaves the donor is exactly what lands on the acceptor.","calc":["product = substrate + (cofactor − spent cofactor)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in any group-transfer reaction, product mass = substrate + (donor − spent donor), and the total mass of the two reactants is unchanged."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-21a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:21#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:21"],"passage":"In ovarian cells, the enzyme aromatase makes estradiol, the main estrogen, in a single step. Its substrate is a 19-carbon steroid that already has all four fused rings and the 17-OH group found in estradiol. Aromatase removes the methyl carbon between rings A and B and makes ring A aromatic, turning its ketone into a phenol. The substrate is an androgen made mainly in other tissues.","q":"Which structure is the substrate of aromatase?","choices":["Testosterone","Oleic acid","Phosphatidylcholine","11-cis-Retinal"],"choiceFigs":["testosterone","oleic-acid","phosphatidylcholine","11-cis-retinal"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A fatty acid is one unbranched chain with no rings, so it cannot supply four fused rings."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A glycerophospholipid is glycerol, two fatty acids and a phosphate head: no ring system at all."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Retinal has a single six-membered ring on a polyene chain, not a four-ring steroid core."}],"rule":"A steroid is made from a lipid that already has the four fused rings (6-6-6-5); a precursor with no rings or one ring is out.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count fused rings in each drawing. Only one has the 6-6-6-5 steroid core; the other three have zero or one ring."},"exp":"Steroids keep the four fused rings of **cholesterol**, their parent. Aromatase acts on the 19-carbon androgen **testosterone**: it removes C19 and aromatizes ring A to give estradiol. Oleic acid and phosphatidylcholine have no rings, and retinal has only one.","how":{"ask":"Which drawing already has the four fused rings and the 19 carbons the passage describes?","know":"• **Steroids** share a four-ring core, three six-membered rings and one five-membered ring.\n• **Cholesterol** (C27) is the parent: side-chain cleavage gives pregnenolone (C21), then progesterone and cortisol (C21), androgens such as testosterone (C19), and estrogens (C18).\n• Fatty acids, phospholipids and retinoids are not steroids.","steps":[{"text":"The passage gives the substrate's skeleton: four fused rings, 19 carbons, a 17-OH and a ring-A ketone.","hl":"already has all four fused rings"},{"text":"Check each drawing for the steroid core.","calc":["testosterone: 4 fused rings, C19, 17-OH, ring-A ketone","oleic acid: 0 rings","phosphatidylcholine: 0 rings","retinal: 1 ring"]},{"text":"No rings means no steroid skeleton to start from.","strike":["Oleic acid","Phosphatidylcholine"]},{"text":"One ring on a long polyene chain is a retinoid, not a steroid.","strike":["11-cis-Retinal"]},{"text":"Answer: testosterone, the C19 androgen."}],"trap":"Choosing a lipid just because it is a lipid: only a molecule with the four fused rings can become a steroid hormone in one step."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"single step","note":"the precursor is one step away"},{"t":"19-carbon steroid","note":"an androgen"},{"t":"all four fused rings","note":"steroid core"}]},{"text":"**One family tree.** Cholesterol → pregnenolone → progesterone → androgens → estrogens; each step trims carbons but keeps the four rings."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** steroid hormones come from cholesterol through precursors that keep the four fused rings; pick the precursor whose ring core and carbon count fit the step described."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-22a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:22#1","pe5-cp:22#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:22"],"passage":"A bacterial protease with a pI of 5.2 is loaded at pH 8.5 onto a column packed with DEAE resin, whose diethylaminoethyl groups carry a positive charge. The protease binds, while most other proteins in the extract wash through. The bound protease is then released with a rising NaCl gradient, up to 0.5 M.","q":"The DEAE step is a purification by:","choices":["anion exchange","cation exchange","affinity","size exclusion"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"A cation exchanger has a negative resin that binds positive proteins; here the resin is positive."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"No specific ligand or tag is involved; salt releases the protein by screening charge, not by competing."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Size exclusion never binds proteins; it only delays small ones in the bead pores."}],"rule":"Name a column by how the protein binds and is released: by charge (salt elutes) → ion exchange named for the ion the resin binds; by a specific tag or ligand (competitor elutes) → affinity.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Positive resin + negative protein + salt elution = an exchanger for negative ions. A tag and a competitor would make it affinity."},"exp":"Above its pI (5.2), the protease is **negative** at pH 8.5, so it binds the **positive** DEAE groups. Rising NaCl displaces it by charge screening. A positive resin that exchanges negative ions is **anion exchange**.","how":{"ask":"What charge do the protein and the resin carry, and what releases the protein?","know":"• Above its **pI**, a protein is negative; below it, positive.\n• **Anion exchange**: + resin (e.g., DEAE) binds − proteins. **Cation exchange**: − resin (e.g., CM) binds + proteins. Salt elutes both.\n• **Affinity**: a specific tag or ligand binds; a competitor elutes (His-tag on Ni²⁺, eluted by imidazole).\n• **Size exclusion**: no binding; large proteins elute first.","steps":[{"text":"Protein charge: pH 8.5 is above the pI, so the protease is negative.","hl":"a pI of 5.2","calc":["pH 8.5 > pI 5.2 → net charge −"]},{"text":"The resin is positive, so it holds negative ions: not a cation exchanger.","hl":"carry a positive charge","strike":["cation exchange"]},{"text":"Salt, not a competing ligand, releases it, and a binding step rules out size exclusion.","strike":["affinity","size exclusion"]},{"text":"Answer: anion exchange."}],"trap":"Naming the column after the resin's own charge: a positive resin binds anions, so it is an anion exchanger."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"pI of 5.2","note":"negative at pH 8.5"},{"t":"carry a positive charge","note":"resin binds anions"},{"t":"rising NaCl gradient","note":"charge-based release"}]},{"text":"**Also:** a step where a His₆-tagged protein binds immobilized Ni²⁺ and is eluted by imidazole is **affinity**: the tag binds a specific metal site, and imidazole, the His side-chain ring, competes for the Ni²⁺."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to categorize a column step, ask what binds the protein and what releases it: charge and salt → ion exchange (named for the ion the resin binds), a tag and a competitor → affinity, no binding → size exclusion."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-23a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:23#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:23"],"passage":"A lab will run the membrane proteins of a bacterium on a denaturing gel. First the proteins must be pulled out of the lipid bilayer and kept dissolved in water. For this analysis, unfolding the proteins is acceptable. Four additives, shown, are on the shelf.","q":"Which additive would do both jobs best?","choices":["Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO)","Hexadecane","Glycerol","Sodium dodecyl sulfate"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe5-dmso","cpt-pe5-hexadecane","p1-glycerol","sodium-dodecyl-sulfate"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"No oily tail: DMSO is a small polar solvent, not a detergent, so it can't coat a membrane protein."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"No polar head: a pure alkane won't dissolve in water, so it can't carry a protein into it."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Glycerol is all polar head with no oily tail; it mixes with water but can't grip the protein's buried surface."}],"rule":"To pull a protein out of a membrane and keep it in water, pick the amphipathic molecule: a long hydrophobic tail and a polar or charged head on one molecule.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Look for one molecule with both a long C–H chain and a polar head. DMSO and glycerol lack a tail; hexadecane lacks a head."},"exp":"A detergent must be **amphipathic**. SDS has a **12-carbon tail** that coats the proteins' oily surfaces and a charged **sulfate head** that faces water; it also unfolds them, which is fine for a denaturing gel. DMSO and glycerol have no tail; hexadecane has no head.","how":{"ask":"Which drawing has, on one molecule, a long C–H chain and a polar or charged head?","know":"• A **detergent** is amphipathic: a long hydrophobic tail plus a polar head. Tails coat the protein's lipid-facing surface; heads face water, forming micelles.\n• Ionic detergents (SDS) also denature; nonionic sugar-headed ones (octyl glucoside, dodecyl maltoside) keep the fold.\n• A molecule that is only polar or only nonpolar cannot bridge the two environments.","steps":[{"text":"Two jobs: cover the proteins' oily belts and stay in water. That takes a tail and a head on one molecule.","hl":"kept dissolved in water"},{"text":"Sort each drawing into tail and head.","calc":["SDS: tail **C₁₂**, head **–OSO₃⁻ Na⁺**","DMSO: tail **none**, small polar S=O","hexadecane: tail **C₁₆**, head **none**","glycerol: tail **none**, **3 O–H**"]},{"text":"DMSO and glycerol are all head.","strike":["Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO)","Glycerol"]},{"text":"Hexadecane is all tail.","strike":["Hexadecane"]},{"text":"Answer: sodium dodecyl sulfate."}],"trap":"Choosing glycerol because it is very water-soluble; with no tail it cannot cover a membrane protein's hydrophobic surface."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"pulled out of the lipid bilayer","note":"must reach lipid chains"},{"t":"kept dissolved in water","note":"must face water"},{"t":"unfolding the proteins is acceptable","note":"a harsh detergent is fine"}]},{"text":"**Read each drawing.** A chain of only C and H is oily (the tail); O–H groups or charges are polar (the head). A detergent has both on one molecule."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to move a membrane protein into water, use an amphipathic detergent (long hydrophobic tail + polar head); choose a nonionic one if the fold must survive."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-24a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:24#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:24"],"passage":"A His-tagged enzyme is released from bacterial cells in a lysis buffer. The buffer contains 50 mM sodium phosphate at pH 8 and 150 mM NaCl, which keeps weakly bound proteins off the resin. NaCl has a molar mass of 58.5 g/mol. Each purification uses 400 mL of lysis buffer.","q":"How many moles and grams of NaCl does one batch of lysis buffer need?","choices":["0.060 mol; 3.5 g","0.60 mol; 35 g","0.0060 mol; 0.35 g","2.7 mol; 160 g"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Turns 400 mL into 4.0 L, a factor-of-ten slip in the volume."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Turns 400 mL into 0.040 L, a factor-of-ten slip the other way."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Divides the volume by the concentration instead of multiplying them."}],"rule":"mol = M × V(L), with mM → M and mL → L; then grams = mol × molar mass. The other buffer components are distractors.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"mM × mL gives μmol: 150 × 400 = 60,000 μmol = 0.06 mol. Times about 60 g/mol is about 3.5 g."},"exp":"n = (0.150 mol/L)(0.400 L) = **0.060 mol**. Mass = 0.060 mol × 58.5 g/mol ≈ **3.5 g**. The phosphate is a separate component and does not change the NaCl amount.","how":{"ask":"What are the NaCl concentration and the batch volume in M and L, and what is their product?","know":"• **mol = M × V**, V in litres; **mM = 10⁻³ M**.\n• **grams = mol × molar mass**.\n• Each solute in a buffer is weighed separately.","steps":[{"text":"Take the NaCl concentration and the batch volume.","hl":"150 mM NaCl","calc":["M = 0.150 mol/L","V = 400 mL = 0.400 L"]},{"text":"Moles, then grams.","calc":["n = 0.150 × 0.400 = 0.060 mol","m = 0.060 × 58.5 ≈ 3.5 g"]},{"text":"A slip in the mL → L step moves both numbers by ten.","strike":["0.60 mol; 35 g","0.0060 mol; 0.35 g"]},{"text":"V ÷ M is not moles: the operation is backwards.","strike":["2.7 mol; 160 g"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.060 mol, about 3.5 g."}],"trap":"Using 50 mM, the phosphate concentration, for the salt: each component has its own concentration."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"150 mM NaCl","note":"the salt asked about"},{"t":"58.5 g/mol","note":"for grams"},{"t":"400 mL","note":"convert to L"}]},{"text":"**Two lines.** mol = M × V(L); g = mol × molar mass. Keep the zeros straight by writing each quantity in base units."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to make a buffer, moles of each solute = its molarity × the volume in litres; multiply by its molar mass for the grams to weigh."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-25a","s":"Protein Structure","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe5-cp:25#1","pe5-cp:25#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:25"],"passage":"A recombinant kinase is purified through three columns for a drug screen. Mass spectrometry gives the mass predicted from its gene, its GST tag is retained, and it elutes from the last column as a single sharp peak. In activity assays, its kcat equals that of kinase isolated from liver.","q":"Which result best shows native folding?","choices":["Its kcat matches the liver enzyme","Its weight fits the gene sequence","Its GST tag survived purification","It went through three column steps"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Mass spectrometry weighs the chain, and an unfolded kinase has exactly the same mass."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A tag stays attached whether the chain is folded or not, so it says nothing about the fold."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Columns show purity, not structure; unfolded proteins pass through columns too."}],"rule":"Only a functional test, matching the native protein's activity, proves the fold; an unfolded chain keeps its mass, its tag and its purity.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Only activity proves the fold: a denatured chain keeps its mass, its tag and its purification history. Matching the liver enzyme's kcat."},"exp":"Turnover needs a properly built **active site**, so a kcat equal to the liver kinase's shows a **native-like fold**. Mass, the retained tag and the column steps would look the same for an unfolded chain.","how":{"ask":"Which of these results would come out differently if the kinase were unfolded?","know":"• The **native fold** (tertiary structure) builds the active site; an unfolded chain keeps the same sequence, mass and tags.\n• **kcat** (turnover number) counts catalytic events per active site per second, so it needs a correctly formed site.\n• Mass, a retained tag and the number of purification steps report identity and purity, not structure.","steps":[{"text":"Look for the one measurement that depends on 3-D shape.","hl":"best shows native folding"},{"text":"Mass spectrometry weighs the chain, and an unfolded kinase has exactly the same mass.","hl":"Mass spectrometry gives the mass predicted from its gene","strike":["Its weight fits the gene sequence"]},{"text":"A tag stays attached, and a protein passes through columns, whether or not it is folded.","strike":["Its GST tag survived purification","It went through three column steps"]},{"text":"Catalysis needs a correctly built active site, so matching the natural enzyme's kcat shows a native-like fold. Answer: its kcat matches the liver enzyme."}],"trap":"Trusting the exact mass: it proves the right chain was made, but a denatured chain weighs the same."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"mass predicted from its gene","note":"identity check"},{"t":"GST tag is retained","note":"tag check"},{"t":"kcat equals that of kinase isolated from liver","note":"function check"},{"t":"native folding","note":"correct 3-D shape"}]},{"text":"**Ask of each result: would an unfolded protein give the same answer?** If yes, it says nothing about the fold."},{"text":"**Also:** mass, a retained tag and the number of purification steps are identical for folded and unfolded protein, so none of them can show the fold."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** only a native-like function (the same kcat, the same ligand Kd) shows a purified protein is folded; mass, tags and purity do not."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-26a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:26#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:26"],"q":"In massive stars, fusing two ¹²C nuclei requires a far hotter core than fusing two ¹H nuclei does. Which statement best explains why?","choices":["Carbon nuclei repel far more strongly","Carbon nuclei feel a weaker strong force","Heavier nuclei move faster when heated","Carbon must first shed its electrons"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"At a given temperature, heavier nuclei move more slowly, which makes fusing them harder, not easier."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The strong force acts only at short range and is not what keeps nuclei apart before they touch."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"In a stellar core both gases are fully ionized plasmas; bare nuclei are present either way."}],"rule":"Fusion needs nuclei fast enough to beat their electric repulsion, which grows with the product of their charges (Z₁Z₂); bigger Z means a hotter plasma is needed.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Coulomb barrier ∝ Z₁Z₂: 1 × 1 for hydrogen versus 6 × 6 for carbon, a 36-fold larger barrier to climb."},"exp":"Nuclei must get within about a femtometre before the short-range strong force can bind them, and their **Coulomb repulsion** grows as Z₁Z₂. Two carbon nuclei (Z = 6) repel 36 times as strongly as two protons, so only much faster nuclei get close enough: a hotter core.","how":{"ask":"What keeps two nuclei apart, and how does it scale with their charge?","know":"• Nuclei are positive and repel (Coulomb), with a barrier ∝ Z₁Z₂.\n• The **strong force** binds nucleons but only within about 10⁻¹⁵ m.\n• Temperature sets the average kinetic energy; at the same T, heavier particles move more slowly.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the charge products.","hl":"two ¹²C nuclei","calc":["H + H: Z₁Z₂ = 1","C + C: Z₁Z₂ = 36"]},{"text":"At equal temperature, heavier nuclei are slower, not faster.","strike":["Heavier nuclei move faster when heated"]},{"text":"The strong force only matters once they are close; and stellar cores are fully ionized anyway.","strike":["Carbon nuclei feel a weaker strong force","Carbon must first shed its electrons"]},{"text":"Answer: carbon nuclei repel far more strongly."}],"trap":"Blaming the strong force: it is the electric repulsion, before contact, that sets the needed temperature."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"two ¹²C nuclei","note":"Z = 6 each"},{"t":"two ¹H nuclei","note":"Z = 1 each"},{"t":"far hotter core","note":"more kinetic energy needed"}]},{"text":"**Two forces.** Electric repulsion acts at long range and pushes nuclei apart; the strong force pulls only once they nearly touch. High temperature gives nuclei the speed to get through the repulsion."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** fusion needs very high temperatures because only very fast nuclei can push through their electric repulsion, and that barrier grows with the product of the charges."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-26b","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:26#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:26"],"q":"The strong nuclear force binds protons inside a nucleus, yet two free protons 10 nm apart only repel each other. Which property of the strong force explains this?","choices":["Its short range","Its weak strength","Its repulsive sign","Its charge dependence"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"False premise: the strong force is the strongest of the four forces, so weakness can't be it."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Wrong sign: between nucleons it attracts; that is why nuclei hold together."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The strong force acts the same on protons and neutrons; it doesn't depend on charge."}],"rule":"The strong force is the strongest and attractive, but it reaches only about a nucleus's width (~1 fm); farther apart, only Coulomb repulsion acts.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Strong force: attractive, strongest, but reaches only ~1 fm. 10 nm is about 10⁷ fm away, far outside its reach, so only Coulomb repulsion acts. Short range."},"exp":"The strong force attracts nucleons and overpowers Coulomb repulsion, but it acts **only over about a nucleon's width, a few femtometres (~10⁻¹⁵ m)**. At 10 nm, ten million times farther, it is effectively zero, so only the electric repulsion remains.","how":{"ask":"Decide why a force that wins inside a nucleus does nothing at a larger distance.","know":"• The **strong force** attracts protons and neutrons alike and is stronger than the electric force at nuclear distances.\n• It is **short-ranged**: it falls to nearly zero beyond a few × 10⁻¹⁵ m.\n• The electric force falls only as 1/r², so it dominates at larger distances.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the separation with the strong force's reach.","hl":"10 nm apart","calc":["10 nm = 10⁻⁸ m","10⁻⁸ m ÷ 10⁻¹⁵ m = 10⁷ times too far"]},{"text":"The strong force is the strongest force, is attractive, and acts the same on protons and neutrons.","strike":["Its weak strength","Its repulsive sign","Its charge dependence"]},{"text":"Answer: its short range."}],"trap":"Calling the strong force weak because it vanishes at distance: it is the strongest force, but only within about one nucleus."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"binds protons inside a nucleus","note":"wins at ~10⁻¹⁵ m"},{"t":"10 nm apart","note":"far beyond its reach"}]},{"text":"**Two forces, two ranges.** Coulomb repulsion reaches far; the strong attraction reaches only about a nucleon's width."},{"text":"**Where each wins.** Inside a nucleus the strong force wins; beyond about 10⁻¹⁵ m, repulsion wins.","calc":["r ≲ 10⁻¹⁵ m → strong force dominates","r ≫ 10⁻¹⁵ m → electric repulsion only"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the strong force beats Coulomb repulsion only within about 10⁻¹⁵ m; beyond that range, like charges simply repel."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-27a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:27#1","pe5-cp:27#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:27"],"q":"(R)-Carvone, shown with its mirror image (S)-carvone, smells of spearmint, while the S form smells of caraway. Which property differs between them?","fig":["cpt-pe5-r-carvone","cpt-pe5-s-carvone"],"choices":["Sign of rotation","Boiling temperature","Infrared spectrum","Solubility in ethanol"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Mirror-image molecules have the same intermolecular forces, so they boil at the same temperature."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"IR sees bonds and functional groups, and mirror images have identical bonds."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Ethanol is achiral, so it interacts with both mirror images identically; their solubility is the same."}],"rule":"Enantiomers match in every achiral property (bp, IR, solubility in achiral solvents); they differ only toward polarized light or other chiral things.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Mirror-image pair = identical in every achiral test (bp, IR, solubility in ethanol). Only plane-polarized light (or a chiral receptor, like smell) tells them apart. Sign of rotation."},"exp":"The two forms are **enantiomers** (mirror images at one stereocenter). In an achiral setting they share boiling point, density, solubility and IR spectrum; they differ only in the **sign of optical rotation** and in how they bind chiral partners, such as the protein odor receptors that tell them apart.","how":{"ask":"Decide which properties can tell two mirror-image molecules apart.","know":"• **Enantiomers** have identical physical properties in an achiral environment: bp, mp, density, solubility in achiral solvents, IR and NMR.\n• They rotate plane-polarized light by **equal amounts in opposite directions**.\n• They interact differently with **chiral** things: enzymes, receptors, chiral columns.","steps":[{"text":"R and S at the same single stereocenter, with the same connectivity, make the two forms enantiomers.","hl":"shown with its mirror image (S)-carvone"},{"text":"Boiling point, IR and solubility in an achiral solvent such as ethanol are the same for both.","strike":["Boiling temperature","Infrared spectrum","Solubility in ethanol"]},{"text":"Answer: the sign of optical rotation."}],"trap":"Concluding from the different smells that the molecules differ in ordinary physical properties: the odor difference comes from chiral receptors."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"mirror image","note":"enantiomers"},{"t":"smells of spearmint","note":"chiral receptor response"}]},{"text":"**Classify the pair.** Same connectivity, opposite configuration at the one stereocenter: enantiomers."},{"text":"**Achiral tests can't tell them apart.** Heat, solvents like ethanol and IR light treat both the same."},{"text":"**Also:** enantiomers smell or act differently because odor receptors and enzymes are made of L-amino acids and are therefore chiral, binding each form differently."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** enantiomers share bp, density, solubility and IR; they differ only in the sign of optical rotation and in interactions with chiral partners."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-28a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:28#1","pe5-cp:28#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:28"],"q":"Water flows at 0.40 m/s through a hose of 2.4 cm inner diameter. What is its speed where the hose narrows to 0.80 cm?","choices":["3.6 m/s","1.2 m/s","0.044 m/s","0.40 m/s"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Divides by the area ratio, making the water slow down where the pipe narrows."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Scales speed by the diameter ratio alone and skips squaring it; flow depends on area."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Speed can't stay the same: the same volume per second must pass through a smaller area."}],"rule":"Continuity: A₁v₁ = A₂v₂ and A ∝ d², so v₂ = v₁(d₁/d₂)². Narrower means faster, by the square of the diameter ratio.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Diameter ratio 2.4/0.80 = 3, so the area falls 9-fold and the speed rises 9-fold from 0.40 m/s."},"exp":"For an incompressible fluid, A₁v₁ = A₂v₂. Since A = πd²/4, v₂ = v₁(d₁/d₂)² = 0.40 × (2.4/0.80)² = 0.40 × 9 = **3.6 m/s**.","how":{"ask":"By what factor does the cross-sectional area shrink, and how does that change the speed?","know":"• **Continuity**: volume flow rate Q = Av is the same everywhere in a pipe.\n• **A = πd²/4**, so area scales with diameter squared.\n• Halving the diameter quarters the area and makes the fluid 4 times faster.","steps":[{"text":"Find the diameter ratio.","hl":"2.4 cm inner diameter","calc":["d₁/d₂ = 2.4/0.80 = 3"]},{"text":"Square it for the area ratio, and scale the speed up by that factor.","calc":["A₁/A₂ = 3² = 9","v₂ = 0.40 × 9 = 3.6 m/s"]},{"text":"Using the diameter ratio unsquared, or dividing by the area ratio, gets the size or the direction wrong.","strike":["1.2 m/s","0.044 m/s"]},{"text":"The same flow through less area can't keep the same speed.","strike":["0.40 m/s"]},{"text":"Answer: 3.6 m/s."}],"trap":"Scaling by the diameter ratio instead of its square: flow depends on area."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"0.40 m/s","note":"v₁"},{"t":"2.4 cm inner diameter","note":"d₁"},{"t":"narrows to 0.80 cm","note":"d₂"}]},{"text":"**Continuity.** What enters each second must leave each second: A₁v₁ = A₂v₂.","calc":["v₂ = v₁ × (d₁/d₂)²"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a narrowing pipe, speed rises by the square of the diameter ratio (halve d → 4× v)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-29a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:29#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:29"],"q":"Which of the following ions has the same ground-state electron configuration as a noble gas?","choices":["Se²⁻","Fe³⁺","Cu⁺","Sn²⁺"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Odd count: 26 − 3 = 23 electrons, and every noble-gas count (2, 10, 18, 36, 54) is even."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"29 − 1 = 28 electrons falls between Ar (18) and Kr (36), not on a noble-gas count."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"50 − 2 = 48 electrons ([Kr]4d¹⁰5s²) sits between Kr (36) and Xe (54)."}],"rule":"Count electrons as Z − charge (add for anions) and compare with the noble-gas counts 2, 10, 18, 36, 54, 86.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Electrons = Z − charge; noble counts 2, 10, 18, 36, 54, 86. Se²⁻: 34 + 2 = 36 (Kr) ✓. Fe³⁺ 23, Cu⁺ 28, Sn²⁺ 48: none."},"exp":"**Electrons = Z − charge.** Se²⁻: 34 + 2 = **36**, the count of krypton. Fe³⁺ has 23, Cu⁺ 28 and Sn²⁺ 48, none a noble-gas count (2, 10, 18, 36, 54); a half-filled or full d subshell is not a noble-gas configuration.","how":{"ask":"Count each ion's electrons and compare with the noble-gas counts.","know":"• **Electrons = Z − charge** (a 2− ion has Z + 2).\n• Noble-gas electron counts: **2 (He), 10 (Ne), 18 (Ar), 36 (Kr), 54 (Xe)**.\n• Transition-metal ions lose s electrons first but keep their d electrons, so they rarely match a noble gas.","steps":[{"text":"Count electrons in each ion.","hl":"same ground-state electron configuration as a noble gas","calc":["Se²⁻: 34 + 2 = **36** = Kr ✓","Fe³⁺: 26 − 3 = 23","Cu⁺: 29 − 1 = 28","Sn²⁺: 50 − 2 = 48"]},{"text":"23, 28 and 48 are not noble-gas counts: Fe³⁺ is [Ar]3d⁵, Cu⁺ is [Ar]3d¹⁰, and Sn²⁺ is [Kr]4d¹⁰5s².","strike":["Fe³⁺","Cu⁺","Sn²⁺"]},{"text":"Answer: Se²⁻."}],"trap":"Choosing an ion with a half-filled or full d subshell: those are stable, but not noble-gas configurations."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"noble gas","note":"2, 10, 18, 36, 54 electrons"},{"t":"ground-state","note":"count electrons"}]},{"text":"**Count electrons.** Subtract a positive charge from Z; add a negative charge to it.","calc":["Se: Z = 34, charge 2− → 36"]},{"text":"**Match a noble gas.** 36 electrons is krypton's count, [Ar]3d¹⁰4s²4p⁶."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** electrons = Z − charge, and an ion is noble-gas-like only if that count is 2, 10, 18, 36 or 54."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-30a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe5-cp:30#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:30"],"passage":"A reagent tags tyrosines in a small enzyme that has three of them, Y21, Y64 and Y118. The percent of each tyrosine tagged was measured by mass spectrometry in two buffers. Buffer A: Y21 70%, Y64 25%, Y118 none. Buffer B: Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none.","q":"Which tyrosine can be tagged selectively, and in which buffer?","choices":["Y21, in buffer A","Y64, in buffer A","Y64, in buffer B","Y118, in buffer B"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"In buffer A another tyrosine is tagged too (25%), so Y21 is never tagged alone there."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"In buffer A that residue is tagged alongside Y21 (70%), so it isn't tagged alone there."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Y118 reads none in both buffers; a residue never tagged can't be tagged selectively."}],"rule":"Selective means one residue tagged while every other reads none: scan each buffer for a column with a single nonzero value.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Selective = exactly one nonzero in a buffer's column. Buffer A tags Y21 and Y64 together; buffer B tags only Y64. Y64, in buffer B."},"exp":"**Selective** means one residue reacts while every other residue reads zero. In buffer B only **Y64** is tagged (50%); in buffer A, Y21 and Y64 are tagged together, and Y118 is never tagged.","how":{"ask":"Under which buffer does exactly one tyrosine react while the others stay at zero?","know":"• **Selective modification**: under one condition, the target is labeled and **every other residue reads 0%**.\n• Read the data **one condition at a time**; a high percentage alone is not enough if another residue is also labeled.","steps":[{"text":"Read buffer A as one row: two tyrosines are tagged.","hl":"Y21 70%, Y64 25%, Y118 none","calc":["buffer A: Y21 and Y64 both > 0 → **not selective**"]},{"text":"So nothing tagged in buffer A is tagged alone: Y21 brings Y64 with it, and Y64 brings Y21.","strike":["Y21, in buffer A","Y64, in buffer A"]},{"text":"Read buffer B as one row: only one tyrosine reacts.","hl":"Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none","calc":["buffer B: Y64 > 0, Y21 = 0, Y118 = 0 → **Y64 alone**"]},{"text":"Y118 reads none in both buffers, so it is never tagged at all, selectively or otherwise.","strike":["Y118, in buffer B"]},{"text":"Answer: Y64, in buffer B."}],"trap":"Picking the largest percentage (Y21 at 70%): it is tagged alongside Y64, so it is not selective."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what each number means**.","marks":[{"t":"Buffer A","note":"first condition"},{"t":"Y21 70%, Y64 25%, Y118 none","note":"two residues react"},{"t":"Buffer B","note":"second condition"},{"t":"selectively","note":"only one reacts"}]},{"text":"**Define the target.** A selective condition labels the residue of interest while all the others read zero. Size of the percentage doesn't matter; exclusivity does."},{"text":"**Scan each condition across.** Count the nonzero entries in each row: one nonzero entry is a selective condition for that residue.","calc":["buffer A: 2 nonzero → none selective","buffer B: 1 nonzero (Y64) → Y64 selective"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to find a selective condition, read the table one condition at a time and pick the condition where only the target residue reacts and every other residue reads 0%."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-31a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:31#1","pe5-cp:31#2","pe5-cp:31#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:31"],"passage":"To add a charge near a binding site, chemists replace a phenylalanine with 4-carboxyphenylalanine, whose ring carries a –COOH opposite the CH₂. Phenylalanine's side chain and benzoic acid, a model of the new ring, are drawn. The ring –COOH has a pKa of about 4.2. Binding is measured at pH 7.0.","q":"At pH 7.0, the side chain of the new residue is best categorized as:","fig":["phenylalanine-sc","p1-benzoic-acid"],"choices":["Acidic","Basic","Hydrophobic","Polar neutral"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"A –COOH donates its proton and turns negative; basic groups accept a proton and turn positive."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skips the pKa check: nearly three units above its pKa, the –COOH is almost fully ionized, so it is charged."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The ring alone is nonpolar, but an ionized carboxylate makes the whole side chain charged and water-loving."}],"rule":"Classify a modified side chain by its new group's pKa versus the pH: an acid with pKa < pH is ionized (acidic, −); a base with pKa > pH is protonated (basic, +).","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"pKa 4.2 vs pH 7.0: the pH is higher, so the –COOH has lost its proton. A side chain carrying –COO⁻ is acidic."},"exp":"With pH 7.0 well above the pKa of 4.2, the ring –COOH is about 99.8% ionized to **–COO⁻**, so the side chain carries a negative charge, like Asp and Glu: it is **acidic**.","how":{"ask":"Is the added group an acid or a base, and is it ionized at pH 7.0?","know":"• **pH > pKa**: the group is mostly deprotonated; **pH < pKa**: mostly protonated. Each unit is a factor of 10.\n• An ionized –COOH (–COO⁻) makes a side chain **acidic** (Asp, Glu).\n• A protonated amine (–NH₃⁺) makes it **basic** (Lys, Arg).","steps":[{"text":"Compare the pKa with the pH.","hl":"pKa of about 4.2","calc":["7.0 − 4.2 = 2.8 units above","COO⁻ : COOH ≈ 10^2.8 ≈ 600 : 1"]},{"text":"An acid that has given up its proton is negative, not positive.","strike":["Basic"]},{"text":"It is charged, so it is neither neutral nor hydrophobic.","strike":["Polar neutral","Hydrophobic"]},{"text":"Answer: acidic."}],"trap":"Calling it hydrophobic because the side chain is mostly a benzene ring: one ionized group outweighs the ring."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"carries a –COOH","note":"an acid added"},{"t":"pKa of about 4.2","note":"compare with the pH"},{"t":"pH 7.0","note":"above the pKa"}]},{"text":"**Compare pKa with pH.** Above the pKa, the group has lost its proton; below it, it keeps it."},{"text":"**Also:** an added amine works the other way. A ring –CH₂NH₂ whose –NH₃⁺ form has pKa ≈ 9.5 stays protonated at pH 7.0, so that side chain is basic (+)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to classify a modified residue, compare the new group's pKa with the pH: an ionized acid makes it acidic, a protonated base makes it basic, and an un-ionized polar group makes it polar neutral."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-32a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:32#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:32"],"passage":"A bacterial hydrolase was assayed in buffers from pH 5 to pH 11. Its activity falls as the pH rises, as plotted, and drops to half near pH 8.3. Its catalytic residue must be protonated to act, and mutating that residue to alanine abolishes activity. No other residue in the active site has a side-chain pKa between 7 and 9.","q":"The catalytic acid is most likely which residue?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Activity of the enzyme versus pH","x":{"label":"pH","min":5,"max":11,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Relative activity (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Activity","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[5,99],[6,98],[6.5,97],[7,95],[7.5,86],[8.3,50],[8.8,22],[9.3,8],[9.8,3],[10.3,1],[11,0]]}]},"choices":["Cysteine","Tyrosine","Aspartate","Arginine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Tyrosine (pKa ≈ 10.1) would stay protonated, and active, until about pH 10, above where this curve drops."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Aspartate (pKa ≈ 3.9) loses its H⁺ near pH 4, far below where this activity drops."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Arginine (pKa ≈ 12.5) would stay protonated, and active, across the whole pH range plotted."}],"rule":"Activity that needs a protonated residue drops near that residue's pKa: read the pH of the drop, then match it (Asp ≈ 4, His ≈ 6, Tyr ≈ 10, Arg ≈ 12.5).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Activity drops where the needed H⁺ leaves, at pH ≈ pKa. The drop here sits near pH 8, matching Cys –SH (~8.3); Asp ~4, Tyr ~10, Arg ~12.5 don't fit."},"exp":"Activity tracks how much of the residue is still protonated, so the **pH at 50% activity equals its pKa**. On this plot that is **pH ≈ 8.3**, which matches cysteine's thiol (–SH, pKa ≈ 8.3). Asp (≈ 3.9), Tyr (≈ 10.1) and Arg (≈ 12.5) would put the midpoint elsewhere.","how":{"ask":"Read the pH at half activity; that pH is the pKa of the residue. Which side chain has that pKa?","know":"• When activity depends on one ionizable group, the **pH at 50% activity = that group's pKa** (Henderson–Hasselbalch: half protonated at pH = pKa).\n• Side-chain pKa values: **Asp ≈ 3.9, Glu ≈ 4.1, His ≈ 6.0, Cys ≈ 8.3, Tyr ≈ 10.1, Lys ≈ 10.5, Arg ≈ 12.5**.\n• A falling curve: the group must be **protonated** (an acid); a rising curve: it must be deprotonated (a base).","steps":[{"text":"The curve falls with pH because the residue loses its proton as pH rises.","hl":"must be protonated to act"},{"text":"Read the midpoint: drop from 50% activity to the x-axis.","calc":["50% activity at **pH ≈ 8.3**","pKa ≈ 8.3"]},{"text":"Aspartate (pKa ≈ 3.9) would lose its proton at much lower pH; its midpoint would sit near 4.","strike":["Aspartate"]},{"text":"Tyrosine (≈ 10.1) and arginine (≈ 12.5) stay protonated past pH 9, so their activity would not drop until pH 10 or higher.","strike":["Tyrosine","Arginine"]},{"text":"Answer: cysteine."}],"trap":"Thinking a residue that must be protonated has to be basic, like arginine: the midpoint, not the direction, identifies the residue."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"falls as the pH rises","note":"loses a proton"},{"t":"must be protonated","note":"acts as an acid"},{"t":"catalytic acid","note":"donates H⁺"}]},{"text":"**Midpoint = pKa.** At pH = pKa a group is half protonated, so an enzyme that needs it protonated runs at half speed there.","calc":["pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA])","at pH = pKa: [A⁻] = [HA] → 50%"]},{"text":"**Match the pKa.** A midpoint near 8.3 fits cysteine's –SH; Asp/Glu sit near 4, His near 6, Lys near 10.5."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the pH at half activity is the catalytic group's pKa; match it to a side chain: Asp/Glu ≈ 4, His ≈ 6, Cys ≈ 8.3, Lys ≈ 10.5."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-32b","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:32#1","pe5-cp:32#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:32"],"passage":"A fluorescent probe binds a pocket of a membrane protein lined by ring-bearing residues. Mutating either a tryptophan or a proline in the pocket blocks binding. Tryptophan and proline each carry a ring in their side chains, and the probe stacks on the larger ring while the smaller one kinks the backbone. Neither ring is a nucleotide base.","q":"Which pair names those two rings, listed in that order?","choices":["Indole; pyrrolidine","Imidazole; pyrrolidine","Indole; pyrimidine","Imidazole; pyrimidine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Imidazole is histidine's ring (two N atoms in a five-membered ring), not tryptophan's."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Pyrimidine is a nucleobase ring (C, T, U); no amino-acid side chain carries one."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Wrong twice: imidazole is histidine's ring, and pyrimidines belong to nucleobases."}],"rule":"Trp: a benzene fused to a five-ring with one N. Pro: a saturated five-ring with one N. His: a five-ring with two N (imidazole).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Trp = indole (benzene fused to a one-N five-ring); Pro = pyrrolidine (saturated one-N five-ring). Imidazole is His; pyrimidine is a nucleobase ring. Indole; pyrrolidine."},"exp":"Tryptophan's side chain is an **indole** (a benzene ring fused to a five-membered N–H ring). Proline's ring is a **pyrrolidine**, a saturated five-membered ring that includes the backbone nitrogen. Imidazole belongs to histidine, and pyrimidines are nucleobases (C, T, U), never side chains.","how":{"ask":"Name the ring on tryptophan, then the ring on proline.","know":"• **His → imidazole** (5-membered, 2 N, aromatic).\n• **Trp → indole** (benzene fused to a 5-membered N–H ring; 9 ring atoms).\n• **Pro → pyrrolidine** (5-membered, saturated, the backbone N is in the ring).\n• **Phe, Tyr → benzene** (phenyl).\n• **Pyrimidines** (cytosine, thymine, uracil) and **purines** (A, G) are nucleobases, not amino-acid side chains.","steps":[{"text":"Tryptophan: a fused two-ring system with one N–H, the indole.","hl":"Tryptophan","fig":"tryptophan"},{"text":"Imidazole is histidine's ring (two N atoms in a five-membered ring), not tryptophan's.","strike":["Imidazole; pyrrolidine","Imidazole; pyrimidine"]},{"text":"Proline: its side chain loops back onto its own α-amino N, closing a saturated five-membered ring, the pyrrolidine.","hl":"proline","fig":"proline"},{"text":"A pyrimidine is a six-membered ring with two N atoms found in nucleic-acid bases; no amino acid carries one.","strike":["Indole; pyrimidine"]},{"text":"Answer: indole; pyrrolidine."}],"trap":"Picking pyrimidine because it sounds like pyrrolidine: pyrimidines are DNA/RNA bases, never side chains."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Tryptophan and proline","note":"two ring residues"},{"t":"in that order","note":"Trp first, then Pro"}]},{"text":"**Ring residues and their rings.** Count the ring atoms and nitrogens to tell them apart.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Side-chain rings of the ring-bearing amino acids","x":{"label":"Residue (ring)"},"y":{"label":"Count","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"categories":["His (imidazole)","Trp (indole)","Pro (pyrrolidine)","Phe (benzene)"],"series":[{"name":"Ring atoms","values":[5,9,5,6]},{"name":"Ring nitrogens","values":[2,1,1,0]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** pyrimidines (cytosine, thymine, uracil) and purines (adenine, guanine) are nucleobases in DNA and RNA. A choice that names one as a side-chain ring is always wrong."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** His = imidazole, Trp = indole, Pro = pyrrolidine, Phe/Tyr = benzene; pyrimidines and purines are nucleobases, never amino-acid side chains."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-33a","s":"Protein Structure","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:33#1","pe6-cp:31#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:33"],"passage":"A tryptophan buried in the core of a bacterial enzyme does not react with a tryptophan-modifying reagent at 20 °C. After the enzyme is held at 70 °C, with no reducing agent or protease in the buffer, the tryptophan reacts. Mass spectrometry shows the unreacted, heated chain has exactly its original mass. In a control, free tryptophan reacts with the reagent equally fast at both temperatures.","q":"Which best explains why the tryptophan reacts after heating?","choices":["Noncovalent contacts broke","The backbone was cut","A disulfide was reduced","Heat made the reagent more reactive"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Hydrolysis adds water and splits the chain, so the mass would change; it stayed the same, with no protease."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"No reducing agent was present, and breaking an S–S bond by reduction adds two H, changing the mass."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The control shows the reagent reacts equally fast at both temperatures, so its reactivity did not change."}],"rule":"Heat denatures a protein by breaking weak noncovalent interactions; if the mass is unchanged and nothing was added to cut bonds, no covalent bond broke.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Same mass + no reductant + no protease = no covalent change. The only thing left that heat can break is the network of weak interactions holding the fold."},"exp":"Heating to 70 °C **denatures** the enzyme: it breaks the **H-bonds, hydrophobic contacts and salt bridges** that hold the fold, so the buried tryptophan becomes exposed. No covalent bond broke, which is why the mass is unchanged; the control rules out a change in the reagent.","how":{"ask":"Which explanation fits all three facts: reaction after heating, unchanged mass, and an unchanged reagent?","know":"• **Denaturation** (heat, urea, pH extremes) breaks noncovalent interactions and unfolds a protein; the covalent chain stays intact.\n• **Hydrolysis** of peptide bonds or **reduction** of disulfides changes the chain chemically (and the mass).\n• Buried residues are shielded by the fold; unfolding exposes them.","steps":[{"text":"Same mass, no protease: the backbone is intact.","hl":"exactly its original mass","strike":["The backbone was cut"]},{"text":"No reducing agent, and a reduction would change the mass too.","hl":"no reducing agent","strike":["A disulfide was reduced"]},{"text":"The control removes the reagent as the cause.","hl":"equally fast at both temperatures","strike":["Heat made the reagent more reactive"]},{"text":"Answer: noncovalent contacts broke, unfolding the enzyme and exposing the tryptophan."}],"trap":"Assuming heat must break covalent bonds to expose a residue: unfolding alone does it."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"buried in the core","note":"shielded by the fold"},{"t":"exactly its original mass","note":"no covalent change"},{"t":"free tryptophan reacts with the reagent equally fast","note":"reagent unchanged"}]},{"text":"**Weak bonds hold the fold.** Tertiary structure rests on many weak interactions; heat breaks them and the chain unfolds, while peptide and disulfide bonds survive."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** when heating exposes a buried residue with no change in mass, the protein has unfolded (denatured) through broken noncovalent interactions, not through any covalent cleavage."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-34a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:34#1","pe6-cp:50#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:34"],"passage":"Mass spectrometry of proteins needs buffers that evaporate completely. A lab makes one by mixing two volatile compounds: Methylamine, CH₃NH₂, is neutralized with formic acid, HCOOH, to make a volatile buffer salt. The proton moves from the acid to the amine nitrogen. When the sample dries, the salt breaks back down into the two gases.","q":"Which salt is in the buffer?","choices":["Methylammonium formate","Methylammonium acetate","Dimethylammonium formate","Methyl formate"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Formic acid gives formate (HCOO⁻); acetate would need acetic acid."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Dimethylammonium needs a base with two methyls, (CH₃)₂NH; methylamine has only one."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An ester, not a salt: an acid plus an amine swap a proton and make ions, not an ester."}],"rule":"Acid + amine = proton transfer: name the protonated amine (-ammonium) plus the acid's anion (-ate), each keeping its own carbons.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Amine + acid = H⁺ hand-off: CH₃NH₂ → methylammonium, HCOOH → formate (formic ↔ formate, acetic ↔ acetate). Methylammonium formate; methyl formate would be an ester."},"exp":"The base takes H⁺ from the acid: **CH₃NH₂ + HCOOH → CH₃NH₃⁺ + HCOO⁻**. The salt is named cation first: **methylammonium formate**.","how":{"ask":"Move one H⁺ from the acid to the base, then name the cation and the anion.","know":"• **Acid + base → salt** of the base's conjugate acid (cation) and the acid's conjugate base (anion).\n• An amine RNH₂ becomes RNH₃⁺ (ammonium-type cation); HCOOH becomes HCOO⁻ (formate).\n• Salts are named **cation, then anion**.","steps":[{"text":"Transfer the proton.","hl":"CH₃NH₂, is neutralized with formic acid, HCOOH","calc":["CH₃NH₂ + HCOOH → CH₃NH₃⁺ + HCOO⁻"]},{"text":"Name the ions: CH₃NH₃⁺ is methylammonium; HCOO⁻ is formate."},{"text":"Acetate needs acetic acid, dimethylammonium needs a base with two methyls, (CH₃)₂NH, and methyl formate is an ester, not a salt.","strike":["Methylammonium acetate","Dimethylammonium formate","Methyl formate"]},{"text":"Answer: methylammonium formate."}],"trap":"Picking the ester: neutralization moves a proton and makes ions; an ester needs an alcohol and a condensation."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Methylamine","note":"the base"},{"t":"formic acid","note":"the acid"},{"t":"neutralized","note":"proton transfer"}]},{"text":"**Move one proton.** The acid gives H⁺; the base accepts it.","calc":["CH₃NH₂ + H⁺ → CH₃NH₃⁺","HCOOH − H⁺ → HCOO⁻"]},{"text":"**Name cation, then anion.** methylammonium + formate."},{"text":"**Also:** a weak base plus a weak acid gives the salt of their conjugates: NH₃ + HCN → NH₄⁺ + CN⁻; name the cation first, then the anion: ammonium cyanide."},{"text":"**Also:** an ionic compound is named cation, then anion, with no Greek prefixes (those belong to molecular compounds such as N₂O₄, dinitrogen tetroxide). NH₄⁺ is ammonium, never \"ammonia\" (ammonia is the neutral base NH₃), and a one-atom anion ends in -ide.","calc":["NH₃ + HBr → NH₄⁺ + Br⁻: ammonium bromide","NH₄⁺ with NO₃⁻: ammonium nitrate"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** acid + base → a salt of the base's conjugate acid and the acid's conjugate base, named cation then anion."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-34b","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:34#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:34"],"passage":"Ion-exchange columns are often eluted with simple organic salts. A lab makes one by neutralizing formic acid, HCOOH, the simplest carboxylic acid, with sodium hydroxide, NaOH. The acid's proton goes to hydroxide, forming water, and the salt stays dissolved. Use standard atomic masses, with Na = 23 g/mol.","q":"What are the salt's formula and molar mass?","choices":["HCOONa, 68 g/mol","CH₃COONa, 82 g/mol","NaHCO₃, 84 g/mol","Na₂CO₃, 106 g/mol"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"CH₃COO⁻ is acetate, from acetic acid; formic acid has an H, not a CH₃, on its carboxyl carbon."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"HCO₃⁻ is bicarbonate, with three O; formate has two O and its H bonded to carbon."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"CO₃²⁻ is carbonate, a 2− ion with three O; a monoprotic acid gives a 1− anion and one Na⁺."}],"rule":"Acid + base → salt: keep the acid's anion (formate HCOO⁻, acetate CH₃COO⁻) and the base's cation; then add atomic masses.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Formic acid minus its H⁺ is formate, HCOO⁻; plus Na⁺: HCOONa. Mass ≈ 1 + 12 + 32 + 23."},"exp":"Removing the acidic H from HCOOH gives **formate, HCOO⁻**; pairing it with Na⁺ gives **sodium formate, HCOONa**. Molar mass = 1 + 12 + 2(16) + 23 = **68 g/mol**. Acetate, bicarbonate and carbonate are different anions.","how":{"ask":"Which anion does formic acid give, and what does the sodium salt weigh?","know":"• **Formate** HCOO⁻ (from HCOOH); **acetate** CH₃COO⁻ (from CH₃COOH).\n• **Bicarbonate** HCO₃⁻ and **carbonate** CO₃²⁻ come from carbonic acid; both have three O.\n• Neutralization: HA + NaOH → NaA + H₂O.","steps":[{"text":"Write the neutralization.","hl":"formic acid, HCOOH","calc":["HCOOH + NaOH → HCOONa + H₂O"]},{"text":"The anion is formate, not acetate, bicarbonate or carbonate.","strike":["CH₃COONa, 82 g/mol","NaHCO₃, 84 g/mol","Na₂CO₃, 106 g/mol"]},{"text":"Add the atomic masses.","calc":["H 1 + C 12 + O₂ 32 + Na 23 = 68 g/mol"]},{"text":"Answer: HCOONa, 68 g/mol."}],"trap":"Writing formate as HCO₃⁻: the H of formate is on carbon, and there are only two oxygens."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"formic acid, HCOOH","note":"anion = HCOO⁻"},{"t":"sodium hydroxide, NaOH","note":"cation = Na⁺"},{"t":"Na = 23 g/mol","note":"for the molar mass"}]},{"text":"**Know the look-alikes.** HCOO⁻ (formate, 2 O), CH₃COO⁻ (acetate, 2 O), HCO₃⁻ (bicarbonate, 3 O), CO₃²⁻ (carbonate, 3 O, 2−)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the salt of a neutralization is the acid's conjugate base paired with the base's cation (ammonium acetate from acetic acid + ammonia, sodium formate from formic acid + NaOH)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-35a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:35#1","pe5-cp:35#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:35"],"passage":"A basic antimicrobial enzyme from egg white is studied in its native form and after succinylation, which caps one lysine with a –CO–CH₂CH₂–COO⁻ group. At pH 5.5, the native protein (N) carries net charge +9, while the succinylated form (S) carries +7. The two forms are within 0.5% of each other in mass. A mixture of the two forms must be resolved on one column eluted with a rising salt gradient.","q":"Which column separates N from S, and which form leaves the column first?","choices":["Cation exchange; S elutes first","Cation exchange; N elutes first","Anion exchange; S elutes first","Size exclusion; N elutes first"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"The more positive form binds a negative resin more tightly, so it needs more salt and comes off later."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Both forms are positive at this pH, so neither sticks to the + resin of an anion exchanger."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Masses within half a percent run together on a size column; the forms differ in charge, not size."}],"rule":"Forms that differ in charge but not size: use ion exchange matched to their sign; the form with less charge of that sign elutes first as salt rises.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Both forms are positive, so use a cation exchanger. The form with fewer positive charges holds on less tightly and is displaced first by the rising salt."},"exp":"Both forms are **positive** at pH 5.5, so they bind a **cation exchanger** (negative resin). Salt displaces the weaker binder first: the **succinylated form (+7)** elutes before the native form (+9). Their masses are nearly equal, so size exclusion cannot separate them.","how":{"ask":"What sign of charge do both forms carry, which resin binds that sign, and which form binds more weakly?","know":"• **Cation exchanger**: negative resin, binds positive proteins. **Anion exchanger**: positive resin, binds negative proteins.\n• A rising salt gradient displaces the **least** charged binder first.\n• **Size exclusion** separates by size only.","steps":[{"text":"Read the charges and the mass difference.","hl":"net charge +9","calc":["native +9, succinylated +7 → both positive","mass difference < 0.5% → not a size separation"]},{"text":"Positive proteins bind a negative resin: a cation exchanger.","strike":["Anion exchange; S elutes first"]},{"text":"Near-identical masses rule out size exclusion.","strike":["Size exclusion; N elutes first"]},{"text":"The +7 form binds less tightly, so it leaves at lower salt; the +9 form comes off later.","strike":["Cation exchange; N elutes first"]},{"text":"Answer: cation exchange, with S eluting first."}],"trap":"Reasoning that the added carboxylate makes the enzyme \"negative\" and choosing an anion exchanger: the net charge is still positive."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"net charge +9","note":"native: positive"},{"t":"succinylated form (S) carries +7","note":"less positive"},{"t":"within 0.5% of each other in mass","note":"size won't work"}]},{"text":"**Match the resin to the sign.** A protein sticks to a resin of opposite charge; the more charge it carries, the more salt it takes to remove it."},{"text":"**Also:** at a pH where both forms are negative (say pH 8, net −3 and −5), the same pair would go on an anion exchanger, and the more negative modified form would bind more tightly and elute later."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to separate a protein from a form that differs only in charge, use ion exchange of the sign both carry; the form with less net charge of that sign elutes first."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-36a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:36#1","pe5-cp:36#2","pe5-cp:36#3","pe5-cp:36#4"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:36"],"passage":"A fruity-smelling liquid isolated from a ripe banana extract has the formula C₄H₈O₂. Its IR spectrum shows a strong, sharp band at 1742 cm⁻¹ and another strong band near 1190 cm⁻¹. There is no broad band anywhere from 2500 to 3600 cm⁻¹. The four candidate structures are drawn as the answer choices.","q":"Based on the passage's data, which compound is the unknown?","choices":["Compound W","Compound X","Compound Y","Compound Z"],"choiceFigs":["butanal","cpt-pe5-2-butanone","cpt-pe5-butanoic-acid","cpt-pe5-methyl-propanoate"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Compound W, an aldehyde, has only one O (C₄H₈O), so it can't match the formula."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Compound X, a ketone, also has one O, and its C=O absorbs lower, near 1715 cm⁻¹."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Compound Y fits the formula, but a carboxylic acid shows a very broad O–H band, which is missing."}],"rule":"Name the carbonyl by what the C=O carbon carries: H (aldehyde), C (ketone), OH (acid), OR (ester). Then check formula and IR: an acid needs a broad O–H; an ester a high C=O and a C–O band.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Two O in the formula leaves the acid and the ester. No broad O–H band rules out the acid."},"exp":"C₄H₈O₂ has two O, so only the acid (Y) and the ester (Z) fit. A carboxylic acid would show a **very broad O–H band** (about 2500–3300 cm⁻¹); none is present. A C=O near 1740 cm⁻¹ plus a strong C–O band near 1200 cm⁻¹ marks an **ester**: Z, methyl propanoate.","how":{"ask":"Which drawing has two O, a C=O, and no O–H?","know":"• **Ketone**: C=O with C on both sides (≈ 1715 cm⁻¹).\n• **Aldehyde**: C=O with an H (≈ 1725; weak C–H pair just below 2900).\n• **Carboxylic acid**: C=O with OH (≈ 1710; very broad O–H 2500–3300).\n• **Ester**: C=O with OR (≈ 1740; strong C–O 1150–1250).","steps":[{"text":"Use the formula first: two O.","hl":"C₄H₈O₂","strike":["Compound W","Compound X"]},{"text":"No broad O–H band, so no carboxylic acid.","hl":"no broad band anywhere from 2500 to 3600 cm⁻¹","strike":["Compound Y"]},{"text":"A high C=O and a strong C–O band fit the ester.","hl":"strong, sharp band at 1742 cm⁻¹","fig":"cpt-pe5-methyl-propanoate"},{"text":"Answer: compound Z, the ester methyl propanoate."}],"trap":"Picking the acid because it matches the formula: a carboxylic acid always shows its broad O–H band."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"C₄H₈O₂","note":"two oxygens"},{"t":"1742 cm⁻¹","note":"ester C=O"},{"t":"no broad band","note":"no O–H"}]},{"text":"**Look past the O.** The carbonyl carbon has two other bonds; name the group from those two neighbors. Here, a carbon and an –OCH₃: an ester.","fig":"cpt-pe5-methyl-propanoate"},{"text":"**Also:** carbon on both sides of the carbonyl carbon makes a **ketone**, as in 2-butanone (CH₃COCH₂CH₃).","fig":"cpt-pe5-2-butanone"},{"text":"**Also:** an H on the carbonyl carbon makes an **aldehyde** (butanal), and an –OH makes a **carboxylic acid** (butanoic acid).","fig":"butanal"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** identify a carbonyl compound from what is bonded to the C=O carbon (H, C, OH or OR), and confirm with the formula and IR: broad O–H for an acid, about 1740 plus C–O for an ester."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-37a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:37#1","pe5-cp:37#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:37"],"passage":"Nitric oxide for a cell-signaling study is made by burning ammonia. A rigid flask at 298 K holds NH₃ and O₂ in a 4:5 mole ratio at 5.4 atm. The mixture is sparked, and 4 NH₃(g) + 5 O₂(g) → 4 NO(g) + 6 H₂O(l) goes to completion. The flask is then brought back to its starting temperature, where the water is liquid, H₂O(l), with negligible vapor pressure.","q":"What pressure remains in the flask?","choices":["2.4 atm","6.0 atm","5.4 atm","12 atm"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Ratio flipped: gas moles drop from 9 to 4, so the final pressure must be below 5.4 atm, not above it."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"That's the starting pressure copied; gas moles change (9 → 4), so in a rigid flask at fixed T it can't stay the same."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Counts the liquid water as gas; a liquid's volume is tiny, so only NO is left pushing on the walls."}],"rule":"Only gases count: P_final = P_start × (gas moles after ÷ gas moles before). Round the pressure and the ratio to 1 digit.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Count gas only: 4 + 5 = 9 mol → 4 NO (water is liquid). Pre-divide: 5.4 ÷ 9 = 0.6, × 4 = 2.4 atm."},"exp":"In a rigid flask at fixed T, **P ∝ moles of gas**. Gas moles go from 4 + 5 = 9 to 4 (the water is liquid), so P = 5.4 atm × 4/9 = **2.4 atm**.","how":{"ask":"Count gas moles before and after from the coefficients, then scale the pressure by that ratio.","know":"• At constant V and T, PV = nRT gives **P ∝ n(gas)**: P_final = P_initial × n_after/n_before.\n• Take the moles from the **coefficients** when the reactants are in the stoichiometric ratio and react completely.\n• Count **only gases**; (l) and (s) species add no pressure (water's small vapor pressure is ignored).","steps":[{"text":"The flask is rigid and the temperature is fixed, so only the gas moles change.","hl":"A rigid flask at 298 K"},{"text":"Gas moles before: both reactants are gases.","hl":"in a 4:5 mole ratio","calc":["n_before = 4 + 5 = **9**"]},{"text":"Gas moles after: the mix is exactly stoichiometric, so nothing is left over; NO is a gas, the water is liquid.","calc":["n_after = 4 (NO) + 0 (H₂O, liquid) = **4**"]},{"text":"Scale the pressure.","calc":["P_final = P_initial × n_after/n_before","P_final = 5.4 atm × 4/9","P_final = **2.4 atm**"]},{"text":"6.0 atm counts the liquid water as gas (5.4 × 10/9); 12 atm flips the ratio (5.4 × 9/4); 5.4 atm assumes the pressure does not change.","strike":["6.0 atm","12 atm","5.4 atm"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.4 atm."}],"trap":"Counting every product coefficient: a liquid or solid product takes up almost no volume and adds no pressure."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"rigid flask","note":"V constant"},{"t":"298 K","note":"T constant"},{"t":"4:5 mole ratio","note":"exactly stoichiometric"},{"t":"H₂O(l)","note":"liquid: not counted"}]},{"text":"**P tracks gas moles.** With V and T fixed, PV = nRT makes P directly proportional to n.","calc":["P₂/P₁ = n₂/n₁"]},{"text":"**Also: read the gas moles from the coefficients.** Add the coefficients of the (g) species on each side.","calc":["left: 4 + 5 = 9 mol gas","right: 4 mol gas (the 6 mol of water is liquid)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** at constant V and T, P_final = P_initial × (gas moles after ÷ gas moles before), counting only (g) species from the balanced equation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-38a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:38#1","pe5-cp:38#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:38"],"passage":"Naphthalene, a nonpolar hydrocarbon, was left mixed with sodium benzoate, a salt, after a reaction. The solids are shaken together with water and dichloromethane in a separatory funnel. The two liquids do not mix and form two layers. The student then drains each layer into its own flask.","q":"Which layers hold naphthalene and the benzoate ion, respectively?","choices":["Organic; aqueous","Aqueous; organic","Organic; organic","Aqueous; aqueous"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Both swapped: the neutral hydrocarbon goes to the organic layer and the ion to the water, not the other way."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Benzoate is an ion, and ions stay in water, not in nonpolar CH₂Cl₂."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Naphthalene is a neutral hydrocarbon; it won't go into water."}],"rule":"Like dissolves like: neutral, nonpolar compounds go to the organic layer; ions (salts, charged acids or bases) stay in water.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Neutral and nonpolar → organic layer (naphthalene). Any ion → water (benzoate⁻ from the sodium salt). Organic; aqueous."},"exp":"**Like dissolves like.** Naphthalene is a neutral hydrocarbon, so it moves into **dichloromethane**; benzoate is an **anion**, held in **water** by ion–dipole attraction. Layer density decides only which phase sits on top.","how":{"ask":"Which compound is neutral and nonpolar, which is an ion, and which solvent does each resemble?","know":"• **Like dissolves like**: neutral, nonpolar molecules go to the organic layer; ions are surrounded by water dipoles and stay in the aqueous layer.\n• Dichloromethane is an organic solvent that does not mix with water.\n• Density only decides which layer is on top (dichloromethane sinks), not where a compound goes.","steps":[{"text":"Naphthalene is two fused benzene rings of C and H only: neutral and nonpolar, so it dissolves in the organic solvent.","hl":"Naphthalene"},{"text":"Sodium benzoate is a salt: in water it is Na⁺ plus the benzoate anion, and water's dipoles surround that charge.","hl":"sodium benzoate"},{"text":"Aqueous; organic swaps both; aqueous; aqueous puts a hydrocarbon in water; organic; organic puts an ion in a nonpolar solvent.","strike":["Aqueous; organic","Aqueous; aqueous","Organic; organic"]},{"text":"Answer: organic; aqueous."}],"trap":"Placing compounds by layer position: dichloromethane is denser and lies under the water, but which layer is on top says nothing about where a compound dissolves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Naphthalene","note":"C and H only"},{"t":"sodium benzoate","note":"a salt: ions"},{"t":"water and dichloromethane","note":"polar vs nonpolar layer"}]},{"text":"**Sort by charge first.** An ion, cation or anion, stays in water: it cannot leave its shell of water dipoles for a nonpolar solvent."},{"text":"**Then by polarity.** A neutral molecule with no polar groups, such as a hydrocarbon, moves into the organic layer."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in an extraction, like dissolves like: a neutral nonpolar compound goes to the organic layer, and an ionized species stays in water."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-39a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe5-cp:39#1","pe5-cp:39#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:39"],"passage":"A new compound blocks a bacterial kinase. Enzyme activity is plotted against log₁₀ of the inhibitor concentration in molar units, as shown. For this assay, the inhibition constant is read as the inhibitor concentration giving half-maximal activity. Each point is the mean of three runs.","q":"What is the inhibition constant, approximately?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Enzyme activity versus inhibitor concentration","x":{"label":"log₁₀([I] / M)","min":-8,"max":-3,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Activity (% of uninhibited)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Activity","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[-8,99],[-7.5,97],[-7,91],[-6.5,76],[-6,50],[-5.5,24],[-5,9],[-4.5,3],[-4,1],[-3,0]]}]},"choices":["0.1 μM","1 μM","6 μM","50 μM"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"One decade off: that is ten times too little inhibitor, where activity is still about 90%."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"6 is the x-axis log value; [I] = 10^(log value) M, never the log number itself."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"50 is the activity percentage on the y-axis, not a concentration; read the x-axis."}],"rule":"Find 50% activity on the y-axis, read its x value (log [I]), then [I] = 10^x M; each x unit is a factor of 10.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"At 50% activity read x: log[I] = −6. Undo the log: 10⁻⁶ M = 1 μM."},"exp":"Read across from **50% activity** to the curve and down: **log[I] = −6**. The x-axis is the log of the molar concentration, so [I] = 10⁻⁶ M, and 10⁻⁶ M × (10⁶ μM per M) = **1 μM**.","how":{"ask":"Find 50% activity, read the x-value below it, then undo the log and convert M to μM.","know":"• On a log-scale plot, the x-value is **log₁₀[I]**: [I] = 10^x in the axis unit.\n• **Check the unit** on the axis label (here M) before converting: 1 μM = 10⁻⁶ M.\n• The half-maximal point (IC₅₀) is read straight down from 50% activity.","steps":[{"text":"Find half-maximal activity on the y-axis and drop to the x-axis.","hl":"half-maximal activity","calc":["50% activity → log₁₀([I]/M) = **−6**"]},{"text":"Undo the logarithm in the axis unit, then convert.","calc":["[I] = 10⁻⁶ M","10⁻⁶ M × 10⁶ μM/M = **1 μM**"]},{"text":"0.1 μM is 10⁻⁷ M, one decade off: it is the point where activity is still about 90%.","strike":["0.1 μM"]},{"text":"6 μM reads the log value as if it were a concentration, and 50 μM reads the percentage instead of the x-axis.","strike":["6 μM","50 μM"]},{"text":"Answer: 1 μM."}],"trap":"Reporting the axis number itself (6) as a concentration: the x-axis is a log, so the concentration is 10 raised to it."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"log₁₀ of the inhibitor concentration","note":"x is a log"},{"t":"molar units","note":"axis unit is M"},{"t":"half-maximal activity","note":"read at 50%"}]},{"text":"**Read across, then down.** Go to 50% on the y-axis, across to the curve, then straight down to the x-axis."},{"text":"**Undo the log and mind the units.** The axis gives log₁₀([I]/M); raise 10 to it to get molar, then convert.","calc":["x = −6 → 10⁻⁶ M","10⁻⁶ M = 1 μM","x = −5 would be 10⁻⁵ M = 10 μM"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on an activity-versus-log[I] curve, find 50% activity, drop to the x-axis, raise 10 to that value in the axis unit, then convert to the asked unit (10⁻⁶ M = 1 μM)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-40a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:40#1","pe5-cp:40#2","pe5-cp:40#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:40"],"passage":"A dimeric enzyme from a hot-spring bacterium binds its substrate at two identical sites. Binding was measured by fluorescence across a wide range of substrate concentrations. A fit of the data gave a Hill coefficient of 0.6. The curve is not S-shaped; it rises steeply at first and then flattens slowly.","q":"What does this value indicate about substrate binding?","choices":["Positive cooperativity","Negative cooperativity","No cooperativity","Exactly two binding sites"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Positive cooperativity needs n > 1; 0.6 is below 1."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"No cooperativity means n = 1 exactly, and 0.6 isn't 1."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The Hill coefficient isn't a site count; it measures how binding at one site shifts the others."}],"rule":"Hill n above 1: one binding helps the next. n = 1: independent sites. n below 1: one binding hinders the next. n isn't a site count.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Hill n: > 1 positive, = 1 none, < 1 negative cooperativity; n is never a site count. 0.6 → negative cooperativity."},"exp":"A Hill coefficient **n < 1** means **negative cooperativity**: binding at one site lowers the affinity of the others, so the curve rises quickly and then flattens out early. n > 1 would be positive cooperativity (sigmoidal), and n = 1 independent sites (hyperbolic). n is not a count of sites; it can only reach the number of sites in the limit of perfect positive cooperativity.","how":{"ask":"Compare the Hill coefficient with 1.","know":"• **n < 1**: negative cooperativity (one binding makes the next harder).\n• **n > 1**: positive cooperativity (one binding makes the next easier; sigmoidal curve).\n• **n = 1**: no cooperativity (independent sites; hyperbolic curve).\n• n measures interaction between sites; it is at most the number of sites, never a direct count.","steps":[{"text":"Compare n with 1.","hl":"Hill coefficient of 0.6","calc":["n = 0.6 < 1 → **negative cooperativity**"]},{"text":"Positive cooperativity needs n > 1, and no cooperativity needs n = 1 exactly.","strike":["Positive cooperativity","No cooperativity"]},{"text":"n is not the number of sites: a dimer has two sites, but n = 0.6 only says each binding hinders the next, and a tetramer could show n = 0.6 too.","strike":["Exactly two binding sites"]},{"text":"Answer: negative cooperativity."}],"trap":"Reading n as the number of binding sites, or calling any n other than 1 positive: n below 1 means the sites hinder each other."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words and numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"dimeric","note":"two subunits"},{"t":"Hill coefficient of 0.6","note":"compare with 1"}]},{"text":"**n < 1: negative cooperativity.** Binding at one site makes binding at the next harder, so the curve rises fast and then flattens out early (the n = 0.6 curve).","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Fractional saturation for three Hill coefficients (same half-saturation, 4 μM)","x":{"label":"[S] (μM)","min":0,"max":20,"step":4},"y":{"label":"Fractional saturation","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"n = 2.5","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.03],[2,0.15],[3,0.33],[4,0.5],[6,0.73],[8,0.85],[12,0.94],[16,0.97],[20,0.98]]},{"name":"n = 1","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.2],[2,0.33],[3,0.43],[4,0.5],[6,0.6],[8,0.67],[12,0.75],[16,0.8],[20,0.83]]},{"name":"n = 0.6","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.3],[2,0.4],[3,0.46],[4,0.5],[6,0.56],[8,0.6],[12,0.66],[16,0.7],[20,0.72]]}]}},{"text":"**n > 1: positive cooperativity.** Binding at one site makes binding at the next easier, so the curve is sigmoidal (the n = 2.5 curve)."},{"text":"**n = 1: no cooperativity.** The sites bind independently, and the curve is a plain hyperbola, like a monomer's."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** read the Hill coefficient against 1: n > 1 is positive cooperativity, n < 1 negative cooperativity, n = 1 none (independent sites)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-40b","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:40#4"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:40"],"passage":"Two oxygen carriers were purified from a marine worm. Carrier X is a single chain with one heme, and Carrier Y has four chains, each with a heme. Binding curves are plotted for the two oxygen carriers, X and Y. Both reach full saturation at high oxygen pressure.","q":"Which carrier binds with no cooperativity, and what Hill coefficient does its curve imply?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Fractional saturation versus ligand concentration","x":{"label":"[L] (μM)","min":0,"max":20,"step":4},"y":{"label":"Fractional saturation","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"X","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.33],[2,0.5],[3,0.6],[4,0.67],[5,0.71],[6,0.75],[8,0.8],[10,0.83],[15,0.88],[20,0.91]]},{"name":"Y","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.01],[2,0.07],[3,0.19],[4,0.35],[5,0.5],[6,0.62],[8,0.79],[10,0.87],[15,0.96],[20,0.98]]}]},"choices":["Y; equal to 1","X; greater than 1","X; equal to 1","Y; greater than 1"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Y's curve is S-shaped, the mark of cooperativity, so it isn't the noncooperative carrier."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"No cooperativity means n = 1 exactly; n > 1 is positive cooperativity, so this pairing contradicts itself."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Y's S-curve does mean n > 1, but that is cooperative, which the question rules out."}],"rule":"Hyperbolic curve (steep from the start, no lag): no cooperativity, n = 1. S-shaped curve: positive cooperativity, n > 1.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Hyperbola (steep from zero, no lag) = independent sites, n = 1; sigmoid = n > 1. X is the hyperbola: X; equal to 1."},"exp":"X traces a **hyperbola**: it rises steeply from the start and bends over smoothly toward full saturation, with no lag. That shape means every site binds independently, **no cooperativity, n = 1**. Y's curve is **S-shaped (sigmoidal)**, the mark of positive cooperativity (n > 1), even though Y reaches half saturation later (about 5 μM vs about 2 μM for X).","how":{"ask":"Look at the shape of each curve, not where it reaches half saturation.","know":"• **Sigmoidal** (S-shaped) binding curve → positive cooperativity, **n > 1**, typical of multi-subunit proteins such as hemoglobin.\n• **Hyperbolic** curve → no cooperativity, **n = 1** (like myoglobin).\n• The half-saturation point measures **affinity**, not cooperativity: a carrier can bind tightly and still be noncooperative.","steps":[{"text":"X rises steeply from the start and bends over, with no flat lag: a hyperbola.","calc":["X: 0.33 at 1 μM → 0.50 at 2 μM → 0.83 at 10 μM"]},{"text":"A hyperbola means independent sites, so n = 1 exactly, not n > 1.","strike":["X; greater than 1"]},{"text":"Y starts flat, then rises steeply: an S shape, so Y is the cooperative one (n > 1) and is not the carrier asked about.","calc":["Y: 0.07 at 2 μM → 0.50 at 5 μM → 0.87 at 10 μM"],"strike":["Y; equal to 1","Y; greater than 1"]},{"text":"Answer: X; equal to 1."}],"trap":"Picking Y because it is half-saturated at a higher [L], as if weaker binding meant no cooperativity: half saturation is affinity; only the shape shows cooperativity."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"no cooperativity","note":"look at the shape"},{"t":"Hill coefficient","note":"n vs 1"}]},{"text":"**Shape, not position.** A sigmoid means binding gets easier as sites fill; a hyperbola means every site binds the same way."},{"text":"**Half saturation is affinity.** X reaches 0.5 at about 2 μM and Y at about 5 μM, so X binds more tightly, but tight binding says nothing about cooperativity: X is noncooperative and Y cooperative."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a sigmoidal binding curve means positive cooperativity (n > 1) and a hyperbolic curve means none (n = 1), whatever their half-saturation points."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-41a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:41#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:41"],"passage":"A flower makes its color from a colorless precursor in three steps, W → X → Y → Z. Enzymes K, L and M catalyze the steps in order. Compound X is a bright pigment that growers want, while both Y and Z are toxic to pollinators. Breeders can knock out any one or two of the enzymes.","q":"Which knockout builds up pigment X but stops both toxins?","choices":["Enzyme K","Enzyme L","Enzyme M","Enzymes K and M"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Knocking out K stops W → X, upstream of everything, so no X pigment builds up."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"M only makes Z; with M gone, toxic Y still forms from X and piles up."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The K knockout shuts the pathway off upstream of X, so no pigment is made."}],"rule":"A knockout piles up the substrate of the blocked step: to build up an intermediate and stop everything after it, block the step right after it.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A dead enzyme piles up its own substrate. X is the substrate of the step X → Y, so knock out that step's enzyme: X builds, and neither Y nor Z can form."},"exp":"To keep an intermediate but block everything it becomes, **inhibit the enzyme that consumes it**. Enzyme L converts X → Y, so knocking it out piles up X and stops both Y and Z. Knocking out K stops X itself; knocking out M still lets toxic Y form.","how":{"ask":"Which enzyme uses up X?","know":"• In a linear pathway, an intermediate **accumulates** when the enzyme that **consumes** it is blocked.\n• Blocking any step **before** the intermediate stops its own production.\n• Blocking a step **further downstream** still lets the products in between form.","steps":[{"text":"Map each enzyme to its step.","hl":"W → X → Y → Z","calc":["K: W → X","L: X → Y","M: Y → Z"]},{"text":"X is made by K and used up by L; block L and X can only accumulate, while Y and Z can no longer form.","hl":"both Y and Z are toxic"},{"text":"Knocking out K shuts the pathway off before X, alone or with M.","strike":["Enzyme K","Enzymes K and M"]},{"text":"Knocking out M stops only Z; toxic Y still forms.","strike":["Enzyme M"]},{"text":"Answer: Enzyme L."}],"trap":"Blocking the last enzyme out of habit: that stops only the final product and leaves the toxic intermediate Y."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"W → X → Y → Z","note":"linear pathway"},{"t":"K, L and M","note":"one enzyme per step"},{"t":"both Y and Z are toxic","note":"stop everything after X"}]},{"text":"**Block the exit, not the entrance.** An intermediate piles up when its consuming step is blocked and production continues."},{"text":"**Upstream blocks lose everything downstream.** If K is missing, X is never made, and neither are Y and Z."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to accumulate an intermediate of a linear pathway and stop all later products, inhibit the enzyme that consumes it (the step right after it)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-42a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:42#1","pe5-cp:42#2","pe5-cp:42#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:42"],"passage":"A crystal structure shows a bacterial kinase bound to a nucleotide analog that carries a doubly charged phosphate on a flat purine ring. The phosphate oxygens sit within 2.9 Å of one residue, making a salt bridge. The purine ring lies parallel to a second side chain's ring at 3.6 Å, a π-stacking contact. No metal ion is present in the site.","q":"Which pair of residues best accounts for these two contacts?","choices":["Lysine and tryptophan","Glutamate and tryptophan","Lysine and threonine","Aspartate and leucine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"A carboxylate is negative, so it would repel the phosphate instead of forming a salt bridge with it."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Aspartate repels a phosphate, and leucine's branched alkyl chain has no π system."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Gets the salt bridge but skips the stacking contact: threonine has no ring to stack on."}],"rule":"Salt bridge to a phosphate (−) needs a + side chain (Lys, Arg); π-stacking requires a flat aromatic side chain (Phe, Tyr, Trp). The pair must pass both tests.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Two filters: + charge for the phosphate, aromatic ring for the stacking. Only one choice has a positive residue paired with an aromatic one."},"exp":"A phosphate is negative, so the salt bridge needs a **positive** side chain: **lysine** (–NH₃⁺). Stacking with a flat purine needs an **aromatic** ring: **tryptophan**'s indole. Glu and Asp are negative; Thr and Leu have no aromatic ring.","how":{"ask":"Which residue is positive at physiological pH, and which has a flat aromatic ring?","know":"• **Salt bridge**: opposite charges; phosphate (−) pairs with Lys or Arg (+).\n• **π-stacking**: two flat aromatic rings face to face; Phe, Tyr, Trp (and His).\n• Asp and Glu are negative; Ser, Thr, Leu and Val have no ring.","steps":[{"text":"First contact: a phosphate is negative, so its partner must be positive.","hl":"making a salt bridge","calc":["phosphate (−) + Lys –NH₃⁺ (+)"]},{"text":"Negative side chains repel the phosphate.","strike":["Glutamate and tryptophan","Aspartate and leucine"]},{"text":"Second contact: stacking requires a flat aromatic side chain; threonine has none.","hl":"a π-stacking contact","strike":["Lysine and threonine"]},{"text":"Answer: lysine and tryptophan."}],"trap":"Checking only one contact: a pair can pass the charge test and still fail the ring test."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"doubly charged phosphate","note":"negative"},{"t":"making a salt bridge","note":"needs + residue"},{"t":"a π-stacking contact","note":"needs aromatic ring"}]},{"text":"**Two filters in a row.** Strike every pair whose first residue is not positive, then every pair whose second residue has no aromatic ring."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a phosphate salt bridge needs Lys or Arg, and π-stacking needs Phe, Tyr or Trp; pick the pair that meets both."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-43a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:43#1","pe5-cp:43#3","pe5-cp:43#4"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:43"],"passage":"Some gut bacteria release a gas that smells of rotten eggs. The enzyme responsible, cysteine desulfhydrase, uses pyridoxal phosphate. It breaks down cysteine: HSCH₂–CH(NH₃⁺)–COO⁻ + H₂O → CH₃–CO–COO⁻ + NH₄⁺ + ___. The product left blank is the gas, which is neutral.","q":"In the passage's equation, what is the missing product?","choices":["H₂S","HS⁻","H₂S + H⁺","SO₂"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Charge check: the sides are already neutral, and HS⁻ adds a − charge and is one H short."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Charge check: both sides are already neutral, so adding H⁺ leaves the right side +1."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Atom check: the 3 O on the left all end up in pyruvate, so no O atoms remain for SO₂."}],"rule":"Balance atoms, then charge: count each element and the net charge on both sides before picking the missing species.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Charge: left 0; pyruvate⁻ + NH₄⁺ = 0, so the missing piece is neutral. Leftover atoms are S + 2 H → H₂S."},"exp":"Count atoms and charge. Left: C₃H₉NO₃S, charge 0. Right so far: pyruvate C₃H₃O₃⁻ plus NH₄⁺ = C₃H₇NO₃, charge 0. The leftover is **S + 2 H with no charge = H₂S**.","how":{"ask":"Subtract the known products' atoms and charge from the reactants'; whatever is left is the missing product.","know":"• Atoms and charge are both conserved.\n• **Missing product = reactant atoms − known-product atoms**, with the leftover charge.\n• Then name the leftover (S + 2 H, neutral = H₂S; N + 4 H, +1 = NH₄⁺) and recheck the total charge.","steps":[{"text":"Total the reactants.","hl":"HSCH₂–CH(NH₃⁺)–COO⁻ + H₂O","calc":["cysteine C₃H₇NO₂S (charge 0) + H₂O","= **C₃H₉NO₃S**, charge 0"]},{"text":"Total the known products.","calc":["pyruvate C₃H₃O₃⁻ + NH₄⁺","= **C₃H₇NO₃**, charge 0"]},{"text":"Subtract.","calc":["C₃H₉NO₃S − C₃H₇NO₃ = **H₂S**, charge 0 − 0 = 0"]},{"text":"HS⁻ has one H too few and a − charge the sides do not need; adding H⁺ leaves a + charge unbalanced; SO₂ adds O atoms that are not available.","strike":["HS⁻","H₂S + H⁺","SO₂"]},{"text":"Answer: H₂S."}],"trap":"Balancing only the atoms: a choice can carry the right sulfur yet break the charge balance."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"HSCH₂–CH(NH₃⁺)–COO⁻","note":"zwitterion, net 0"},{"t":"NH₄⁺","note":"known product, +1"},{"t":"missing product","note":"atom bookkeeping"}]},{"text":"**Count the left side.** Cysteine C₃H₇NO₂S plus water.","calc":["C₃H₉NO₃S, charge 0"]},{"text":"**Count the right side.** Pyruvate (−1) and ammonium (+1).","calc":["C₃H₇NO₃, charge 0"]},{"text":"**Name the leftover.** S and 2 H, no charge: H₂S."},{"text":"**Also:** the same subtraction names an ammonium ion: a leftover of one N, four H and a +1 charge is NH₄⁺, as when an amino acid loses its amino group."},{"text":"**Also:** finish by checking charge: here 0 on the left and −1 + 1 + 0 = 0 on the right."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** missing product = reactant atoms − known-product atoms; name what is left, then confirm the total charge matches on both sides."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-43b","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:43#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:43"],"passage":"Soil bacteria use urease to release nitrogen from urea. Urease hydrolyzes urea in water: (NH₂)₂CO + ___ + H⁺ → 2 NH₄⁺ + HCO₃⁻. The products are the ions shown, 2 NH₄⁺ + HCO₃⁻, and the only other reactant is the solvent. Each side of the equation must carry the same atoms and charge.","q":"Which reactant completes the balanced equation?","choices":["2 H₂O","3 H₂O","1 O₂","2 OH⁻"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Atom check: 3 H₂O brings one O and two H more than the right side holds."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"No H: the right side has 9 H vs 5 on the left, and O₂ adds none."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Charge check: the right side is +1 (2+ and 1−), and 2 OH⁻ would make the left −1."}],"rule":"Balance O first, then H, then check charge: both sides must match atom for atom and in total charge.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"O first: right has 3 O, urea has 1, so add 2 O as neutral water (charge already +1 both sides). 2 H₂O also brings the 4 missing H."},"exp":"The right side has 3 O and 9 H; urea and H⁺ supply only 1 O and 5 H. **Water supplies the missing O and H**: 2 H₂O adds 2 O and 4 H, giving CH₉N₂O₃ with charge +1 on both sides.","how":{"ask":"Find which side is short of O and H, and add water to fix both.","know":"• In aqueous (biochemical) reactions, **H₂O supplies missing O and H** (hydrolysis).\n• Add water to the side short of O, then recheck H and charge.","steps":[{"text":"Count the products.","hl":"2 NH₄⁺ + HCO₃⁻","calc":["2 NH₄⁺: N₂H₈ (+2); HCO₃⁻: HCO₃ (−1)","total C H₉ N₂ O₃, charge +1"]},{"text":"Count the known reactants.","calc":["(NH₂)₂CO: CH₄N₂O; H⁺: H (+1)","total C H₅ N₂ O, charge +1"]},{"text":"The left is short 2 O and 4 H, which is exactly 2 H₂O.","calc":["missing: H₄O₂ = 2 H₂O"]},{"text":"3 H₂O adds too many atoms, O₂ brings no H, and 2 OH⁻ brings only 2 H and a 2− charge.","strike":["3 H₂O","1 O₂","2 OH⁻"]},{"text":"Answer: 2 H₂O."}],"trap":"Adding O₂ to fix the oxygen count: in a hydrolysis the extra O arrives with H, as water."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"hydrolyzes","note":"water is a reactant"},{"t":"2 NH₄⁺ + HCO₃⁻","note":"3 O, 9 H"}]},{"text":"**Find the shortfall.** Compare O and H on both sides.","calc":["O: 3 right vs 1 left → need 2","H: 9 right vs 5 left → need 4"]},{"text":"**Fill it with water.** 2 O and 4 H together are 2 H₂O; the charge stays +1 on each side."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in an aqueous reaction, add H₂O to the side short of O, then recheck H and charge."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-44a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:44#1","pe5-cp:44#2","pe5-cp:44#3","pe6-cp:55#1","pe6-cp:55#2","pe6-cp:55#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:44"],"passage":"A chemist studies the hydrolysis of an activated ester used to label proteins. Reactions with barriers under 35 kJ/mol run within seconds at room temperature. The free-energy profile for the forward reaction is shown. Reactants sit at the start of the curve and products at its end.","q":"Based on the free-energy profile, the forward reaction is:","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Free energy along the reaction coordinate","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Free energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":90,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"Reaction","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,50],[1,50],[2,53],[3,61],[4,71],[5,75],[6,68],[7,48],[8,30],[9,21],[10,20]]}]},"choices":["Fast and favorable","Slow and favorable","Fast and unfavorable","Slow and unfavorable"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign: products (20) end below reactants (50), so ΔG < 0 and the reaction is spontaneous."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Products sit below reactants: downhill means spontaneous, so nonspontaneous fails here too."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The barrier is peak minus reactants (75 − 50), under the 35 kJ/mol cutoff, so it isn't slow."}],"rule":"Speed from the barrier (peak − reactants vs the cutoff); spontaneity from the ends (products − reactants < 0). Read each separately.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Two separate reads: peak − start = 75 − 50 = 25 < 35 → fast; end − start = 20 − 50 = −30 < 0 → spontaneous. Fast and spontaneous."},"exp":"Read three heights: reactants **50**, peak **75**, products **20 kJ/mol**. **Ea = 75 − 50 = 25 kJ/mol**, under 35, so it is fast. **ΔG = 20 − 50 = −30 kJ/mol**, below zero, so it is spontaneous.","how":{"ask":"Read Ea (peak − reactants) for the speed and ΔG (products − reactants) for spontaneity, separately.","know":"• **Ea = transition state − reactants**: a low Ea means a fast reaction.\n• **ΔG = products − reactants**: ΔG < 0 means spontaneous.\n• Speed (kinetics) and spontaneity (thermodynamics) are independent: read each from its own pair of heights.","steps":[{"text":"Read the three heights from the plot.","calc":["reactants = **50 kJ/mol**","peak = **75 kJ/mol**","products = **20 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"Speed from the barrier.","hl":"barriers under 35 kJ/mol","calc":["Ea = 75 − 50 = **25 kJ/mol** < 35 → **fast**"]},{"text":"A low barrier rules out both slow choices.","strike":["Slow and favorable","Slow and unfavorable"]},{"text":"Spontaneity from the ends.","calc":["ΔG = 20 − 50 = **−30 kJ/mol** < 0 → **spontaneous**"],"strike":["Fast and unfavorable"]},{"text":"Answer: fast and favorable (ΔG < 0, spontaneous)."}],"trap":"Measuring the barrier from the products (75 − 20 = 55 kJ/mol): Ea for the forward reaction starts at the reactants."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words and numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"free-energy profile","note":"reactants left, products right"},{"t":"barriers under 35 kJ/mol","note":"cutoff for fast"}]},{"text":"**Fast means low Ea.** The barrier height above the reactants sets the rate.","calc":["Ea = peak − reactants"]},{"text":"**Spontaneous means ΔG < 0.** Products lower than reactants is downhill.","calc":["ΔG = products − reactants"]},{"text":"**Also:** if no profile is given, infer the same two facts from words: \"occurs within seconds\" or \"rapid\" means a low Ea; \"favored\", \"releases free energy\" or \"proceeds to completion\" means ΔG < 0."},{"text":"**Also:** a solid that sits unchanged until a spark or shock sets it off is two things at once. Thermodynamically it is favorable: reacting releases heat, so products lie below reactants (exothermic). Kinetically it is stuck behind a tall barrier (a large Ea), so nothing happens until a trigger supplies that energy. Read any energy diagram with the same two subtractions: end heights give ΔH on an enthalpy diagram (ΔG on a free-energy diagram), peak minus reactants for Ea.","calc":["explosive: reactants 30, peak 200, products −250 kJ/mol","ΔH = −250 − 30 = −280 kJ/mol → exothermic","Ea = 200 − 30 = 170 kJ/mol → high: needs a trigger"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on an energy profile, the rate is set by Ea (peak − reactants; low = fast) and spontaneity by ΔG (products − reactants; < 0 = spontaneous); read each separately."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-45a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:45#1","pe5-cp:45#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:45"],"q":"Fully protonated glutamic acid, shown with its acidic groups labeled A–C, is titrated with NaOH. The first proton removed comes from which group?","fig":"cpt-pe5-glu-sites","choices":["Group B","Group A","Group C","All at once"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"B is the side-chain COOH (pKa ≈ 4.3); it is the second group to lose its H⁺, after a stronger acid."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A is the α-NH₃⁺ (pKa ≈ 9.7), the weakest acid, so it gives up its H⁺ last."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Separate pKa values (≈ 2, 4, 10) mean the groups lose H⁺ one at a time, not together."}],"rule":"Protons leave in pKa order, lowest first: α-COOH (≈ 2), side-chain COOH (≈ 4), then α-NH₃⁺ (≈ 10).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Glu loses H⁺ by pKa: α-COOH ~2, side-chain COOH ~4, α-NH₃⁺ ~10. First off = the α-COOH, group C."},"exp":"Groups lose H⁺ in order of **increasing pKa**: the **α-COOH (C, ≈ 2.2)** first, then the side-chain COOH (B, ≈ 4.3), then the α-NH₃⁺ (A, ≈ 9.7). The pKa values are far apart, so the groups titrate one after another.","how":{"ask":"Rank the labeled groups by pKa; the most acidic group (smallest pKa) gives up H⁺ first.","know":"• As base is added, groups deprotonate in order of **increasing pKa**.\n• Typical pKa: α-COOH ≈ 2, side-chain COOH ≈ 4, α-NH₃⁺ ≈ 9–10, basic side chains (Lys ε-NH₃⁺ ≈ 10.5, Arg ≈ 12.5) last.","steps":[{"text":"Identify each label.","hl":"labeled A–C","calc":["A = α-NH₃⁺ (≈ 9.7)","B = side-chain COOH (≈ 4.3)","C = α-COOH (≈ 2.2)"]},{"text":"Order by pKa.","calc":["C (2.2) < B (4.3) < A (9.7)","**first C**, second B, third A"]},{"text":"B goes second, A goes last, and the well-separated pKa values rule out a single step.","strike":["Group B","Group A","All at once"]},{"text":"Answer: group C, the α-carboxyl."}],"trap":"Picking the side-chain group first because it is farther out: the α-COOH is the most acidic, pulled by the nearby NH₃⁺."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Fully protonated","note":"every group holds its H⁺"},{"t":"titrated with NaOH","note":"rising pH"},{"t":"first","note":"lowest pKa"}]},{"text":"**Lowest pKa goes first.** The α-COOH (≈ 2) is made extra acidic by the neighboring α-NH₃⁺."},{"text":"**Then the side-chain acid.** Glutamate's γ-COOH has pKa ≈ 4.3.","calc":["order: α-COOH → side-chain COOH → α-NH₃⁺"]},{"text":"**Also:** in a basic amino acid the order ends with the side chain: lysine goes α-COOH (≈ 2.2), α-NH₃⁺ (≈ 9.0), then ε-NH₃⁺ (≈ 10.5)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a fully protonated amino acid loses H⁺ in order of increasing pKa: α-COOH, acidic side chain, α-NH₃⁺, basic side chain."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-46a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe5-cp:46#1","pe5-cp:46#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:46"],"q":"The enthalpy profile shown compares a reaction uncatalyzed and with an enzyme. What are the catalyzed forward activation energy and ΔH?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Enthalpy along the reaction coordinate, uncatalyzed and catalyzed","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Enthalpy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":160,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Uncatalyzed","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,30],[1,30],[2,40],[3,72],[4,118],[5,140],[6,128],[7,100],[8,75],[9,62],[10,60]]},{"name":"Catalyzed","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,30],[1,30],[2,36],[3,55],[4,80],[5,90],[6,85],[7,74],[8,65],[9,61],[10,60]]}]},"choices":["60 kJ/mol; +30 kJ/mol","110 kJ/mol; +30 kJ/mol","50 kJ/mol; −30 kJ/mol","90 kJ/mol; +30 kJ/mol"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign: the products sit above the reactants on the profile, so ΔH is positive, not negative."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That's the catalyzed peak's height above zero; subtract the reactants' energy to get the barrier."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"That barrier uses the uncatalyzed peak (140); the enzyme's path is the lower peak."}],"rule":"Ea = peak − reactants on the curve asked about; ΔH = products − reactants, and a catalyst lowers the peak but never changes ΔH.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A catalyst can't move ΔH, so it's +30 for both paths (60 − 30). Catalyzed Ea = lower peak − reactants = 90 − 30 = 60 kJ/mol."},"exp":"Catalyzed **Ea = 90 − 30 = 60 kJ/mol**: the climb from the reactant level to the enzyme's lower peak. **ΔH = 60 − 30 = +30 kJ/mol** (endothermic) on both paths: an enzyme lowers the barrier but leaves the reactant and product levels where they were.","how":{"ask":"Measure Ea from the reactants to the catalyzed peak; measure ΔH from the reactants to the products.","know":"• **Ea = transition-state energy − reactant energy**, using the peak of the path asked about.\n• **ΔH = products − reactants**; a catalyst lowers the peak but leaves both ends, so ΔH is the same on either path.","steps":[{"text":"Read the levels.","calc":["reactants = **30**","uncatalyzed peak = **140**","catalyzed peak = **90**","products = **60** kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Catalyzed activation energy.","hl":"catalyzed forward activation energy","calc":["Ea(cat) = 90 − 30 = **60 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"110 kJ/mol uses the uncatalyzed peak (140 − 30), and 90 kJ/mol is the peak's height above zero, not above the reactants.","strike":["110 kJ/mol; +30 kJ/mol","90 kJ/mol; +30 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Enthalpy change, the same on both paths.","calc":["ΔH = 60 − 30 = **+30 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"50 kJ/mol is only how far the enzyme lowers the peak (140 − 90), and −30 flips the sign of ΔH by subtracting products from reactants.","strike":["50 kJ/mol; −30 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Answer: 60 kJ/mol; +30 kJ/mol."}],"trap":"Reading the peak's height straight off the axis (90): Ea is measured from the reactant level, not from zero."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"uncatalyzed and with an enzyme","note":"two paths"},{"t":"forward activation energy","note":"from reactants up"},{"t":"ΔH","note":"products − reactants"}]},{"text":"**Ea: reactants to the peak of the path asked.** The enzyme's curve has the lower peak.","calc":["Ea = TS − reactants = 90 − 30 = 60 kJ/mol","uncatalyzed: 140 − 30 = 110 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** ΔH = products − reactants, and both curves start and end at the same levels, so ΔH is the same with or without a catalyst.","calc":["ΔH = 60 − 30 = +30 kJ/mol (endothermic)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ea = transition-state energy − reactant energy (using the catalyzed peak for the catalyzed path) and ΔH = products − reactants, which a catalyst never changes."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-47a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:47#1","pe5-cp:47#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:47"],"q":"Crates of 6.0 kg and 10 kg hang on a rope over a frictionless pulley. Using g = 10 m/s², what is their acceleration?","choices":["2.5 m/s²","6.7 m/s²","40 m/s²","0.40 m/s²"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"That is the net force in newtons reported as an acceleration; it is also four times g."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Divides total mass by net force, flipping the ratio."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Divides the net force by the lighter crate only, skipping the heavier one that must also accelerate."}],"rule":"Atwood machine: a = (m₂ − m₁)g / (m₁ + m₂): net force over total mass. The answer must be less than g.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Net force = (10 − 6.0)(10) = 40 N; total mass = 16 kg. a = 40/16."},"exp":"Net force = (m₂ − m₁)g = (10 − 6.0)(10) = 40 N, and it accelerates both crates, 16 kg in all: a = 40/16 = **2.5 m/s²**.","how":{"ask":"What is the net force on the system, and what total mass does it move?","know":"• Treat both masses and the rope as one system.\n• **Net force** = weight difference = (m₂ − m₁)g.\n• **a = F_net / (m₁ + m₂)**; always less than g.","steps":[{"text":"Find the net force.","hl":"6.0 kg and 10 kg","calc":["F_net = (10 − 6.0)(10) = 40 N"]},{"text":"Divide by the total mass.","calc":["a = 40 / 16 = 2.5 m/s²"]},{"text":"Net force is in newtons, not m/s², and the inverted ratio has the wrong units too.","strike":["40 m/s²","0.40 m/s²"]},{"text":"Both crates accelerate, so divide by both masses.","strike":["6.7 m/s²"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.5 m/s²."}],"trap":"Dividing by one crate's mass: the net force must accelerate everything that moves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"6.0 kg and 10 kg","note":"m₁ and m₂"},{"t":"frictionless pulley","note":"no friction, no mass"},{"t":"g = 10 m/s²","note":"given g"}]},{"text":"**One system.** The rope makes the two crates move together, so the weight difference drives the combined mass.","calc":["a = (m₂ − m₁)g / (m₁ + m₂)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for two masses over a pulley, acceleration = (weight difference) ÷ (total mass), which is always less than g."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-48a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:48#1","pe5-cp:48#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:48"],"q":"The heating curve shown follows 2.0 g of ice warmed from −40 °C to liquid water at 90 °C. Which step takes the most heat?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Heating curve for 2.0 g of water","x":{"label":"Heat added (kJ)","min":0,"max":2,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Water","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,-40],[0.17,0],[0.84,0],[1.59,90]]}]},"choices":["Warming the ice","Melting the ice","Warming the water","Boiling the water"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"The curve stops at 90 °C, below the boiling point, so no water vaporizes at all."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Ice has a small specific heat and rises only 40 degrees: the shortest stretch of the heat axis."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Melting takes 334 J/g, but heating liquid water through 90 degrees takes about 4.18 × 90 ≈ 376 J/g."}],"rule":"Each step's heat equals its width along the heat-added axis; compare mL (plateaus) with mcΔT (slopes) instead of assuming phase changes win.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Read widths on the heat axis: ice slope ≈ 0.17 kJ, plateau ≈ 0.67 kJ, liquid slope ≈ 0.75 kJ. The widest segment wins."},"exp":"Per gram: warming ice 2.1 × 40 ≈ 84 J; melting 334 J; warming water 4.18 × 90 ≈ 376 J. For 2.0 g, that is about 0.17, 0.67 and **0.75 kJ**: **warming the liquid** takes the most heat here. No boiling occurs.","how":{"ask":"How wide is each segment of the curve along the heat axis?","know":"• **Plateaus** (phase changes): q = mL; L_fus(water) = 334 J/g, L_vap = 2260 J/g.\n• **Slopes** (one phase warming): q = mcΔT; c(ice) ≈ 2.1, c(water) = 4.18 J/(g·°C).\n• Heat for a step = its horizontal width on a heat-added axis.","steps":[{"text":"Check which steps occur.","hl":"liquid water at 90 °C","strike":["Boiling the water"]},{"text":"Compute each step for 2.0 g.","calc":["ice: 2.0 × 2.1 × 40 ≈ 0.17 kJ","melt: 2.0 × 334 ≈ 0.67 kJ","water: 2.0 × 4.18 × 90 ≈ 0.75 kJ"]},{"text":"The ice slope is narrowest, and the melting plateau is narrower than the liquid slope.","strike":["Warming the ice","Melting the ice"]},{"text":"Answer: warming the water."}],"trap":"Assuming the phase change always takes the most heat: here 90 degrees of liquid warming outweighs melting."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"−40 °C","note":"start: ice"},{"t":"liquid water at 90 °C","note":"end: no boiling"},{"t":"most heat","note":"widest segment"}]},{"text":"**Read widths, not heights.** On a heat-added axis, each segment's horizontal width is the heat it took."},{"text":"**Also:** if the curve had gone on to steam, vaporizing would dwarf every other step: L_vap = 2260 J/g, almost seven times L_fus and more than five times the heat to warm water from 0 to 100 °C."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to find the step of a heating curve that takes the most heat, compare mL for each plateau and mcΔT for each slope (or read widths); vaporization wins only if boiling is part of the curve."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-49a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:49#1","pe5-cp:49#2","pe5-cp:49#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:49"],"passage":"A walking robot is tested on soft flooring. A robot's square foot pad, 4.0 cm on a side, presses straight down on the floor with 72 N when one foot is lifted. The flooring dents when the pressure under the pad goes above a set limit. Engineers compare the pad pressure with that limit.","q":"What pressure does the pad exert on the floor?","choices":["45 kPa","450 kPa","4.5 kPa","450 Pa"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Too big: that's 72 N on just 1.6 cm², ten times too little area; the pad is 16 cm²."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"That's 72 ÷ 16, a value in N/cm² mislabeled as kPa; convert cm² to m² first."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Area slip: that uses 1 cm² = 10⁻² m²; one cm² is really 10⁻⁴ m²."}],"rule":"P = F ÷ A with A in m², and 1 cm² = 10⁻⁴ m². Divide in N/cm² with rounded numbers, then × 10⁴ for Pa.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Divide in cm² first: 72 N ÷ 16 cm² = 4.5 N/cm². Each m² is 10⁴ cm², so × 10⁴ → 45,000 Pa = 45 kPa."},"exp":"Pressure is force over area in square metres, **P = F/A**. The pad is 0.040 m × 0.040 m = 1.6 × 10⁻³ m², so P = 72 N ÷ 0.0016 m² = 45,000 Pa = **45 kPa**. One cm² is 10⁻⁴ m², not 10⁻² m².","how":{"ask":"Take the force from the sentence, find the pad's area in square metres, then divide.","know":"• **Pressure: P = F/A**, in Pa = N/m².\n• **1 cm = 10⁻² m, so 1 cm² = 10⁻⁴ m²**: multiply the side lengths in cm, then × 10⁻⁴.\n• 1 kPa = 10³ Pa.","steps":[{"text":"Pull the force out of the sentence: it is the push straight down on the floor.","hl":"presses straight down on the floor with 72 N","calc":["F = **72 N** (perpendicular to the floor)"]},{"text":"Area in cm², then in m².","hl":"4.0 cm on a side","calc":["A = 4.0 cm × 4.0 cm = 16 cm²","A = 16 × 10⁻⁴ m² = **1.6 × 10⁻³ m²**"]},{"text":"Divide the force by the area.","calc":["P = F ÷ A","P = 72 N ÷ (1.6 × 10⁻³ m²)","P = 4.5 × 10⁴ Pa = **45 kPa**"]},{"text":"450 Pa converts the area with 10⁻² instead of 10⁻⁴; 4.5 kPa is 72 ÷ 16, a value in N/cm² mislabeled as kPa; 450 kPa slips one extra power of ten into the area.","strike":["450 Pa","4.5 kPa","450 kPa"]},{"text":"Answer: 45 kPa."}],"trap":"Squaring the length but not the conversion: 1 cm² is (10⁻² m)² = 10⁻⁴ m²."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"square foot pad","note":"area = side × side"},{"t":"4.0 cm on a side","note":"convert to m"},{"t":"72 N","note":"force F"},{"t":"What pressure","note":"P in Pa"}]},{"text":"**P = F/A.** Pressure is the force spread over each square metre of contact."},{"text":"**Also: convert cm² to m².** Multiply the sides in cm, then × 10⁻⁴.","calc":["4.0 × 4.0 = 16 cm²","16 cm² × 10⁻⁴ = 0.0016 m²","72 ÷ 0.0016 = 45,000 Pa = 45 kPa"]},{"text":"**Also: take F from the passage.** A passage states the force in a sentence; pick the number in newtons that describes the push on the surface, here the 72 N pressing straight down."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** P = F/A with A in m²; for sides in cm, multiply them and then × 10⁻⁴ before dividing."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-50a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:50#1","pe5-cp:50#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:50"],"passage":"A portable heater warms a lab sample with brief pulses of current. A 30 μF capacitor charged to 800 V holds 9.6 J. On each pulse it empties fully through a heating coil in 3.0 ms. The coil's resistance is constant.","q":"What average current flows during a pulse?","choices":["8.0 A","8.0 mA","3.2 kA","24 mA"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"The time was left in milliseconds; 3.0 ms is 0.0030 s, so the current is 1000 times off."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"J ÷ s gives watts, a power, not amperes: that divides the stored energy, not the charge, by the time."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That's the charge, Q = 0.024 C, relabeled; a current needs Q ÷ t."}],"rule":"Current is charge per second: Q = CV, then divide by t in seconds; the joules are a distractor. Round C and V to 1 digit, keep the powers of 10.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Amps = coulombs per second, so ignore the joules. Q = 30 μ × 800 = 24 000 μC = 0.024 C; ÷ 0.003 s: 24 ÷ 3 = 8, powers cancel → 8 A."},"exp":"**Q = CV** = 30 × 10⁻⁶ F × 800 V = 0.024 C. **I = Q/t** = 0.024 C ÷ (3.0 × 10⁻³ s) = **8.0 A**. The 9.6 J of stored energy is not needed.","how":{"ask":"Find the charge that leaves the plates, then divide it by the discharge time in seconds.","know":"• **Q = CV** (C in farads, V in volts, Q in coulombs).\n• **Average current: I = Q/t** (A = C/s).\n• **μ = 10⁻⁶, m = 10⁻³**: convert μF and ms first.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the capacitance to farads.","hl":"30 μF","calc":["C = 30 μF = 30 × 10⁻⁶ F"]},{"text":"Find the charge on the plates.","hl":"charged to 800 V","calc":["Q = CV","Q = 30 × 10⁻⁶ F × 800 V","Q = **0.024 C**"]},{"text":"All of that charge flows out in 3.0 ms; convert the time and divide.","hl":"in 3.0 ms","calc":["t = 3.0 ms = 3.0 × 10⁻³ s","I = Q ÷ t","I = 0.024 C ÷ (3.0 × 10⁻³ s)","I = **8.0 A**"]},{"text":"8.0 mA divides by 3.0 with the time left in milliseconds; 24 mA is the charge, 0.024 C, read as a current without dividing by t.","strike":["8.0 mA","24 mA"]},{"text":"3.2 kA divides the energy by the time, 9.6 J ÷ 0.0030 s, which is a power in watts, not a current.","strike":["3.2 kA"]},{"text":"Answer: 8.0 A."}],"trap":"Reaching for the stored energy: current is charge per time, so use Q = CV and skip the joules."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"30 μF","note":"C: convert μ"},{"t":"800 V","note":"V"},{"t":"9.6 J","note":"energy: not needed"},{"t":"3.0 ms","note":"t: convert m"},{"t":"average current","note":"I = Q/t"}]},{"text":"**Q = CV.** The stored charge is capacitance × voltage, in farads × volts.","calc":["Q = 30 × 10⁻⁶ × 800 = 0.024 C"]},{"text":"**Also: average current = charge ÷ time**, with μF → 10⁻⁶ F and ms → 10⁻³ s.","calc":["3.0 ms = 0.0030 s","I = 0.024 ÷ 0.0030 = 8.0 A"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a capacitor discharge, I_avg = Q/t = CV/t, with μF → 10⁻⁶ F and ms → 10⁻³ s; the stored energy is a distractor."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-51a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:51#1","pe5-cp:51#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:51"],"passage":"A touch sensor stores charge on a parallel-plate capacitor. The capacitor stays wired to a 12 V battery while the sensor is on. Designers want more stored charge so the signal is larger. The space between the plates is air.","q":"What change raises the amount of charge its plates hold?","choices":["Inserting a glass sheet","Moving the plates apart","Cutting the plates smaller","Swapping to a 6 V battery"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"C = κε₀A/d: a bigger d lowers C, so Q = CV falls."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Smaller plates shrink A, which lowers C, so the charge drops instead of rising."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Q = CV: halving V halves Q, so a smaller battery can't raise the charge."}],"rule":"With the battery attached, Q = CV: only a change that raises C (bigger A, smaller d, a dielectric) or raises V adds charge.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Battery attached means V is locked, so Q rises only if C rises. C = κε₀A/d: only a dielectric (bigger κ) helps; apart, smaller plates or lower V all cut Q."},"exp":"The battery holds V at 12 V, so the charge **Q = CV** can grow only through a larger C. Since **C = κε₀A/d**, a glass sheet (κ ≈ 5) raises C about fivefold, and the battery moves about five times the charge onto the plates.","how":{"ask":"Hold V fixed and decide which change raises C = κε₀A/d.","know":"• **Q = CV**; with the battery connected, **V is fixed**.\n• **C = κε₀A/d**: C rises with larger area A, smaller separation d, or a dielectric (κ > 1).\n• At fixed C, a higher V also raises Q.","steps":[{"text":"The battery stays connected, so V is fixed and Q follows C.","hl":"stays wired to a 12 V battery","calc":["Q = C × 12 V"]},{"text":"A glass sheet is a dielectric (κ ≈ 5): C rises about fivefold, and so does Q.","hl":"Inserting a glass sheet","calc":["C' = κC ≈ 5C","Q' = 5C × 12 V = 5Q"]},{"text":"Moving the plates apart raises d, and cutting the plates shrinks A; both lower C and so lower Q.","strike":["Moving the plates apart","Cutting the plates smaller"]},{"text":"A 6 V battery halves V, which halves Q at the same C.","strike":["Swapping to a 6 V battery"]},{"text":"Answer: inserting a glass sheet."}],"trap":"Thinking a dielectric lowers the charge because it weakens the field: that happens only in an isolated capacitor; with the battery connected, V and E stay fixed and the plates take on κ times the charge."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"parallel-plate capacitor","note":"C = κε₀A/d"},{"t":"stays wired","note":"V fixed"},{"t":"12 V battery","note":"V"},{"t":"amount of charge","note":"Q = CV"}]},{"text":"**Q = CV and C = κε₀A/d.** At fixed V, anything that raises C raises Q."},{"text":"**Also: the four ways up.** More plate area, a smaller gap, a dielectric, or a higher voltage each increase Q.","calc":["A ×2 → Q ×2","d ×½ → Q ×2","κ = 5 → Q ×5","V ×2 → Q ×2"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** with Q = CV and C = κε₀A/d, charge rises with larger area, smaller separation, an inserted dielectric, or a higher voltage."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-52a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:52#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:52"],"passage":"A camera flash stores charge on a capacitor before each picture. In the old design, a 15 μF capacitor charged to 800 V supplies each flash. The new design swaps it for a 40 μF capacitor. The flash tube needs the same charge per flash as before.","q":"What voltage would give the new capacitor the same charge?","choices":["300 V","2100 V","800 V","490 V"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Ratio upside-down: a bigger capacitor needs less voltage for the same Q, so V must drop below 800 V."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"That keeps the old voltage; the same V on a bigger C puts more charge on it (Q = CV)."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"That matches the stored energy (½CV²), a square-root ratio; equal charge needs Q = CV."}],"rule":"Same charge means C₁V₁ = C₂V₂: a bigger capacitor needs a smaller voltage. Multiply V by old C ÷ new C, rounded to 1 digit.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Same Q means V ∝ 1/C. C grows 15 → 40, a factor 8/3, so V = 800 × 3/8 = 100 × 3 = 300 V."},"exp":"**Same Q: C₁V₁ = C₂V₂.** Q = 15 μF × 800 V = 0.012 C, so V₂ = 0.012 C ÷ 40 μF = **300 V**. A larger capacitor needs proportionally less voltage to hold the same charge.","how":{"ask":"Write the charge on the first capacitor and solve for the voltage that puts that charge on the second.","know":"• **Q = CV**; equal charge means **C₁V₁ = C₂V₂**.\n• So **V₂ = C₁V₁/C₂**: a bigger C needs less V.\n• The μF units cancel in the ratio C₁/C₂.","steps":[{"text":"Find the charge on the first capacitor.","hl":"a 15 μF capacitor charged to 800 V","calc":["Q = C₁V₁ = 15 × 10⁻⁶ F × 800 V","Q = **0.012 C**"]},{"text":"Solve for V₂.","hl":"a 40 μF capacitor","calc":["V₂ = C₁V₁ ÷ C₂","V₂ = (15 μF × 800 V) ÷ 40 μF","V₂ = **300 V**"]},{"text":"2100 V multiplies by C₂/C₁ instead of C₁/C₂; 800 V keeps the old voltage, which would put more charge on the larger capacitor.","strike":["2100 V","800 V"]},{"text":"490 V matches the stored energy (½CV²) instead of the charge.","calc":["800 V × √(15/40) ≈ 490 V"],"strike":["490 V"]},{"text":"Answer: 300 V."}],"trap":"Flipping the ratio: a larger capacitor holds the same charge at a lower voltage, so V₂ must come out below 800 V."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"15 μF","note":"C₁"},{"t":"800 V","note":"V₁"},{"t":"40 μF","note":"C₂"},{"t":"same charge","note":"Q₁ = Q₂"}]},{"text":"**Equal charge: C₁V₁ = C₂V₂.** Since Q = CV, set the two charges equal."},{"text":"**Solve as a ratio.** The μF cancel.","calc":["V₂ = 800 V × 15/40","V₂ = 800 V × 0.375 = 300 V"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for equal charge on two capacitors, V₂ = C₁V₁/C₂; the larger capacitor needs proportionally less voltage."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-53a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:53#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:53"],"passage":"Deep-tissue microscopes use longer wavelengths because they scatter less in tissue. A lab retunes its laser, in air, from a wavelength of 500 nm to 1500 nm to image deeper into a brain slice. The laser's power is kept the same. The speed of light in air does not depend on wavelength.","q":"After the change, the light's frequency is:","choices":["one-third as high.","three times as high.","unchanged.","one-ninth as high."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Frequency and wavelength are inversely related, so a longer wavelength cannot raise the frequency."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"With c fixed, changing λ must change f; only the power was kept the same."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Squares the ratio: c = fλ is a first-power relation, so f scales by the ratio itself."}],"rule":"c = fλ with c fixed: f ∝ 1/λ, so scaling λ by n scales f by 1/n (not 1/n²).","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"The wavelength triples (500 → 1500 nm). Since fλ is constant, the frequency falls by the same factor."},"exp":"In air, c = fλ is constant. The wavelength goes from 500 nm to 1500 nm, three times longer, so the frequency falls to **one-third** of its old value. Power does not affect frequency.","how":{"ask":"By what factor did λ change, and how must f change to keep fλ constant?","know":"• **c = fλ**; in one medium c is fixed.\n• So **f ∝ 1/λ**: doubling λ halves f, tripling λ cuts f to one-third.\n• Photon energy E = hf also falls by that factor.","steps":[{"text":"Find the wavelength ratio.","hl":"from a wavelength of 500 nm to 1500 nm","calc":["1500 / 500 = 3"]},{"text":"Keep fλ constant.","calc":["f₂ = f₁ × (500/1500) = f₁/3"]},{"text":"Longer λ cannot mean higher f.","strike":["three times as high."]},{"text":"f must change, and by the first power of the ratio, not its square.","strike":["unchanged.","one-ninth as high."]},{"text":"Answer: one-third as high."}],"trap":"Squaring the ratio as if f ∝ 1/λ²: the relation is first power."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"500 nm to 1500 nm","note":"λ × 3"},{"t":"does not depend on wavelength","note":"c fixed"}]},{"text":"**Inverse scaling.** With c = fλ fixed, any factor on λ appears as its reciprocal on f."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in one medium, f = c/λ, so multiplying the wavelength by n divides the frequency by n."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-54a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:54#1","pe5-cp:54#2","pe5-cp:54#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:54"],"passage":"A night-vision study compares two light sources. One emits infrared light and the other ultraviolet light. Each ultraviolet photon carries three times the energy of an infrared photon. Both beams travel in air. The detector counts single photons.","q":"Compared with the infrared photon, the ultraviolet photon has:","choices":["3× the frequency; ⅓ the wavelength.","3× the frequency; 3× the wavelength.","⅓ the frequency; 3× the wavelength.","9× the frequency; ⅓ the wavelength."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"λ = c/f, so the wavelength must fall when the frequency rises; it can't triple too."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"E = hf: more energy means more frequency, never ⅓ of it."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"E = hf is linear, with nothing squared: tripling the energy triples the frequency, it doesn't make it 9×."}],"rule":"E = hf and λ = c/f: energy and frequency change by the same factor, and wavelength changes by the inverse factor.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"E ∝ f and E ∝ 1/λ. Triple the energy: f triples, λ drops to a third. Only one choice pairs 3× with ⅓."},"exp":"**E = hf**: energy is proportional to frequency, so 3× the energy means **3× the frequency**. **E = hc/λ**: energy is inversely proportional to wavelength, so 3× the energy means **⅓ the wavelength**.","how":{"ask":"Scale frequency and wavelength by the energy factor.","know":"• **E = hf** (h is a constant): f ∝ E.\n• **E = hc/λ** (c fixed): λ ∝ 1/E.\n• A photon with k times the energy has k times the frequency and 1/k the wavelength.","steps":[{"text":"The energy factor is k = 3.","hl":"three times the energy"},{"text":"Frequency scales directly with energy.","calc":["f = E/h → f(UV) = 3 × f(IR)"]},{"text":"Wavelength scales inversely with energy.","calc":["λ = hc/E → λ(UV) = λ(IR) ÷ 3"]},{"text":"Wavelength cannot rise with energy, frequency cannot fall, and nothing squares here.","strike":["3× the frequency; 3× the wavelength.","⅓ the frequency; 3× the wavelength.","9× the frequency; ⅓ the wavelength."]},{"text":"Answer: 3× the frequency and ⅓ the wavelength."}],"trap":"Scaling wavelength the same way as frequency: since c = fλ is fixed, they always move in opposite directions."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"three times the energy","note":"k = 3"},{"t":"ultraviolet photon","note":"higher-energy one"}]},{"text":"**Frequency follows energy.** E = hf, so f is proportional to E.","calc":["E × 3 → f × 3"]},{"text":"**Wavelength opposes energy.** E = hc/λ, so λ is inversely proportional to E.","calc":["E × 3 → λ ÷ 3"]},{"text":"**Also:** a photon with k times the energy has 1/k the wavelength, because f and λ multiply to the fixed speed c."},{"text":"**Also:** amplitude and intensity describe a beam, not one photon. A brighter beam has more photons per second, but each photon's energy, frequency and wavelength stay the same."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hf = hc/λ, so k times the energy means k times the frequency and 1/k the wavelength."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-55a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:55#1","pe5-cp:55#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:55"],"passage":"A plant-growth chamber uses LEDs tuned to the light that leaves absorb. One diode emits blue-green light with a wavelength of 480 nm in air. Its frequency sets the energy each photon delivers. The light passes through air before it reaches the leaves.","q":"Approximately what is the frequency of this light?","choices":["6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz","6.3 × 10⁵ Hz","6.3 × 10¹⁷ Hz","1.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ Hz"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Skips the nm → m step (10⁻⁹); 10⁵ Hz is radio, far below visible light."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Uses picometres instead of nanometres, 1000 times too high: 10¹⁷ Hz is X-ray, not visible light."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Flipped: that's λ ÷ c, a time in seconds; frequency is c ÷ λ."}],"rule":"f = c/λ with λ in metres (1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m). Divide the leading digits and the powers of 10 separately.","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"f = c/λ: 3 × 10⁸ ÷ 4.8 × 10⁻⁷. Digits 3 ÷ 4.8 ≈ 0.63; powers 10⁸⁺⁷ = 10¹⁵ → 0.63 × 10¹⁵ = 6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz. Visible light always lands near 10¹⁴."},"exp":"**f = c/λ** with λ in metres: 480 nm = 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m, so f = (3 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ (4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m) ≈ **6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz**, inside the visible band of about 4–7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz.","how":{"ask":"Convert the wavelength to metres, then divide the speed of light by it.","know":"• **c = fλ**, so **f = c/λ**, with c ≈ 3 × 10⁸ m/s and λ in **metres**.\n• 1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m.\n• Visible light (about 400–750 nm) has f ≈ 4–7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz, so any visible answer must land near 10¹⁴–10¹⁵ Hz.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the wavelength to metres.","hl":"480 nm","calc":["480 nm × 10⁻⁹ m/nm = 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m"]},{"text":"Divide c by λ.","calc":["f = (3 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ (4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m)","= (3 ÷ 4.8) × 10¹⁵ Hz","= 0.625 × 10¹⁵ ≈ **6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz**"]},{"text":"Check the band: blue-green light must sit between 4 and 7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz. 10⁵ Hz skips the nm conversion (radio range), 10¹⁷ Hz uses picometres (X-ray range), and 10⁻¹⁵ divides λ by c.","strike":["6.3 × 10⁵ Hz","6.3 × 10¹⁷ Hz","1.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ Hz"]},{"text":"Answer: 6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz."}],"trap":"Dividing by 480 instead of 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m: keep λ in metres, and use the visible band as a sanity check."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and its unit.","marks":[{"t":"480 nm","note":"convert to m"},{"t":"blue-green light","note":"visible: expect ~10¹⁴ Hz"}]},{"text":"**Fix the unit.** Speed of light is in m/s, so λ must be in m.","calc":["480 × 10⁻⁹ m = 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m"]},{"text":"**Divide.**","calc":["f = c/λ = 3 × 10⁸ ÷ 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ ≈ 6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz"]},{"text":"**Also:** visible light spans about 400–750 nm, which is about 7.5 × 10¹⁴ down to 4 × 10¹⁴ Hz; a visible-light frequency far outside 10¹⁴–10¹⁵ Hz signals a unit error."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** f = c/λ with λ in metres (nm × 10⁻⁹), then check that visible light lands near 4–7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-56a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:56#1"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:56"],"passage":"Near-infrared light passes through skin far better than visible light. A wearable glucose sensor uses an LED whose photons each carry 1.55 eV. The product of Planck's constant and the speed of light is about 1240 eV·nm. The emitted light travels through air before reaching the skin.","q":"The wavelength of the LED's light is approximately:","choices":["800 nm","80 nm","8000 nm","1900 nm"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Off by a factor of ten: a decimal slip in the division puts the light in the far ultraviolet."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Off by a factor of ten the other way, far into the infrared."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Multiplies hc by E instead of dividing; λ = hc/E falls as energy rises."}],"rule":"λ(nm) ≈ 1240 / E(eV). Visible light is about 1.8–3.1 eV (700–400 nm); lower energy means longer wavelength.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"1240 ÷ 1.55: 1.55 × 800 = 1240. A bit below 1.8 eV puts it just past red, in the near infrared."},"exp":"E = hc/λ, so λ = hc/E = (1240 eV·nm)/(1.55 eV) = **800 nm**, near-infrared light, just past red.","how":{"ask":"What is hc/E in nanometres?","know":"• **E = hf = hc/λ**, so λ = hc/E.\n• **hc ≈ 1240 eV·nm**.\n• Visible: 400 nm (≈ 3.1 eV) to 700 nm (≈ 1.8 eV).","steps":[{"text":"Take E and hc from the passage.","hl":"each carry 1.55 eV","calc":["λ = hc/E"]},{"text":"Divide.","calc":["λ = 1240 / 1.55 = 800 nm"]},{"text":"A factor-of-ten slip either way leaves the near infrared.","strike":["80 nm","8000 nm"]},{"text":"hc × E has the wrong units and the wrong trend.","strike":["1900 nm"]},{"text":"Answer: about 800 nm."}],"trap":"Multiplying instead of dividing: higher photon energy must give a shorter wavelength."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"1.55 eV","note":"photon energy"},{"t":"1240 eV·nm","note":"hc"},{"t":"Near-infrared","note":"expect a bit over 700 nm"}]},{"text":"**Shortcut constant.** With hc = 1240 eV·nm, λ in nm is 1240 divided by E in eV."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** λ(nm) = 1240/E(eV); check the answer against the visible range, 400–700 nm."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-57a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:57#1","pe5-cp:57#2","pe5-cp:57#3"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:57"],"q":"Hypochlorous acid, HOCl, has a pKa of 7.5 in water. What is Kb of its conjugate base, the hypochlorite ion?","choices":["3.2 × 10⁻⁷","3.2 × 10⁻⁸","3.2 × 10⁻²²","6.5"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Kb = Kw/Ka; multiplying Ka by Kw instead gives 10⁻²², far too small."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"6.5 is pKb (14 − 7.5); the last step, Kb = 10^−pKb, was skipped. Kb for a weak base is a small power of ten."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"That's Ka of the acid itself, 10^−7.5, taken straight from the given pKa; the base's Kb is different."}],"rule":"Conjugate pairs: pKa + pKb = 14 (Ka·Kb = 10⁻¹⁴), so the weaker the acid, the larger its conjugate base's Kb.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"pKa + pKb = 14 → pKb 6.5. 10^−6.5 = 10^0.5 × 10⁻⁷, and 10^0.5 ≈ 3.2, so Kb ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻⁷."},"exp":"**pKa + pKb = 14**, so pKb = 14 − 7.5 = 6.5, and **Kb = 10^−pKb** = 10⁻⁶·⁵ ≈ **3.2 × 10⁻⁷**. Check: Ka·Kb = (3.2 × 10⁻⁸)(3.2 × 10⁻⁷) ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ = Kw.","how":{"ask":"Get pKb from the pKa, then turn pKb into Kb.","know":"• For a conjugate pair: **pKa + pKb = 14** and **Ka·Kb = Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴** (at 25 °C).\n• **Kb = 10^−pKb**.\n• A weaker acid (higher pKa) has a stronger conjugate base (larger Kb).","steps":[{"text":"Start from the acid's pKa.","hl":"pKa of 7.5","calc":["pKb = 14 − 7.5 = **6.5**"]},{"text":"Convert pKb to Kb.","calc":["Kb = 10⁻⁶·⁵ = 10⁰·⁵ × 10⁻⁷ ≈ 3.16 × 10⁻⁷ ≈ **3.2 × 10⁻⁷**"]},{"text":"Check with Ka·Kb = Kw.","calc":["Ka = 10⁻⁷·⁵ ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻⁸","Kb = Kw/Ka = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 3.2 × 10⁻⁸ ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻⁷ ✓"]},{"text":"3.2 × 10⁻⁸ is Ka itself, 3.2 × 10⁻²² multiplies Ka by Kw instead of dividing, and 6.5 is pKb, not Kb.","strike":["3.2 × 10⁻⁸","3.2 × 10⁻²²","6.5"]},{"text":"Answer: 3.2 × 10⁻⁷."}],"trap":"Stopping at pKb = 6.5: the question asks for Kb, so finish with Kb = 10^−pKb."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"pKa of 7.5","note":"the acid's strength"},{"t":"Kb of its conjugate base","note":"base strength"}]},{"text":"**Use the pair rule.**","calc":["pKa + pKb = 14 → pKb = 6.5"]},{"text":"**Undo the log.**","calc":["Kb = 10⁻⁶·⁵ ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻⁷"]},{"text":"**Also:** Kb = 10^−pKb; a half-unit pKb gives 10⁰·⁵ ≈ 3.16 in front of the power of ten."},{"text":"**Also:** Ka·Kb = Kw, so Kb = Kw/Ka; the weaker the acid (smaller Ka), the stronger its conjugate base."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pKb = 14 − pKa and Kb = 10^−pKb (equivalently Kb = Kw/Ka)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-58a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:58#1","pe5-cp:58#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:58"],"q":"Acetic acid and ethanol form ethyl acetate and water over an acidic resin catalyst at 70 °C. Which change would NOT raise the equilibrium amount of ester?","choices":["Adding more ethanol","Adding a drying agent","Adding extra glacial acetic acid","Grinding the resin finer"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Adding a reactant lowers Q below K, so the reaction shifts toward more ester."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A drying agent removes water, a product, so the equilibrium shifts right and makes more ester."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"More of the other reactant also lowers Q below K and pushes the system toward ester."}],"rule":"In a NOT question, three choices shift the equilibrium (add reactant, remove product); the odd one changes only the rate, such as anything done to the catalyst.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Sort the choices: reactant added, product removed, reactant added... and one that only changes how fast equilibrium is reached. Rate is not yield."},"exp":"Adding ethanol or acetic acid (reactants) or removing water (product) shifts the equilibrium toward ethyl acetate (Le Châtelier). A catalyst, in any amount or particle size, speeds both directions equally: it reaches equilibrium sooner but **does not change K or the yield**.","how":{"ask":"Which change only alters the rate, leaving Q and K untouched?","know":"• **Le Châtelier**: adding a reactant or removing a product shifts the reaction toward products.\n• A **catalyst** lowers Ea for the forward and reverse steps equally: faster equilibrium, same position.\n• Only temperature changes K.","steps":[{"text":"Write the equilibrium.","calc":["CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O"]},{"text":"Adding either reactant lowers Q below K, so more ester forms.","strike":["Adding more ethanol","Adding extra glacial acetic acid"]},{"text":"Removing water, a product, also lowers Q.","strike":["Adding a drying agent"]},{"text":"Finer resin only exposes more catalyst surface: faster, not further.","hl":"acidic resin catalyst"},{"text":"Answer: grinding the resin finer."}],"trap":"Thinking more catalyst means more product: a catalyst never moves the equilibrium position."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"acidic resin catalyst","note":"rate only"},{"t":"NOT","note":"find the one that fails"},{"t":"equilibrium amount","note":"yield, not speed"}]},{"text":"**Two kinds of change.** Concentration changes move Q away from K and shift the reaction; catalyst changes leave Q and K alone and only speed things up."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to raise an equilibrium yield, add a reactant or remove a product; changing the catalyst (amount, surface area or type) changes only the rate."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-59a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe5-cp:59#1","pe5-cp:59#2"],"twinOf":["pe5-cp:59"],"q":"A robot drags a sled 12 m on a level track. The graph shows its parallel pull and the friction on the sled. How much work does the pull do?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Forces on the sled versus position","x":{"label":"Position (m)","min":0,"max":12,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Force (N)","min":0,"max":50,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"Pull","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[2,20],[4,40],[6,40],[8,40],[10,40],[11,20],[12,0]]},{"name":"Friction","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,5],[12,5]]}]},"choices":["360 J","480 J","300 J","240 J"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"40 N × 12 m treats the pull as 40 N all the way, but the graph ramps up and down."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"6 m × 40 N counts only the flat top and drops both ramp triangles."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"That subtracts friction's 5 N × 12 m: it is the net work, but the question asks for the pull's work alone."}],"rule":"Work by one force = area under that force's line on a force-vs-distance graph; count ramps as triangles, ½ × base × height.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Area under the pull line = trapezoid: average of the two bases × height. Top 6 m, bottom 12 m → 9 × 40 = 360 J. Friction line is irrelevant."},"exp":"Work done by a changing force is the **area under its F–x line**. Split the pull into a triangle, a rectangle and a triangle: ½(4)(40) + (6)(40) + ½(2)(40) = 80 + 240 + 40 = **360 J**. Friction is not part of the pull's work.","how":{"ask":"Find the area under the pull line only, from 0 to 12 m.","know":"• **W = area under the F–x graph** (N × m = J) for a force along the motion.\n• Split the area into **rectangles (base × height)** and **triangles (½ × base × height)**.\n• Use only the force the question names.","steps":[{"text":"Read the corners of the pull line off the graph.","hl":"How much work does the pull do","calc":["0 → 4 m: rises 0 → 40 N","4 → 10 m: flat at 40 N","10 → 12 m: falls 40 → 0 N"],"graph":{"focus":["Pull"]}},{"text":"Find the area of each piece.","calc":["ramp up: ½ × 4 m × 40 N = 80 J","plateau: 6 m × 40 N = 240 J","ramp down: ½ × 2 m × 40 N = 40 J"],"graph":{"focus":["Pull"],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":12,"text":"area = work by the pull"}]}},{"text":"Add the pieces.","calc":["W = 80 J + 240 J + 40 J = **360 J**"]},{"text":"480 J is 40 N × 12 m, treating the pull as 40 N the whole way; 240 J keeps only the flat part.","strike":["480 J","240 J"]},{"text":"300 J subtracts friction's 5 N × 12 m = 60 J, which gives the net work, not the pull's work.","strike":["300 J"]},{"text":"Answer: 360 J."}],"trap":"Multiplying the peak force by the whole distance; the ramps are triangles, worth only half of their rectangles."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"12 m","note":"total distance"},{"t":"parallel pull","note":"all of the pull does work"},{"t":"work does the pull do","note":"pull only, not friction"}]},{"text":"**Work is force × distance**, but this pull changes with position, so no single force works. Instead, the **work equals the area under the pull line**.","graph":{"focus":["Pull"],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":12,"text":"area = work"}]}},{"text":"**Also: split the shape into rectangles and triangles.** Here: a rising ramp, a flat top and a falling ramp.","calc":["triangle: ½ × base × height","rectangle: base × height"]},{"text":"**Ignore the friction line.** It shares the axes, but the question asks only about the pull.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Friction","x":6,"text":"friction: not asked","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the work by a varying force is the area under the line of that force on an F–x plot; cut it into rectangles and triangles, add the pieces, and use only the force asked about."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-01a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:1#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:1"],"passage":"A student reduced cyclohexanone with sodium borohydride in methanol and then quenched the mixture with dilute acid. The crude six-carbon liquid was distilled, and its IR spectrum shown in the figure was recorded. The student expected the reduced product but also considered three other outcomes: no reaction at all, loss of water from the product during the acidic workup, or cleavage of the ring to an acid.","q":"Based on the IR spectrum, which compound did the student isolate?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"IR spectrum of the unknown liquid","x":{"label":"Wavenumber (cm⁻¹)","min":500,"max":4000,"step":500},"y":{"label":"Transmittance (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Unknown","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[500,88],[600,80],[800,72],[1000,45],[1070,30],[1150,70],[1250,78],[1350,62],[1450,55],[1550,88],[1650,92],[1700,92],[1800,92],[2000,94],[2500,92],[2800,85],[2860,45],[2930,30],[3000,65],[3100,50],[3200,30],[3350,15],[3450,22],[3550,45],[3650,82],[3800,92],[4000,94]]}]},"choices":["Cyclohexanol","Cyclohexanone","Cyclohexene","Hexanoic acid"],"choiceFigs":["cyclohexanol","cyclohexanone","cpt-pe6-cyclohexene","cpt-pe6-hexanoic-acid"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A ketone's C=O makes a deep, sharp dip near 1700 cm⁻¹; this trace stays above 90% there."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An alkene has no O–H, so it can't make the broad, deep dip centered near 3350 cm⁻¹."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A carboxylic acid has a C=O too, so it would dip hard near 1700 cm⁻¹; the trace stays flat there."}],"rule":"Check two IR windows: a broad deep dip near 3300 cm⁻¹ means O–H; a sharp strong dip near 1700 means C=O. Keep the compound matching both.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"IR pair to memorize: broad ~3300 = O–H, sharp ~1700 = C=O. Broad O–H and no 1700 dip → alcohol, so cyclohexanol (acid would also show C=O)."},"exp":"The trace has a **broad, deep dip centered near 3350 cm⁻¹ (O–H)** and **nothing near 1700 cm⁻¹ (no C=O)**, plus C–H dips just under 3000 and a C–O dip near 1070. An O–H with no C=O is an alcohol: cyclohexanol. A carboxylic acid would also show a strong C=O near 1710.","how":{"ask":"Is there a broad dip between 3200 and 3600 cm⁻¹, and is there a sharp dip near 1700 cm⁻¹?","know":"• IR windows: **O–H** broad, 3200–3600 cm⁻¹; **N–H** 3300–3500, narrower; **C–H** 2850–3100; **C=O** sharp and strong, ~1700; **C=C** weaker, ~1650.\n• Carboxylic acid = C=O plus a very broad O–H that spreads down toward 2500.\n• Alcohol = O–H with no C=O; ketone = C=O with no O–H.","steps":[{"text":"Check the O–H window first: a broad, deep dip centered near 3350 cm⁻¹.","hl":"IR spectrum shown","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Unknown","x":3350}],"shade":[{"x1":3200,"x2":3600,"text":"O–H window"}]},"calc":["3350 cm⁻¹: about **15 %** transmittance → strong, broad O–H"]},{"text":"Now the C=O window: the trace stays near the top at 1700 cm⁻¹, so there is no carbonyl. That removes the ketone and the acid.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Unknown","x":1700}],"shade":[{"x1":1650,"x2":1780,"text":"C=O window"}]},"calc":["1700 cm⁻¹: about **92 %** → no C=O"],"strike":["Cyclohexanone","Hexanoic acid"]},{"text":"An alkene has no O–H; its weak C=C band would sit near 1650 and could not make the deep dip at 3350.","strike":["Cyclohexene"]},{"text":"Also: the other windows. N–H (3300–3500) needs nitrogen, which no choice has; the C–H dips at 2850–2930 appear for all four compounds, so they never decide."},{"text":"Answer: cyclohexanol.","fig":"cyclohexanol"}],"trap":"Seeing a broad O–H and picking the acid: an acid always shows a strong C=O near 1700 too, and this spectrum has none."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"six-carbon liquid","note":"all choices are C6"},{"t":"IR spectrum","note":"which bonds are present"}]},{"text":"**Read dips, not peaks.** Low transmittance means a bond absorbed that light."},{"text":"**Know five windows.**","calc":["O–H: 3200–3600, broad","N–H: 3300–3500","C–H: 2850–3100","C=O: ~1700, sharp","C=C: ~1650, weak"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** check 3200–3600 cm⁻¹ for a broad O–H and ~1700 for a sharp C=O: O–H alone = alcohol, C=O alone = ketone or aldehyde, both (with a very broad O–H) = carboxylic acid."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-01b","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:1#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:1"],"passage":"Heating an alcohol with a strong acid can give several different products, and the reaction sometimes stalls. A student heated cyclohexanol with H₃PO₄ and slowly distilled out the volatile material as it formed. IR spectra were recorded before and after the reaction, on the starting alcohol and on the distillate, as plotted in the figure.","q":"Which compound is the distillate, based on the IR spectra?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"IR spectra of the starting alcohol (Before) and the distillate (After)","x":{"label":"Wavenumber (cm⁻¹)","min":500,"max":4000,"step":500},"y":{"label":"Transmittance (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Before","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[500,86],[600,82],[800,78],[1000,60],[1100,30],[1200,70],[1380,58],[1460,55],[1600,90],[1720,90],[1800,92],[2000,94],[2500,92],[2800,85],[2880,45],[2970,30],[3050,60],[3150,45],[3250,25],[3350,15],[3450,22],[3550,48],[3650,85],[4000,94]]},{"name":"After","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[500,88],[600,85],[800,80],[1000,82],[1170,75],[1300,80],[1370,60],[1460,58],[1600,90],[1650,68],[1720,90],[1780,88],[2000,94],[2500,93],[2800,88],[2880,50],[2980,35],[3025,62],[3150,90],[3300,93],[3600,94],[4000,95]]}]},"choices":["Cyclohexene","Cyclohexanol","Cyclohexanone","Hexanoic acid"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe6-cyclohexene","cyclohexanol","cyclohexanone","cpt-pe6-hexanoic-acid"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skips the change: the starting alcohol’s broad dip near 3350 cm⁻¹ is gone from the After trace."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A ketone shows a deep, sharp C=O dip near 1715 cm⁻¹; the After trace stays near the top there."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An acid would show both a very broad O–H and a strong C=O near 1710 cm⁻¹; the After trace has neither."}],"rule":"Name an IR product by the bands it lost and gained: no broad 3200–3600 dip means no O–H; no sharp ~1700 dip means no C=O.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Two windows: broad ~3300 = O–H, sharp ~1700 = C=O. The After trace lacks both, so the product has no O at all: the alkene from losing water."},"exp":"Hot H₃PO₄ removes water from an alcohol: **C–OH plus a neighbor H becomes C=C**. The After trace has **lost the broad O–H dip near 3350 cm⁻¹** and shows **no C=O dip near 1700 cm⁻¹**, so the distillate is neither the alcohol, the ketone nor an acid. Only cyclohexene, with C–H dips near 2950 and a weak C=C near 1650, fits.","how":{"ask":"Which bands are missing from the After trace, and which choice lacks the groups that make them?","know":"• IR windows: **O–H** broad, 3200–3600 cm⁻¹; **C=O** sharp and strong, ~1700; **C=C** weak, ~1650; **C–H** 2850–3100.\n• A carboxylic acid shows C=O plus a very broad O–H; an alcohol shows O–H only; a ketone shows C=O only.\n• A product that gains an –OH shows a new broad band above 3000; one that loses it shows that band vanish.","steps":[{"text":"Hot H₃PO₄ can pull water out of an alcohol: the –OH and an H on the next carbon leave, and a C=C forms.","hl":"heated cyclohexanol with H₃PO₄","fig":["cyclohexanol","cpt-pe6-cyclohexene"]},{"text":"Compare at 3350 cm⁻¹: a broad deep dip Before, flat After. The distillate has no O–H, so it is not the starting alcohol.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Before","x":3350},{"series":"After","x":3350}],"shade":[{"x1":3200,"x2":3600,"text":"O–H window"}]},"calc":["Before at 3350: ~15 % → **O–H**","After at 3350: ~93 % → no O–H"],"strike":["Cyclohexanol"]},{"text":"Compare at 1720 cm⁻¹: flat in both traces. No C=O formed, which rules out a ketone and an acid.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Before","x":1720},{"series":"After","x":1720}]},"calc":["After at 1720: ~90 % → no C=O"],"strike":["Cyclohexanone","Hexanoic acid"]},{"text":"Also: the C–H dips near 2950 stay in both traces, so they never decide; a weak dip near 1650 fits a C=C."},{"text":"Answer: cyclohexene.","fig":"cpt-pe6-cyclohexene"}],"trap":"Picking the unchanged alcohol because the C–H dips look the same: the decisive change is the broad O–H dip near 3350 that vanished."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"cyclohexanol","note":"starting alcohol: O–H"},{"t":"H₃PO₄","note":"strong acid, heat"},{"t":"before and after","note":"compare the traces"}]},{"text":"**Map each choice to its bands.**","calc":["alcohol: broad O–H only","ketone: sharp C=O only","acid: C=O + very broad O–H","alkene: neither (weak C=C ~1650)"]},{"text":"**Read the After trace in two windows.** No dip at 3200–3600 and none at ~1700 leaves only the compound with no O–H and no C=O."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to name a product from IR, check the broad O–H window (3200–3600 cm⁻¹) and the sharp C=O window (~1700): the band that appears or vanishes tells you which group was gained or lost."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-02a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe6-cp:2#1","pe6-cp:2#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:2"],"passage":"A new chiral catalyst was designed to reduce a ketone to just one enantiomer of a secondary alcohol. The purified product ran as a single spot on TLC, and every molecule had the same functional groups and the same connectivity. It could still be a mixture of the R and S forms, which are mirror images. A pure sample of the R alcohol is known to rotate plane-polarized light by a fixed angle, and the S alcohol by the same angle in the opposite direction.","q":"Which method would best measure the R : S ratio of the product?","choices":["Polarimetry","NMR integration","Infrared spectrum","Mass spectrometry"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Mirror-image isomers give identical ¹H NMR spectra in an ordinary solvent, so their peaks overlap and cannot be split into a ratio."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"IR shows which functional groups are present; both mirror images have the same groups and the same bands."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Both mirror images have the same mass and break apart the same way, so their mass spectra match."}],"rule":"To measure an isomer ratio, pick the method whose signal differs between the isomers: NMR areas for constitutional isomers, optical rotation for mirror images.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Mirror images differ only in how they rotate plane-polarized light. Observed rotation ÷ pure rotation gives the excess, and from it the ratio."},"exp":"The two products are **enantiomers**: same groups, same connectivity, same mass. NMR, IR and MS all read them the same. Only a chiral measurement tells them apart: **polarimetry**. The observed rotation divided by the pure R rotation gives the enantiomeric excess, and from that the R : S ratio. (For constitutional isomers, ¹H NMR integration would be the tool, since their peaks differ.)","how":{"ask":"What kind of isomers are these, and which measurement gives a different signal for each of them?","know":"• **Constitutional isomers** sharing functional groups: IR looks alike, but ¹H NMR peaks differ, and each integral ∝ number of H, so integrals give the ratio.\n• **Enantiomers**: identical NMR, IR and MS in an achiral setting; they differ only in the sign of optical rotation.\n• % ee = observed rotation ÷ rotation of the pure enantiomer × 100.","steps":[{"text":"Same groups, same connectivity, mirror images: the two products are enantiomers.","hl":"mirror images"},{"text":"IR only identifies functional groups, which both share.","strike":["Infrared spectrum"]},{"text":"NMR integration works for constitutional isomers, whose peaks differ, but mirror images give the same peaks; their masses also match.","strike":["NMR integration","Mass spectrometry"]},{"text":"The passage gives the rotation of the pure R form; the measured rotation of the mixture gives the excess of one form.","hl":"rotate plane-polarized light","calc":["ee = observed ÷ pure","e.g. ee 60% → 80 : 20"]},{"text":"Answer: polarimetry."}],"trap":"Reaching for NMR integration because it measures ratios of constitutional isomers: enantiomers give one identical spectrum."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"same functional groups","note":"IR can’t separate them"},{"t":"same connectivity","note":"not constitutional isomers"},{"t":"mirror images","note":"enantiomers"}]},{"text":"**Match the method to the difference.**","calc":["different groups → IR","different H environments → ¹H NMR areas","mirror images → optical rotation"]},{"text":"**Integration counts H.** For two constitutional isomers, divide each unique peak’s area by its H count to get the mole ratio; enantiomers never give two separate peaks to compare."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to measure how much of each isomer is present, choose the technique whose signal differs between them: ¹H NMR integrals for constitutional isomers, polarimetry for enantiomers; IR only names functional groups."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-03a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:3#1","pe6-cp:3#2","pe6-cp:3#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:3"],"passage":"Lewis acids can isomerize an epoxide into a carbonyl compound. In Step 1, 2,2-dimethyloxirane was stirred with BF₃ etherate: the ring opened toward the more stable carbocation, a 1,2-hydride shift followed, and the carbonyl compound formed showed an aldehyde C–H stretch in its IR spectrum. In Step 2, this carbonyl compound was treated with ethylmagnesium bromide in anhydrous ether, and an acidic aqueous workup came last.","q":"In the passage's sequence, what does Step 2 produce?","fig":"cpt-pe6-dimethyloxirane","choices":["2-Methylpentan-3-ol","3-Methylpentan-3-ol","2-Methylpentan-3-one","2-Methylpropanoic acid"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Stops halfway: a Grignard adds to an aldehyde and acid workup leaves an alcohol, not a new ketone."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"That comes from butan-2-one, the methyl-shift ketone; the passage says a hydride shifted and an aldehyde formed."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"No oxidant is present: a Grignard adds carbon to C=O, it never makes a carboxylic acid."}],"rule":"Epoxide + Lewis acid: open to the most stable cation, shift H or alkyl to make C=O. Then RMgBr adds R to the carbonyl carbon; acid gives the alcohol.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Tertiary cation on C2, H shifts from C1 → (CH₃)₂CH–CHO. EtMgBr adds Et to the CHO carbon → (CH₃)₂CH–CH(OH)–Et, a secondary alcohol."},"exp":"BF₃ binds the epoxide O and the ring opens toward the **tertiary cation on C2**. A **1,2-hydride shift** from C1 moves the + to C1, where O forms C=O: **2-methylpropanal, (CH₃)₂CH–CHO**. In Step 2 the ethyl Grignard adds to that carbonyl carbon, and aqueous acid protonates the alkoxide: **(CH₃)₂CHCH(OH)CH₂CH₃**, a secondary alcohol.","how":{"ask":"Which carbonyl compound does Step 1 make, and what does an ethyl Grignard plus acid do to it?","know":"• A Lewis acid (BF₃) takes a lone pair of the epoxide O; the ring opens to the **more stable carbocation** (3° > 2° > 1°).\n• A **1,2-hydride or alkyl shift** from the C–O carbon then builds C=O there: an H left on it → aldehyde; only carbons → ketone.\n• RMgBr + aldehyde → (acid workup) secondary alcohol; RMgBr + ketone → tertiary alcohol.","steps":[{"text":"BF₃ opens the ring toward the tertiary cation on C2 (two methyls), not the primary CH₂.","hl":"2,2-dimethyloxirane","fig":"cpt-pe6-oxirane-cation","calc":["C2⁺: 3 carbon neighbors → 3°","C1⁺: 1 carbon neighbor → 1°"]},{"text":"An H shifts from C1 to C2, and the O on C1 forms C=O. C1 keeps one H, so it is an aldehyde, as the passage says. A methyl shift would instead give butan-2-one.","hl":"1,2-hydride shift","calc":["(CH₃)₂C⁺–CH₂–O–BF₃⁻ → (CH₃)₂CH–CHO"],"strike":["3-Methylpentan-3-ol"]},{"text":"Step 2: the ethyl group attacks the C=O carbon; acid then protonates the O⁻. The product is an alcohol, not a ketone, and nothing oxidizes it.","hl":"ethylmagnesium bromide","calc":["(CH₃)₂CH–CHO + CH₃CH₂MgBr → (CH₃)₂CH–CH(O⁻)–CH₂CH₃","H₃O⁺ → (CH₃)₂CH–CH(OH)–CH₂CH₃"],"strike":["2-Methylpentan-3-one","2-Methylpropanoic acid"]},{"text":"Answer: 2-methylpentan-3-ol, (CH₃)₂CH–CH(OH)–CH₂CH₃, a secondary alcohol."}],"trap":"Opening the epoxide at the less hindered CH₂ as in a basic opening, or shifting a methyl instead of the hydride the passage names: either leads to the wrong carbonyl and the wrong alcohol."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"BF₃","note":"Lewis acid"},{"t":"2,2-dimethyloxirane","note":"C2 has two CH₃"},{"t":"aldehyde C–H stretch","note":"Step 1 gives an aldehyde"},{"t":"acidic aqueous workup","note":"O⁻ → OH"}]},{"text":"**Lewis acid on O, open toward the stable cation.** BF₃ accepts a lone pair from the epoxide O, and the C–O bond that breaks leaves the tertiary cation.","fig":"cpt-pe6-dimethyloxirane"},{"text":"**Pick the intermediate from the product.** The shift comes from the C–O carbon: if it keeps an H the carbonyl is an aldehyde, if only carbons remain it is a ketone.","calc":["C2⁺ then H shift from C1 → aldehyde"]},{"text":"**Grignard step.** R⁻ adds to the carbonyl carbon; the acid workup gives OH.","calc":["aldehyde → 2° alcohol","ketone → 3° alcohol"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** with a Lewis acid, an epoxide opens to its most stable carbocation and a 1,2-shift builds the C=O; then a Grignard adds its R group to that carbonyl carbon, and acid workup gives the alcohol."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-04a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:4#1","pe6-cp:4#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:4"],"passage":"Many fragrance alcohols are chiral, and the two enantiomers of one compound can smell quite different. A perfumer analyzed a floral-smelling sample of 3-methylhexan-1-ol, whose structure is shown with four carbons marked W, X, Y and Z. Because the sample turned out to be a single enantiomer, the perfumer wanted to know which carbon is responsible for its handedness.","q":"Which marked carbon is a chirality center?","fig":"cpt-pe6-methylhexanol-sites","choices":["W","X","Y","Z"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"The CH₂OH carbon has two H atoms: two identical groups rule it out."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A CH₃ carbon carries three identical hydrogens, so it can never be a chirality center."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Z is a CH₂ in the chain; its two hydrogens are identical groups, so it fails the four-groups test."}],"rule":"A chirality center is an sp³ carbon with four different groups: any CH₂ or CH₃ (repeated H) is out at a glance.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Stereocenter needs four different groups, so skip every CH₂ (two H) and CH₃ (three H). Only the CH branch carbon, X (H, CH₃, propyl, CH₂CH₂OH), is left."},"exp":"To be a **chirality center**, a tetrahedral (sp³) carbon must hold **four different groups**. **X (C3)** carries H, CH₃, CH₂CH₂OH and CH₂CH₂CH₃. W and Z are CH₂ carbons (two H each) and Y is a CH₃. With exactly one such center and no mirror plane, the molecule is **chiral**.","how":{"ask":"Check each marked carbon for four different attached groups.","know":"• **Chirality center**: sp³ carbon with **four different groups**.\n• Instant rejects: **CH₃, CH₂** (repeated H), and any **C=C, C=O or aromatic** carbon.\n• One chirality center and no internal mirror plane → the **molecule is chiral**.","steps":[{"text":"Look at the drawn structure and list the groups on each marked carbon.","hl":"3-methylhexan-1-ol","fig":"cpt-pe6-methylhexanol-sites"},{"text":"Reject carbons with repeated hydrogens.","calc":["W: CH₂OH → H, H ✗","Y: CH₃ → H, H, H ✗","Z: CH₂ → H, H ✗"]},{"text":"W, Y and Z each carry at least two hydrogens.","strike":["W","Y","Z"]},{"text":"Check the remaining carbon.","calc":["X: H, CH₃, CH₂CH₂OH, CH₂CH₂CH₃ → all different ✓"]},{"text":"Answer: X."}],"trap":"Picking the carbon that holds the OH: being next to a heteroatom doesn't help if it still has two H atoms."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"3-methylhexan-1-ol","note":"six-carbon chain, CH₃ on C3"},{"t":"chirality center","note":"four different groups"}]},{"text":"**The four-groups test.** An sp³ carbon is a chirality center only if all four substituents differ; two hydrogens on one carbon fail at once.","calc":["CH₃, CH₂: never","CH with three different other groups: yes"]},{"text":"**Compare the chains, not just the first atom.** On C3, both CH₂ neighbors start alike, but one leads to OH and the other to a CH₃ end, so they differ.","fig":"cpt-pe6-methylhexanol-sites"},{"text":"**Also:** a molecule with exactly one chirality center has no internal mirror plane, so it is chiral: its mirror image cannot be superimposed and the two are enantiomers."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** only a tetrahedral carbon holding four different groups can be a chirality center (skip every CH₂, CH₃ and double-bonded carbon); one such center and no mirror plane makes the molecule chiral."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-05a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:5#1","pe6-cp:5#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:5"],"passage":"On a film set, a camera dolly runs along a straight, level track. For each shot, a motor starts the dolly from rest, holds a steady cruising speed, and finally slows it to a stop beside the actors. A sensor logs the dolly’s position, plotted in the figure from the moment the motor switches on at 1 s.","q":"In which time window does no net force act on the dolly?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Dolly position over time","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":1,"max":10,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Position (m)","min":0,"max":21,"step":3},"series":[{"name":"Dolly","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[1,0],[1.5,0.13],[2,0.5],[2.5,1.13],[3,2],[3.5,3.13],[4,4.5],[5,7.5],[6,10.5],[7,13.5],[8,16.5],[8.5,17.81],[9,18.75],[9.5,19.31],[10,19.5]]}]},"choices":["1 to 4 s","4 to 8 s","8 to 10 s","1 to 10 s"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"The whole trip includes the speed-up and the braking, where the velocity changes, so a net force acts for part of it."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Here the curve gets steeper: the dolly speeds up from rest, so a net force points along its motion."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Here the curve flattens out: the dolly slows to a stop, so a net force points against its motion."}],"rule":"Zero net force means constant velocity: on a position–time graph, pick the straight sloped stretch; any bending stretch means acceleration.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"F_net = 0 ⇔ a = 0 ⇔ constant v ⇔ straight x–t line. Skip the curving start and stop; the straight middle stretch is the answer."},"exp":"On a position–time graph the **slope is the velocity**. A net force changes the velocity, so it shows as a **curve**. Between 1 and 4 s the slope grows as the dolly speeds up, and between 8 and 10 s it shrinks as the dolly brakes. From **4 to 8 s** the line is straight: 3 m every second, a constant 3 m/s, so **a = 0 and F_net = 0**.","how":{"ask":"Where is the position graph a straight sloped line, so the velocity is not changing?","know":"• Slope of an x–t graph = **velocity**; slope of a v–t graph = **acceleration**.\n• Newton’s 2nd law: F_net = ma, so F_net = 0 exactly when v is constant.\n• Straight x–t → constant v; steepening curve → speeding up; flattening curve → slowing.\n• Area under a v–t graph = **displacement**.","steps":[{"text":"No net force means no acceleration: find the stretch where the slope stays the same.","hl":"no net force"},{"text":"From 1 to 4 s the gains grow each half-second: the dolly speeds up from rest. Force along the motion.","calc":["1 → 2 s: +0.5 m","3 → 4 s: +2.5 m"],"strike":["1 to 4 s"]},{"text":"From 4 to 8 s the dolly gains 3 m every second: a straight line.","calc":["slope = (16.5 − 4.5) m ÷ (8 − 4) s = **3 m/s**, constant","a = 0 → F_net = 0"]},{"text":"From 8 to 10 s the gains shrink to zero: braking, force against the motion. So the whole trip also fails.","calc":["8 → 9 s: +2.25 m","9 → 10 s: +0.75 m"],"strike":["8 to 10 s","1 to 10 s"]},{"text":"Also: the slopes give the v–t graph, and its area returns the distance on the x–t graph.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"The same dolly: velocity versus time","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":1,"max":10,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Velocity (m/s)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Dolly","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[1,0],[4,3],[8,3],[10,0]]}]},"calc":["1–4 s: triangle ½ × 3 s × 3 m/s = 4.5 m","4–8 s: rectangle 3 m/s × 4 s = 12 m","8–10 s: triangle ½ × 2 s × 3 m/s = 3 m","total **19.5 m**, the final position"]},{"text":"Answer: 4 to 8 s."}],"trap":"Thinking the net force is largest where the dolly moves fastest: a steady velocity, however large, needs no net force; only a change in velocity does."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"from rest","note":"it must speed up first"},{"t":"steady cruising speed","note":"constant v: zero net force"},{"t":"slows it to a stop","note":"slowing: force against v"},{"t":"no net force","note":"a = 0"}]},{"text":"**Slope of x–t = velocity.** Steep = fast, flat = stopped, a straight line = steady speed."},{"text":"**Slope of v–t = acceleration; area under v–t = displacement.** By F_net = ma, a straight x–t stretch (flat v–t) has zero net force.","calc":["speeding up: F along v","slowing: F against v","steady v: F_net = 0"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on x–t, slope = velocity, so a straight sloped stretch means constant v and zero net force; on v–t, slope = acceleration and area = displacement."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-06a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:6#1","pe6-cp:6#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:6"],"passage":"To check a restriction digest, a student loaded the cut plasmid and a DNA size ladder into wells beside the negative electrode of an agarose gel. When the field was applied, the DNA moved through the gel because its phosphate backbone carries negative charge. The ladder bands’ distances are graphed against their sizes in the figure. One band from the digest, fragment P, traveled 2.9 cm.","q":"What is the approximate length of fragment P?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"DNA ladder: distance traveled versus length","x":{"label":"Fragment length (bp)","min":0,"max":9000,"step":1000},"y":{"label":"Distance from well (cm)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Ladder","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[250,6.4],[500,5.4],[1000,4.4],[2000,3.4],[4000,2.4],[8000,1.4]]}]},"choices":["About 290 bp","About 1200 bp","About 2800 bp","About 6000 bp"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"copied","why":"That turns the 2.9 cm distance into a size; a fragment that short would run past the 250 bp band, near the bottom."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Anything under 2000 bp runs farther than 3.4 cm, but fragment P stopped short of that band."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A fragment over 4000 bp would stop before 2.4 cm, yet fragment P ran past that band."}],"rule":"Short DNA runs farther: find the two ladder bands that bracket the distance and pick a size between their sizes.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Bracket it: 2.9 cm lies between the 2000 bp (3.4 cm) and 4000 bp (2.4 cm) bands, so the size is between them; halfway on a log scale is about 2800."},"exp":"DNA is negative (one phosphate per nucleotide), so it runs from the − wells toward the + electrode, and **shorter fragments move farther** through the gel mesh. 2.9 cm lies **between the 2000 bp band (3.4 cm) and the 4000 bp band (2.4 cm)**, halfway in distance. Distance tracks log(size), so halfway between 2000 and 4000 is 2000 × √2 ≈ **2800 bp**.","how":{"ask":"Which two ladder bands bracket 2.9 cm, and where between them does fragment P fall?","know":"• DNA’s phosphate backbone is negative, so it moves toward the + electrode (the anode).\n• In a gel, shorter fragments move farther; a ladder gives known sizes at known distances.\n• Distance falls about linearly with log(size): here each doubling costs 1.0 cm.","steps":[{"text":"Find 2.9 cm on the distance axis.","hl":"traveled 2.9 cm"},{"text":"Bracket it with ladder bands.","calc":["2000 bp → 3.4 cm (ran farther)","4000 bp → 2.4 cm (ran less far)","2.9 cm lies between → **2000–4000 bp**"],"strike":["About 1200 bp","About 6000 bp"]},{"text":"Place it: 2.9 is halfway between 3.4 and 2.4, so it is half a doubling above 2000.","calc":["2000 × 2^0.5 ≈ 2000 × 1.41 ≈ **2800 bp**"]},{"text":"290 bp reuses the distance digits as a size; a fragment that short would run past the 250 bp band.","strike":["About 290 bp"]},{"text":"Answer: about 2800 bp."}],"trap":"Thinking longer DNA runs farther, which flips the bracket: the gel mesh holds long fragments back."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"negative electrode","note":"wells at −; DNA heads to +"},{"t":"DNA size ladder","note":"known sizes"},{"t":"traveled 2.9 cm","note":"read across"}]},{"text":"**Why DNA moves.** One negative phosphate per nucleotide gives DNA a net − charge, so the field pulls it toward the + electrode, the anode."},{"text":"**Short runs far.** The gel is a mesh: small fragments slip through, long ones are held back. Bracket the unknown between two ladder bands, then place it on the curve."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** DNA is negative and runs toward +; shorter DNA migrates farther, so size an unknown by the two ladder bands whose distances bracket it, reading the curve, not a straight line."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-06b","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:6#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:6"],"passage":"A forensic lab amplified a short region of DNA and ran the product beside a size ladder in an agarose slab gel whose wells sat at the negative electrode. After the run, the gel was stained and photographed under UV light. The figure plots migration distance against fragment size for the DNA ladder. The unknown fragment on the same gel traveled 4.6 cm.","q":"The unknown fragment’s length is closest to:","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"DNA ladder: distance traveled versus size","x":{"label":"Fragment size (bp)","min":0,"max":5000,"step":1000},"y":{"label":"Distance from well (cm)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Ladder","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[150,6.2],[300,5.1],[600,4],[1200,2.9],[2400,1.8],[4800,0.7]]}]},"choices":["About 200 bp","About 400 bp","About 900 bp","About 4600 bp"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Read backwards: farther means smaller, and 4.6 cm is past the 600 bp band (4.0 cm), so the fragment is under 600."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"That copies the 4.6 cm distance as a size; a fragment that long would stop near 0.7 cm."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A 200 bp fragment would run past the 300 bp band, beyond 5.1 cm, but this one stopped short of it."}],"rule":"Small fragments run farther: find the two ladder bands that bracket the distance and pick a size between their sizes.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Bracket it: 4.6 cm sits between the 300 bp (5.1) and 600 bp (4.0) bands, so the size is between 300 and 600. Only ~400 bp fits."},"exp":"Shorter DNA slips through the gel faster, so it travels farther. 4.6 cm lies **between the 300 bp band (5.1 cm) and the 600 bp band (4.0 cm)**, so the fragment is between 300 and 600 bp; on the curve it reads **about 400 bp**.","how":{"ask":"Which two ladder bands bracket 4.6 cm, and where between them does the unknown fall?","know":"• In a gel, shorter fragments move farther; the ladder gives known sizes at known distances.\n• Find the two ladder bands whose distances bracket the unknown; its size lies between theirs.\n• Distance falls roughly with log(size): on this gel each doubling of size costs about 1.1 cm.","steps":[{"text":"Find 4.6 cm on the distance axis.","hl":"traveled 4.6 cm"},{"text":"Bracket it with ladder bands.","calc":["300 bp → 5.1 cm (ran farther)","600 bp → 4.0 cm (ran less far)","4.6 cm lies between → **300–600 bp**"]},{"text":"Place it: 4.6 is 0.5 cm short of 5.1, a bit under half of the 1.1 cm doubling step.","calc":["(5.1 − 4.6) ÷ 1.1 ≈ 0.45 of a doubling","300 × 2^0.45 ≈ 300 × 1.37 ≈ **410 bp**"]},{"text":"A 200 bp fragment would run past the 300 bp band (beyond 5.1 cm), and a 900 bp one would stop short of the 600 bp band (before 4.0 cm).","strike":["About 200 bp","About 900 bp"]},{"text":"4600 bp copies the distance number as a size; a band that long would sit near 0.7 cm.","strike":["About 4600 bp"]},{"text":"Answer: about 400 bp."}],"trap":"Thinking longer DNA runs farther, which flips the bracket: the gel mesh holds long fragments back."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"migration distance","note":"y axis: cm"},{"t":"DNA ladder","note":"known sizes"},{"t":"traveled 4.6 cm","note":"read across"}]},{"text":"**Short runs far.** The gel is a mesh: small fragments slip through, large ones are held back."},{"text":"**Bracket, then place.** Find the ladder bands just above and below the unknown's distance; the size lies between theirs."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** shorter DNA migrates farther; size an unknown by the two ladder bands whose distances bracket it, reading the curve, not a straight line."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-07a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:7#1","pe6-cp:7#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:7"],"passage":"Cultured cells release lactic acid as they grow, so their bicarbonate-buffered medium slowly acidifies. A technician noticed that the phenol red indicator in an overcrowded flask had turned yellow. At room temperature, where pure water has pH 7.0, a meter read the medium in that flask at pH 6.4.","q":"Relative to pure water, the medium in that flask has:","choices":["[H⁺] up 4-fold; [OH⁻] down 4-fold.","[H⁺] down 4-fold; [OH⁻] up 4-fold.","[H⁺] up 4-fold; [OH⁻] fixed.","[H⁺] up 1.1-fold; [OH⁻] down 1.1-fold."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction flipped: a pH below 7 means more H⁺ than pure water, not less."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skips Kw: [H⁺][OH⁻] stays 10⁻¹⁴, so when H⁺ rises, OH⁻ must fall by the same factor."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"That divides the two pH values; pH is a log scale, so the gap must be undone with 10^Δ."}],"rule":"Each pH unit is a factor of 10 in [H⁺]; a gap of Δ units is 10^Δ. Below 7, H⁺ rises and OH⁻ falls by the same factor (Kw).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Log anchors: 10^0.3 ≈ 2, 10^0.6 ≈ 4. pH 6.4 is 0.6 below 7, so H⁺ is about 4× higher and OH⁻ 4× lower."},"exp":"Pure water at room temperature has **[H⁺] = [OH⁻] = 1 × 10⁻⁷ M**. pH 6.4 is **0.6 units below 7**, so [H⁺] = 10^0.6 ≈ **4 times** higher (≈ 4 × 10⁻⁷ M): weakly acidic. Because **[H⁺][OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴**, [OH⁻] falls by the same factor to ≈ 2.5 × 10⁻⁸ M, **4 times lower**.","how":{"ask":"How far is the medium’s pH from 7, and what factor does that gap make for each ion?","know":"• At room temperature, pure water: **[H⁺] = [OH⁻] = 1 × 10⁻⁷ M, pH 7**.\n• pH just below 7 → weakly acidic; just above → weakly basic.\n• A gap of Δ pH units is a factor of **10^Δ** in [H⁺]; Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴ moves [OH⁻] the opposite way by the same factor.","steps":[{"text":"Compare with neutral: the medium is below 7, so it has more H⁺ than water.","hl":"pH 6.4","strike":["[H⁺] down 4-fold; [OH⁻] up 4-fold."]},{"text":"Turn the gap into a factor with 10^Δ, not by dividing the pH values.","calc":["Δ = 7.0 − 6.4 = 0.6","10^0.6 ≈ 4"],"strike":["[H⁺] up 1.1-fold; [OH⁻] down 1.1-fold."]},{"text":"Apply Kw: the two ions move in opposite directions by the same factor.","calc":["[H⁺] ≈ 4 × 10⁻⁷ M","[OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ (4 × 10⁻⁷) ≈ 2.5 × 10⁻⁸ M"],"strike":["[H⁺] up 4-fold; [OH⁻] fixed."]},{"text":"Answer: H⁺ up 4-fold and OH⁻ down 4-fold."}],"trap":"Reading a 0.6 pH drop as a small change: pH is a log scale, so 0.6 units is a factor of about 4."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"pH 6.4","note":"just below neutral"},{"t":"pH 7.0","note":"neutral reference"},{"t":"room temperature","note":"Kw = 10⁻¹⁴"}]},{"text":"**Neutral is the reference.** Below 7, [H⁺] exceeds [OH⁻]; above 7, the reverse. Close to 7 means weakly acidic or weakly basic."},{"text":"**Log factors to remember.**","calc":["10^0.3 ≈ 2","10^0.5 ≈ 3","10^0.6 ≈ 4","10^0.7 ≈ 5","10^1 = 10"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare a solution with water by the pH gap Δ from 7: [H⁺] changes by 10^Δ, and [OH⁻] changes by the same factor the other way."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-08a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:8#2","pe6-cp:8#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:8"],"passage":"An enzyme assay needs a buffer made from a weak acid HA and its sodium salt, NaA. To find the acid’s pKa, a student recorded the curve in the figure, which shows 30 mL of a weak acid HA titrated with NaOH of equal concentration. The assay works best at pH 3.4, so the buffer will be made at that pH by mixing HA with NaA.","q":"What [A⁻]/[HA] ratio does the assay buffer need?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Titration of 30 mL of weak acid HA with NaOH","x":{"label":"Volume of NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":40,"step":10},"y":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"HA + NaOH","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,2.26],[3,2.75],[6,3.1],[9,3.33],[12,3.52],[15,3.7],[18,3.88],[21,4.07],[24,4.3],[27,4.65],[29,5.16],[30,8.29],[31,11.39],[33,11.85],[36,12.13],[40,12.33]]}]},"choices":["2.0","0.30","1.0","0.50"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"pH 3.4 is below the pKa, so acid must outnumber base: the ratio has to be under 1; 2.0 flips it."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"0.30 is the pKa − pH gap used as the ratio itself; the gap is a log, so undo it with 10^x."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A 1:1 mix sits exactly at the pKa, the half-equivalence pH (3.7 at 15 mL), not at 3.4."}],"rule":"pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]); read the pKa at half-equivalence. pH below pKa → ratio under 1; 0.3 log units is a factor of 2.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Half-equivalence (15 mL) pH = pKa ≈ 3.7. Target is 0.3 lower, and 0.3 log units = factor 2, so base/acid = ½ = 0.50."},"exp":"Equivalence is at **30 mL** (the steep jump), so half-equivalence is **15 mL**, where pH = **pKa ≈ 3.7**. Henderson–Hasselbalch: 3.4 − 3.7 = −0.3 is the log of the base:acid ratio, so **[A⁻]/[HA] = 10^(−0.3) ≈ 0.50**.","how":{"ask":"Read the pKa at half-equivalence, then raise 10 to (pH − pKa).","know":"• **pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA])**, so **[A⁻]/[HA] = 10^(pH − pKa)**.\n• At the **half-equivalence volume**, [A⁻] = [HA] and pH = pKa.\n• Equivalence = the midpoint of the steep jump.","steps":[{"text":"Find the equivalence volume from the steep jump, then halve it.","hl":"30 mL of a weak acid HA titrated with NaOH of equal concentration","calc":["equivalence ≈ 30 mL → half-equivalence = 15 mL"]},{"text":"Read the pH there: that is the pKa.","calc":["pH at 15 mL ≈ 3.7 = pKa"]},{"text":"Apply the ratio form of Henderson–Hasselbalch.","hl":"pH 3.4","calc":["pH − pKa = 3.4 − 3.7 = −0.3","ratio = 10^(−0.3) ≈ **0.50**"]},{"text":"2.0 inverts the ratio, 1.0 is the buffer at its pKa, and 0.30 uses the exponent as the ratio.","strike":["2.0","1.0","0.30"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.50."}],"trap":"Reading the pKa at the equivalence point (pH ≈ 8.3) instead of at half the equivalence volume."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"30 mL","note":"acid volume; equal molarity → equivalence at 30 mL"},{"t":"pH 3.4","note":"target buffer pH"},{"t":"[A⁻]/[HA]","note":"base over acid"}]},{"text":"**pKa from the curve.** Halfway to equivalence, half the acid has become A⁻, so the log term is zero and pH = pKa.","calc":["V(½ eq) = 15 mL → pKa ≈ 3.7"]},{"text":"**Ratio form.** Solve Henderson–Hasselbalch for the ratio.","calc":["[A⁻]/[HA] = 10^(pH − pKa) = 10^(−0.3) ≈ 0.50"]},{"text":"**Also:** a pH exactly 1 unit below the pKa gives a base:acid ratio of 0.1, and a pH equal to the pKa gives 1; each unit is a factor of 10."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** base:acid = 10^(pH − pKa), with the pKa read at the half-equivalence volume."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-09a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:9#1","pe6-cp:9#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:9"],"q":"Supercooled water at −10 °C freezes spontaneously once seeded, and its surroundings warm. Which set of signs describes this freezing?","choices":["ΔH negative, ΔS negative, ΔG negative","ΔH negative, ΔS positive, ΔG negative","ΔH positive, ΔS negative, ΔG negative","ΔH negative, ΔS negative, ΔG positive"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign flipped: liquid → crystal is more ordered, so ΔS is negative, not positive."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"ΔH > 0 means heat is absorbed; here the surroundings warm, so the water releases heat and ΔH is negative."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"A process that happens on its own has ΔG < 0 by definition; a negative ΔS does not forbid it."}],"rule":"Read each sign from the story: heat given off → ΔH < 0, heat taken in → ΔH > 0; more order → ΔS < 0, less → ΔS > 0; happens on its own → ΔG < 0.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Read signs from the stem: spontaneous → ΔG < 0; surroundings warm → heat released, ΔH < 0; liquid → solid is more ordered, ΔS < 0. All three negative."},"exp":"Spontaneous means **ΔG < 0**, the only requirement. The surroundings warm, so heat leaves the water: **ΔH < 0**. Freezing orders the molecules: **ΔS < 0**. ΔG = ΔH − TΔS stays negative because |ΔH| > T|ΔS| at this low temperature: an enthalpy-driven process.","how":{"ask":"Read each sign from an observation: spontaneity, heat flow and order.","know":"• **ΔG = ΔH − TΔS**; spontaneous ⇔ ΔG < 0, the only sign that is required.\n• Surroundings warm → the system releases heat → ΔH < 0; surroundings cool → ΔH > 0.\n• More order (gas → liquid → solid, fewer particles) → ΔS < 0.","steps":[{"text":"Spontaneous: ΔG is negative.","hl":"freezes spontaneously","strike":["ΔH negative, ΔS negative, ΔG positive"]},{"text":"The surroundings warm, so heat flows out of the water: exothermic.","hl":"its surroundings warm","strike":["ΔH positive, ΔS negative, ΔG negative"]},{"text":"Liquid → crystal is more ordered, so ΔS is negative.","strike":["ΔH negative, ΔS positive, ΔG negative"]},{"text":"Check: ΔG = ΔH − TΔS is negative only if the enthalpy term wins.","calc":["ΔH < 0, and −TΔS > 0 (it opposes)","ΔG < 0 → |ΔH| > T|ΔS| (enthalpy-driven)"]},{"text":"Answer: ΔH negative, ΔS negative, ΔG negative."}],"trap":"Believing entropy must rise for anything spontaneous; only the total entropy (system + surroundings) must rise, and the released heat raises the surroundings' entropy."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"freezes spontaneously","note":"ΔG < 0"},{"t":"its surroundings warm","note":"heat released: ΔH < 0"},{"t":"Supercooled water","note":"liquid → solid: ΔS < 0"}]},{"text":"**Only ΔG is required.** Spontaneous ⇔ ΔG < 0; nothing forces ΔH or ΔS to a particular sign."},{"text":"**Also: ΔH and ΔS can each have either sign**, as long as ΔG = ΔH − TΔS comes out negative:","calc":["ΔH −, ΔS + → spontaneous at every T","ΔH −, ΔS − → spontaneous at low T (enthalpy-driven)","ΔH +, ΔS + → spontaneous at high T (entropy-driven)","ΔH +, ΔS − → never spontaneous"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** spontaneity needs only ΔG < 0; read ΔH from heat flow and ΔS from order, then check that ΔH − TΔS is negative."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-10a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:10#1","pe6-cp:10#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:10"],"q":"A protein kinase is given ATP whose terminal phosphate carries ³²P, plus four peptides that differ only in the side chain shown. Which peptide becomes radioactive?","choices":["Tyrosine","Phenylalanine","Leucine","Cysteine"],"choiceFigs":["tyrosine-sc","phenylalanine-sc","leucine-sc","cysteine-sc"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Same aromatic ring but no –OH on it: a kinase needs a side-chain oxygen to attach the phosphate to."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An all-carbon side chain has no hydroxyl group, so there is nothing for the phosphate to bond to."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An –SH is not what Ser/Thr/Tyr kinases modify; they form a phosphate ester on an –OH oxygen."}],"rule":"Kinases attach phosphate to an –OH: only Ser, Thr and Tyr side chains qualify; a ring or chain without an OH cannot be phosphorylated.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Phosphorylation = a phosphate ester on a side-chain OH. Scan the four drawings for –OH; only the phenol ring carries one."},"exp":"A kinase moves the terminal (γ) phosphate of ATP onto a **side-chain –OH**, forming a phosphate ester; here the ³²P label rides along. Of the four side chains, only **tyrosine** has an –OH (on its phenol ring). Phenylalanine has the same ring without the OH, leucine is all carbon and hydrogen, and cysteine has an –SH, which these kinases do not phosphorylate.","how":{"ask":"Which drawn side chain has an –OH for the kinase to put the labeled phosphate on?","know":"• Protein kinases transfer ATP’s γ-phosphate to an –OH of **Ser, Thr or Tyr**.\n• Phe = Tyr without the OH; Val, Leu, Ile, Ala, Met have no OH.\n• The radioactive label goes wherever the γ-phosphate goes.","steps":[{"text":"The label sits on the phosphate the kinase transfers, so the peptide that gets phosphorylated becomes radioactive.","hl":"terminal phosphate carries ³²P"},{"text":"Check each side chain for an –OH.","calc":["phenol ring with OH → yes","ring with no OH → no","isobutyl, all C–H → no","CH₂–SH → no"]},{"text":"Phenylalanine and leucine have no oxygen at all, and cysteine’s sulfur is not a kinase target.","strike":["Phenylalanine","Leucine","Cysteine"],"fig":"phenylalanine-sc"},{"text":"Answer: tyrosine.","fig":"tyrosine-sc"}],"trap":"Picking phenylalanine because it looks like tyrosine: without the –OH there is no oxygen for the phosphate."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"protein kinase","note":"adds phosphate to –OH"},{"t":"terminal phosphate carries ³²P","note":"label follows the phosphate"},{"t":"differ only in the side chain","note":"compare side chains only"}]},{"text":"**What a kinase needs.** An –OH oxygen attacks the γ-phosphorus of ATP; the product is a phosphate ester (phospho-Ser, -Thr or -Tyr)."},{"text":"**Spot the OH.**","calc":["Ser: CH₂–OH","Thr: CH(OH)–CH₃","Tyr: phenol OH","Phe, Leu, Val, Ala, Met: none"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** only side chains with an –OH (Ser, Thr, Tyr) can be phosphorylated by a protein kinase; Phe is Tyr without the OH and cannot."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-11a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:11#1","pe6-cp:11#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:11"],"q":"The resistance R of a copper wire was measured for several lengths L, as plotted. Which relationship between R and L do the data support?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Resistance of a copper wire versus its length","x":{"label":"Length L (m)","min":0,"max":2.5,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Resistance R (Ω)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Wire","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[0.6,1.5],[1.2,3],[1.8,4.5],[2.4,6]]}]},"choices":["R ∝ L","R ∝ L squared","R ∝ square root of L","R ∝ inverse of L"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"An inverse law means a longer wire reads lower R; here every longer wire reads higher."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"With R ∝ L², doubling L (0.6 → 1.2 m) would make R 4 times larger; it only doubles (1.5 → 3.0 Ω)."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A square-root law would raise R only about 1.4× when L doubles; the data double it."}],"rule":"Doubling test: double x and see what y does: ×2 linear, ×4 square, ×1.4 square root, ×½ inverse.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Double L, R doubles (1.5 → 3.0); ×4 L gives ×4 R. Same factor in and out means direct proportion: R ∝ L."},"exp":"Doubling L from 0.6 to 1.2 m doubles R from 1.5 to 3.0 Ω, and quadrupling L (to 2.4 m) quadruples R (to 6.0 Ω). The ratio R/L stays **2.5 Ω/m**, and the points fall on a straight line through the origin: **R ∝ L**.","how":{"ask":"Divide each R by its L: is that ratio constant, or is R/L² constant instead?","know":"• y ∝ x: y/x is constant and the graph is a straight line through the origin; doubling x doubles y.\n• y ∝ x²: y/x² is constant; doubling x multiplies y by 4.\n• y ∝ √x: doubling x multiplies y by about 1.4. y ∝ 1/x: y × x is constant, and y falls as x grows.","steps":[{"text":"Test the ratios instead of eyeballing the line.","hl":"Which relationship between R and L","calc":["R/L: 1.5 ÷ 0.6 = **2.5**; 3.0 ÷ 1.2 = **2.5**","4.5 ÷ 1.8 = **2.5**; 6.0 ÷ 2.4 = **2.5** Ω/m"]},{"text":"R/L² changes from point to point, so the square law fails.","calc":["1.5 ÷ 0.6² = 1.5 ÷ 0.36 ≈ 4.2","6.0 ÷ 2.4² = 6.0 ÷ 5.76 ≈ 1.0"],"strike":["R ∝ L squared"]},{"text":"Doubling test: L × 2 (0.6 → 1.2 m) gives R × 2 (1.5 → 3.0 Ω), which is linear. A square-root law would give only × 1.4, and an inverse law would halve R.","calc":["√L: 1.5 × 1.41 ≈ 2.1 Ω (measured 3.0)","1/L: 1.5 ÷ 2 = 0.75 Ω (measured 3.0)"],"strike":["R ∝ square root of L","R ∝ inverse of L"]},{"text":"Answer: R ∝ L."}],"trap":"Calling any rising graph \"proportional\": a square law also rises, so test a ratio. Here only R/L stays fixed."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"resistance R","note":"y axis: ohms"},{"t":"several lengths L","note":"x axis: meters"},{"t":"relationship between R and L","note":"which power law?"}]},{"text":"**Two ratio tests.** Divide y by x and by x²; whichever ratio stays fixed names the law.","calc":["y/x fixed → y ∝ x","y/x² fixed → y ∝ x²"]},{"text":"**Doubling shortcut.** Double x and watch y: × 2 → linear, × 4 → square, × 1.4 → square root, × ½ → inverse."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** test y/x and y/x² across the data (or double x): a constant y/x, or y doubling with x, means y ∝ x; a constant y/x², or y × 4, means y ∝ x²."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-12a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:12#1","pe6-cp:12#2","pe6-cp:12#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:12"],"q":"A chemist draws trans-1-tert-butyl-3-methylcyclohexane, shown, in its lower-energy chair. Which substituent occupies an axial position in that chair?","fig":"cpt-pe6-trans-tbu-3-me-chx","choices":["Only the methyl","Only the tert-butyl","Both substituents","Neither substituent"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"Trans at C1 and C3 puts the groups on opposite faces, which on a chair is always one axial plus one equatorial; both axial can't happen."},{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"Trans at C1 and C3 means opposite faces, and on a chair that is always one axial plus one equatorial bond; no diequatorial chair exists."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"tert-Butyl is the bulkiest common group: axial, it hits the two axial H's on its face (1,3-diaxial strain), so the favored chair keeps it equatorial."}],"rule":"Cis/trans fixes how many groups must be axial; when one must be, the favored chair puts the bulkier group equatorial.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Trans-1,3 on a chair = one up-axial, one equatorial. tert-Butyl always takes equatorial (it is huge), so the methyl is the axial one."},"exp":"In a trans-1,3 ring the groups sit on opposite faces, and a chair then gives C1 and C3 **one axial and one equatorial** group in either conformation. The ring flips so the bulky **tert-butyl is equatorial**, which leaves the **methyl axial**.","how":{"ask":"Can this substitution pattern put both groups equatorial? If not, which group takes the axial spot?","know":"• Around a chair the axial bonds alternate up, down, up, so C1 and C3 point their axial bonds to the same face.\n• **Diequatorial is possible only for cis-1,3, trans-1,2 and trans-1,4**; cis-1,2, cis-1,4 and trans-1,3 always keep one group axial.\n• When one group must be axial, the favored chair gives that spot to the **smaller** group (tert-butyl ≫ isopropyl > ethyl ≈ methyl).","steps":[{"text":"Read the pattern: trans means opposite faces, and the groups sit on C1 and C3.","hl":"trans-1-tert-butyl-3-methylcyclohexane","fig":"cpt-pe6-trans-tbu-3-me-chx"},{"text":"At C1 and C3 same-face bonds are the same type, so opposite faces force one axial and one equatorial group in both chairs. Both axial and both equatorial are impossible.","calc":["trans-1,3 → one axial + one equatorial","both axial ✗; both equatorial ✗"],"strike":["Both substituents","Neither substituent"]},{"text":"Compare the two chairs by the cost of the axial group: tert-butyl axial is far worse than methyl axial.","calc":["CH₃ axial: ≈ 7 kJ/mol of strain","tert-butyl axial: > 20 kJ/mol","favored chair: tert-butyl equatorial"],"strike":["Only the tert-butyl"]},{"text":"Answer: only the methyl is axial."}],"trap":"Assuming the favored chair always has every group equatorial; that is true only for cis-1,3, trans-1,2 and trans-1,4 patterns."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"trans-1-tert-butyl-3-methylcyclohexane","note":"opposite faces, C1 and C3"},{"t":"lower-energy chair","note":"best placement of groups"},{"t":"axial position","note":"straight up or down"}]},{"text":"**Big groups go equatorial.** An axial group bumps the two axial H atoms on its own face; the bigger the group, the bigger the penalty, so tert-butyl is effectively locked equatorial.","fig":"cpt-pe6-trans-tbu-3-me-chx"},{"text":"**Check the pattern.** Axial bonds alternate up and down around the ring, so C1 and C3 share a direction.","calc":["same face at C1, C3 → same type (cis-1,3)","opposite faces at C1, C3 → one of each (trans-1,3)"]},{"text":"**Also:** the patterns that can be diequatorial are cis-1,3, trans-1,2 and trans-1,4; for those, the favored chair puts both groups equatorial."},{"text":"**Also:** in every other pattern (cis-1,2, cis-1,4, trans-1,3) one group must be axial, and the favored chair hands that spot to the smaller group."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** place the biggest group equatorial first, then ask whether the pattern lets the second group be equatorial too; if not, the smaller group is axial."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-13a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:13#1","pe6-cp:13#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:13"],"passage":"Phosphatase activity in blood samples is often measured with the colorless substrate p-nitrophenyl phosphate, shown with four atoms labeled I to IV. The enzyme cuts the P–O bond to the ring and releases p-nitrophenolate, which turns the solution yellow. In a related assay, the released product is first trapped by an amine that links to a carbonyl compound through a Schiff base, an imine (C=N) bond.","q":"Which labeled atom of the substrate has trigonal planar geometry?","fig":"pnpp-sites","choices":["Atom I","Atom II","Atom III","Atom IV"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A terminal O⁻ is bonded to one atom only, so it has no bond angle at all, let alone a planar set of three."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Phosphorus here is bonded to four oxygens with no lone pair: four domains, tetrahedral, about 109.5°."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The bridging O has two bonds and two lone pairs: four domains, so it is bent, near 105°."}],"rule":"Count electron domains: an atom in a double or aromatic bond with three domains is sp², trigonal planar, ~120°; four domains means sp³, ~109.5°.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ring or double-bond carbon ⇒ sp² ⇒ planar, ~120°. P with four O and an O with two lone pairs are both four-domain atoms."},"exp":"Trigonal planar means **three electron domains: sp², about 120°**. Atom IV is a **benzene-ring carbon**, bonded to three atoms with no lone pair: sp² and planar. Atom II, phosphorus, has four bonded O (tetrahedral, sp³); atom III, the bridging O, has two bonds and two lone pairs (bent, sp³); atom I is a terminal O⁻ with only one bond. The same three-domain count makes the **C=N carbon of a Schiff base** sp², about 120°.","how":{"ask":"Which labeled atom has exactly three electron domains and no lone pair?","know":"• Domains = bonded atoms + lone pairs (a double bond counts once).\n• 4 domains → sp³, ~109.5° (bent or pyramidal with lone pairs); 3 → sp², 120°; 2 → sp, 180°.\n• Aromatic ring carbons and every C=O, C=C or C=N carbon are sp² and planar; a **Schiff base** is an imine, C=N.","steps":[{"text":"Trigonal planar is the three-domain case: sp², about 120°, like the C=N carbon of a Schiff base.","hl":"imine (C=N)"},{"text":"Count domains on each labeled atom.","fig":"pnpp-sites","calc":["I: terminal O⁻, one bond → no angle","II: P with four O → 4 → tetrahedral","III: O with two bonds + two lone pairs → 4 → bent","IV: ring carbon, three bonds → 3 → **sp²**"]},{"text":"Atoms I, II and III are not three-domain centers.","strike":["Atom I","Atom II","Atom III"]},{"text":"Answer: atom IV, the aromatic ring carbon."}],"trap":"Calling the bridging O planar because it has only two atoms attached: its two lone pairs make four domains, so it is bent."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Schiff base","note":"an imine, C=N"},{"t":"trigonal planar","note":"3 domains, sp², 120°"},{"t":"four atoms labeled I to IV","note":"count each one"}]},{"text":"**Domains decide the shape.** Count each bonded atom once (double bonds too) and add lone pairs.","calc":["3 domains → sp², 120°","4 domains → sp³, 109.5°"]},{"text":"**Double and aromatic bonds mark sp² carbons.** Every ring carbon of benzene and every C=O, C=C or C=N carbon is planar."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a carbon in a double or aromatic bond (C=O, C=N, C=C, benzene) has three electron domains, so it is sp² and trigonal planar (~120°); four domains, counting lone pairs, means sp³."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-14a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:14#1","pe6-cp:14#2","pe6-cp:14#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:14"],"passage":"Drug D was tested on a liver enzyme. Initial rates were measured at many substrate concentrations with and without the drug, and the fitted constants were tabulated: without D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 2.0 mM; with D, Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 6.0 mM. The researchers then plotted rate versus [S] for both conditions on the same axes.","q":"Compared with the no-drug curve, the curve with drug D has:","choices":["same plateau; higher KM.","same plateau; lower KM.","lower plateau; same KM.","lower plateau; higher KM."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction flipped: KM rose with the drug, so the curve reaches half its plateau at a larger [S], not a smaller one."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Swaps the two constants: the table changes KM, the half-plateau [S], and leaves Vmax, the plateau height, alone."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Vmax is identical with and without D, and Vmax alone sets how high the curve levels off."}],"rule":"On rate vs [S], Vmax is the plateau height and KM is the [S] at half that height. Read each constant’s change from the table onto its own feature.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Plateau = Vmax, half-plateau [S] = KM. Vmax same → curves level off together; KM tripled → the drug curve rises more slowly at low [S]."},"exp":"On a Michaelis–Menten plot the **plateau height is Vmax** and the **[S] at half the plateau is KM**. With drug D, Vmax stays 45 μmol/min, so both curves **level off at the same height**; KM triples from 2.0 to 6.0 mM, so the drug curve reaches half-height (22.5 μmol/min) only at **a higher [S]**. That pattern, same Vmax with larger KM, is what a competitive inhibitor gives.","how":{"ask":"Which table entry changed, and which feature of the curve does that entry control?","know":"• **Vmax** sets the height of the plateau.\n• **KM** is the [S] at which v = Vmax/2; a larger KM shifts the half-plateau point right.\n• Same KM → the half-max points coincide even if the plateaus differ; same Vmax → same plateau even if KM differs.","steps":[{"text":"Vmax is the same in both rows, so both curves level off at the same height.","hl":"Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 6.0 mM","strike":["lower plateau; same KM.","lower plateau; higher KM."]},{"text":"KM rises with the drug, so half of that plateau is reached at a larger [S].","calc":["without D: half-height at 2.0 mM","with D: half-height at 6.0 mM"],"strike":["same plateau; lower KM."]},{"text":"Also: if instead Vmax changed and KM stayed fixed, the plateaus would differ while the half-plateau points lined up at one [S]."},{"text":"Answer: same plateau, higher KM."}],"trap":"Thinking any inhibitor lowers the plateau: only a change in Vmax moves the plateau; a KM change moves the half-plateau point."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 2.0 mM","note":"no drug"},{"t":"Vmax = 45 μmol/min and KM = 6.0 mM","note":"with D: only KM changed"},{"t":"rate versus [S]","note":"Michaelis–Menten curve"}]},{"text":"**Two features, two constants.**","calc":["plateau height = Vmax","[S] at half-plateau = KM"]},{"text":"**Read the table row by row.** Same Vmax → same plateau. Bigger KM → the curve climbs more slowly and reaches half-height further right."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Vmax sets how high a rate-versus-[S] curve levels off, and KM is the [S] where it reaches half that height; a table change in one constant moves only its own feature."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-15a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:15#1","pe6-cp:15#2","pe6-cp:15#3","pe6-cp:15#4"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:15"],"passage":"Compound Q, a candidate antibiotic, was tested against a bacterial cell-wall enzyme. Initial rates were measured over a range of substrate concentrations, once without Q and once with a fixed amount of Q. Each point is the mean of three trials. The results are shown in the figure as a double-reciprocal (Lineweaver–Burk) plot.","q":"What kind of inhibitor is compound Q?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Double-reciprocal plot with and without compound Q","x":{"label":"1/[S] (mM⁻¹)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"1/v (min/μmol)","min":0,"max":30,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"No Q","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,2],[1,4],[2,6],[3,8],[4,10]]},{"name":"With Q","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,2],[1,8],[2,14],[3,20],[4,26]]}]},"choices":["Competitive inhibition","Uncompetitive inhibition","Noncompetitive inhibition","Mixed inhibition"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"An uncompetitive inhibitor gives parallel lines, shifting both intercepts; these two lines have different slopes."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Pure noncompetitive raises the y-intercept (lower Vmax) at a fixed x-intercept; here the y-intercept does not move."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Mixed lines cross away from the y-axis because Vmax and KM both change; these lines meet on the axis."}],"rule":"Lineweaver–Burk: same y-intercept → Vmax unchanged; same x-intercept → KM unchanged; parallel lines → both fall; crossing elsewhere → mixed.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"y-intercept = 1/Vmax. Both lines start at 2 on the y-axis, so Vmax is unchanged and only the slope (KM/Vmax) grew: KM up, Vmax same."},"exp":"On a double-reciprocal plot the **y-intercept is 1/Vmax** and the **x-intercept is −1/KM**. Both lines meet the y-axis at 2 min/μmol, so **Vmax is unchanged** (0.5 μmol/min). The line with Q is three times steeper, so its x-intercept moves toward zero: **KM is tripled**. KM up with Vmax unchanged is **competitive inhibition**: Q competes with substrate, and enough substrate overcomes it.","how":{"ask":"Do the lines share a y-intercept, an x-intercept, a slope, or none of these?","know":"• y-intercept = 1/Vmax; x-intercept = −1/KM; slope = KM/Vmax.\n• Competitive: KM up, Vmax same → lines meet on the y-axis.\n• Noncompetitive: Vmax down, KM same → meet on the x-axis.\n• Uncompetitive: both down by the same factor → parallel lines.\n• Mixed: Vmax down and KM changed → lines cross off both axes.","steps":[{"text":"Read the y-intercepts: both lines start at 2.","calc":["1/Vmax = 2 min/μmol in both → Vmax same"],"strike":["Noncompetitive inhibition","Mixed inhibition"]},{"text":"Compare slopes: the Q line is steeper, so the lines are not parallel.","calc":["no Q: slope 2","with Q: slope 6 → KM ×3"],"strike":["Uncompetitive inhibition"]},{"text":"Same Vmax and higher KM means the inhibitor competes with substrate for the active site.","hl":"double-reciprocal (Lineweaver–Burk) plot"},{"text":"Answer: competitive inhibition."}],"trap":"Reading the steeper line alone as \"less activity, so Vmax fell\": check the y-intercept, which holds steady."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"once without Q and once with a fixed amount of Q","note":"compare two lines"},{"t":"double-reciprocal","note":"1/v vs 1/[S]"}]},{"text":"**What each part of the line means.**","calc":["y-intercept = 1/Vmax","x-intercept = −1/KM","slope = KM/Vmax"]},{"text":"**Four patterns.**","calc":["competitive: KM ↑, Vmax same","noncompetitive: Vmax ↓, KM same","uncompetitive: both ↓, parallel","mixed: Vmax ↓, KM changed"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a Lineweaver–Burk plot, lines meeting on the y-axis mean competitive, on the x-axis noncompetitive, parallel lines uncompetitive, and crossing elsewhere mixed; the same patterns read straight from a table of KM and Vmax."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-16a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe6-cp:16#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:16"],"passage":"Aspartate transcarbamoylase (ATCase) catalyzes the first committed step of pyrimidine synthesis: carbamoyl phosphate + aspartate → N-carbamoylaspartate + Pi. The enzyme gives a sigmoidal rate curve for aspartate. CTP, an end product of the pathway, binds to separate regulatory subunits and shifts the enzyme toward its low-activity T state. In the experiment, carbamoyl phosphate was kept saturating while [aspartate] and [CTP] were varied.","q":"Under which conditions would ATCase form N-carbamoylaspartate fastest?","choices":["High [Asp] and high [CTP]","High [Asp] and low [CTP]","Low [Asp] and high [CTP]","Low [Asp] and low [CTP]"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Both settings flipped: this starves the enzyme of substrate and loads it with its feedback inhibitor."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"CTP is an allosteric inhibitor: plenty of it holds the enzyme in the T state even when substrate is abundant."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"With little aspartate the active sites sit mostly empty, so the rate stays low even with no inhibitor."}],"rule":"For the fastest rate: substrate high, allosteric activators high, allosteric inhibitors low. Name each molecule’s role before choosing.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"End products inhibit their own pathway (feedback). So keep CTP low and the substrate aspartate high."},"exp":"Rate rises with **substrate** until saturation, so aspartate should be **high**. **CTP is the pathway’s end product** and binds the regulatory subunits to favor the low-activity T state: it is a **feedback (allosteric) inhibitor**, so it should be **low**. High [Asp] with low [CTP] gives the fastest formation of N-carbamoylaspartate. (With an allosteric activator, such as AMP for glycogen phosphorylase, you would want the activator high instead.)","how":{"ask":"Is each varied molecule a substrate, an activator or an inhibitor, and which level of each speeds the reaction?","know":"• More substrate → faster, up to Vmax.\n• Allosteric activator (favors the R state) → want it high; allosteric inhibitor (favors T) → want it low.\n• End products of a pathway often feed back to inhibit its first committed step.","steps":[{"text":"CTP is an end product that pushes the enzyme into the T state: an inhibitor, so it must be low.","hl":"shifts the enzyme toward its low-activity T state","strike":["High [Asp] and high [CTP]","Low [Asp] and high [CTP]"]},{"text":"Aspartate is a substrate (carbamoyl phosphate is already saturating), so more of it fills more active sites.","hl":"carbamoyl phosphate + aspartate","strike":["Low [Asp] and low [CTP]"]},{"text":"Also: if the regulator were an activator, as AMP is for glycogen phosphorylase, the best choice would be high activator plus high substrate."},{"text":"Answer: high [Asp] and low [CTP]."}],"trap":"Choosing \"high\" for everything: a high level of a feedback inhibitor slows the enzyme, whatever the substrate level."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"CTP, an end product","note":"feedback inhibitor"},{"t":"low-activity T state","note":"inhibitor, keep low"},{"t":"carbamoyl phosphate was kept saturating","note":"only Asp limits"},{"t":"fastest","note":"maximize the rate"}]},{"text":"**Sort each molecule by role.**","calc":["substrate → high","activator → high","inhibitor → low"]},{"text":"**Feedback control.** The final product of a pathway shuts down its first committed step, so a cell rich in CTP stops making pyrimidines."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the fastest conditions combine high substrate, high allosteric activator and low allosteric inhibitor; identify each molecule’s role from the passage first."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-17a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:17#1","pe6-cp:17#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:17"],"passage":"A handheld glucose meter reads blood sugar with a small electrochemical cell. On the test strip, a reaction at one electrode releases electrons that flow out through copper leads to the meter’s circuit. In a reference version of the sensor, the two half-cells are joined by a strip of agar gel saturated with KCl, which completes the circuit. The meter then reads a steady current of a few microamps.","q":"Inside the KCl–agar gel, the charge is carried by:","choices":["Ions; electrolytic","Electrons; metallic","Electrons; electrolytic","Ions; metallic"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Free electrons carry current in metals such as the copper leads; a salt gel has no sea of mobile electrons."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Electrons cannot drift through a water-based gel; electrolytic conduction never uses free electrons."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Metallic conduction means electrons moving through a metal lattice; agar gel is not a metal."}],"rule":"Name the carrier by the material: metal wires → free electrons (metallic conduction); solutions, gels and salt bridges → dissolved ions (electrolytic).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Metal = electron sea; salt solution = K⁺ and Cl⁻ that move. The gel is a salt solution, so the carriers are charged particles of the salt."},"exp":"The gel is water saturated with **KCl**, so it holds **K⁺ and Cl⁻ ions** free to move: K⁺ drifts one way and Cl⁻ the other. Charge carried by moving ions is **electrolytic conduction**. In the **copper leads**, by contrast, current is carried by **free electrons** drifting through the metal lattice (metallic conduction).","how":{"ask":"Is the gel a metal or a salt solution, and what moves in each kind of conductor?","know":"• **Metallic conduction**: free (delocalized) electrons drift through a metal lattice; wires, leads.\n• **Electrolytic conduction**: dissolved ions migrate; solutions, gels, salt bridges, cell fluids.\n• A circuit can use both: electrons in the wire, ions in the solution.","steps":[{"text":"The gel is saturated with KCl, so it contains dissolved K⁺ and Cl⁻.","hl":"agar gel saturated with KCl"},{"text":"A watery gel is not a metal, so the metallic labels are out.","strike":["Electrons; metallic","Ions; metallic"]},{"text":"Free electrons do not travel through an electrolyte; only ions move there.","strike":["Electrons; electrolytic"]},{"text":"Also: in the copper leads the carriers are free electrons, which is metallic conduction.","hl":"copper leads"},{"text":"Answer: ions, electrolytic conduction."}],"trap":"Assuming electrons carry current everywhere in a circuit: they do in the wires, but in a salt bridge or gel the ions move."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"copper leads","note":"metal: electrons"},{"t":"saturated with KCl","note":"dissolved ions"},{"t":"charge is carried by","note":"name the carrier"}]},{"text":"**Two kinds of conductor.**","calc":["metal → free electrons → metallic","electrolyte → ions → electrolytic"]},{"text":"**One circuit, two carriers.** Electrons leave an electrode through the wire; inside the solution or bridge, ions carry the charge the rest of the way around."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a metal the charge carriers are free electrons (metallic conduction); in a solution, gel or salt bridge they are ions (electrolytic conduction)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-18a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"physics","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:18#1","pe6-cp:18#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:18"],"passage":"A jeweler recycles scrap gold by melting it in a small graphite crucible. In one batch, 150 g of gold was heated until it was already at its melting point but still entirely solid. For gold, L_fusion = 6.4 × 10⁴ J/kg and c = 129 J/kg·K.","q":"How much more heat is needed to melt the batch completely?","choices":["9.6 × 10³ J","6.4 × 10⁴ J","4.3 × 10⁵ J","9.6 × 10⁶ J"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Using 150 g in place of 0.150 kg with L in J/kg inflates the answer 1000-fold."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"That divides L by the mass instead of multiplying: heat for melting grows with mass, Q = mL."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"That is L for a whole kilogram, copied from the stem; the sample is only 0.150 kg, so the heat must be smaller."}],"rule":"Melting at the melting point: Q = mL with m in kg; no temperature change, so c is a distractor. Round m and L to 1 digit first.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Already at the melting point: ΔT = 0, so the c value is a decoy. q = mL = 0.15 × 6.4 × 10⁴: 0.15 × 6.4 = 0.96 → 9.6 × 10³ J."},"exp":"The gold is already at its melting point, so there is **no temperature change** and the specific heat is not needed. **q = mL** = 0.150 kg × 6.4 × 10⁴ J/kg = **9.6 × 10³ J**.","how":{"ask":"Which heat formula applies when a solid changes phase at constant temperature?","know":"• **Phase change:** q = m·L (no ΔT); L_fusion for melting, L_vap for boiling.\n• **Temperature change:** q = m·c·ΔT, only when T changes.\n• Match units: L in J/kg needs m in kg (g ÷ 1000).","steps":[{"text":"Already at the melting point: no ΔT, so q = mL only; the specific heat is a distractor.","hl":"already at its melting point"},{"text":"Convert the mass to kilograms.","hl":"150 g","calc":["150 g ÷ 1000 = 0.150 kg"],"strike":["9.6 × 10⁶ J"]},{"text":"Multiply mass by the heat of fusion.","calc":["q = 0.150 kg × 6.4 × 10⁴ J/kg","q = 0.96 × 10⁴ J = 9.6 × 10³ J"],"strike":["6.4 × 10⁴ J","4.3 × 10⁵ J"]},{"text":"Answer: 9.6 × 10³ J."}],"trap":"Adding an m·c·ΔT term; the solid is already at its melting point, so there is no warming step and c goes unused."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"already at its melting point","note":"no ΔT"},{"t":"150 g","note":"→ 0.150 kg"},{"t":"6.4 × 10⁴ J/kg","note":"L_fusion"},{"t":"129 J/kg·K","note":"distractor"}]},{"text":"**Pick the formula.** Melting at a fixed temperature uses q = mL; m·c·ΔT is for warming or cooling."},{"text":"**Also: units first.** L is per kilogram, so grams must become kilograms before multiplying.","calc":["150 g = 0.150 kg"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** heat for a phase change at constant T is q = m·L with m in kg; use m·c·ΔT only when the temperature moves."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-19a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:19#1","pe6-cp:19#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:19"],"passage":"Some sweeteners and imaging tracers are made by swapping hydroxyl groups of sugars for halogen atoms. A chemist designed a simple chlorinated sugar analog: glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆, 180 g/mol) with one hydroxyl group replaced by a chlorine atom (35.5 g/mol). Its molar mass was needed to prepare solutions of known concentration.","q":"What is the analog’s approximate molar mass?","choices":["163 g/mol","197.5 g/mol","198.5 g/mol","215.5 g/mol"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That removes the OH (17) and stops; the chlorine atom that takes its place still adds 35.5."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That adds the Cl but never takes the OH away: a swap is one subtraction plus one addition, not just an addition."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"That subtracts 18, as if a whole water left; OH weighs 17 (16 + 1), one H less."}],"rule":"A swap is minus the old group, plus the new atom (OH = 17). Round the masses to whole numbers, then subtract and add.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Swap, don't rebuild: 180 − OH (17) + Cl (35.5) = 180 + 18.5 = 198.5 g/mol."},"exp":"Start from glucose, **180 g/mol**. Take off one **OH (16 + 1 = 17)** and put on one **Cl (35.5)**: 180 − 17 + 35.5 = **198.5 g/mol** (formula C₆H₁₁ClO₅: 72 + 11 + 35.5 + 80 = 198.5).","how":{"ask":"Adjust the parent sugar's molar mass for the group that leaves and the atom that enters.","know":"• A substitution changes the molar mass by **(mass added) − (mass removed)**.\n• OH = 16 + 1 = **17 g/mol** (not 18: that is a whole water).\n• Cl ≈ **35.5 g/mol**.\n• Net change for OH → Cl: **+18.5 g/mol**.","steps":[{"text":"Start from the parent molecule's molar mass.","hl":"C₆H₁₂O₆, 180 g/mol","calc":["parent = 180 g/mol"]},{"text":"Subtract the hydroxyl group that leaves.","hl":"one hydroxyl group replaced","calc":["OH = 16 + 1 = 17","180 − 17 = 163 g/mol"]},{"text":"Add the chlorine atom that takes its place.","hl":"chlorine atom (35.5 g/mol)","calc":["163 + 35.5 = **198.5 g/mol**"]},{"text":"163 stops after the subtraction, and 215.5 adds the chlorine without removing the OH.","strike":["163 g/mol","215.5 g/mol"]},{"text":"197.5 subtracts 18, as if a whole water left, but only the OH (17) is replaced.","strike":["197.5 g/mol"]},{"text":"Answer: 198.5 g/mol."}],"trap":"Subtracting 18 for the leaving group (197.5 g/mol): in a direct swap only OH (17) leaves, not H₂O."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"180 g/mol","note":"parent sugar"},{"t":"one hydroxyl group","note":"leaves: −17"},{"t":"chlorine atom (35.5 g/mol)","note":"enters: +35.5"}]},{"text":"**Start from the parent.** The new molecule is glucose with one change, so its molar mass is glucose's plus a correction.","calc":["M(new) = M(parent) − M(removed) + M(added)"]},{"text":"**Weigh the swapped pieces.** OH is one O (16) and one H (1); Cl is 35.5.","calc":["−17 + 35.5 = +18.5","180 + 18.5 = 198.5 g/mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same method works for any swap. Glucose with one OH replaced by ¹⁸F (mass 18) changes by −17 + 18 = +1."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a substituted molecule, molar mass = parent − group removed + group added; OH (17) → Cl (35.5) is a net +18.5."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-20a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:20#2","pe6-cp:20#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:20"],"passage":"Carbon-14 forms in the upper atmosphere and is taken up by living things along with ordinary carbon-12, which is stable. After an organism dies, its carbon-14 decays by beta-minus emission to nitrogen-14, while its carbon-12 remains. Both carbon isotopes have six protons in the nucleus.","q":"How does carbon-14’s n : p ratio compare with carbon-12’s, and what happens in its nucleus as it decays (n = neutron, p = proton)?","choices":["Higher; n → p","Higher; p → n","Lower; n → p","Lower; p → n"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction flipped: β⁻ emission ejects an electron, which turns a neutron into a proton, not the reverse."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Ratio flipped: carbon-14 has eight neutrons for six protons, more than carbon-12’s six for six."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"That pair describes proton-rich positron emitters such as carbon-11; β⁻ emitters carry extra neutrons."}],"rule":"Same Z, different A: extra neutrons → n:p too high → β⁻ (n → p); too few neutrons → n:p too low → β⁺ or electron capture (p → n).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Isotopes share Z; only N differs. Heavier isotope → more neutrons → n:p up. β⁻ fixes a neutron excess by converting one neutron into a proton."},"exp":"Isotopes of carbon all have **6 protons**; carbon-14 has **8 neutrons** and carbon-12 has 6, so carbon-14’s **n : p ratio is higher**. It sheds the excess by **β⁻ decay**: a **neutron becomes a proton** plus an emitted electron (and an antineutrino), giving nitrogen-14 (7 p, 7 n). A proton-poor-in-neutrons isotope does the reverse: carbon-11 (6 p, 5 n) decays by positron emission, turning a proton into a neutron and becoming boron-11.","how":{"ask":"How many neutrons does each isotope have, and which particle change does β⁻ emission cause?","know":"• Isotopes: same Z (protons), different N (neutrons); N = A − Z.\n• **β⁻ decay**: n → p + e⁻; Z up 1, A same; fixes too many neutrons.\n• **β⁺ (positron) decay** or electron capture: p → n; Z down 1, A same; fixes too few neutrons.","steps":[{"text":"Count neutrons: both have six protons.","hl":"six protons","calc":["carbon-12: 12 − 6 = 6 n → n:p = 1.0","carbon-14: 14 − 6 = 8 n → n:p ≈ 1.33"],"strike":["Lower; n → p","Lower; p → n"]},{"text":"β⁻ emission ejects an electron from the nucleus: a neutron becomes a proton, so Z rises to 7 (nitrogen).","hl":"beta-minus emission","calc":["¹⁴₆C → ¹⁴₇N + e⁻"],"strike":["Higher; p → n"]},{"text":"Also: positron emission is the opposite fix, for neutron-poor nuclei: carbon-11 (5 n) turns a proton into a neutron and becomes boron-11."},{"text":"Answer: higher n:p, and a neutron becomes a proton."}],"trap":"Mixing up β⁻ and β⁺: a negative electron leaving means the nucleus gained positive charge, so a neutron became a proton."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Carbon-14","note":"6 p, 8 n"},{"t":"carbon-12, which is stable","note":"6 p, 6 n"},{"t":"beta-minus emission","note":"n → p"}]},{"text":"**Isotopes differ only in neutrons.** Same Z, so the heavier isotope has the higher n : p ratio."},{"text":"**Decay fixes the ratio.**","calc":["too many n → β⁻: n → p","too few n → β⁺ or capture: p → n"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare isotopes by neutron count (N = A − Z); a neutron-rich nucleus decays by β⁻ (n → p, Z up 1), and a neutron-poor one by positron emission or capture (p → n, Z down 1)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-21a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:21#1","pe6-cp:21#2","pe6-cp:21#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:21"],"passage":"Salt bridges between oppositely charged side chains help hold a folded protein together. In the crystal structure of a heat-stable enzyme from a hot-spring microbe, one salt bridge pairs the side chains –(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺ and –(CH₂)₂–COO⁻. Mutating either residue lowers the enzyme’s melting temperature by several degrees.","q":"Which two amino acids form this salt bridge?","choices":["Lysine and glutamate","Arginine and glutamate","Lysine and aspartate","Histidine and glutamine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Arginine ends in a guanidinium, –NH–C(=NH₂⁺)–NH₂, after three CH₂; a plain –NH₃⁺ after four CH₂ is a different residue."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Aspartate's side chain is –CH₂–COO⁻, one CH₂; the stem's carboxylate sits after two."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Histidine's side chain is an imidazole ring and glutamine ends in an amide (–CONH₂); neither ends in NH₃⁺ or COO⁻."}],"rule":"Count the CH₂ groups and read the end group: Lys –(CH₂)₄NH₃⁺, Arg guanidinium, Asp –CH₂COO⁻, Glu –(CH₂)₂COO⁻.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Count CH₂ links: 4 then NH₃⁺ = lysine; 2 then COO⁻ = glutamate (aspartate has 1, arginine ends in guanidinium). Lys + Glu."},"exp":"**Four CH₂ then –NH₃⁺** is lysine (arginine ends in a guanidinium after three CH₂). **Two CH₂ then –COO⁻** is glutamate (aspartate has one CH₂; glutamine ends in –CONH₂). Lys⁺ and Glu⁻ form the salt bridge.","how":{"ask":"Name each side chain by its end group, then count its CH₂ groups.","know":"• **Lys**: –(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺. **Arg**: –(CH₂)₃–NH–C(=NH₂⁺)–NH₂ (guanidinium).\n• **Asp**: –CH₂–COO⁻. **Glu**: –(CH₂)₂–COO⁻.\n• **Asn**: –CH₂–CONH₂. **Gln**: –(CH₂)₂–CONH₂.","steps":[{"text":"First side chain: four CH₂ and a plain ammonium end. Arginine would end in a guanidinium; histidine is a ring.","hl":"–(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺","calc":["(CH₂)₄ + NH₃⁺ → **lysine**","arginine: (CH₂)₃ + guanidinium","histidine: imidazole ring"],"strike":["Arginine and glutamate","Histidine and glutamine"]},{"text":"Second side chain: two CH₂ and a carboxylate. Aspartate has only one CH₂.","hl":"–(CH₂)₂–COO⁻","calc":["(CH₂)₂ + COO⁻ → **glutamate**","aspartate: one CH₂","glutamine: –CONH₂, no charge"],"strike":["Lysine and aspartate"]},{"text":"Answer: lysine and glutamate."}],"trap":"Aspartate and glutamate share the carboxylate end; only the CH₂ count tells them apart."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"–(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺","note":"side chain 1"},{"t":"–(CH₂)₂–COO⁻","note":"side chain 2"},{"t":"salt bridge","note":"+ meets −"}]},{"text":"**Name the end group first.** A lone –NH₃⁺ is lysine's; a carbon bonded to three N with a + charge is arginine's guanidinium.","fig":["lysine-sc","arginine-sc"],"calc":["–(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺ → Lys","–(CH₂)₃–NH–C(=NH₂⁺)–NH₂ → Arg"]},{"text":"**Then count the CH₂ groups** to tell close relatives apart.","calc":["–CH₂–COO⁻ → Asp","–(CH₂)₂–COO⁻ → Glu"]},{"text":"**Also:** side-chain amides: –CH₂–C(=O)NH₂ is asparagine, and one more CH₂ makes glutamine. Neither is charged."},{"text":"**Also:** side-chain carboxylates: aspartate (one CH₂) and glutamate (two CH₂) are the negative residues at pH 7."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** read the end group (ammonium, guanidinium, amide, carboxylate), then count CH₂ groups: Lys 4, Arg 3, Asp and Asn 1, Glu and Gln 2."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-22a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:22#1","pe6-cp:22#2","pe6-cp:22#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:22"],"passage":"P-type ion pumps are powered by phosphorylating one of their own aspartate residues. In the proposed mechanism, the aspartate side chain (the drawn model compound) attacks the terminal phosphorus of ATP with a lone pair, forming an acyl phosphate. After water later removes the phosphoryl group, the same atom takes a proton from a nearby water molecule. Four sites of the model are labeled I to IV.","q":"Which labeled site performs both steps?","fig":"asp-ca-sites","choices":["Site I","Site II","Site III","Site IV"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"A carbonyl carbon is electron-poor: it is attacked by nucleophiles rather than attacking, and it has no lone pair for H⁺."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"An amide N’s lone pair is tied up in resonance with C=O, so it is a poor nucleophile and a poor base."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An sp³ C–H has no lone pair: it can neither attack phosphorus nor accept a proton."}],"rule":"Attacking with a lone pair (nucleophile) and later accepting H⁺ (general base) both need a free lone pair: look for O⁻ or a free N, never a C–H or amide N–H.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Both jobs use one lone pair. The only labeled atom with a negative charge and free lone pairs is the carboxylate oxygen."},"exp":"Attacking phosphorus with a lone pair makes the atom a **nucleophile**; taking a proton later makes it a **general base**. Both need an **available lone pair**. Site I is the **carboxylate O⁻**: negatively charged with three lone pairs. Site II is an amide N–H (lone pair delocalized), site III a C–H (no lone pair), and site IV the carbonyl carbon, an electrophile.","how":{"ask":"Which labeled atom has a free lone pair to attack phosphorus and later to take a proton?","know":"• **Nucleophile**: donates a lone pair to an electron-poor atom (P, C=O carbon).\n• **General base**: removes a proton; **general acid**: donates one.\n• Good lone-pair donors: O⁻ (carboxylate, alkoxide), neutral amine or imidazole N. Poor: amide N, C–H.","steps":[{"text":"Both steps need a lone pair: one to bond to P, one to bond to H⁺.","hl":"attacks the terminal phosphorus of ATP with a lone pair"},{"text":"Check each site.","fig":"asp-ca-sites","calc":["I: carboxylate O⁻, three lone pairs → ✓","II: amide N–H, lone pair in resonance → poor","III: C–H, no lone pair","IV: C=O carbon, electron-poor"]},{"text":"The carbonyl carbon is the electrophile in such reactions, the opposite role.","strike":["Site IV"]},{"text":"The amide N and the C–H have no usable lone pair.","strike":["Site II","Site III"]},{"text":"Answer: site I, the carboxylate oxygen: nucleophile first, general base later."}],"trap":"Picking the carbonyl carbon because the reaction \"happens at the carboxyl group\": the carbon is attacked; the oxygen does the attacking."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"with a lone pair","note":"nucleophile"},{"t":"takes a proton","note":"general base"},{"t":"the same atom","note":"one site, both roles"}]},{"text":"**Three roles to name.**","calc":["gives a lone pair to an electrophile → nucleophile","removes H⁺ → general base","donates H⁺ → general acid"]},{"text":"**Who has a free lone pair?** O⁻ and neutral amine N do; amide N shares its pair with C=O; carbons in C–H or C=O have none to give."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an atom that attacks with a lone pair is a nucleophile, one that takes a proton is a general base, and one that gives a proton is a general acid; both of the first two need a free lone pair, as on O⁻ or a neutral N."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-23a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:23#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:23"],"passage":"Flavodoxin holds its cofactor, flavin mononucleotide (FMN), so tightly that the two can be separated only by denaturing the protein. In the crystal structure, an arginine side chain in the binding pocket points an N–H toward the flavin ring and forms a hydrogen bond to it. FMN is shown with four atoms labeled I to IV.","q":"Which labeled site could take the arginine’s hydrogen bond yet has no H to give?","fig":"fmn-sites","choices":["Atom I","Atom II","Atom III","Atom IV"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"That ring N carries an H, so it can donate; its lone pair is spread into the two flanking C=O groups."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"This N bonds to three carbons and its lone pair is part of the ring’s π system, so it is a poor acceptor."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Phosphorus here is bonded to four oxygens and has no lone pair, so it can neither accept nor donate."}],"rule":"H-bond acceptor = N, O or F with an available lone pair; donor = an H on N, O or F. Acceptor-only sites are lone-pair atoms with no H.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Acceptor-only = lone pair, no H. Skip any N–H or O–H (donors) and any atom whose lone pair is busy; the ring N with a free in-plane pair is left."},"exp":"To **accept** a hydrogen bond an atom must be N, O or F **with an available lone pair**; to **donate** it needs an **H bonded to N, O or F**. Atom II is a ring nitrogen with **no H** and a lone pair pointing out in the ring plane: an acceptor only. Atom I is an N–H (a donor), atom III is a ring N whose lone pair belongs to the π system, and atom IV is a phosphorus with no lone pair. The same logic makes the C=O and carboxylate oxygens of a ligand such as pyruvate acceptors only.","how":{"ask":"Which labeled atom has a free lone pair but no attached H?","know":"• **Donor**: H on N, O or F. **Acceptor**: an N, O or F atom bearing a lone pair.\n• C=O oxygens, carboxylate O⁻, ether O and pyridine-like ring N (no H) are acceptors only.\n• An N whose lone pair is delocalized (amide N, pyrrole-like N) is a poor acceptor.","steps":[{"text":"The arginine supplies the H, so the flavin atom must accept: it needs a lone pair, and the question says it has no H.","hl":"points an N–H toward the flavin ring"},{"text":"Check each labeled atom.","fig":"fmn-sites","calc":["I: N–H → donor","II: ring N, no H, free lone pair → acceptor only ✓","III: N bonded to three C, pair in the π system","IV: P, no lone pair"]},{"text":"Atom I can donate, and atoms III and IV have no usable lone pair.","strike":["Atom I","Atom III","Atom IV"]},{"text":"Answer: atom II."}],"trap":"Picking the N–H because it \"looks like\" the binding site: an N–H is a donor, so it fails the \"no H to give\" condition."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"points an N–H toward the flavin ring","note":"arginine donates"},{"t":"take the arginine’s hydrogen bond","note":"needs a lone pair"},{"t":"no H to give","note":"not a donor"}]},{"text":"**Donor vs acceptor.**","calc":["donor: N–H, O–H, F–H","acceptor: N, O or F bearing a lone pair","acceptor only: lone pair, no H"]},{"text":"**Common acceptor-only groups.** Carbonyl O, carboxylate O⁻, ether O, and a ring N with no H (pyridine-like)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an atom can accept but not donate a hydrogen bond when it is N, O or F with a free lone pair and no H attached, such as a C=O oxygen, a carboxylate oxygen or a pyridine-like ring N."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-24a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:24#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:24"],"passage":"Lysozyme cuts the glycosidic bond between two sugars in bacterial cell walls. In the accepted mechanism, a glutamic acid residue at the active site keeps its proton at the reaction pH because it sits in a nonpolar pocket. As the C–O bond to the departing sugar breaks, this glutamic acid hands its proton to the leaving oxygen. Afterward it is reprotonated by water from the solvent, not by the substrate.","q":"This glutamic acid most likely functions to:","choices":["act as a general acid.","act as a general base.","relay protons.","act as a nucleophile."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction flipped: the residue gives its proton away to the substrate; a base would take one from it."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"A relay takes a proton from one reacting group and passes it to another; this residue never took one from the substrate."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"It forms no bond to the sugar carbon; it only transfers H⁺ to the leaving oxygen."}],"rule":"Track the proton: gives H⁺ → general acid; takes H⁺ → general base; takes H⁺ from one group and hands it to another → proton shuttle; bonds to carbon → nucleophile.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ask where the residue’s proton goes. It starts protonated and gives H⁺ to the leaving group, so it is a proton donor, a general acid."},"exp":"The glutamic acid starts **protonated** (a nonpolar pocket raises its pKa) and **donates its H⁺** to the oxygen of the leaving sugar as the C–O bond breaks. A residue that gives a proton to a substrate is a **general acid catalyst**. A **proton shuttle** would first take a proton from one group of the substrate and then pass that same proton to another, as the threonine-like relays in some carboxylases do.","how":{"ask":"Does the residue give a proton, take one, or take one from one group and pass it to another?","know":"• **General acid**: donates H⁺ to the substrate (e.g. to a leaving group).\n• **General base**: removes H⁺ from the substrate.\n• **Proton shuttle**: accepts H⁺ from one group, then gives it to another group.\n• **Nucleophile**: forms a bond to an electrophilic atom.","steps":[{"text":"The residue keeps its proton and then hands it to the leaving oxygen: it donates H⁺.","hl":"hands its proton to the leaving oxygen","strike":["act as a general base."]},{"text":"It is reprotonated by solvent water, not by the substrate, so it never moved a proton from one reacting group to another.","hl":"not by the substrate","strike":["relay protons."]},{"text":"It makes no bond to the sugar carbon.","strike":["act as a nucleophile."]},{"text":"Answer: it acts as a general acid."}],"trap":"Calling any residue that gains and loses a proton a \"shuttle\": a shuttle takes the proton from one reacting group and gives it to another."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"keeps its proton","note":"starts as HA"},{"t":"hands its proton to the leaving oxygen","note":"donates H⁺"},{"t":"reprotonated by water from the solvent","note":"not a relay"}]},{"text":"**Follow the proton.**","calc":["gives H⁺ → general acid","takes H⁺ → general base","takes from A, gives to B → shuttle"]},{"text":"**pKa and environment.** A nonpolar pocket keeps a carboxylic acid protonated at a pH where it would usually be ionized, so it can donate H⁺."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** name a catalytic residue’s role by where its proton goes: donates H⁺ → general acid, removes H⁺ → general base, carries H⁺ from one reacting group to another → proton shuttle."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-25a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:25#1","pe6-cp:25#2","pe6-cp:25#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:25"],"q":"Complete hydrolysis of an unknown tripeptide releases twice as much Phe as Ser. Partial hydrolysis gives the dipeptides Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser. What is the tripeptide's sequence?","choices":["Phe–Phe–Ser","Ser–Phe–Phe","Phe–Ser–Phe","Phe–Ser–Ser"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Dipeptides read N → C: this chain holds Ser–Phe, the reverse of the observed Phe–Ser, and no Phe–Ser at all."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Two Ser and one Phe reverses the count: complete hydrolysis showed twice as much Phe as Ser."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Its neighbor pairs are Phe–Ser and Ser–Phe; with no Phe next to Phe, it cannot release a Phe–Phe dipeptide."}],"rule":"Get the composition first, then overlap the fragments; dipeptides are read N → C, so each must appear in that order in the chain.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Chain the dipeptides on their shared Phe: Phe–Phe then Phe–Ser overlap into Phe–Phe–Ser, which also matches 2 Phe : 1 Ser."},"exp":"Counts: **2 Phe + 1 Ser**. Each dipeptide is a pair of neighbors written N → C. Overlap them on the shared Phe: Phe–**Phe** + **Phe**–Ser → **Phe–Phe–Ser**, which releases exactly Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser.","how":{"ask":"Which order of two Phe and one Ser contains both Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser as neighbor pairs?","know":"• Complete hydrolysis breaks every peptide bond: it gives the **composition**.\n• Partial hydrolysis leaves short pieces that **keep their order** (N → C).\n• Overlap the pieces on a shared residue to build the chain, then check the counts.","steps":[{"text":"Composition: twice as much Phe as Ser in three residues means two Phe and one Ser, which rules out two Ser.","hl":"twice as much Phe as Ser","calc":["2 Phe + 1 Ser"],"strike":["Phe–Ser–Ser"]},{"text":"List the neighbor pairs each remaining order would release.","calc":["Phe–Phe–Ser → **Phe–Phe**, **Phe–Ser**","Ser–Phe–Phe → Ser–Phe, Phe–Phe","Phe–Ser–Phe → Phe–Ser, Ser–Phe"]},{"text":"Only one order gives both observed pieces: the others give Ser–Phe or lack Phe–Phe.","hl":"Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser","strike":["Ser–Phe–Phe","Phe–Ser–Phe"]},{"text":"Answer: Phe–Phe–Ser."}],"trap":"Ser–Phe is not the same piece as Phe–Ser; the order is fixed N → C, so a reversed pair is a different dipeptide."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Complete hydrolysis","note":"all bonds cut: counts"},{"t":"twice as much Phe as Ser","note":"2 Phe, 1 Ser"},{"t":"Partial hydrolysis","note":"some bonds cut: neighbors"},{"t":"Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser","note":"two neighbor pairs"}]},{"text":"**Each dipeptide shows two neighbors**, in N → C order.","calc":["Phe–Phe: a Phe right before a Phe","Phe–Ser: a Phe right before Ser"]},{"text":"**Assemble one chain that contains both**: overlap them on the shared Phe.","calc":["Phe–Phe","     Phe–Ser","→ Phe–Phe–Ser"]},{"text":"**Check against the counts.**","calc":["Phe–Phe–Ser: 2 Phe, 1 Ser ✓"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** get the composition from complete hydrolysis, overlap the partial pieces on shared residues, and keep only the order that explains every piece and the counts."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-26a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:26#1","pe6-cp:26#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:26"],"q":"How many liters of O₂ at STP does the complete combustion of 29 g of butane, C₄H₁₀, consume?","choices":["11.2 L","44.8 L","72.8 L","145.6 L"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"Uses one O₂ per butane; the balanced equation needs 13 O₂ for every 2 butane, so the coefficient step was skipped."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"That is the CO₂ volume (4 CO₂ per butane), copied in place of the O₂ coefficient."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"Copies the 13 from the doubled equation as O₂ per butane, forgetting that it serves 2 butane molecules."}],"rule":"Combustion volume: balance first, then mol fuel × (O₂ coefficient ÷ fuel coefficient) × 22.4 L at STP.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"C₄H₁₀ + 6.5 O₂ → 4 CO₂ + 5 H₂O. 29 g ÷ 58 g/mol = 0.50 mol; × 6.5 = 3.25 mol O₂; × 22.4 ≈ 73 L."},"exp":"Balance: **2 C₄H₁₀ + 13 O₂ → 8 CO₂ + 10 H₂O**, so each butane needs **6.5 O₂**. Butane is 58 g/mol, so 29 g = **0.50 mol**. O₂ = 0.50 × 6.5 = **3.25 mol**, and at STP 3.25 × 22.4 L = **72.8 L**.","how":{"ask":"How many O₂ does one butane need once the equation is balanced, and how many moles of butane are there?","know":"• Balance CₓHᵧ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O: C first, then H, then O (halves allowed, then double).\n• Moles of gas × 22.4 L/mol = volume at STP.\n• Molar mass of C₄H₁₀ = 4(12) + 10(1) = 58 g/mol.","steps":[{"text":"Balance the combustion.","hl":"butane, C₄H₁₀","calc":["C: 4 CO₂; H: 5 H₂O","O needed: 8 + 5 = 13 O atoms = 6.5 O₂","×2 → 2 C₄H₁₀ + 13 O₂ → 8 CO₂ + 10 H₂O"]},{"text":"Convert the mass of butane to moles.","hl":"29 g","calc":["29 g ÷ 58 g/mol = 0.50 mol"]},{"text":"Scale by the O₂ ratio and convert to liters.","calc":["0.50 × 13/2 = 3.25 mol O₂","3.25 × 22.4 L = **72.8 L**"]},{"text":"11.2 L skips the 6.5 ratio, 44.8 L is the CO₂, and 145.6 L uses 13 O₂ per butane.","strike":["11.2 L","44.8 L","145.6 L"]},{"text":"Answer: 72.8 L."}],"trap":"Reading the 13 in the doubled equation as O₂ per butane: it is O₂ per 2 butane."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"29 g","note":"0.50 mol butane"},{"t":"O₂ at STP","note":"22.4 L per mol"},{"t":"complete combustion","note":"CO₂ + H₂O"}]},{"text":"**Balance, then ratio.**","calc":["2 C₄H₁₀ + 13 O₂ → 8 CO₂ + 10 H₂O","O₂ : butane = 13 : 2"]},{"text":"**Moles to liters.** At STP, every mole of an ideal gas fills 22.4 L."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for gas used in a combustion, balance CₓHᵧ + O₂ first, convert the fuel to moles, multiply by the O₂ : fuel ratio, then by 22.4 L/mol at STP."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-27a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:27#2","pe6-cp:27#1","pe6-cp:27#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:27"],"q":"Four small oxygen-containing compounds are shown. Which one gives a ¹H NMR signal that is a doublet integrating to 6H?","choices":["2-Methyl-1-propanol","1-Propanol","Ethanol","tert-Butyl methyl ether"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe6-isobutanol","1-propanol","cpt-ethanol","cpt-pe6-mtbe"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"1-Propanol's CH₃ sits next to a CH₂ (2 H), so it is a 3H triplet, not a 6H doublet."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Ethanol's CH₃ sits next to a CH₂ (2 H), so it is a 3H triplet, not a 6H doublet."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Its (CH₃)₃C– carbon bears no H, so those 9 H are a singlet, and the OCH₃ is a singlet too: no doublet at all."}],"rule":"A 6H doublet means two equal CH₃ groups on a carbon with one H: look for an isopropyl-type (CH₃)₂CH– group.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"A 6H doublet = an isopropyl pattern: two CH₃ on one CH. Only 2-methyl-1-propanol has (CH₃)₂CH–."},"exp":"**n + 1 rule:** a signal is split into n + 1 peaks by the n H on adjacent carbons. A doublet means **one neighbor H**. In 2-methyl-1-propanol, (CH₃)₂CH–CH₂OH, the two equivalent CH₃ groups (6 H) border a single CH, so they appear as a **6H doublet**. 1-Propanol's and ethanol's CH₃ are triplets (next to CH₂), and the ether gives only singlets.","how":{"ask":"Which compound has six equivalent H on carbons that border a carbon carrying exactly one H?","know":"• n + 1 rule: n H on neighboring carbons split a signal into n + 1 peaks.\n• Doublet → 1 neighbor H; triplet → 2; quartet → 3; singlet → 0.\n• A 6H signal = two equivalent CH₃ groups. A (CH₃)₃C– group sits on a carbon with no H, so its 9 H give a singlet.","steps":[{"text":"6 H means two equivalent CH₃ groups; a doublet means exactly one H next door.","hl":"doublet integrating to 6H"},{"text":"2-Methyl-1-propanol is (CH₃)₂CH–CH₂OH: each CH₃ borders the CH, which carries 1 H.","calc":["n = 1 → 1 + 1 = **2 peaks** (doublet)","2 × CH₃ = **6 H**"]},{"text":"1-Propanol's CH₃ borders a CH₂, and so does ethanol's.","calc":["n = 2 → 2 + 1 = 3 peaks (triplet), only 3 H"],"strike":["1-Propanol","Ethanol"]},{"text":"Also: in the ether the central carbon of (CH₃)₃C– has no H, so its 9 H have n = 0 and give a singlet; the OCH₃ is a singlet as well.","calc":["n = 0 → 0 + 1 = 1 peak (singlet)"],"strike":["tert-Butyl methyl ether"]},{"text":"Answer: 2-methyl-1-propanol.","fig":"cpt-pe6-isobutanol"}],"trap":"Counting the H in the group itself instead of its neighbors: a CH₃ is split by the H next door, not by its own three."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"doublet","note":"1 neighbor H"},{"t":"6H","note":"two equivalent CH₃"}]},{"text":"**Also: the n + 1 rule.** Count the H on the adjacent carbons (not the O–H) and add 1.","calc":["0 neighbors → singlet","1 → doublet","2 → triplet","3 → quartet"]},{"text":"**Also:** a group whose neighbor carbon has no H is never split: the 9 H of a (CH₃)₃C– group show as one singlet."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** splitting = n + 1 from the H on neighboring carbons, so a 6H doublet points to (CH₃)₂CH– and an unsplit 9H signal to (CH₃)₃C–."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-27b","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:27#4","pe6-cp:27#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:27"],"q":"An alcohol with formula C₅H₁₂O gives four ¹H NMR signals: a 6H singlet, a 3H triplet, a 2H quartet and a 1H broad singlet. Which compound is it?","choices":["Pentan-1-ol","Pentan-2-ol","3-Methylbutan-2-ol","2-Methylbutan-2-ol"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pentan-1-ol","cpt-pentan-2-ol","cpt-3-methylbutan-2-ol","cpt-2-methylbutan-2-ol"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A straight chain has no carbon holding two CH₃ groups, so it cannot give a 6H singlet."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Its lone branch CH₃ sits next to a CH and shows as a 3H doublet; nothing gives 6H."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Its two CH₃ groups sit on a CH, so they appear as a 6H doublet, not a singlet."}],"rule":"n + 1 rule: a signal splits into (neighboring H + 1) peaks. A 6H singlet means two CH₃ on a carbon with no H; an O–H usually shows as an unsplit singlet.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"6H singlet ⇒ (CH₃)₂C– on a carbon with no H ⇒ a tertiary carbinol. Triplet 3H + quartet 2H ⇒ an ethyl group on that carbon."},"exp":"A **6H singlet** means two equivalent CH₃ groups on a carbon bearing **no hydrogen**; the only such choice is the tertiary alcohol **(CH₃)₂C(OH)CH₂CH₃**. Its ethyl group gives the **3H triplet** (CH₃ next to CH₂) and the **2H quartet** (CH₂ next to CH₃), and the **O–H** is a broad, unsplit 1H singlet, since rapid exchange erases its coupling.","how":{"ask":"Which choice has two CH₃ groups on a carbon that carries no H, plus an ethyl group?","know":"• **n + 1 rule**: n equivalent H on neighboring carbons split a signal into n + 1 peaks.\n• Singlet = no neighboring H; doublet = one; triplet = two; quartet = three.\n• An O–H usually appears as a broad singlet (fast exchange removes splitting).\n• Integration gives the number of H in each signal.","steps":[{"text":"Read the 6H singlet: two CH₃ with zero neighboring H, so they sit on a carbon with no H, the C–OH carbon of a tertiary alcohol.","hl":"a 6H singlet","calc":["6H = 2 × CH₃","singlet → n = 0 neighbors"]},{"text":"Pentan-1-ol has no such pair of methyls; in pentan-2-ol and 3-methylbutan-2-ol the CH₃ groups sit next to a CH, so they are doublets.","strike":["Pentan-1-ol","Pentan-2-ol","3-Methylbutan-2-ol"]},{"text":"Check the rest: the triplet and quartet are an ethyl group, and the broad singlet is the OH.","hl":"a 3H triplet, a 2H quartet","calc":["CH₃ next to CH₂ → 2 + 1 = triplet","CH₂ next to CH₃ → 3 + 1 = quartet","O–H → broad singlet"],"fig":"cpt-2-methylbutan-2-ol"},{"text":"Answer: 2-methylbutan-2-ol."}],"trap":"Expecting the OH to split its neighbors or be split: in a routine spectrum it exchanges too fast and appears as a broad singlet."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"C₅H₁₂O","note":"all choices fit"},{"t":"6H singlet","note":"2 CH₃, no neighbor H"},{"t":"1H broad singlet","note":"the O–H"}]},{"text":"**Splitting counts neighbors.**","calc":["0 neighbors → singlet","1 → doublet","2 → triplet","3 → quartet"]},{"text":"**Classic patterns.** tert-Butyl = 9H singlet; isopropyl = 6H doublet + 1H septet; ethyl = 3H triplet + 2H quartet; O–H = broad singlet."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** match each NMR signal to a group by its integration (how many H) and its n + 1 splitting (how many neighboring H); groups on a carbon with no H appear as singlets, and an O–H is usually an unsplit singlet."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-28a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe6-cp:28#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:28"],"q":"A block floats in fresh water (1000 kg/m³) with 60% of its volume submerged, and in an oil with 80% submerged. What is the oil’s density?","choices":["600 kg/m³","750 kg/m³","800 kg/m³","1330 kg/m³"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Ratio flipped: the block sinks deeper in the oil, so the oil must be less dense than water, not more."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That is the block’s own density; the second step, dividing it by the fraction under in oil, was skipped."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"Copies the 80% submerged fraction as a density; the fraction equals ρ_block ÷ ρ_oil, not ρ_oil."}],"rule":"Floating: fraction under the surface = ρ_object ÷ ρ_liquid. Get ρ_object from the first fluid, then divide it by the fraction in the second.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"ρ_block = 0.60 × 1000 = 600 kg/m³. In oil, ρ_oil = 600 ÷ 0.80 = 750 kg/m³: sinking deeper means a thinner liquid."},"exp":"A floating object displaces its own weight, so **fraction under the surface = ρ_object ÷ ρ_liquid**. In water: ρ_block = 0.60 × 1000 = **600 kg/m³**. In oil: 0.80 = 600 ÷ ρ_oil, so ρ_oil = 600 ÷ 0.80 = **750 kg/m³**. The block rides lower in oil, which fits a liquid less dense than water.","how":{"ask":"What is the block’s density from the water data, and what oil density makes it 80% submerged?","know":"• Floating: buoyant force = weight, so ρ_liquid × V_sub = ρ_object × V.\n• Fraction under the surface = ρ_object ÷ ρ_liquid; ρ_liquid = ρ_object ÷ fraction.\n• Deeper float → less dense fluid; higher float → denser fluid. Fully submerged and level with the surface → ρ_liquid = ρ_object.","steps":[{"text":"Find the block’s density from the water float.","hl":"60% of its volume submerged","calc":["ρ_block = 0.60 × 1000 = 600 kg/m³"],"strike":["600 kg/m³"]},{"text":"Use the oil float to find the oil’s density.","hl":"80% submerged","calc":["ρ_oil = 600 ÷ 0.80 = **750 kg/m³**"]},{"text":"It floats lower in the oil, so the oil is less dense than water; 1330 flips the ratio and 800 copies the fraction.","strike":["1330 kg/m³","800 kg/m³"]},{"text":"Answer: 750 kg/m³."}],"trap":"Dividing the wrong way: sinking deeper means a thinner fluid, so the second density must come out below the first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"1000 kg/m³","note":"reference fluid"},{"t":"60% of its volume submerged","note":"ρ_block ÷ 1000"},{"t":"80% submerged","note":"ρ_block ÷ ρ_oil"}]},{"text":"**Fraction under the surface = ρ_object ÷ ρ_liquid.** The same block has one density, so use the first fluid to get it."},{"text":"**Edges of the range.** Fully submerged at the surface → fluid density equals the object’s; the smallest fraction a scale shows → the densest fluid it can read."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a floating object, fraction under the surface = ρ_object ÷ ρ_liquid; find ρ_object from one fluid, then ρ_liquid = ρ_object ÷ fraction for any other."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-28b","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe6-cp:28#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:28"],"q":"A hydrometer has an average density of 0.90 g/cm³. Its scale is marked from fully submerged down to 75% submerged. What is the highest liquid density it can read?","choices":["0.68 g/cm³","0.83 g/cm³","0.90 g/cm³","1.20 g/cm³"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"0.68 multiplies 0.90 × 0.75; floating gives ρ_fluid = ρ_object ÷ fraction, so a fraction below 1 must raise the density."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Ratio upside-down (0.75 ÷ 0.90); riding higher needs a denser liquid, so the answer must sit above 0.90."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"0.90 g/cm³ is the hydrometer's own density, the fully submerged reading: the lowest on the scale, not the highest."}],"rule":"Fluid density = object density ÷ fraction submerged: the less it sinks, the denser the liquid, so the top reading uses the smallest fraction.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"ρ fluid = ρ object ÷ fraction under: smallest fraction gives the densest liquid. 0.90 ÷ 0.75 = 0.9 × 4/3 = 1.20 g/cm³."},"exp":"From fraction = ρ_object ÷ ρ_fluid, **ρ_fluid = ρ_object ÷ fraction**. Fully submerged (fraction 1) gives the **lowest** reading, 0.90 g/cm³. The smallest marked fraction gives the **highest**: 0.90 ÷ 0.75 = **1.20 g/cm³**.","how":{"ask":"At which mark does the hydrometer float highest, and what liquid density puts it there?","know":"• Floating: submerged fraction = ρ_object ÷ ρ_fluid, so ρ_fluid = ρ_object ÷ fraction.\n• Denser liquid → floats higher → smaller fraction under.\n• Range: fraction 1 → lowest density (= ρ_object); smallest marked fraction → highest density.","steps":[{"text":"The highest density is where the hydrometer floats highest: the smallest marked fraction.","hl":"75% submerged"},{"text":"Solve the floating rule for the liquid's density.","calc":["ρ_fluid = ρ_object ÷ fraction","= 0.90 ÷ 0.75 = **1.20 g/cm³**"]},{"text":"0.83 flips the ratio (0.75 ÷ 0.90) and 0.68 multiplies; both land below 0.90, yet riding higher needs a denser liquid.","strike":["0.83 g/cm³","0.68 g/cm³"]},{"text":"0.90 g/cm³ is the fully submerged reading: the bottom of the range.","strike":["0.90 g/cm³"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.20 g/cm³."}],"trap":"Thinking the fully submerged mark is the top of the scale: a hydrometer sinks deepest in the thinnest liquid, so full submersion is its lowest reading."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"average density of 0.90 g/cm³","note":"ρ_object"},{"t":"fully submerged","note":"fraction 1"},{"t":"75% submerged","note":"smallest fraction"},{"t":"highest liquid density","note":"floats highest"}]},{"text":"**Flip the floating rule.**","calc":["fraction = ρ_obj ÷ ρ_fluid → ρ_fluid = ρ_obj ÷ fraction"]},{"text":"**Both ends of the scale.**","calc":["fraction 1.00 → 0.90 ÷ 1.00 = 0.90 g/cm³ (lowest)","fraction 0.75 → 0.90 ÷ 0.75 = 1.20 g/cm³ (highest)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a hydrometer reads ρ_fluid = ρ_object ÷ fraction submerged: fully under is its lowest reading, the smallest marked fraction its highest."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-29a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:29#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:29"],"passage":"Isoelectric focusing showed that protein X, a small basic enzyme, has an isoelectric point (pI) of 7.5. The protein was then loaded into the middle of three gel strips buffered at pH 5.5, 7.5 and 9.5, each with the positive electrode at one end and the negative electrode at the other. All three strips were run for the same time at the same voltage.","q":"Which prediction about protein X’s movement is correct?","choices":["To cathode at 5.5; none at 7.5","To anode at 5.5; none at 7.5","To cathode at 5.5; to anode at 7.5","To anode at 5.5; to cathode at 7.5"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Below its pI a protein carries net + charge, so it heads for the − electrode, not the + one."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Both halves flipped: + charge below the pI heads to the cathode, and at the pI there is no net charge to pull on."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"At pH equal to the pI the net charge is zero, so the protein does not migrate in that strip."}],"rule":"pH below pI → net + → moves to the cathode (−); pH = pI → net 0 → stays put; pH above pI → net − → moves to the anode (+).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Compare pH with pI. Lower pH → extra H⁺ on the protein → positive → toward −. Equal → neutral → no movement."},"exp":"Below its pI a protein holds extra protons, so it carries a **net positive charge** and drifts toward the **negative electrode, the cathode**: at pH 5.5 (two units below the pI of 7.5) protein X heads to the cathode. At **pH 7.5 = pI** its net charge is **zero**, so it stays put. (At pH 9.5, above the pI, it would be net negative and move toward the anode.)","how":{"ask":"Is each buffer’s pH below, equal to or above the pI, and what net charge does that give?","know":"• At pH = pI, net charge = 0 → no migration.\n• pH < pI → net + → toward the cathode (−).\n• pH > pI → net − → toward the anode (+).\n• In electrophoresis the anode is +, the cathode −.","steps":[{"text":"pH 5.5 is below the pI of 7.5: the protein is net positive and moves toward the negative electrode.","hl":"pI) of 7.5","calc":["5.5 < 7.5 → net + → cathode"],"strike":["To anode at 5.5; none at 7.5","To anode at 5.5; to cathode at 7.5"]},{"text":"pH 7.5 equals the pI: no net charge, no movement.","calc":["7.5 = pI → net 0 → stays"],"strike":["To cathode at 5.5; to anode at 7.5"]},{"text":"Also: at pH 9.5 the protein is net negative and would move toward the anode."},{"text":"Answer: toward the cathode at pH 5.5, no movement at 7.5."}],"trap":"Mixing up the electrode names: in a gel the cathode is the negative electrode, where positive proteins go."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"isoelectric point (pI) of 7.5","note":"net charge 0 at pH 7.5"},{"t":"pH 5.5, 7.5 and 9.5","note":"below, at, above pI"},{"t":"positive electrode","note":"the anode"}]},{"text":"**pH vs pI sets the sign.**","calc":["pH < pI → +","pH = pI → 0","pH > pI → −"]},{"text":"**Opposites attract.** A positive protein moves toward the cathode (−); a negative one toward the anode (+); a neutral one stays."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a protein is net positive below its pI, neutral at its pI and net negative above it, so it moves toward the cathode, stays put, or moves toward the anode."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-30a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:30#1","pe6-cp:30#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:30"],"passage":"During weeks of storage, a purified enzyme slowly forms soluble aggregates. On SDS-PAGE without a reducing agent, the aggregates run as covalent dimers and trimers; when dithiothreitol (DTT), which breaks S–S bonds, is added to the sample buffer, only the monomer band remains. Mass spectrometry finds no dityrosine or other radical cross-links in the stored protein.","q":"Which side chain forms the cross-links?","choices":["Cysteine","Methionine","Threonine","Lysine"],"choiceFigs":["cysteine-sc","methionine-sc","threonine-sc","lysine-sc"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Its sulfur is a thioether with no S–H, so it cannot join another side chain through an S–S bond."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A hydroxyl does not form covalent links between chains that a disulfide reducer can break."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An amine does not form S–S bonds, so a link through it would survive the reducing agent."}],"rule":"Covalent links undone by DTT or β-mercaptoethanol are disulfides from two –SH groups; radical links such as dityrosine survive reducing agents.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Undone by a reducing agent ⇒ S–S bond ⇒ needs two thiols. Only one drawn side chain ends in –SH."},"exp":"The links are **covalent** (they survive SDS) but **break with DTT**, a reducing agent that cuts **disulfide (S–S) bonds**. A disulfide forms when two **cysteine thiols (–SH)** are oxidized together, so cysteine forms the cross-links. Methionine’s sulfur has no H to lose, and radical-made links such as dityrosine, which join two tyrosine rings by a C–C bond, would not be reversed by DTT and were not found.","how":{"ask":"What kind of covalent bond does DTT break, and which side chain can make it?","know":"• **Disulfide**: Cys–SH + HS–Cys → Cys–S–S–Cys (oxidation); DTT or β-mercaptoethanol reduces it back.\n• **Dityrosine**: radicals oxidize tyrosine phenol rings, which join by a C–C bond; reducing agents do not undo it.\n• Methionine is a thioether (C–S–C): no S–H.","steps":[{"text":"The links survive SDS, so they are covalent, and DTT erases them, so they are S–S bonds.","hl":"which breaks S–S bonds"},{"text":"An S–S bond needs a thiol on each partner: only cysteine has –SH.","fig":"cysteine-sc","calc":["2 Cys–SH → Cys–S–S–Cys + 2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻"]},{"text":"Methionine has a thioether sulfur with no H; threonine’s –OH and lysine’s –NH₂ make no S–S bonds.","strike":["Methionine","Threonine","Lysine"]},{"text":"Also: radical damage joins two tyrosines into dityrosine, a C–C link that DTT cannot break; the passage rules it out.","fig":"cpt-pe6-dityrosine"},{"text":"Answer: cysteine."}],"trap":"Picking methionine because it contains sulfur: without an S–H it cannot form a disulfide."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"covalent dimers and trimers","note":"bonds, not sticking"},{"t":"which breaks S–S bonds","note":"disulfides"},{"t":"no dityrosine","note":"radical link ruled out"}]},{"text":"**Two covalent cross-links.**","calc":["Cys–S–S–Cys: oxidation; DTT reverses","dityrosine (Tyr–Tyr C–C): radicals; not reversible"]},{"text":"**Sulfur alone is not enough.** A disulfide needs an –SH; methionine’s S is already bonded to two carbons."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a covalent protein cross-link that a reducing agent such as DTT breaks is a disulfide between two cysteine thiols; a radical-made dityrosine link between two tyrosine rings is not reversed."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-31a","s":"Protein Structure","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe6-cp:31#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:31"],"passage":"A lab compared four treatments of a soluble enzyme. After each one, the share of molecules unfolded was estimated from the shift in tryptophan fluorescence: heat at 70 °C, 92%; 3 M urea, 41%; ammonium sulfate, 3%; plain buffer at room temperature, 2%. The goal was to find the condition that best exposes the enzyme’s nonpolar core to solvent.","q":"Which treatment leaves the most nonpolar side chains in contact with water?","choices":["Heat, 70 °C","Urea at 3 M","Ammonium sulfate","Plain buffer"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Picks the least-unfolded sample: a folded protein keeps its nonpolar side chains buried in the core."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Urea does denature proteins, but at this dose fewer than half of the molecules unfolded."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"This salt stabilizes the fold (it is used for salting out); almost none of the enzyme unfolded."}],"rule":"The most exposed core belongs to the most unfolded sample: rank the data, whatever the denaturant’s name.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Scan for the largest % unfolded. Unfolding brings buried nonpolar side chains to the surface, so the top value wins."},"exp":"In a folded protein the **nonpolar side chains sit hidden** inside, away from water. Unfolding exposes them, so the condition that unfolds the **largest share** exposes the most: **heat at 70 °C (92%)**. 3 M urea unfolded only 41%, ammonium sulfate (a stabilizing salt) 3%, and plain buffer 2%.","how":{"ask":"Which treatment unfolded the largest share of the enzyme?","know":"• Folding buries nonpolar (hydrophobic) side chains in the core; denaturing exposes them to water.\n• Denaturants: heat, urea, guanidinium, detergents, extreme pH. Stabilizing salts (ammonium sulfate) do the opposite.\n• The amount of a denaturant matters: read the data, not just the name.","steps":[{"text":"Exposure of the core tracks unfolding, so rank the treatments by percent unfolded.","hl":"share of molecules unfolded","calc":["heat 92% > urea 41% > salt 3% > buffer 2%"]},{"text":"Buffer and the salt leave the fold intact, and the urea dose unfolded under half.","strike":["Plain buffer","Ammonium sulfate","Urea at 3 M"]},{"text":"Answer: heat at 70 °C."}],"trap":"Choosing urea because it is the textbook denaturant: here the data show heat unfolded far more of this enzyme."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"heat at 70 °C, 92%","note":"most unfolded"},{"t":"3 M urea, 41%","note":"partial"},{"t":"nonpolar core","note":"buried when folded"}]},{"text":"**Unfolded = core exposed.** The hydrophobic effect buries nonpolar side chains; denaturing undoes that."},{"text":"**Let the data decide.** Rank by % unfolded; the denaturant’s reputation does not matter."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** hydrophobic side chains are most exposed to water in whichever condition denatures the protein most, so pick the highest percent unfolded in the data."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-32a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:32#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:32"],"passage":"Iodine-131, given by mouth as sodium iodide, collects in the thyroid and is used to treat an overactive gland. Its nucleus emits a beta particle, an electron created inside the nucleus, and becomes xenon-131 in an excited state. The excited xenon nucleus then relaxes by emitting a gamma ray, which a scanner outside the body can detect.","q":"In the beta step, what happens to Z and to A?","choices":["Z rises by one; A same","Z falls by one; A same","Z same; A same","Z rises by one; A falls by one"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction flipped: an electron leaving means a neutron became a proton, so the nuclear charge goes up."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"That describes the gamma step that follows; the beta step changes the element from iodine to xenon."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An electron carries almost no mass and the nucleon count is unchanged: a neutron simply became a proton."}],"rule":"α: Z −2, A −4. β⁻: Z +1, A same. β⁺ or capture: Z −1, A same. γ: neither changes; the nucleus only sheds energy.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"β⁻: n → p + e⁻. One more proton, same total nucleons: Z up one, A unchanged (iodine 53 → xenon 54)."},"exp":"In **β⁻ decay** a neutron turns into a proton and an electron is ejected: **Z rises by one** (iodine, Z = 53, becomes xenon, Z = 54) and **A is unchanged** (131 nucleons before and after). The **gamma ray** that follows comes from the excited xenon nucleus dropping to its ground state: a photon with **no change in Z or A**.","how":{"ask":"What particle leaves in the beta step, and how does that change the proton and nucleon counts?","know":"• **α**: ⁴₂He leaves → Z −2, A −4.\n• **β⁻**: n → p + e⁻ → Z +1, A same.\n• **β⁺ / electron capture**: p → n → Z −1, A same.\n• **γ**: an excited nucleus emits a photon → Z and A unchanged.","steps":[{"text":"The beta particle is an electron made in the nucleus: a neutron became a proton.","hl":"an electron created inside the nucleus","calc":["n → p + e⁻"],"strike":["Z falls by one; A same"]},{"text":"One more proton, same number of nucleons.","calc":["¹³¹₅₃I → ¹³¹₅₄Xe* + e⁻","Z: 53 → 54; A: 131 → 131"],"strike":["Z rises by one; A falls by one"]},{"text":"No change in either belongs to the next step: the excited nucleus emits a gamma ray.","hl":"emitting a gamma ray","strike":["Z same; A same"]},{"text":"Answer: Z rises by one; A stays the same."}],"trap":"Mixing the two steps: the gamma ray changes nothing, but the beta emission before it makes a new element."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"beta particle","note":"β⁻: Z +1"},{"t":"excited state","note":"will emit γ"},{"t":"gamma ray","note":"no change in Z or A"}]},{"text":"**Four emissions to know.**","calc":["α: Z −2, A −4","β⁻: Z +1, A 0","β⁺: Z −1, A 0","γ: Z 0, A 0"]},{"text":"**An excited nucleus relaxes by gamma emission.** Like an excited atom giving off light, it only loses energy, so the element and the mass number stay the same."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** match the emission to its change: α lowers Z by 2 and A by 4, β⁻ raises Z by 1, β⁺ or capture lowers Z by 1, and a γ photon from an excited nucleus changes neither."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-33a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:33#1","pe6-cp:33#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:33"],"passage":"To follow shape changes in an enzyme, researchers attached a fluorescent dye to it and ran the product on SDS-PAGE beside untreated enzyme. The gel was imaged under UV light before being Coomassie-stained for protein. In the sample lane the stain showed two bands: one level with untreated protein, and a shifted band, one-third as dark, that fluoresces.","q":"Roughly what percent of the protein stayed unlabeled?","choices":["About 25%","About 75%","About 100%","About 33%"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"25% is the labeled share: the faint glowing band is the dye-bearing protein. Assigning it to unlabeled protein flips the answer."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"33% copies the stem's 'one-third' as is; that is a band-to-band ratio, not a share of all the protein."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The shifted band takes the protein stain AND fluoresces, so some protein carries dye; not all of it can be unlabeled."}],"rule":"Turn a darkness ratio into shares of the total (a : b means a ÷ (a + b)), and decide which band is which first.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Unshifted, non-glowing band = unlabeled; shifted, glowing = labeled. Darkness 3 : 1 → unlabeled share 3 of 4 = 75%."},"exp":"Coomassie shows all protein; fluorescence shows dye. The band level with untreated protein **stains but does not glow**: unlabeled. The shifted band that fluoresces is **labeled** protein. Darkness 3 : 1, so unlabeled = 3 ÷ (3 + 1) ≈ **75%**.","how":{"ask":"Which stained band is the unlabeled protein, and what share of the total stain does it hold?","know":"• **Protein stain** (Coomassie) shows every protein band; **fluorescence** shows only dye.\n• A lane of untreated protein marks where unlabeled protein runs.\n• Band darkness is proportional to amount, so a fraction = that band ÷ the sum of all protein bands.","steps":[{"text":"Assign each band by the two tests.","hl":"one level with untreated protein","calc":["level with untreated: stain ✓, glow ✗ → **unlabeled**","shifted band: stain ✓, glow ✓ → **labeled**"]},{"text":"The labeled band both stains and glows, so some of the protein carries dye.","hl":"that fluoresces","strike":["About 100%"]},{"text":"Turn darkness into a share of the total. 25% is the labeled share, and 33% is the 1 : 3 ratio, not a share of all the protein.","hl":"one-third as dark","calc":["unlabeled : labeled = 3 : 1","unlabeled = 3 ÷ (3 + 1) = **0.75 = 75%**","labeled = 1 ÷ 4 = 25%"],"strike":["About 25%","About 33%"]},{"text":"Answer: about 75% stayed unlabeled."}],"trap":"Reading 1 : 3 as one-third; a fraction divides by the total of both bands (4 parts), not by the other band."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Coomassie-stained","note":"shows all protein"},{"t":"level with untreated protein","note":"where unlabeled runs"},{"t":"fluoresces","note":"carries dye"},{"t":"one-third as dark","note":"1 part vs 3 parts"}]},{"text":"**Compare the lanes.** The untreated-protein lane is the ruler: a sample band at the same height is protein with no dye on it."},{"text":"**Two tests per band.**","calc":["stain ✓ glow ✓ → labeled protein","stain ✓ glow ✗ → unlabeled protein","stain ✗ glow ✓ → free dye"]},{"text":"**Turn darkness into a share.**","calc":["unlabeled 3 parts + labeled 1 part = 4 parts","unlabeled = 3/4 = 75%"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** find the unlabeled band by its match to the untreated lane (stains, no glow), then divide its darkness by the total of all protein bands."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-33b","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:33#3","pe6-cp:33#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:33"],"passage":"To label an enzyme for fluorescence studies, a dye was attached to its cysteines, and the product was run on SDS-PAGE beside untreated enzyme. Under UV light the sample lane showed a bright band at a shifted position and a second glowing band at the very bottom of the gel. After Coomassie staining, the shifted band was dark, a faint band appeared level with the untreated enzyme at one-quarter of the shifted band’s intensity, and the bottom band stayed blank.","q":"About what fraction of the enzyme was left unlabeled?","choices":["About 20%","About 80%","Almost none","About 33%"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Flipped: the dark shifted band is the labeled enzyme, so it is the larger share, not the unlabeled one."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Reads only the UV image: unlabeled enzyme cannot glow, so it shows up only in the stained lane."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Counts the glowing bottom band as protein; it never stains, so it is free dye, not enzyme."}],"rule":"Unlabeled share = the stained band at the unlabeled position ÷ all stained protein bands; a band that glows but never stains is free dye.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Stained protein only: labeled 4 parts, unlabeled 1 part. Unlabeled = 1 ÷ (4 + 1) = 1/5."},"exp":"Only the **Coomassie stain** shows all protein. It shows a dark **shifted (labeled)** band and a faint band **level with untreated enzyme** at one-quarter the intensity: labeled : unlabeled = 4 : 1, so the unlabeled share is 1 ÷ 5 ≈ **20%**. The bottom band glows under UV but **never stains**, so it is **free dye**, not protein, and is left out of the count.","how":{"ask":"Which bands are protein, and what share of the stained protein sits at the unlabeled position?","know":"• A protein stain (Coomassie) shows every protein band; UV shows only dye.\n• Labeled protein = stains and glows; unlabeled protein = stains only; free dye = glows only, runs near the bottom.\n• Fraction unlabeled = unlabeled band ÷ all protein bands, compared by intensity.","steps":[{"text":"Sort the bands: shifted = labeled (stains and glows); level with untreated = unlabeled (stains only); bottom = free dye (glows only).","hl":"the bottom band stayed blank","strike":["About 33%"]},{"text":"Unlabeled enzyme is invisible under UV, so the UV image alone says nothing about it.","strike":["Almost none"]},{"text":"Compare stained intensities.","hl":"one-quarter of the shifted band’s intensity","calc":["labeled : unlabeled = 4 : 1","unlabeled = 1 ÷ 5 = **20%**"],"strike":["About 80%"]},{"text":"Answer: about 20%."}],"trap":"Counting the bottom band as protein: free dye glows but never takes up a protein stain."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Under UV light","note":"shows dye only"},{"t":"Coomassie staining","note":"shows all protein"},{"t":"level with the untreated enzyme","note":"unlabeled protein"}]},{"text":"**Two readouts, two questions.** UV asks \"is dye here?\"; the stain asks \"is protein here?\". Labeled protein answers yes to both."},{"text":"**Compare lanes.** The untreated lane marks where unlabeled protein runs; a band there in the sample lane is unlabeled enzyme."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** estimate the unlabeled share from the protein-stained gel, as the band at the unlabeled position over all protein bands; a band that glows but does not stain is free dye, not protein."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-34a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:34#1","pe6-cp:34#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:34"],"passage":"Many fluorescent labels are attached through a reactive group that is attacked by nucleophilic side chains. An electrophilic dye is mixed with a protein in neutral buffer. The protein has no cysteine, and the four side chains shown are the most exposed on its surface.","q":"Of the side chains shown, which is the strongest nucleophile toward the dye?","choices":["Lys","Thr","Gln","Glu"],"choiceFigs":["lysine-sc","threonine-sc","glutamine-sc","glutamate-sc"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"An alcohol O–H is a weak nucleophile in water; that is why amine- and thiol-reactive dyes leave Ser and Thr alone."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An amide N's lone pair is delocalized into the C=O, so Gln (like Asn) barely reacts as a nucleophile."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A carboxylate spreads its charge over two O atoms by resonance, so Glu is a weak nucleophile."}],"rule":"In protein labeling the strong nucleophiles are a free amine or thiol lone pair; O–H, amides and carboxylates are weak.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"At pH 7 the strong side-chain nucleophiles are amine (Lys) and thiol (Cys). No Cys listed, so Lys; OH, amide and carboxylate are weak."},"exp":"Lysine's ε-amine (pKa ≈ 10.5) is mostly –NH₃⁺ at pH 7, but its small **neutral –NH₂ share** has a free lone pair and reacts fast. Thr's –OH, Gln's amide and Glu's resonance-stabilized carboxylate are **poor nucleophiles**. Cysteine's –SH/–S⁻ is the other strong nucleophile at pH 7.","how":{"ask":"Which side chain has a lone pair that is free (not delocalized, not tightly held) at pH 7?","know":"• Good nucleophiles at pH 7: **Lys –NH₂** (the unprotonated share) and **Cys –SH/–S⁻**.\n• Poor: alcohol O (Ser, Thr), amide N (Asn, Gln), carboxylate O (Asp, Glu).\n• Amide and carboxylate lone pairs are delocalized by resonance.","steps":[{"text":"The dye needs an electron-pair donor at pH 7.","hl":"electrophilic dye","fig":"lysine-sc"},{"text":"Rule out the weak donors: Thr holds its O lone pairs tightly, and Gln and Glu spread theirs by resonance.","calc":["Thr: R–OH, weak O nucleophile","Gln: R–C(=O)NH₂, N lone pair in resonance","Glu: R–COO⁻, charge spread over two O"],"strike":["Thr","Gln","Glu"]},{"text":"Lys: only a tiny share is neutral –NH₂ at pH 7, but that free amine reacts, and more deprotonates as it is used up.","calc":["share as –NH₂ = 1 ÷ (1 + 10^(10.5 − 7))","= 1 ÷ (1 + 3162) ≈ 0.03%"]},{"text":"Answer: Lys."}],"trap":"Picking Glu because it carries a full negative charge; charge alone doesn't make a good nucleophile when resonance spreads it over two oxygens."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"electrophilic dye","note":"needs a lone-pair donor"},{"t":"neutral buffer","note":"pH ≈ 7: check protonation"},{"t":"strongest nucleophile","note":"most available lone pair"}]},{"text":"**Two strong nucleophiles at pH 7:** lysine's –NH₂ (amine) and cysteine's –SH (thiol, partly S⁻).","fig":["lysine-sc","cysteine-sc"]},{"text":"**Also:** Asp, Glu, Ser, Thr, Asn and Gln are poor nucleophiles at pH 7: alcohol oxygens hold their lone pairs tightly, and amides and carboxylates delocalize theirs."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to find a labeling site, look for an available lone pair: the Lys amine and the Cys thiol react; O–H, amide and carboxylate side chains do not."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-34b","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:34#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:34"],"passage":"Two common reactive dyes label proteins in different ways: an NHS ester acylates unprotonated –NH₂ groups, while a maleimide alkylates –SH groups. A protein with no cysteine was mixed with an NHS-ester dye at pH 8.3, and after the reaction it was strongly fluorescent. A parallel sample treated with a maleimide dye showed no labeling at all.","q":"Which side chain carries the NHS-ester dye?","choices":["–(CH₂)₄NH₂","–CH₂OH","–CH₂COO⁻","–(CH₂)₂CONH₂"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A hydroxyl is a weak nucleophile in water and is not the group NHS esters are built to acylate."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A carboxylate is not an amine; NHS esters react with –NH₂, and this group has none."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An amide NH₂ has its lone pair delocalized into C=O, so it is not nucleophilic toward the dye."}],"rule":"Match the dye to its group: NHS esters and isothiocyanates → amines (Lys, N-terminus); maleimides and iodoacetamides → thiols (Cys).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"NHS ester = amine-reactive. The only side chain ending in a free –NH₂ (not an amide) is lysine’s."},"exp":"An **NHS ester** is an activated ester that acylates **free amines**, forming an amide; at pH 8.3 enough lysine ε-amino groups are unprotonated to react. The labeled side chain is **lysine, –(CH₂)₄NH₂**. Serine’s –OH and aspartate’s –COO⁻ are not amines, and glutamine’s –CONH₂ is an amide whose N lone pair is delocalized. The maleimide failed because a maleimide needs a cysteine –SH, which this protein lacks.","how":{"ask":"Which group does an NHS ester react with, and which drawn side chain has it?","know":"• **Amine-reactive dyes** (NHS esters, isothiocyanates) label Lys –NH₂ and the N-terminus.\n• **Thiol-reactive dyes** (maleimides, iodoacetamides) label Cys –SH.\n• Asp, Glu, Ser, Thr, Asn and Gln are poor nucleophiles at pH 7–8.","steps":[{"text":"The NHS ester targets unprotonated –NH₂ groups; the maleimide result confirms there is no –SH.","hl":"acylates unprotonated –NH₂ groups"},{"text":"Only one drawn group is a free amine: the lysine side chain.","fig":"lysine-sc","calc":["–(CH₂)₄NH₂ → Lys"]},{"text":"Serine’s –OH and aspartate’s carboxylate are not amines, and glutamine’s amide N is not nucleophilic.","strike":["–CH₂OH","–CH₂COO⁻","–(CH₂)₂CONH₂"]},{"text":"Answer: the lysine side chain."}],"trap":"Picking glutamine because it ends in NH₂: an amide NH₂ shares its lone pair with C=O and does not react."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"NHS ester","note":"amine-reactive"},{"t":"no cysteine","note":"no –SH"},{"t":"pH 8.3","note":"some Lys –NH₂ free"}]},{"text":"**Two dye families.**","calc":["NHS ester → –NH₂ (Lys)","maleimide → –SH (Cys)"]},{"text":"**Nucleophile check.** Free amines and thiols react; hydroxyls, carboxylates and amides do not, under labeling conditions."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** amine-reactive dyes such as NHS esters label lysine (and the N-terminus); thiol-reactive dyes such as maleimides label cysteine."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-35a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:35#1","pe6-cp:35#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:35"],"passage":"Hypertonic saline is used to reduce brain swelling after a head injury. Its 3% (w/v) strength corresponds to about 0.51 M NaCl, compared with 0.9% for normal saline. A hospital pharmacist needs to prepare 200 mL of the hypertonic solution (Na 23.0 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol).","q":"About how many grams of NaCl does the pharmacist need?","choices":["0.10 g","2.3 g","6.0 g","6000 g"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Uses 200 L instead of 0.200 L: convert mL to L before multiplying by the molarity."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"Stops at moles of NaCl; multiply by the molar mass to get grams."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Uses only sodium’s 23.0 g/mol; NaCl’s molar mass also includes chlorine’s 35.5 g/mol."}],"rule":"Grams = M × V(L) × molar mass. Convert mL to L first, and build the molar mass from every atom in the formula.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"mol = 0.51 × 0.200 ≈ 0.10; × 58.5 g/mol ≈ 6 g. Check: 3% w/v = 3 g per 100 mL → 6 g in 200 mL."},"exp":"Moles = M × V = 0.51 mol/L × 0.200 L ≈ **0.102 mol**. NaCl’s molar mass is 23.0 + 35.5 = **58.5 g/mol**, so mass ≈ 0.102 × 58.5 ≈ **6.0 g**. The % (w/v) gives the same answer: 3 g per 100 mL × 200 mL = 6 g. (Normal saline, 0.9%, is 0.15 M.)","how":{"ask":"How many moles are in 200 mL at 0.51 M, and what do they weigh?","know":"• **mol = M × V(L)**; **mass = mol × molar mass**.\n• Normal saline = 0.9% (w/v) NaCl ≈ 0.15 M; % (w/v) = grams per 100 mL.\n• NaCl = 23.0 + 35.5 = 58.5 g/mol.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the volume and find moles.","hl":"200 mL","calc":["200 mL = 0.200 L","0.51 × 0.200 ≈ 0.102 mol"],"strike":["6000 g"]},{"text":"Build the molar mass from both atoms, then convert to grams.","hl":"Na 23.0 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol","calc":["58.5 g/mol","0.102 × 58.5 ≈ **6.0 g**"],"strike":["0.10 g","2.3 g"]},{"text":"Check with % (w/v): 3 g per 100 mL → 6 g per 200 mL.","hl":"3% (w/v)"},{"text":"Answer: about 6.0 g."}],"trap":"Multiplying molarity by milliliters: M × mL gives millimoles, so convert or the answer is off by 1000."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"0.51 M","note":"mol per liter"},{"t":"200 mL","note":"0.200 L"},{"t":"Na 23.0 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol","note":"sum = 58.5"}]},{"text":"**Two steps.**","calc":["mol = M × V(L)","g = mol × molar mass"]},{"text":"**Saline anchors.** Normal saline is 0.9% (w/v), about 0.15 M NaCl, and 9 g per liter; % (w/v) means grams per 100 mL."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mass of solute = molarity × volume in liters × molar mass; for normal saline, 0.15 M NaCl is 0.9 g per 100 mL."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-36a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:36#1","pe6-cp:36#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:36"],"passage":"For a binding assay, a student pipettes 1.5 mL of an antibody fragment solution at 16 mg/mL into a tube. Mass spectrometry shows the fragment, made in bacteria, is 80 kDa. The rest of the stock is frozen for later experiments.","q":"How many moles of fragment were transferred?","choices":["0.3 nmol","3 μmol","0.3 mmol","0.3 μmol"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"That divides by 1000 one time too many; 24 mg is 0.024 g, one factor of 1000 only."},{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"That uses 8000 g/mol; 80 kDa is 80 000 g/mol, so the result is 10 times too big."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"That divides grams by 80 g/mol: 1 kDa = 1000 g/mol, so the molar mass is 80 000 g/mol, not 80."}],"rule":"Mass = volume × concentration; 1 kDa = 1000 g/mol, and mg ÷ (g/mol) gives mmol. Round every number to 1 digit first.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"mg ÷ kDa gives μmol (10⁻³ g ÷ 10³ g/mol = 10⁻⁶ mol). 16 × 1.5 = 24 mg; 24 ÷ 80 = 0.3 → 0.3 μmol."},"exp":"**Mass = concentration × volume**: 16 mg/mL × 1.5 mL = 24 mg = 0.024 g. **80 kDa = 80 000 g/mol.** Moles = 0.024 g ÷ 80 000 g/mol = 3 × 10⁻⁷ mol = **0.3 μmol**.","how":{"ask":"Get the mass in grams, convert kDa to g/mol, then divide.","know":"• **mass = (mg/mL) × mL**, then mg ÷ 1000 = g.\n• **1 kDa = 1000 Da**, and Da per molecule = g/mol, so **kDa × 1000 = g/mol**.\n• **mol = g ÷ (g/mol)**; 10⁻⁶ mol = 1 μmol.","steps":[{"text":"Mass delivered = concentration × volume.","hl":"1.5 mL of an antibody fragment solution at 16 mg/mL","calc":["16 mg/mL × 1.5 mL = 24 mg","24 mg ÷ 1000 = 0.024 g"]},{"text":"Convert the size to a molar mass.","hl":"80 kDa","calc":["80 kDa × 1000 = 80 000 g/mol"]},{"text":"Divide grams by g/mol.","calc":["0.024 ÷ 80 000 = 3 × 10⁻⁷ mol","3 × 10⁻⁷ mol = **0.3 μmol**"]},{"text":"0.3 mmol skips the kDa conversion, 3 μmol uses 8000 g/mol, and 0.3 nmol divides by 1000 one time too many.","strike":["0.3 mmol","3 μmol","0.3 nmol"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.3 μmol."}],"trap":"Dividing by 80 instead of 80 000: kDa must become g/mol before you divide."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"1.5 mL","note":"volume"},{"t":"16 mg/mL","note":"mass per volume"},{"t":"80 kDa","note":"molar mass, ×1000"}]},{"text":"**Mass from concentration.** A mg/mL concentration times a volume in mL gives mg directly.","calc":["16 × 1.5 = 24 mg = 2.4 × 10⁻² g"]},{"text":"**Moles from mass.** Divide by the molar mass in g/mol, after turning kDa into g/mol.","calc":["2.4 × 10⁻² g ÷ 8.0 × 10⁴ g/mol = 3.0 × 10⁻⁷ mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** writing every quantity in powers of ten keeps the zeros honest: 10⁻² ÷ 10⁴ = 10⁻⁶, the micro range, as expected for milligrams of a large protein."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mol of protein = (mg/mL × mL ÷ 1000) ÷ (kDa × 1000)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-37a","s":"Protein Structure","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:37#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:37"],"passage":"To follow a kinase inside cells, researchers tried attaching a fluorescent tag. An amine-reactive dye labels a single lysine of the kinase. Circular dichroism shows that the labeled kinase stays folded but loses all activity.","q":"Where is that lysine most likely?","choices":["In the substrate cleft","On a distant surface loop","Inside the nonpolar core","Anywhere on the protein"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A tag on a loop far from the catalytic pocket leaves activity intact, as most surface labels do."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Lysine is charged and faces water; a dye in buffer can't reach the buried core while the kinase stays folded."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Most surface labels leave activity untouched, so a total loss points to one special spot, not a random one."}],"rule":"One small label that kills activity but not folding must sit where the protein does its work; buried sites are out of reach.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Still folded yet fully dead: the tag must block function directly, so it sits in the substrate cleft. A distant loop wouldn't matter; the core is unreachable."},"exp":"A dye in water reaches exposed residues. A tag on an exposed patch **far from the catalytic pocket** leaves activity unchanged. A tag that **abolishes activity while the fold is intact** must sit where substrate binds, physically blocking it.","how":{"ask":"Activity is lost but the fold is intact: which spot must a bulky dye be blocking?","know":"• A water-soluble reagent labels **exposed** residues; buried ones are out of reach while the protein is folded.\n• Exposed sites far from the catalytic pocket: label tolerated, activity unchanged.\n• Label in or at the mouth of the catalytic pocket: substrate blocked, activity lost.","steps":[{"text":"Two clues: still folded, all activity gone.","hl":"stays folded but loses all activity"},{"text":"Folded means the core is closed to the dye, and a charged lysine sits at the surface anyway.","hl":"amine-reactive dye","strike":["Inside the nonpolar core"]},{"text":"A tag far from the catalytic pocket would leave activity intact, and most surface sites are like that.","strike":["On a distant surface loop","Anywhere on the protein"]},{"text":"Answer: in the substrate cleft, where the dye blocks substrate binding."}],"trap":"Assuming any label disturbs an enzyme; most surface labels are harmless, so a total loss of activity pinpoints the catalytic pocket."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"amine-reactive dye","note":"tags exposed Lys"},{"t":"stays folded","note":"core still closed"},{"t":"loses all activity","note":"catalytic pocket hit"}]},{"text":"**Where can a dye in water reach?** Only exposed residues; a folded protein keeps its core shut."},{"text":"**Activity lost + fold intact** → the dye sits where substrate binds."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a water-borne label reaches only exposed residues; unchanged activity puts it far from the catalytic pocket, and lost activity with an intact fold puts it in the pocket."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-38a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:38#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:38"],"passage":"PCR mixtures need Mg²⁺ because the DNA polymerase uses it at its active site. A technician draws 3.0 mL of a 3.5 mM magnesium chloride (MgCl₂) stock into a syringe. The salt dissociates completely in water.","q":"What amount of chloride ion, in moles, does the syringe hold?","choices":["10.5 μmol","21 μmol","21 mmol","21 nmol"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"mM × mL gives μmol, not mmol: (10⁻³ mol/L)(10⁻³ L) = 10⁻⁶ mol."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"That applies one 10⁻³ too many; only the mM and the mL each carry a factor of 10⁻³."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That is the MgCl₂ itself; each formula unit releases two Cl⁻, so the ×2 was skipped."}],"rule":"mM × mL = μmol; then multiply by the ions per formula unit, read from the formula’s subscript.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"mM × mL = μmol: 3.5 × 3.0 = 10.5 μmol MgCl₂. Two Cl⁻ per formula unit → 21 μmol."},"exp":"**mM × mL = μmol**: 3.5 mM × 3.0 mL = 10.5 μmol of MgCl₂. Each **MgCl₂ gives 2 Cl⁻**, so n(Cl⁻) = 2 × 10.5 = **21 μmol** (2.1 × 10⁻⁵ mol).","how":{"ask":"Multiply concentration by volume with matched prefixes, then count chlorides per formula unit.","know":"• **n = M × V.** Prefix pairs: **M × L = mol**, **M × mL = mmol**, **mM × mL = μmol**.\n• Ions per formula unit come from the subscripts: MgCl₂ → 1 Mg²⁺ + 2 Cl⁻.","steps":[{"text":"Multiply, keeping the prefixes.","hl":"3.0 mL of a 3.5 mM","calc":["3.5 mM × 3.0 mL = 10.5 μmol MgCl₂","check: 3.5 × 10⁻³ mol/L × 3.0 × 10⁻³ L = 1.05 × 10⁻⁵ mol"]},{"text":"Count chloride ions.","hl":"MgCl₂","calc":["2 × 10.5 μmol = **21 μmol Cl⁻**"]},{"text":"10.5 μmol is the salt, not the chloride; 21 mmol and 21 nmol misplace a factor of 1000.","strike":["10.5 μmol","21 mmol","21 nmol"]},{"text":"Answer: 21 μmol."}],"trap":"Writing mM × mL as mmol: two milli factors make micro."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"3.0 mL","note":"volume, 10⁻³ L"},{"t":"3.5 mM","note":"10⁻³ mol/L"},{"t":"chloride ion","note":"two per MgCl₂"}]},{"text":"**Milli × milli = micro.** 10⁻³ × 10⁻³ = 10⁻⁶, so the product of mM and mL comes out in μmol.","calc":["3.5 × 3.0 = 10.5 → 10.5 μmol"]},{"text":"**Ions per unit.** Dissolving one MgCl₂ gives one Mg²⁺ and two Cl⁻.","calc":["n(Cl⁻) = 2 × 10.5 μmol = 21 μmol"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** n = M × V with matched prefixes (mM × mL = μmol, M × mL = mmol), then multiply by the ions per formula unit."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-39a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:39#1","pe6-cp:39#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:39"],"passage":"Magnetite is the richest common iron ore. A geology student crushes a rock and finds that a 12.0 g rock sample is 60% magnetite, Fe₃O₄ (Fe 56 g/mol, O 16 g/mol), by mass. Quartz, which contains no iron, makes up the remainder.","q":"Approximately how much iron does the sample contain?","choices":["1.7 g","5.2 g","7.2 g","8.7 g"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That counts one Fe per formula unit; Fe₃O₄ holds three iron atoms, so the fraction is 3 × 56, not 56."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"7.2 g is the mass of magnetite in the rock; part of that oxide is oxygen, so the iron must weigh less."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That skips the 60%: it treats the whole 12.0 g rock as magnetite."}],"rule":"Chain the fractions: rock mass × % mineral × (Fe mass in formula ÷ formula mass). Round each fraction to 1 digit, then multiply.","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"Magnetite = 0.6 × 12 = 7.2 g. Fe fraction 168/232 ≈ 0.7, so iron ≈ 0.7 × 7.2 ≈ 5. Only 5.2 g is near (7.2 is all magnetite)."},"exp":"Magnetite in the rock: 0.60 × 12.0 g = 7.2 g. **Fe₃O₄ = 3(56) + 4(16) = 232 g/mol**, and iron's mass fraction is **168 ÷ 232 ≈ 0.72**. Iron = 0.72 × 7.2 g ≈ **5.2 g**.","how":{"ask":"Find the oxide's mass, then the iron fraction of the oxide, and multiply.","know":"• **Mass fraction of an element = (atoms of it × atomic mass) ÷ formula mass.**\n• Mass of the element = fraction × mass of the compound.\n• In a mixture, first find the compound's own mass: % × total.","steps":[{"text":"Mass of the compound in the mixture.","hl":"12.0 g rock sample is 60% magnetite","calc":["0.60 × 12.0 g = 7.2 g Fe₃O₄"]},{"text":"Formula mass and iron's share.","hl":"Fe₃O₄","calc":["3 × 56 = 168; 4 × 16 = 64","168 + 64 = 232 g/mol","168 ÷ 232 ≈ 0.72"]},{"text":"Multiply the fraction by the oxide's mass.","calc":["0.72 × 7.2 g ≈ **5.2 g Fe**"]},{"text":"7.2 g stops at the oxide, 8.7 g skips the 60%, and 1.7 g counts only one iron atom.","strike":["7.2 g","8.7 g","1.7 g"]},{"text":"Answer: 5.2 g."}],"trap":"Counting one Fe atom per formula unit: the subscript 3 multiplies the iron's mass."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"12.0 g","note":"whole rock"},{"t":"60%","note":"share that is oxide"},{"t":"Fe₃O₄","note":"three Fe per unit"}]},{"text":"**Peel the layers from outside in.** Rock → oxide (60%) → iron (its mass fraction in the oxide).","calc":["mass Fe = total × %oxide × (n·M_Fe ÷ M_oxide)"]},{"text":"**Mass fraction of the metal.** Count the metal atoms, multiply by the atomic mass, and divide by the formula mass.","calc":["(3 × 56) ÷ 232 = 168 ÷ 232 ≈ 0.72"]},{"text":"**Also:** multiply that fraction by the mass of the compound to get the element's mass: 0.72 × 7.2 g ≈ 5.2 g."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** element mass = (atoms × atomic mass ÷ formula mass) × compound mass, after taking the compound's % of any mixture."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-40a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:40#1","pe6-cp:40#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:40"],"passage":"Prototype zinc-ion batteries use a thin cathode coating of manganese oxide on carbon paper. Each electrode carries 8.0 mg of active coating, and lab tests show that the coating stores 250 mAh per gram. A milliamp-hour (mAh) is the charge delivered by a current of one milliamp flowing for one hour.","q":"What is the most charge one electrode can deliver?","choices":["0.56 C","2.0 C","7.2 C","7200 C"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Treats 8.0 mg as 8.0 g; convert milligrams to grams before multiplying by the capacity per gram."},{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Divides by the mAh-to-coulomb factor instead of multiplying: one mAh is more than one coulomb."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"That is the charge in mAh; one more step converts milliamp-hours to coulombs."}],"rule":"Charge = capacity per gram × mass in grams, then mAh → C using 1 mAh = (10⁻³ A)(3600 s).","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"0.0080 g × 250 mAh/g = 2.0 mAh. 1 mAh = 10⁻³ A × 3600 s = 3.6 C, so 2.0 × 3.6 = 7.2 C."},"exp":"Charge stored = **capacity × mass** = 250 mAh/g × 0.0080 g = **2.0 mAh**. One mAh is 10⁻³ A for 3600 s = **3.6 C**, so the electrode can deliver 2.0 × 3.6 = **7.2 C**.","how":{"ask":"How many mAh does the coating hold, and how many coulombs is one mAh?","know":"• Q = I × t: 1 A for 1 s = 1 C.\n• 1 mAh = 10⁻³ A × 3600 s = 3.6 C.\n• Specific capacity (mAh/g) × mass (g) = total capacity (mAh).","steps":[{"text":"Convert the mass and multiply by the capacity.","hl":"8.0 mg of active coating","calc":["8.0 mg = 0.0080 g","0.0080 g × 250 mAh/g = 2.0 mAh"],"strike":["7200 C"]},{"text":"Build the mAh-to-coulomb factor from its definition.","hl":"one milliamp flowing for one hour","calc":["1 mAh = 10⁻³ A × 3600 s = 3.6 C"]},{"text":"Multiply, do not divide, and do not stop at mAh.","calc":["2.0 mAh × 3.6 C/mAh = **7.2 C**"],"strike":["0.56 C","2.0 C"]},{"text":"Answer: 7.2 C."}],"trap":"Stopping at 2.0 because the units look finished: mAh is a unit of charge, but not coulombs."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"8.0 mg","note":"0.0080 g"},{"t":"250 mAh per gram","note":"specific capacity"},{"t":"one milliamp flowing for one hour","note":"define mAh"}]},{"text":"**Charge = current × time.**","calc":["1 mAh = 0.001 A × 3600 s = 3.6 C","1 Ah = 3600 C"]},{"text":"**Capacity × mass.** A rating per gram times the grams present gives the total."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** total charge = specific capacity × mass (in grams), then convert milliamp-hours to coulombs with 1 mAh = 3.6 C."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-41a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:41#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:41"],"passage":"Aluminum–air batteries pair an aluminum-metal anode with a porous carbon cathode that reduces oxygen from the air in a KOH electrolyte. At the cathode, O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻, for which E°red = +0.40 V. The complete cell delivers E°cell = 2.70 V, which makes it attractive for electric vehicles.","q":"Based on the passage, E° for the aluminum half-reaction, written as a reduction, is:","choices":["+2.70 V","+3.10 V","−2.30 V","−0.40 V"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Adds the two potentials; the cell potential must be subtracted from the cathode’s, not added."},{"c":0,"tag":"copied","why":"Copies the whole-cell potential as if it were one electrode’s; the cathode’s share must be taken out."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"Copies the cathode value with a minus sign; the cell potential has not been used at all."}],"rule":"E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode, so E°anode = E°cathode − E°cell; a cell voltage larger than the cathode’s means a negative anode potential.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"E°anode = E°cathode − E°cell = 0.40 − 2.70 = −2.30 V."},"exp":"With both half-reactions written as reductions, the **cell potential equals the cathode’s reduction potential minus the anode’s**. So the anode’s value is the cathode’s minus the cell’s: +0.40 V − 2.70 V = **−2.30 V**. The strongly negative value fits aluminum, an active metal that is easily oxidized.","how":{"ask":"Which formula links the cell potential to the two reduction potentials, and what does it give for the anode?","know":"• **E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode** (both as reduction potentials).\n• A spontaneous (galvanic) cell has E°cell > 0.\n• Active metals used as anodes (Li, Mg, Al, Zn) have negative reduction potentials.","steps":[{"text":"Write the cell relation and solve for the anode.","hl":"E°cell = 2.70 V","calc":["E°anode = E°cathode − E°cell"]},{"text":"Substitute the passage values.","hl":"+0.40 V","calc":["0.40 − 2.70 = **−2.30 V**"]},{"text":"+3.10 V adds instead of subtracting, +2.70 V is the whole cell’s value, and −0.40 V ignores the cell potential.","strike":["+2.70 V","+3.10 V","−0.40 V"]},{"text":"Answer: −2.30 V."}],"trap":"Using E°cell = E°cathode + E°anode, which treats the anode value as an oxidation potential; with reduction potentials, subtract."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"+0.40 V","note":"E°cathode (reduction)"},{"t":"2.70 V","note":"E°cell"},{"t":"written as a reduction","note":"E°anode as reduction"}]},{"text":"**One formula.**","calc":["E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode","→ E°anode = E°cathode − E°cell"]},{"text":"**Sense check.** A big cell voltage with a modest cathode needs a very negative anode: an active metal."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** with both half-cells as reduction potentials, E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode, so the anode’s potential is the cathode’s minus the cell’s."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-42a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:42#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:42"],"passage":"Aspartate side chains that hold Ca²⁺ at neutral pH release it in strong acid. To see why, a student considered free aspartate dissolved in a solution at pH 1.2. Its acid–base groups have these values, pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6.","q":"Which form of free aspartate, shown, dominates at pH 1.2?","choices":["Cation (net +1)","Zwitterion (net 0)","Anion (net −1)","Dianion (net −2)"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe6-asp-plus1","cpt-pe6-asp-net0","cpt-pe6-asp-minus1","cpt-pe6-asp-minus2"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Net 0 needs the α-COOH ionized, at pH above 1.9; at pH 1.2 it still holds its H⁺."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"−1 has both carboxyls ionized, the pH 7 form that grips Ca²⁺; pH 1.2 is below both carboxyl pKa values."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"−2 needs the amine deprotonated, at pH above 9.6; at pH 1.2 the amine is NH₃⁺."}],"rule":"Below a group's pKa it keeps its H⁺: compare the pH with each pKa, then add up the charges.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"pH below every pKa → every group keeps its proton. Carboxyls neutral, amine +1 → net +1, the cation."},"exp":"pH 1.2 is **below every pKa**, so every group keeps its H⁺: α-COOH (0), side-chain COOH (0), α-NH₃⁺ (+1) → **net +1**. With its side chain as neutral –COOH instead of –COO⁻, aspartate can no longer hold Ca²⁺, so the ion is released.","how":{"ask":"For each group, is pH 1.2 above or below its pKa? Then add up the charges.","know":"• **pH < pKa → protonated** (COOH neutral, NH₃⁺ +1); pH > pKa → deprotonated (COO⁻ −1, NH₂ neutral).\n• Metal ions are held by negative **–COO⁻**; protonating it to –COOH releases the metal.","steps":[{"text":"Compare 1.2 with each pKa.","hl":"pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6","calc":["α-COOH: 1.2 < 1.9 → COOH (0)","side-chain COOH: 1.2 < 3.7 → COOH (0)","α-NH₃⁺: 1.2 < 9.6 → NH₃⁺ (+1)","net = 0 + 0 + 1 = **+1**"]},{"text":"The matching drawing has two COOH groups and NH₃⁺.","fig":"cpt-pe6-asp-plus1"},{"text":"Ionized carboxyls need pH above their pKa values (1.9 and 3.7), and a neutral amine needs pH above 9.6.","strike":["Zwitterion (net 0)","Anion (net −1)","Dianion (net −2)"]},{"text":"Answer: the cation, net charge +1; the side chain is neutral –COOH and lets go of Ca²⁺."}],"trap":"Stopping after the side chain: check the α-COOH too. pH 1.2 is even below its pKa of 1.9, so the zwitterion is not the main form."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Ca²⁺","note":"held by –COO⁻"},{"t":"release it in strong acid","note":"–COO⁻ picks up H⁺"},{"t":"pH 1.2","note":"very acidic"},{"t":"pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6","note":"compare each"}]},{"text":"**Carboxylates bind metal ions.** At pH 7 the side chain is –COO⁻, and its negative oxygens grip Ca²⁺.","fig":"cpt-pe6-asp-minus1"},{"text":"**Lower the pH below the side chain's pKa** and –COO⁻ picks up H⁺; the neutral –COOH cannot hold the metal, so the ion is released."},{"text":"**Then check every group** against its own pKa and add the charges.","calc":["below pKa → H⁺ on","above pKa → H⁺ off"],"fig":"cpt-pe6-asp-plus1"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** metal-binding carboxylates release the metal once pH falls below their pKa; to find the dominant form, compare pH with each group's pKa and add the charges."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-43a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe6-cp:43#1","pe6-cp:43#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:43"],"passage":"In a common alkaline cell, a zinc-powder anode and a manganese dioxide cathode press against opposite faces of a thin sheet of porous paper. The paper is soaked in concentrated KOH solution, and metal terminals on the outer case connect the electrodes to an external circuit. If the paper tears, the cell heats up and soon goes flat, even with nothing connected.","q":"What is the main job of the paper sheet itself?","choices":["Separates the electrodes","Conducts electrons through the cell","Stores the cell’s charge","Reacts at the cathode"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Electrons take the outside wire; inside the cell, the soaked paper carries ions, not electrons."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The energy is stored chemically in the zinc and the MnO₂; the paper holds no reactant."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Paper is inert here; the reaction at the cathode is the reduction of MnO₂."}],"rule":"In a cell, the electrodes react, the electrolyte carries ions between them, the external wire carries electrons, and a separator keeps the electrodes from touching.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Tear it and the cell shorts out internally: the electrodes touch. So the sheet’s job is to keep them apart while its KOH lets ions pass."},"exp":"The porous sheet is the **separator**. It keeps the zinc and MnO₂ from touching, so electrons must take the **external circuit**, while the **KOH solution** soaked into it carries **OH⁻ and K⁺ ions** between the electrodes. If it tears, the electrodes touch, electrons flow straight across inside the cell (an internal short), and the cell heats and drains.","how":{"ask":"What goes wrong when the sheet tears, and which job does that reveal?","know":"• **Electrolyte**: carries ions between the electrodes (completes the internal circuit).\n• **External wire**: carries electrons from anode to cathode.\n• **Separator**: porous, electrically insulating; keeps electrodes apart but lets ions through.\n• Electrodes: where oxidation (anode) and reduction (cathode) happen.","steps":[{"text":"A torn sheet makes the cell heat and drain with nothing connected: the electrodes touch and short out.","hl":"If the paper tears"},{"text":"So the sheet keeps the electrodes apart; its soaked-in KOH carries ions between them.","hl":"soaked in concentrated KOH solution"},{"text":"Electrons go through the external circuit, the charge is stored in the zinc and MnO₂, and the cathode reaction is MnO₂’s.","strike":["Conducts electrons through the cell","Stores the cell’s charge","Reacts at the cathode"]},{"text":"Answer: it separates the electrodes."}],"trap":"Thinking electrons cross the inside of the cell: if they could, the cell would short out; they must go around through the wire."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"porous paper","note":"lets ions through"},{"t":"soaked in concentrated KOH solution","note":"the electrolyte"},{"t":"external circuit","note":"electron path"},{"t":"If the paper tears","note":"electrodes touch"}]},{"text":"**Four parts, four jobs.**","calc":["anode: oxidized","cathode: reduced","electrolyte: carries ions","wire: carries electrons"]},{"text":"**Why separate?** If the electrodes touched, electrons would flow directly between them, and no current would reach the outside circuit."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the solution between a cell’s electrodes is the electrolyte and carries ions, electrons travel only through the external wire, and a porous separator keeps the electrodes from touching."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-44a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:44#1","pe6-cp:44#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:44"],"q":"The four lipids shown were all extracted from human skin. Which one is NOT a structural lipid of cell membranes?","choices":["Sphingomyelin","Galactosyl ceramide","Cetyl palmitate","Phosphatidylinositol"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe6-sphingomyelin","cpt-pe6-galactocerebroside","cpt-pe6-cetyl-palmitate","cpt-pe6-phosphatidylinositol"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Sphingomyelin is a sphingolipid with a phosphocholine head: a classic membrane lipid, rich in the outer leaflet."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Galactosyl ceramide is sphingosine, one acyl chain and a sugar head: a glycosphingolipid of membranes."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Phosphatidylinositol is glycerol + two acyl chains + a phosphate–inositol head: a glycerophospholipid, so it sits in membranes."}],"rule":"Membrane lipids are amphipathic, with a polar head (phosphate or sugar) on a glycerol or sphingosine backbone; a wax ester has no head.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Name pattern: ‘-yl -ate’ (alcohol + fatty acid ester, e.g. cetyl palmitate) is a wax with no polar head, so no membrane role. Phospho- and sphingo- lipids are membrane lipids."},"exp":"Membrane lipids carry a polar head: **glycerophospholipids** (phosphatidylinositol), **sphingolipids** (sphingomyelin, galactosyl ceramide) and **sterols** (cholesterol). **Cetyl palmitate** is a wax: a fatty acid esterified to a long alcohol, with no polar head. Like storage fat, it is not built into membranes; it waterproofs the skin surface.","how":{"ask":"Which lipid lacks the polar head that every membrane lipid needs?","know":"• **Membrane lipids:** glycerophospholipids (PE, PS, PI), sphingolipids (sphingomyelin, cerebrosides, gangliosides) and sterols (cholesterol).\n• **Not membrane lipids:** neutral storage fats, free fatty acids (fuel), waxes (protective coatings).\n• Quick test: no polar head group → not a membrane lipid.","steps":[{"text":"Sort each lipid by its backbone and its head group.","hl":"NOT a structural lipid"},{"text":"Phosphatidylinositol: glycerol, two acyl chains and a phosphate–inositol head, so a glycerophospholipid.","strike":["Phosphatidylinositol"]},{"text":"Sphingomyelin and galactosyl ceramide: sphingosine, one acyl chain and a polar head (phosphocholine or galactose), so sphingolipids.","strike":["Sphingomyelin","Galactosyl ceramide"]},{"text":"Cetyl palmitate: palmitic acid esterified to a C16 alcohol. Two oily chains and no head: a wax.","calc":["C₁₅H₃₁–CO–O–C₁₆H₃₃: no phosphate, no sugar, no free OH"]},{"text":"Answer: cetyl palmitate."}],"trap":"Rejecting a sphingolipid because it has no glycerol; membranes are built from glycerol-based and sphingosine-based lipids alike."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"NOT a structural lipid","note":"find the odd one out"},{"t":"cell membranes","note":"bilayer lipids have polar heads"}]},{"text":"**Glycerophospholipids:** glycerol, two fatty acyl chains, a phosphate and a head group (ethanolamine, serine, inositol…). The bulk of every membrane."},{"text":"**Sphingolipids:** built on sphingosine instead of glycerol. Sphingomyelin carries phosphocholine; a cerebroside carries one sugar. Both are membrane lipids."},{"text":"**Sterols:** cholesterol's four fused rings and single OH tuck between phospholipids and tune fluidity, so it is a membrane lipid too."},{"text":"**Also:** test every choice for a polar head: a lipid made only of acyl chains on a backbone, with no phosphate, sugar or OH head (a neutral storage fat or a wax), is not a membrane lipid."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a membrane lipid has a polar head on an oily body: phospholipid, sphingolipid or sterol. Head-less lipids (fat, wax) store energy or waterproof."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-44b","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:44#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:44"],"q":"Which lipid shown, mixed alone with water, forms micelles rather than a bilayer?","choices":["SDS","Trimyristin","Phosphatidylserine","Ergosterol"],"choiceFigs":["sodium-dodecyl-sulfate","cpt-pe6-trimyristin","cpt-pe6-phosphatidylserine","cpt-pe6-ergosterol"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Trimyristin's three acyl chains carry no polar head, so in water it just separates as oil droplets."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Phosphatidylserine has two tails under one head: a cylinder that packs into flat bilayers, not micelles."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Ergosterol is fused rings with one small OH: too little head to assemble on its own."}],"rule":"Shape decides: one tail under a big head (a cone) → micelle; two tails (a cylinder) → bilayer; no head → oil.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Shape rule: one tail + big head = cone → micelle; two tails = cylinder → bilayer. SDS is the single-tail detergent."},"exp":"Assembly follows shape. **SDS** (sodium dodecyl sulfate) has **one tail under a big charged sulfate head**: a **cone**, and cones pack into spheres, so it forms **micelles**. Phosphatidylserine (two tails + head) is a cylinder and forms bilayers; trimyristin has no head and separates as droplets; ergosterol's tiny OH cannot organize it alone.","how":{"ask":"Which molecule has one tail under a big polar head, the cone shape that packs into a sphere?","know":"• **Shape decides the assembly:** one tail + big head = cone → **micelle**; two tails + head = cylinder → **bilayer**; no head → oil droplet.\n• Detergents and fatty acids form micelles; phospholipids and sphingolipids form bilayers.\n• Sterols and triacylglycerols cannot assemble alone.","steps":[{"text":"For each molecule, look for a polar head and count the oily tails.","hl":"forms micelles rather than a bilayer"},{"text":"Phosphatidylserine: two acyl tails under a charged head, a cylinder, so it forms a bilayer, not micelles.","calc":["head width ≈ 2 tails → flat sheet → bilayer"],"strike":["Phosphatidylserine"]},{"text":"Trimyristin: three tails, no head, so an oil droplet. Ergosterol: fused rings with one small OH, too little head to assemble alone.","strike":["Trimyristin","Ergosterol"]},{"text":"SDS: one C12 tail on a big sulfate head, a cone.","calc":["wide head + 1 tail → cone → packs into a sphere: micelle"]},{"text":"Answer: SDS (sodium dodecyl sulfate)."}],"trap":"Choosing phosphatidylserine because it is the most familiar membrane lipid; its two tails make a cylinder, which builds bilayers, not micelles."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"micelles","note":"one-tailed cones"},{"t":"rather than a bilayer","note":"two tails would give a sheet"},{"t":"alone with water","note":"it must work alone"}]},{"text":"**Amphipathic first.** A lipid needs a water-loving head and oily tails to organize in water at all; trimyristin has no head."},{"text":"**Then count tails.** One tail (fatty acid, SDS) = cone → micelle. Two-tailed glycerophospholipids such as phosphatidylserine (and sphingolipids) are cylinders → bilayer."},{"text":"**Sterols need partners.** Ergosterol and cholesterol slip between phospholipids but cannot assemble on their own."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count tails under a polar head: one tail (cone) → micelle; two tails (cylinder) → bilayer; no head → oil."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-45a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:45#4","pe6-cp:45#1","pe6-cp:45#2","pe6-cp:45#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:45"],"q":"The deoxynucleotide dCTP is shown. How many carbon atoms does one molecule of dCTP contain?","fig":"cpt-pe6-dctp","choices":["8","9","11","12"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Deoxyribose keeps all 5 carbons; only the 2′ OH is gone, so no carbon is lost from the sugar."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Cytosine's ring has 6 atoms, but 2 of them are N; counting every ring atom as carbon gives 5 + 6 = 11."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Phosphates hold P and O but no carbon; adding one C per phosphate is the trap."}],"rule":"Count by part: sugar (deoxyribose, 5 C) + only the carbons of the base ring (not N) + phosphates (no C).","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Count by part: sugar 5 C + cytosine 4 C + phosphates 0 C = 9. Deoxy removes an O, never a carbon."},"exp":"Count by parts: **deoxyribose 5 C** (deoxy = one O missing, not a C) + **cytosine 4 C** + **three phosphates 0 C** = **9 C**.","how":{"ask":"Add the carbons of the sugar, the base and the phosphates.","know":"• **Sugar:** ribose and deoxyribose are both 5-carbon pentoses (C1′–C5′); 'deoxy' means one O fewer at C2′.\n• **Bases:** adenine, guanine and thymine have 5 C each; cytosine and uracil have 4 C.\n• **Phosphate:** PO₄ has no carbon, so one, two or three of them add 0 C.","steps":[{"text":"Split dCTP into its three parts: sugar, base, phosphates.","hl":"dCTP is shown"},{"text":"Sugar: deoxyribose keeps 5 carbons; only the 2′ OH is gone.","calc":["deoxyribose: 5 C"],"strike":["8"]},{"text":"Base: cytosine's ring holds 4 C and 2 N, with an NH₂ and a C=O attached.","calc":["cytosine: 4 C, 3 N"],"strike":["11"]},{"text":"Phosphates: three PO₄ groups, no carbon.","calc":["3 × 0 = 0 C","5 + 4 + 0 = 9 C"],"strike":["12"]},{"text":"Answer: 9 carbon atoms."}],"trap":"Counting deoxyribose as 4 carbons because 'deoxy' sounds like something was removed; it lost an oxygen, not a carbon."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"deoxynucleotide","note":"sugar + base + phosphate(s)"},{"t":"carbon atoms","note":"count C only"}]},{"text":"**Also: the sugar.** Deoxyribose has 5 C, the same as ribose; deoxy only means no O on C2′.","calc":["deoxyribose C₅H₁₀O₄: 5 C"]},{"text":"**Also: the bases.** Adenine and guanine each have 5 C (a six-ring fused to a five-ring that share 2 C); thymine has 5 C (4 ring C + a methyl); cytosine has 4 C."},{"text":"**Also: the phosphates** have no carbon, so dCMP, dCDP and dCTP all have the same 9 C."},{"text":"**Also: purines vs pyrimidines.** Adenine and guanine bring 5 C, one more than cytosine, so a purine nucleotide has one carbon more than the matching cytosine nucleotide."},{"text":"**Also:** count N the same way, by parts: sugar and phosphate have none, so every N sits in the base; dCTP has cytosine's 3 N."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** atoms in a nucleotide = sugar + base + phosphate; sugar 5 C, adenine, guanine and thymine 5 C, cytosine and uracil 4 C, phosphate 0 C."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-46a","s":"Fluids","disc":"biology","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:46#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:46"],"q":"The walls of the atria are much thinner than the walls of the ventricles. Which feature of the atria best accounts for this difference?","choices":["They work against low pressure","They move a bigger stroke volume","They receive venous return","They hold oxygen-poor blood"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Atria pass on less blood per beat than the ventricles eject, and a larger volume would not explain thinner walls."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"True, but receiving blood takes no force; wall thickness tracks the pressure a chamber must generate."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Only the right atrium does; the left atrium holds oxygen-rich blood and is just as thin."}],"rule":"Heart wall thickness tracks the pressure a chamber must generate: left ventricle (systemic) > right ventricle (lungs) > atria.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Muscle mass follows workload = pressure. Atria only top up the ventricles next door, so they need little force and little muscle."},"exp":"Muscle builds to match the **pressure a chamber must generate**. The atria only push blood a short way into the relaxed ventricles below them, at a few mmHg, so their walls are thin. The **left ventricle** must drive blood through the whole **systemic circulation** (about 120 mmHg) and is thickest; the **right ventricle** pumps only to the lungs (about 25 mmHg) and is in between.","how":{"ask":"What sets how much muscle a heart chamber needs?","know":"• Wall thickness ∝ the pressure a chamber must generate (Laplace: wall stress rises with pressure).\n• LV → systemic circuit, high resistance, ~120 mmHg; RV → pulmonary circuit, ~25 mmHg; atria → ventricles, a few mmHg.\n• Both sides pump the same volume per beat.","steps":[{"text":"Wall thickness follows pressure, not volume or blood type.","hl":"much thinner"},{"text":"Atria fill the adjacent ventricles at low pressure; no high-resistance circuit lies ahead of them.","calc":["atria: a few mmHg","RV: ~25 mmHg","LV: ~120 mmHg"]},{"text":"Stroke volume is not larger in the atria, receiving blood takes no force, and the left atrium carries oxygen-rich blood.","strike":["They move a bigger stroke volume","They receive venous return","They hold oxygen-poor blood"]},{"text":"Also: the same rule explains why the left ventricle has a thicker wall than the right: it must overcome systemic resistance, while the right pumps only to the lungs."},{"text":"Answer: they work against low pressure."}],"trap":"Linking wall thickness to blood volume: both ventricles move the same volume each beat, yet their walls differ because their pressures differ."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"atria","note":"fill the ventricles"},{"t":"much thinner","note":"less force needed"},{"t":"ventricles","note":"pump into circuits"}]},{"text":"**Pressure sets muscle.**","calc":["LV: systemic, highest","RV: pulmonary, lower","atria: lowest"]},{"text":"**Same volume, different pressure.** Left and right ventricles eject equal volumes; only the resistance ahead of them differs."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a heart chamber’s wall is as thick as the pressure it must generate: the left ventricle, which must drive blood around the whole body, is thickest; the atria, which only fill the ventricles, are thinnest."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-47a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:47#1","pe6-cp:47#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:47"],"q":"A beam of ground-state nitrogen atoms (1s² 2s² 2p³) passes through a nonuniform magnetic field. What is each atom's total electron spin, and how does it behave?","choices":["7/2; paramagnetic","½; paramagnetic","3/2; diamagnetic","3/2; paramagnetic"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"7/2 needs all seven spins parallel, but Pauli forces the two electrons in each of 1s and 2s to pair with opposite spins."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"½ pairs two of the 2p electrons; Hund's rule puts one electron in each 2p orbital before any pairing."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Diamagnetic means every electron is paired; any unpaired electron makes an atom paramagnetic."}],"rule":"Count only unpaired electrons, ½ spin each; Hund's rule fills p orbitals one electron each first. Any unpaired electron → paramagnetic.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Paired electrons cancel; Hund's rule leaves N's three 2p electrons unpaired: 3 × ½ = 3/2. Any unpaired electron → paramagnetic."},"exp":"By the **Pauli principle** the 1s and 2s pairs have **opposite spins** and cancel. **Hund's rule** spreads the three 2p electrons over the three 2p orbitals, all spin-up, so the total spin is **3 × ½ = 3/2**. Unpaired electrons make nitrogen **paramagnetic**: drawn toward the stronger field.","how":{"ask":"Pair the electrons orbital by orbital, add the leftover spins, then classify the magnetism.","know":"• **Pauli exclusion**: two electrons in one orbital must have opposite spins (+½ and −½), so a full orbital contributes **0**.\n• **Hund's rule**: in a partly filled subshell, electrons occupy separate orbitals with parallel spins first.\n• **Unpaired electrons → paramagnetic** (attracted); **all paired → diamagnetic** (weakly repelled).","steps":[{"text":"Read the configuration.","hl":"1s² 2s² 2p³","calc":["1s: 2 electrons, 2s: 2 electrons","2p: 3 electrons in 3 orbitals"]},{"text":"Pair the full orbitals, then add the 2p spins.","calc":["1s: +½ − ½ = 0; 2s: +½ − ½ = 0","2p: +½ +½ +½ = 3/2","total S = **3/2**"]},{"text":"Unpaired electrons mean the atom is paramagnetic.","strike":["3/2; diamagnetic"]},{"text":"7/2 ignores Pauli pairing; ½ pairs two 2p electrons against Hund's rule.","strike":["7/2; paramagnetic","½; paramagnetic"]},{"text":"Answer: 3/2; paramagnetic."}],"trap":"Adding ½ for every electron: paired electrons in the same orbital always cancel."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"ground-state nitrogen atoms","note":"lowest-energy filling"},{"t":"1s² 2s² 2p³","note":"two full orbitals, a half-full p set"},{"t":"nonuniform magnetic field","note":"tests para- vs diamagnetism"}]},{"text":"**Pauli in one line.** No two electrons in an atom share all four quantum numbers, so two electrons in the same orbital must have opposite spins.","calc":["full orbital: +½ + (−½) = 0"]},{"text":"**Also:** an atom whose subshells are all filled, such as Be (1s² 2s²) or Ne, has total spin 0, so it is diamagnetic and is weakly pushed out of a strong field."},{"text":"**Also:** any unpaired electron (H, Li, N) leaves net spin, so the atom is paramagnetic and is drawn into the field."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** paired electrons cancel (Pauli); count only unpaired ones for the spin, and any unpaired electron means paramagnetic."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-48a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:48#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:48"],"passage":"Many sugar-binding proteins hold a pyranose ring by stacking it on a flat aromatic side chain. In one binding pocket, a tyrosine ring stacks on the sugar's face, and its O–H also H-bonds to a ring oxygen of the sugar. That extra hydrogen bond slows the release of the product. Engineers want to keep the stacking but remove every hydrogen-bond donor at this position.","q":"Which side chain best meets the engineers' goal?","choices":["Phe","Ser","Leu","His"],"choiceFigs":["tyrosine-sc","asparagine-sc","valine-sc","proline-sc"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Serine has no ring to stack on the sugar, and its O–H still donates a hydrogen bond."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Leucine's branched alkyl side chain has no flat aromatic ring, so the stacking is lost."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Histidine's ring can stack, but its ring N–H still donates a hydrogen bond."}],"rule":"Keep stacking, drop donation: pick a flat aromatic ring that carries no N–H or O–H.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Two filters: flat aromatic ring? (Phe, His pass) Any N–H or O–H? (His fails). The ring with no donor wins."},"exp":"The goal keeps one feature and drops another: a **flat aromatic ring** for stacking, but **no hydrogen-bond donor**. **Phenylalanine's benzyl ring** stacks and has no N–H or O–H. Serine has no ring and still donates, leucine has no flat ring, and histidine's ring N–H still donates.","how":{"ask":"Which side chain has a flat aromatic ring but no H on N or O?","know":"• **Stacking** needs a flat aromatic ring (Phe, Tyr, Trp, His).\n• **H-bond donors** have H on N or O (Ser, Thr, Tyr, Trp, His, Asn, Gln, Lys, Arg).\n• Removing one contact while keeping another means checking both features for every choice.","steps":[{"text":"List the goal: keep the stacking ring, remove every donor.","hl":"keep the stacking but remove every hydrogen-bond donor"},{"text":"Serine has no ring; leucine's side chain is alkyl, not a flat aromatic ring.","strike":["Ser","Leu"]},{"text":"Histidine stacks, but its ring N–H is a donor.","strike":["His"]},{"text":"Phenylalanine: a flat ring with only C–H bonds.","calc":["ring ✓ + no donor ✓ → stacking kept, H-bond gone"]},{"text":"Answer: the aromatic ring with no donor."}],"trap":"Picking histidine because it is aromatic; its ring N–H still donates a hydrogen bond."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"stacks on the sugar's face","note":"needs a flat aromatic ring"},{"t":"H-bonds to a ring oxygen","note":"the contact to remove"},{"t":"remove every hydrogen-bond donor","note":"no N–H or O–H"}]},{"text":"**List the jobs.** Keep: a flat aromatic ring. Remove: any H on N or O."},{"text":"**Score each candidate** against both filters; only one passes both.","calc":["ring: Phe ✓, His ✓, Ser ✗, Leu ✗","no donor: Phe ✓, His ✗"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to change one contact and keep another, test every choice against both features: ring for stacking, H on N or O for donating."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-49a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:49#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:49"],"passage":"Enzymes added to laundry detergent must stay dissolved in wash water. To help one bind greasy stains, engineers made a variant with most of its surface serines and threonines replaced by valines and leucines. Its active site and its buried core were left unchanged.","q":"In water, the variant would most likely:","choices":["clump together in solution.","form extra disulfide bonds.","lose its catalytic activity.","bind more water molecules."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Val and Leu cannot H-bond, so the variant holds less water, not more."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Disulfides need two cysteine –SH groups; valine and leucine contain no sulfur."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Residues scattered over the surface are not the active site; catalysis comes from a few positioned groups in a pocket."}],"rule":"Swapping polar surface residues for nonpolar ones makes the surface hydrophobic; molecules then hide those patches against each other.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Polar OH on the surface → hydrophobic side chains on the surface: oily patches stick together, so the protein aggregates. Val/Leu add no S, so no disulfides."},"exp":"Surface Ser and Thr **–OH groups hydrogen-bond with water**, building the hydration shell around the protein. Swapping them for **hydrophobic** Val and Leu removes those H-bonds and exposes oily patches, which stick to one another: the variant **aggregates**.","how":{"ask":"What do surface –OH groups do with water, and what happens when oily side chains replace them?","know":"• Ser and Thr carry polar, uncharged –OH groups at pH 7: they donate and accept H-bonds with water.\n• Those H-bonds build a **hydration (solvation) shell** around the protein.\n• Exposed hydrophobic side chains (Val, Leu, Ile, Phe) pull together to avoid water → aggregation.","steps":[{"text":"Note the swap: polar –OH side chains out, oily alkyl side chains in, all on the surface.","hl":"replaced by valines and leucines"},{"text":"No sulfur enters, so no new S–S bridges; and surface residues are not the catalytic pocket.","strike":["form extra disulfide bonds.","lose its catalytic activity."]},{"text":"Val and Leu cannot H-bond, so the variant holds less water, not more.","strike":["bind more water molecules."]},{"text":"Oily patches on neighboring molecules stick together (the hydrophobic effect).","calc":["fewer –OH···H₂O bonds → thinner water shell → molecules clump"]},{"text":"Answer: it clumps together in solution."}],"trap":"Assuming any big surface change must hit catalysis; the active site is a small pocket, while surface polarity governs how the protein mixes with water."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"surface serines and threonines","note":"polar –OH groups"},{"t":"valines and leucines","note":"oily, no H-bonding"}]},{"text":"**What the –OH does.** Each Ser or Thr –OH hydrogen-bonds with water molecules, adding to the protein's hydration shell."},{"text":"**Charge check.** Ser and Thr are uncharged at pH 7 (their –OH pKa is about 16), so the swap changes polarity, not charge."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** polar surface groups (–OH, amides, charges) hold the water shell that keeps proteins dispersed; exposed hydrophobic groups make them aggregate."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-50a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:50#2","pe6-cp:50#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:50"],"passage":"A white solid sold as a toilet-bowl cleaner dissolves completely in water, giving Na⁺ ions and HSO₄⁻ ions in equal numbers. Dissolved at 0.20 M, it gives a pH of about 1.5, far more acidic than table salt in water. A much weaker version of the same effect makes ammonium salt solutions slightly acidic.","q":"Which option names the solid correctly and identifies the ion that acidifies the water?","choices":["Sodium hydrogen sulfate; HSO₄⁻ donates H⁺","Sodium hydrogen sulfate; Na⁺ donates H⁺","Sodium sulfate; HSO₄⁻ donates H⁺","Sodium hydrogen sulfide; HSO₄⁻ donates H⁺"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Na⁺ comes from a strong base; it neither gives nor takes protons, so it leaves the pH alone."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Sulfate is SO₄²⁻; the ion here still carries an H, and the name must show it."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The -ide ending means sulfur with no oxygen (HS⁻); this ion contains four O atoms."}],"rule":"Name the ions first (-ate has oxygen, -ide has none, \"hydrogen\" marks a leftover H), then ask which ion can give H⁺ to water; Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻ and NO₃⁻ do nothing.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"HSO₄⁻ = hydrogen sulfate, an acid (it still has an H to give). Na⁺ is a spectator, so the anion is the proton donor."},"exp":"HSO₄⁻ is the **hydrogen sulfate** ion (sulfate, SO₄²⁻, with one H left on it), so the salt is **sodium hydrogen sulfate**. That anion still has an acidic proton, **HSO₄⁻ + H₂O ⇌ SO₄²⁻ + H₃O⁺**, which makes the solution strongly acidic. Na⁺ is a spectator. The same logic explains ammonium chloride: there the acidic ion is the cation, **NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺**, while Cl⁻ is a spectator.","how":{"ask":"What is the correct name of HSO₄⁻, and which of the two ions can donate a proton to water?","know":"• Naming: -ate = oxyanion (SO₄²⁻ sulfate), -ide = no oxygen (S²⁻ sulfide), \"hydrogen\" = an H still attached (HSO₄⁻ hydrogen sulfate); NH₄⁺ = ammonium.\n• Ions of strong acids and bases (Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻, NO₃⁻) do not change pH.\n• Acidic ions: NH₄⁺ (weak acid), HSO₄⁻ (fairly strong acid).","steps":[{"text":"Name the anion: sulfate with one H attached is hydrogen sulfate, not sulfate and not sulfide.","hl":"HSO₄⁻","strike":["Sodium sulfate; HSO₄⁻ donates H⁺","Sodium hydrogen sulfide; HSO₄⁻ donates H⁺"]},{"text":"Find the proton donor: HSO₄⁻ can give its H to water; Na⁺ cannot.","calc":["HSO₄⁻ + H₂O ⇌ SO₄²⁻ + H₃O⁺"],"strike":["Sodium hydrogen sulfate; Na⁺ donates H⁺"]},{"text":"Also: in ammonium chloride the roles swap: NH₄⁺, a weak acid, gives the proton, and Cl⁻ is the spectator, so its solution is only slightly acidic.","calc":["NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺"]},{"text":"Answer: sodium hydrogen sulfate, with HSO₄⁻ as the proton donor."}],"trap":"Assuming a metal cation makes a solution acidic: Na⁺ is a spectator; look for an ion that still carries a removable H."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Na⁺ ions and HSO₄⁻ ions","note":"name each ion"},{"t":"pH of about 1.5","note":"acidic"},{"t":"ammonium salt","note":"NH₄⁺ is a weak acid"}]},{"text":"**Name by the ion.**","calc":["NH₄⁺ ammonium","SO₄²⁻ sulfate","HSO₄⁻ hydrogen sulfate","S²⁻ sulfide"]},{"text":"**Who gives the proton?** Only an ion that still holds an acidic H (NH₄⁺, HSO₄⁻); ions of strong acids and bases are spectators."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to explain a salt’s pH, name each ion and ask which can donate or accept a proton; NH₄⁺ (as in ammonium chloride) and HSO₄⁻ are acids, while Na⁺ and Cl⁻ are spectators."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-51a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:51#1","pe6-cp:51#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:51"],"passage":"Fluorine NMR is used to watch fluorinated drugs bind their targets, because the body contains almost no natural fluorine. In one study, a protein–drug complex was placed in a spectrometer whose magnet makes ¹H nuclei resonate at 400 MHz. Absorbing one radio-frequency photon flips one nuclear spin. Planck’s constant is h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s.","q":"Each ¹H spin flip absorbs a photon with what energy?","choices":["2.6 × 10⁻²⁵ J","2.6 × 10⁻³¹ J","2.6 × 10⁻²⁸ J","1.7 × 10⁻⁴² J"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Uses 400 Hz instead of 400 × 10⁶ Hz; convert MHz to hertz before multiplying."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Converts MHz with 10³ instead of 10⁶: mega means a million."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Divides h by f; a photon’s energy is h times f."}],"rule":"E = hf with f in hertz (MHz × 10⁶). Radio-frequency NMR photons carry roughly a millionth of a visible photon’s energy.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"400 MHz = 4 × 10⁸ Hz. 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ × 4 × 10⁸ ≈ 26 × 10⁻²⁶ = 2.6 × 10⁻²⁵ J."},"exp":"A photon’s energy is **E = hf**, with f in hertz: 400 MHz = **4.0 × 10⁸ Hz**. Multiplying Planck’s constant by 400 million cycles per second gives **about 2.6 × 10⁻²⁵ J**. That tiny energy is typical of radio waves; visible photons carry about 10⁻¹⁹ J.","how":{"ask":"What is the frequency in hertz, and what is h times that frequency?","know":"• **E = hf** (h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s); f must be in Hz.\n• Prefixes: k = 10³, M = 10⁶, G = 10⁹.\n• Scale check: radio (NMR) photons ~10⁻²⁵ J; visible ~10⁻¹⁹ J.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the frequency.","hl":"400 MHz","calc":["400 MHz = 400 × 10⁶ Hz = 4.0 × 10⁸ Hz"],"strike":["2.6 × 10⁻³¹ J","2.6 × 10⁻²⁸ J"]},{"text":"Multiply by Planck’s constant.","hl":"h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s","calc":["6.6 × 4.0 = 26.4","10⁻³⁴ × 10⁸ = 10⁻²⁶","26.4 × 10⁻²⁶ ≈ **2.6 × 10⁻²⁵ J**"],"strike":["1.7 × 10⁻⁴² J"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.6 × 10⁻²⁵ J."}],"trap":"Dropping the 10⁶ in MHz: the answer then lands at about 10⁻³¹ J, absurdly small even for radio waves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"400 MHz","note":"f = 4.0 × 10⁸ Hz"},{"t":"h = 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s","note":"Planck’s constant"},{"t":"one radio-frequency photon","note":"E = hf"}]},{"text":"**One equation.**","calc":["E = hf","f in Hz, h in J·s → E in J"]},{"text":"**Order-of-magnitude check.** Radio photons are about a million times weaker than visible ones, so an NMR answer near 10⁻²⁵ J is right."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** photon energy E = hf with the frequency converted to hertz; an NMR (radio) photon carries roughly 10⁻²⁵ J."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-52a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:52#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:52"],"passage":"Carbon-13 NMR detects the rare heavy isotope of carbon in organic molecules. Each kind of nucleus has a gyromagnetic ratio γ, defined as its angular resonance frequency (two pi times the resonance frequency f) divided by the magnetic field B. In a spectrometer with an 11.7 T magnet, ¹³C nuclei resonate at 125 MHz.","q":"Using the passage's data, what is γ for ¹³C?","choices":["6.7 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)","1.1 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)","9.2 × 10⁹ rad/(s·T)","6.7 × 10¹ rad/(s·T)"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Leaves the frequency in MHz; convert to hertz (× 10⁶) before dividing by the field."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Multiplies by B instead of dividing; γ is angular frequency per tesla."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Divides f by B but leaves out the factor of two pi that turns f into angular frequency."}],"rule":"Plug into the passage’s formula exactly as defined: convert MHz to Hz, keep every constant (2π), and divide by the field, not multiply.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"γ = 2πf/B = 6.28 × 1.25 × 10⁸ ÷ 11.7 ≈ 7.9 × 10⁸ ÷ 11.7 ≈ 6.7 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)."},"exp":"From the passage, **γ = 2πf / B**. With f = 125 MHz = **1.25 × 10⁸ Hz** and B = 11.7 T: 2π × 1.25 × 10⁸ ≈ 7.85 × 10⁸ rad/s, and 7.85 × 10⁸ ÷ 11.7 ≈ **6.7 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)** (that is, γ/2π ≈ 10.7 MHz/T).","how":{"ask":"What does the passage’s definition give when the frequency (in Hz) and the field are plugged in?","know":"• Translate the words into a formula first: γ = (2π × f) ÷ B.\n• Convert MHz to Hz; tesla stays as given.\n• Units follow the formula: rad/s per T.","steps":[{"text":"Turn the definition into a formula.","hl":"two pi times the resonance frequency f","calc":["γ = 2πf / B"]},{"text":"Convert and substitute.","hl":"11.7 T magnet","calc":["f = 125 MHz = 1.25 × 10⁸ Hz","2πf ≈ 7.85 × 10⁸ rad/s","γ ≈ 7.85 × 10⁸ ÷ 11.7 ≈ **6.7 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)**"]},{"text":"Leaving MHz unconverted, multiplying by B, or dropping 2π each gives a wrong power or factor.","strike":["6.7 × 10¹ rad/(s·T)","9.2 × 10⁹ rad/(s·T)","1.1 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)"]},{"text":"Answer: 6.7 × 10⁷ rad/(s·T)."}],"trap":"Using f instead of 2πf: the passage defines γ with the angular frequency."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"125 MHz","note":"f = 1.25 × 10⁸ Hz"},{"t":"11.7 T","note":"B"},{"t":"divided by the magnetic field B","note":"divide, don’t multiply"}]},{"text":"**Read the definition as an equation.** \"Two pi times f, divided by B\" is γ = 2πf/B."},{"text":"**Unit check.** Hz ÷ T → s⁻¹·T⁻¹; adding the radian from 2π gives rad/(s·T)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** when a passage defines a quantity in words, write it as a formula, convert every value to base units (MHz → Hz), and keep each constant such as 2π."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-53a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:53#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:53"],"passage":"A student makes hydrogen gas by dropping identical strips of magnesium into excess hydrochloric acid and collects the gas in a syringe. Both runs are done at room temperature and go to completion. The first run is done at ordinary sea-level pressure; the second is done inside a sealed chamber held at twice that pressure, and the gas collected there fills a much smaller volume.","q":"Which explanation best fits the smaller volume in the second run?","choices":["Same moles at higher pressure","Less hydrogen was produced","The magnesium did not react","The gas was colder in the chamber"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"Gas was collected in both runs, so the metal did react."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Identical strips reacted to completion with excess acid, so both runs made the same moles of H₂."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Both runs were at room temperature; temperature is not what changed between them."}],"rule":"Use V = nRT/P: list which of n, T and P differ between the runs; the one that changed explains the volume difference.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"n same (identical strips, completion), T same (room temperature), P doubled → V halves (Boyle). Nothing else changed."},"exp":"Identical strips with excess acid going to completion make the **same moles of H₂** in both runs, and both runs are at room temperature. Only the **pressure** differs, and for a fixed amount of gas at constant T, **V ∝ 1/P** (Boyle’s law): doubling the pressure halves the volume. Likewise, at constant pressure, the same moles at a lower temperature would take up less volume (V ∝ T, Charles’s law).","how":{"ask":"Which of n, T and P differs between the two runs?","know":"• **PV = nRT**, so V = nRT/P.\n• Constant n and T: V ∝ 1/P (Boyle). Constant n and P: V ∝ T in kelvin (Charles).\n• Moles of gas formed are set by the limiting reagent, not by the conditions of collection.","steps":[{"text":"Moles: identical strips, excess acid, run to completion → the same n.","hl":"identical strips of magnesium","strike":["Less hydrogen was produced"]},{"text":"Gas formed in both runs, and both were at room temperature.","hl":"Both runs are done at room temperature","strike":["The magnesium did not react","The gas was colder in the chamber"]},{"text":"Only the pressure doubled, so the same gas fills about half the volume.","hl":"twice that pressure","calc":["V = nRT/P → P × 2 → V ÷ 2"]},{"text":"Also: the temperature version of the same idea: at constant pressure, the same moles of gas fill less volume when colder (V ∝ T)."},{"text":"Answer: the same moles of gas, compressed by the higher pressure."}],"trap":"Assuming a smaller gas volume means less gas formed: volume also depends on pressure and temperature."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"go to completion","note":"same moles"},{"t":"room temperature","note":"same T"},{"t":"twice that pressure","note":"P doubled"}]},{"text":"**Hold the variables steady.** Check n, T and P one at a time; whichever differs explains the change.","calc":["V ∝ 1/P (n, T fixed)","V ∝ T (n, P fixed)"]},{"text":"**Amount vs volume.** The reaction fixes the moles; the conditions fix the volume those moles fill."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** when the same reaction gives different gas volumes, use V = nRT/P: if n is fixed, the volume change comes from T (V ∝ T) or P (V ∝ 1/P)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-54a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:54#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:54"],"passage":"Compound R is being tested as a refrigerant, so its behavior under pressure matters. Its phase diagram is shown. In one test, its gas, held at a constant −25 °C, is compressed from 1 to 9 atm.","q":"As the passage's cycle proceeds, which changes occur, in order?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of compound R (sketch)","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-90,"max":50,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-90,0.3],[-70,0.7],[-50,1.2],[-30,2]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-30,2],[-25,6],[-20,10]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"straight","points":[[-30,2],[-10,3],[10,4.5],[30,6.5],[50,9]]}]},"choices":["Condensation, then freezing","Deposition only","Freezing, then condensation","Condensation only"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"A gas must condense into liquid before that liquid can freeze; going up in pressure meets the liquid–gas line first."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That stops at the first line; the solid–liquid line sits at 6 atm, below 9 atm, so the path crosses it too."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Deposition needs the solid–gas line, which lies only left of the triple point (−30 °C); a path at −25 °C never meets it."}],"rule":"Trace the path on the diagram: move straight along it at that temperature and name each boundary line you cross, in order.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Constant T = straight up the diagram. Above the triple-point temperature you cross gas→liquid, then liquid→solid: condensation, then freezing."},"exp":"Constant temperature = a **vertical** path at −25 °C. Going up, it first crosses the **liquid–gas line (≈ 2.3 atm): condensation**, then the **solid–liquid line (≈ 6 atm): freezing**, ending as a solid at 9 atm. It never touches the solid–gas line, which lies left of the triple point (−30 °C, 2 atm).","how":{"ask":"Draw the path at −25 °C from 1 to 9 atm: which lines does it cross, in order?","know":"• On a phase diagram, **pressure is up, temperature is across**; constant T = a vertical path, constant P = a horizontal path.\n• Each boundary crossed is one phase change: liquid–gas = vaporization/condensation; solid–liquid = melting/freezing; solid–gas = sublimation/deposition.\n• Name the change from the region you leave to the region you enter.","steps":[{"text":"Place the start: −25 °C and 1 atm lies in the gas region (low pressure, right of the triple point).","hl":"constant −25 °C"},{"text":"Move straight up. First boundary: liquid–gas, near 2.3 atm, so gas becomes liquid.","calc":["liquid–gas line at −25 °C: 2 + (5/20) × 1 ≈ 2.3 atm","gas → liquid = condensation"],"strike":["Deposition only"]},{"text":"Keep going: the solid–liquid line sits at 6 atm, below 9 atm, so the liquid becomes solid.","calc":["6 atm < 9 atm → freezing"],"strike":["Condensation only","Freezing, then condensation"]},{"text":"Answer: condensation, then freezing."}],"trap":"Choosing deposition because the path starts as gas and ends as solid; at −25 °C, warmer than the triple point, a liquid region lies between them."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"constant −25 °C","note":"a vertical path"},{"t":"compressed from 1 to 9 atm","note":"move up"},{"t":"phase diagram","note":"read regions and lines"}]},{"text":"**Read the map:** solid at upper left, liquid in the wedge, gas at lower right; the three lines meet at the triple point (−30 °C, 2 atm).","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of compound R (sketch)","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-90,"max":50,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-90,0.3],[-70,0.7],[-50,1.2],[-30,2]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-30,2],[-25,6],[-20,10]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"straight","points":[[-30,2],[-10,3],[10,4.5],[30,6.5],[50,9]]}]}},{"text":"**Draw the path and list each line it crosses**, in order, naming the region it leaves and the one it enters.","calc":["gas → liquid (condensation) → solid (freezing)"]},{"text":"**Also:** a path held below the triple-point pressure, such as warming at 1 atm from −80 °C, crosses only the solid–gas line: sublimation, with no liquid."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** follow the path (vertical for constant T, horizontal for constant P) and name each boundary it crosses in order; sublimation or deposition happens only below the triple-point pressure."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-55a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:55#1","pe6-cp:55#2","pe6-cp:55#3"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:55"],"passage":"Nitroglycerin can sit unchanged in a cool, still bottle for years, yet a sharp blow makes it detonate, releasing a great deal of heat and gas. The energy diagram for its decomposition, with energies per mole of reaction, is shown. Once the reaction starts, the heat it releases pushes neighboring molecules over the barrier.","q":"According to the diagram, the decomposition is (energies in kJ/mol):","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Energy along the reaction coordinate for the decomposition","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":350,"step":50},"series":[{"name":"Decomposition","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,150],[1,150],[2,165],[3,210],[4,270],[5,300],[6,260],[7,170],[8,90],[9,45],[10,40]]}]},"choices":["exothermic; Ea = 150.","endothermic; Ea = 150.","exothermic; Ea = 300.","exothermic; Ea = 260."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Products sit below the reactants, so energy is released; calling it endothermic flips the sign of ΔH."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is the reverse barrier, peak minus products; the forward barrier is measured up from the reactants."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"Reads the peak height from zero; Ea is the climb from the reactants’ level to the peak."}],"rule":"On an energy diagram: ΔH = products − reactants (products lower → exothermic); Ea = peak − reactants, never peak − zero or peak − products.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Two reads: is the right end lower than the left (exothermic)? How far is the peak above the left end (Ea)? 300 − 150 = 150 kJ/mol."},"exp":"The reactants start at **150 kJ/mol** and the products end at **40 kJ/mol**, so ΔH = 40 − 150 = **−110 kJ/mol: exothermic**. The peak is at 300 kJ/mol, so the forward **Ea = 300 − 150 = 150 kJ/mol**. A large release of energy plus a **high barrier** is exactly why the compound is stable until struck, then explosive.","how":{"ask":"Where do the products sit relative to the reactants, and how high is the peak above the reactants?","know":"• ΔH (or ΔG) = products − reactants: negative → exothermic (exergonic).\n• Forward Ea = transition-state energy − reactant energy; reverse Ea = transition state − products.\n• Kinetically stable but thermodynamically unstable = large energy release, high Ea.","steps":[{"text":"Read the two ends of the curve.","calc":["reactants: 150 kJ/mol","products: 40 kJ/mol","ΔH = 40 − 150 = −110 → exothermic"],"strike":["endothermic; Ea = 150."]},{"text":"Read the peak and measure up from the reactants.","calc":["peak: 300 kJ/mol","Ea = 300 − 150 = **150 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"300 measures from zero and 260 is the reverse barrier.","strike":["exothermic; Ea = 300.","exothermic; Ea = 260."]},{"text":"This matches the passage: it sits unchanged for years (high barrier) and releases great heat once started (exothermic).","hl":"sit unchanged in a cool, still bottle for years"},{"text":"Answer: exothermic, with Ea = 150 kJ/mol."}],"trap":"Reading Ea as the peak’s height on the axis: the barrier is measured from where the reactants start."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"sit unchanged","note":"high activation energy"},{"t":"releasing a great deal of heat","note":"exothermic"},{"t":"energy diagram","note":"read ends and peak"}]},{"text":"**Two separate questions.**","calc":["thermodynamics: products vs reactants → ΔH","kinetics: peak vs reactants → Ea"]},{"text":"**Stable but explosive.** A big negative ΔH with a high Ea: nothing happens until a blow or spark supplies the barrier energy, then the released heat keeps the reaction going."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on an energy diagram, ΔH is products minus reactants and the forward Ea is the peak minus reactants; a compound that is stable until triggered yet releases energy is exothermic with a high Ea."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-56a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:56#1","pe6-cp:56#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:56"],"passage":"Baker’s ammonia is an old leavening agent that leaves no solid residue in thin cookies. It decomposes completely when heated: NH₄HCO₃(s) → NH₃(g) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(g). A chemist collects all of the gas from a sample heated in a closed oven-safe flask.","q":"Water vapor makes up what mole fraction of the gas produced?","choices":["0.25","0.33","0.67","1.0"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is the share of the other two gases, the complement, not water's share."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"A fraction of 1.0 would mean water is the only gas; the ammonia and carbon dioxide must go in the total too."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"That counts the solid in the total (1 of 4); only gases belong in a gas-phase mole fraction."}],"rule":"Mole fraction = moles of that gas ÷ moles of all gases; count gas coefficients only, never solids or liquids.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Count only gas coefficients: 1 + 1 + 1 = 3, water is 1 of them → 1/3 ≈ 0.33. The solid reactant is not in the gas."},"exp":"Count gas moles from the coefficients: **1 NH₃ + 1 CO₂ + 1 H₂O = 3 mol**; the solid is not part of the gas. **x(H₂O) = 1 ÷ 3 ≈ 0.33**.","how":{"ask":"Divide water's coefficient by the sum of the gas coefficients only.","know":"• **Mole fraction x_i = n_i ÷ n_total**, always between 0 and 1.\n• For a complete reaction, coefficients give mole ratios.\n• **Count only (g) species**: solids and liquids are not in the gas mixture.","steps":[{"text":"Pick out the gaseous products.","hl":"NH₃(g) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(g)","calc":["gas moles = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3"]},{"text":"Divide the water's share by the total.","calc":["x(H₂O) = 1 ÷ 3 ≈ **0.33**"]},{"text":"0.25 puts the solid in the total, 0.67 is the other two gases, and 1.0 forgets them entirely.","strike":["0.25","0.67","1.0"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.33."}],"trap":"Adding the solid reactant's coefficient to the total: a gas mole fraction counts gases only."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"NH₄HCO₃(s)","note":"solid: not in the gas"},{"t":"H₂O(g)","note":"1 mol of the gas asked"},{"t":"mole fraction","note":"part ÷ whole"}]},{"text":"**Coefficients are moles.** Per mole of solid that decomposes, the products are 1 mol each of NH₃, CO₂ and H₂O.","calc":["n(gas) = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3 mol"]},{"text":"**Part over whole.**","calc":["x(H₂O) = 1/3 ≈ 0.33","x(NH₃) + x(CO₂) = 2/3; all three sum to 1"]},{"text":"**Also:** leave every (s) and (l) out of the total; the phase labels decide what counts in a gas mixture."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mole fraction of a gaseous product = its coefficient ÷ the sum of the gas coefficients, ignoring solids and liquids."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-57a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:57#1","pe6-cp:57#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:57"],"q":"Technetium-99m (half-life 6.0 h) arrives at a clinic with an activity of 48 mCi. What is its activity 24 h later?","choices":["1.5 mCi","3.0 mCi","6.0 mCi","12 mCi"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Stops after three halvings; 24 hours holds four half-lives, so halve once more."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Divides by the number of half-lives; each half-life halves the activity, so the factor is 2 raised to that number."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Halves five times; 24 h ÷ 6.0 h gives four half-lives, not five."}],"rule":"Activity after time t: n = t ÷ half-life, then A = A₀ × (1/2)ⁿ. Halve n times; never divide by n.","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"24 ÷ 6.0 = 4 half-lives: 48 → 24 → 12 → 6 → 3 mCi."},"exp":"The number of half-lives is **n = 24 h ÷ 6.0 h = 4**. Activity halves each time: 48 → 24 → 12 → 6 → **3.0 mCi**, the same as 48 × (1/2)⁴ = 48 ÷ 16.","how":{"ask":"How many half-lives fit in 24 hours, and what is left after halving that many times?","know":"• n = elapsed time ÷ half-life.\n• A = A₀ × (1/2)ⁿ (the same rule for mass or number of nuclei).\n• After 1, 2, 3, 4 half-lives: ½, ¼, ⅛, 1/16 remain.","steps":[{"text":"Count half-lives.","hl":"half-life 6.0 h","calc":["24 h ÷ 6.0 h = **4**"]},{"text":"Halve four times.","hl":"48 mCi","calc":["48 → 24 → 12 → 6 → **3.0 mCi**","check: 48 × 1/16 = 3.0"]},{"text":"6.0 mCi stops one halving short, 1.5 mCi halves once too often, and 12 mCi divides by 4 instead of halving 4 times.","strike":["6.0 mCi","1.5 mCi","12 mCi"]},{"text":"Answer: 3.0 mCi."}],"trap":"Dividing the starting activity by the number of half-lives: decay is a repeated halving, (1/2)ⁿ, not 1/n."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"half-life 6.0 h","note":"one halving per 6 h"},{"t":"48 mCi","note":"starting activity"},{"t":"24 h later","note":"four half-lives"}]},{"text":"**Count, then halve.**","calc":["n = t ÷ t½","A = A₀ × (1/2)ⁿ"]},{"text":"**Fractions to know.** 1 half-life → ½, 2 → ¼, 3 → ⅛, 4 → 1/16, 5 → 1/32."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** divide the elapsed time by the half-life to get n, then multiply the starting activity by (1/2)ⁿ."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-58a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:58#1"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:58"],"q":"Radon-222 (Z = 86), a gas that seeps into basements, is an alpha emitter. Given Po (Z = 84), At (Z = 85) and Ra (Z = 88), what is the daughter nucleus?","choices":["Astatine-218","Radium-226","Polonium-218","Polonium-220"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Radium adds the alpha particle instead of removing it; emission makes the nucleus lighter."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"An alpha particle carries 2 protons, so Z drops by 2 (to 84), not by 1."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An alpha particle has mass number 4, so A drops by 4; that subtracts only 2."}],"rule":"Alpha emission: A drops by 4 and Z drops by 2; then match the new Z to the element's name.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Alpha = lose 4 in mass, 2 in Z. 222 − 4 = 218; 86 − 2 = 84 = Po → polonium-218."},"exp":"An **alpha particle is ⁴₂He**, so **A falls by 4 and Z by 2**: A = 222 − 4 = 218, Z = 86 − 2 = 84 = **polonium**. ²²²Rn → **²¹⁸Po** + ⁴He.","how":{"ask":"Subtract 4 from A and 2 from Z, then name the element from the new Z.","know":"• **Alpha decay** emits ⁴₂He: **A − 4, Z − 2**.\n• Z alone fixes the element's identity.\n• Check: mass numbers and charges balance on both sides.","steps":[{"text":"Read the parent.","hl":"Radon-222 (Z = 86)","calc":["A = 222, Z = 86"]},{"text":"Remove a helium-4 nucleus.","calc":["A: 222 − 4 = 218","Z: 86 − 2 = 84 → Po"]},{"text":"Balance check.","calc":["222 = 218 + 4 ✓; 86 = 84 + 2 ✓"]},{"text":"Astatine drops Z by only 1, radium adds the alpha, and polonium-220 subtracts 2 from A instead of 4.","strike":["Astatine-218","Radium-226","Polonium-220"]},{"text":"Answer: polonium-218."}],"trap":"Subtracting 2 from the mass number too: the alpha carries 2 protons but 4 nucleons."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"Radon-222","note":"A = 222"},{"t":"Z = 86","note":"protons"},{"t":"alpha emitter","note":"loses ⁴₂He"}]},{"text":"**What leaves.** An alpha particle is two protons and two neutrons.","calc":["ΔA = −4, ΔZ = −2"]},{"text":"**Name the daughter by Z.** The new proton count picks the element from the list.","calc":["Z = 84 → polonium; A = 218"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** alpha decay lowers A by 4 and Z by 2; the new Z names the daughter."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-59a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:59#1","pe6-cp:59#2"],"twinOf":["pe6-cp:59"],"q":"In the octet Lewis structure of the nitrosonium ion, NO⁺, which bond joins N and O, and what is oxygen’s formal charge?","choices":["Triple; positive","Triple; zero","Double; positive","Double; zero"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"A double bond leaves nitrogen with only six electrons around it; more sharing is needed before octets are complete."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Stops at a double bond, which leaves nitrogen short of an octet, and then counts oxygen’s charge for the wrong structure."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"With one lone pair and three bonds, oxygen owns 5 electrons, one fewer than its 6 valence electrons."}],"rule":"Count total valence electrons, add multiple bonds until every atom has an octet, then formal charge = valence − (lone-pair electrons + bonds).","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"NO⁺ has 5 + 6 − 1 = 10 valence e⁻, like CO and N₂: a triple bond plus a single lone pair on both N and O. O: 6 − (2 + 3) = +1."},"exp":"NO⁺ has 5 + 6 − 1 = **10 valence electrons**. Only **:N≡O:⁺** gives both atoms octets: a **triple bond** (6 shared electrons) plus a single lone pair on both N and O. Formal charges: **N = 5 − (2 + 3) = 0** and **O = 6 − (2 + 3) = +1**, so oxygen carries the positive charge (they sum to the ion’s +1).","how":{"ask":"How many valence electrons does NO⁺ have, which bond gives both atoms octets, and what does oxygen own in that structure?","know":"• Total valence electrons: add each atom’s, subtract one per + charge (add one per − charge).\n• Use multiple bonds until every atom has an octet.\n• **Formal charge = valence e⁻ − (lone-pair e⁻ + number of bonds)**; the charges add up to the ion’s charge.","steps":[{"text":"Count electrons.","hl":"nitrosonium ion, NO⁺","calc":["N 5 + O 6 − 1 = 10 e⁻"]},{"text":"Place them: a double bond leaves N short; a triple bond with a single lone pair on both N and O gives both octets.","calc":[":N≡O: → 6 shared + 2 + 2 = 10 ✓"],"strike":["Double; positive","Double; zero"]},{"text":"Formal charge on oxygen in that structure.","calc":["O: 6 − (2 + 3) = **+1**","N: 5 − (2 + 3) = 0","sum = +1 ✓"],"strike":["Triple; zero"]},{"text":"Answer: triple bond; oxygen is positive."}],"trap":"Putting the + charge on the less electronegative atom by instinct: formal charge comes from electron bookkeeping, and here it lands on oxygen."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"NO⁺","note":"10 valence e⁻"},{"t":"octet Lewis structure","note":"forces a triple bond"},{"t":"formal charge","note":"valence − owned"}]},{"text":"**Isoelectronic family.** N₂, CO, CN⁻ and NO⁺ all have 10 valence electrons and a triple bond with a single lone pair on both N and O."},{"text":"**Bookkeeping.**","calc":["owned = lone-pair e⁻ + one per bond","formal charge = valence − owned"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count valence electrons, add bonds until every atom has an octet, then assign formal charge = valence − (lone-pair electrons + bonds); in a 10-electron diatomic the bond is triple."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-01a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:1"],"passage":"Glucokinase phosphorylates glucose in liver cells, and its activity is low when blood glucose is low. Compound G was found in a screen for small molecules that bind glucokinase at a site away from the active site. When Compound G was added to assays with purified glucokinase, the rate of glucose-6-phosphate formation rose about threefold at every glucose level tested. After each assay, Compound G was recovered unchanged, and no phosphorylated form of it was detected.","q":"With respect to the activity of glucokinase, Compound G acts as:","choices":["a glucokinase substrate","a glucokinase agonist","a glucokinase antagonist","a phosphorylation catalyst"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"An antagonist lowers activity, but the rate rose about threefold once Compound G was added."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"A substrate is changed by the enzyme; Compound G was recovered unchanged and never phosphorylated."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Glucokinase itself is the catalyst; Compound G binds the enzyme away from the active site and only tunes it."}],"rule":"Role words: ask two things. Was the molecule changed (substrate) or not? Did the enzyme work faster (agonist) or slower (antagonist)? The enzyme is the catalyst.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Find the two clues: \"recovered unchanged\" rules out substrate, and \"rose about threefold\" means activity went up, so it is the role that raises activity."},"exp":"Compound G is **not changed** (recovered unchanged, never phosphorylated), so it is not a substrate. It **raises** glucokinase activity about threefold, so it is an **agonist** (activator). The enzyme, not Compound G, is the catalyst; an antagonist would lower the rate.","how":{"ask":"Name the role Compound G plays for glucokinase from what the passage says it does.","know":"Four roles, told apart by two questions. **Changed by the enzyme?** Yes → substrate. **No → does activity go up or down?** Up → agonist (activator); down → antagonist (inhibitor). The **catalyst** is the enzyme itself.","steps":[{"text":"Was Compound G changed? No: it came back intact, so it is not a substrate.","hl":"Compound G was recovered unchanged","strike":["a glucokinase substrate"]},{"text":"Did activity go up or down? Up: the product formed about three times faster.","hl":"rose about threefold","strike":["a glucokinase antagonist"]},{"text":"Glucokinase is the catalyst; Compound G binds it at a separate site and boosts it.","hl":"at a site away from the active site","strike":["a phosphorylation catalyst"]},{"text":"Unchanged and activity up: Compound G is a glucokinase agonist."}],"trap":"Calling anything that speeds a reaction a \"catalyst\": the enzyme is the catalyst, and a molecule that boosts the enzyme is an agonist."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues** about what happened to the molecule and to the activity.","marks":[{"t":"recovered unchanged","note":"not consumed → not a substrate"},{"t":"rose about threefold","note":"activity went up"}]},{"text":"**Question 1: was it changed?** A substrate is converted into product. A molecule recovered intact is a binder (agonist or antagonist), not a substrate."},{"text":"**Question 2: up or down?** Binding that raises activity = agonist (activator). Binding that lowers it = antagonist (inhibitor), as a transition-state analog does."},{"text":"**Also:** the catalyst is the enzyme; an activator binding an allosteric site does not itself catalyze the reaction."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Changed? → substrate. Unchanged and activity up → agonist; unchanged and activity down → antagonist. The enzyme is the catalyst."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-02a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:2"],"passage":"Compound M is a sugar-derived antiviral lead isolated from a soil fungus. Its structure has seven stereogenic carbons and no internal mirror plane. Both of its C=C double bonds lie inside rings, so neither can show E/Z isomerism. Only one stereoisomer blocks the viral polymerase, so chemists plan an asymmetric synthesis rather than separating isomers at the end.","q":"Counting every possible configuration, how many stereoisomers can Compound M have at most?","choices":["14","49","128","512"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"That squares the number of centers; the count is 2 raised to the number of centers, not the reverse."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"Doubling the number of centers adds the choices instead of multiplying them: each center doubles the total."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"This also counts the ring C=C bonds, but the passage says they cannot show E/Z isomerism."}],"rule":"Maximum stereoisomers = 2ⁿ, where n counts stereocenters plus E/Z-capable double bonds; no mirror plane means no meso forms to subtract.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"Each stereocenter doubles the count: 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, then 2⁷ for seven centers. Ring double bonds add nothing."},"exp":"Each stereogenic center has two configurations, so the maximum is **2ⁿ**. Compound M has **seven** stereocenters and no mirror plane (no meso forms), and its ring C=C bonds cannot be E/Z, so the maximum is 2⁷ = **128**.","how":{"ask":"Count the stereogenic units, then raise 2 to that power.","know":"**Maximum stereoisomers = 2ⁿ**, n = stereocenters + double bonds that can be E or Z. A mirror plane would create meso forms and lower the count.","steps":[{"text":"Count the stereocenters: seven.","hl":"seven stereogenic carbons"},{"text":"The ring double bonds cannot be E/Z, so they add nothing.","hl":"neither can show E/Z isomerism","strike":["512"]},{"text":"No mirror plane, so no meso forms: use the full 2ⁿ.","calc":["2⁷ = **128**"]},{"text":"14 is 2 × 7 and 49 is 7²; neither is 2 raised to the count.","strike":["14","49"]}],"trap":"Counting every double bond as a stereo unit: only C=C bonds that can be E or Z count, and ring C=C bonds in small rings cannot."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the stereo units** in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"seven stereogenic carbons","note":"n = 7"},{"t":"no internal mirror plane","note":"no meso forms"},{"t":"neither can show E/Z isomerism","note":"add nothing"}]},{"text":"**Each unit doubles the count.** Two choices at each of n independent units give 2ⁿ combinations.","calc":["2⁷ = 128"]},{"text":"**Also:** a molecule with an internal mirror plane has meso forms, so its real count is below 2ⁿ."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Max stereoisomers = 2ⁿ (stereocenters + E/Z double bonds); subtract only when a mirror plane makes meso forms."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-03a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:3"],"passage":"A water-testing lab checks whether a well sample turns cloudy when silver nitrate is added, which signals chloride. Silver chloride (AgCl) is poorly soluble, with Ksp = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰ at 25 °C. One sample contains 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ M chloride. The technician adds silver nitrate until the free Ag⁺ concentration is 5.0 × 10⁻⁵ M, before any solid could form.","q":"At that Ag⁺ level, does AgCl precipitate from the sample, and on what basis?","choices":["AgCl forms; Q above Ksp","No solid; Q below Ksp","AgCl forms; [Ag⁺] above Ksp","No solid; Q equals Ksp"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Multiply the exponents correctly: the ion product lands just below Ksp, not above it, so no solid forms."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Ksp is compared with the product of both ion concentrations, never with one ion alone."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Q equals Ksp only in a saturated solution; this product differs from Ksp."}],"rule":"Compute Q = [cation][anion] with the starting concentrations and compare it with Ksp: Q above → solid forms; Q below → stays dissolved.","fast":{"kind":"round","how":"Multiply the coefficients and add the exponents separately: 2.0 × 5.0 = 10, and 10⁻⁶ × 10⁻⁵ = 10⁻¹¹, so Q = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰. Compare with 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰."},"exp":"Q = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻] = (5.0 × 10⁻⁵)(2.0 × 10⁻⁶) = **1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰**. That is **below** Ksp (1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰), so the solution is unsaturated and AgCl **stays dissolved**.","how":{"ask":"Compute the ion product Q, compare it with Ksp, and say whether a solid forms.","know":"For AgCl, **Q = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻]**. Q > Ksp → precipitate forms; Q < Ksp → unsaturated, no solid; Q = Ksp → saturated.","steps":[{"text":"Pull out both ion concentrations.","hl":"5.0 × 10⁻⁵ M"},{"text":"Multiply them.","calc":["Q = (5.0 × 10⁻⁵)(2.0 × 10⁻⁶) = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰"]},{"text":"Compare: 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ is less than 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰, so no solid and Q is not equal to Ksp.","strike":["AgCl forms; Q above Ksp","No solid; Q equals Ksp"]},{"text":"One ion alone is never compared with Ksp.","strike":["AgCl forms; [Ag⁺] above Ksp"]}],"trap":"Comparing a single ion concentration with Ksp: Ksp is a product, so compare it with the product Q."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰","note":"Ksp"},{"t":"2.0 × 10⁻⁶ M","note":"[Cl⁻]"},{"t":"5.0 × 10⁻⁵ M","note":"[Ag⁺]"}]},{"text":"**Build Q like Ksp**, with the concentrations present before any solid forms.","calc":["Q = (5.0 × 10⁻⁵)(2.0 × 10⁻⁶) = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰"]},{"text":"**Compare.** 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ < 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰: the solution can still hold more ions, so it stays clear."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Q > Ksp → precipitate; Q < Ksp → no solid; Q = Ksp → saturated. Always compare the product of the ions."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-04a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:4"],"passage":"Chymotrypsin is a digestive serine protease made by the pancreas. It hydrolyzes peptide bonds on the carboxyl side of residues with large aromatic side chains, such as phenylalanine, tyrosine and tryptophan. It does not cut after small residues such as glycine or serine. In a lab exercise, students incubate the tetrapeptide Ser–Gly–Phe–Val with chymotrypsin and identify the fragments by mass spectrometry.","q":"Which two fragments do the students detect?","choices":["Ser–Phe and Gly–Val","Ser–Gly and Phe–Val","Ser–Gly–Phe and Val","Ser and Gly–Phe–Val"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"Hydrolysis only breaks a bond; it never joins residues that were not neighbors in the chain."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"That cuts on the amino side of Phe; chymotrypsin cuts on its carboxyl side."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"That cuts after Ser, a small residue the passage says chymotrypsin does not cut after."}],"rule":"Find the residue the protease recognizes, cut on the side the passage names, and keep both pieces in their original order.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Put a slash right after the aromatic residue: Ser–Gly–Phe / Val. Read the two pieces off."},"exp":"Chymotrypsin cuts on the **carboxyl (C-terminal) side** of aromatic residues. The only aromatic residue is **Phe**, so the Phe–Val bond breaks, giving **Ser–Gly–Phe and Val**.","how":{"ask":"Find the bond the protease breaks and read off the two pieces.","know":"Chymotrypsin: cuts **after** (C-terminal side of) Phe, Tyr, Trp. Hydrolysis splits one peptide bond and keeps sequence order.","steps":[{"text":"Find the recognized residue: Phe is the only aromatic one.","hl":"Ser–Gly–Phe–Val"},{"text":"Cut on its carboxyl side, between Phe and Val.","hl":"on the carboxyl side","strike":["Ser–Gly and Phe–Val"]},{"text":"Ser is small, so no cut after it; and pieces never swap residues.","strike":["Ser and Gly–Phe–Val","Ser–Phe and Gly–Val"]},{"text":"Fragments: the tripeptide ending in Phe, plus free Val."}],"trap":"Cutting on the wrong side of the recognized residue: \"carboxyl side\" means the bond after it."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the cutting rule**.","marks":[{"t":"on the carboxyl side of residues with large aromatic side chains","note":"cut after Phe/Tyr/Trp"},{"t":"does not cut after small residues","note":"no cut after Gly or Ser"}]},{"text":"**Mark the cut.** Ser–Gly–Phe | Val: the bond after Phe breaks."},{"text":"**Also:** trypsin uses the same logic for Lys and Arg."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Locate the recognized residue, break the bond on the named side, and keep the order of every residue."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-05a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:5"],"passage":"Liposomes are hollow vesicles bounded by a single lipid bilayer about 5 nm thick. In one experiment, liposomes were filled with a solution low in K⁺ and placed in a bath rich in K⁺. Adding valinomycin, a carrier that lets only K⁺ cross the bilayer, caused K⁺ to flow into the vesicles until the lumen sat at +60 mV relative to the bath. The researchers then used this voltage to drive a transporter reconstituted in the membrane.","q":"After the voltage settles, which way does the electric field point inside the bilayer?","choices":["zero inside the bilayer","inward, toward the lumen","outward, toward the bath","sideways, along the bilayer"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"A voltage across a thin layer means a field inside it; zero field would mean no potential difference."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Field lines run from high to low potential, so they cannot point toward the positive lumen."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The potential differs between the two faces, so the field runs across the bilayer, not along it."}],"rule":"E runs downhill in potential, + side to − side: find which side is positive, then point away from it, straight across the membrane.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Field = from + to −. The lumen is the positive side, so point from the lumen to the other side."},"exp":"The lumen is at **+60 mV relative to the bath**, so it is the higher-potential side. The field points from **high to low potential**, straight across the bilayer: **outward, toward the bath**.","how":{"ask":"Find which face is at the higher potential, then point the field away from it.","know":"**E runs from the + side to the − side** (the way a positive charge would be pushed). Across a membrane it runs perpendicular to the surface.","steps":[{"text":"Which side is positive? The lumen.","hl":"the lumen sat at +60 mV relative to the bath"},{"text":"There is a voltage, so the field is not zero, and it runs across the layer.","strike":["zero inside the bilayer","sideways, along the bilayer"]},{"text":"High to low: from the lumen toward the bath, not the reverse.","strike":["inward, toward the lumen"]}],"trap":"Assuming every membrane field points inward, as in a resting neuron: here the inside is the positive side."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the sign** of each side.","marks":[{"t":"+60 mV relative to the bath","note":"lumen is the higher-potential side"}]},{"text":"**Field goes downhill in potential**, from + to −, perpendicular to the two faces."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Identify the positive side; the field inside the membrane points straight from it to the negative side."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-06a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"biology","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:6"],"passage":"In the peripheral nerves of a hypomyelinated mouse strain, each Schwann cell wraps its axon only a few times instead of dozens of times. Axon radius in the mutant is the same as in normal mice. Each wrap adds two more lipid bilayers in series around the axon, so it thickens the insulating layer between the cytoplasm and the outside fluid. Conduction velocity in the mutant nerves is far below normal.","q":"Relative to normal internodes, the mutant internodes' membrane capacitance and membrane resistance are, in order:","choices":["decreased; increased","unchanged; unchanged","increased; decreased","increased; increased"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Fewer wraps make a thinner insulator; that is the change produced by adding wraps, not removing them."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Same radius, but the insulating layer is thinner, and both C and R depend on its thickness."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Thinning the insulator lets more current leak across, so resistance cannot rise."}],"rule":"Membrane as a capacitor and resistor: thicker insulation → lower C, higher R; thinner insulation → higher C, lower R.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Think of the limit: zero wraps is bare membrane, the high-C, low-R extreme. Fewer wraps move toward it."},"exp":"Capacitance falls as the dielectric gets **thicker** (C ∝ 1/d), and resistance rises with **more layers in series**. With **fewer** wraps the insulation is **thinner**, so capacitance is **increased** and resistance is **decreased**.","how":{"ask":"Decide how thinner insulation changes C and R, then keep the order the stem asks for.","know":"**C = εA/d**: thinner layer → more capacitance. **Resistances in series add**: fewer layers → less resistance.","steps":[{"text":"What changed? Fewer wraps, same radius: thinner insulation.","hl":"only a few times instead of dozens of times"},{"text":"Thinner dielectric → C goes up; fewer series layers → R goes down.","calc":["C ∝ 1/d ↑","R = R₁ + R₂ + … ↓"]},{"text":"Reject the reversed pair, \"no change\", and a rise in R.","strike":["decreased; increased","unchanged; unchanged","increased; increased"]}],"trap":"Reading \"respectively\" backwards: capacitance comes first, resistance second."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what changed** in the insulator.","marks":[{"t":"only a few times","note":"fewer layers"},{"t":"two more lipid bilayers in series","note":"each wrap adds resistance in series"}]},{"text":"**Capacitor view.** Plates = the two fluids; dielectric = the wraps. Thinner dielectric stores more charge per volt."},{"text":"**Resistor view.** Each bilayer is a resistor in series; remove layers and the total drops."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** More myelin → lower C, higher R; less myelin → higher C, lower R."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-07a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:7"],"passage":"Crown ethers are ring-shaped molecules whose oxygen atoms point into a central cavity. A crown ether binds most strongly the alkali-metal cation whose radius best fits its cavity. Of the common crown ethers, 12-crown-4 has the smallest cavity, and 18-crown-6 has a larger one. In an extraction study, a chemist tested each crown ether with solutions of LiI, RbI and CsI.","q":"Of the ions in the chemist's solutions, which one has the smallest radius?","choices":["Cs⁺","Rb⁺","Li⁺","I⁻"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Cesium sits lowest in Group 1, with the most electron shells: it is the biggest cation here."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Rubidium is smaller than cesium but still has several more shells than the top alkali metal."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Iodide is an anion with many shells and an extra electron; it is far larger than any of these cations."}],"rule":"Ion radius: fewer shells → smaller (move up a group); cations shrink, anions swell; same electron count → more protons, smaller.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Pick the ion highest in its group and positively charged; reject any anion from low in the table."},"exp":"Radius grows **down a group** as shells are added, and **anions are larger** than cations. Of Li⁺, Rb⁺, Cs⁺ and I⁻, **Li⁺** has only one filled shell (1s²), so it is the smallest.","how":{"ask":"Rank the ions by size and pick the smallest.","know":"**Down a group → larger.** Cation smaller than its atom; anion larger. For ions with the same electron count, more protons → smaller.","steps":[{"text":"List the ions in the solutions.","hl":"LiI, RbI and CsI"},{"text":"Iodide is a large anion from period 5.","strike":["I⁻"]},{"text":"Among the alkali cations, size grows down the group: Li⁺ < Rb⁺ < Cs⁺.","strike":["Cs⁺","Rb⁺"]}],"trap":"Assuming the heaviest ion is the most compact: more shells make it bigger."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the ions** named in the passage.","marks":[{"t":"LiI, RbI and CsI","note":"Li⁺, Rb⁺, Cs⁺ and I⁻"}]},{"text":"**Shells set size.** Each step down a group adds a shell, so the radius grows."},{"text":"**Also:** the smallest crown ether cavity fits the smallest alkali cation."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Smallest ion = fewest shells and, for equal electrons, the most positive charge."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-08a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 3","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:8"],"passage":"Researchers record compound action potentials from a frog sciatic nerve with a pair of electrodes placed along it. The amplifier can resolve a delay between the signals at the electrodes only if the delay is at least 0.40 ms. In untreated nerves the impulse travels at a steady 35 m/s. After the nerve is cooled to 5 °C, the impulse slows to 12 m/s.","q":"In the untreated nerve, what is the shortest electrode spacing that gives a delay the amplifier can resolve?","choices":["88 m","0.14 m","0.014 m","0.0048 m"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"Dividing speed by time gives m/s², not meters, and the milliseconds were never converted."},{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"A factor of ten went astray in the ms-to-s step: 0.40 ms is 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ s."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"That uses the cooled-nerve speed; the stem asks about the untreated nerve."}],"rule":"Shortest spacing = speed × shortest resolvable delay. Convert ms to s first and check that m/s × s leaves meters.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"m/s × s = m. 35 × 4 × 10⁻⁴ = 140 × 10⁻⁴ m = 1.4 × 10⁻² m."},"exp":"The delay equals spacing ÷ speed, so the shortest spacing is d = v × t_min = 35 m/s × 0.40 × 10⁻³ s = **0.014 m** (1.4 cm).","how":{"ask":"Multiply the untreated speed by the minimum delay, in seconds.","know":"**d = v·t**. 1 ms = 10⁻³ s.","steps":[{"text":"Take the untreated speed, not the cooled one.","hl":"travels at a steady 35 m/s","strike":["0.0048 m"]},{"text":"Convert the delay to seconds.","hl":"at least 0.40 ms","calc":["0.40 ms = 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ s"]},{"text":"Multiply.","calc":["d = 35 × 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 1.4 × 10⁻² m"]},{"text":"Dividing instead, or slipping a power of ten, gives the other values.","strike":["88 m","0.14 m"]}],"trap":"Leaving the delay in milliseconds: the answer then comes out a thousand times too big."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers** and which condition each belongs to.","marks":[{"t":"0.40 ms","note":"shortest resolvable delay"},{"t":"35 m/s","note":"untreated speed"},{"t":"12 m/s","note":"cooled speed (not asked)"}]},{"text":"**Below the minimum spacing**, the impulse covers the gap faster than the amplifier can resolve."},{"text":"**Multiply in SI units.**","calc":["35 m/s × 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ s = 1.4 × 10⁻² m"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** distance = speed × time, with time in seconds; let the units confirm you multiplied."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-15a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:15"],"q":"Assuming it dissociates completely in water, what is the K⁺ concentration in a 0.12 M solution of potassium phosphate, K₃PO₄?","choices":["0.040 M","0.36 M","0.48 M","0.12 M"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"That divides by the subscript; each formula unit releases several K⁺, so the ion level exceeds the salt level."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"That counts every ion, phosphate included; the stem asks only for K⁺."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"That repeats the salt concentration without using the subscript on K."}],"rule":"Ion concentration = salt concentration × that ion's subscript in the formula. Count only the ion asked for.","fast":{"kind":"scale","how":"K₃ means three K⁺ per unit: triple the molarity of the salt."},"exp":"K₃PO₄ → **3 K⁺** + PO₄³⁻. So [K⁺] = 3 × 0.12 M = **0.36 M**.","how":{"ask":"Multiply the salt concentration by the number of K⁺ per formula unit.","know":"Complete dissociation: each formula unit releases its ions in the ratio of the subscripts.","steps":[{"text":"Write the dissociation.","hl":"K₃PO₄","calc":["K₃PO₄ → 3 K⁺ + PO₄³⁻"]},{"text":"Multiply by the K subscript.","calc":["[K⁺] = 3 × 0.12 = 0.36 M"]},{"text":"Not divided, not all ions, not the bare salt molarity.","strike":["0.040 M","0.48 M","0.12 M"]}],"trap":"Adding in the phosphate ions: the stem asks for one ion only."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the formula and molarity**.","marks":[{"t":"0.12 M","note":"formula units per liter"},{"t":"K₃PO₄","note":"three K⁺ each"}]},{"text":"**Subscript = multiplier.**","calc":["3 × 0.12 M = 0.36 M"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** [ion] = [salt] × subscript of that ion."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-17a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:17"],"q":"Treating each group as fully charged or uncharged, what is the net charge of the peptide Arg–His–Lys–Glu–Lys–Arg in blood plasma?","choices":["+6","+3","+4","+5"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"This counts the basic groups and His but leaves out the negative Glu side chain."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That adds the free amino end and His but drops every negative group, including the free carboxyl end."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"His (pKa about 6) is mostly neutral in plasma, so it adds no charge."}],"rule":"At blood pH: Lys and Arg +1, Asp and Glu −1, His 0, and the free ends cancel (+1 and −1). Add the side chains.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Termini cancel. Count Arg/Lys (+1 each) and Asp/Glu (−1 each); skip His. Four plus, one minus."},"exp":"At blood pH the **N-terminus (+1) and C-terminus (−1) cancel**. Side chains: Arg +1, His 0, Lys +1, Glu −1, Lys +1, Arg +1. Net = 4 − 1 = **+3**.","how":{"ask":"Add the charges of the ionizable groups at blood pH.","know":"Near pH 7.4: **Lys, Arg +1; Asp, Glu −1; His ≈ 0** (pKa ≈ 6); free amino end +1 and free carboxyl end −1.","steps":[{"text":"The two ends cancel.","calc":["+1 (N-terminus) − 1 (C-terminus) = 0"]},{"text":"Count the side chains: four basic (Arg, Lys, Lys, Arg), one acidic (Glu), His neutral.","hl":"Arg–His–Lys–Glu–Lys–Arg","calc":["+4 − 1 = +3"]},{"text":"Counting His as +1 gives one too many; dropping Glu as well, or every negative group, gives more.","strike":["+4","+5","+6"]}],"trap":"Counting His as positive: its pKa is about 6, so at blood pH it is mostly uncharged."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **sort each residue** by its side chain.","marks":[{"t":"Arg–His–Lys–Glu–Lys–Arg","note":"four basic, one acidic, one His"},{"t":"blood plasma","note":"pH about 7.4"}]},{"text":"**Ends cancel**, so only the side chains matter in a linear peptide."},{"text":"**Add them.** Lys/Arg +1 each, Glu −1, His 0.","calc":["+1 + 0 + 1 − 1 + 1 + 1 = +3"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** At pH 7–7.4, net charge = (number of Lys + Arg) − (number of Asp + Glu); His and the cancelling ends add nothing."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-23p","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"physics","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["aamc-phys-qpack:Q23"],"q":"Heat flows steadily into a piece of gallium, melting point 30 °C, that is already at its melting point. While any solid gallium remains, the metal's temperature:","choices":["dips a little.","keeps climbing.","holds at 30 °C.","climbs, then levels out."],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Heat is flowing in, so the sample cannot cool while it absorbs energy."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"The added energy goes into breaking the solid apart (latent heat), not into raising temperature."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The sample starts at its melting point, so there is no warming stage before the plateau."}],"rule":"During a phase change at fixed pressure, added heat changes the phase, not the temperature: the reading stays flat until the change is complete.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Solid and liquid together at fixed pressure = pinned at the melting point. Pick the flat line."},"exp":"While solid and liquid coexist, the heat supplied is **latent heat of fusion**: it breaks the solid lattice rather than speeding molecules up. The temperature therefore **holds at the melting point, 30 °C**, until all the gallium has melted.","how":{"ask":"Decide what the temperature does while the substance is changing phase.","know":"Two phases in equilibrium at fixed pressure → temperature fixed at the transition temperature. **q = mL** during the change, not mcΔT.","steps":[{"text":"Start: already at the melting point, with solid present.","hl":"already at its melting point"},{"text":"Heat in cannot cool it, and there is no warming stage left.","strike":["dips a little.","climbs, then levels out."]},{"text":"Heat goes into melting, so the temperature is flat.","strike":["keeps climbing."]}],"trap":"Expecting a steady rise because heat keeps flowing in: during melting the energy changes the phase."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the state** of the sample.","marks":[{"t":"already at its melting point","note":"phase change in progress"},{"t":"While any solid gallium remains","note":"two phases coexist"}]},{"text":"**Latent heat.** Energy breaks intermolecular bonds; average kinetic energy, and so temperature, stays the same."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Two phases together at fixed pressure → temperature stays at the transition point until one phase is gone."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-27a","s":"Waves & Sound","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:27"],"q":"A roadside radar unit uses the Doppler shift of microwaves reflected from an approaching car to find the car's speed. What inputs does it need?","choices":["Car's speed, c, and distance","Sent frequency and car's distance","Echo wavelength and beam power","Both frequencies and c"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"The car's speed is the unknown being solved for, so it cannot be an input."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Without the returned frequency there is no shift to measure, and distance does not enter the Doppler equation."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Beam power affects echo strength only; one wavelength alone gives no shift."}],"rule":"A Doppler speed needs the frequency shift (emitted and received) and the wave speed in the medium; distance and power never enter.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"v_source ≈ (Δf/f) × wave speed: two frequencies and one wave speed are the only inputs."},"exp":"The Doppler shift gives speed from **Δf/f = 2v/c** for a reflected wave. The unit must know the **emitted frequency**, measure the **received frequency**, and know the **wave speed (c for microwaves)**. Distance and power do not appear.","how":{"ask":"List the inputs of the Doppler equation used to solve for the reflector's speed.","know":"Reflected wave, slow target: **Δf ≈ 2vf/c**, so v ≈ cΔf/(2f). Inputs: f emitted, f received, wave speed.","steps":[{"text":"The car's speed is the output, not an input.","hl":"to find the car's speed","strike":["Car's speed, c, and distance"]},{"text":"The shift needs both frequencies; distance plays no role.","strike":["Sent frequency and car's distance"]},{"text":"Power and a single wavelength cannot give a shift.","strike":["Echo wavelength and beam power"]}],"trap":"Thinking the distance to the car matters: Doppler measures speed from frequency, not position."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the unknown**.","marks":[{"t":"Doppler shift","note":"frequency change"},{"t":"the car's speed","note":"what is solved for"}]},{"text":"**Write the equation** and read off its inputs.","calc":["v ≈ c(f_received − f_sent)/(2f_sent)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Doppler speed ← emitted frequency + received frequency + wave speed in the medium."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-28a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:28"],"q":"Pentane and neopentane share the formula C₅H₁₂, yet pentane boils at 36 °C and neopentane at 9.5 °C. Why does pentane boil so much higher?","choices":["Pentane is heavier","Neopentane is the more polar molecule","Pentane H-bonds","More chain contact in pentane"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"Same molecular formula means the same molar mass, so mass cannot explain the gap."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Greater polarity would raise neopentane's boiling point, yet it boils lower."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Hydrocarbons have no H on N, O or F, so neither compound can hydrogen-bond."}],"rule":"Same formula, different boiling points in alkanes: compare shape. Long chains touch over more area → stronger dispersion → higher bp.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Same mass, no polarity, no H-bonds: the only lever left is shape. The straight chain wins."},"exp":"Both are nonpolar alkanes with the same molar mass, held together only by **London dispersion forces**. Pentane's straight chain gives **more surface contact** between molecules than compact, spherical neopentane, so its attractions are stronger and its boiling point is higher.","how":{"ask":"Pick the intermolecular-force difference that explains which compound boils higher.","know":"Alkanes: dispersion only. At equal mass, **more surface area (less branching) → stronger dispersion → higher boiling point**.","steps":[{"text":"Same formula: same mass.","hl":"share the formula C₅H₁₂","strike":["Pentane is heavier"]},{"text":"Hydrocarbons are nonpolar and cannot H-bond.","strike":["Pentane H-bonds","Neopentane is the more polar molecule"]},{"text":"What differs is shape: the chain has more contact area than the ball."}],"trap":"Reaching for hydrogen bonding whenever boiling points differ: it needs H on N, O or F."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what is the same** and what differs.","marks":[{"t":"share the formula C₅H₁₂","note":"same mass"},{"t":"boils at 36 °C","note":"straight chain, higher"}]},{"text":"**Rule out forces that cannot be there**: no H-bonds, no real dipoles in alkanes."},{"text":"**Shape decides dispersion.** Branching makes a molecule compact, so neighbors touch less."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** For isomeric alkanes, more branching → lower boiling point."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-29a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:29"],"q":"A sodium electron is promoted from a 3s orbital to a 3p orbital. Which feature of the occupied orbital changes most?","choices":["Its shell (n)","Its shape","Its electron capacity","Its spin"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"Both labels begin with the same number, so the principal level and shell size do not change."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Every orbital, s or p, holds at most a pair of electrons."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Spin belongs to the electron, not the orbital, and promotion does not have to flip it."}],"rule":"Read the orbital label: the number (n) sets size and shell; the letter (ℓ) sets shape; the subscript (mℓ) sets orientation.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Same number, different letter: the letter is ℓ, and ℓ controls the shape."},"exp":"Going from **3s to 3p** keeps n = 3 (same shell and similar size) but changes ℓ from 0 to 1. ℓ sets the orbital's **shape**: spherical s to dumbbell-shaped p.","how":{"ask":"Find which quantum number changes, then name the property it controls.","know":"**n** → size and energy level; **ℓ** → shape (s, p, d, f); **mℓ** → orientation. Each orbital holds two electrons.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the labels: the number stays, the letter changes.","hl":"from a 3s orbital to a 3p orbital","strike":["Its shell (n)"]},{"text":"The letter is ℓ, which sets shape."},{"text":"Capacity is the same for every orbital, and spin is not an orbital property.","strike":["Its electron capacity","Its spin"]}],"trap":"Answering \"size\" by habit: size follows n, and n did not change."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **read both labels**.","marks":[{"t":"3s orbital","note":"n = 3, ℓ = 0"},{"t":"3p orbital","note":"n = 3, ℓ = 1"}]},{"text":"**Map each quantum number to its property.** n: size; ℓ: shape; mℓ: orientation."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Number changes → size; letter changes → shape; subscript changes → orientation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-38a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:38"],"passage":"In the liver, alcohol dehydrogenase oxidizes ethanol to acetaldehyde, and aldehyde dehydrogenase then oxidizes acetaldehyde to acetate. Neither enzyme breaks a carbon–carbon bond; each step only changes the oxidation state of the carbon that carries the oxygen. To trace this pathway, researchers gave rats ethanol labeled with ¹³C only at C-1, the carbon bonded to the hydroxyl group. They then isolated acetate from the blood and located the label by NMR.","q":"In the acetate the researchers isolated, where is the ¹³C label?","choices":["A carboxylate oxygen","The methyl carbon","The carboxylate carbon","Split evenly over both carbons"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"A carbon isotope stays a carbon atom; it cannot turn up on an oxygen."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"The methyl carbon was the unlabeled C-2; the label sat on the carbon bearing the oxygen."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"No C–C bond breaks, so the two carbons never trade places or scramble."}],"rule":"Track the labeled atom through each step: if no bond to it breaks, it stays in the same position, even as its oxidation state changes.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"The O-bearing carbon stays the O-bearing carbon: CH₂OH → CHO → COO⁻."},"exp":"C-1 of ethanol carries the OH. Oxidation turns it into the aldehyde carbon and then the **carboxylate carbon** of acetate. No C–C bond breaks, so the methyl carbon stays unlabeled.","how":{"ask":"Follow the labeled carbon through each oxidation step.","know":"Ethanol (CH₃–CH₂OH) → acetaldehyde (CH₃–CHO) → acetate (CH₃–COO⁻). Only the O-bearing carbon is oxidized.","steps":[{"text":"Where is the label? On the carbon bonded to the OH.","hl":"the carbon bonded to the hydroxyl group"},{"text":"That carbon becomes CHO, then COO⁻; the methyl group is untouched.","strike":["The methyl carbon"]},{"text":"No C–C bond breaks and a carbon cannot become oxygen.","hl":"Neither enzyme breaks a carbon–carbon bond","strike":["Split evenly over both carbons","A carboxylate oxygen"]}],"trap":"Thinking oxidation moves atoms around: it changes bonds to oxygen, not the carbon skeleton."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the label and what breaks**.","marks":[{"t":"labeled with ¹³C only at C-1","note":"the O-bearing carbon"},{"t":"Neither enzyme breaks a carbon–carbon bond","note":"skeleton intact"}]},{"text":"**Number the carbons and follow C-1.**","calc":["¹³CH₂OH → ¹³CHO → ¹³COO⁻"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** A label stays on its atom unless a bond to that atom breaks; trace it step by step."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-39a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:39"],"passage":"Musky odorants used in perfumes are often macrocyclic lactones. One is made by closing 12-hydroxydodecanoic acid into a ring: the oxygen of the terminal hydroxyl group attacks the carboxyl carbon at the other end of the chain, and water is lost. The carbonyl oxygen stays outside the ring. High dilution is used so that each chain reacts with its own end rather than with a neighbor.","q":"How many atoms make up the ring of this macrocyclic lactone?","choices":["11","12","13","14"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Counting only the carbons leaves out the hydroxyl oxygen that closes the ring."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That drops a chain carbon as well as the ring oxygen; the carboxyl carbon is in the ring."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The carbonyl oxygen sticks out of the ring and is not part of it."}],"rule":"Lactone ring = every carbon from the carboxyl carbon to the hydroxyl carbon, plus the one ring oxygen. Exclude the C=O oxygen.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"The locant of the OH carbon counts the ring carbons (C-1 to C-n); add 1 for the ring O."},"exp":"The ring contains **C-1 (the carboxyl carbon) through C-12 (the carbon bearing the OH)**, which is 12 carbons, plus the **ring oxygen** from the hydroxyl group: **13 atoms**. The carbonyl O is outside the ring.","how":{"ask":"Count the carbons from the carboxyl carbon to the hydroxyl carbon, then add the ring oxygen.","know":"Lactone = cyclic ester. Ring atoms = carbons C-1 … C-n (n = OH locant) + one O.","steps":[{"text":"The OH is on C-12, the chain end.","hl":"12-hydroxydodecanoic acid"},{"text":"Ring carbons: C-1 to C-12. Add the hydroxyl oxygen that closed the ring.","calc":["12 C + 1 O"],"strike":["12","11"]},{"text":"The carbonyl oxygen is outside the ring.","hl":"The carbonyl oxygen stays outside the ring","strike":["14"]}],"trap":"Forgetting the ring oxygen, which turns the count into the number of carbons only."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the locant of the OH** and how the ring closes.","marks":[{"t":"12-hydroxydodecanoic acid","note":"OH on C-12"},{"t":"the oxygen of the terminal hydroxyl group attacks the carboxyl carbon","note":"that O joins the ring"}]},{"text":"**Count:** carbons C-1 through C-12, then the one ring O."},{"text":"**Also:** the same count gives a five-membered ring from a 4-hydroxy acid."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Lactone ring size = OH locant + 1."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-40a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:40"],"passage":"Carvone, the ketone that gives spearmint its smell, can be reduced with sodium borohydride. In one teaching lab, students reduced carvone's carbonyl group to a secondary alcohol, carveol, and left both of its C=C double bonds untouched. A TLC check showed that no carvone remained. The students then purified carveol and recorded its infrared spectrum.","q":"What should the IR spectrum of the purified carveol display?","choices":["Only C≡N stretch","C=O, no O–H","Both C=O and broad O–H","Broad O–H, no C=O"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"Carveol contains no nitrogen, so it cannot show a nitrile stretch."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is the starting ketone's pattern; the reduction removed the carbonyl and created a hydroxyl."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"TLC showed no carvone left, so no ketone C=O should remain in the purified product."}],"rule":"Predict IR from the product's groups: alcohol → broad O–H near 3300 cm⁻¹; ketone or ester → sharp C=O near 1700 cm⁻¹. Lost group → lost band.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Reduction swaps C=O for C–OH: the carbonyl band goes, a broad O–H band appears."},"exp":"Reducing the ketone turns **C=O into CH–OH**. Pure carveol therefore shows a **broad O–H stretch** (about 3200–3550 cm⁻¹, hydrogen-bonded) and **no C=O stretch**. Its C=C bonds remain but are not among the choices.","how":{"ask":"Identify which functional groups the purified product has and which it lost.","know":"**O–H (alcohol):** broad, ~3300 cm⁻¹. **C=O:** strong, sharp, ~1700 cm⁻¹. **C≡N:** ~2250 cm⁻¹.","steps":[{"text":"What changed? The carbonyl became a secondary alcohol.","hl":"reduced carvone's carbonyl group to a secondary alcohol","strike":["C=O, no O–H"]},{"text":"Nothing is left of the ketone.","hl":"no carvone remained","strike":["Both C=O and broad O–H"]},{"text":"There is no nitrogen anywhere.","strike":["Only C≡N stretch"]}],"trap":"Keeping the starting material's C=O band in the product: the reaction used it up."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the groups** before and after.","marks":[{"t":"to a secondary alcohol","note":"O–H gained"},{"t":"no carvone remained","note":"C=O gone"}]},{"text":"**Map groups to bands.** O–H: broad, high wavenumber; C=O: strong, near 1700 cm⁻¹."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** IR shows the groups the product has: gained group → new band; lost group → band disappears."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-41a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:41"],"passage":"Acetophenone, an achiral ketone, can be reduced to 1-phenylethanol, which has one stereocenter. When a ketoreductase from yeast performs the reduction, only (S)-1-phenylethanol forms. When sodium borohydride in methanol performs the same reduction, the product is a 50:50 mixture of the (R) and (S) alcohols. Sodium borohydride and methanol are both achiral.","q":"What best explains the product mixture from the borohydride reduction?","choices":["The (R) alcohol is more stable","Acetophenone has a stereocenter","NaBH₄ is a chiral reagent","Both faces react equally"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"A stability difference would favor one product, yet the mixture is exactly even."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The passage calls acetophenone achiral; its carbonyl carbon is trigonal, not a stereocenter."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The passage says sodium borohydride is achiral, so the reagent cannot favor one face."}],"rule":"Achiral substrate + achiral reagent + achiral medium → racemic product. A single enantiomer requires something chiral, such as an enzyme's active site.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Nothing chiral anywhere in the flask → no preference → 50:50."},"exp":"The flat carbonyl of an achiral ketone has two faces. With **achiral** NaBH₄ in **achiral** methanol, the two faces are mirror images and are attacked **equally**, so the (R) and (S) alcohols form 50:50. Only a chiral environment, like the enzyme's active site, can favor one face.","how":{"ask":"Explain why the chemical reduction shows no preference between the two enantiomers.","know":"Enantiomers have equal energy. Selectivity needs a **chiral** influence: substrate, reagent, catalyst or solvent.","steps":[{"text":"Is anything chiral? Substrate, reagent and solvent are all achiral.","hl":"Sodium borohydride and methanol are both achiral","strike":["NaBH₄ is a chiral reagent","Acetophenone has a stereocenter"]},{"text":"Enantiomers have identical stability, and the result is even, not lopsided.","strike":["The (R) alcohol is more stable"]},{"text":"So hydride adds to either face of the carbonyl at the same rate."}],"trap":"Explaining selectivity by product stability: enantiomers are equally stable, so only a chiral environment can choose."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what is chiral** in each reaction.","marks":[{"t":"an achiral ketone","note":"two mirror-image faces"},{"t":"a 50:50 mixture","note":"no preference"},{"t":"only (S)-1-phenylethanol forms","note":"chiral enzyme chooses"}]},{"text":"**Mirror-image paths have equal energy** unless something chiral breaks the tie."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** All-achiral conditions → racemic; one enantiomer → a chiral catalyst or site favors one face."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-42a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:42"],"passage":"Many lipophilic hormones cross the plasma membrane and bind receptors inside the cell. In the adrenal glands and gonads, cholesterol is first cut down to pregnenolone, which is converted into other hormones such as androstenedione. The liver converts cholesterol into bile acids such as cholic acid, which keep cholesterol's fused ring system. Thyroxine, made in the thyroid gland, also binds a nuclear receptor and regulates gene expression.","q":"Which of these lipophilic hormones and metabolites is NOT a steroid?","choices":["Pregnenolone","Cholic acid","Androstenedione","Thyroxine"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Pregnenolone is cut from cholesterol and keeps its four fused rings: it is the parent of the steroid hormones."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Bile acids keep cholesterol's fused ring system, so cholic acid is a steroid."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Androstenedione is made from pregnenolone and is a steroid precursor of the sex hormones."}],"rule":"Steroid = four fused rings (6-6-6-5) from cholesterol. Acting on a nuclear receptor does not make a hormone a steroid; check what it is built from.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ask \"made from cholesterol?\" for each. The one built from an amino acid is the odd one out."},"exp":"Pregnenolone, androstenedione and cholic acid are all made from **cholesterol** and keep its **four fused rings**. **Thyroxine** is built from **two iodinated tyrosines**; it is lipophilic and acts on a nuclear receptor, but it is an amino-acid-derived hormone, **not a steroid**.","how":{"ask":"Pick the compound that is not built on cholesterol's four-ring skeleton.","know":"**Steroids:** cholesterol, its hormones (pregnenolone, progestins, corticoids, androgens, estrogens) and bile acids. **Not steroids:** eicosanoids, thyroid hormones, peptide hormones.","steps":[{"text":"Which ones come from cholesterol?","hl":"cholesterol is first cut down to pregnenolone","strike":["Pregnenolone","Androstenedione"]},{"text":"Bile acids keep the ring system.","hl":"keep cholesterol's fused ring system","strike":["Cholic acid"]},{"text":"Thyroxine shares the nuclear-receptor action, but not the skeleton."}],"trap":"Classifying by action: thyroid hormone acts like a steroid hormone, but its structure comes from tyrosine."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what each compound is made from**.","marks":[{"t":"pregnenolone","note":"from cholesterol"},{"t":"cholic acid","note":"bile acid, from cholesterol"},{"t":"Thyroxine","note":"from tyrosine"}]},{"text":"**Steroid = skeleton, not job.** Four fused rings from cholesterol define the class."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** In a NOT-a-steroid question, strike everything made from cholesterol; eicosanoids and amino-acid hormones remain."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-43a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:43"],"q":"A teenager's eyeball is slightly too long, so light from distant objects focuses in front of her retina. Which corrective lens does she need?","choices":["Converging lens","Diverging lens","Prism lens","Cylindrical lens"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"A converging lens pulls the focus even farther forward; it corrects an eye that focuses behind the retina."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A prism only shifts where the image lands sideways; it does not move the focal point back."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A cylindrical lens corrects astigmatism, a direction-dependent blur, not an overall focusing error."}],"rule":"Image lands short of the retina (nearsighted) → spread the light first, negative power. Image lands past it (farsighted) → converge it, positive power.","fast":{"kind":"edge","how":"Ask where the image lands. In front → move it back → the lens must spread rays (negative power). Behind → the opposite."},"exp":"The eye is **myopic**: its image forms **short of** the retina. A **diverging (negative-power)** lens spreads the rays slightly so the eye's optics bring them to focus farther back, on the retina.","how":{"ask":"Decide whether the light must be converged or spread to move the focus onto the retina.","know":"**Myopia** (long eye, focus in front) → diverging lens. **Hyperopia** (short eye, focus behind) → converging lens.","steps":[{"text":"Where does the image form? Short of the retina.","hl":"focuses in front of her retina"},{"text":"To push the focus back, the lens must spread the rays; a converging lens would move it forward.","strike":["Converging lens"]},{"text":"Prisms and cylindrical lenses fix other problems (aim and astigmatism).","strike":["Prism lens","Cylindrical lens"]}],"trap":"Mixing up near- and farsightedness: the long eye focuses too early and needs negative power."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find where the image lands**.","marks":[{"t":"slightly too long","note":"myopic eye"},{"t":"in front of her retina","note":"focus too early"}]},{"text":"**Move the focus back** by making the incoming rays diverge a little before they enter the eye."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Image short of the retina → negative (diverging) lens; behind it → positive (converging) lens."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-47a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:47"],"passage":"Inhibitor K was designed to block a trypsin-like protease that prefers positively charged substrates. Its structure contains four guanidino groups (pKa ≈ 12.5), one primary amine (pKa ≈ 10.5), one imidazole ring (pKa ≈ 6.0) and one carboxylic acid group (pKa ≈ 4.5). All binding assays were run in buffer at pH 7.5. Inhibitor K bound the protease tightly in these assays.","q":"At the buffer pH used, Inhibitor K carries a net charge of about:","choices":["+7","+4","+5","+6"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"That counts the carboxylic acid as positive; an acid above its pKa loses H⁺ and turns negative."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"This leaves out the deprotonated carboxylate, which is −1 above its pKa."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The imidazole pKa is below the buffer pH, so it is mostly deprotonated and neutral."}],"rule":"For each group compare pKa with pH: below pKa the group keeps its H (bases +1, acids 0); above pKa it loses it (bases 0, acids −1). Then add.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Bases with pKa above the pH are +1; acids with pKa below the pH are −1. Count +5 bases, imidazole off, one acid −1."},"exp":"At pH 7.5: each guanidino (pKa 12.5) is protonated, **+1 × 4**; the amine (pKa 10.5) is protonated, **+1**; imidazole (pKa 6.0) is **neutral**; the carboxylic acid (pKa 4.5) is **−1**. Net = 4 + 1 + 0 − 1 = **+4**.","how":{"ask":"Decide the charge of each ionizable group at the buffer pH, then add them.","know":"**pH < pKa → protonated; pH > pKa → deprotonated.** A protonated base is +1; a deprotonated acid is −1.","steps":[{"text":"Find the pH.","hl":"pH 7.5"},{"text":"Bases above the pH: four guanidino + one amine, all +1.","calc":["+4 + 1 = +5"]},{"text":"Imidazole (pKa 6.0) is below the pH: neutral. Counting it gives too much.","strike":["+6"]},{"text":"Carboxylic acid (pKa 4.5) is deprotonated: −1. Leaving it out, or counting it as +1, misses.","calc":["+5 − 1 = +4"],"strike":["+5","+7"]}],"trap":"Counting imidazole as charged: with pKa 6.0 it is mostly neutral at pH 7.5."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **list each group with its pKa** and the pH.","marks":[{"t":"pKa ≈ 12.5","note":"guanidino: +1 each"},{"t":"pKa ≈ 10.5","note":"amine: +1"},{"t":"pKa ≈ 6.0","note":"imidazole: 0"},{"t":"pKa ≈ 4.5","note":"acid: −1"}]},{"text":"**Compare pKa with pH** for each group, one at a time."},{"text":"**Add the charges.**","calc":["4(+1) + (+1) + 0 + (−1) = +4"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Protonated base = +1, deprotonated acid = −1; pH above pKa removes the H. Sum the groups."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-48a","s":"Metabolism & Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:48"],"passage":"Long-chain fatty acids cannot cross the inner mitochondrial membrane as acyl-CoA. On the outer membrane, carnitine palmitoyltransferase I moves the acyl group from CoA onto carnitine, a small molecule that carries a hydroxyl group, a carboxylate and a trimethylammonium group. The product, acylcarnitine, is an ester rather than a thioester. A translocase carries acylcarnitine across the inner membrane, where a second transferase returns the acyl group to CoA.","q":"In acylcarnitine, the acyl carbonyl carbon is bonded to which part of carnitine?","choices":["A thiol sulfur","Its ammonium nitrogen","Its hydroxyl oxygen","Its carboxylate oxygen"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"Carnitine has no sulfur, and the passage says the product is not a thioester."},{"c":1,"tag":"impossible","why":"A trimethylammonium nitrogen has four bonds and no lone pair, so it cannot form a new bond."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Acylating a carboxylate would give an anhydride, not the ester the passage describes."}],"rule":"Name the bond type the passage gives (ester, thioester, amide), then find the carrier atom that can form it: O–H → ester, S–H → thioester, N–H → amide.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"\"Ester\" = acyl C bonded to an O that was an alcohol O–H. Find the alcohol group on the carrier."},"exp":"The passage calls acylcarnitine an **ester**. An ester forms when an alcohol **O–H** attacks the acyl carbonyl, so the acyl carbon is bonded to carnitine's **hydroxyl oxygen**.","how":{"ask":"Match the bond type named in the passage to the carrier's functional group.","know":"**Ester:** acyl–O–R from an alcohol. **Thioester:** acyl–S–R from a thiol. **Amide:** acyl–N from an amine with an N–H.","steps":[{"text":"What bond forms? An ester, not a thioester.","hl":"is an ester rather than a thioester","strike":["A thiol sulfur"]},{"text":"Which group can make an ester? The alcohol.","hl":"a hydroxyl group"},{"text":"A quaternary N cannot bond again, and a carboxylate would give an anhydride.","strike":["Its ammonium nitrogen","Its carboxylate oxygen"]}],"trap":"Assuming every acyl carrier uses a thiol, as CoA does: carnitine forms an oxygen ester."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the bond type and the groups** on carnitine.","marks":[{"t":"an ester rather than a thioester","note":"O, not S"},{"t":"a hydroxyl group","note":"the alcohol that forms the ester"}]},{"text":"**Ester = acyl bonded to an alcohol oxygen.** The O–H oxygen attacks the carbonyl carbon and keeps the acyl group."},{"text":"**Also:** CoA and acyl carrier protein hold acyl groups as thioesters on a terminal thiol."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Read the bond name: ester → hydroxyl O, thioester → thiol S, amide → amine N."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-50a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:50"],"passage":"Spermidine is a polyamine whose three nitrogen atoms are all protonated at cellular pH, giving it a net charge of +3. An enzyme that acetylates spermidine was crystallized with the polyamine bound in its active site. The residues lining the pocket within 4 Å of the bound spermidine are Asp88, Lys90, Ser92, Arg127, Glu131 and Gln135. Mutating residues near the polyamine changed its binding affinity.","q":"Salt bridges to the bound polyamine would most likely come from which two residues?","choices":["Lys90 and Arg127","Asp88 and Lys90","Asp88 and Glu131","Ser92 and Gln135"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Lys and Arg are positive like the polyamine, so they would repel it, not bind it ionically."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Only one of this pair carries the opposite charge; the other is positive like the ligand."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Ser and Gln have uncharged polar side chains: they can hydrogen-bond but cannot form salt bridges."}],"rule":"Ionic interaction = opposite charges. Find the ligand's charge, then pick residues whose side chains carry the opposite charge at cell pH.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ligand +, so look for −: the acidic residues Asp and Glu."},"exp":"Spermidine is **positive (+3)**, so salt bridges need **negatively charged** side chains. Asp and Glu are deprotonated (−1) at cellular pH, so **Asp88 and Glu131** can bind it ionically.","how":{"ask":"Find the ligand's charge and pick the two residues of opposite charge.","know":"At cellular pH: **Asp, Glu −1; Lys, Arg +1**; Ser, Thr, Asn, Gln uncharged polar.","steps":[{"text":"Ligand charge: positive.","hl":"a net charge of +3"},{"text":"Positive residues repel it; a mixed pair has only one partner.","strike":["Lys90 and Arg127","Asp88 and Lys90"]},{"text":"Neutral polar residues only hydrogen-bond.","strike":["Ser92 and Gln135"]},{"text":"Both acidic residues remain."}],"trap":"Picking the basic residues by habit because they usually bind phosphates: check the ligand's sign first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the ligand charge and the residues**.","marks":[{"t":"a net charge of +3","note":"cation"},{"t":"Asp88, Lys90, Ser92, Arg127, Glu131 and Gln135","note":"sort by charge"}]},{"text":"**Opposites attract.** A cationic ligand pairs with carboxylate side chains."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Salt bridge partners carry the opposite full charge: anion ligand → Lys/Arg; cation ligand → Asp/Glu."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-51a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:51"],"passage":"Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) interconverts pyruvate and lactate using the NAD⁺/NADH pair. To measure LDH released from damaged heart cells, a clinical assay supplies lactate and NAD⁺ to a serum sample. NADH absorbs strongly in the near-ultraviolet at a wavelength where NAD⁺ does not. In samples from patients after a heart attack, the absorbance at that wavelength climbed steadily for several minutes.","q":"In these patient samples, the steady climb in absorbance shows that:","choices":["NADH is used up.","lactate is building up.","NADH is forming.","NADPH is forming."],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Losing the only absorbing species would make the reading fall, not climb."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"The assay supplies lactate; LDH consumes it here, and lactate does not absorb at that wavelength anyway."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"LDH uses NAD⁺/NADH, and the assay supplies no NADP⁺ to make NADPH from."}],"rule":"Absorbance tracks the concentration of the absorbing species: rising signal → that species is being made; falling → it is being used.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Only NADH absorbs. Signal up = NADH up."},"exp":"Absorbance is proportional to the concentration of the absorbing species (Beer–Lambert). Only **NADH** absorbs at that wavelength, so a **rising** absorbance means NADH is **forming**: LDH oxidizes lactate to pyruvate while reducing NAD⁺ to NADH.","how":{"ask":"Tie the direction of the absorbance change to the concentration of the absorbing molecule.","know":"**A = εbc**: absorbance rises and falls with concentration. Lactate + NAD⁺ → pyruvate + NADH.","steps":[{"text":"Which species absorbs? NADH only.","hl":"NADH absorbs strongly in the near-ultraviolet at a wavelength where NAD⁺ does not"},{"text":"Which way did it go? Up.","hl":"climbed steadily","strike":["NADH is used up."]},{"text":"Lactate is the supplied substrate and NADPH is not part of this pair.","strike":["lactate is building up.","NADPH is forming."]}],"trap":"Assuming the familiar coupled assay in which the signal falls: check the direction of the reaction the passage describes."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the absorber and the trend**.","marks":[{"t":"NADH absorbs strongly","note":"the only absorber"},{"t":"climbed steadily","note":"concentration rising"}]},{"text":"**Beer–Lambert.** More absorber in the light path → higher absorbance."},{"text":"**Write the reaction.** Lactate + NAD⁺ → pyruvate + NADH, so NAD⁺ is reduced as NADH appears."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Signal up → the absorbing species is being produced; signal down → it is being consumed."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-53a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:53"],"passage":"Fluorine-18 is a cyclotron-made isotope used to label glucose for PET scans. A hospital physicist measured the activity of one dose at regular intervals after it arrived. The dose read 640 MBq on arrival, 320 MBq after 110 minutes, 160 MBq after 220 minutes and 80 MBq after 330 minutes. The dose must be injected while its activity is still high enough to give a clear image.","q":"From the physicist's readings, what is the half-life of fluorine-18?","choices":["55 min","110 min","220 min","330 min"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Halving the interval confuses half-life with half the interval; read the time for activity to halve."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"By that time the activity has halved twice, to a quarter of the start."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"That is just the last time listed, when an eighth of the activity remained."}],"rule":"Half-life = how long the activity takes to drop by half. Read it from one pair of readings and confirm with the next pair.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Find the first reading that is half the starting value; its time is the half-life."},"exp":"The activity halves in each interval: 640 → 320 → 160 → 80 MBq. Each halving takes **110 minutes**, so that is the half-life.","how":{"ask":"Find how long the activity takes to drop by half.","know":"**After each half-life the activity halves.** After n half-lives, A = A₀/2ⁿ.","steps":[{"text":"Start: 640 MBq. Half of that is 320 MBq.","hl":"320 MBq after 110 minutes"},{"text":"Check: 320 → 160 also takes the same interval."},{"text":"220 and 330 minutes are two and three half-lives; 55 minutes is half of one.","strike":["220 min","330 min","55 min"]}],"trap":"Reading off when the dose is down to a quarter or an eighth: each halving step is one half-life."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the readings**.","marks":[{"t":"640 MBq on arrival","note":"A₀"},{"t":"320 MBq after 110 minutes","note":"half of A₀"}]},{"text":"**Halves are equal steps in time.** 640 → 320 → 160 → 80, one equal interval each."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** One half-life separates any reading from the reading at half its value."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-54a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:54"],"passage":"Iodine-131 is used to treat an overactive thyroid gland, because the gland takes up iodide from the blood. Each iodine-131 nucleus decays by β⁻ emission to xenon-131, as a neutron in the nucleus becomes a proton. The charged particle released deposits its energy within about a millimeter of tissue, destroying nearby thyroid cells. A gamma ray emitted soon afterward escapes the body and lets clinicians image the gland.","q":"In this decay of iodine-131, which emitted particle carries an electric charge?","choices":["Antineutrino","Positron","Electron","Alpha particle"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"An antineutrino is electrically neutral, so it cannot be the charged particle."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"A positron comes from β⁺ decay, when a proton becomes a neutron; here a neutron becomes a proton."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Alpha particles come from alpha decay of heavy nuclei; β⁻ decay does not release one."}],"rule":"β⁻: n → p + e⁻ + antineutrino. β⁺: p → n + e⁺ + neutrino. Gamma rays and neutrinos carry no charge.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Neutron → proton means the nucleus gained +1, so a −1 particle must leave to conserve charge."},"exp":"In **β⁻ decay** a neutron becomes a proton and the nucleus emits an **electron (β⁻)** and an antineutrino. Charge is conserved: 0 → +1 + (−1). The gamma ray and the antineutrino are neutral.","how":{"ask":"Write the β⁻ decay and pick the emitted particle with charge.","know":"**β⁻:** n → p + e⁻ + ν̄. **β⁺:** p → n + e⁺ + ν. γ and neutrinos are neutral.","steps":[{"text":"Which decay? β⁻: a neutron turns into a proton.","hl":"a neutron in the nucleus becomes a proton","strike":["Positron"]},{"text":"Balance charge: 0 → +1 + (−1), so a negative particle leaves."},{"text":"The antineutrino is neutral, and no alpha particle is involved.","strike":["Antineutrino","Alpha particle"]}],"trap":"Mixing up β⁻ and β⁺: β⁺ releases the positive particle."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the decay type**.","marks":[{"t":"β⁻ emission","note":"beta-minus"},{"t":"a neutron in the nucleus becomes a proton","note":"charge +1 gained"}]},{"text":"**Conserve charge.** The nucleus gains +1, so the emitted particle carries −1."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** β⁻ emits e⁻ (plus an antineutrino); β⁺ emits e⁺ (plus a neutrino); only the beta particle is charged."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-55a","s":"Waves & Sound","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:55"],"passage":"Echocardiography uses ultrasound pulses to image the heart. In ordinary pulse-echo mode, the machine times each echo to find how deep a reflecting surface lies, which gives chamber sizes and wall thickness. In Doppler mode, it compares the frequency of the returning echoes with the emitted frequency. A cardiologist studying a patient with heart failure recorded the left-ventricle diameter, the wall thickness, the depth of the heart below the chest wall, and how fast the ventricle wall moved during relaxation.","q":"Which of the cardiologist's measurements needed Doppler mode rather than pulse-echo mode?","choices":["Ventricle diameter","Wall relaxation speed","Wall thickness","Depth below the chest wall"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A chamber diameter is a distance between two echoing surfaces, found by echo timing."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Thickness is the gap between two reflecting surfaces, which pulse-echo timing gives directly."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Depth comes straight from the round-trip time of one echo; no frequency shift is needed."}],"rule":"Pulse-echo times echoes, so it gives distances; Doppler compares frequencies, so it gives speeds of moving reflectors.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Ask of each choice: is it a distance or a speed? Only a speed needs a frequency shift."},"exp":"Pulse-echo imaging converts **echo time to distance**, so it handles diameters, thicknesses and depths. Only a **velocity** of a moving reflector needs the **frequency shift** that Doppler mode measures: how fast the ventricle wall moved.","how":{"ask":"Sort each measurement into a distance (echo timing) or a speed (frequency shift).","know":"**Pulse-echo:** depth = (sound speed × round-trip time)/2. **Doppler:** speed from the frequency shift of a moving reflector.","steps":[{"text":"Doppler mode compares frequencies, which reveals motion.","hl":"compares the frequency of the returning echoes with the emitted frequency"},{"text":"Diameter, thickness and depth are distances: pulse-echo handles them.","strike":["Ventricle diameter","Wall thickness","Depth below the chest wall"]},{"text":"The one speed in the list needs Doppler."}],"trap":"Thinking a moving organ needs Doppler for every measurement: sizes of a moving heart still come from echo timing."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what each mode measures**.","marks":[{"t":"times each echo","note":"distances"},{"t":"compares the frequency","note":"speeds"}]},{"text":"**Classify the measurements.** Diameter, thickness, depth: lengths. Wall motion: a speed."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Distances → pulse-echo timing; velocities → Doppler frequency shift."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-56a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:56"],"passage":"In a study of muscle blood flow, volunteers pedaled a stationary cycle ergometer while a tracer was injected. The ergometer's brake was set so that each volunteer delivered a constant mechanical power. One volunteer held 60 W for 3.0 minutes, then rested while images were acquired. Leg blood flow rose roughly in proportion to the power delivered.","q":"What total work did this volunteer perform during the bout?","choices":["0.18 kJ","648 kJ","3.6 kJ","10.8 kJ"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"That multiplies watts by minutes; a watt is a joule per second, so the time must be in seconds."},{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"The minutes were converted to seconds twice, inflating the work sixtyfold."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"That is the work for one minute only; the bout lasted three."}],"rule":"Work = power × time, with time in seconds (1 W = 1 J/s). Convert minutes × 60 exactly once.","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"W × s = J. 3.0 min = 180 s; 60 × 180 = 10,800 J."},"exp":"At constant power, **W = P·t**. t = 3.0 min × 60 s/min = 180 s, so W = 60 W × 180 s = 10,800 J = **10.8 kJ**.","how":{"ask":"Multiply the power by the time in seconds.","know":"**W = P·t**; 1 W = 1 J/s; 1 min = 60 s.","steps":[{"text":"Pull out the power and time.","hl":"60 W for 3.0 minutes"},{"text":"Convert time once.","calc":["3.0 min × 60 = 180 s"]},{"text":"Multiply.","calc":["60 W × 180 s = 10,800 J"]},{"text":"Watts × minutes, a double conversion, or a single minute give the other values.","strike":["0.18 kJ","648 kJ","3.6 kJ"]}],"trap":"Multiplying watts by minutes: the watt is defined per second."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find power and time**.","marks":[{"t":"60 W","note":"J each second"},{"t":"3.0 minutes","note":"180 s"}]},{"text":"**Energy = rate × duration.**","calc":["W = 60 × 180 = 10,800 J"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** W = P × t with t in seconds; check that W·s gives J."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-57a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 3","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:57"],"q":"A cell-free reaction must be held at pH 5.5. Which weak acid, identified by its pKa, would make the best buffer?","choices":["3.5","5.6","7.0","8.5"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is 14 minus the target pH, a pOH mix-up; the buffer pKa should match the pH itself."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That pKa sits two units below the target, outside the ±1 range where a buffer works well."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Neutral pH has nothing special for buffering; capacity peaks where pH equals the pKa."}],"rule":"Best buffer: pKa within 1 unit of the target pH, and the closer the better.","fast":{"kind":"anchor","how":"Pick the pKa nearest the target pH; anything more than 1 unit away is out."},"exp":"A buffer resists pH change best when **pH ≈ pKa**, where [HA] = [A⁻]. Only a pKa within ±1 of 5.5 works, and **5.6** is the closest.","how":{"ask":"Choose the pKa closest to the target pH.","know":"**Henderson–Hasselbalch:** pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]). Maximum capacity at pH = pKa; useful range pKa ± 1.","steps":[{"text":"Target pH.","hl":"pH 5.5"},{"text":"Keep only pKa values within one unit of it.","strike":["3.5","7.0","8.5"]},{"text":"The remaining choice is nearly equal to the target."}],"trap":"Picking a neutral pKa because the system is biological: match the stated pH, not 7."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the target pH**.","marks":[{"t":"pH 5.5","note":"want pKa near this"}]},{"text":"**Equal acid and base forms** sit at pH = pKa, where added acid or base is absorbed best."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Buffer pKa ≈ target pH (within ±1)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-58a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:58"],"q":"A 0.40 kg aluminum block absorbs 7200 J of heat while warming from 22 °C to 42 °C. What is the specific heat of aluminum?","choices":["144 J/(kg·°C)","360 J/(kg·°C)","429 J/(kg·°C)","900 J/(kg·°C)"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"That multiplies by the mass instead of dividing by it; c = q ÷ (m·ΔT)."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"That divides by ΔT but leaves out the mass of the block."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"That uses the final temperature, 42 °C, instead of the change in temperature."}],"rule":"c = q ÷ (m × ΔT), with ΔT = final − initial. Check units: J ÷ (kg·°C).","fast":{"kind":"units","how":"Let J/(kg·°C) guide you: divide joules by kilograms and by degrees of change. 0.40 × 20 = 8."},"exp":"**q = mcΔT**, so c = q/(mΔT). ΔT = 42 − 22 = 20 °C. c = 7200 J ÷ (0.40 kg × 20 °C) = 7200 ÷ 8 = **900 J/(kg·°C)**.","how":{"ask":"Rearrange q = mcΔT for c and use the temperature change.","know":"**q = mcΔT**; ΔT = T_final − T_initial.","steps":[{"text":"Find ΔT.","hl":"from 22 °C to 42 °C","calc":["ΔT = 42 − 22 = 20 °C"]},{"text":"Divide q by (m × ΔT).","calc":["c = 7200 ÷ (0.40 × 20) = 7200 ÷ 8"]},{"text":"Multiplying by m, dropping m, or using 42 °C give the other values.","strike":["144 J/(kg·°C)","360 J/(kg·°C)","429 J/(kg·°C)"]}],"trap":"Plugging in a temperature reading instead of the change: specific heat uses ΔT."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find q, m and the two temperatures**.","marks":[{"t":"7200 J","note":"q"},{"t":"0.40 kg","note":"m"},{"t":"from 22 °C to 42 °C","note":"ΔT = 20 °C"}]},{"text":"**Rearrange and divide.**","calc":["c = q/(mΔT) = 7200/(0.40 × 20)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** c = q/(m·ΔT); the units J/(kg·°C) show exactly what to divide by."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-st-59a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":[],"twinOf":["sample-cp:59"],"q":"In strongly acidic solution, the keto acid oxalosuccinate (three carboxylate groups, all ionized) is reduced by NADPH to neutral isocitric acid, with NADP⁺ formed. How many H⁺ must be added to the left side to balance the equation?","choices":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That covers only the new alcohol H; each carboxylate in the neutral acid also needs its own proton."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"That treats NADP⁺ as neutral; it carries +1, so the right side is +1, not 0."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"That uses a −1 substrate, as for pyruvate; this keto acid carries three carboxylates, so it is −3."}],"rule":"Balance H⁺ by charge: (substrate charge) + x = +1 for NAD(P)⁺ plus a neutral product; count every carboxylate.","fast":{"kind":"rearrange","how":"Charge only: left −3 + x, right +1 (NADP⁺) + 0 (neutral acid). Solve for x."},"exp":"Charge check: left = −3 (oxalosuccinate) + 0 (NADPH) + x; right = 0 (isocitric acid) + 1 (NADP⁺). So −3 + x = +1 and **x = 4**. Hydrogen check: one H arrives as hydride from NADPH; free H⁺ supply the rest: one finishes the alcohol and three protonate the carboxylates.","how":{"ask":"Find the H⁺ coefficient that balances charge (and hydrogen).","know":"NADPH → NADP⁺ + H⁻ (hydride). **NADPH is neutral, NADP⁺ is +1.** A neutral carboxylic acid needs every carboxylate protonated.","steps":[{"text":"Charges on each side.","hl":"three carboxylate groups, all ionized","calc":["left: −3 + x","right: 0 + 1 = +1"]},{"text":"Solve for x; forgetting the +1 on NADP⁺ gives a value one too small.","calc":["−3 + x = +1 → x = 4"],"strike":["Three"]},{"text":"Check H: hydride gives one H; protons must finish the alcohol and all three carboxylates, so one or two H⁺ fall short.","strike":["One","Two"]}],"trap":"Treating the substrate as −1 like pyruvate, or forgetting that NADP⁺ is +1."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **note the charges** on every species.","marks":[{"t":"three carboxylate groups, all ionized","note":"−3"},{"t":"neutral isocitric acid","note":"0"},{"t":"NADP⁺","note":"+1"}]},{"text":"**Balance charge.**","calc":["−3 + x = +1"]},{"text":"**Confirm with H.** Hydride from NADPH reduces C=O; protons finish the alcohol and every carboxylate."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** With NAD(P)H → NAD(P)⁺, add H⁺ until the charges on both sides match; count every carboxylate, then double-check H atoms."}]}
];
